INTRODUCTION
History can never be undone.
European economic integration and the unification of the internal market are achievements which will mark the end of this century.
Of course, some instruments of this policy have yet to be put into place and I will keenly apply myself to that task.
One of these, the Office for Harmonization in the Internal Market (trade marks and designs), is of vital importance to industrial and commercial enterprises which are now aware that the protection of Intellectual property Is essential for success in international competition.
The Office will enable them to obtain uniform protection of trade marks for their goods and services throughout the Union at lower cost.
They will also be able to benefit in the near future from the protection of industrial designs under the same conditions.
The establishment of this Office, which has its headquarters in Alicante, is a new and important sfage in European Union policy aimed at promoting its economic and social development.
