Nathalie Christmann secretary of the European Ombudsman
Ilta Helkama, information officer Daniela Tirelli, administration of complaints
THE EUROPEAN OMBUDSMAN
WHAT ARE THE INSTITUTIONS AND BODIES ?
One of the rights of European citizens is to complain to the European Ombudsman.
You can use a standard form to help you in drafting your complaint.
This is available from the Ombudsman's office and from the national Ombudsmen's offices in the Member States.
If you have access to the Internet, the form can be downloaded from http://www.europarl.eu.int.
A complaint must be made within two years of the date when you got to know the facts on which your complaint is based.
The European Ombudsman investigates complaints about maladministration by institutions and bodies of the European Community.
Tfie Ombudsman cannot deal with complaints concerning national, regional or local adminis trations of the Member States.
You need not be individually affected by the maladministration, but you must already have contacted the institution or body concerned, for example by a letter.
The European Ombudsman is elected by the European Parliament for the du ration of its term of office.
The Ombudsman does not deal with matters that are currently before a court or that have already been settled by a court.
The main institutions and bodies of the European Community are: The European Commission The Council of the European Union The European Parliament The Court of Auditors The Court of Justice (except in its judicial role) The Economic and Social Committee The Committee of the Regions The European Monetary Institute The European Investment Bank The European Investment Fund
Mr. Jacob Söderman, former Finnish Parliamentary Ombudsman, was elec ted the first European Ombudsman in 1995.
WHAT WILL HAPPEN TO YOUR COMPLAINT ?
WHAT IS MALADMINISTRATION ?
The Ombudsman will first examine your complaint to see if he can deal with it.
If he can, the Ombudsman begins an inquiry.
If he cannot, you will be told why.
If you are a citizen of a Member State of the Union or living in a Member State, you can make a complaint to the European Ombudsman.
Businesses, associations or other bodies with a registered office in the Union may also complain to the Ombudsman.
Complaints are normally dealt with in a public way, but are dealt with confi dentially if the complainant so requests.
This occurs if an institution fails to do something it should have done, if it does it in the wrong way or if it does something that ought not to be done.
Some examples are: administrative irregularities unfairness discrimination abuse of power lack or refusal of information unnecessary delay
You can complain to the Ombudsman about maladministration in the activi ties of Community institutions and bodies.
HOW TO COMPLAIN ?
Some cases are resolved in the initial stages of an inquiry.
In other cases, the Ombudsman tries to find a friendly solution.
If necessary, he can make re commendations to the body concerned on how to solve the case.
The body must then inform the Ombudsman within three months about the measures it intends to take.
If it does not accept the recommendations made by the Ombudsman, the Ombudsman can make a special report about the case to the European Parliament.
The Ombudsman always informs the complainant of the outcome of his in quiries.
Write to the Ombudsman in any of the 11 official languages of the Union, setting out clearly who you are, which institution or body of the European Community you are complaining against and the grounds for your com plaint.
