EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
REPORT OF THE COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY
Mr President,
In accordance with Rule 136(10) of the Rules of Procedure, we hereby submit to you a report on the results of the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System and request that you invite the Parliament to hold a debate at the next part-session.
The report is divided into four volumes:
Volume I Final Report and Recommendations Volume Π Oral evidence Volume ΠΙ Written contributions Volume TV Contributions from the Institutions of the Union
ChaVman: John TOMLJJMSON
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
19 February 1997
FINAL REPORT AND RECOMMENDATIONS
ENGLISH VERSION
Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Membership
Chairman: Mr John TOMLINSON
1st Vice-Chair: Mr Stefano DE LUCA
2nd Vice-Chair: Mrs Edith MÜLLER
Members
Mr Freddy BLAK Mr Rinaldo BONTEMPI Mr Herbert BOSCH Mr Gérard CAUDRON Mrs Marianne ERIKSSON Mr Juan M. FABRA VALLES Mr Hervé FABRE-AUBRESPY Mr Jan MULDER Mr Roy PERRY Mr Bartho PRONK Mr Raul Miguel ROSADO FERNANDES Mr Reino PAASILINNA (replaced Mr M. RÖNNHOLM) Mr Paul RÜBIG (replaced Mr F. KÖNIG) Mr Marcel SCHLECHTER Mr Gianni TAMINO (replaced Mrs P. McKENNA) Mr Jaak VANDEMEULEBROUCKE Mrs P. RUSANEN (until November 1996)
Michael Shackleton, Head of Secretariat, Stephen Clark, Helmut Betz, Regnar Buch, Brenda James, Viviane Johnson, Sylvie Schill
A. The Treaty on European Union (Article 138c) provided a legal base for the European Parliament to establish a temporary Committee of Inquiry to investigate alleged contraventions or maladministration in the implementation of Community law.
It also specified that the detailed provisions governing the exercise of this right should be determined by common accord of the European Parliament, the Council and the Commission.
B. In April 1995 the three institutions adopted a decision laying down these detailed provisions (OJ LI 13) and subsequently the European Parliament amended its Rules of Procedure to incorporate a specific rule (Rule 136) governing the operation of committees of inquiry.
Establishment
C. On the basis of a proposal from the Conference of Presidents, the Parliament decided at its plenary session in December 1995 to set up a temporary Committee of Inquiry to examine alleged contraventions or maladministration in the Community transit system.
This decision was published in the Official Journal of the European Communities on 12 January 1996 (C 7).
D. The committee held its constituent meeting on Tuesday, 16 January 1996.
At this meeting it elected Mr John TOMLINSON as Chairman and Mr Stefano DE LUCA and Mrs Edith MÜLLER as first and second Vice-Chairs respectively.
At the same meeting it appointed Mr Edward KELLETT-BOWMAN as its rapporteur.
Pattern of work
E. The activity of the committee spread over 13 months from January 1996 to February 1997, after it requested and obtained a single three month extension of its original twelve month mandate in October 1996.
During this period, the committee: held 37 meetings, including 16 sessions of evidence, during which 62 individuals from 33 organisations testified before the committee (see Volume II) ; issued a formal call for evidence, through the Official Journal and on the Internet, which generated 41 written contributions (see Volume III); arranged five visits of members of the committee to Geneva and Berne, the Austrian frontier with Slovakia and Hungary, Rome and Naples, Hamm and Antwerp (see Volume ΠΙ); established a network of contacts with the authorities of the Member States through their Permanent Representations in Brussels, including sending to them a questionnaire on the transit system (see Volume III);
obtained detailed answers to specific questions from the Commission and the Court of Auditors in the course of hearings and by written procedure (see Volume TV) ; examined over 100 pieces of supplementary background material from outside and within the Parliament (see Appendix in this volume) ; and organised two consultative meetings in January 1997 with the operators and managers of the transit system to discuss a preliminary version of the draft final report.
In the light of the information it received, the committee examined three progress reports from its rapporteur, Mr KELLETT-BOWMAN, and at its last meeting on 19 February 1997 adopted with 12 votes in favour and one against the final report and recommendations contained in this volume for submission to the Parliament in accordance with Rule 136 (10) of the Rules of Procedure.
The following were present during the vote: Tomlinson, Chairman; De Luca and Müller, Vice Chairs; Kellett-Bowman, Rapporteur; Blak, Bosch, Bontempi, Caudron, Dankert, Fabra Valles, Fabre Aubrespy, Garriga Polledo, Katiforis, Marset Campos, Mulder, Perry, Pronk, Rübig, Sarlis, Schlechter, Tantino, Theato, Theorin, Vandemeulebroucke and Wemheuer.
The committee also adopted with 12 votes in favour and 1 vote against a draft recommendation (A4-0054/97) in accordance with Rule 136 (10), paragraph 3.
The minority opinion of Mr Thierry Jean-Pierre is included at the end of this volume of the report.
The report was submitted on 20th February 1997.
