The Community Transit system, though requiring that the principal at the outset notify the customs office of departure of the point of destination, does not at present disallow a change in the office of destination while the goods are en route.
This change of destination does not have to be communicated to the authorities.
Similarly, there is no general obligation for the transporter to use a specified route between the offices of departure and destination.
Nevertheless, the Commission recently introduced provisions applying to sensitive goods allowing the office of departure to prescribe a binding itinerary for a consignment and removing the option to change office of destination without explicit permission from the customs office of departure7.
Common Transit
The Common Transit system is a close relative of the Community Transit system.
It relies on the same documentation and procedures, is subject to (almost) identical rules and has similar guarantee arrangements.
It differs from Community Transit in three important respects:
- It is used for operations involving the EU countries plus the EFTA countries and/or the Visegrad countries;8
- It is not compulsory (the TIR system may be used as an alternative)
- It is legally based on an international Convention rather than an internal EU regulation.
Convention on 20 May 19879, and extended to include the Visegrad states with effect from 1 July 199610.
It is managed by a Joint Committee composed of the Heads of the contracting parties' customs administrations.
This body has the power to make operational decisions, but must refer decisions affecting the Convention itself to the contracting States for a consensus decision.
Thus the rules governing the guarantees for sensitive goods were extended to the Common Transit system in the summer of 1996.
The TIR Convention
The TIR System (TIR = Transports Internationaux Routiers) is similarly based on an international convention, signed on 14 November 1975.
At present, 58 countries are contracting parties to the convention.
The TIR System relies on a document known as the "TIR Carnet" which is printed and distributed by the Geneva-based International Road Transport Union (IRU) for issue to the carnet users by the national guarantee associations which are authorised for this purpose by the Contracting parties administrations.
On a day to day basis, the IRU and those guarantee associations are responsible for the good functioning of the commercial aspects of the TIR
The amendment procedure which provides for objection by one Contracting party only to defeat amendments so agreed, means in practice that unanimity is required.
The TIR System shares a number of characteristics with the Community/Common Transit systems.
The TIR carnet contains a set of matching copy 1 and copy 2 sheets, on which all necessary information is included, with one matching pair for each territory traversed.
At the beginning of the operation, the first sheet is deposited with the customs office of departure and "cleared" upon the return of the first matching copy from the customs office of exit from the same customs territory.
This procedure is repeated for each territory crossed with successive matching pairs from the same "carnet".
For the purposes of the TIR Convention, the European Union is a single customs territory.
The system therefore requires, in a way analogous to Community Transit that, within the EU, the customs office of final destination or exit return a paper document to the customs office of departure or entry into the Union.
The fundamental difference between the two systems is that the transport may have originated in or be destined for any of the 58 contracting states, potentially as far afield as Kirghizstan.
However, the guarantee arrangements of TIR system are radically different from those in Community Transit.
Each TIR Carnet is a security document giving rise to a guarantee of $50,000 per lorry-load transported.
A special Tobacco/alcohol carnet (no longer available) was introduced in 1993 for the carriage of tobacco and alcohol, with an increased guarantee limit of $200,000 per carnet.
The TIR Carnet is issued by the guarantee association of the country of departure with the guarantee provided thereby covered by the IRU and a central insurance pool in Switzerland.
Consequently, the guarantee system in TIR is a centralised one, limited in amount and depends on the presence of a secure document.
The issue of TIR carnets is therefore more tightly controlled than as is the case with Tl documents and limited in theory to transporters able to provide assurances of trustworthiness and whose vehicles meet certain minimum standards, relating in particular to security.
In the case of non-completion of a TIR operation, it is the guarantee association of the country in which the last "open" TIR form was deposited which is liable under the guarantee to the customs service concerned.
This proved of major significance in the autumn of 1996, when one of the German guaranteeing associations threatened to cease providing guarantees, following claims against it for goods originating in the UK.12
Comparison between TIR and Community Transit
The following table makes a synoptic analysis of the key differences between TIR and Community transit.
Community Transit*
15 EU Member States
Based on Commission Regulation and managed by Commission
Based on Convention and managed by UN Committees and IRU
Guarantee by transit principal, each responsible for own insurance
Centrally insured guarantee managed by IRU and national affiliates
No fixed guarantee amount
Fixed $50,000 maximum guarantee per load
18 million transactions per year
2.3 million carnets issued per year
Common transit follows Community Transit, with the key exception that it is based on a Convention rather than regulation.
Other Transit Arrangements
Besides the main systems described above, a number of special customs regimes exist which may involve bonded transport of duty free goods.
Examples include the inward processing regime (whereby goods can be imported to be processed and re-exported) or the warehousing regime (whereby goods can be stored in bonded warehouses pending a decision on their final destination.
These regimes are of secondary importance from the point of view of this report and will not generally be considered except where specifically relevant.
A Changing Context
The Community Transit system was created in 1968 in response to the introduction of a common customs tariff in that year.
This system, which in its essentials remains unchanged, covered a European Community of six countries, three of which were already in a customs union, and a volume of trade which was a small fraction of current levels.
The context within which the Community Transit system is required to work has changed radically since its inception.
The Community has itself expanded to include 15 Member States.
The Iron Curtain, which reduced East/West trade to a trickle, has disappeared.
Internal customs borders within the EU have been abolished.
Common Transit has been extended to EFTA and the Visegrad countries.
At the same time, the volume of trade between European countries has increased exponentially.
The TIR system has undergone similar revolutions.
It was established initially in 1948 and has since expanded to include contracting countries as remote from each other as Morocco and Kazakhstan.
Community customs regulations have contained a range of statistical requirements on national customs authorities.
According to the Commission however, these have "never really been applied by the Member States"13.
In the face of the difficulties in obtaining reliable information, the Committee of Inquiry resorted to the inclusion in its questionnaire to Member States of a range of statistical questions.
In part, the exercise served to confirm the impression that statistics were simply not available in many areas.
However, at least as far as volumes are concerned, the Committee met with some success.
Question 1 of the questionnaire asked each Member State to provide figures for the number of Tl documents handled, both as country of departure and as country of destination.
In an attempt to establish trade flows, it also asked for a breakdown by country of destination for outgoing traffic and country of departure for incoming traffic. All Member States (plus
Switzerland14) provided global figures for mcoming/outgoing traffic, but only six (mainly smaller ones)15 could provide the detailed breakdown.
The replies show the following volumes of Tl trade since the introduction of the Single Market in 199316:
"OUTGOING" (Tis issued)
"INCOMING" (Copy 5s of Tl form returned)
Figures for 1993 and 1994 do not include the three new Member States, Austria, Finland and Sweden.
Two interesting facts emerge from these figures.
First, they are huge, and the 1994 figures at least confirm the Commission's "18 milhon" estimate of the number of Tis issued.
(The reason for the "blip" in 1994 is unclear - though it may be connected with enlargement).
Second, there is a stubborn disparity between the number of Tis issued and those returned.
Explanations for this disparity are not easy to find, especially in view of its size, but some hypotheses are possible:
- Given that Tis can be issued for both Community and Common Transit, it is possible that some outgoing Tis were cleared in non-respondent countries.
Swiss figures are included, so in 1993 and 1994 these could include Austria, Finland, Norway, Sweden and Iceland, and in 1995 Norway and Iceland only.
It seems improbable that these countries could absorb the entire difference.
- There is inevitably some "overhang" from one year to the next.
A substantial number of Tis issued in one year will be cleared in the following year.
In an evolving market, this effect would take some time to stabilise.
- Documents are simply not cleared, leaving operations open.
This possibility is the crux of the problem in the transit system and can be attributed to fraud, other irregularity or administrative error.
It would be rash to assume the entire difference between the two sets of figures is attributable to non-clearance, but there are strong indications that a substantial
proportion can be accounted for in this way.
The figures above indicate that this may be a moderate estimate.
This subject will be examined in greater detail in the next section.
A breakdown of figures by Member State also provides an interesting illustration of how the transit system functions.
The following graph shows 1995 figures for outgoing operations (i.e. Tl forms issued as office of departure) and incoming operations (i.e. copy 5s of the TI form sent back as office of destination) for each Member State plus Switzerland:
This graph shows the uneven burden of transit over the Community.
There are several Member States which largely by virtue of their geographical position, can be described as "transit states", while others, more peripheral, see relatively little transit traffic.
Germany is obviously the country most involved, in part because of its long and highly active external land border, in part because of its major trading port, Hamburg, and of course, in part because of the size and productivity of its economy which brings with it a high level of extra-Community trade.
Traditional trading nations possessing large international ports which serve a hinterland extending across the entire continent, the Netherlands and Belgium, play a role disproportionate
A Vulnerable System
Weaknesses of the paper-based system
Without doubt the greatest single weakness of the Community Transit System is the fact that it relies on presentation and exchange of paper documents.
A system designed to cope with a relatively small amount of trade in the late sixties within a group of six Community countries simply cannot cope with today's volumes of trade across a group of 23 countries.
In the words of one witness, the system relies on nineteenth century techniques, such as customs stamps, applied to late twentieth century trade18.
It is sufficient to consider the volumes involved.
There are approximately 3000 customs offices in the Community and Common Transit area acting as offices of departure or destination.
These share - very unevenly - the load of processing up to 18 million transit operations per year.
Each operation involves at least three manual procedures: issuing the Tl, stamping and returning the copy 5 and matching returned copy 5s with original copies.
This produces an annual average of 18,000 operations per office per year (about 69 per working day).
In reality, the picture is worse, in that most member states have central sorting offices for Tl documents, to which and from which copy 5s are dispatched, thus adding a further two manual steps to each operation.
Additionally, some offices and countries see a much greater flow of documents than others.
The Committee's visit to the German central transit sorting office in Hamm, illustrated this graphically: some 19,800 documents are processed by this single office, with a staff of less than ten, every day!20
Additionally, those involved in the trade have great difficulties in verifying the progress and completion of transit movements for which they are responsible.
This practical inability systematically to check documentation renders the system all the more vulnerable to one of the simplest and common forms of fraud - the forging of customs stamps.
The paper-based system relies on a simple rubber stamp on the return copy to verify clearance of a transit operation.
Not only are stamps easy to forge, but it is also practically impossible to do anything more, except in cases of particular suspicion, than merely check whether a stamp is present.
Matters are made worse by the fact that there exists a bewildering range of customs stamps in the countries involved in the Community/Common transit procedures (at times even within one state), many of which would not automatically be recognisable to the overworked eye of a customs official.
Perhaps worse than the impossibility of checking forms is the straightforward inefficiency engendered by the physical movement of such a mass of paper.
It is inevitable that documents will go astray, be sent to the wrong customs office, be lost and misfiled, etc.
Anecdotal evidence even exists of forms simply being discarded by drivers unaware of their purpose.
Problems of this very banal kind are far from negligible: one witness from the
The lower estimates appear to arise from a more restrictive definition of "irregularity" (e.g. problems not resolved by an initial enquiry).
It should come as no surprise following the above that delays are endemic in the transit system.
According to the rules of the transit system, a vehicle should reach its destination within 8 days of departure.
The customs office of destination is required to return the copy 5 of the Ή form within 10 days.
In practice, this simply does not happen.
Figures obtained from the Member States in response to the Committee's questionnaire reveal this plainly.
The following table shows the time on average each Member State claims it takes to return a copy five, and the average time it takes to receive a return copy five back from other Member States.
The discrepancy between the two columns is revealing.
Most Member States clearly have the impression that they are quicker than their colleagues in other countries.
Unless copy 5s are systematically sitting in post offices for days, which seems improbable, this discrepancy merely reveals that customs services probably have very little idea of how long it takes in reality to return a form.
