In contrast to the centralised TIR system, the fragmented nature of the Community transit militates against the efficient collection of statistical information.
This fact is already apparent from the figures given above.
The truth is that neither the Commission nor the Member States know with any degree of certainty how much transit traffic there is, where it is going, how many operations are irregular or fraudulent, how much tax and duty is outstanding, etc.
For example, a French representative admitted that he had no idea how long it takes the 220 French customs offices to return copy 5s.24
This problem greatly conditioned the responses the Member States were able to provide to the Committee's questionnaire.
The German authorities in particular, who, besides having by far the largest number of transit operations, also have to cope with the collection of data in a federalised and as yet uncomputerised customs service, had a great difficulty in providing the figures requested by the Committee within the rimescale requested.
An ineffective inquiry system
The current legislation for the transit system requires that if a customs office of departure has not received a returned copy 5 within 10 weeks, it shall initiate an inquiry
procedure, using a form known as a TC 20.
In practice, this takes the form of a message sent by the office of departure to the declared office of destination asking for confirmation of clearance of the operation in question.
If the declared office of destination is unable to provide such confirmation, it is up to the customs service in the country of departure to establish where the goods under transit have gone.
If the goods have not been cleared in a regular fashion through any customs office, a presumption arises that the goods have fraudulently entered free circulation within the Community.
At this point, the customs office of departure has the responsibility of establishing where the offence took place, in order to attribute to the appropriate Member State the responsibility for investigating the offence and recovering the lost revenue.
If it is unable to do this, it retains responsibility for the case.
The practical difficulties of investigating possible frauds of this nature are such that the customs service concerned usually claims the lost revenue under the principal's guarantee.25
This inquiry procedure is obviously extremely inefficient.
Since the introduction of the Single Market, no border checks exist between EU Member States.
A vehicle travelling, for example, between a point of departure in Denmark to a destination in Portugal would probably travel through Denmark, Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium, France and Spain before reaching Portugal.
None of these countries would normally be aware of the presence of the vehicle on their territory.
Unless therefore a fraudster is caught red-handed unloading goods from the transit irregularly, it is almost impossible to establish under whose jurisdiction the offence took place.
The situation is made worse by the fact that vehicles have eight days to complete their journeys, generally follow no prescribed routes, and usually have the option of changing the office of destination without prior notice to the customs authorities.
In practice therefore, a "lost" vehicle could be almost anywhere in the Community.
Not only are there problems in correctly establishing jurisdiction, there are also substantial practical difficulties to be overcome.
The delays in the system in the first place lead to the initiation of many unnecessary inquiry procedures, constituting a substantial waste of customs time and resources.
Moreover, the inquiry system itself is slow and cumbersome, with simple inquiries typically taking months to process.
In practice, the standard inquiry system has deteriorated into a formalised paperchase for missing documents rather than an effective instrument for investigating fraud.
Figures obtained by the Commission illustrate the extent of the paperchase26.
In 1993, nearly half a million inquiries were received by the 12 Member States which provided figures and some states initiated over 400,000 inquiries.
Some of these states also indicated how many of these inquiries were still pending one year or more after the transit declaration and the figures were not insubstantial: 4,000 in the UK, 2,280 in Portugal and 1,110 in Sweden.
The Committee saw a specific illustration of the problem in Antwerp where a customs official informed the Committee's delegation that it was simply not worth initiating an inquiry at the expiry of the stipulated 10-week deadline for the return of a Tl copy 5 as most of the documents outstanding would simply return late in the following weeks.
Belgian customs in Antwerp therefore has a unilaterally established policy of only initiating inquiries after 20
It is clear from the above that the transit system is vulnerable to a significant degree of administrative error.
Some witnesses in the Committee of Inquiry have pointed to this fact as indicating that the problems in transit are not as severe as they are sometimes painted28.
While true that administrative error is not as serious a problem as fraud from the point of view of public revenue collection, its consequences should not be underestimated.
An enormous amount of public time and money is wasted chasing up lost documentation: in the meantime, principals' guarantees remain outstanding, thus creating often massive potential liabilities in their balance sheets.
The latter problem is not a purely technical matter as some Member States may refuse to allow principals to continue to trade when their outstanding potential liability exceeds their guarantee amount.
In addition, freight forwarders can be made technically insolvent by this liability and may encounter extra costs and difficulties with their banks and insurers.
A fraud-prone system
All the problems detailed above create an opening for the fraudster.
Most "classical" transit fraud29falls into one of two categories:
- Transit transactions remain incomplete. - Transit transactions are completed fraudulently.
All the cases described to the Committee of Inquiry of fraud within the transit system itself fit into one of these two broad categories, though there are a number of different means by which the fraudulent end can be achieved.
In the first case, the fraud usually relies on the inefficiency of the system.
Goods can simply be unloaded clandestinely en route and placed on the black market in the confident expectation that by the time the deadlines for arrival at destination, return of copy 5, initiation of inquiry procedure and replies to the inquiries have passed, the fraudster will have had adequate time to make his profit and disappear.
Indeed, in many cases, the fraudster has time to carry out repeat fraudulent operations by the time the customs service notice even the first transaction was irregular.
A prime example of this was a case brought before the Committee of Inquiry where 76 fraudulent transit operations involving truckloads of tobacco and alcohol were undertaken before the fraud was discovered30.
The amount involved in the fraud quoted by the company reached more than 7 billion Escudos (Ecu 36 milhon).
Although this is a dramatic case, repeat offences of this kind are common.
A slightly more sophisticated fraud involves the false clearance of a transit operation.
This can be achieved by means of a forged or stolen customs stamp or by means of a corrupt or bogus customs official using a genuine stamp.
Cases falling into these categories are also common31.
There are two principal advantages to be derived from false clearance of a transit operation.
In the first place, it is unlikely that the fraud would be detected at all, allowing it to be repeated indefinitely.
Secondly, unless subsequently discovered, the fraud will not give rise to any claim on the principal's guarantee.
This fact is clearly useful if the freight forwarder is himself involved in the fraud, but is also helpful if the freight forwarder is innocent as he remains unaware of the problem and will continue to provide the forwarding service required.
Particularly since the introduction of the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods, a further sub-category of fraud within the transit system has become attractive without presenting enormous risks to the fraudster.
This is simply to misdeclare the goods on the transit documentation, thereby avoiding the heavier financial liability arising from sensitive goods and the extra attentions of the customs services which such goods attract.
Misdeclaration can only be detected by way of physical checks which are small in number - especially in the transit regime which is perceived as a facilitation to trade.
Key Points
The basic failing of the transit system is that it is based on an archaic paper-based procedure originally developed for a handful of countries involved in afar lower level of trade.
The system has not kept pace with the growth of trade, the introduction of the single market, the expanding territory it is required to cover or indeed the increased sophistication of the criminal organisations wishing to exploit it.
As a result, the volume of paper generated by the system exceeds the capacity of customs services to manage it, especially in view of the cumbersome manual procedures involved.
This has often led to often serious delays in the treatment of single transit operations and an unacceptably high level of administrative error, both of which have a considerable adverse financial impact on the users of the system.
Worse though is the manner in which the antiquated and inefficient nature of Community Transit renders it vulnerable to fraud.
Such are the delays and so frequent are the errors that, in the normal course of events, fraud is at best detected only after a long delay and even then is generally dealt with by means of a formalistic enquiryprocedure which is unlikely to lead to the detection of the fraudster.
It is of the utmost importance to improve the statistics of Community Transit Traffic.
