More traditional techniques, such as misdeclaration of goods, can affect the transit system as well as other customs systems.
Is there a crisis in the transit system?
Though apparently simple, this question has been the subject of some heated debate.
Almost all of the Committee's interlocutors have agreed that transit is in a state of crisis.
The most passionate proponent of this point of view is the man generally credited with the initial design of the transit system, Mr Jean Duquesne, now president of ODASCE (a Paris-based body committed to automation and simplification of the procedures for external trade), who testified before the Committee on 4 September 1996.
His written submission to the Committee includes the following assessment:
"The present situation conceals massive and steadily increasing fraud which amounts to the rapid development of a "grey" market throughout Europe's economy; ...
This situation is extremely dangerous for everyone.
Within the Community, it is likely to jeopardise the achievements of the single market.
In Europe as a whole, it hampers legitimate economic growth and will inevitably lead to restrictive measures which may seriously damage the relations between peoples, thus having an effect totally at variance with the ideals of the United Nations."1
Though not everyone employs language as dramatic as that of Mr Duquesne, his view is one shared by most of the economic operators encountered by the Committee.
FreightForward Europe, discussing the overhang of as yet uncollected debts, expressed the problem in more pragmatic terms:
"If all outstanding duties resulting from fraud and transit were to be collected, a large number of forwarding companies would go bankrupt"2
Many witnesses saw problems not only for themselves but for the system as a whole.
CLECAT, the European association of smaller freight forwarders and hauliers, for example, talks of "changes that should be made in order to save the system from collapsing altogether".3
"Collapse" is a term which has arisen frequently in the Committee's hearings.
It applies equally, perhaps more so, to the TIR system, in that accumulating claims can threaten the system as a whole, rather than individual operators.
At one point collapse seemed an imminent and real possibility.
Reeling from huge claims on its guarantee which the central pool of insurers was refusing to cover, the German guarantee association, BDF4, threatened to withdraw completely from the TIR system at the end of September 1996.
The consequence would have been to render the TIR system inoperable within the European Union, probably thus causing East-West trade to grind to a near-standstill.5
Public authorities, though generally more circumspect in their language, often take a similar line.
The French and Spanish Customs authorities, for example, concurred in saying that the current system in obsolete.6The Commission itself readily employs the term "crisis" to describe the present situation.7
Dr Kunas, Head of the German Customs service emphasised in evidence that only about 3,000 operations out of a total of 10 million were "possible cases of fraud", representing 0.01% of operations.8While acknowledging that this relatively small number of cases presented a large potential fiscal loss (1 billion DM - 516 million ECU), he refused to acknowledge that it represented a crisis, particularly as the German exchequer would recover the money through the principal's guarantee.
Mr Reiner Ehmcke of the German Federal Ministry of Finance, speaking at a workshop on "crime in transport" in Paris9, explicitly rejected the notion of a crisis in the transit system, contending that in the Community Transit System at least, the risk of collapse was to be discounted because the guarantee system was decentralised.
Needless to say, the trade representatives at the same meeting - in effect the "decentralised" guarantors - were not as sanguine.
The amounts involved
If statistics are a problem in the case of transit volumes, when it comes to transit fraud they are a nightmare.
From day one of its inquiry, the Committee has attempted to establish some sort of reasonable benchmark figure for the amount of loss attributable to transit fraud and irregularity.
Any estimate of fraud relies on a degree of guesswork, as fraudsters do not generally submit data to their national statistics offices.
However, it might have been reasonable to expect that Member States would know how much outstanding customs debt is in their books relating to incomplete transit transactions, or how much they have recovered under the principal's guarantee.
In practice, even these figures are extremely unclear and often fail to match with the figures provided by the debtors themselves.
Furthermore, nobody has any clear idea of how much fraud goes undetected.
On occasion the Committee has been given estimates of the percentages of transit operations potentially or actually fraudulent without any clear indication of the amounts involved.
In these circumstances, it is not unfair to provide a cross-section of the estimates it has received to the question "how much fraud is there in the transit system".
The following list is broadly chronological: this helps illustrate the confusion surrounding this subject.
