Outstanding claims stand at about Ecu 930m in EU as a whole for Community Transit plus Ecu 718m for TIR system.26
The above estimates serve as an illustration of how difficult it is to obtain any reliable figures in this field.
It will not for example pass unobserved that some of these figures are mutually contradictory.
Moreover, many estimates are made on different criteria.
Outstanding customs debt relates to claims against transit principals, which are not necessarily fraud cases.
On the other hand, they do not include amounts repaid by freight forwarders.
Estimates of the percentage of cases involved do not give a reliable guide to value.
Witnesses have not generally attempted to estimate undetected fraud.
Moreover, very few are able to range beyond their national perspective.
On the basis of this information, the Committee of Inquiry cannot arrive at a single clear conclusion.
It does however seem that the absolute minimum figure for the accumulated loss due to fraud in the transit system must be at least Ecu 1 billion since 1990, on the basis of an update of the Commission's early estimate.
Indeed, one almost arrives at this figure simply by adding the current outstanding customs debt in Germany and the Netherlands.
However, this figure does not even attempt to take account of undetected fraud.
Given the complexities and inefficiencies of the transit system it seems probable that detection rates for transit fraud are relatively low.
It is therefore likely that the total amount defrauded from the Community Transit system runs into several billion Ecus, to which must be added the fraud perpetrated against the TIR system.
Another general impression can be drawn from the various estimates, namely that transit fraud (as opposed to irregularities and administrative errors) affects a tiny proportion of transit operations: probably in the region of 1% or less.
However, the value of this fraud is disproportionate as it is focused on those products where the profits are the greatest - high-excise goods (cigarettes, alcohol, etc.), generally referred to as "sensitive goods".
The vagueness of the figures for the scale of the crisis in the transit system contrasts sharply with the information available from the IRU concerning developments in the TIR system.
As the body responsible for managing the whole TIR system, the IRU has been able to provide a very precise view of claims relating to the TIR guarantee.
The graph below indicates the dramatic rise in claims over the 1990-1996 period, the same period as that concentrated on by the Committee in connection with the transit system.
The graph shows the cumulative total of what the IRU calls "litigious files" by year.
Who pays?
Public Revenue
Fraud against the transit system essentially involves placing goods in free circulation without payment of taxes and duty on them.
There is therefore a loss of revenue for the public authorities.
This revenue can be broken down into three components: VAT, customs duties and excise duties.
Of these, customs duties are entirely payable to the European Community budget in the form of traditional own resources (less 10% "collection fee" for Member States' customs services).
Excise duties are payable entirely to the Member State in which the goods are ultimately placed in free circulation.
VAT is primarily Member State income, but of course a small proportion is payable to the European Community.
Both the Member States individually and the European Union therefore have a direct financial interest in preventing transit fraud.
As far as the European Community budget is concerned, the amounts at stake can be identified by considering the proportion of VAT and customs duties within the overall budget.
In 1996, for example, out of total budget revenue of Ecu 81.3 billion, 12.9 billion (15.9%) was made up of customs duties and 39.8 billion (48.9%) of VAT.
In the system of Community finance, any shortfall in such revenue (along with agricultural levies) is in practice compensated by higher payments from the Member States under the so-called "fourth resource", calculated on the basis of national GNP.
The cost of transit fraud to the Community is therefore ultimately borne by the Member States and thus by all European taxpayers.
This occurs above all in cases where agricultural goods covered by the Common Agricultural Policy are involved and the transit system abused as a means fraudulently to obtain export refunds. (For an example of such fraud, see paragraph 5.3.4.1.)
On the overall hypothesis that it is high excise goods which are generally targeted by fraudsters, the direct losses are probably not shared equally by the Union and the Member States.
In its initial document on transit fraud, the Commission divided its estimate of Ecu 750 million into Ecu 320 million own resources and Ecu 430 million VAT/excise27.
However, Mr Eland, the witness from UK Customs, estimated that of the annual £100 million loss due to transit fraud, about 80% was Member State rather than EC revenue.
This, he said, was because the bulk of the fraud concerned cigarettes, which are subject to high excise duties, a national tax28.
The taxpayer does not necessarily suffer a direct loss of revenue as a result of transit fraud.
In all cases where the transaction is not cleared, the customs service can turn to the principal's guarantee to recover the lost revenue.
The guarantee will not always cover the full amount outstanding, especially if the comprehensive guarantee is used.
In some cases, the recovery of lost revenue via the guarantee may have sufficiently serious consequences for the guarantor to cause the customs service to hesitate before enforcing it.
Nonetheless, in the case of sensitive products, the guarantee given now has to cover the entire tax value in transit, thus securing public revenue more effectively.
Cases outstanding from the period prior to 1996, however, do not generally benefit from this 100% guarantee.
If, for example, goods are illicitly placed on the black market in France, it is the French state which loses revenue.
Thus the Danes collect revenue (at Danish rates) which they would not normally have received and the French continue to take the loss.
To this extent, transit fraud is arguably beneficial to the public purse in countries where operations begin.
The Trade
The greater part of the adverse financial consequences are therefore suffered by the principal of the transit operation.
In practice, this figure is almost always a freight forwarding and/or transport company, though some major vertically integrated importers/exporters act as their own principals.
The principal is liable for the total amount of taxes, duties and excise unpaid owing to an incomplete transit operation, regardless of the amount of the guarantee provided.
The provision of the guarantee is required in order to provide assurance of payment to the customs service.
It is therefore generally provided by the principal's insurer or banker.
In case of claim from customs, therefore, the first consequence is that the principal is approached by customs for reimbursement of outstanding taxes, duties and excise.
If he is unable to pay, customs turn to the financial institution providing the guarantee.
The principal of course remains liable to the bank or insurer for the full amount.
Cases of fraud can produce a major financial impact on freight forwarding companies which are not always able easily to absorb it.
However, the impact is not limited to the single payment.
Bank charges and insurance premiums will reflect not only past claims on the principal's guarantee but also the perceived level of risk in providing insurance or bank guarantee cover to a freight forwarder.
These charges are becoming intolerable, especially following the introduction of legislation in October of 1996 increasing the comprehensive guarantee to 100% of weekly turnover and providing for specific guarantees on operations involving sensitive goods.
One witness, the Rothmans cigarette company, illustrated this latter point well in its contribution to the Committee, stating that "the cost implications of operating Single Guarantee facilities can be measured in terms of being 30 times more than the cost of operating the Comprehensive Guarantee".
The extra cost in this company's case can be estimated at Ecu 1.16 million.29
Moreover, even without any claims being made, the potential liability which has to be accounted for in the financial statements of freight forwarders now constitutes a substantial financial burden, insofar as creditors and potential creditors will treat the company with greater caution.
Hence the reluctance of freight forwarders to name the companies with high outstanding guarantee debts.
The freight forwarding industry argues that this financial burden, whether actual or contingent, is unfair.
Except in those cases where a freight forwarder is involved in a fraud, the guarantee mechanism has the effect that an innocent party can be called upon to foot the bill for the crimes of another.
It has been argued, on the other hand, that it is part of a freight forwarder's business to provide a transit guarantee, that either profit or business advantage derive from the operation and that a forwarder always has the option of refusing to take on business.
To an extent, this is of course true.
The transit regulations do not specify who the principal should be, as long as they can provide a sound guarantee, and freight forwarders have made it their business to offer this service as part of a forwarding package.
To this extent, they cannot complain if the guarantee is called upon now and then.
However, the system, which is designed and operated by public authorities, has a "duty of care" to its users.
If the system becomes so flawed that the provision of a guarantee constitutes an unreasonable risk, the principal is justified in complaining at being alone in bearing the financial consequences.
This point has been reached in the Community Transit system.
The question is not only one of the fairness of the system.
It is clear from evidence given to the Committee that claims under the principal's guarantee are hanging like a sword of Damocles over the freight forwarding industry.
Many companies, especially the smaller ones, are already technically insolvent as a result of their liabilities.
FreightForward Europe's contention that the enforcement of the debt would lead to widespread company failures has already been quoted30; the political consequences of this give a new twist to the issue :
"Governments do not collect claims because they know that if they collect, it will lead to bankruptcies. ... in the current circumstances of unemployment etc. we think that politically it is very difficult to let companies go bankrupt."31
Public authorities are understandably reluctant to admit this practice on the public record, though there is much anecdotal evidence to suggest that claims are often not pressed as hard as they might.
In evidence, the Germans expressed a compromise position between legal severity and practical leniency:
"We are not allowed to lift the debt for reasons of equity; even the threat of bankruptcy is no reason for Community law to renounce collection, but nationally we may use the gentler method of delaying payment."33
If it is true that customs services make allowances of this sort, the effect is to move the loss back onto the taxpayer, without removing the liability from the operator.
While this is a political choice which this report does not intend to contest, this practice is in contravention
of Community regulations, could create competitive disparities and, in any case, does nothing to address the underlying problem.
The Economy
In assessing the harm done by transit fraud, the effects on the general economy should not be ignored.
The effects of transit fraud (and related forms of fraud) are both direct and indirect and can be out of proportion even to the substantial financial sums involved.
In the first place, there are the direct effects for a specific industrial sector - the transport/freight forwarding sector.
The threat to the livelihood of these companies, and in any case the extra costs imposed upon them put jobs at risk and potentially have knock-on effects for related areas of the economy.
More insidious is the malign influence of the "grey" economy which is fed by transit fraud and customs fraud in general.
This parallel economy thrives at the expense of the legitimate economy, again leading to unemployment and slower growth.
It is not only legitimate trade within the EU that suffers, but also its competitive position vis-à-vis the outside world.
This effect derives from the extra costs imposed on EC business both by transit fraud and by the countermeasures (100% guarantee, etc.) supposed to combat it.
The risk is that external trade otherwise passing through EU ports and businesses will be discouraged from doing so by higher costs.
The visit of the Committee on Budgetary Control to Rotterdam found some evidence that this was occurring in the field of high tax goods which were apparently being diverted into nonEU ports.35This point was confirmed in the written evidence of Eurocommerce to the Committee:
It became evident that strict controls at the points of entry in the Union will create a diversion of goods and ships to other European points of entry with less vigorous control; this does not only apply for transit and the rules of origin but also for the controls on ships' safety, drugs, environmental and health standards and copy cat labels.
Those who apply the rules are punished for their efforts.
Stiff competition, especially between harbours, might therefore increase fraud in an indirect way.
Customs fraud, smuggling, etc. (at least that involving legal goods) are often viewed as "victimless" crimes, and thus treated with some tolerance. Certainly, they are not widely
considered serious crimes.
The point is worth making that transit fraud is not as "innocent" or "victimless" as it might on occasion be thought.
In the first place, goods entering the black market are not subject to the statutory checks for health and safety which would normally be applied.
This point is particularly relevant for the transit system, as many sensitive products are agricultural products covered by the common agricultural policy.
Several cases have been reported to the Committee involving the clandestine trade in meat.
Since the essence of the fraud is to bring the product into free circulation, the result is that dubious meat of uncertain origin reaches EU consumers without any health checks at all.
In the present climate of anxiety about meat, this should be a matter of some concern.
Also in the field of health, government policies aimed at discouraging the over-consumption of hazardous products through taxation are undermined by the availability of such products at lower prices on the black market.
A second area where adverse social consequences potentially arise is in connection with the economic effects of the parallel economy, which have been said to lead to the dependency of entire communities upon illicit activities and to exclude them from normal jobs and thus regular social and employment provision.
The illegal economy poses also a threat to the revenues of the social security and health funds.
This question falls outside the Committee's terms of reference and is not therefore covered in this report.
It may however deserve closer attention elsewhere.
However the most obvious and convincing reason to disabuse the public of the notion that transit fraud is a "victimless" crime is its link with other forms of criminality.
One of the few non-controversial points to emerge from the replies to the Committee's questionnaire and in evidence is that most transit fraud is considered to be carried out by large-scale international organised crime.
Confidential meetings of the Committee showed that this is no vague impression, but one based on firm intelligence obtained by public authorities concerning the persons involved.
The connection was explicitly made between supposedly "harmless" cigarette and alcohol smuggling and traffic in arms, drugs and, recently, the introduction and exploitation of illegal immigrants in the EU.
The same persons, the same routes and often the same methods are used.
If there is any doubt as to the ferocity of the criminality associated with much transit fraud, it is sufficient to consider the widespread gang-warfare and high death toll associated with cigarette smuggling in Germany reported by the German press especially in early 1996.
Lowrisk profits from cigarette smuggling are channelled into all manner of criminal activity, up to and including terrorism.
(The next two sections will examine this subject further.)
Considering that transit fraud is an integral part of the activities of organized crime within and outside the Community, it is surprising how little is known on a European level about it. Member States are reluctant either to investigate the extent of organized crime in their territory or to make results of investigations public.
It should also be noted that transit fraud, like other crimes, generates large sums of black money which are laundered in various ways and channelled back into the official economy, enabling criminals or criminal organizations to infiltrate, in a very competitive way, into the established business world.
Key Points
The lack offoresight by the Commission in the introduction of the single market, which has been guided by the principles of market liberaluation and the abolition of controls rather than those of safeguarding revenue and maintaining the Community's own resources, has led to a crisis within the transit procedure.
This crisis involves the loss of several billion Ecu in uncollected VAT, customs duty and excise revenue to the European Community budget and to national exchequers and, on occasion, the loss of fraudulently obtained export refunds on agricultural products.
Though impossible to quantify, because of the lamentable absence of reliable statistics in this field, the amount in question certainly runs into several billion ecu over the last five years. While the number of cases may be small in percentage terms, the cumulative losses can be huge.
In addition, goods transit fraud results in unfair competition which significantly reduces the volume of business transacted by companies competing on the market, with all that that implies.
Mr Vandenberghe, evidence of FreightForward Europe : 25.4.96.
An identical point was made by FENEX in Contribution no. 13 - PE 216.395
The General Director of the BDF, Mr Karlheinz Schmidt, appeared before the Committee on 4 November 1996
Workshop organised at OECD Headquarters in Paris on 4 December 1996 by the European Conference of Ministers of Transport (ECMT) and attended by the Committee of Inquiry's rapporteur.
(See report of meeting in Notice to Members no. 64 - PE 220.197)
Communication on Fraud in the Transit Procedure... (COM(95)108, para.l.) and many subsequent occasions.
