Introduction
Confidentiality of sources
This and the next section of the report, more than any others, are based on information received by the Committee and its members in closed session, in documents given to the Committee in confidence and on private contacts outside the context of the full Committee.
It will not be possible therefore to cross-refer the assertions made in these sections to the published evidence as fully as in most sections, nor even to state explicitly the sources of the information on which they are based.
Moreover, in accordance with the terms of the Interinstitutional Agreement1, it will not be possible to cite the names of individuals or organisations the identities of which have been communicated to the Committee in closed session as being implicated in transit fraud and related crimes.
Subject matter
The transit system is part of a much wider trading and customs system.
The Committee's inquiries have indicated that the provisions covering Community Transit and the TIR system have an effect not only on fraud against those specific regimes, but are intimately and immediately related to other forms of criminal activity in the customs field.
The nature of this link is explained below.
It is therefore impossible, and would be irresponsible, artificially to divorce fraud specific to the transit system from related criminal activities.
In short, transit fraud cannot be seen in isolation, and this (and the next) section will look at the wider subject of which it forms an integral part.
What is transit fraud?
The common thread connecting transit fraud with related crimes is the basic concept that goods on which customs levies, VAT and excise duties have not been paid are placed on the internal market.
The fraudster's profit lies in the difference between the duty-free price at which the goods were obtained (unless they were straightforwardly stolen) and the price obtained for them on the black market.
This price will inevitably be lower than the tax-paid price, in order to create the incentive to buy, but can still be considerably higher than the duty-free price.
The loss to the public and/or the principal will however correspond to the entire amount.
The greater the difference between the duty-free and the duty-paid price the greater the margin and the greater the profit to the fraudster. Hence the attractiveness of high-tax
"sensitive" products.
The duty-paid price of the most commonly involved product, cigarettes, is composed of at least 69% tax in the countries of the EU, in some Member States more (see next section2).
A single standard lorry-load of cigarettes has a tax/duty value of about Ecu 1 million.
The incentive is clear.
If, as in the case presented to the Committee as evidence and described in the box below3, some 70 lorries are involved, the incentive is even clearer.
To make the cost-benefit analysis even better, the product smuggled is in itself not an illegal one and therefore typically attracts relatively lenient treatment by the authorities if things go wrong -certainly in comparison to drugs.
A case of transit fraud
In 1993 a small freight forwarding business in Portugal, established by a major shipping agency to handle a contract for a specific client, lost the contract and was then offered business involving transport, initially of alcohol, from the USA to the Czech Republic. The customer was apparently in the USA, but contact was by fax and telephone only.
The alcohol shipments came to a bonded warehouse in Lisbon by container and were then unloaded, stored and reloaded onto lorries provided by the customer.
The freight forwarding agency was responsible for these services and for the paperwork, including acting as principal, but had no control over the onward transport.
The guarantee was much lower than the tax and excise applicable to the consignments.
The seventh and subsequent shipments were for cigarettes, but the office of destination remained the same - on the Eastern border of Germany.
The first tobacco shipment originated in Hungary; it was repacked in the Lisbon warehouse and then reloaded for transit to the Czech Republic.
Apart from three TIR consignments coming from Hungary, Austria and Switzerland, all other operations involved either goods arriving by sea from the USA or, more frequently, lorries coming from the Netherlands with consignments intended for destinations in the Ukraine, Lithuania and Belarus.
After nine or ten shipments the Portuguese Customs insisted that the business be put on a proper basis with the correct forms and the freight forwarder negotiated an arrangement with a larger forwarding agency which henceforth acted formally as the signatory of the documents (and was therefore the principal) in exchange for a fee, although the work continued to be carried out in practice by the first forwarding agency.
No objections were raised by Customs to the transit procedures involved which were assumed by both the agency and the customs to be regular operations despite the strange itineraries of the goods.
A total of 76 operations were carried out before the shipping agency, owners of the small freight forwarding business, became suspicious and put a hah to the consignments.
However, when the procedures were not properly concluded with the customs stamp on the fifth copy from the office of destination, the forwarders were eventually asked by Customs in 1994 - one year after the first consignment - to provide evidence of their arrival at the German border.
Faxed copies of the TI certificate were obtained by the firm, but these turned out to be stamped with forged stamps.
One such certificate received by the first agency turned out to have been stamped with the same false stamp as the certificates for 24 other operations carried out in the name of the second firm.
No other evidence was obtainable of the proper completion of the procedures for any of the consignments, whether of alcohol or cigarettes.
Both customer and transport firm disappeared, although a German company with an address in Hamburg, which was involved in 42 of the consignments, was contacted. A bill for ECU 2.3 million was then presented by Customs to the first forwarding agency for the tax and excise which should have been paid on the first ten consignments.
A second bill for at least 60 consignments was presented to the other agency, amounting to over ECU 35 million.
Both companies would be bankrupted if these debts were enforced.
The simplest method of committing a transit fraud is simply to ensure the goods "disappear" somewhere between the office of departure and the office of destination. In practice, this need not in reality be en route between the two offices.
A vehicle can cross any internal EU frontier without checks normally taking place and, in the absence of any prescribed itineraries, even if a vehicle were to be checked and were not on any reasonable route between its point of departure and alleged point of destination, it would still not be committing any infraction if the 8-day deadline had not expired.
Technically therefore, a lorry bound for Spain from Le Havre could be stopped in Sweden two days later without any irregularity having taken place.
Given the openness of EU borders, this kind of fraud is extremely easy to commit and will not usually be detected for a considerable length of time.
The paper-based procedures of the Community transit system described in Section 3 mean that this period can extend for several months - ample time to carry out repeat frauds and to make good an escape.
The drawback for the fraudster of this system is that the fraud will at some point be discovered, and an investigation, at least formally, initiated.
Moreover, the freight forwarders' guarantee will be activated, thus either (i) alerting the freight forwarder to the problem and scaring him off further operations, or (ii) giving rise to a cost if the freight forwarder is involved in the fraud.
Fraudulent completion of transit procedures
To avoid such difficulties, a fraudster may choose to create the impression that the transit operation has been discharged.
This involves either a forged stamp on the return copy 5 of the Tl document, or the use of a stolen stamp.
According to the Committee's information, both forged and stolen stamps are easily obtained in criminal circles.4
To avoid the minimal risk of such a forgery being spotted, a fraudster may wish to obtain a genuine customs stamp on the copy 5.
This can be obtained either by bribing or threatening customs officials.
There is evidence, in cases of which the Committee has been informed, that customs officials in some Member States are sometimes corruptible.
As an alternative, bogus customs officials have also been employed.
Cases of forged stamps and misused stamps have been detected on numerous occasions by the customs service, but generally where a suspicion already existed that a fraud was taking place.
Otherwise it is difficult to see how, in the mass of paper currently flowing through this transit system, such forgeries can be detected, notwithstanding the investment by some customs services in sophisticated equipment for doing so.
Fraud involving the guarantee
A third category of fraud involves the fraudulent use of the guarantee.
This may take the form of a forged guarantee certificate which transpires to be valueless, or it may involve the illicit use of a genuine guarantee.
Some Member States take the view that a principal knowingly assuming total potential tax liabilities in excess of the amount of that principal's comprehensive guarantee commits a fraud.5They would therefore wish to see a system whereby the liability on each operation is deducted from the total comprehensive guarantee available to a freight forwarder to cover further operations.
Upon exhaustion of the guarantee amount, the principal would have to cease operations until outstanding liabilities were cleared, thus allowing new operations to take place.
Given that no such system exists, this kind of "fraud" is extremely difficult to control.
However, other Member States do not treat the comprehensive guarantee in this way.
They see it as an "actuarial" assessment of risk and operate on the basis that the liability on each single operation must be covered by the guarantee.
They do not therefore consider liabilities exceeding guarantee cover as a fraud or even as an irregularity.
A variation of the guarantee fraud theme occurs within the TIR system, where the TIR carnet itself constitutes a guarantee.
The result is that a flourishing secondary market in forged TIR carnets has been created.
Research suggests that such carnets are freely available in several EU, Visegrad and other countries and goes hand-in-hand with corruption of customs officials.6
The actors in transit fraud
Active participants
Depending on the precise fraud mechanism employed, it is clear that a successful fraud depends on the participation on the ground of one or more of the following: drivers, transport companies, freight forwarders or customs officials.
In its questionnaire to Member States, the Committee attempted to obtain estimates as to the likely involvement of these categories based on the experience of the customs services.7
Of the 16 respondents, 7 did not reply to this question, several stating that the information was not available to them.
(If true, it should be said that a lack of such basic intelligence on the part of those customs services is worrying.)
Among the others, there was a broad consensus that drivers and transport companies were the most implicated, with forwarders occupying third place.
Understandably, but rather implausibly, only one customs service, France, even admitted the possibility that customs officers might ever be involved.
Indeed, in oral evidence to the Committee, it went further, describing a case of transit fraud in which a customs officer was involved and informing the Committee that "every year, ten French customs officials appear before the courts charged with corruption".8
Non-active participants
The beneficiaries of the fraud, generally associated with organised crime, are of course the ultimate consignees of the goods.
Consignees, presumably the owners/importers, are often shadowy figures in the transit system, even from the point of view of the freight forwarder working on their behalf.
(The Portuguese case described above illustrates this fact clearly.)
Certainly under current arrangements they bear no financial responsibility under the guarantee for the outcome of a transit operation.
Often a number of regular minor orders are placed with freight forwarders in order to build up confidence before the fraud starts in earnest; on other occasions even this is not necessary as freight forwarders have been known to accept business on the basis of a telephone call and a fax in exchange for good returns.
It is in the nature of these companies that they are created and disappear with startling rapidity, even if the individuals involved regularly transpire to be the same.
Such are the profits potentially involved in transit fraud that it is worth establishing a company in order to carry out a single transit fraud.
By and large, the organisations behind these companies are based in nonEU countries offering favourable tax regimes and secretive banking laws.
Payment for goods frequently arrives therefore from locations as dispersed as Switzerland, Cyprus, Lebanon, the British Virgin Islands, Cayman Islands or the Isle of Man.
It is therefore very difficult for law enforcement agencies within the EU to trace and investigate the organisers of large scale fraud.
The question of the role of nonEU countries will be examined in greater depth in the next section.
Transit Fraud and Smuggling - The "Displacement Effect"
The introduction of the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods early in 1996 has been followed by a marked decline in the amount of the kinds of transit fraud so far described in this report.
Law enforcement agencies which have spoken to the Committee emphasise a direct correlation between the level of the transit guarantee and the amount of fraud in the system.
The Commission itself has indicated for example a stated 95% drop in transit fraud involving cigarettes in Spain.
Since the introduction of the 100% guarantee, freight forwarders have understandably grown more cautious about who they do business with.
Most serious operators in the business now take care thoroughly to check the credentials of those on whose account they will provide a transit guarantee.
Consequently, the relationship between forwarders, transporters and consignees/importers have become very important.
Whereas it was possible in recent years to criticise freight forwarders for accepting business without due care as to the identity and purpose of the client (often because the returns for dubious business are excellent), the potential risk involved in such business has obliged legitimate but careless freight forwarders to mend their ways.
It should be pointed out, however, that the 100% guarantee for transit operations should not be seen as an antifraud measure, but purely and simply as a measure to protect tax revenue.
Following the entry into force of this provision, there have been clear signs of a shift away from transit fraud towards traditional smuggling and the false declaration of goods.
Because of the additional difficulties they face in abusing a legitimate transit procedure, criminals are now resorting to smuggling goods into the Community's customs territory.
Nor is the problem only one of displacement into other forms of criminality, it also concerns other forms of transport within the transit system where the principal's guarantee does not exist, such as rail and sea.
Relatively tittle is known about the amount of fraud present in
At the moment there is some degree of switching to maritime transport.
It is clear that something is going on with cigarettes".9
This is not to say that the transit system is suddenly "clean".
Apart from the problem of misdeclaration, which permits goods to use the system without the required guarantee cover, even completely legal transit operations regularly form part of the routes used by smuggled goods destined for Europe's black markets.
Nonetheless, the displacement phenomenon could be considered to obviate the principal rationale for the Committee of Inquiry.
In fact, it illustrates the necessity of considering the transit system within a wider context.
It is futile to attack the problems of the transit system in isolation, as the solutions proposed may simply create new problems.
The Committee has therefore taken the view that it must examine the wider picture of smuggling and contraband in order to reach meaningful conclusions about the role of the transit system within customs activity in general. One sector played a central role in this examination.
Agricultural Goods and Cigarettes
Transit fraud and related criminal activities cover a range of products but tend to be focused on so-called sensitive goods.
For example, the Committee has in its possession a statement by the President of the Court of Auditors concerning the shipment of meat from Poland to North Africa in the period from 1988 to 1994.
The initially inexplicable fall in meat prices in the period 1989-1990 in some Member States occasioned an inquiry.
It was established that, during this period, organized criminal associations had falsified the papers of approximately 1,000 lorry loads of live animals and a further 300 lorry loads of meat.
Altogether, the operation involved 150,000 animals (mainly cows, bulls and sheep) and 6,000 tons of meat.
According to the transit documents, the goods were due to transit through the territory of the Community and leave it from a number of Spanish customs offices (Algeciras, Malaga, Cadiz and Barcelona), with Morocco and Tunisia as their final destination.
The customs office at Frankfurt/Oder received copy 5s of the Tl documents which were later found to be fraudulent.
Some copies were stamped by Spanish customs offices which had once existed but had since been closed down.
In some cases use was also made of transit stamps which were no longer valid.
Other stamps looked too beautiful and had been placed on the documents with extraordinary care, something which rarely occurs in practice.
Thus, whereas the animals and meat were officially 'in transit' to North Africa, in reality they never left the Community. Instead, they were dumped on the black market.
Some of the consignments were probably exported from the Community to third countries. Export refunds were paid in respect of these exports!
This sum does not include non-collected VAT revenue in the countries where the animals and meat were dumped on the black market. Nor does it include the wrongly paid export refunds.
Of the total of Ecu 50 million, it has so far proved possible to recover only about 1 million from the forwarding
However, the largest fraud cases brought to the attention of the Committee of Inquiry involved chicanery with cigarettes.
There is little doubt that the bulk of transit fraud committed in recent years (certainly in terms of lost revenue) involves cigarettes.
UCLAF suggested that known revenue losses due to cigarette fraud amounted to Ecu 759m out of Ecu 976m (78%) over the period 1990-1995.
Until very recently, this fraud took the form of a direct abuse of the transit system in the manner outlined at the beginning of this section.
Since the introduction of the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods, the displacement phenomenon has resulted in a return to traditional smuggling.
Nonetheless, the currently outstanding liabilities of freight forwarders mainly concern cigarette fraud in the 1990-1995 period.
Even in terms of the EC budget, which does not include excise duties, cigarette fraud accounts for the largest proportion of losses incurred.
The following table shows the breakdown of traditional own resources lost to the EC budget through reported transit fraud by product over the 1990-1995 period:11
PRODUCT
The Committee therefore made a special study of cigarette-related crime and this forms the subject of the following section.
Key Points
Fraud specific to the transit procedure depends on two main mechanisms, the non-completion of transit operations and the fraudulent completion of operations.
Both are common and both rely on the characteristic inefficiency of the system.
In addition, the guarantee system provides openings for fraudsters, either through forged guarantee documentation, or, in the TIR system, forged ΤΠΖ carnets.
The organisations concerned have their origins in the European Union and increasingly in the newly open countries of Central and Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union and frequently cooperate on an opportunistic basis, often using Switzerland as a base.
The international nature of the organisations in question is underlined by their ready exploitation of tax havens and countries whose legislation and administrative practices prevent easy investigation and prosecution.
The Committee has also established that transit fraud in its specific sense cannot be divorced from the wider context of customs fraud and organized crime in general.
Indeed, countermeasures taken in the transit procedure, such as the introduction of a 100% guarantee for sensitive goods, rather than preventing crime, have demonstrably "displaced" criminal activity into more traditional forms of customs crime (including within the transit system itself) such as misdeclaration of goods or old-fashioned smuggling.
A switch to transit underforms of transport where guarantees are not required - air, sea, rail - is ateo observable.
It is important therefore not to imagine that the problems of transit can be solved in isolation.
In this connection, it should be noted that no authoritative list of either authentic customs stamps or known false stamps exists.
See Notice to Members no.
3 (PE 216.332) published in Volume IV.
ANALYSIS (IV) - THE ILLICIT TRADE IN CIGARETTES
Introduction: Why Cigarettes?
Large profits
The attractiveness of cigarettes to the fraudster has already been mentioned - it lies in the size of the difference between the duty-free and the duty paid price of a cigarette, giving scope for substantial profits even at the relatively low street prices needed to attract the consumer.
The following table illustrates this point, showing the total tax incidence on the purchase price on a packet of cigarettes in EU Member States1:
The attractiveness of cigarettes to the fraudster is further enhanced by the relative ease with which they can be handled.
Other high-tax products, such as petroleum products, or even alcohol, although frequently the subject of fraud, cannot compete on a tax-value per weight basis, nor in terms of the conditions required for their transport.
One so-called "masterpack" of 10,000 cigarettes is the size of the sort of cardboard box common in supermarkets.
A container load of cigarettes carries a potential fiscal value of about Ecu 1 million.
Almost all of this is potential profit for the smuggler.
It should be stressed that the interest for the smuggler lies in obtaining cigarettes at duty-free prices.
Even though there is a potential price difference of up to 240% between the cheapest cigarettes in Europe (Portugal: Ecu 1.53 per packet) and the most expensive (Denmark: Ecu 3.67 per packet), the duty-free dockside price for American cigarettes is vastly more attractive at Ecu 0.302.
Studies have shown that cross-border smuggling of duty-paid cigarettes is relatively unimportant3within the EU, though the same may not apply to the smuggling of tax-paid cigarettes from Eastern Europe.
Ready markets
Large-scale smuggling also requires a willing market and a good local distribution network to supply it.
Such markets and networks have existed for many years, especially in Italy and Spain where tobacco smuggling is a long-standing problem.
The recent expansion in cigarette smuggling has therefore to some degree exploited these countries as a base for its infiltration of markets in the rest of the EU, this being most spectacularly the case in Germany, where the activities of Vietnamese gangs have made headlines in the spring of 1996.4
This accounts for the fact that, while the tax "incentives" given in the table above would tend to indicate that tobacco smuggling should be a South-North trade, it is in fact North-South.5In the absence of controlled internal borders within the EU however, it would be extremely complacent of the authorities in Northern Europe to view cigarette fraud as a Southern problem.
Indeed, cigarette smuggling is now a genuinely pan-European problem, as the crime begins to match the opportunity and the corresponding criminal organisations spring up.
Legitimate sales and revenue volumes
The EU's estimated 96.7 million smokers annually consume in excess of 611 billion cigarettes per year.
The total value of tobacco products consumed adds up to about Ecu 69 billion.
The total tax revenue accruing to the Member States reaches Ecu 50 billion.
The following table breaks these amounts down by Member State:6
Value of domestic sales
Total receipts from excise and VAT on tobacco
% of total government revenue
Austria
Finland
Sweden
Most EU cigarette consumption is covered by production within the EU (using imported tobacco).
Nevertheless, some 80 billion third country cigarettes are imported into the EU each year, of which 90%+ pass through the port of Antwerp.
These are intended for re-export to third countries and, in small part, for duty-free sales in ports, airports, etc.
Illicit sales and revenue volumes
It is naturally far more difficult to estimate the size and value of the illegal market.
Nevertheless, attempts have been made.
The number of cigarettes smuggled in the world annually has been estimated at about 280 billion per annum, revenue loss to governments at over $16 billion.7
In a cross section of 8 EU countries, the market share of contraband is estimated by the European Confederation of Cigarette Retailers (CEDT) as follows:8
Conirabarcd "Market Share*
Proportion of "blond" (i.e. Virginia) cigarettes
The CEDT also puts lost fiscal revenues at about Ecu 5 billion per year (10% of total tobacco tax revenues) and believes the lost profits of legitimate cigarette retailers to amount to Ecu 900 million.
On this basis, the market for contraband cigarettes would appear to be in the region of 60 billion cigarettes per year.
Furthermore, the situation is said to be worsening:
"My former capacity in British Customs was to be in charge of criminal investigations and I know that we had cases where containers of duty-free cigarettes were going from bonded warehouses within the European Union ostensibly for export outside the European Union but were not getting there and those cigarettes were being disposed of within the European Union itself.
In addition, through anecdotal discussions with customs officials I hear that, within the European Union, there is a higher proportion of duty-free cigarettes being sold within its territory.
Taking these two together, I think the observation I made that the problem is getting a great deal worse is an accurate one.»9 »9
The statement by the CEDT regarding the estimate that Ecu 5 billion per annum is being lost in tax revenue seems accurate, particularly when compared with the statement made by Germany's Finance Minister Theo Waigel in August 1996, on the occasion of the presentation of the annual customs report for 1995, that the German tax authorities had lost some DM 1 billion (Ecu 500 million) in revenue in 1995 alone because of the activities of cigarette smugglers.
According to customs services and other law-enforcement agencies, intelligence information and actual cigarette seizures indicate that by far the most popular contraband brands are American.
Seizures indicate that the cigarettes concerned were not normally originally packaged for the domestic EU market.
For example health warnings are either absent or in inappropriate languages, production codes show that they were produced in factories not normally supplying the EU market, etc.
The question therefore arises, how do these cigarettes arrive at their point of illicit sale?
In the 1990-1995 period, contraband cigarettes arrived by road using the transit system in the way described in earlier sections of this report.
They were transported under initially quite legitimate transit procedures which were subsequently not completed or fraudulently completed.
Most of the large outstanding freight forwarders' debts reflect this boom in the abuse of the transit system.
Thanks to the defects in the transit procedure, this kind of fraud was very easy.
The introduction of the 100% guarantee, plus a growing consciousness of the problem among the legitimate business community, seems to have reduced this straightforward abuse of the transit system.
But, to quote Mr Weatherburn of Rothmans:
"It [the 100% guarantee] has not eliminated fraud, it has simply created a monster called smuggling."10
Not everyone is of this opinion.
In correspondence with the Committee, the cigarette producer Philip Morris, contested the assertion that there exists a causal link between levels of transit fraud and contraband activity, describing the displacement theory as "a confusion between two unrelated issues".11
However this may be, three mechanisms have been cited to the Committee by which fraudsters overcome the difficulties presented by the 100% guarantee.
The first is to continue to use the transit system under the cover of falsely declared goods.
If innocuous goods are declared, the 100% guarantee is not applied and the goods do not normally attract special attention on the part of customs officials.
The beauty of this system from the point of view of the smuggler is that once past the port of entry the goods are on the open road in a borderless Europe and stand very little chance of being checked.
Furthermore, as will be demonstrated in the next section, the chance of a physical check at the external border is slim.
Even if the transit procedure is not completed, the guarantee to be paid will be far less than the real tax value of the goods and the customs authorities are unlikely in normal circumstances even to attempt to investigate the irregularity.
The third way is to resort to clandestine transport of the cigarettes - old-fashioned smuggling.
It should be noted that the transit system is still often used to get the cigarettes to a location from which they can easily be smuggled, but that it is in these cases completed in a completely regular fashion.
Lastly, mention should be made of the importance, at least for one EU Member State, of the direct smuggling of cigarettes from neighbouring territories which do not belong to the customs territory of the EU; this is the case with Gibraltar and Andorra in particular.
One of the more absorbing aspects of the Committee's work has been to learn of the complexity of the routes by which contraband cigarettes now enter free circulation in the Union.
Besides the continuing direct abuse of the transit system (via false declaration or of the kind suffered by Rothmans in the example given above), the routes taken by smuggled cigarettes are numerous.
Many routes exist - and single cases of several have been related to the Committee, but law enforcement agencies have however identified three principal flows.
All begin around the ports of northern Europe, particularly Antwerp, Ostend and Rotterdam.
The port of Antwerp provides cigarette warehousing facilities unparalleled anywhere in Europe.
At any one time, as the Committee itself observed14, some 5 billion cigarettes are stored in Antwerp in warehouses managed by two companies, each serving a major American cigarette manufacturer.
All in all, 70-80 billion cigarettes pass through the port each year.
Aside from the relatively small proportion intended for duty free sale, none of these cigarettes are destined for the EU market, but are meant for export to third countries.
At the time the Committee visited the warehouses, it was informed for example that "almost all" the cigarettes were to be on-sold to Russian clients.
Cigarettes in storage in Antwerp are therefore already packaged for their intended markets, with health warnings in appropriate languages.
As the large American producers supply their legitimate EU markets entirely out of EU production facilities, any person wishing to purchase American duty-free cigarettes for European black markets - including clandestine markets - is likely to purchase products warehoused in Antwerp.
This is simply because that is where the cigarettes are.
From Antwerp, the first two major trade routes involve the transport of cigarettes into Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union.
- a first transit operation is used to transfer cigarettes by road from the low countries to a free zone in Switzerland.
The operation is concluded quite regularly there.
Cigarettes then leave Switzerland under a new transit procedure for a destination in Central/Eastern Europe or one of the former Soviet Republics.
This operation too is regular.
- a transit operation is used to transfer cigarettes from port warehouses to regional airports in Belgium or the Netherlands.
Cargo aircraft fly the cigarettes (up to five container loads per aircraft) to destinations to the East.
Nothing illegal takes place while the cigarettes are under EU customs control.
There is no way of knowing therefore, except by prior intelligence, which trade is wholly legitimate and which forms part of a criminal plan.
This said, abnormal volumes of traffic destined for countries which cannot absorb such quantities of cigarettes could give grounds for some reasonable suspicion, though this is unlikely to provide grounds for customs action.
From their destinations in Eastern Europe, cigarettes return to the EU in a number of ways.
The two countries most affected are Germany and Italy.
In Germany, the problem has been described as "ant-smuggling" ("Ameisenschmuggel"): the clandestine transport of cigarettes across the eastern border in a vast number of small consignments, carried in private cars and small vans.
In the light of the huge number of border crossings made every day, this form of smuggling is practically impossible to prevent.
In Italy, the route is a slightly more conventional one.
Cigarettes are transported in vast quantities from the Republics of the former Yugoslavia and Albania13across the Adriatic in fast boats and landed along the long coastline.
From there, they supply not only the Italian market, but also other EU countries beyond.
The third route, to some extent replacing traditional routes through Gibraltar and Andorra, involves sea transport from Northern European ports.
Documents are prepared indicating the delivery of cigarettes to destinations in North Africa.
The route taken involves passing close to Spanish territorial waters, across which a short trip in a fast boat suffices to land the cigarettes on the Spanish coast.
Information from the Spanish authorities indicates that this route represents a return to traditional methods employed before the transit system offered an easier alternative:
"fraud relating to the transit of tobacco and other sensitive products has declined over the past year and there are signs of a return to traditional tobacco smuggling by sea".16
This route was illustrated by the interception on the high seas by Spanish customs in January 1997 of a Honduran-registered ship Wendy-I under operation "Columbus".
The vessel, which was carrying cigarettes from warehouses in the port of Antwerp but which had departed from Ostend, was boarded 100 miles of the Spanish coast.
It was estimated that this ship had been responsible for the illicit importation of 27 million cartons of cigarettes with a tax value of Ptas 7.5 billion (Ecu 44.8 million) in 1996.17
Importantly in connection with this mode of cigarette smuggling, Operation Colombus relied on the successful application of Article 23 of the 1958 Geneva Convention, which allows national authorities to waylay shipping on the high seas in suitably justified cases.
As this was the first time that this article had been used in Spain, the previous experience of the Italian Guardia di Finanza in applying the Convention proved invaluable in assisting the Spanish customs service to carry out an effective operation.
This case therefore also demonstrates the
Sophisticated organisation
The importance of well organised networks has already been mentioned.
The logistics of large-scale smuggling imply a relatively sophisticated distribution system even at ground level.
Local criminal organisations are able to provide this service in most countries, though the degree of organisation and the capacity for absorbtion obviously varies.
Numerous witnesses have pointed to a worrying rise in the phenomenon of organised crime in Eastern Europe.
In combination with their more established counterparts in the West, they have enthusiastically exploited the new opportunities offered by the opening of the iron curtain.
The players behind the scenes are also closely connected with organised crime, though there is some indication that the association is often a loose one between opportunistic "Mr Bigs", ad hoc international organisations and locally implanted criminal organisations.
The important point about the organisers, from the point of view of the Committee, is their location.
In cases unearthed by the authorities, one of the most interesting aspects of the frauds has been the financial arrangements.
The key point for a fraudster is not to be discovered.
One mechanism employed to render investigation as difficult as possible is to arrange for a consignment of cigarettes to pass through a bewildering range of owners in a short space of time.
The object is to make the final owner untraceable, and to make the links between successive owners as obscure as possible.
Transactions therefore typically involve rapid changes of ownership, usually between shell companies set up for the purpose and generally based in countries providing legislation inimical to later investigation.
Payment often also originates from such locations.
It is not unusual for payment for cigarettes to be made on cash terms (i.e. in immediately liquid instruments - not credit) and arrives via intermediaries18from well-known tax havens and countries favouring bank secrecy.
Even therefore if the ultimate organisers of the cigarette fraud business are known to the authorities (and some are), it is near-impossible to investigate their activities, let alone prosecute them.
The role of the cigarette producers
An unavoidable question
The cigarette industry is dominated by a relatively small number of major corporations.
The largest of them is the eighth-largest company in the world, and commands 30% of the European market.
It is impossible not to ask, in cases where companies' products are subject to widespread and large-scale fraud, to what extent those companies are aware of what is
Do manufacturers gain from smuggling?
A good place to start is to ask what possible interest a cigarette producer might have in the existence of smuggling.
A strong argument can be made that they do derive some benefit from it.
As one witness put it:
"They benefit from smuggling in several ways.
First they gain their normal profit by selling the cigarettes (legally) to distributors.
The cigarettes then find their way onto the black market to be sold at greatly reduced prices, stimulating demand.
This puts pressure on governments not to increase tax because of the loss of revenue, which may also result in lower prices and higher consumption.
The industry uses this to urge governments to reduce or not to increase taxes.
Finally, contraband cigarettes that are intercepted by customs have then to be replaced - yet more sales."19
To this one might add that smuggled cigarettes constitute market share as much as legitimate cigarettes.
Cheaper cigarettes on the black market may help expand market-share at the expense of rivals, especially insofar as they encourage the young to take up smoking.
On the other hand, major cigarette manufacturers have an interest in maintaining a good name, unsullied by association with criminal activity.
Moreover, there exist economic reasons for a producer to prefer to sell high-margin EU-produced products on the legitimate market as opposed to low margin product destined for contraband.
On the basis of the limited evidence it received, the Committee was unable to reach a conclusive verdict on this issue.
Awareness of fraud
Cigarette manufacturers are aware that their products are smuggled.
Mr Weatherburn of Rothmans for example stated to the Committee his belief that maybe 5% of Europe's contraband cigarettes were manufactured by his company.20All producers are likewise broadly aware of the extent to which their product is sold on the black market.
The real question is whether producers have any prior knowledge of smuggling of their product.
A convincing case can be made to suggest that manufacturers must be aware that at least a proportion of their sales will end up on the black market.
Simple arithmetic suggests this to be the case.
If a manufacturer produces and packages a quantity of cigarettes for sale on a particular country's domestic market and that quantity vastly exceeds the country's potential to absorb cigarettes (unless, in the words of one source, "all adults, all children and all domestic animals are smoking twenty-a-day"), suspicions should normally arise.
All manufacturers explicitly state that they pre-approve all purchasers.
This process is likely to involve checks on (i) identity, (ii) business reputation, (iii) legal status and (iv) financial solidity.
However, cigarette producers are not law enforcement agencies and do not
have access to more sensitive information. The checks they run would not necessarily eliminate the kind of purchasers located in tax havens and paying on cash terms, which are often involved in fraudulent business.
"Pre-approval" helps, but is not a fraud-proof filter.
Greater involvement? - the chain of ownership
Nobody has produced any evidence to the Committee demonstrating that cigarette manufacturers are actively involved in cigarette smuggling.
Fraudulent transactions typically involve several separate buyers between the initial purchase of the cigarettes and their disappearance from legitimate circulation.
Intermediate changes of hands can take place almost simultaneously in far-flung locations around the world, generally in countries which discourage inquisitive investigators.
Payments tend to arrive from similar places and, according to the Committee's evidence, is often "in cash or by prepayment by unknown companies, thus making any subsequent identification of the buyers virtually impossible".21
The cigarettes themselves are strictly accounted for whilst in storage in Antwerp.
Depending on the terms of sale, changes of ownership can begin while the cigarettes are still in the warehouse.
At this point, the buyer can request, for example, that identifying labels be removed from cartons prior to shipment.
This is a regular business practice, is perfectly legal and can have an irreproachable commercial purpose (the avoidance of parallel trading22).
Nevertheless, it certainly does not assist in tracing the origin of goods which may subsequently be intercepted on lonely beaches or bought on street corners.
Once physically on the move, cigarettes travelling by road can similarly undergo procedures which will serve to disguise their origins.
In the cases of detected cigarette frauds in Europe, the Committee has unequivocally been told that the "majority" involve transit through Swiss free zones at one point or another.
Despite the Swiss authorities' claim that these zones are not "Ali Baba caves",23 from the fraudster's point of view, they have the merit (i) of being on Swiss territory, thus falling under Swiss banking secrecy laws, and (ii) of allowing further sales of the merchandise without there being the obligation to notify customs of the change in ownership.24Furthermore, it is still not an crime under Swiss law to commit a fiscal offence if it does not affect Swiss financial interests.
Thus a form of immunity is given to the individuals concerned even if their identity is known (which it frequently is).
Switzerland is an ever-present element in cigarette fraud.
Its free zones, within which, under Swiss law, the Swiss customs authorities have limited jurisdiction, provide an ideal environment for disguising the origins of cigarettes.
Moreover, Swiss banking secrecy makes financial transactions inaccessible to investigators and the inability of the Swiss authorities to cooperate at the judicial level with EU states on what they define as "tax evasion" conspires to protect smugglers based there.
All in all, to quote the Head of the Commission's antifraud unit, UCLAF, Swiss facilities and secrecy laws are "a boon to fraudsters".25
Producers - conclusion
Key Points
The product most often targeted by the transit fraudster has consistently been cigarettes.
Following the introduction of the 100% guarantee, the displacement effect has led to a return to smuggling as opposed to "pure" transit fraud, though the transit system is still frequently used to move cigarettes to a place from which the smuggling can most advantageously be carried out Cigarettes, in themselves a legal product, offer the fraudster a huge potential margin between the duty-free cost and the domestic sale-price in EU countries.
Moreover, highly developed black-market distribution networks have existed in a number of European countries for some time.
Contraband cigarettes have been identified as generally originating outside the EU, though often transit through EU ports, Antwerp in particular.
It is also possible, of course, illicitly to re-import previously exported EU-produced cigarettes.
Law enforcement agencies have identified a number of routes typically used by cigarette smugglers, often involving legitimate transit operations via Switzerland into the former Eastern bloc countries, from where they return clandestinely into Germany and Italy.
Sea routes to fictitious African destinations allowing contraband to be dropped off along the Spanish coast are also much used.
A characteristic of cigarette smuggling operations are highly sophisticated financial operations and the involvement of certain nonEU countries offering legislation convenient to the fraudster, such as Switzerland, Cyprus, and a range of familiar offshore tax-havens, either as the source of illicit payments or as a haven for those organising cigarette crime.
Inevitable question marks arise over the role of major cigaretteproducers.
Though these are clearly aware that their products are the subject of criminal activity, no evidence has been produced of any direct involvement in cigarette smuggling.
Nevertheless, it remains hard to believe that producers can always be unaware of the likely illicit destination of their cigarettes, and that therefore greater efforts could not be made to cooperate with law-enforcement agencies or to ensure the legitimacy of the clients acquiring cigarettes from the producers and wholesalers.
Figures quoted for Marlboro in "Smuggling and cross border shopping of tobacco in Europe" (British Medical Journal: 27.5.1995) idem.
The expansion of cigarette smuggling in Germany was described in detail by Dr Rump of the German customs investigation branch in evidence to the Committee on 27.6.96
This point was confirmed by Mr Currie of the Commission: evidence of 17.7.96 (Notice to Members no. 54 - PE 220.261)
The Greek Government provided the Committee with specific information on the Albanian connection: Notice to Members no. 14 - PE 219.432
i.e. the customer of an intermediary discovering the intermediary's supplier and henceforward dealing direct with them
Information on free zones provided by the Swiss authorities: Contribution no. 41 - PE 220.873
Per Brix Knudsen: quoted in "Financial Times" - 4.12.96
ANALYSIS (V) - CUSTOMS CONTROL OF TRANSIT
Resources
The introduction of the European Single Market in 1993, with the consequent removal of internal customs borders, was seen by most Member States' governments as a justification for substantial reductions in the numbers of staff employed in national customs services.
With the single exception of Italy, such reductions have affected all EU countries, ranging from the 41% reduction seen in Portugal to the relatively moderate 7% in Austria.
The following table shows the replies received to question 13 of the Committee's questionnaire, inquiring as to numbers of customs agents in each Member State:
20,014 1.521 10,280 475 6,549 no reply
German figures include personnel of former GDR from 1991 * number of officers 100% dedicated to the transit regime.
Not only have the staff reductions described above undoubtedly had an effect on the ability of customs to cope with the volumes of paperwork connected with the transit system, but they have also clearly demotivated some customs services considerably.
Several witnesses have cited this demotivation as one of the reasons that customs are often unwilling to pursue detailed investigations1.
Some Member States even applied a systematic reduction to their customs personnel in the hope that this would assist them in achieving the Maastricht criteria for monetary union2.
A case in point, Germany, has subsequently reversed the policy in the awareness that the effectiveness of the customs service was being compromised.
Although Western European customs services are generally expected to be familiar with transit procedure and the rules affecting it, customs services in the Visegrad countries, often originally trained as border guards, which since 1 July 1996 have the responsibility of managing the common transit programme, are not in the same position.
The European Union, through its Phare programme, has offered support for training for officials in the Visegrad countries, but there are some doubts as to how effective this has been in reaching officers on the ground.
For instance, in spite of "good progress" so far, Mr Tweddle of the World Customs Organisation thought that "there is still quite a long way to go and that the countries of Central and Eastern Europe still need a great deal of support if their customs services are going to be effective".3 Mr Dusquesne also suggested that, while Phare-funded training was good, the essential problem was that the beneficiaries are in practice "dotted about" and "have no chance of putting their training into operation. "4
In the EU Member States, the Community funds the Matthaeus programme in order to promote training at a European level for customs officials.
It has become apparent to the Committee that in spite of the existence of this programme, customs officers are insufficiently aware of the powers, competences and activities of their colleagues in other Member States.
This affects adversely the ability of different services to cooperate in case of need (see below).
Material Resources
The customs services of different Member States clearly enjoy varying degrees of political priority in their own countries.
During the Committee's visit to Antwerp for example, Belgian customs officials complained that their access to the corridors of political power in Belgium was comparatively limited (certainly as compared to the Netherlands), and that therefore the priority for state funding afforded to them was lower5.
During the visit of the Committee on Budgetary Control to Rotterdam on the other hand, Dutch customs officials complained of uncertainties connected with state funding for new container scanning equipment6.
Perhaps the most extraordinary case to come to the attention of the Committee was that of the German customs service, whose offices apparently are at times equipped with neither fax machines nor computer equipment7.
Ironically, the Committee has also heard that the Visegrad states, in particular Czech Republic, are far better equipped with computer equipment than their large EU neighbour.
According to the Committee's witness from the German freight forwarders association: "the Hungarian and the Czechian administration will be very surprised at the slow and old-fashioned way the administrations of the existing EU states deal with them. "8
What is clear is that the customs services in different Member States feel themselves differently treated by their own governments in terms of the budgetary resources available to them.9In many respects, resources available reflect the customs "culture" of each country.
In France, for example, the customs service enjoys a high level of prestige and aclrninistrative powers amongst the most highly developed in the EU.
This is reflected for example in the provision of sophisticated container scanning equipment which is already available in Le Havre.
Physical Checks and Risk Analysis
The preventive activity par excellence of customs services is constituted by physical checks of merchandise carried out at borders and in airports.
Given the ever present, and probably increasing risk of misdeclaration of goods and indeed old-fashioned smuggling of merchandise, there is no reasonable alternative to physical checks as a dissuasive and preventive measure.
In its questionnaire to Member States, the Committee requested details of the number of physical checks carried out by customs services of goods under the transit procedure10.
The results are summarised in the following table:
Physical checks earned out on transit goods ias a
Average time to check the contents of a container ·■; ^(minutes).'■■:'; percentage).
D GR no reply 60
10 sensitive goods: 100%; others: 1%
What is striking about this table is the disparity between the number of checks carried out between different Member States.
Within the EU, the Dutch authorities physically check 0.5% of goods in transit while others attempt to approach 10%.
An inverse relation is observable between the claimed percentage level of checks and the time taken to check a container.
The Dutch authorities say that a container can take up to 12 hours to check while the Spanish set the time taken at 10 minutes.
There is clearly an underlying difference between these Member States as to what form a physical check should take.
The Dutch authorities in particular are very insistent that physical checks are of little or no use if not adequately targeted.
Their position is clearly conditioned by the volume of traffic passing through their main port, Rotterdam.
To quote the head of the Dutch customs service :
"Is it possible to check everything?
No, you cannot check everything.
If we did, we would have a queue stretching back to the middle of the North Sea or the Atlantic Ocean. ... We in Dutch customs made a very conscious choice in favour of the system of risk analysis."11
The Dutch customs service is a pioneer in the area of risk analysis.
It has concluded agreements with shipping companies which allow it to examine the manifests of incoming vessels before they arrive in order effectively to target controls.
A significant degree of success is claimed for the system: Mrs Nepperus mentioned, as an example, the fact that of the 14 containers stopped for physical checks in the Delta container terminal in Rotterdam on the day that the Committee on Budgetary Control visited, half showed irregularities of one sort or another.
Most Member States and DG XXI of the Commission now accept the indispensable role of risk analysis.
There is, however, less agreement on how it should work in practice and what level of physical checks it is necessary to carry out, even with the most advanced risk analysis techniques.
These differences of interpretation have led to a degree of mutual recrimination between EU customs services.
This issue will be dealt with below.
Even the much-vaunted possibilities offered by technology do not offer a solution to the trade-off between the flow of goods and the quantity of physical checks.
The container scanner system already operated by the French authorities in Le Havre, and on order for Rotterdam can deal at most with about 130,000 containers per year.12In terms of Rotterdam, this is a drop in the ocean.
Such instruments are a useful addition - in Mrs Nepperus' words, an "extra dimension".
En route checks - the powers of customs
In the context of the transit system, it is not only the physical checks carried out at ports which are important, but also the ability of the authorities to intercept vehicles inside the European Community.
Such checks, which can be similarly targeted by risk analysis techniques, be based on intelligence information or be completely random, have a useful role to play in dealing with fraud.
The powers enjoyed by the authorities in Member States to carry out such checks are, however, by no means uniform.
The following table summarises the differences in the powers of the authorities to intercept vehicles relating to inland checks13:
DO YOU HAVE POWERS TO STOP AND CONTROL INLAND?
YES, up to 25 KM from border or port
YES, up to 25 KM from border or port YES, if vehicle under customs regime NO, at present, YES under forthcoming legislation
SLOVAKIA HUNGARY POLAND SPAIN
YES, but in practice by antifraud units or in cooperation with police or Guardia Civil.
NO, but can cooperate with police or "brigade fiscal" YES, full powers YES, specialised teams deployed NO. but can cooperate and instruct police in some cases YES, if reasonable suspicion exists
UNITED KINGDOM
YES, greater powers than police.
(Practical problem: facilities are at border only)
YES, in practice by antifraud units or in cooperation with police
GERMANY NETHERLANDS IRELAND AUSTRIA FINLAND ITALY SWEDEN DENMARK
YES, mobile control units deployed YES, full powers, but suspicion required YES, "targeted checking" NO, but a customs flying squad has some powers YES, powers same as police YES.
Can cooperate with police or Guardia di Finanza YES, if vehicle identiñably under customs regime NO, can cooperate with police
This table again highlights the differences between Member States' customs services.
Whereas some enjoy powers equivalent to those of the police to intervene throughout the territory of the country concerned, others are extremely limited outside port areas and rely on the assistance of the police or other national law enforcement agencies.
In some Member States, the picture is further complicated by the presence of state police services with fiscal competences which have no equivalents in other countries.
In these cases, there could be overlap between the activities of the customs services and those of the fiscal police.
A high degree of coordination amongst the customs authorities is required in such cases and there is inevitably some competition for resources from national budgets.14
Early Warning System
One new preventive tool employed by customs services is the so-called Early Warning system operating through the SCENT network15.
This is a network connecting the customs services and other law enforcement agencies in the EU.
This system can be used to transmit electronic messages from the offices of departure to inform countries of destination of the arrival of sensitive goods.
A copy of each Early Warning message is received by the Commission's
The system is a relatively ad-hoc one insofar as it relies on a network which was not designed specifically for this purpose.
Nevertheless, it has been useful in establishing patterns of trade and in detecting cases of fraud.
However, the use made of the system by Member States leaves much to be desired.
Figures provided by UCLAF indicate that certain Member States make little use of the potential in the system.
In the period June to September 1996, Commission figures indicate that while the Belgians transmitted 3,066 messages, the Germans 5,085 and the Dutch 4,824, the Greeks sent one, the Irish none, the Portuguese three and the Swedish two.16
A similar example was given by Spanish customs in its evidence to the Committee.
In what was described as a "lamentable statistic", in 1995 of the 226,000 notifiable operations involving Spain as a destination, only around 1,000 notifications were given.17
Even therefore when systems exist, they are not necessarily applied adequately and do not fully serve their purpose.
Intelligence Sharing - Mutual assistance
A system also exists for the transmission of information between Member States in order to tackle offences which have already occurred.
The Customs Information system (CIS) is likewise a computer-based intelligence sharing system, but one based on requests for information from the responsible customs service.
In theory, a customs office wishing to investigate a fraudulent operation involving other Member States can transmit an inquiry through the CIS, requesting information on specific operations, individuals, vehicles, etc. from the competent authorities.
The indications are that such cooperation and exchange of information exists more in theory than in practice.
Although several witnesses have made relatively upbeat comments about the cooperation that exists between the custom services of Member States, many have suggested that there is room for improvement in the intelligence sharing mechanisms they employ.
Mr Douglas Tweddle of the World Customs Organisation for example saw "much greater scope for customs cooperation and increased mutual assistance"18.
Similarly, Mr Eland of British Customs pointed to the need for a better information sharing system at Community level.19
One particular problem arises in the field of the protection of information.
Mutual assistance relies above all on the quality, depth and usefulness of the information available through the CIS.
Difficulties arise with some national legislations in connection with data protection law.
The German authorities have particular difficulties in this context, though the problem is not limited to them.
A more general, practical, problem with the mutual assistance system is simply that too many requests for assistance are being made.
So rife has fraud become in the transit system that Member States' customs services no longer have the collective capacity to handle them.
Practical cooperation
The evidence received by the Committee has been contradictory on the question of how well custom services cooperate in practice.
Numerous examples have been cited, often of cases still under judicial investigation, where cooperation seems to have been exemplary.
The head of the British customs, for example, told the Committee "our relationships with other customs services and with other competent authorities in other Member States, such as the Guardia di Finanza in Italy are extremely good.
We receive good cooperation from them."23The same impression was given by Mr Tweddle who stated that "from my personal experience, I know that there are some very good bilateral relationships between the customs services of Western Europe24.
The witnesses from French customs gave another example: of an officer on secondment to Europol.25
Again however, the picture is not always as harmonious.
Mr Pinto of UCLAF, testifying before the Committee on 30 September 1996, said the following:
"Some Member States are more efficient than others and react more rapidly.
As to identifying the Member States concerned and saying which ones cooperate less, that is a delicate area.
I am not saying that they do not cooperate, but some cooperate less than others."26
The impression given by this comment is borne out by many of the contacts the Committee has had outside the context of formal meetings.
Cooperation appears to work in an extremely ad hoc fashion, with informal bilateral contacts being extremely important in making cooperation work.
Certainly the Committee has been given the impression that the formal undertakings entered into by Member States to cooperate in customs investigations do not in themselves achieve swift and effective cooperation.
In specific cases however, goodwill on both sides has achieved some highly successful investigations.
Many witnesses have pointed to the facilitating role that UCLAF can play in bringing about the vital bilateral contacts mentioned in the previous paragraph.
Information provided by UCLAF and meetings arranged in its Brussels headquarters or in the Member States have often proved fruitful.
Seminars, such as that held in Limerick on 19 November 1996 on Tobacco fraud, play an essential role in bringing together customs investigators working on similar problems.
In the words of Mr Pinto of UCLAF:
"Member States fortunately realize that they will never be able to carry out direct investigations themselves and, as organised transnational fraud is involved, they call on UCLAF to undertake responsibility for contacting the various authorities of the different Member States and obtaining maximum information"27
Disparities between Member States
The difference between the powers of the customs services of Member States has already been mentioned.
While some enjoy powers almost equivalent to (or even exceeding) those of the police, others are much more limited.
Some are predominantly administrative services without the resources or equipment to pursue fraudsters or make arrests.
This discrepancy between the powers of customs services can give rise to difficulties in achieving effective cooperation.
For example, the French customs service which has wideranging powers to stop and search throughout France could not request similar action on the part of its Danish equivalent.
In reply to the Committee's questionnaire, a number of Member States made mention of powers enjoyed by their counterparts elsewhere which they would find useful.
Particularly cited were the powers necessary to undertake criminal investigations or to impose admiriistrative penalties,28 a factor mentioned also by Mr Tweddle of the WCO:
"In some countries customs services have full police powers of investigation and arrest, in other countries they do not have those powers and rely entirely on the police agencies to carry out the hard end of enforcement work. ...
I believe that customs services with their own investigation and arrest powers tend to be more effective than those which do not have them."29
Customs "Culture"
A far more contentious issue emerges in relation to the approach of different customs services to their jobs.
On numerous occasions, the Committee has heard complaints that the customs services of other Member States were less rigorous in their controls and less dynamic in their investigations.
In Rotterdam for example, the Committee on Budgetary Control encountered a clear perception on the part of Dutch customs that the customs services of neighbouring countries applied less strict standards to their work, thus obtaining a competitive advantage for the ports in which they worked.
Ironically, the same point was made with great force in relation to the Netherlands customs service in other parts of Europe.
During its visit to Leonardo da Vinci airport in Rome for example the Italian customs service expressed an awareness that the more "repressive" attitude of the Italian customs service had the effect of diverting traffic to northern European airports, of which Schiphol was one named example.
There is a cultural north/south divide in the Communities' customs services.
Whereas for example the customs services of France, Italy and Spain place strong emphasis on suppressing fraudulent activity, even where they are aware that this might be damaging to commercial interests, in the Netherlands, Belgium and the United Kingdom for example,
customs openly state their interest in placing as few obstacles in the way of commerce as possible.
Though these positions are not mutually exclusive, they do represent a considerable difference of emphasis, which may lead in time to a distortion of the "level playing field" on which the single market relies.
The Commission has so far made little attempt to establish common practices for customs services.
It is not for the Committee of Inquiry to favour one approach above the other, particularly as the circumstances in the high volume ports of the north differ from those in the Mediterranean.
Moreover, the Committee has not heard any convincing evidence that one approach is necessarily more effective than another.
The Committee does however note the level of complaints on this issue and the adverse effects it has on effective cooperation between customs.
Although different approaches have undoubtedly developed as a result of different needs, the coming of the single market would suggest that a more harmonious approach to customs intervention in trade is needed.
Policy on Investigation
Do customs investigate irregularities sufficiently often?
Whatever the investigative instruments at the disposal of customs services, a frequent complaint heard by the Committee is that they do not use ¿hem.
The complaint naturally comes predominantly from the trade who are called upon to make guarantee payments relating to incomplete transit operations.
This point was made at the beginning of the Committee's work by Freight Forward Europe in particular:
" customs administrations and Member States are no longer pursuing the real perpetrators of fraud but have made a collection of duties from freight forwarders a routine procedure Customs are no longer controlling the transit system but they consider it an insurance system"30.
Given the difficulties in carrying out investigations described not only above but also in section 3.3 of this report, it is not altogether surprising that customs are often unable to investigate in any meaningful sense irregularities of which they are notified.
The Committee would accept therefore the truth of the freight forwarders assessment that not all cases are adequately investigated, but would likewise accept that the failings of the system are such that effective investigations are not always possible.
In this sense, as has already been stated, the freight forwarders are the fall guys not only for the failings of customs but for those of the system as a whole.
Key Points
Customs services have suffered not only from a recent squeeze on their staff numbers over the last few years, but also the significant demotivation which has arisen from iL In contrast to this common experience however, marked differences are discernable between the customs services of EU member States and other countries involved in Common Transit, ranging from the legal powers they possess and their material resources to their "culture" and practice in carrying out their tasks.
For this reason among others, cooperation between customs is ineffective.
This ineffectiveness begins with the reluctance to share basic operational intelligence (even to use the SCENT early warning system) and ends with practical investigative cooperation which is, notwithstanding the best but necessarily limited efforts of UCLAF, successful only on an occasional basis.
In the face of the difficulties in handling the transit system, all customs services have to varying degrees displayed an inability either to respect the terms of the basic legislation (particularly the deadlines for the various procedures) or effectively to investigate frauds and potential frauds.
So unlikely is it in most cases that fraudsters will be apprehended that customs often do not attempt an investigation but turn instead to the transit principal for recovery of lost revenue as a first resort.
In this sense transit is now managed by the authorities as an insurance system for public revenue rather than a system for the facilitation of trade.
See Mission Report of Committee on Budgetary Control on the visit to Rotterdam - PE 217.467
Based on minutes of the Ad Hoc Working Group "Transit Reform" of 58 November 1996: Notice to Members no. 67 - PE 220.793
See in particular the report of the visit to Rome and Naples : Notice to Members no. 61 - PE 220.195
Described in Notice to Members no. 47 -PE 220.102
S. ANALYSIS (VI) - PROSECUTION AND LEGAL SANCTIONS
Court cases - statistics
Introduction
In May 1996, the Committee of Inquiry submitted a request to Member States requesting information concerning court cases in the transit sector since 1990 in each country.
Answers have been received from all Member States.1The following statistics have been prepared on the basis of the available figures.
The request was for information of the year of the infringement, the products concerned, their value, the point of entry, the destination and the penalties imposed.
Volumes of cases
It is not strictly possible to compare the figures provided by the Member States as legal systems vary.
It may be for example that the "numerous" cases concluded in the United Kingdom in the form of out of court settlements (with a fine and the recovery of the lost revenue), which do not feature in the statistics would feature in those of other Member States.
Nevertheless, the figures show a boom in the number of cases in the 1990-1994 period.
In terms of the value of the duties and taxes involved, most of the amounts concerned arose from a very few high-value consignments.
The graph below shows the number of prosecutions brought in connection with transit fraud in the EU from 1989 to 1995.
Place of prosecution
In most cases, the place of prosecution was in the Member State in which the transit operation began. Unsurprisingly, as the following graph shows, the largest number of cases were prosecuted in the countries most involved in the transit system.
The graph shows accumulated cases over the 1986 to 1996 period in each Member State:
Breakdown by product
The following chart shows the breakdown of the reported cases by the product involved.
A marked preponderance of "sensitive goods" (either because of high excise or CAP-related incentives) is discernable.
It should be noted that the breakdown given here is a numerical one, and does not reflect value.
Judicial Cooperation
Case study - a failed prosecution
The following is a description of a prosecution relating to a major alleged transit fraud.2
On 15 May 1996 UK Customs and Excise lost a case in Southwark Crown Court in London.
The case involved the fraudulent diversion of 7 lorry loads of cigarettes (5 into Italy and 2 into Spain) which were due to be transported under a TIR Carnet from the UK to a destination outside the EU.
The judge did not accept that the defendants had a case to answer and instructed the jury formally to acquit them.
The first two charges were rejected because the judge excluded from evidence the certificate of Spanish law.
This certificate was designed to establish that the defendants were cornmhting a serious offence under Spanish law but the judge did not consider it to be sufficiently clear.
He was left uncertain, for example, as to the extent of the penalty that the offence should carry.
The conspiracy charge was rejected by the judge on the grounds that the provisions of the relevant act did not cover an investigation of smuggling in Italy.
For their part the customs had not used the same argument as with the Spanish certificate because they were not sure whether cigarette smuggling was a serious offence in Italy at the time of the alleged crime.
The customs service considered that they would have had a better chance to obtain a conviction if: in relation to the certificate of Spanish law, they had considered bringing a live witness, whose specific knowledge would warrant the expenditure involved; and in relation to the conspiracy charge, they had argued that the conspiracy was directed not at them but at the Freight Transport Association by putting the organisation at financial risk on the guarantee that it had issued.
They also admitted that they would have been able to use French motorway toll receipts found on one of the drivers as evidence if they had been able to produce a certificate showing that the computerised machine was working.
As it was, the judge excluded the receipts as evidence.
The practical obstacles to cooperation
Just as the practice of investigative cooperation fails to match the theory, so judicial cooperation proves in reality to be "slow, formalistic and not responsive to the needs of the prosecution"3.
While the instruments, indeed the requirement, for cooperation exist, the formal structures are such as to make the system slow and ineffective.
According to Mr De Beaufort, a state prosecutor based in Rotterdam who testified before the Committee, a request for assistance in the preparation of a prosecution addressed to the authorities of another Member State is required to pass upward through the national hierarchy, across the diplomatic structures of EU Member States and thence downward to the particular service competent in the country concerned.
Even then there is no guarantee that the matter will be afforded either the priority or the urgency it would receive in the country of prosecution.
Such are the delays in the system that it may not be worth pursuing a case to the exclusion of other demands on valuable court time.
Certainly in a country such as the Netherlands, where prosecution is not compulsory, considerations of this kind can weigh heavily with the competent authorities, especially when administrative sanctions may offer a more rapid and cheaper solution.
Furthermore, there is financial pressure on the authorities not even to go so far as to impose administrative sanctions.
According, for example, to Mr Linington, "a number of authorities have decided that their resources are better invested in going after the guarantor who is available, tangible and has a cheque-book.
I am afraid that is a knock-on effect of this"4.
Again in a manner similar to investigative cooperation, judicial cooperation (which of course overlaps in the case of a system employing investigating magistrates) works most effectively when immediate, direct and personal contacts are possible.
In most cases, as Mr De Beaufort indicated, a state prosecutor "does not know who to phone".
The problem is a simple but significant one, as it prevents the kind of immediate personal contact which proves time and again to be the only effective means of making international cooperation work.
Structural obstacles
The problem is compounded by the different legal structures in different Member States.
For example, in some countries, the correct interlocutor for an investigating magistrate would be a police officer, whose perspectives and professional attitudes might be different.
Legal obstacles
The Southwark Crown Court case cited above (see box), is an illustration of the fact that, however good the investigative and preliminary legal cooperation (in this case it was "exemplary"5), it can be rendered void by unforeseen legal difficulties.
In the case concerned, there were two other Member States potentially involved.
The greater part of the offence in value terms took place in Italy, but here the prosecution did not even try to make a case:
"all charges relating to the goods that were diverted onto the Italian market were dropped because again there was an inability on the part of the prosecution to bring the
The problem here was apparently that the prosecution could not demonstrate that cigarette smuggling is classified as a "serious offence" in Italy, this being a prerequisite for the prosecution of the offence in the UK.7The UK customs was "unclear about the Italian law at that time".8
The second Member State involved was Spain, and in this case the prosecution proceeded on the basis of a certificate from the Spanish authorities confirming that the crime committed in Spain was a "serious offence"9.
The prosecution fell when the counsel for the defence successfully argued that the Spanish certificate did not comply with the requirements of English law.
Moreover, other evidence submitted - French motorway toll receipts - were not admissible because the prosecution could not produce a certificate stating that the machines which emitted them were in good working order.
Obviously, the key problem is one of the standards of evidence required from other Member States.
At present there are no common EU standards of evidence.
Furthermore, there was nobody in UK Customs with the expertise to ensure that the evidence corresponded to English standards and practices.
Though to some extent comprehensible, insofar as the case relied on untested law (the power to bring a prosecution for an offence taking place on the territory of a third country), the defects in the preparation of prosecution were serious, and potentially undennine both the willingness of customs to bring cases of this sort and the dissuasive effect of the trial on others.
The difficulties of the prosecution were compounded by the nature of the case itself.
Besides the problems presented by the evidence, transit fraud is in any case not an easy offence to prosecute.
The following excerpt is quoted in full because it is so apposite in illustrating not only the legal difficulties, but also the outside world's view of the transit system and, in consequence, one of the main reasons the present report is being drawn up.
"They were faced with new legislation.
They were faced with a judge who was having difficulty in grasping exactly what crime had been committed and where.
From my observation of the jury, it was totally bewildered by what was being said because introduced into the whole case there were companies that had been set up in the Isle of Man, companies set up in Ireland, goods shipped from Cyprus, goods moving from Switzerland to bonded warehouses in Morocco; and there was not a body, they could not actually see who had been damaged by all of this.
Of course within that the defence were advancing the fact that this whole system was incredibly complicated, that there were many claims by customs authorities which were due to defects in the system itself, that they were due to errors because pieces of paper had not passed from one customs authority to another and even if the case had gone ahead I would not have put money on anybody being convicted."10
Not all Member States have exactly the same difficulties.
During its visit to Antwerp, the Committee heard from a Belgian state prosecutor that Belgian law accepted as admissible evidence that which would meet the standards of its country of origin.11Evidence from Switzerland has thus been usable in Belgian Courts.
Even however where evidence did not produce a problem, the mechanism of legal cooperation employed - the commission rogatone -
Switzerland also presents particular problems of legal cooperation.
Under Swiss law, the Swiss authorities do not offer cooperation for offences of fiscal evasion.
While in recent cases (which cannot be quoted directly here) cooperation has improved as a result of goodwill on the part of a senior Swiss prosecutor in linking ostensible fiscal evasion cases with offences for which cooperation does exist, such as money laundering, criminal association, etc.
According to the Committee's information, Switzerland has provided legal cooperation in 26 cases since 1993.12Nevertheless, it would be true to say that such cooperation has not yet become systematic.
Just as UCLAF increasingly plays a role in bringing investigators together, it is increasingly facilitating contacts between prosecutors and offering advice on the legal systems of Member States.
Some prosecutors have been enthusiastic in their support for the activities of UCLAF in this field, pointing to the contribution it has made in specific cases.
However, the praise for UCLAF is qualified by the institutional limits placed on it.
Mr De Beaufort for example said that cooperation with UCLAF "works strangely"13, because it has difficulties with the formal position of the Commission.
At present UCLAF has no formal role in the field of legal cooperation, and no clear relationship with the legal systems of Member States.
Its interventions remain ad hoc and at times stretch the terms of its mandate to the limit.
Sanctions
There still exists no operational common definition of fraud in legal systems of the countries of the EU14, nor any harmonised system of sanctions.
While some admit an offence of fraud against the EC budget (e.g. Italy) others have to use other indictments according to the precise nature of the crime.
In the UK case cited above, the first count on which the indictment was brought was "Assisting in or inducing conduct outside the United Kingdom which involves the commission of a serious offence against the law of another member state"13.
Though the case fell, it should be noted that only the UK and Germany currently have legislation expressly permitting the prosecution of an offence taking place in another Member State.
Sanctions
The study of the court cases undertaken by the Committee of Inquiry show that the outcomes can be rather different depending on the Member State concerned.
While for example a substantial proportion of the cases brought in Germany and the Netherlands resulted in prison sentences, very few occurred in Belgium.
Conclusions
The final hurdle
This is not a detailed legal review of the current system of legal cooperation in the EU.
This has been the subject of a great deal of laborious work which cannot be matched in a report about the transit system.
Nevertheless, if this section illustrates how the undoubted defects in the system of judicial cooperation with the EU and its partner states undermines the ability of the authorities to prosecute transit fraud, it will already have served a useful purpose.
Any law enforcement system must combine prevention, investigation and sanction.
The legal situation currently ensures that, even if the multitude of difficulties faced by investigators are overcome, the final hurdle - a successful prosecution - presents excessive obstacles.
In these circumstances the disincentives to defrauding the transit system look even less convincing and the fraudster's cost-benefit analysis even more favourable.
Key Points
Transit fraud L· almost by definition multinational in nature, complicating its investigation from the start.
Even where cases of transit fraud are successfully investigated by the authorities, the deterrent effect this may have is severely undermined by the legal difficulties in bringing effective prosecutions in cases involving more than one country.
Prosecutors point in the first place to the practical obstacles to legal cooperation, which can be as simple as not knowing whom to contact in another Member State, and to the formalistic and burdensome procedures required to request assistance in a prosecution.
When cooperation is obtained, prosecutions can flounder on the differences between national legal systems.
Standards of evidence vary, creating potential problems with the admissibility in court of documents and evidence from other Member States.
At present there is no agreement between Member States on definitions of fraud, the sanctions to be applied or the procedures by which cross-border prosecutions can be brought.
The result is that similar offences receive substantially different treatment depending on the place of prosecution.
A particular difficulty surrounds prosecutions involving cooperation with the Swiss authorities.
Under Swiss legislation no legal assistance can be provided to other countries on tax evasion - including most customs crime - and Swiss banking secrecy impedes investigation of the financial aspects of smuggling operations.
Even when cooperation in prosecutions does take place, the mechanism required - the "commission rogatoire " - is time-consuming and bureaucratic and provides no guarantee that a case will even be heard in the EU Member State affected.
An analysis of the information received was published in Information Note No. 5 - PE 219.293/rev. The statistics in this section are extracted from this document.
The case was discussed at length in the Committee's hearing of 27.11.96, and an assessment of the reasons for the failure of the prosecution by UK Customs is contained within Contribution No. 39 - PE 220.193
Expression used in the report of the Committee on Budgetary Control on its visit to Rotterdam (PE 217.467) and quoted by the chairman of the Inquiry Committee to Mr De Beaufort, state prosecutor in Rotterdam.
Mr De Beaufort confirmed that the expression accurately summarised his own view.
(Evidence of 27.11.96)
Defined under English law as punishable by a penalty of "a maximum of twelve months or more" (UK Criminal Justice Act - Section 71 (2))
A general definition of fraud was agreed by the Member States in the context of Council Regulation 2988/95 of 18.12.95 (OJ L312 of 23.12.95) and in the Convention on the protection of the financial interests of the Community (OJ C316 of 27.11.95).
However, the fewer is not yet operational in national legal systems and the latter remains to be ratified by national parliaments.
The Question of Responsibilities
The preceding sections have described the failings of the transit system, examined the extent of the crisis, the nature of the fraud perpetrated in and around it and the obstacles in the way of those who are supposed to clean up the mess it leaves behind.
Before going on to look at some of the options for reforms by which the system might be improved, an important part of the work of any Committee of Inquiry is to attribute responsibilities for the situation which has been allowed to arise.
If an obsolete, error-ridden, fraud-prone and unpoliceable system has emerged - as this report contends it has - then those who proposed it, legislated for it, managed it and even used it must answer the question: what was your role in this?
That is what this section is about.
The terms of the Treaty, the Interinstitutional Agreement and Parliament's own rules of procedure covering the activities of the Committee have been described in detail in Section 2 of this document.
It should be stressed here that a Committee of Inquiry of the European Parliament is not a judicial or even quasi-judicial body. Whether it should be is a separate issue, but at present a Committee of Inquiry cannot:
- adrriinister oaths, - legally oblige specific individuals to testify, - publish names of persons implicated in the course of the inquiry, - publish information received in confidential session, or - apply sanctions for false or inaccurate testimony.
Attributions of responsibility are therefore of a political rather than a judicial nature, and must not directly concern individuals.
They are therefore intended to have political consequences, in the manner which will be laid out in the conclusions of this report.
Managing Growth of the System
As has been pointed out, the Community Transit System was designed in 1968 for a Community consisting of 6 Member States, three of which were already in a customs union.
Trade between the Member States was then a fraction of the levels we are familiar with today.
Since, the European Community has been enlarged to 15 Member States, the Cold War has ended, the Single European Market has been introduced and, most recently, the Common Transit System has been extended to encompass the 4 Visegrad states.
As a consequence of these events, a transit system designed to cover 6 countries at a relatively low level of trade now covers some 23 countries at a vastly increased level of international traffic.
It is remarkable in these circumstances that the Community Transit System, and its cousin the Common Transit System, remain almost unchanged in their essentials since their inception.
A competent administration does not create a system unless it also has the instruments necessary to manage it.
In 1968, the instruments of the Transit System were probably sufficient, but in 1992, as this report has already made clear, the removal of internal frontiers rendered them effectively obsolete, if they were not so already.
It is generally agreed that "the removal of the customs barriers within the European Union has led to an increase in fraud in both the excise and VAT areas"1.
It is therefore remarkable that the introduction of the single market saw no major reform of the transit system, other than the automatic abolition of the T2 system.
Only in early 1995, when the Commission published its communication to the European Parliament and to the Council on the failings of the Transit System,2did the Commission initiate a serious attempt to bring the attention of the political world to bear on the crisis which had developed.
By this time, the Commission had been contacted by numerous organisations, among whom were several of the Committee's witnesses,3 with complaints of growing fraud and ineffectiveness on the part of the customs authorities in dealing with it.
To compound matters, the Commission, extraordinarily in the light of its earlier communication, failed to alert the political authorities to the possible consequences of extending the Common Transit System to the 4 Visegrad states.
European Parliament implicit in its acceptance of the relevant "Europe Agreements"5, the Commission did not raise the matter with the political authorities in any clear way, and did not react to expressions of concern about the formcoming extension in the European Parliament6.
(A description of the events surrounding the extension of the Common Transit System is provided in the boxed section below.)
Extension of the Common Transit System
In 1992 the Visegrad countries - Poland, Hungary, the Czech Republic and Slovakia - applied to accede to the "Convention on a common transit procedure", which had linked the Community transit system to the EFTA countries since 1987.
The subsequent negotiations should be seen in the light of the conclusion in December 1993, and then ratification in December 1994, of the Association Agreements, commonly known as "Europe Agreements', between the EU and these countries.
These Agreements constitute a framework for their gradual integration into the EU and contain provisions on customs cooperation including the establishment of links between the transit procedures of the Community and the associated states.
Following approval by the Council of a Commission proposal that the Community agree to an extension of the Common Transit Convention, the joint committee consisting of the Directors-General of customs administrations participating in the Convention, established pursuant to Article 15 of the 1987 Convention, decided to invite the four applicants to accede with effect from 1 July 1996.
This decision was taken at Interlaken on 26 October 1995.
The European Parliament expressed its reserves about this extension in its resolution of 17 April 1996 on preparation for accession of East European countries (Oostlander report).
The EP noted the considerable defects in the current EU transit procedure and rejected its extension before reform, before resources in all customs administrations available to deal with transit were adequate and before the legal powers of customs officials throughout the customs area were sufficient to police it.
However, enlargement of the membership of the Convention did take place following ratification by the parties and it now includes the EU Member States, the four countries concerned and Switzerland (inc. Liechtenstein), Norway and Iceland.
Article 14 of the Convention provides that the Joint Committee, which administers the Convention, shall act by mutual agreement, inter alia, in regard to amendments to the procedures to be applied f or common transit.
The issue of further extension of the system has arisen now that applications for accession to the common transit Convention have also been received from Bulgaria, Romania, Slovenia, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania and Turkey.
The EP invited Commissioner Monti to comment on these applications in an oral question with debate at the plenary sitting of 5 September 1996.
The Commissioner made it clear that technical assistance, such as that given to the Visegrad states, would be provided in preparation for future extensions of the regime.
However, no decision would be taken to admit these other countries until a satisfactory level of preparation was attained and they were fully able to accept their obligations in a transit system as reformed.
Mr Monti subsequently clarified this point in a letter to the Committee of Inquiry (Notice to Members No.37, PE 219.601 of 17.9.96) in which he gave an assurance that further extension of the common transit convention would not take place until the common transit system was reformed and computerised.
Though the Committee would not, in any way, wish to question the political priorities of the Community over recent years, it is incumbent on it to point out that enthusiasm for an open economy has apparently led to a neglect of the measures required to ensure that the openness of the economy and the ease with which goods could be moved around within the European Union did not render it extremely vulnerable to fraud.
Similar enthusiasm was shown in the years following 1990 when the Community wished to open trading relations with the countries of Central and Eastern Europe.
No one would wish to see the replacement of the Berlin Wall, but a responsibility has to be borne by the political authorities, which include the Commission, for the fact that the policy was pursued without due consideration of the potential drawbacks.
It can of course be argued that the Commission bears only a partial responsibility for the lack of emphasis that was given to preventing the adverse effects of an open trading system.
It is nevertheless true that there are matters for which the Commission bears sole responsibility, namely administrative steps which could have been taken to deal with potential problems.
Commission Inactivity
Apart from the introduction of the Early Warning System in 1992, the essential remedial measures to deal with short-term problems only began to appear in mid-1995.
For example, the introduction of the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods, probably the most significant counter-measure taken by the Commission to deal with fraud (though its credentials as an antifraud measure are highly questionable) was only introduced in February 1996, long after severe problems in the transit system had been brought to the attention of the Commission and long after the French customs service, according to their evidence, for one had proposed this very measure.7
Other measures, such as the increase in the value of the comprehensive guarantee to 100% of weekly turnover, and the introduction of possible compulsory itineraries and the removal of the automatic right to change office of destination without mforrning the customs authorities, are much more recent.8
The Committee has also had disturbing indications that the Commission has at times been a force for inertia in the reform of the TIR system. During its visit to Geneva,9the
Committee heard that the Commission had, for example, stood in the way of the inclusion of barcodes on TIR carnets as an additional (and relatively simple) security measure, in the context of its participation in the relevant UN working party, WP 30.
The same picture emerged in evidence later:
"It is a mystery to me why this [barcodes proposal] has not been enthusiastically endorsed by those other members of WP30, unfortunately the ones that count. ... (in response to chairman) I mean those who are representing national bodies as either customs representatives, ministry representatives or representing the European Union through the Commission".10
when the need for transit computerisation should already have been apparent.
In these circumstances it is absurd that NCTS should be a current (and incomplete) project in 1997.
A Frustrated Commission
Although there might be a whole set of more or less legitimate reasons why the Commission failed to take autonomous action and for its tardiness in alerting the political authorities to a growing crisis within the transit system, the Commission cannot escape its responsibilities, as conferred on it by the Treaty.
The service of the Commission responsible for the transit system, DG XXI does not always work in the easiest of institutional frameworks.
One witness, for example, expressed a certain degree of support and understanding of DG XXI:
"The better people from the customs services tend to get to DG XXI.
I am aware of some of the difficulties and frustrations they have of operating in the environment they are in.
I have to say that they do it well."11
The structures of the European Union are of course such that the Commission is severely constrained in the legislative area, and relies upon the support and cooperation of the political authorities, particularly the Council of Ministers.
Member States
Introduction
Responsibility for the current state of affairs in the transit system is shared by the Member States.
- complementary national legislation and the provision of the necessary services and resources lies in the hands of the Member State governments acting individually; and
The following three sub-sections take each of these levels in turn, though naturally, they are intimately interconnected as government policy and therefore Council action are necessarily conditioned by the internal political process in the Member State at the heart of which will be the National Customs Service.
Member States (1) - The Council
Politically, legally and morally, the Council, as the European Union's main legislative body, bears the responsibility for the quality of the legislation adopted.
As far as the transit system is concerned, the Community Customs Code (CCC) is the basic text,12and it takes the form of a Council Regulation.
Other legislation which will have a marked impact on the functioning of customs services in the European Union, Customs 2000, is a decision of the Council and the European Parliament in the framework of the co-decision procedure.
It is the view of the European Parliament that neither of these basic texts provide an adequate framework for the good functioning of the transit system nor of the customs services in general.
(Customs 2000 was the subject of a conciliation procedure between the European Parliament and the Council, in which the Council flatly rejected several European Parliament proposals aimed at achieving greater efficiency and coherence in Community customs.)
Policy Balance
Just as the Commission, as proposer of legislation, failed adequately to deal with some of the consequences of the introduction of the Single Market, the Council, adopted the legislation concerned.
There is no question that the grand political design of the Single Market, figured rather more prominently in the minds of the Council of Ministers than did the consequences in terms of fraud.
Though this approach is natural, the Council did not merely neglect the antifraud aspects associated with Single Market legislation, it actively resisted measures which would have helped deal with them.
To quote Commissioner Monti:
"The Council refuses almost systematically to agree to concrete measures to strengthen the Commission's powers of supervision in the Member States."13
On the basis of the remarks by Commissioner MONTI, the contradictory and ambiguous attitude of the Member States and the Council should be condemned in strong terms. That attitude consists of making declarations of intent extolling the virtues of more effective European Union measures to combat fraud, but of acting in such a way as to make such measures practically impossible or even deliberately hindering them by seeking to preserve, for whatever purpose, the prerogatives of the national administrations to the detriment of the Commission's powers and by consolidating the administrative compartmentalization which thwarts any Community progress in this area.
The unwillingness to pass appropriate legislation is not limited only to the definition of the Commission's monitoring powers, That unwillingness can be seen more generally in the fundamental areas of judicial and police cooperation and administrative assistance in customs matters, the only measures likely to offset the perverse impact of the judicial borders which still exist between the Member States.
In order to illustrate the Council's failings, reference should be made to the inexplicable delays in implementing to Community policies (the adoption of the draft regulation on administrative assistance in the customs sphere has been pending for more than three years!) or third pillar policies (late implementation of genuine judicial and police cooperation, Europol, the convention on the protection of financial interests, etc.).
Member States (2) - National Governments
Awareness of Issues
The evidence given by customs authorities has not in general indicated a high political priority on the part of national finance and transport ministries for the improvement of the transit system.14A meeting attended by the Committee's rapporteur in Paris, at which preparations were made for a meeting of ministers on the subject of crime in transport,10revealed that, by and large, national transport ministers at least were generally unaware of the scale of the crisis.
This has also been the impression gathered by members of the Committee of Inquiry on delegation visits during which they have met national ministers of finance and other responsible politicians.
The degree of unawareness or unconcern shown by finance ministers, and their consequent inaction, has been particularly surprising to the Committee in view of the substantial losses to public revenue involved at a time of general budgetary stringency.
The lack of political priority mentioned above, has led to problems in the necessary funding for the Commission's forthcoming computerisation project.
In the responses to the Committee's questionnaire on the likelihood of computerisation (i) being completed on schedule, and (ii) being adequately funded by Member States' governments, most customs services expressed clear doubts as to whether either of these pre-conditions of computerisation would be achieved - a view reflected in testimony:
"The Member States on the other hand are saying that they cannot commit themselves to that because they do not know at all if they can finance it."16
Commissioner Gradin went a little further concurring with the chairman's suggestion that there was "administrative resistance" to reform, and saying that she had "come across it in certain Member States."17
Even though the relative amount involved in the computerisation project is small, there seems little enthusiasm on the part of the Member States, notwithstanding the supportive resolution of Council, for the necessary material support.
By the same token, figures provided in this report have already indicated that all Member States (bar one) have substantially cut the size of national customs services.
Though ostensibly a response to the introduction of the Single Market and the abolition of internal frontiers, this action is generally perceived to have more to do with budgetary cuts and meeting Maastricht convergence criteria than with any genuine reduction in the needs of customs services.18In a similar vein, funding for equipment of the customs services is a contentious issue with several Member States' customs services expressing disappointment at the unwillingness of national authorities to meet relatively moderate requests for equipment.19
Cooperation between Member States
The previous section outlined the extent to which the existing systems for cooperation at both the investigative and judicial levels are slow, formalistic and inefficient.
In this context, action by individual Member States to improve cooperation would be both possible and fruitful.
However, notwithstanding the clear mutual interest of the Member States in achieving better practical cooperation, there is as yet relatively little concrete action to demonstrate the will to translate this interest into practice.
Relations between individual Member States' services and UCLAF are illustrative of this phenomenon.
Though some Member States are working to achieve more effective cooperation and to place relations with UCLAF on a formal footing20, others Member States have not yet acted.
As so often, it is probable that the problem lies not so much in an antipathy towards the necessary measures (though questions of sovereignty frequently arise in this context), but in a general bureaucratic inertia when confronted with problems which are not at the top of the public agenda.
Inertia of this kind was cited to the Committee by one witness in particularly vehement terms.
Mr Sven Buhrkall of Dansk Speditørforening "Danish Freight Forwarders Association" was highly critical.
In complaining of the failure of customs services to return copy 5 of the Tl form to the office of departure, he observed:
"Furthermore, it seems to us that the Danish Minister of Taxation is ignoring this problem.
There is a lack of will to develop cooperation between the customs authorities themselves and between customs authorities and the forwarding industry.
Quite vital items of information concerning fraud, knowledge of false stamps, have never been passed on to Dansk Speditørforening."21
Conclusions
It is clear that Member State governments are failing adequately to address the problems of the transit system, in spite of the fact that in practice, the bulk of the losses occasioned by transit fraud are suffered by the Member States rather than by the European Community budget.
This lack of awareness of the issues and the inertia it engenders, are transmitted to the decision-making instances of the European Union at Council level.
However, they are also transmitted downwards to the customs services themselves.
Member States (3) - Customs Services
Customs Under Attack
The Committee of Inquiry has at times been shocked by the virulence with which traders have been prepared to criticise customs authorities.
Three examples will give a taste of the anger felt in the freight forwarding industry about the role of customs:
- "In our opinion, the administration is slipshod, arbitrary and qualitatively very poor. ...
One might well form the impression that it is too easy for the customs services to offload their
- "Customs is only collecting money.
What can we do about it?"24
Besides the general resistance of customs services to cooperating with the forwarding industry, the anger of the trade is directed at the customs services for two essential reasons: first, its inefficiency; and second, its failure to investigate irregularities and suspected fraud.
Inefficiency
Beyond the overall general bureaucratic inefficiency of which the trade often accuses customs services (the fact which can be attributed as much to the system itself as to the services' management of it), the main reproach levelled at customs services is that they do not comply with legislation and rules connected with the transit procedure.
Article 356 of the Customs Code implementing provisions23, requires that customs services return the copy 5 of the Tl document "without delay".
Similarly, there are clear rules on the deadlines for inquiry procedures and specific requirements on Member States in terms of the investigation of the suspected irregularities of fraud.
It is incontrovertibly clear that these provisions are not in the main respected by customs services (at times in fact it would be futile to do so26).
The effect in practice is to add considerable financial burdens to transit principles as a result of their guarantees being outstanding for often great periods of time, not to mention the burden constituted by such guarantees being called upon.
Failure to investigate
The customs services are reproached for not undertaking a sufficient number of serious investigations, and in particular for reinterpreting the transit system as an insurance scheme for the collection of public revenue, rather than a facilitation system for the transport and circulation of goods within the EU.
In addition, the customs services are often very slow in starting their investigations.
The accusation that customs services do not comply with legal requirements is a serious one and evokes specific legal responsibilities as well as political responsibilities.
It is true however, at the same time, that the rules and procedures with which Member States customs services do not comply are hopelessly unrealistic in the light of the system which exists.
Customs services, as has been reported earlier in this document, feel that a rigorous application of the letter of the regulations would be counterproductive in terms of customs resources.
There is some weight in this argument, but only insofar as it depends on the ineffectiveness of fellow customs services. Thus do vicious circles begin.
Conclusion
Customs services are not managing the transit system effectively.
Arguably, the customs services of particular Member States are more at fault than those of others.
particular worry is the tendency of Member States to view the transit system as insurance for public revenue.
This attitude ignores the fundamental duty a customs service to provide a fair and regulated environment for trade and to prevent and contain fraud within that environment.
In some senses, customs services were unlucky in the timing of the crisis in the transit system.
The worst problems arose in transit as a result of the introduction of the Single Market, while at the same time, most customs services saw substantial redundancies and a consequent demotivation of staff.
To quote Freight Forward Europe:
"We feel that customs officials have a hard time.
As we have said, they have declining budgets, their profile in ûie total structure of public service and their image is worsening and their work is made very difficult.
For example, I remember one customs official who told me that after the GATT round, ... boxes of fish came from Africa from particular countries which could contain 85% of fish from that region and 15% of fish from another region.
The customs official said, How can you expect us to control all that?"28
There is therefore a sense in which customs services are also victims of circumstance as well as being responsible in large part for poor management of transit.
A shared responsibility
It is important, amongst other reasons for the sake of balance, to remember that the trade too bears a certain responsibility in the problems of the transit system.
In the words of Commissioner Gradin:
"We have also been given many examples of how naive some of the private sector players can be.
I am surprised that big shipping agents have agreed to carry obviously dubious goods."29
Freight forwarders have demonstrably not been immune to the charge that they have taken business at times without adequate investigation of their clients.
The Portuguese case illustrated in Section 6 is a case in point.
A company accepted business on the basis of flimsy documentation, presumably because the returns on offer were good.
In the event, the company did not enjoy the benefit of the good returns on offer, as the guarantee claims which arose exceeded the revenue made massively.30
This said, the indications are that most major and serious freight forwarders are now much more careful about the clients on whose behalf they assume guarantee liabilities, particularly in the field of sensitive goods.
The case is clearly one of "once bitten, twice shy":
"While we accept that we should have been more careful and were, probably to some extent incompetent, we are not criminals, have not gained significantly from this traffic and do not deserve to be eliminated as a result"31
Key Points
Given the overlapping problems described in this report, it is impossible to attribute sole responsibility for the development of the present crisis to any one person or even institution.
Sins have generally been of omission rather than commission, insofar as a variety of institutions and organisations share the responsibility for failing to respond to political and economic developments which foreseeably would lead to an increase in fraud.
The defining moment at which this failure occurred was upon the introduction of the Single Market, with its removal of internal frontiers.
It was evident, in part thanks to repeated warnings from the trade, that an already creaking transit system would be exposed to large-scale fraud at this point, yet the responsible technical and political authorities took no action to avert the crisis.
No attempt was made to reform the system itself and no attempt was made to provide any effective instrument of control or enforcement.
Customs services remained structured on a national basis and unprepared for the Single Market, no serious effort was made to enhance cooperation or coordination between them and, at the same time, their effectiveness was compromised by large-scale personnel cuts.
Overall such omissions represent almost wilful negligence and a failure to protect the legitimate interests of those using the transit system, the taxpayer at large and the economy as a whole.
Even after the crisis had developed, the degree of official complacency on the issue, particularly in the Member States, and the degree to which customs services have been prepared to let the situation ride were startling.
Responsibility for the state of affairs is thus shared between all the legislators and managers of the system.
First of ail, the involvement of representatives of the Commission and the Member States in the work of the Transit Committee has failed to bear fruit, since that Committee has played only a marginal role in the moves to prevent the crisis affecting the transit system.
It must now be asked whether such a body, which has failed to carry out its taskproperly, should be retained in its current form.
The technocrats of the Commission were slow to react to a looming crisis or even to bring it to the attention of the politicians.
The members of Council failed to meet the challenge of the Single Market project, leaving it half-complete and their citizens exposed to the resultant widespread fraud and the wider criminality financed by it.
The officials of the customs services, confronted with the resultant difficulties, were prepared to take the easy option and in effect charge the bill for their own inability to manage the unmanageable to the trade, or, in an effort to ease the pressure on traders, to the taxpayer.
Mr Douglas Tweddle of World Customs Organisation: Evidence of 4.9.96 COM(95)108 - 19.3.95
