THE BASIC QUESTIONS
Introduction
The preceding sections of this report have made it clear that the transit system is defective in many respects.
The bulk of the remainder of this report will deal with the possible measures to be taken to improve matters.
Caution should however be exercised.
Reform has to be undertaken on the basis of a clear understanding of exactly what needs to be reformed, with what purpose in mind and what the "collateral" effects of such reform might be.
The Underlying Issues
It is also important to understand the deeper reasons for which the transit system is currently failing to function effectively.
The previous three sections of this document in particular hinted that the problems of transit are related to political realities which may prove less susceptible to reform than the rules and procedures of the transit regime.
Insofar as these realities have a direct effect on the functioning of the transit regime and add to its weaknesses, the Committee of Inquiry has a clear duty to point this fact out, even where much wider political positions may be affected.
The Committee, in accordance with its brief, will follow the logic of the transit system and it should be understood that any comments with political resonances are made only in that connection, without any more general motive.
The Costs of "Non-Transit"
Is transit necessary?
At the beginning of its work, the Committee was interested to ask the simple question: "do we need transit?"
Given the logic that the Single Market is supposed to represent a single customs area within which goods and persons move freely, the idea that it should have a simple customs frontier at which all customs duties, excise and VAT would be gathered on incoming goods held a strong superficial attraction.
In many respects it still does.
The idea however flounders almost immediately when confronted with the realities of commercial life within the European Union.
Diverse taxation regimes
At present, the European Union is composed of fifteen Member States, all of which set their own rates of excise and VAT.
Though the rates tend increasingly to resemble each other through the workings of market forces, the differences between them are still significant from the point of view of the trader, who will justifiably object, say, to paying Finnish excise on Russian vodka imported into Spain simply because the route taken by his lorry traverses Finland.
It is not only in the interests of the trader that the transit system allows him to pay his taxes in Spain, the Spanish authorities too might object to the Finnish authorities gathering as national revenue amounts which would otherwise be due to them.
Pushing the argument even further, the Finns would also be concerned in our hypothetical case about the competitive disadvantage its higher excise rates might have for Finnish ports, transport companies, customs agents, etc.
Without transit, the only answer to all these problems would be a complex system of reimbursement of taxes and excises between Member States.
Even the most sophisticated system of this sort would constitute a bureaucratic nightmare of proportions which would surpass even the exaggerated reputation of the European Union.
As long as (i) EU countries have different tax/excise rates and (ii) collect (even harmonised) taxes on a national basis, transit will remain necessary.
Business costs
If transit did not exist as a concept in one form or another, the additional costs for business would be enormous.
In the first place, importers throughout the Union would be required to maintain agents at the external borders of the Union.
In the second place, "up-front" payment of duties, taxes and excise would impose far higher working capital requirements on companies, pending on-sale of goods or re-export from the Union.
As far as "pure" transit is concerned, i.e. goods entering and re-exiting the Community, payment and reimbursement of customs charges would be a further unacceptable burden and indeed be in contravention of the Community's obligations under GATT (Art. 5).1
Practical considerations
To paraphrase the words of Mrs Nepperus quoted in Section 7, stopping every vehicle would result in "queues back half way back across the Atlantic"2.
There is no question that the imposition of payment of customs charges at the external borders would result in congestion on an unimaginable scale.
The level of traffic across the German-Polish border for example, already results, according to those in the business,3in tailbacks of 68 kilometres every day, the consequences of systematically stopping every vehicle do not bear thinking about.
Trade opportunities
Without doubt, the problems arising from the absence of a transit system of some sort would constitute a severe impediment to trade, both through Europe's sea ports and across the continent's land borders.
In a European Union committed both to free trade and to the development of the economies of Central and Eastern Europe, such constriction would be highly regrettable.
The economic activities of EU traders would also be seriously affected, with all the economic and social consequences this would bring.
Fraud
The rather apocalyptic scenario described would also provide a further incentive for fraud.
Though it seems paradoxical to say so in this report, the absence of a transit system would undoubtedly bring about a boom in smuggling, aimed at avoiding not only taxes, duties and excise, but also the costs and inconvenience associated with any import.
Conclusion
Throughout the evidence taken by the Committee, one of the rare points of utter concord between the public authorities responsible for adrninistering the transit system and its users has been that the system is economically vital.
A cross-section of views will illustrate this point:
Commissioner Monti:
"The transit system ... responds to a real economic need in terms of simplifying transport and speeding up trade. ... [it] enables a large number of major European economic sectors to remain competitive on the world market and not to relocate a greater part of their activity outside the Union.4
French Customs:
"France is very committed to transit systems.
It needs them, economically and commercially.
It cannot envisage the disappearance of transit systems."3
"You asked if we could manage without the transit procedure. ... we should be back in the Middle Ages.
Then it would be out with your wallet and down with your cash at the frontier and cash payments at the other end."6
Though nobody was ever able to provide a clear assessment of the monetary value of the transit system to trade, there was widespread agreement that it is economically vital, or at least that the disadvantages of its abolition would be too disastrous to contemplate.
In short, business, the Member States and their citizens need transit arrangements to facilitate trade.
Introduction
If a transit system is necessary, what are the obstacles to it working efficiently?
The design of the system must take into account the context in which it must operate.
Indeed, is the creation of a workable transit system doomed to failure from the outset?.
One Market: Fifteen Customs
The Single Market, and the existence of the Community Customs Code, imply that the European Union represents a single customs area.
The concept of a single customs area implies not only that goods move freely across the internal frontier, but also that the authorities control the customs area in a uniform, coherent and effective manner.
One might go further and suggest that a single market logically implies that tax and excise rates should be harmonised.
However, this point is not one which figures among the specific concerns of the Committee of Inquiry, as distortions created within the Community predominantly affect intra-Community tax-paid trade, whereas transit is a system for the management of duty/tax-free third country goods crossing Community territory.
The prime interest in tax differentials from the point of view of transit is the extent to which they motivate transit fraud (though as this report has pointed out, the main incentive lies in the duty-free/duty-paid differential) and the fact that they provide one reason for transit to exist (sub-section 10.2 above).
On the other hand, the issue of the coherence of action by customs lies at the heart of the Committee's concerns.
Many of the difficulties affecting transit - administrative delays, errors, slow inquiries, inefficient cooperation, poor intelligence, formalistic legal cooperation, to name but a few - are a direct consequence of the fact that fifteen different jurisdictions are present in the territory of one customs area.
This fragmentation of customs jurisdiction represents a logical contradiction which cannot be ignored.
It amounts to a massive defect in the Single Market, with consequences which are apparent for all to see.
The weight of history is doing Europe a huge disservice in this respect.
Consider a putative "founding father" who proposed a constitution for his new Union in which goods and persons moved freely around without any check or impediment, but the authorities had to stop at internal borders, his career as a founding father would be short-lived.
In the European Union however, this is exactly the situation.
The Single Market works effectively for crime and criminals, but barely works at all for law enforcement.
Its introduction in 1993 was a huge boon for trade and economic activity of all sorts, including the fraudulent variety, but left the authorities with one arm tied behind their backs.
The losers include national and Community budgets (hence taxpayers), business at large, the economy in general and the law enforcement agencies themselves, who have been given an impossible job to do.
No serious reform of the transit system can be envisaged unless action is taken to tackle this fundamental issue.
The solutions tried so far have, as this report has shown, not worked.
Tax/excise rates
Another controversial issue to be considered is the extent to which the European Union and its Member States bring fraud on their own heads by providing large incentives, especially in the form of high excise rates.
This issue is a hugely contentious one, with a political balance to be struck between the purpose for which high excise rates are levied (revenue, dissuasive effects, health grounds, price support, etc.) and the degree to which those rates are so high as to make evasion too attractive.
This balance obviously depends on a range of factors, including the ability of the authorities to prevent or repress evasion.
In today's transit systems, and indeed in the customs field generally, this ability is severely compromised - putting this delicate equilibrium at risk.
It is not for the Committee of Inquiry to take a view on the level at which duties and excise should be set, but merely to point to the relationship between high-excise goods and fraud and the difficulty in achieving the correct balance.
As an extreme instance of this problem, Mr Douglas Tweddle of the World Customs Organisation cited the example of lesser developed countries with very high customs tariffs which "might lose up to 50% of their customs revenue because of fraud, which, putting it in context, is perhaps 40% of the government's revenue."7He did not suggest that this was the situation in any EU country, but the point is well made.
Key questions
Is there a tolerable level of fraud?
Views put by some official witnesses before the Committee suggest that they believe that transit fraud remains at an "acceptable" level, or certainly that an acceptable level exists.
Representatives of both the German and the Austrian customs authorities have given this impression, in stressing the relatively tiny percentage of transit operations which are fraudulent.
To quote the latter (Dr Fuchs) :
"The percentage of fraud cases is small, however.
It is so tiny that it does not per se merit any reaction at all.
But the damage caused is so great that something needs to be done to reduce that tiny percentage somewhat.
But, here too, our motto is "don't flog a hundred because two are guilty"8
The logic implicit in this approach was described to the Committee by another witness, Mr Schmidt of BDF:
"I believe a kind of shoplifting syndrome has begun to manifest itself.
If I run a supermarket in a rough area, I simply assume that 5 to 10% of my goods will be stolen.
I add that to my retail prices and can rest assured that the supermarket will still pay its way.
This could be a similar political calculation."9
It has consistently maintained that the transit system should be designed in such a way as to be fraud-proof, with adequate countermeasures in place to "mop up" fraud which nevertheless finds its way into the system.
To rely instead on the guarantee mechanism to "cover" a low structural incidence of fraud cases is firstly to take a very narrow view of the role of customs (i.e. revenue collection only), and secondly to ignore the reality that a relatively small number of cases can nevertheless produce a financial loss of crisis proportions.
Would the effects of reform of the transit system be wholly good?
A "devil's advocate" question perhaps, but one which highlights important issues.
If, as this report contends, a displacement effect exists between a tighter transit system and contraband in general, then an effective reform of transit will result in more contraband.
The revenue loss involved in such contraband does not even have the merit of being covered by a principal's guarantee.
Though therefore the principal will be happy, the taxpayer at large will lose revenue, without the overall level of fraud having been reduced.
Thus the reformer of the transit system will ultimately have done a disservice to both Community and Member States.
This provocative question is also a useful one because it stresses the importance of looking at the whole picture before proposing reforms.
As Mr Wolstenholme of FreightForward Europe suggested tongue-in-cheek, the problems of transit could be solved with "border points everywhere" and if traders had "to pay duties at every border".10As the witness clearly wished to imply, this policy would regard the dismantlement of the Single Market as an acceptable side-effect of dealing successfully with fraud.
The cure must not be worse than the disease itself.
This is a risk run for example by the 100% guarantee, which imposes a severe cost on all operators and, while reducing fraud in the transit system, seems merely to have pushed fraud elsewhere.
Whether the fraud outside the system is as great as that within is a question to which nobody can supply an answer, but the growing phenomenon of displacement does exist and must be taken into consideration.
Needless to say, policy choices of this kind will also depend on an assessment of the relative importance of the interests involved.
Key Points
Transit, at least in its theory, is and will remain indispensable to the functioning of the Single Market and thus to the European economy as a whole.
Stopping all vehicles at the external borders would impede trade, impose enormous extra costs on business and create unimaginable levels of congestion, besides, in allprobability, provoking a boom in smuggling and contraband to make today's levels appear negligible.
However, the European Union is caught in a fundamental contradiction of its own making: it has a Single Market in which persons and goods move freely across internal borders while the authorities charged with administering and policing that market remain constrained by those same borders.
In practice, there exists a single market in fraud but not in law enforcement.
This is a logical contradiction with practical effects in the authorities' capacity to address the crisis in transit.
Asfar as the current transit system is concerned, it is consequently insufficient to suggest either that more draconian measures (such as ever-higher guarantee requirements) will deal with the problem, or that the problem is at a numerically "tolerable" level.
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (I) - INTRODUCTION
The Purpose of the Following Sections
The role of the Committee of Inquiry
The Committee of inquiry is not setting itself up as a panel of technical experts.
It is not its role either to design or to manage the Community Transit system, let alone the Common Transit system or the TIR Convention.
The following sections will not therefore deal with the technical detail of specific reform proposals.
February 1997.1Though the Committee has followed this technical process attentively,does not intend to express a view on each minor rule change, technical specification, etc.
The nature of the analysis
A more helpful role for the Committee of Inquiry is a complementary one to the exercise undertaken by the Commission.
The following sections will examine the areas in which reforms could be proposed and attempt to establish the principles for such a reform.
The Committee's ultimate recommendations will therefore not be, for instance, a list of modifications to the transit system implementing regulations, but rather indications as to the direction to be taken.
In working towards these, the following sections will pick up on the key political issues emerging from earlier parts of the report.
While the analysis will deal at times with relatively detailed points, it will not encroach on tasks which are rightly the Commission's responsibility.
Information notes on the numerous workshops, advisory groups, working parties etc. have been circulated to the members of the Committee (e.g. Notice to Members No. 67 on the ad hoc "Transit Reform" working group - PE 220.793)
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (II) - THE BASIC SYSTEM
Introduction
Subject Area
The purpose of this section is to review the reforms which could be introduced within the Community Transit system.
They concern the procedures to be followed in all "normal" transit operations, rather than specific antifraud measures.
The Commission's interim report1treats this category of technical proposals (which are drawn in general from written contributions to the Committee of Inquiry) with a commendable thoroughness with which this report neither could nor wishes to compete.
The following therefore covers the ground from the point of view of the key issues at stake.
Long-term and Short-term Corrective Measures
In this subject area in particular, it is important to distinguish between those measures which are seen as long-term reforms of the system (these being the most interesting to this Committee of Inquiry) and the immediate, "emergency" measures needed to shore up the existing system until longer term solutions can be introduced.
This section will focus on the former category.
The European Commission has very clearly nailed its colours to the mast on the question of computerising the transit system:
"We cannot overemphasise the strategic significance of the transit computerisation project as a means of coping with the current crisis because it both offers the definitive practical solution to some of the problems arising from the processing of declarations and the insecurity of procedures and allows better monitoring of operations and preventive measures and controls."2
The Commission has initiated its transit computerisation project known as NCTS (New Computerised Transit System), in cooperation with the Member States customs services and other directly interested parties3.
The programme is already quite advanced and is supposed to be in place by the end of 1998.
Mention has been made in this report of the general scepticism (even within the Commission itself) as to whether the deadline of 1998 can be met.
What though is the transit computerisation system designed to do?
What is it for?
The NCTS replaces the paper-based documentation with electronic messages containing the information required for the transit procedure, and includes, as its principal advance on the paper system, the sending of prior information to the office of destination thus
allowing more effective controls at the conclusion of an operation and providing a basis for bener risk analysis.
It will lessen the probability of errors being made.
A second effect of computerisation will be to provide a degree of security not present in the current paper and rubber stamp system.
Forgeries of stamps have proved a significant problem, with verification of stamps also being difficult.
Given appropriate security measures within the computerised system, the risk of falsification of transit documents should be substantially lower.
Nevertheless, the perfect system is as yet undiscovered, and the potential outcome of a breach in security of the NCTS could have grave consequences.
It is less clear whether the Commission has considered wider applications of the NCTS, potentially modifying transit itself rather than building on existing procedures.
Some witnesses have expressed a degree of doubt as to whether the utility of the system will be optimised on the basis of the Commission's project.4
One of the key areas where computerisation offers substantial potential advantages is in the collection of statistics.
The inability of either the Commission, the Member States or the customs services to provide any reliable figures on volumes and flows of transit trade or of the fraud perpetrated against it has been the subject of repeated complaints throughout the Committee's existence, especially insofar as it impedes a full understanding of how transit is used and thus the optimum targeting of antifraud efforts.
A computerised system almost automatically makes such data available and yet the Commission seems , in its "basic principles" document, to pay little attention to the question of the collection of statistics.3
From evidence gathered by the Committee, it would appear that the Commission has not thought enough about what the computer system potentially offers in terms of added value.
Implementation of Project
At the level of general principle there is universal support for the computerisation.
However, throughout the Committee's existence, various witnesses have expressed doubt as to whether the computerised system will be ready on time.
There are two particular difficulties.
Some Member States' administrations seem cool about NCTS and do not prioritise it.
More seriously, ministries of finance do not appear universally willing to make funds available.
These reservations cause concern, because, as the Commission points out, it is "dependent upon national administrations putting their systems in place."6
As far as Common transit is concerned, it is also dependent on nonEU states putting their systems in place.
Here it has encountered some resistance from the Swiss authorities which, while supporting computerisation in principle, have "expressed reservations and asked questions."7 They went so far as to hand a written critique of the proposal to the Committee's delegation to Geneva and Berne.8
Wishing to look further into the attitude of the Member States, the Committee included in its questionnaire a question9 on the subject of computerisation.
The Member States were asked whether they believed that NCTS would (i) help reduce fraud (ii) be completed in time and (iii) be adequately funded.
The following table shows the results:
Help reduce fraud?
Be completed In time?
Be adequately funded in your Member State?
Β DK no reply yes yes yes
D GR uncertain yes uncertain yes yes yes yes, but no guarantee yes ε
1 LUX yes no yes yes no
NL uncertain yes yes yes yes uncertain yes yes uncertain
A F SF yes yes yes yes yes yes yes yes yes yes yes yes
S UK CH yes no reply yes no reply no no no yes uncertain no
The results of the questionnaire are disappointingly ambiguous.
Ten of the Member States are either sure that the system will be overdue or are at least uncertain about its readiness on schedule.
One notes that while many are sure the system will not be ready on time, most of the same states are confident that they themselves will be ready.
Given that it is the Member States which will bear the principal responsibüity for implementing the Commission system at a national level, such uncertainty about when it will happen is highly disconcerting.
Such hesitancy is in marked contrast to the commitment of the European Parliament manifested in its provision of EC budgetary funds for computerisation in the 1997 budget.
Potential problems also arise in the compatibility of Member States' existing systems with the new systems.
The Austrian Customs service in particular surprised the Committee with the statement "The current Austrian system is not compatible, that is regrettable.
But we had to implement it rapidly."10The Commission however remains confident that such difficulties can be overcome.11
The comments in the previous paragraph raise the question of whether or not it will be possible for the transit system to be managed on the basis of a partially computerised system.
This scenario is not improbable, given that some Member States are less likely than others to be able to meet deadlines.
Expert opinion suggests that it will be both possible and inevitable, on an interim basis, to manage transit without all European Union countries being "on line".12This
Situation, even if partly reassuring, can hardly be considered satisfactory, as it is likely to give rise to "weak links" in the system, which organised crime will lose no time in exploiting.
The Commission is confident that the NCTS will reduce fraud substantially, it estimates the benefit over a five-year period at around Ecu 5-10 billion.
This estimate is in their view based on moderate assumptions.
On optimistic assumptions the figures could be ten times greater.13
However, the NCTS does not in itself offer direct antifraud functions.
Its antifraud potential lies in helping to elirninate some of the weaknesses in the system and in providing riskanalysis material for the better targeting of controls by Member States.
Above all though, it should ehminate the current delays in the return of the confirmation that a transit transaction has satisfactorily been completed, which permit repeat offences.
By the same token, it will allow non-clearance to be identified immediately.
Many witnesses have said that the computerised system offers no guarantees against fraud, but agree, in line with the Member States' questionnaire replies, that it will substantially restrict some of the easier opportunities for it.
A vehicle could still "disappear" en route (the NCTS offers no help at points between the office of departure and office of destination), but at least the authorities would be able to start looking for it with relative alacrity.
The principal problem not to be addressed by NCTS is that of false declarations, which are at present made more likely by the hefty guarantee requirements on sensitive goods.
The only genuine counter-measure remains physical checks of vehicles.
The only contribution made by NCTS to physical checks - which may, in enlightened customs services, be a considerable one -is to release staff from the manual checking of Tl forms, allowing them to be re-assigned to control activities.
A further area where NCTS, as currently envisaged, makes little contribution is to the control of vehicles between departure and destination.
Controls are the subject of the next section, but it is worth mentioning here that, if the computer network were to be instantly accessible to law-enforcement agencies en route (especially to their mobile units), it could provide useful antifraud information.
It would for example allow police/customs to verify whether a particular vehicle is under a transit regime, whether it is on a reasonable route, whether it is on schedule, etc.
Such information, obtained at short notice, might assist in targeting physical checks.
Benefits for the trade
For the trade this acceleration of procedures presents major advantages.
Not only would operators be alerted to frauds much earlier, but, even in the normal course of events, guarantee obligations would be outstanding for much shorter periods of time (probably days rather than an equivalent number of months) and would considerably sweeten the currently extremely bitter pill constituted by outstanding guarantee liabilities.
In order to maximise the benefits to the trade, it is important that economic operators should have read-only access to the system enabling them to verify the progress and clearance of the operations for which they are responsible.14
Security
The final and obvious point to be made is that the NCTS must be safe.
Though the Commission has given guarantees that the system will be secured by state-of-the-art technology, no system is completely secure.
Fail-safe mechanisms are required to cover the possibility of unauthorised intrusion.
Other technological options
Introduction
In addition to the Commission's flagship computerisation project, the Committee has been contacted by a range of organisations (usually commercial in nature) proposing the application of technological techniques to the transit system.
The two generic technologies for application to the basic transit system of greatest prima facie interest were smart cards and optical cards.
The Committee held one hearing explicitly to examine these possibilities.15
The following cannot and should not be an assessment of these products in themselves.
The Committee is only able to assess the extent to which, on the basis of the information, the products concerned appeared to be of interest for the transit system.
It should be clear that the Committee cannot, even on the basis of its conclusions on the above, envisage making recommendations on whether or not such technology should be adopted.
The fundamental question is whether they have received the attention they deserve from the Commission.
Have Smart Cards and Optical Cards had a fair hearing?
, and in written contributions17 are that smart card and optical card technology could offer substantial potential benefits if applied to the transit systems.
The former in particular could assist significantly with secure documentation with controllable access able to establish an "audit trail" for goods in transit.
They could also help ease en route controls, given the relatively cheap, simple and portable technology required to read and/or modify them.
Furthermore, and this is a crucial practical point, they could be used in combination with the Commission's NCTS project.
In evidence to the Committee, the Commission has stated its willingness to examine all possible options ("We have our eyes shut to nothing"18) and that smart card technology has been studied in detail.
However other indications from the Commission are less open.
In Interim Report on Transit, it argues that a smart card:
"simply replaces paper with card leaving existing procedure (involving documents to be returned) unchanged and providing no preliminary information/control on the
Operation except when coupled with an information exchange network still complex and competes with computerisation in terms of cost and time without all the advantages in terms of administration/surveillance of the system in real time and of fraud prevention by anticipating traffic"19
When these points were put to the smart card experts heard by the Committee, they were contested with vigour.
One reason for the Commission's coolness towards smart cards appears to be its view that they are presented as an alternative rather than a complement to computerisation.
It would appear from the Committee's understanding of the possibilities offered by this technology that it deserves open-rriinded attention alongside NCTS.
Optical card technology is laser-based, enables large quantities of data to be stored and is characterized by a high degree of durability and security against unauthorized access and attempts to manipulate data.
Optical cards can easily be linked up to any computer and may be combined with other types of card.
They can be used most effectively in combination with NCTS and would probably solve most of the security problems associated with NCTS.
Greater differentiation between sensitive products and other goods
The Committee's attention has been focused throughout on fraud involving sensitive goods.
According to law enforcement agencies, practically all transit fraud has involved such goods.
In the circumstances, it would seem appropriate to concentrate antifraud measures on such products rather than taking a broad brush approach which would have the effect of imposing extra costs where they are not justified.
Many proposals have been submitted to the Committee which rely on the broad concept of extra measures applying only to sensitive goods.
These might be considered to constitute a "T1 + " system.
There is certainly some merit in focusing often burdensome additional procedures and costly safeguards on the products most likely to be involved in fraud.
Dangers in such differentiation
This approach is not without its downside.
Just as a displacement effect has occurred between transit fraud and conventional smuggling, so might a tighter system for sensitive goods simply encourage misdeclaration in order to avoid a presumably higher level of control.
The choice would appear, therefore, to be between a targeted system which can be avoided or a comprehensive system which imposes substantial extra costs and inconvenience across the board.
This underlying issue should be borne in mind whenever considering potential reforms of the transit system.
The main area in which this distinction between sensitive products and ordinary goods is already made is in the principal's guarantee for a transit transaction.
This matter has been discussed at length in this report, and it has emerged that the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods has provoked exactly the kind of displacement into misdeclaration described above.
In this context, reforms to the guarantee system must be treated within the context of the system as a whole and on the basis of a clear appreciation of what one wishes to achieve with the guarantee.
What is the guarantee for?
The guarantee is viewed in the Committee's experience by the public authorities as an antifraud instrument.
This report has already quoted the view that the higher the guarantee the less the fraud.
On the other hand, the trade representatives who have addressed the Committee take the view that the guarantee imposes extra financial burdens on them for little extra benefit in terms of combatting fraud.
In the light of the Committee's view that higher guarantees in the transit system have resulted in higher smuggling levels outside it, it would tend to share the view of the latter.
Nonetheless, there has been clear evidence to the Committee that the introduction of the 100% guarantee has led to a substantial fall in the amount of crime in transit.
However, in view of the side effects both for the trade and for smugglers, the measure has to be considered "overkill" in antifraud terms to quote one contributor20.
If this is true, as most Member States implicitly acknowledge, what purpose does the guarantee serve?
In practice, it is clear that the reliance on the guarantee mechanism which now characterises the transit system has transformed Community transit from a facilitating system for the movement of goods into an insurance system for public revenue when the facilitation goes wrong.
This development does not represent a true reflection of the purpose of the transit system and has tended to make freight forwarders pay the bill for its administrative defects.
Future changes to the guarantee system should be examined in this light.
The 100% Guarantee - A Temporary Measure?
When the Commission introduced its ban on the use of the comprehensive guarantee, the measure was intended to be a temporary one.
It has already been extended, and some Member States wish to see the system made permanent.
This issue raises the question of fairness.
Is it right to impose on all business, legitimate or otherwise, substantial costs for their regular business, and large bills when a fraudster commits a transit fraud, when the system does not provide them with reasonable assurance of security?
When the computerised transit system is introduced, some of the sting should be taken out of the 100% guarantee for principals, because guarantee liabilities will normally last for only a few days.
In the case of regular business therefore, costs will be reduced substantially.
In the case however of fraud, in which the freight forwarder is not involved, the problem will remain as severe as ever.
After computerisation, the antifraud credentials of the 100% guarantee relative to its negative impact will improve.
In the meantime though, freight forwarders continue to contemplate a period of possibly several years before the advantages of computerisation become available to them.
Spread of Responsibility
In equity, it does not seem right that freight forwarders, who often do little more than prepare the documentation for shipments they do not even see should always be exclusively responsible for the total tax and duties payable on a missing lorry load.
This is even more clearly the case when the customs services have been less than effective in applying the basic provisions of the Community transit system.
At the same time, freight forwarders are paid for the service they provide, and have the option of refusing to stand as principals in a transit operation.
One contributor to the Committee, not operating as a principal for transit operations and presumably therefore without a direct financial interest, put very cogently the basic principle that the guarantee should respect:
"It is thought to be a good principle that the direct financial burden, and potential liability, should go with the responsibility for the care and control of the goods during transit."21
This raises the general idea of spreading potential liability further.
Guarantors could reasonably include transporters, importers and "owners"22(i.e those having 'beneficial control' of the goods).
Certainly in cases where importers or producers have acted as their own principals, the Committee has noted that security on vehicles appears much greater and instances of fraud fewer.
To this end, it should be possible to increase the information, particularly as to owners, consignors and consignees, to be included on the Tl document.
Refinement of Guarantee Mechanism
One of the key objections to the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods, and even for the 100% comprehensive guarantee on a week's turnover for other goods, has been its universality.
All traders are required to provide the guarantee regardless of their size, respectability or proven track record.
Indeed, most frauds involve, at some point, relatively new or unknown players.
In cases where long-standing business relationships have been established between trusted partners or in cases where the security guarantees offered by transporters are good, it seems unreasonable to require the same degree of security as in the case of entirely unknown operators.
Furthermore, the customs services have made the argument in the past that guarantees are not required for rail or air transit because the companies involved (often nationalised) are of sufficient size and standing not to need formal guarantees to satisfy the customs service that
A very similar case could be made for the largest companies in the freight forwarding and transport business, which have become substantial multinational companies in their own right.
It would not be unreasonable to apply the same criteria to such companies.
The question is whether this will also be the case in future.
It is possible to envisage a system whereby the guarantee requirements on freight forwarders (and other players to whom the obligation might be spread), might be tailored to meet their circumstances more closely.
For example, an established, forwarding company working only with established transport companies which had encountered no transit fraud in its business for a lengthy period could perhaps reasonably expect not to have to provide a 100% guarantee for every load forwarded.
By the same token, a new operator with no track record or working on an occasional basis with a variety of transporters might pay a higher guarantee.
Other criteria could also be taken into account: the existence of memoranda of understanding between a company and the customs service on the provision of information, the availability of advanced security devices on the vehicles used, etc.
In the TIR system, participating transporters are required to meet technical specifications for their vehicles in order to be issued with a TIR carnet.
On the same principle, specifications for vehicles in the Community transit system could give advantages in terms of the level of the guarantee required.
A further step in the direction of tailoring the system more closely to the standing and security offered by traders would be to limit access to the system altogether, only permitting approved traders to handle sensitive goods.
The drawback of such a change would be that it would limit access for new players and would open the system to challenge under competition rules.
Nevertheless, given that the transit system is an important concession to the trade, it might not be considered unreasonable to award it only to trustworthy operators.
In the context of refining the guarantee mechanism however, it will be necessary for the authorities to establish clear criteria according to which the level of a guarantee is set.
This will require vastly improved analytical information to be available to customs services, which should be integral part of the new computerised system.
The same argument applies to the new rules covering the comprehensive guarantee which provide for lower rates for bona fide operators.
In both cases, it must be emphasised that the criteria in question must be both established and applied at a European level.
In the context of transit, national lists of bonafide operators and their guarantee requirements are at best useless and at worst a serious distortion of fair competition.
Limits on Application of Guarantee
Another frequent and justified complaint heard by the Committee has been that the customs service make calls on the guarantee very late and in cases where customs services in other Member States have failed to respect the legal provisions of the system.
The proposal has therefore been made that the customs services' opportunity to call on the guarantee should be restricted in some way.
Suggestions that this restriction be based on the "negligence" of customs present probable legal difficulties.
A simpler and "cleaner" solution would be to apply time limits.
of an inquiry procedure, let alone the official deadlines for the return of copy 5s of the Tl form, a new tighter deadline, bearing a closer relationship to the time limits customs is supposed to respect would not only provide greater certainty for operators, but also give incentives to the customs services to respect the legislation.
Extension of the Guarantee System
Introduction
At present, the transit guarantee is only required for road traffic.
However, there is substantial evidence, not least from the customs services themselves, that tougher guarantee requirements have led to a displacement of fraudulent traffic into other means of transport.
In the cigarette market, maritime transport has been specifically mentioned.
In the case of rail transport, customs services acknowledge that little is known about the level of fraud taking place.
In its questionnaire to Member States, the Committee addressed this issue specifically23, asking whether Member States would be in favour of extending the transit guarantee system to rail, sea, inland waterway and air transport.
The results are shown in the following table:
Rail transport?
Aft- transport?
Maritime transport?
Inland waterway transport?
UK CH no no no no reply no no* no no* no no no no yes no no no no yes no no reply no uncertain* n/a no* no no no no reply yes no reply n/a no no yes no no reply yes no* no no* no yes and no no reply yes yes yes yes no reply no yes** no no reply no no* n/a no* no** no no yes yes yes and no** no no
*: As inferred from a general answer. **: The answer is no for state owned railways, for privately-owned railway companies the answer is yes.
There is some merit in the suggestion that extending the guarantee system to other forms of transport would help combat transit fraud insofar as it affects other transport modes.
It would represent a further tightening of the transit system and could be envisaged on the same basis as the guarantee system in road transport (i.e on the basis of the comments made above).
In any event, to ensure that fraud is combated effectively, extension of the comprehensive guarantee to maritime transport should be insisted upon.
However, in the light of the comments in the report about displacement of crime into other types of fraud, it is not possible to be very confident that an extension of the guarantee system would ultimately be effective in fighting fraud in general.
Short-term Measures
Introduction
Since the emergence of the crisis in the transit system, the Commission has taken a number of emergency measures to prevent its immediate collapse.
As has been shown, these focus above all on the guarantee system, because they are easy to introduce and enforce.
Pending the arrival of the computerised transit system, however, a number of other relatively short-term procedural measures have been mooted.
These are dealt with in detail by the Commission in its interim report, but do not figure large in this document because they do not represent long-term solutions to the problems of transit.
Such proposals concern for example the introduction of compulsory routes (on the basis of a freight forwarder's declaration) improved stamps, a reduction in the number of transit offices, improved seals on vehicles, external identification of vehicles under transit, alternative proof of clearance, the use of fax and e-mail, shorter deadlines more information on Tl forms and so on.
As these are in general short-term management measures, the Committee does not need to examine them in depth.
Ultimately, they cannot be anything more than interim solutions to the failings of the transit system.
Key Points
Computerisation of the transit system is a vital and overdue reform which will contribute significantly to eliminating the endemic delays and errors which provide such scope for the fraudster.
Even on the Commission's most modest estimates of what computerisation will save in terms of revenue, the ratio of the relatively small investment required to the financial advantage to Member States is so favourable as to make the hesitancy of the Member States in providing the necessary budgetary funding incomprehensible.
Notwithstanding its support for computerisation however, the Committee is not convinced that the Commission has done enough, or been able to do enough, to maximise its advantages, for example in facilitating control of vehicles en route between offices of departure and destination, in providing access for traders to monitor their own operations or as a source of improved statistics and information about transit trade.
Moreover, the Committee notes that computerisation is not, in itself, an antifraud measure and alone does not constitute a solution to all the problems of transit.
Other technological options for the management of transit, such as smart cards, have been given short shrift by the Commission, on the assumption that they are in competition with the computerisation project.
Smart cards in particular seem to have interesting potential applications to transit in combination with computerisation.
Some review of the guarantee system appears vital.
Reforms could fall into two main categories: spreading the responsibility for the transit guarantee and refining the guarantee mechanism itself to take account of the degree of security provided for the goods.
In the former area, it would be appropriate for those holding a financial interest in the goods themselves and those physically in control of them to take a share of the responsibility for the suspended taxes, duties and excise.
This would involve extending the liability to the owner and the transporter of the goods.
Secondly, it should be possible for the customs service to recognise the availability of special security measures for goods and/or an established track-record on the part of principals and transporters by way of concessions on the amount of guarantee required.
Though the liability would continue to exist, not all companies would be required necessarily to back it up with an expensive formal guarantee, on the same logic that is applied for example to air and rail transport, namely that the companies involved can be expected to meet their obligations without such backing.
Conversely, transit under other forms of transport should be brought within the guarantee system, on the basis that the distinction made between them is now largely an historical anomaly and that some displacement of fraud into other forms of transport has been observed since the introduction of the 100% guarantee for road transport.
Sea transit is the ripest for inclusion within the guarantee system, though there are indications that rail and air are similarly affected.
Guarantee liabilities would of course be assessed on the same principles as apply to road transport in the sense indicated above.
It is not the Committee's intention to deal at length with short-term corrective measures.
Together with its technical experts, the Commission is reviewing a range of short-term measures.
Insofar as these arefeasible and do not conflict with the principles of reform set out in this report, the Committee would support the Commission in taking whatever short-term steps are necessary to minimise the level of fraud within transit until more permanent reforms can be introduced.
The basic principles of NCTS are laid out in the internal Commission document DGXXI/2128/95 Rev.2.00
"I have no fear about the interchange of information between the various systems" - Mr Walker of the Commission: evidence of 24.9.96 idem.
Hearing of 14.10.96: Witnesses on these subjects comprised representatives of Canon Svenska (optical cards) and Mr Banerjee of Card Dynamics Ltd (smart cards)
In particular, voluminous documentation was provided by Mr Harold Landau on the 3T smart card project.
See especially CLECAT: Contribution no.
Question 30
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (III) - CONTROL
Introduction
Ensuring compliance
The reforms discussed in the previous section aim to tighten up the Community Transit system.
They are not specifically intended to combat fraud, but to reduce the slack and inefficiencies in the system that provide scope for the activities of the fraudster.
In themselves therefore they do not offer any comprehensive assurance of a "clean" system.
To address the problem posed by the professional fraudster, it is necessary to examine the means available to control the operation of and access to the system and to make it as effective as possible, while respecting the needs of Hae users, for whose benefit the system was created.
This section therefore reviews areas where the techniques used to ensure compliance with the transit rules could be improved.
Physical checks
For as long as customs services have existed - and customs is one of the oldest services of the state - physical checks of goods have constituted the basic method by which compliance with customs regulations has been enforced.
In a political system which sets greater store by personal and commercial freedom, not to mention the economic benefits deriving from free trade, physical checks cannot be so intrusive as to constitute a significant obstacle to the movement of goods.
Physical checks remain the only genuine defence the authorities have against misdeclaration of goods.
There exists no other sure method of verifying whether goods in transport, under the transit system or otherwise, are actually what they are declared to be.
A balance has therefore to be struck between the need for control and the needs of trade.
This balance lies in achieving a level of physical checks sufficient to dissuade the potential fraudster from attempting to misdeclare goods, while at the same time permitting the smooth and rapid flow of goods into and around the Union.
It has proved difficult for the Committee of Inquiry to establish what level of checks constitutes this optimum balance.
Most customs services of all Member States have asserted that they seek such a balance, while providing figures pointing to very different policies in terms of the number of checks carried out.1We must assume that individual customs services are not pursuing a deliberate policy of laxness (though this accusation is sometimes levelled at them -at times, in private, by other customs services). Hence an explanation for the divergence has to be sought in two areas: the use of risk analysis and the powers and resources available to customs.
Risk analysis
It has already been pointed out in this report that risk analysis enjoys the broad acceptance of Europe's customs services and the Commission, as a means by which physical checks can effectively be targeted on consignments most liable to irregularity.
Notwithstanding this broad agreement, the practical application of risk analysis and the amount of faith placed in it by customs are obviously not constant.
Risk analysis cannot be considered an optional extra for Europe's customs services.
The volume of trade is such that no other approach can offer a basis for a practical and dissuasive level of control.
Hence a common view of risk analysis is required which will enable European customs services to apply physical controls in a consistent manner, on the basis of agreed criteria and in a fashion which enjoys broad confidence.
The current situation, whereby customs services look askance at each others' methods, operates to the advantage of the fraudster alone.
It is not for the Committee of Inquiry to say what risk analysis should be or how it should be applied; it is not a technical body with the means to make the necessary judgements.
The Committee merely notes that the technique exists and has to be used.
It is up to the Commission, as the centrally placed customs "authority", to act as a broker in establishing a common policy on what risk analysis is and what it can be expected to achieve.
In this context, it is important to reiterate the need to obtain a clearer understanding of transit flows.
As has been indicated on several occasions, the customs services of Europe are currently unable to provide reliable statistics as to the flow of transit trade.
Without better information, risk analysis will continue to be seriously impaired.
The Commission should continue to press to obtain better information of this kind.
The role of the trade in risk analysis has to be examined, as much of the information required to make it effective derives from business organisations.
Fortunately in this context, the trade shares an interest in making risk analysis work properly and therefore has an incentive to cooperate with customs:
"Increasing the use of risk analysis ... requires attention.
The systematic charting of declaration agents, licensees, importers, exporters, etc. is very likely to be an effective means of controlling the parties in international trade.
Examples of the measures we have in mind are: the opening of files on the goods they are trading in, both in physical and financial terms, on their fiscal record, on the usage they make of and their wishes for customs regulations".2
This view points to the need for agreements - often referred to as "memoranda of understanding" - between customs and traders.
The existence of such agreements, especially if they include, as the above suggests, customs access to company files and financial information on a real time basis, would not only improve the quality of the risk analysis undertaken by customs, but also provide a potential trade-off for the company concerned in terms of its guarantee obligations.
Customs powers and resources
Agreed rules on physical checks are not enough.
A consistent and effective approach to the control of goods in transit will only be possible if the customs services of the EU have the legal powers to apply the rules.
All customs authorities are able to stop and check vehicles at border-crossing points, but in a transit regime operating in a Europe without internal frontiers, this vision of customs is outdated.
It is much more important for customs to be able to stop and check vehicles within the territories of the Member States, especially as Single Market legislation forbids the use of "systematic" checks at borders within the EU.
As the table in paragraph 7.2.2.2. showed, the ability of different EU customs services, and indeed the services of partner countries, to carry out this sort of control ranges from the sweeping to the almost non-existent.
The inevitable consequence is to create "soft spots" within Europe where the fraudster is less likely to be apprehended.
It is also necessary to look beyond what is strictly possible from a legal point of view to the management decisions that are made on the deployment of resources.
In this context, the delegation of the Committee which visited the German authorities in Hamm3 was impressed by the policy of the German customs service to deploy mobile control units operating throughout the territory of the Federal Republic.
Material resources also play an important role in determining the ability of customs to carry out an effective number of physical checks.
Staff numbers are a key issue.
As has been demonstrated in the table in paragraph 7.1.1.2., the numbers of customs officers available to nearly all customs services have been in decline for some years.
At the same time, the administrative task of managing the transit system has grown continually.
In these circumstances, the number of officers available to carry out physical checks is in decline, even if computerisation of the transit system holds out some hope that this decline may be reversible in the medium term.
Technology too offers some possibilities.
The French authorities employ container scanners at the port of Le Havre.
These allow the contents of entire containers to be checked without the need physically to open the container or disturb the goods.
(In effect, the scanner is a giant version of the equipment used at airports to check hand-luggage.)
The Dutch authorities have similar equipment on order and other customs services are showing an interest in acquiring scanners.
Such machines are no panacea - they have a limited capacity4and the costs involved are substantial.
Nevertheless, they do offer a useful instrument by which, in combination with risk analysis, physical checks can be made more effectively dissuasive for the smuggler.
Here too consistency is vital.
It is of little use for one major port to be well equipped in terms of its capacity for physical checks when a rival port, not far along the coast, is less so.
Physical Checks - Conclusions
It would be unwise to take a prescriptive approach to customs checks.
Circumstances differ in various parts of the transit "area" and a mechanistic solution to the problem of physical checks - e.g. a 5% rule along the lines of that applied to CAP exports - is insufficiently responsive to the real needs of the transit system and to the different volumes of traffic in different parts of the Union.
Nonetheless, physical checks remain indispensable.
They are the only means of dealing with misdeclaration of goods.
Given resource limitations, and the need not to interfere unduly with the flow of goods, they must be targeted by way of risk analysis.
The key issue is to create a universal approach to physical checks, at external borders, at points of destination and at intermediate points within the customs territory of the Union.
This requires a common approach to risk analysis and the nature of controls, comparable powers and adequate resources.
The analysis in preceding sections shows that these prerequisites do not currently exist.
The potential solutions will be dealt with in the next section.
Restricted Admission to the Transit System
Introduction
Another way of exercising control is to intervene directly to determine who is allowed to use the transit system.
This question is linked to the question of the acceptance of guarantees discussed in the previous section5, but extends beyond it to encompass the general concepts of "black listing" and "white listing".
The concept of black-listing is simply that traders who have been involved in fraud are prevented from making any further use of the transit system.
The concept is one which is available in the TIR Convention, but which is fraught with potential difficulties:
- What level of involvement would justify black-listing? (Active participation, passive complicity, involvement of an employee?)
- What level of proof would be required? (Conviction, customs intelligence, legitimate suspicion?)
- Who would be responsible for preparing and holding the black-list and who would have access to it? (Customs authorities, other law-enforcement agencies, the Commission (UCLAF/DG XXI), traders?)
- What would be the legal status of such a black-list?
How would its fairness be guaranteed?
What protection would be available to traders actually or potentially included?
Notwithstanding the substantial difficulties, the Commission favours some kind of black-listing system allowing the identification of traders for the purposes of their exclusion from the transit system.
In its document "Specification of a strategy for the reform of the transit regimes" submitted to the heads of customs services meeting in the Customs Policy Committee7, the Commission invited them to approve, under the heading "Measures which the Commission wishes to adopt but in respect of which the member states have yet to declare their support" the "creation of a black-list allowing the exclusion of undesirable operators".
It should not be forgotten in this connection that a precedent for black-listing does exist in Community law, in the form of the CAP black-listing directive.
This relies on previous convictions as the basis of black-listing.
The alternative to formal black-listing is to turn the concept on its head and impose entry conditions on those wishing to make use of the transit system.
This approach becomes a control measure insofar as those conditions relate to the assurances which can be obtained as to the security of the goods.
Again, the idea is closely related to the "refined" guarantee system discussed in the previous section.
However, rather than being primarily concerned with guaranteeing public revenue, it deals with the security of the goods.
Nevertheless, In practice, the sort of conditions which would be applied - good track record, established business relationships, availability of special security devices, etc. - would be identical.
It seems likely therefore that some form of system could be constructed combining the merits of an open system with graduated guarantee requirements with the possibility of excluding participation in the transit system altogether.
Moreover, the approach has again to be a Europe-wide measure, administered by the Community, in order not to distort fair competition.
One proposal put to the Committee of Inquiry represents an interesting variation on the white-listing theme.
The private sector inspection company, SGS, has proposed that major companies in the freight forwarding and transport industries could form a limited access "Club" to use the transit system.8The idea relies on a group of companies, working only with each other, setting up a parallel transit monitoring system.
This would not replace the official system, but would provide the Club's members with greater assurances about those with whom they work with and concerning the successful completion of transit operations.
At the same time, insurers would be able to cover the liabilities of members of the Club with greater confidence and legitimate importers/exporters could entrust their goods to carriers with fewer concerns (illicit traders would be forced outside the system).
An interface would also be established with customs allowing official use to be made of the information collected by the Club.
There is a degree of exclusivity about the Club idea which might be construed as anti-competitive.
However, it is not a cartel insofar as membership would be open to all those
able to meet the entry conditions. From the point of view of the Committee of Inquiry it is interesting as a variant on the white-listing theme and as an example of the sort of private-sector, self-help approaches to the problems of transit discussed below.
Monitoring of Transit - New technology'
Introduction
Throughout its existence, the Committee of Inquiry has been interested in exploring the possibilities offered by technology in making the transit system less prone to fraud and in assisting the detection of fraud.
To this end, for example, it dedicated two of its sixteen formal hearings to the examination of possible technological applications to transit.9
It did not have to seek out such technologies.
In response to its call for evidence, numerous persons and organisations came forward with proposals and suggestions for how various techniques might prove useful in securing the transit system.
Two of the ideas the Committee looked at, smart cards and optical cards, applied to the basic Community transit system and were covered in the previous section.
The following is a review of those technologies which potentially add control possibilities to the basic system in operation.
As was the case with technologies covered elsewhere in this report, the Committee cannot speak authoritatively on the technological merits of the various proposals.
Once again therefore there will be no specific recommendations on applications.
However, it is possible to observe what possibilities exist and the role they might have to play in making transit manageable.
The idea of monitoring the movement of road vehicles through a satellite monitoring system has smacked to some official sources of "James Bond fantasies"10.
Consequently, the public sector has shown relatively little interest in pursuing the idea.
The Commission, in its review of options for reform in the interim report, is not dismissive in an outright fashion, but nor is it enthusiastic:
"a private system could be interesting for honest operators, but a generalised system would be expensive for either a private or a public service provider"11
Public sector scepticism has however to be put in context.
Numerous private sector witnesses have informed the Committee that they (or their members) already make use of satellite tracking technology for their vehicles, particularly when carrying sensitive goods.
This was the case, for example, of Danish freight forwarders.12These witnesses have made it clear that where the potential guarantee liability is high - as in the case of cigarettes - the costs of satellite monitoring are justified from a commercial operator's point of view.
In fact, the technology of satellite monitoring is far less esoteric than official witnesses seem to believe.
It is now a relatively simple matter to track any vehicle to an accuracy of ten metres using satellite-based GPS (Global Positioning System) technology.
The main advantage of a satellite tracking system is its "early warning" function.
It allows the owner of a vehicle to track its journey in real time throughout its journey and thus to identify immediately any deviation from its expected itinerary, unscheduled stops, etc.
The point is often made that, though the system allows a vehicle to be tracked, the same does not necessarily apply to its load.
This is a fair comment, though the Committee has also heard that new technologies are available to tackle this problem, for instance a transmitter in a seal which emits a signal when the integrity of the load is violated.
Such tracking systems, or indeed GSM-based tracking on which the Committee also took evidence,13 clearly have a role to play in the monitoring of high-tax goods.
Marking of goods
The Committee also obtained information about techniques whereby goods can be invisibly marked in order to trace their origins and ownership.14It heard about technology allowing goods of all types to be uniquely identified by way of microscopic DNA-marked tags.
Such tags have already been used to mark oil, with a view to detecting illegal discharges.
Their use could be extended to products in the transit regime.
In liquid form, the tags could be sprayed into a master case of cigarettes, making it possible to trace the origins of any packet wherever it ended up by a simple field test.
Notwithstanding the sophistication of the technique, its utilisation appeared to be sufficiently straightforward to make it a potentially very useful tool for the authorities and the owners of products alike.
Private sector solutions
In the words of one witness, "in seeking a solution to the current problems of the Community Transit System, trade will have to look to their own resources in the first instance".13Several of the control possibilities briefly delineated in this section are already used by the trade on its own initiative, thereby suggesting that it is at least partially in the hands of the private sector to come up with solutions to the defects of transit.
This argument has some validity.
Just as the trade shares responsibility for allowing the system to deteriorate, so it must share the burden of tightening it up, in its own interest as well of that as the market as a whole.
However, the argument cannot be pushed too far.
The responsibility of the trade is much smaller than that of the public authorities.
They created and manage a system for the benefit of the economy and society as a whole and thus owe a "duty of care" to the system's users.
The argument that the private sector has a role to play cannot therefore be used as an alibi for the abdication of responsibilities on the part of the public authorities.
Nevertheless, market mechanisms have at times encouraged operators to seek their own solutions.
Interesting possibilities present themselves both for other private users and for the public authorities.
Thus the issue of how economic operators and customs interact once more arises.
A refinement of the guarantee required is one benefit which might accrue, as suggested in the previous section, but the advantages to a trader could be extended.
These might include, at the lower end of the scale, the more ready acceptance by customs of alternative proof of conclusion of a transit operation where the official system fails, rising to allowing for highly reliable operators to enjoy complete "self-management" within the system.
Key Points
Physical checks are indispensable to the control of the transit system, as they are to all customs control, as they are the only sure method of detecting misdeclared goods.
Asfar as transit is concerned, the more emphasis is put on sensitive goods, the more misdeclaration is likely to be afavoured fraud technique.
Thus physical checks will grow in importance rather than diminish.
Moreover, it is vital that the dissuasive effect and efficiency of physical checks is uniform across the EU, as any weak link in its collective defences will provide fraudsters with access to the entire internal market, thus invalidating the efforts of those places where control is more effective.
At the same time, physical checks cannot be so frequent as to constitute a serious obstacle to trade.
The only answer to this apparent conflict, reflected in the different approaches of various customs services, is for physical checks to be carried out on the basis of a universal system of risk analysis and using comparable levels of human and material resources.
Furthermore, controls should increasingly take place within the customs territory of the EU rather than at borders.
This will require harmonisation of customs services'powers to carry out such checks and the resources to do so.
Another control method is to limit admission to the transit system, particularly in connection with the transport of sensitive goods.
Black-listing and white-listing are the options available, of which the latter appears to present fewer difficulties in legal terms.
White-listing could take the form of reasonable entry conditions to use the transit system, either administered by the public authorities, or on the basis of aprivate-sector "Club " concept, which would rely on market forces rather than regulation for its effect, but which could be recognised by the public authorities by way of reduced guarantee obligations and a degree of self-management.
Any such white-listing must be open to a process of appeal.
The private sector also offers a certain amount of scope for additional security measures which are beyond the means and management resources of public administration.
The economic viability of such measures is demonstrated by initiatives already taken by numerous transport companies to equip their vehicles with satellite monitoring and other security devices.
A correlation is observable between those employing such equipment and those whose guarantee has only infrequently been called upon.
These additional security guarantees should be recognised by public authorities.
Similarly, other security measures which are commercially available, such as microscopic DNA marking, deserve official consideration.
French customs officer: Quoted from record of the meeting of the ad hoc working group on transit (meeting of 58 September 1996)
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (TV) - POLICING THE SYSTEM: CUSTOMS SERVICES
Introduction
It has already been said in this report that the customs services of Europe respond to a logic which has been overtaken by events and no longer offer the kind of service required in the Single Market.
Indeed, the disparateness of EU customs arguably constitutes in itself one of the major imperfections in the Single Market.1The following have been repeated themes throughout this report:
- The material and human resources available to customs services, the powers they possess and their attitudes vary substantially across the Union.
- Customs generally remain structured as services for the control of the frontiers of a nation state, while the Single Market has made this function in many respects secondary.
- The management of the transit system has been shown to be inefficient and often at variance with the legislation governing it.
- Customs' response to the crisis in the transit system has been fragmentary and inadequate.
- Investigative cooperation, though effective on an ad hoc basis, is neither systematic nor reliable.
It would be impossible therefore to envisage a report of this kind without addressing some of the ways in which customs services themselves, rather than just the transit system they administer, can adapt in order to play a more effective role.
Modernising EU customs administrations - areas of reform
Staff and resources
All the customs services of Europe have recently undergone a prolonged period of uncertainty, and in most cases, deep cuts in personnel.
This has not only compromised their ability to do the job required of them, but also the motivation and enthusiasm with which they have undertaken that task.
At the same time, the material resources available to customs vary markedly between one Member State and the next, creating a disparity in their capacity to operate effectively and providing an opening for the fraudster with the result that the customs services do not function in such a way as to protect the fundamental interests of the European Union, which was originally based on a customs union.
Two conclusions arise.
First, Member States must re-examine their policies on the resources made available to customs services.
If customs is to work effectively, they must have the tools to do so.
This affirmation is not the product of an uncritical acceptance of customs' pleas for more funding (all public services want this) but based on patently observable fact.
Second, customs services must be comparably funded and resourced.
To repeat an earlier point, if one port is well-equipped to detect smuggled goods, but a neighbouring port in another Member State is not, not only will illicit activity concentrate on the latter, but access will be gained to the markets of both.
Powers
In an almost precisely analogous way, the powers of customs services have to be both sufficient and compatible.
In the Single Market, the role of national customs in policing internal EU borders is drastically curtailed.
Nevertheless, the legal powers of many customs services are based predominantly on this function.
In policing (as opposed to administering) the transit system, the role of customs within the territories of Member States is as important, if not more important, than their activities at the border.
Yet it is here that weaknesses and disparities are most evident.
Again therefore it is for the Member States and the Commission to look at how the powers of customs services and the administrative and legal powers of customs officials can be better aligned.
Practice and "culture"
Though it is always dangerous to talk in terms of "culture", the Committee of Inquiry has observed an indisputable difference of approach from one customs service to the next.
Such differences are reflected predominantly in the degree of account which is taken of the interests of traders and the economic sectors supporting them in relation to the interests of the public purse.
Whereas some customs services define their job narrowly as the collection and protection of public revenue, others are more conscious of a trade facilitation role.
The Committee of Inquiry has no wish to become embroiled in a fruitless discussion about where exactly the balance should be struck.
It is however obliged to note that a polemic exists on this subject between customs services themselves and that traders are prone to detect differences between their treatment at the hands of different customs authorities.
Real or perceived, these distinctions are important and damaging for the effective management of the transit system.
A key example of this difference is in the policy on physical checks discussed in the previous section.
The response of the Committee of Inquiry has to be similar - it is for the Commission to look at means by which customs services can be induced to take a coherent approach.
The obvious "top-down" approach to the problem would be for the customs services, under the guidance of the Commission, to agree a manual of procedures, based on a common policy towards the respective interests of traders, forwarders, ports and the public at large.
Though this measure enjoys a degree of support, not least within some customs services, and would moreover go some way to eliminating the grounds for the differences in emphasis currently in evidence, it remains rather mechanistic and does not address the root of the problem.
For this reason, a greater emphasis is required on joint training of customs officials from different Member States. Though the Commission has made efforts in this direction," an increased level of joint training remains one of the priority needs of customs.
Besides assisting in establishing a uniform approach to common procedures, such training would also have the beneficial side-effect of creating a "network" of officials known to each other, and presumably as a result more disposed to cooperation in their later careers.
Management of the transit system
Many of the criticisms made of the customs services in connection with the management of the transit system are the direct result of the failings of the system itself In the face of these customs are often as helpless as traders.
To an extent customs are being asked to achieve the impossible, and the inefficiencies of management will ultimately only be resolved by the reform of the system.
At the same time, traders are sometimes asked to accept the unacceptable and to bear the financial consequences for customs' inability to cope with the system.
As a result, it is not surprising that some trade witnesses have put forward proposals aimed either at penalising customs for failing to respect transit legislation, especially deadlines, or at encouraging them to do better.
One of the more radical suggestions made was that the Commission should take Member States to the Court of Justice for their failure to respect Community legislation in respect of the transit system.
Though there is some appeal in this idea, it is strongly mitigated by the probability that such a course of action would do almost nothing to improve matters while creating a kind of ill-feeling between the Commission and the Member States entirely unconducive to constructive action.
At a more practical level, there have been suggestions that customs should only be able to enforce guarantee liabilities in cases where all procedural deadlines are rigorously respected, or in cases where there has been no negligence on the part of customs.
The trade's position on this point is comprehensible.
However, the interests of the taxpayer and the duty of customs services to protect public revenue cannot be ignored, even where, for reasons often beyond their control, customs cannot meet unrealistic deadlines.
It may however be desirable to seek a compromise position on this question, as the system is undoubtedly tipped heavily in favour of customs at the expense of the trader.
In this context, a six-week/six-month solution has been mooted, the former being the deadline for the copy 5 of the Tl form, the latter the deadline for the call on the guarantee.
As far as incentives are concerned, it has been suggested that customs officials should receive personal inducements to operate the system more effectively through performance-related
Customs Cooperation
The first obstacle to more effective cooperation between EU customs services are the barriers in the way of better sharing of intelligence.
In several Member States, as this report has stated, these are legislative in nature and connected with data protection rules.3There is no easy solution to this difficulty.
As long as the sharing of customs intelligence remains a transnational matter, it is unlikely that the Member States concerned will be prepared to modify their rules.
As a result, what is theoretically the EU's prime intelligence sharing mechanism, mutual assistance through the CIS (Customs Information System), is constrained from working as efficiently as it could.
In a Single Market, and in the transit system in particular, the interests of the authorities of one Member State cannot be distinguished from those of another.
The sharing of customs intelligence is therefore not a "favour" to another service, but a form of self-interest.
It is to be hoped that mechanisms can be found whereby valuable information is made available to the customs services in need of it.
Certainly, several official witnesses have bewailed the lack of information of and prior intelligence on illicit movements.
Among the alternative ideas put to the Committee in this connection was that of a RJLO (Regional Intelligence Liaison Office) the role of which would be to "take information in from each of the customs services ... to look at that information, to identify trends, new smuggling methods and to produce reports which would then be available to all the Members of the Western European RILO".4As proposed, this RILO would not be an EU body, though it would include all EU states, the Visegrad countries plus the EFTA countries.
It would undoubtedly provide a useful strategic service, even if it would address neither the data protection difficulties mentioned above nor the more immediate operational intelligence needs of customs services.
Investigative cooperation
The provision of "tactical" intelligence is one form of investigative cooperation.
However, operational cooperation is also important.
This could involve the tracking of vehicles across a series of EU Member States, the interception of vehicles at the request of one Member State in another, parallel enquiries in different countries, coordinated arrests or simply the provision of information on request.
On occasions, EU customs services have managed all these forms of cooperation, allowing some substantial fraudulent operations to be closed down.
However, there currently exists no adequate framework within which customs services can work together.
If, for example, an investigating magistrate in one Member State required investigative information from another Member State, he would have no guarantee that his request would be met, or, if so, when.
None is specific to the customs area, though all would cover fraud against the transit system.
The other thing all three have in common is that they fail, through a lack of political will, to fulfil their antifraud potential.
UCLAF is the only one of the three elements actually operational.
It has received repeated praise in the Committee of Inquiry for its facilitating role in putting investigators in different Member States in touch with each other, and, as its name suggests, coordinating specific investigations.
Nevertheless, UCLAF is forced to operate by proxy.
It has no authority to initiate criminal investigations, order or carry out searches, make arrests, prioritise particular areas of inquiry, etc.
Moreover, it remains a small operation relying heavily on the personal contacts of national officials seconded to it for limited amounts of time.
Consequently, though highly effective in those cases where it is involved, and though possessing highly qualified investigative staff, its ability to operate on the ground is extremely limited.
UCLAF therefore has as yet unexploited potential.
Still a relatively new organisation, it relies for its future development on the respect it is able to command with the customs and police services of the Member States.
At present, the signs are good, in that it is establishing a reputation for serious and effective investigative work, providing an indispensable extra dimension to combatting fraud in Europe.
With this in mind, the EU should soon consider a further enhancement of UCLAF's role and powers.
It must nevertheless be appreciated that any success UCLAF has enjoyed has been largely by default - in the absence of any organisation with the formal powers to fill the investigative gap in the EU's antifraud resources, UCLAF has shown imaginativeness and creativity in creating a role for itself within the institutional limits placed on it.
The fact remains that a true European investigative authority is required and does not yet exist.
Though dealing with police matters, largely unrelated to customs activities, Europol is the nearest thing the EU has to an EU-wide investigative body.
As such, it offers a concept which could potentially overcome the inefficiencies of relying on cooperation between and coordination of 15 different customs services, not to mention other law enforcement agencies which can be involved in investigating transit fraud.
The possible implications are discussed below.
Towards an EU Customs Service
A logical step
All the problems discussed in this section centre on one basic issue: the fragmentation of customs authority into fifteen separate services, each of which has different powers, resources, traditions and culture and the difficulty of achieving effective cooperation between them.
These problems arise from an essential contradiction between the existence of a single customs territory and the absence of one customs authority covering that territory.
The situation is, not to put too fine a point on it, a gift to fraudsters.
The logic of the matter is straightforward: a single customs area a single customs area requires a single customs service, with homogeneous powers, resources allocated according to objective pan-European criteria, the ability and the capacity to carry out investigations throughout the territory for which it is responsible and the internal command structures to distribute information where it is needed and to target its antifraud efforts at a European level.
None of the currently endemic problems surrounding cooperation and the sharing of intelligence would arise in a single service.
The European Parliament has already given its support to the idea of a Single Customs Service in the context of the negotiations on Customs 2000.
The Committee of Inquiry cannot but reach the same conclusion on the basis of the evidence it has received.
To a significant extent, the very existence of this report is a consequence of the EU not possessing a single customs service.
It is hard to imagine how the state of affairs it is designed to address could have arisen in a Europe with one integrated service, although a single service would produce problems of its own which the Committee of Inquiry has inevitably been unable to consider.
To take one example: computerisation, currently the subject of ^terminable political discussion and soul-searching would undoubtedly have been the rule years ago, just as it is now at a national level.
Of course there are problems.
These are both practical and political.
At the practical level, the creation of a single service would cause severe organisational upheaval, require the creation of new infrastructures and give rise to reams of new legislation.
Politically, the obstacles are probably greater.
The idea of an EU customs service is anathema to most governments, on the grounds of the potential loss of sovereignty, and to most customs services.
In the case of the latter, the Committee's questionnaire to customs authorities asked two questions:5
- Would you agree to become part of a unified European customs service? - Would such a unified service help in the fight against fraud?
The responses showed near unanimity in rejecting both ideas.
Two countries however, Spain and Italy, swam against the tide and saw unity as a means of allowing customs to cooperate more effectively and consequently expressed openness to the idea of an EU customs service.
None of these objections can undermine the irrefutable logic of a single customs service.
That logic is based on the notion of subsidiarity itself, i.e. that decisions be taken at the most appropriate level.
It is hard to think of a case in which the concept of subsidiarity has so blatantly been ignored.
If there is one area where a European approach is required, it is this.
Even on a minimalist, "free-trade" reading of what the EU should be, of which the Single Market is the ultimate expression, the failure to provide a service able to police and protect that free trade is almost wilful negligence, which has exposed Europe's traders and taxpayers to fraud on a vast scale.
Intermediate solutions
However, the Committee of Inquiry cannot afford to be utopian.
Though it must draw the logical conclusions which arise from the evidence it has received, it cannot turn a blind eye to the political, practical and adrninistrative realities which condition the way customs services are currently organised.
In short, however theoretically desirable it may be, a single EU customs service will not be created overnight.
Nevertheless, it is possible to begin to address the issues arising on a partial basis.
The means by which this can be achieved are mainly explained in the earlier parts of this section.
First, there is the suggestion made by the Irish in reply to the questionnaire that customs shall act "as if" they were one.
If this were to be taken seriously, it would involve a much higher degree of systematic cooperation, based on more use of exchange schemes, joint training, sharing of information, and so on.
It would also imply that the powers of customs services be brought more closely into line.
Pursuing the idea though, the area where internationalisation is most urgently needed is in the prevention and investigation of fraud.
This implies building on the possibilities offered by UCLAF and/or Europol to set up a true European customs investigation service.
The crucial point about any such service would be that it did not have to depend on the vagaries of cooperation to achieve results.
It would have to be independent, well-equipped and able to operate on its own initiative within the terms of its field of competence: cross-border customs fraud.
Though such a body would be a step in the right direction, as long as it continued to co-exist with 15 national customs services, each in possession of its own powers, hierarchies, adrninistrative and investigative information databases, its operational effectiveness would be compromised.
Conclusion
Based on the Committee's evidence, there can be no dispute with the logical conclusion that the EU needs a single customs service.
The consequences of it not having one include the development of a crisis in the transit system, and the inability of the authorities to deal with this crisis.
If the political difficulties of forming a single service are allowed to continue to prevent it coming into being, it will be at the price of a structural inability on the part of the authorities throughout Europe to police the Single Market and tackle customs fraud.
Given that the political obstacles to a single customs service are so substantial as to make its immediate realisation unlikely, there are a number of interim measures which could
In the investigative field, a joint European customs investigative service, with autonomous resources and able to act on its own initiative would also be useful.
Such measures should not however be confused with the objective of a fully integrated customs service.
It must be emphasized that, in addition to the technical and administrative measures advocated - the implementation of which is undeniably necessary if the management of the transit system is to be improved - the crucial and fundamental problem remains that of the real political will of the Member States of the Council of the European Union, the sole key factor which can lead to the genuine mobilization of resources, institutions, laws and individuals with a view to acknowledging and defending the European Union's economic and financial interests and combating fraud in the sphere of Community transit.
Without that will, any progress towards establishing technical or legal instruments will remain blatantly inadequate, if not doomed to failure.
Key Points
The problems of transit cannot be addressed without thorough consideration the reform of customs itself At the very least, the powers, resources and activities of customs throughout the Single Market must be uniformly effective in dissuading, detecting and investigating fraud.
This implies a common approach to training, the control of traffic, common powers to stop and check vehicles, unrestricted and immediate sharing of information and tactical intelligence, joint investigations, the deployment of resources across the entire EU on the basis of operational requirements and the existence of an authority able to make the necessary decisions at the European level.
The logic of the situation points unequivocally at the need for a single European customs service to police the Single Market.
The absence of such a service is in many respects to blame for the development of the current crisis in transit.
If the political will to create such a service does not exist, then it is for Europe's legislators, the same as created the Single Market, to account for the consequences.
Unless the Member States demonstrate a clear will to establish effective cooperation between their respective customs services there will inevitably be increased pressure for a single European customs service.
In the meantime, a number of intermediate measures are necessary to address the structural inability of customs to control the Single Market.
These predominantly involve the internationalisation of customs investigation.
At a relatively uncontroversial level, the creation of an EU RILO (Regional Intelligence Liaison Office) would provide a pool of intelligence information for customs services.
However, in order to overcome the real obstacles to effective cooperation, a genuine European investigative service is required, able to gather and collate information, to carry out its own investigations throughout the EU and with the status of an official body in national legal systems.
In embryonic form, the EU alreadypossesses such a body in the shape of UCLAF, whose valuable efforts to add a genuinely European dimension to investigating European fraud have been constantly hampered by its small sue and by the legal constraints placed on it.
Building on this basis, not necessarily within the current context of the European Commission, UCLAF offers potential for the future.
See especially Sections 7 and 10. e.g. in "Customs 2000"
Questions 40 and 40.A
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (V) - LEGAL SYSTEMS AND PROSECUTION
Introduction
A two-tiered issue
Though the questions arising in the legal sphere in relation to the transit system bear close resemblance to those covered in the last section (fragmented jurisdiction, poor exchange of information, ineffective cooperation, etc.) the answers present greater difficulties.
The deficiencies in the way in which the legal systems of the Member States address the problem of transit fraud, and in particular the prosecution of offences, can be addressed at two levels.
On the one hand, one can attempt to make the existing system work better.
(On paper, some of the necessary mechanisms exist for the prosecution of customs offences involving different Member States.)
On the other, one can look at the legal systems themselves and conclude, as in the case of customs enforcement, that the existing fragmentation of jurisdiction is untenable in the long run.
The latter approach is a completely justifiable one.
The existence of fifteen different legal systems in one economic area creates problems in dealing with fraud of which Member States are well aware, and which have been illustrated in the present report in the failed Southwark prosecution.
In contrast to the previous section however, the legal issues are largely independent of either the transit system or the customs field in general.
Though it is only right that the Committee point out the implications of the existing legal context on the transit system, any reform of that context hes outside its terms of reference.
This section will not therefore tackle the "deeper" level of the legal issue - let this be the subject of a study commissioned by the Commission.
It will however, look very briefly at how the EU might contrive to make the existing possibilities work more effectively in dealing with transit fraud.
However, the Committee should point to the absence from the legislation of various Member States of a specific and unambiguous definition of the offence of transit fraud against Community financial interests in general.
The Committee believes that a Council Directive based on Article 100a establishing such an offence, punishable in each and every state independently of the place where the crime was physically perpetrated, is an essential step in the fight against fraud.
Establishing contacts
The evidence received by the Committee suggests above all that the simplest yet most fundamental problem faced in bringing a prosecution is finding the appropriate person to talk to.
In cases where, thanks to a UCLAF seminar or the like, two prosecutors happen to know each other, they may be able to short-circuit the official system and work effectively while
Before turning to the question of the bureaucracy and diplomacy therefore, how might these kinds of immediate working contacts be favoured.
The role of the Commission is potentially important, as it represents the best-placed actor to provide the kind of central information point which is called for.
In practice, as the Committee heard, UCLAF already attempts to provide this kind of service on an ad hoc basis.
In the circumstances it would not appear impossible for UCLAF to manage, on a more systematic basis, a directory of competent persons (i.e. state prosecutors, magistrates, customs officers, police officers, etc. - depending on the national system) with whom a counterpart may wish to get in touch.
If Member States were to be obliged to keep such a directory updated, any person responsible for a prosecution need only make an initial telephone call to UCLAF.
Naturally, such a system would be facilitated if prosecuting services and police/customs services were able to appoint specialists in legal areas likely to be affected.
Some, though not all, already do this, thus creating a pool of expertise in often complex legislation and, over time, allowing virtuous networks of fellow specialists in different Member States to be built up.
It should be said in passing that this is a plea which has been heard not only from investigators and prosecutors, but also from the trade in respect of civil cases.
The following is from the German Freight Forwarders' Association:
"Attempts by the principal to seek recourse through the courts of another country are usually financially pointless ... and extremely difficult for practical reasons (language problems, finding a specialist lawyer, translations, etc.).
How much easier it would be for the European administration which would only have to establish a properly functioning system of adrriinistrative assistance"2
Standards of evidence
The Southwark case3illustrated the need not only for the prosecutors to be in touch, but also to provide a "product" which is usable in each other's jurisdictions.
Again on a rather ad hoc basis, UCLAF, using the in-house expertise available to it, does its best to ensure that the evidence to be presented in a court in any given Member State meets that Member State's requirements.
In the light of the Southwark case (in which UCLAF was not involved), this service is clearly a useful one.
It is not one however which should be left to the workings of chance.
UCLAF, or another Community body if preferable for functional purposes, could take on the role of a "clearing-house" for cross-border evidence as a service to prosecutors.
In this sense, it would work as a consultancy, simply ensuring that cases do not fall on avoidable technicalities.
It is a relatively small step beyond this to envisage a Community prosecution office which would be responsible, either actively or passively, for collecting, collating and certifying information and evidence for use in court.
The existence of such an office would not necessarily require any radical change to existing legislation if it operated purely in a consultative capacity,
This potentially extremely complex matter has been resolved relatively simply in Belgium for example, where if evidence is admissible in court in its country of origin, it is admissible in Belgium, even if it does not meet "native" Belgian standards.4This form of "mutual recognition" of standards has been successfully applied in other fields, and would have the merit that the provider of evidence would be on relatively sure ground.
Status of Community bodies
In areas covered by Community law, such as the transit system, there is a substantial likelihood that Community bodies, such as UCLAF, will be in possession of evidence and intelligence required during prosecutions.
At present however, information provided by UCLAF does not enjoy the "official" status of information provided by national police forces, customs agents, etc.
The recognition by national courts of Community bodies such as UCLAF as official sources of evidence would facilitate the prosecution of cross-border crimes significantly.
Rather than, for example requiring a prosecution to collect and assemble evidence from the authorities in several Member States relating to the same offence, UCLAF could supply the same information as a "package", making it more comprehensible and coherent.
Indeed, given the undoubted fact that most information held by UCLAF in any case either derives from or is shared with Member State authorities, the question is genuinely one of packaging rather than substance, though one of potentially enormous benefit to prosecutors.
Simplified procedures
Apart from the reforms possible in legal practice, the weight of the procedures for legal cooperation should be considered.
The Committee heard in evidence that the arrangements for simply requesting legal assistance from another Member State still rely on messages passing through entire legal hierarchies, foreign ministries, and diplomatic channels.
In the context of a group of Member States which have agreed to form a single customs area, this approach is formalistic to the point of absurdity and, through sheer time-wasting, compromises successful prosecutions.
The formalities of requests for assistance have clearly to be reviewed.
Specialised courts
So far, the possible options for reform in this section have deliberately, for the reasons given at the beginning, give rise to no need for substantial revision of the legal status quo.
This may not be true of the last area, which is nonetheless one relating specifically to transit and to the evidence the Committee has heard.
Though specialisation of prosecutors has been mentioned, the same has not been said of courts.
Yet the Southwark case was one illustration of how complex cases involving Community law can exceed the reasonable competence of a court.
Key Points
The Committee of Inquiry cannot, within the scope of this report, address the issue of the wideranging legal reforms in Europe which are necessary to deal with cross-border fraud. It can only support all initiatives taken to provide greater legal clarity and more effective and consistent prosecution of fraud, while noting with disappointment the apparent lack of political will to translate any such initiatives into effective action.
At a more modest level, the Committee can however note a series of intermediate measures which would help facilitate the prosecution of customs crime.
First, the practical issue of establishing a contact network between prosecuting officials could be resolved relatively simply by the establishment of a European legal directory, indicating the persons and departments responsible for certain types of prosecution n particular places.
A directory of this sort could be backed up by national liaison officers responsible for ensuring that requests for assistance and legal cooperation were directed to the correct persons.
The currently excessive bureaucratic procedures for such requests (likely to become more frequent) should at the same time be substantially simplified.
Second, greater clarity is necessary in connection with the standards of evidence required for prosecution.
A preferred solution, modelled on other European legislation, would be "mutual recognition " of evidence.
Evidence acceptable in its country of origin, if necessary certified as such, would thus be admissible in all other Member States.
Given the legal difficulties in arriving at mutual recognition, which would certainly require substantial legislation, an interim solution, requiring no such legislation, would be to create a European legal "clearing house" composed of seconded national experts able to ensure that all cross-border evidence met the standards required in the courts of the Member State concerned.
Such a body could also provide wider consultative services on national legal systems.
In time, though not in the immediate future, the clearing house could provide the launch pad for a European prosecution service able to bring cases falling within its sphere of competence in cases throughout the EU.
Whilst awaiting this last development, it would be extremely useful to give UCLAF, which alreadyfulfils, within the limits of its existing competences and resources, some of these functions, the status of an official investigative body within national legal systems, thereby enabling it to present the evidence it gathers in national courts on the basis of a legal status equivalent to national authorities.
A delegation of the Committee was informed of this point of Belgian law by a state prosecutor it met during its visit to Antwerp.
See Notice to Members no. 65 - PE 220.791
OPTIONS FOR REFORM (VI) - POLITICAL OPTIONS
Introduction
Seeking political solutions
The Committee has been reminded on several occasions that some of the action to be taken in response to the crisis in transit has to be of an essentially political nature.
One of the reasons for the crisis arising in the first place is that for too long it remained an issue dealt with almost exclusively in the world of experts, outside active political control.
Hence the need to ensure that the political element is reintroduced into fundamental decision making on transit.
This report in a way represents the return of political accountability to transit and will, if heeded, contribute to generating a range of important reforms within the system.
However, four key subjects addressed in this report are essentially political, insofar as, rather than requiring administrative or technical reforms, they require political decision. These span:
- the question of an amnesty/moratorium on outstanding debts owed by principals to the customs authorities,
- the question of further extension of the Community/Common Transit Systems, and
Outstanding Customs Debts
Pragmatism or justice?
Figures given earlier in this report show the huge overhang of outstanding debt which has arisen from fraud in the transit system.1The point was made that the enforcement of this debt would almost certainly lead to widespread bankruptcies in the transport/forwarding sector.
As a result, customs authorities have frequently drawn back from demanding immediate repayment, thus sparing companies in the short term, but creating an ever-greater burden of the past.
The question arises of how this burden should be dealt with.
A lucid approach to this question comes from the German freight forwarders' association:
"The claims by the customs authorities for the payment of duties by principals who have become debtors following criminal acts of third parties has now reached an extent where, in many cases, the livelihood of the principal's undertaking is at risk.
Estimates and information supplied by individual firms indicate claims Community-wide of at least Ecu 3 billion.
Initial results of a BSL survey have confirmed this.
In individual cases firms have been asked to pay more than DM 30 million.
Anyone who understands the problems that enforcing these claims involves for the firms and guarantors concerned (e.g. bankruptcy and job-losses), must consider unconventional methods: more specifically, a general remission of duty payable as a result of fraud in the past."2
The economic argument is backed up by a moral one:
"Is it right that, in the wake of obvious and proven abuse of a customs procedure by third parties, the declarant - the victim of fraud - is forced through strict application of customs law into bankruptcy, which in turn will lead to a loss of tax and social insurance revenue and of jobs?
Can the administration afford to facilitate, indirectly or directly, the criminal behaviour of third parties..."3
There is force in these arguments, which have been reiterated, unsurprisingly, by numerous representatives of the trade who have appeared before the Committee.4 Others have added not only that they are victims of third party fraud, but also of customs incompetence or uncommunicativeness.
In one case of which the Committee has been informed, a major haulier was allowed to guarantee 19 shipments of cigarettes known by the customs to be fraudulent.
The haulier was still required to pay under its guarantee when the goods were lost.
Though understandable that customs may wish to operate secretly in order to trace criminal networks, it is surely to add injury to insult to expect the principal to pay for the losses too.
The trade therefore bases it plea for some sort of remission of debt on grounds of both economic pragmatism and moral justice.
A balance of interests
There are reasons for hesitating on this issue:
- First, the trade's request for an amnesty rests on a presumption of innocence which cannot be accepted uncritically.
A broad amnesty would almost certainly let a number of fraudsters off their debts along with honest traders.
- Second, the trade cannot deny that guarantors enter into guarantee obligations on a commercial basis, aware of the obligation thus assumed.
To release them from the obligation now would be to release them from legal obligations knowingly entered into.
- Third, how would even-handedness be ensured between those principals which, however unwillingly, have paid their customs debts, and those which have founds means of delaying payment?
- Fourth, what would be the message of an amnesty for future guarantors?
- Finally, who can grant such an amnesty, given that the outstanding debt is a complex mix of national and Community revenue?
Would the taxpayer get a good deal for bis lost revenue?
For reasons of equity, any amnesty or moratorium would have to be global, and Community-wide.
If it were to involve both the national and Community component of the debt, it would have to be agreed by all Member States and by the EU budgetary authority. An
attempt to split national and Community revenue would undoubtedly be complicated, but is not inconceivable.
It could not however be flagged in advance, for fear of a period of "free-for-all" in which guarantee obligations would not be considered binding.
The public, which would suffer the loss of revenue would have to be convinced that the amnesty represented a good investment of public money.
A Tricky Political Decision
Such are the issues facing the political authorities.
The path of least resistance remains the maintenance of the status quo.
However, if, as this report contends, there is a crisis in the system, bold and imaginative political action is required.
Such action may include deeds of indemnity and other measures intended to ease the repayment of outstanding debts, but cannot be limited to it.
An amnesty can only make sense if combined with an equally radical approach to reform of the system itself.
The choice is between stagnation or reform, with both options coming as packages.
In seeking solutions for the future of the transit system, its past cannot be ignored.
Outstanding debts are currently swamping transit principals, representing a threat not only to them but to the system as a whole.
At the same time, these are debts knowingly and legally entered into.
A balance has to be struck between the needs of the trade and the interests of the economy and taxpayer generally.
A probable solution would therefore involve a combination of write-offs for debts where the public administration is at fault and a suspension of other amounts pending a global reform of transit.
A key weakness in the system
There is no need to reiterate the difficulties which arise from the involvement of Switzerland in Common Transit.
Switzerland is a problem for the EU in terms of customs.
The basic problem is that, for reasons of geography, the EU bizarrely finds itself in a customs union with a country which rarely provides judicial cooperation on customs offences (at least in their fiscal aspect).
At the same time, Switzerland makes such offences easier to commit through its legislative and aclministrative systems.
This fact is well-known to the authorities of EU Member States and to the EU institutions, and yet is allowed to persist.
Though Switzerland's geographical position gives it a strong card to play in dealing with the EU on customs matters, it is hard to believe that the combined negotiating position of the EU and its Member States is not strong enough to achieve some greater movement from the Swiss authorities, particularly in the field of legal cooperation
in the customs field. There is goodwill in parts of the Swiss legal establishment, and this deserves to be exploited better.
Greater diplomatic pressure is therefore needed on the Swiss authorities to improve the level of cooperation they provide.
It should be remembered that the opportunity to participate in a Common transit system provides substantial benefits to Switzerland as well as to EU traders, and that the opportunity to participate in it brings with it obligations.
In the longer term therefore, it may be necessary for the EU to consider what measures may be possible to encourage a more cooperative attitude from Switzerland.
Important progress was made on 22 January 1997 with the initialling of a mutual assistance protocol, providing for administrative cooperation between Switzerland and the European Community.
It remains to be seen what this agreement will mean in practice, and, in any case, it does not alter the existing rules on judicial cooperation or bank secrecy.
Further Extension of Community/Common Transit
The extension of the Common Transit to the Visegrad countries, described in Section 9 of this report, represented an important lesson for the Community.
The transit system can have significant repercussions at the political level and cannot be treated as a purely technical matter.
In the light of the Visegrad experience, the Commission has given an assurance to the Parliament that no further such extension will take place until the Common Transit system has been reformed and computerised.6
One element not covered in the new "Monti doctrine" is the "automatic" extension of the Community transit system which would occur upon expansion of the Community.
Given the number of applicant countries, this is not a theoretical question.
Some of the political interest in the place of transit in enlargement negotiations derives from the fact that some of the countries concerned have been cited as locations though which numerous fraudulent operations have been routed, or which, through the sheer volume of trade in sensitive goods through their territories, potentially represent high-risk elements in the transit system.
Cyprus is a case in point.
It has been indicated as a point of departure for many fraudulent consignments of cigarettes, and has a substantial legitimate trade in this product.
Though one cannot visit the sins of fraudsters on the authorities, one can, as in the case of Switzerland, ask them to put in place a system providing maximum assurance that such trade is controllable and being controlled.
Political Will
Raising consciousness
This report represents an attempt to increase the awareness of the political authorities in all participating countries of the problems in transit.
Political ignorance of a situation which has been getting out of hand for several years was one of the main contributory factors to allowing it to happen.
Whatever the responsibilities for the late ringing of the alarm bell, the fact remains that political action is needed urgently, if the system is to be salvaged and the severe loss of funds staunched.
Pushing for action
Tools are to hand for the Community authorities to achieve more dynamism from national authorities.
It is frequently forgotten that the Community budget pays for the services it receives from customs services, by way of the 10% of own resources retained by Member States as an "adrninistrative charge".
This amount is fixed, and bears no relation to the quality of the service provided.
Given that the customs services bear a substantial responsibility for the poor administration of the transit system, it seems that the EU budgetary authority would be justified in looking again at the mechanism by which it pays for a service which leaves much to be desired.
This may seem an extreme position to the Member States but the Committee has at times been shocked by the complacency of some public authorities when faced with massive losses of public revenue.
If this report does Utile more than make governments aware that there is a serious problem to be addressed, this will be an important achievement.
Should this new awareness not be matched by prompt action, the Union would be right to examine radical measures which make Member States pay for their negligence in managing the transit system.
Key Points
Reform of transit imposes not only technical and legal reforms but distinct political decisions and action.
In the first place, a decision on how to deal with the mountain of current outstanding customs debts has to be reached.
On the one hand, to insist on immediate collection of aü outstanding amounts would have disastrous economic consequences for many operators.
On the other, an amnesty would be unfair on those principals which have already met their obligations and would provide a competitive advantage to its beneficiaries.
An intermediate solution could include phased repayment over a period of years which could be set off against possible cost-benefit advantages which could arise from the operators concerned equipping their vehicles with additional security equipment.
Secondly, firm Community action is needed to deal with those countries whose legislation contributes to the problems encountered in the transit system, first and foremost Switzerland.
Within the context of the ongoing negotiations on the relevant EEA Protocol,
satisfactory solutions on such matters as banking secrecy, free zone legislation and legal cooperation on fiscal offences must be obtained.
Thirdly, and as far as another problem case is concerned, Cyprus, the EU has the convenient lever that this country is an applicant to accede to the Union.
It should be made clear that Members of the EU are required to meet certain standards of control and provide certain assurances of enforcement.
Such questions should feature in accession negotiations.
Finally, the mechanism by which Member States retain 10% of the customs duties collected on behalf of the European Union may have to reviewed in the light of their apparent unwillingness to take the measures necessary to secure the transit system and thus the Community's income.
e.g. FreightForward Europe (evidence of 25.4.96), Freight Transport Association (Contribution no. 6 - PE 217.350), etc.
Case recounted in letter from Mr Rossi of the Neuchâteloise Insurance company, further to evidence of 14.10.96, involving its client Kühne und Nagel and the French customs authorities.
The case involved transit operations from France to Portugal (ostensible final destination Africa) cleared with false customs stamps.
Notice to Members no. 72 - PE 220.845
Commissioner Monti speaking in European Parliament on 5 September 1995
Introduction
On the basis of the analysis and the conclusions contained within this report, the Committee of Inquiry formulated the recommendations that follow.
The Committee is convinced that not just the viability of the transit system, but also the credibility of the institutions of the Union depend upon the Commission, the Council and the Member States acting swiftly and effectively to put these recommendations into practice, thereby addressing a major weakness in the operation of the Single Market.
Notwithstanding the crisis described in this report and the substantial damage it has caused, the Committee of Inquiry has nevertheless concluded that transit arrangements are vital to the economic interests of the European Union and its citizens.
The following conclusions are therefore based on the premise that transit is necessary as a way of effectively guaranteeing a European economic structure and ensuring the well-being of the people of Europe and must be made to function effectively in a secure and controllable environment.
Customs
In order to provide economic operators and the public at large with the necessary confidence that the Single Market trading environment is adequately protected, the Committee of Inquiry has concluded that the creation of a single EU framework for customs services must be a long-term objective of the EU.
Recommendation 1:
The European Union must establish a framework for customs services leading to national customs services functioning as if they were one.
The Committee of Inquiry calls on the Commission to make proposals to this end.
A number of measures aimed at allowing customs services to function in a more uniform and more consistent manner, should be taken.
Recommendation 2:
A common customs investigation body should be set up as soon as possible, building on the Commission's existing UCLAF antifraud unit.
The Committee of Inquiry calls on the Commission to make proposals to this end.
Recommendation 3:
Intelligence-sharing arrangements between national customs authorities should be enhanced immediately through the creation of a centralised EU customs intelligence office, to be incorporated when possible within the above-mentioned investigative service, to which all EU customs services shall pass all strategic and operational antifraud intelligence in their possession.
Recommendation 4:
Common training programmes and transnational exchange schemes for national customs officers must be expanded and linked to career progression within national services.
The Committee of Inquiry has observed that under-performance in customs services is connected with demotivation arising from persistent staff reductions and inadequate finding:
Recommendation 5:
Member States must recognise the continuing vital role of customs services in the European Single Market, review the widespread policy of staff reductions and provide customs services with comparable levels of material resources to carry out their tasks.
The Commission should monitor and report to Parliament on the development of national customs services.
The Committee of Inquiry has noted that the legal powers of customs services are often inappropriate to the present context and out of step with each other.
Recommendation 6:
The legal and administrative powers of national customs services must be better adapted to the Single Market, in particular in connection with their ability to carry out inland checks.
Efforts must be made to approximate the powers of different customs services as closely as possible.
The Commission should formally invite the heads of the national customs services to examine this issue and should report to the Parliament on the possibilities to achieve this imperative.
Control measures
Physical checks remain crucial in deterring criminal activity and must therefore be as effective and uniform as possible throughout the EU.
At the same time they must not be allowed to present a major obstacle to the flow of trade.
Recommendation 7:
The Commission, in agreement with the Member States, must establish a common policy on physical checks, maximum journey times and restricted routes, based on an agreed risk analysis technique.
These must be applied in a consistent manner by customs services.
Recommendation 8:
Material resources facilitating and enhancing physical checks, such as container scanning equipment, must be made as widely available as possible.
Such facilities should exist at a comparable level in EU ports, particularly those in direct competition with each other, as well as at important land frontier crossing points.
This measure should be taken at the request of the customs authority where sensitive goods or certain carriers are concerned.
If a forwarding agent voluntarily fits such a device, it should qualify for lower customs guarantee rates.
In some cases, existing control instruments available to customs services are only partially or inconsistently used.
Recommendation 9:
The Commission must encourage customs services to exploit more fully the potential offered by existing information-sharing and control mechanisms such as the SCENT early-warning system for the control of sensitive goods and the Customs Information System.
The Community/Common Transit system is a concession to traders and need not therefore necessarily be automatically accessible to all traders, especially when used for the transport of sensitive goods.
Limitations on access must not however present small traders with unfair entry barriers.
Recommendation 10:
Formal recognition should be given by the national customs authorities in cooperation with the services of the European Commission in terms of access to the transit system and/or guarantee requirements, to all private initiatives on the part of traders which add to the security of goods under transit.
Recommendation 11:
The Committee of Inquiry calls on the Commission to develop a flexible system of access to the Community/Common Transit systems.
The Committee of Inquiry has found that cigarettes present an extremely high risk of transit and other forms of customs fraud, and that the greatest impediment to investigating the illicit trade in cigarettes is the lack of transparency in the chain of ownership of cigarettes and the obscure nature of the financial transactions involved.
Recommendation 12:
In view of the pan-European nature of the cigarette smuggling problem, the Commission should insist that the Joint Committee on Common Transit establish a working group to examine means by which the trade in cigarettes can be made more transparent,
particularly in terms of the chain of ownership and the financial transactions involved.
Recommendation 13:
One of the main tasks of the working group would also be to examine which sanctions should be taken/introduced against persons or companies perpetrating or facilitating the illicit trade in cigarettes.
This group should report within twelve months and its conclusions should be brought before the Parliament.
Legal issues
The initial impediment to legal cooperation is the difficulty in establishing contacts between prosecuting officers in different Member States.
Recommendation 14:
The Council should invite the Member States and the Commission to examine how this can be implemented and report to the Parliament on progress.
Recommendation 15:
A directory should be prepared and maintained by the European Commission of contact prosecuting officers in the Member States. This directory should be available to prosecutors throughout the EU and be supported by national legal liaison officers.
Perhaps the main obstacle to successful cross-border prosecutions lies in the differences between national legal systems and varying standards of evidence.
Recommendation 16:
The Convention for the protection of the European Communities' financial interests must be ratified by all Member States without delay.
Both the Convention and the Regulation on the protection of the European Communities' financial interests must be rendered operational.
Recommendation 17:
The Member States should introduce a system of "mutual recognition of evidence" whereby evidence originating in another Member State should be deemed admissible if it meets the national requirements in its country of origin.
Recommendation 18:
same clearing house should offer consultancy services to national prosecuting officers on the preparation of cases.
Recommendation 19:
The Committee of Inquiry invites the Member States to enact legislation so that the courts of a Member State may be given jurisdiction over criminal customs offences even though such offences have not been committed in the territory of that Member State.
UCLAF currently does not enjoy legal recognition within Member States for the purposes of giving evidence.
This impedes its ability to intervene in prosecutions and undermines the usefulness of the evidence it gathers.
Recommendation 20:
Member States which do not have such a provision should amend their legislation on evidence so that UCLAF is able to initiate criminal proceedings and to give evidence in court on the'same basis as national authorities.
The transit system
The Commission's transit computerisation project (NCTS) is in progress and is an essential short-term condition of bringing the Community/Common Transit system under control.
Recommendation 21:
The Commission's NCTS project should be implemented without delay.
All Member States must back their political commitment to the project by making available the necessary budgetary funding, complementing the action of the European Parliament which secured the necessary Community funding in the 1997 budget.
The Commission should report to the Parliament if they note that Member States are failing to respect the timetable for this project.
Recommendation 22:
The Commission should ensure maximum exploitation of the potential offered by computerisation, by continuing to pursue the following areas: complementary applications of other modern technologies, such as Ε-mau, optical and smart cards; the exploitation of information made available by the system for the purposes of risk analysis, the targeting of controls and the establishment of guarantee/access obligations for traders;
The Committee of Inquiry has concluded that the financial liability for transit operations is unfairly concentrated on a single transit principal, usually unable fully to control the goods, and is not necessarily in proportion to the risk involved.
Recommendation 23:
The Commission should make provision, by way of suitable contractual arrangements, for the wider spread of liability for suspended taxes, duties and excise among the persons having a financial interest in and/or physical control over the goods in transit.
To facilitate this action, the information required on the Tl document should include details of all relevant persons.
Recommendation 24:
The Commission should propose that for sensitive goods, the amount of the guarantee required to cover a transit operation should be established in a more flexible and pragmatic manner, on the basis of the following: the track records of the parties involved in terms of past fraud and irregularities; the financial standing of the guarantor(s); the provision by the guarantor(s) of concrete proof as to the security of the goods in transit, including proof of adequate insurance cover of the goods in transit by reliable underwriters; the risk associated with the goods in transit.
The Committee has noted displacement of transit fraud from road transport into other modes of transport.
Recommendation 25:
The Commission should examine the extent to which fraud problems exist outside the road transport sector, and make appropriate proposals for the extension of the transit guarantee system to other modes of transport, especially sea transport.
The Committee of Inquiry has found that customs services frequently do not respect the terms of transit legislation.
Recommendation 26:
Unrealistic deadlines and other provisions within the present transit system should be made more realistic in the short term, in order that they may be respected by all customs services alike while being fair to operators.
The Commission should include in its reforms the shortening of the deadline within which customs must enforce guarantee obligations taking into account the level of computerisation of the system.
Prior notification of changes of destination should also be made compulsory.
Political choices
Many operators are subject to outstanding customs debt arising from transit fraud and irregularities which, if enforced, threaten the continued existence of the companies concerned.
Arrangements for the removal and/or suspension of these debts must be considered.
Recommendation 27:
The Commission should include, as an essential part of its reform package for the transit system, a proposal for writing off outstanding debts where these can be shown to be the result of maladministration.
Until these arrangements have been agreed, customs administrations should be invited to agree a suspension of outstanding claims of repayment dating from before the introduction of the 100% guarantee for sensitive goods.
The Committee has concluded that legal and administrative cooperation with the Swiss authorities present major difficulties and must be improved.
Recommendation 28:
The initialling in January 1997 of a mutual assistance protocol with the Swiss authorities represents potentially significant progress and is to be welcomed, its implementation however merits close monitoring.
The Parliament's Committee on Budgetary Control should ensure such monitoring.
Recommendation 29:
In the ongoing negotiations with Switzerland on a bilateral agreement, the Council should include in the Commission's negotiating mandate a demand that the Swiss legal authorities achieve more frequent and effective legal cooperation on customs fraud offences, notably where these are committed outside Switzerland and/or involve financial interests outside Switzerland, and remove the obstacles placed in the way of investigators by Swiss bank secrecy laws.
The Commission should report annually to Parliament on cooperation with the Swiss authorities.
The transit system has suffered from prolonged neglect by the highestpolitical authorities of the European Union, which, in conjunction with a degree of official complacency, is to a large degree responsible for the deterioration in the situation over recent years.
Recommendation 30:
Each presidency of the Council of Ministers should report to Parliament on the progress made in implementing the recommendations of the Committee of Inquiry in the course of its six-month period of office.
Recommendation 31:
The Community budgetary authority should review the functioning of the mechanism whereby Member States retain 10% of traditional own resources collected.
Entitlement to this sum must be linked to evidence that Member States are taking appropriate measures to protect the financial interests of the Community, in accordance with Article 209a of the Treaty.
Recommendation 32:
The Commission should propose that Community legislation be amended to include the concept of a "duty of care" on the part of customs authorities towards the users of the transit system in accordance with a protocol on harmonised customs assistance, with appropriate sanctions available in cases where a user can demonstrate a failure of that duty of care.
The question of further extension of the Community/Common Transit system is of concern, and arises in connection with possible future enlargements of the EU, particularly in the case of Cyprus.
Recommendation 33:
The Commission should ensure that the security of the Community/Common Transit system is included as one of the key concerns within the context of enlargement negotiations.
Prospective Member States must offer appropriate guarantees as to their ability to control transit procedures.
In the medium to long term, the existence of three different transit regimes will become increasingly difficult to sustain.
The Committee has become aware of the circulation of large numbers of forged TIR carnets within Europe.
Recommendation 35:
Given the significant undermining of the European Community's fiscal sovereignty by the widespread circulation of forged TIR carnets, the Committee on Budgetary Control should investigate the scale of the problem and possible countermeasures.
New regulations are often imposed in an attempt to resolve wider social and political questions which cannot be solved in the framework of customs tarification.
The multiplication of regulations in the context of the Common Agricultural Policy effectively renders control extremely difficult:
Recommendation 36:
The Commission should propose a simplification of existing customs regulations with the purpose of enabling controls to be effective.
Recommendation 37:
The Commission, Council and European Parliament, when examining proposals for legislation, trade agreements and treaties, should consider whether these proposals are realisable under the conditions given.
The recommendations included within this report must be acted upon.
Action taken should be monitored by the European Parliament:
Recommendation 38:
The European Parliament should confer on its Committee on Budgetary Control, if necessary in cooperation with the other competent committees, the task of monitoring the implementation of the recommendations set out in this report and instruct it to report to Parliament, in connection with the discharge in respect of the 1996 financial year, on the action taken.
