COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Final report (rapporteur: Mr Kellett-Bowman)
MINORITY OPINION delivered pursuant to Rule 136(10) of the Rules of Procedure
In this document, the Group of Independents for a Europe of Nations (I-EDN), represented on the Temporary Committee of Inquiry by Thierry JEAN-PIERRE, full member, and Hervé FABRE-AUBRESPY, substitute member, expresses, pursuant to Rule 136(10) of Parliament's Rules of Procedure, an opinion different to that held by a majority of members of the committee.
This disagreement does not essentially concern the report's analysis of the extent of the shortcomings in the Community transit system.
Nor does it concern the conclusion, set out in paragraph 17.2.1. of the report, that 'transit is necessary' and that it 'must be made to function effectively in a secure and controllable environment'.
It mainly relates to the report's conclusion urging the establishment of a 'single European framework for customs services', presented throughout the report as a genuine 'common customs service'.
In addition to the reservations which the very principle of such a measure arouses among the members of the I-EDN Group, which is committed to a European integration process which respects the identity and sovereignty of the nations involved, the report contains many points which lead us to question the raison d'être of such a European customs service.
Neither the very real failings of the Community transit system, presented as the result of the ineffectiveness of national customs services, nor the very considerable problems which its establishment would entail, nor the political drawbacks to such a decision argue in favour of that choice.
- It is astonishing to note the determination of the Committee of Inquiry to blame the Member States and national customs officials for the shortcomings in the Community transit system.
This accusation underpins the 'irrefutable logic' which inevitably leads on to blinkered Communitization in the form of the establishment of a European customs service.
The committee is thus at pains to criticize 'the inability of the customs authorities to resolve the problem' of fraud, thereby clearing the ground for its advocacy of the single customs service, whereas in fact it was the introduction of the single market and the abolition of border checks which clearly led most of the Member States to reduce the size of their customs services.
The Member States were then no more successful than the Commission in spotting the new possibilities which the disappearance of internal customs borders offered to fraudsters.
Moreover, the bureaucratic inefficiency which the national customs services are accused of in the report would not be reduced by the establishment of a single customs service, a gargantuan version of the national services; in fact, the reverse is true.
- The I-EDN Group takes the view that a further serious attack on national sovereignty, in the form of the Communitization of the customs services, would be politically unacceptable.
This is, moreover, the opinion held by most of the EU governments, which take the view that a Community customs service would give rise to serious political problems.
The particular significance of such a decision has not escaped the vast majority of the Member States' customs authorities, which not only flatly reject the idea of a unified customs service, but also take the view that such a service could not fight fraud effectively, setting aside the practical problems which the establishment of the European customs service would pose, problems quite rightly referred to in paragraph 14.4.1.5. of the report: 'At the practical level, the creation of a single service would cause severe organizational upheaval, require the creation of new infrastructures and give rise to reams of new legislation'.
- The proposal highlighted in this way, on the basis of a hard-won compromise in the committee, is mere window-dressing. It cannot disguise the fact that the committee refused to be satisfied with immediate, interim solutions which were technically effective, but which, in the eyes of its members, clearly did not go far enough towards Communitization.
The committee's report represents an excellent investigation of the shortcomings of the Community transit system and the extent of fraud.
As a testimony to the quality of the committee's work, the report thus fulfils the general task of a committee of inquiry, as set out in Rule 136(1) of the Rules of Procedure: '[to] investigate alleged contraventions of Community law or alleged maladministration in the application of Community law'.
However, it is in its conclusions that the committee has erred, endorsing the radical view held by its rapporteur for whom the only salvation in the long-term is the transition to a single customs service.
Nevertheless, the report is full of proposals for effective solutions which do not necessarily involve such an extreme step.
Thus, in paragraph 14.4.3.2., it states that 'there are a number of interim measures which could usefully be taken in terms of harmonization of powers and practice, coordination of activities and sharing of intelligence'.
The report is right to state, in paragraph 14.4.1.8., that 'if there is one area where a European approach is required, it is this'.
However, it is wrong to reject, in favour of the establishment of a single customs service, the idea that such an approach could be based on a recognition of the problems by the Member States.
That will lead to the release of fresh resources, the only way the Member States can combat fraud against the transit system effectively, in close cooperation with their partners.
- However, effective cooperation of this kind among Member States does not seem to be acceptable to the committee, which takes the view that all the 'interim' measures referred to in the report 'should not be confused with the main objective: the establishment of a fully integrated customs service'.
Yet this was not the committee's objective, which was to highlight the shortcomings in the Community transit system and establish the extent of fraud as a prelude to putting forward possible
Finally, it is impossible not to condemn the fact that the problem of the possible extension of the Community transit system should be mentioned, in connection with future EU enlargements, only with regard to Cyprus, largely ignoring the countries of Central Europe other than the Visegrad States, countries which were discussed at length by the committee when starting its work.
Instead, this report, which marks the completion of the work of a 33-member committee which held 38 meetings (two of which took up two days), should have been used to bring home to the Member States the fact that the problem has acquired alarming proportions and that it is time to take effective and rapid measures to combat fraud against the Community transit system; closer cooperation among the Member States and the harmonization of customs policies should, therefore, have been advocated.
- In this connection, the report was right to pay particular attention to the lack of judicial cooperation.
Here again, however, Communitization is not the solution.
The report points out that the difficulties inherent in the inquiry procedure can be overcome, and that it is the legal proceedings which are problematic.
Cooperation among the national judicial systems could thus be improved with a view to achieving the results called for in paragraph 17.5. of the report: removing the impediment linked to the inability to establish who should be contacted in another Member State in the context of an mquiry; simplifying and speeding up the cumbersome, formal procedures applying to requests for legal assistance between Member States in connection with judicial proceedings.
Such judicial cooperation instruments exist, as the report points out at the end of paragraph 8.2.2.1.; however, rather formal structures are likely to make the system ineffective.
It is here that the role of the Commission's antifraud unit, UCLAF, is becorning more and more important, both as regards cooperation between investigators and liaison between those responsible for judicial proceedings.
However, the I-EDN Group cannot accept the recommendation, made in paragraph 17.3.2. of the report, that UCLAF should be transformed as soon as possible into a European customs investigation body.
Giving UCLAF full investigative powers and allowing it to exercise them throughout the European Union represent further measures likely to undermine the sovereignty of the Member States.
It would be enough, moreover, to develop priority cooperation in these areas, somewhat along the lines of the Irish solution referred to in paragraph 14.4.2.2. of the report: a much higher degree of systematic cooperation, based, for example, on greater use of intelligence sharing schemes, which would be made possible by the establishment of the 'centralized intelligence office' referred to in paragraph 17.3.2. of the report.
The committee acknowledges the validity of such solutions by pointing out that the situation would be improved quite simply by the Member States agreeing on a definition of fraud, on the penalties to be imposed and on the procedures for trans-frontier prosecutions.
- Most of the instruments required for more effective European customs cooperation already exist, but they are used either incorrectly or too little.
This is true, for example, of the SCENT network linking the customs administrations, the systems for the exchange of information, or the project for the computerization of the transit system (NCTS), which does indeed represent 'an essential short-term condition for bringing the Community transit system under control' (paragraph 17.6.1.).
However, the existing measures and instruments must be optimized, along with the use made of them by the customs aatodnistrations of the Member States.
It is thus incumbent on the latter to make the effort needed to establish without delay a genuine policy of customs cooperation.
Much more effective cooperation among the Member States, both political cooperation in the Council and the more technical cooperation involving the customs administrations, the remedying of shortcomings and the optimization of the existing mechanisms thus seem to us to take priority over the Communitization of customs services, which is primarily being advocated on ideological grounds.
The Committee of Inquiry's work and its report should enable the Member States to recognize the strategic importance of combating fraud against the Community transit system and then to embark without delay on effective cooperation at European level, setting aside appropriate financial and human resources and employing working methods tailored to the new customs situation which has resulted from the entry into force of the single market.
The I-EDN Group expected the work of the Committee of Inquiry to produce recommendations of this kind urging closer cooperation among the Member States and customs authorities, and not the establishment - even if it is finally envisaged as a long-term measure in the vague form of a 'European framework' - of an additional Community body.
We can on no account give our support to the European customs service advocated in the report and dear to the heart of the European Parliament, which has already given its backing in the discussions on the Customs 2000 programme.
That is why the Group of Independents for a Europe of Nations, faithful to its concept of Europe, voted against the Committee of Inquiry's report.
