Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by Freight Forward Europe, Brussels
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Brussels, January 16,1996
EU System for Transit of Goods and Freight Forwarders At Risk
Contents:
Highlights of the contribution Freight forwarders facilitate the flow of goods How the system is designed to function Example of how the system should function What is the problem?
Examples of fraud cases Why this problem arose Dimensions of the problem Consequences of the problem Internal control measures create unlevel playing field The view of the European Commission Measures proposed by FreightForward Europe
Companies participaiins; in FreightForward Europe are: ASCì. BiKpediiion. Dachser. Dan/as.
Highlights of the Contribution
As a result of fraud under transit, a breakdown of the Community Transit regime can no longer be excluded if no adequate measures are taken soon.
The regulations of the Community Transit regime currently in place were designed at a time when customs control between borders still existed.
It is no longer adapted to the requirements of the single market and to the increasing fraudulent abuse of the system.
Freight forwarders are held liable, irrespective of any negligence on their part, for all duties and taxes that are foregone by customs under the common transit regime provisions.
The single market's trade facilitation has been achieved at the expense of transit operators which now face huge bills from customs.
• nere is no longer insurance available in the market to cover the risks of freight forwarders in a transit operation.
The Transit system is putting the financial and policing burden on the freight forwarders instead of the customs authorities.
Because customs is not pursuing the perpetrators of fraud, freight forwarders are unwillingly subsidising organised crime.
Customs claims outstanding against freight forwarders are so high that they are merely theoretical.
Although they are no longer recoverable, they will provoke many bankruptcies in the industry.
The problem's dimension means that there will be damages to the transport sector, to the insurance sector, to industry and to EU and national treasuries.
The fact that there is no precise evaluation of the damages involved shows that it is no longer possible to master the system.
Customs administrations should be required to make a clear-cut and unambiguous audit of the present situation.
Measures proposed by FFE include restricting the access to the transit regime, making all parties involved more accountable and relieving the freight forwarder of his liability in cases where there is no proven negligence on his part.
Freight Forwarders, Issuing TI Documents, Facilitate the Flow of Goods
With a view to relieving international transport from a number of practical problems, international operators and freight forwarders use the EU-wide uniform paper "Tl".
Under that system, actual payment of customs duties at the border is waived against liability of the party issuing the Tl, "the principal".
The principal has to ensure a proper discharge of the document when the goods are produced at the customs office of destination.
If, for any reason, the procedure is not correctly complied with, the principal has to pay all customs duties.
In order to secure such liability, the principal has to deposit an indemnity with customs authorities.
Acting as principals, freight forwarders issue Tl documents and thus facilitate the rapid and undisturbed flow of goods within the EU.
The only other available document for transit of goods by road in the EU is the carnet TIR.
This cannot be used, however, for transport of bonded goods within the EU.
With the carnet TIR, the indemnity is given by the IRU in Geneva, backed by a pool of insurance companies.
How the System Is Designed to Function
The sender of goods gives an order form to the freight forwarder.
The freight forwarder's bank indemnity with the customs authorities enables him to issue a Tl document so that the duties do not have to be paid at the border.
The Tl document consists of several copies.
One copy is kept by the customs office of departure (where the goods are entering the EU).
Another copy is kept by the freight forwarder issuing the document.
The carrier/transporter receives another copy as well as two additional copies that he needs to deliver with the goods at the customs office of destination.
When the carrier presents the goods at the customs office of destination, copy number 5 is validated with an official customs stamp and sent back by the customs officials to the central customs office of the country where the Tl was issued.
After verification of the validating stamps, that central office sends a confirmation to the customs office of departure where the Tl was originally issued as a notification that the Tl document has been cleared.
It is at this stage that the procedure comes to an end.
However, the freight forwarder does not receive a notification of correct compliance with the Tl procedure.
It is important to note that in rail and maritime transport, the transit procedure is applied differently: here the carrier takes over responsibility for the goods when he accepts them.
The railway company or the shipping company is not required to deposit an indemnity with the customs authorities.
Example of How the System Should Function
Let's suppose goods are imported from Poland into Spain.
At a certain point, the goods arrive at the German border without any other transit documents.
Instead of having to pay and recover duties and taxes at each country's border, the freight forwarder issues a Tl transit document.
This means that he takes responsibility as a principal for bringing the goods to Spain where the applicable duties and taxes will be paid.
In order to be able to do this, he must have deposited a guarantee with the customs authorities.
The customs office in Germany, in Waithaus for example, stamps the Tl document and keeps one copy in its files.
The transporter, who is actually transporting the goods as a sub-contractor of the freight forwarder, receives the remaining copies of the Tl document that he takes with him to Spain.
Once in Spain, the documents and the goods are presented to the Spanish customs office, in Algeciras for example.
Customs in Algeciras validates the document by means of its stamp and keeps copy nr. 5.
Customs in Algeciras sends copy nr. 5 by regular mail to the central German customs office in Hamm which in turn sends it to the customs office of departure in Waithaus.
The customs office in Waithaus compares it with the original copy: if everything is correct, the freight forwarder is discharged from his liability.
As long as the customs office of departure does not receive copy nr. 5, the Tl document has not been cleared, i.e. the freight forwarder is liable for all duties and taxes.
What Is the Problem?
The current problems partly result from inefficiencies by the customs authorities, but are mainly due to fraud by criminal organisations.
Freight forwarders, issuing Tl documents on behalf of their clients, are held strictly liable, irrespective of any negligence on their part, for all taxes and duties if the procedure is not complied with correctly.
Unintentional negligence by customs arises because customs offices, sometimes suffering from a lack of organisation, forget to send back copy nr. 5 to the customs office of departure or the copy nr.
5 is sent back with enormous delays. Alternatively, copy nr.
5 can get lost in the mail. In such cases, principals are held liable.
Customs experts estimate that the number of non-returned Tl documents equals the amount of one year of traffic.
EU customs authorities are still using ordinary rubber stamps which can easily be falsified by third parties.
The customs authorities themselves do not know exactly or in detail which stamps are used in which cases by other EU-customs authorities with whom they correspond in view of clearing the Tl documents.
Criminal organisations have increasingly misused the system's flaws and the freight forwarders' legal obligations.
Customs stamps are falsified and customs officials are
In all cases where there is no proper clearing of the Tl procedure, customs claims the unpaid duties and taxes from the freight forwarder who is legally liable.
In case of unpaid duties and taxes because of criminal involvement, there is a tendency on the part of customs administrations to consider the collection of these duties and taxes from principals as a routine administrative régularisation.
Customs administrations have up to 11 months after the goods were presented at the office of arrival to notify the freight forwarder and to claim the duties.
Probably due to a lack of means, customs administrations are not chasing the perpetrators of fraud but collect the moneys from the principals.
In effect, freight forwarders are unwillingly subsidising organised crime.
Examples of Fraud Cases
All fraud cases have different circumstances.
However, the following examples of fraud give an indication of how the system is invaded by criminal organisations.
These examples are fictitious but are copied from actual cases.
March 1993:
A sender wants to ship cigarettes from Poland to Spain.
At the Polish-German border, the carrier contracted by the sender asks the freight forwarder to issue a Tl document.
Because of the sensitivity of the goods, the freight forwarder issues a Tl document for one container and decides to wait with the next one until the first Tl document has been cleared by customs.
April 1993:
The first Tl document is returned with customs stamps and signatures.
The freight forwarder issues a Tl document for the second container.
May - October 1993: The freight forwarder issues Tl documents according to the same procedure for 11 more containers.
January 1994:
The freight forwarder is notified that the customs stamps on all Tl documents were forged and that it is liable for duties and taxes because the goods are not proven to have left Germany.
Had the freight forwarder been informed earlier, he would never have issued the following Tl documents.
March 1994:
A sender wants to export several trucks of live cattle from Denmark to Hungary and asks the freight forwarder to issue a Tl document for transit through the European Community under suspension of duties.
The freight forwarder, having checked the shipment order according to its internal check list, issues the Tl document.
All in all, 5 Tl documents are issued.
January 1995:
The freight forwarder is notified by Danish customs that there is no proof that the live cattle have left the Danish territory.
As the carrier has disappeared and as the Danish exporter has gone bankrupt, the Danish customs administration claims all duties and taxes from the freight forwarder.
November 1994: Cigarettes are imported from the US for transit to Morocco.
The freight forwarder, upon instruction by the consignor, issues a Tl document for the transit after having verified extensively the circumstances of this shipment.
December 1994: The carrier, a long-time and reliable business partner based in France, hands the Tl document, cleared by customs in Algeciras, Spain, with an official Spanish stamp, to the freight forwarder which considers its liability terminated.
October 1995:
The freight forwarder receives a claim from Dutch customs for the duties and taxes on the November 1994 shipment of cigarettes.
The stamps were forged by a criminal organisation and there is no proof that the cigarettes have left the Dutch territory.
There are estimated to be thousands of such cases across the European Union.
Why This Problem Arose
The regime for Community Transit - the Common Transit Regime - was designed in the 1960s for importers and exporters in an EC of six countries with internal borders.
Today, transit is organised by freight forwarders acting as intermediaries to the transaction in a European Union of 15 countries without internal borders.
Customs budgets have been reduced in relative terms and customs authorities have not been able to keep up with the rapidly increasing workload.
Furthermore, the regime is increasingly subject to fraud by organised crime.
Later this year, the Visegrad countries will join the common transit regime.
Although the circumstances have changed radically over the last thirty years, the Τ transit regime has not been adapted.
The single market's trade facilitation has been achieved at the expense of transit operators, mostly freight forwarders that are now facing huge bills from customs.
Indeed, as has been pointed out before, freight forwarding companies are held liable, irrespective of any negligence of their own, for all taxes and duties if the transit procedure is not correctly complied with.
A key element of the problem is the structure of the liability: the party with the biggest economic interest - the buyer and/or seller which holds the cargo interest - is not legally responsible, while the party that is morally not responsible - the principal, in most cases the freight forwarder who only acts as an intermediary - is legally responsible.
Dimensions of the Problem
The fact that neither the European Commission nor the customs administrations know how many claims are pending proves that it is no longer possible to master the transit system in the European Union.
It also creates a high level of risk and uncertainty in which it is difficult to continue to do business.
The estimates of the amount of claims vary, but it clearly concerns billions of Ecus.
Every year, 18 million Tl documents are issued across the EU.
Each Tl having an average level of duties and taxes of 24,000 Ecu (based on IRU calculations), some 432 billion Ecu of taxes and duties are channelled through the Tl system every year.
An incidence of fraud of 0.2%, the figure calculated for the TIR regime, means that freight forwarders are liable for more than 3.5 billion Ecu since 1991.
It is important to note that the problems have reached such dimensions since 1991 but given the timeconsuming administrative procedures, they only appear years later.
Another estimate can be based on the number of claims presented under the TIR recime, where the situation is easier to oversee thanks to the TIR regime's central management.
Some 2.3 million carnets TIR are issued every year.
The guaranteeing
If these figures are extrapolated proportionately to the common transit regime, where the liability is unlimited, it appears that freight forwarders could be liable for more than 20 billion Ecu. (i)
In its Communication on "Fraud in the Transit Procedure, Solutions Foreseen and Perspectives for the Future", the European Commission says that fraud in transit, which involved organised crime, is known to have had a financial impact for the Community and Member States of a minimum of 750 million Ecu, during the period 1990 - 1994.
The European Commission rightly points out that this amount does not take into account economic damage to business.
However, what it fails to point out is that the figures do not report the number of cases that are still pending but which customs administrations do not report for fear of a political backlash.
Nor does it include the claims from 1993, 1994 and 1995 which are still unknown and which can also result from administrative inefficiencies.
Considering the rate at which claims are issued by customs and the size of these claims, the freight forwarding industry estimates that it is liable for more than 8 billion Ecu.
In the Netherlands alone, freight forwarders have received tax bills of nearly 900 million Ecu.
Consequences of the Problem
It is clear that no industry can pay the sums indicated above.
If these claims are collected, as the law foresees, a considerable part of the freight forwarding industry will go bankrupt.
If the current level of risk continues and no appropriate measures are taken soon, the whole transit regime could break down.
The costs to society of a collapse of the common transit regime would be enormous.
In theory, 432 billion Ecu will have to be paid at the borders.
It is easy to imagine the resulting chaos and queuing at the EU border posts and the enormous burden of administering the system.
It is easy to predict a sky-rocketing increase in illegal traffic and smuggling, accompanied by the continued establishment and strengthening of criminal networks.
A collapse of the transit system will also re-erect considerable barriers to international trade.
European unification and the European single market would lose a lot of its credibility.
Looking at the total cost picture of a collapse of the transit regime, the following, mostly unquantifiable, costs appear:
The principal's liability in fraud cases is high because the taxes and duties on tobacco, alcohol and other sensitive products is very high - an average of 500,000 Ecu is estimated to be a minimum.
There arc 7.8 times more Tl documents per year than TIR carnets.
The following (theoretical) calculation is then made: 7,000 χ 500,000 χ 7.8 = 27.3 billion Ecu.
• • • an unknown but certain percentage of the 432 billion Ecu will disappear.
But even in the absence of a collapse of the transit regime, the costs of the current situation are intolerable.
The following aspects should be noted:
• for industry and international trade
As a result of the risks involved, the cost of transit to industry is set to increase, which will decrease the competitiveness of European industry.
As there is no longer insurance available to cover the risks of freight forwarders in a transit operation, the cost of issuing Tl documents will rise.
Currently, one Tl document produces a revenue of 30 Ecu on average for the freight forwarder while one container of cigarettes accounts for between 1 and 2 million Ecu in duties and taxes.
• for the fight against fraud
Despite the fact that all parties involved in the transit transaction are responsible for the correct compliance with regulations, in practice the customs administrations often claim missing duties and taxes directly from the freight forwarder, without investigating the fraud and without taking recourse against the perpetrators of the fraud.
In the context of current regulations, customs and public authorities have no incentive to fight fraud as their revenues from taxes and duties are always guaranteed through the liability of the freight forwarders, who are in fact therefore subsidising crime.
In addition, in the present circumstances, there is a disincentive for the principal to cooperate with customs.
Principals detecting irregularities with a shipment once a Tl document has been issued will not report these irregularities to customs because of the risk they run that customs will then claim the duties and taxes from them.
• for EU and Member States' budgets
The sums at stake are so large that they are no longer recoverable by member states.
In a certain way, these claims have become theoretical and will have to be written off at some stage.
It will not be possible to collect the claims currently outstanding without provoking a series of bankruptcies.
This is precisely the reason why so many have not yet been collected.
However, even if the claims are not collected, they still pose a serious problem for companies under normal accounting rules.
Accounting rules in most countries require companies to provide for reserves against such claims in their balance sheets.
It is possible that companies neglect to mention customs claims in their balance sheet to avoid technical bankruptcy -a perverse effect of the current situation.
Internal Control Measures Create Unlevel Playing Field
Given the risks involved in the transit business, the larger freight forwarders have taken all possible precautions since the problem emerged in 1991.
Some examples of a whole series of measures are:
The company does not act as a Τ form principal for the transport of tobacco and alcohol products, or agricultural goods where Common Agricultural Policy levies are involved unless previously authorised by the country Chief Executive.
The company does not act as a Τ form principal without being the carrier of goods in question either through drivers employed by the company or through approved subcontractors under the company's control.
The sub-contracted carrier must have undergone the necessary credit and reputation checks.
Where the company acts as a Τ form principal, the goods must be consigned to the company itself at destination, who should be given pre-advice of the goods' departure by the origin station.
That office at destination is then responsible for ensuring that the Τ form is discharged, and a signed copy stamped by Customs at destination is returned to the issuing office.
Some freight forwarders no longer issue Τ documents for alcohol and tobacco at all, others have gone so far as to no longer issue Τ documents for any shipments to and from eastern Europe. Ultimately, freight forwarders may have to abandon the Tl system.
As a result of their internal measures, freight forwarders are increasingly faced with an unlevel playing field on the one hand and with pressure to continue facilitation at the borders on the other hand.
Their strict guidelines for control have led to increasing queues at the border.
Truck drivers get angry because they have to wait longer and customs officers, themselves under political pressure to clear the queues, complain to freight forwarders about the long internal control procedures.
companies, for example, have been found to set up small limited companies in Germany, which pay 20,000 DM guarantee to the German customs for a Tl licence.
They issue thousands of Tl documents a month until they receive the first tax bill from customs for a Tl document that has not been properly discharged.
In such a case they simply go bankrupt and set up a new limited company, starting all over.
For the EU and the national authorities, these foregone tax revenues are lost definitively.
In the meantime, the legitimate freight forwarders are faced with a dilemma.
If they do not put in place strict control measures, they risk bankruptcy as a result of their liability towards customs under the Tl procedure.
If they adhere to strict control measures, their clients get angry and switch to companies which operate fraudulently outside the EU's legal framework.
European Commission's View of the Problem
In response to a question by Mr. Floras Wijsenbeek, member of the European Parliament, Mrs. Scrivener answered on behalf of the European Commission that the operators who accept to issue Tl documents for their clients against payment are aware of the risks involved.
Mrs. Scrivener wrote that freight forwarders have to be careful when issuing a Tl document.
This way, they will contribute to the fight against fraud and illegal traffic.
She added: "Diminishing the responsibilities (of the freight forwarders) with regard to the collection of duties would be detrimental to safeguarding the financial interests of the Community and the protection of the legal trade, for which the Commission cannot be responsible."
Since then, the European Commission has always kept to that view.
All its decisions have been based on the opinion that the issuing of transit documents involves a normal commercial risk against which the principal can insure itself.
First of all, given the risks involved, insurance companies have withdrawn from this market and insurance covers are mostly limited to 50,000 Ecu against high premiums.
Secondly, in light of the organised crime that is invading and abusing the transit system, the risk for freight forwarders can no longer be considered to be of a normal commercial nature.
FreightForward Europe (FFE) argues furthermore that the current system is not fair in that control should be assured by the public authorities, in this case customs.
The role of freight forwarders is to facilitate international trade and transport, not to fight against fraud.
If freight forwarders are expected to control fraud, they should get the same means and competences as the customs authorities.
Freight forwarders cannot be expected to act as a police force without having investigation competences.
Under general principles of law, customs has to fulfil two functions, namely collecting fiscal revenues and protecting the economy against fraud.
FFE has many reasons to believe that customs is totally neglecting the latter function in many cases.
As responsible companies, the FFE participants propose a number of measures to preserve the Τ transit regime:
• clear-cut and unambiguous audit of the present situation by customs and other authorities. In the meantime, in order to preserve the transit regime and to facilitate cooperation between all parties,
• a suspension of pending claims is required for a certain period of time, at least until the audit has produced a clear analysis of the situation.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the European Conferation of Tobacco Retailers (CEDT), Brussels.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
CONTRIBUTION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT INQUIRY COMMITTEE ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT
EUROPEAN CONFEDERATION OF TOBACCO RETAILERS (CEDT)
INTRODUCTION
The origins of tobacco contraband go back many years.
Recently, however, there has been an increase in illegal trade of merchandise, especially so-called "sensitive goods", including tobacco and tobacco products, but also frozen meat, milkpowder, butter, sugar, alcoholic drinks, etc.
Although this point is not universally agreed, it is our opinion that this has been facilitated both by the abolition of the internal borders within the European Union and the principle of free circulation of goods.
During the 1980s, contraband tobacco products were predominantly introduced into the European countries by sea.
This was organised by large contraband organisations, mainly located along the Atlantic shores.
Duty-free zones such as Gibraltar and Andorra were alternative, but less important, routes for introduction.
Recently, however, this latter method has become increasingly popular.
The EU countries most affected by tobacco contraband are Spain and Italy, but the problem also exists in Germany and "in crescendo" in the other countries of the EU.
The problem of contraband has become significantly worse since January 1, 1993, with the introduction of the principle of free flow of people, goods and capital.
This is when contraband became a really large problem.
It mainly concerns the road transit systems, TIR and Tl.
These systems are used not only by legitimate commercial operators but also by contraband organisations which are aware of the large profits to be made by using the transit systems to illegally introduce tobacco products into the EU.
We would also like to mention the failure to apply effective sanctions systems in the Member States, both administratively and judicially.
This has led to the impression that contraband is socially acceptable.
As an example of the above, we can refer to the recently adopted Spanish legislation on the repression of contraband repression, where the Customs Vigilance Service (S.V.A.) is no longer involved in the fight against contaband.
We would like to point out that the associations of tobacconists in the EU have already informed the EU's unit for combating fraud (UCLAF) about this problem, and have provided them with a large amount of documentation, which demonstrates both the size and seriousness of the problem of fraud relating to contraband of tobacco in Europe.
The Inquiry Committee on Fraud in Transit will find all the relevant information within UCLAF.
METHODS OF FRAUD USED BY THE TOBACCO CONTRABAND ORGANISATIONS
A) ROAD TRANSPORT (TRANSIT OF MERCHANDISE)
American tobacco arrives in the EU, where it is usually deposited in the free zones in the ports of Amberes and Rotterdam.
As the tobacco enters duty free, it is not subject to any taxes or duties.
The international operators, who have not only the information but also authorisation to access the free warehousing areas, contact the main contraband networks.
The largest volumes of cantraband tobacco are brought into the European market by sophisticated white collar criminal organisations that have discovered the profitability of these illegal operations.
For example: a trailer containing 1,000 boxes of blond tobacco, produces a loss to any EU Member State of 688,000 Ecu via lost taxes.
Most of these forgone taxes become profits for the criminal networks.
Once the contrabandists have established contact with the TIR or Tl operators, they state the destination of the merchandise as being either a 3rd (non EU) country or a free warehouse.
Usually the merchandise is sent to a false addressee, from where the products are deviated for consumption inside community territory.
In order to carry out this type of transport, the consignor companies must, among other administrative requirements, deposit global guarantees with customs.
However, these guarantees do not cover even 109r of the value of the merchandise.
However, once it became apparent that many of the tobacco shipments did not arrive at destination, the European Union decided to increase the guarantee.
For tobacco, the grarantee required will be equal to 100% of the customs duties that apply on it as of 1 February 1996.
Document where the commercial operations done are guaranteed by the company.
INVOICE
By a perfect cooperation between customs administrations and by an effective control of these documents, it should be possible to identify the persons that are physically, legally and morally responsible for the contaband.
It is important to note that, due to the huge amount of fraud perpetrated under the TIR regime, the IRU (International Road Transport Union), removed the use of the TIR system for transit of tobacco and alcohol.
However, since August 1, 1995, it has been allowed again TIR, although in very small quantities which is the only way to safeguard the TIR regime.
The European Commission, notably UCLAF, and other relevant institutions have achieved good results with the PIS "Prior Information System".
However, the contraband organisations have continued to sophisticate methods by which to introduce so-called sensitive products, such as tobacco, into the European Union.
It is important to note that while there has been great progress with regard to safeguarding community transit by road, transit by sea needs more attention.
As the contraband networks increasingly connect with the big tobacco companies, effective control of ocean going transport in the late 1990s will not only require cooperation between the different national administrations but also the collaboration with private companies.
As mentioned earlier, use of sea transport for tobacco contraband declined in the 1980s due to the disappearance of the internal EU borders, making it easier and cheaper for the contraband organisations to use the community transport system for their illegal purposes.
However, due to various new measures that customs administrations have introduced, contraband organisations have renewed their interest in this more traditional method of fraud.
Large shipments of tobacco (between 10,000 and 15,000 boxes) are loaded onto ships known as "wet nurse" ships, in the harbours of Amberes or Rotterdam.
These shipments are destined in theory for export to third countries, usually Africa.
Once the ships reach international waters, the tobacco boxes are taken off in high speed boats, and reintroduced to the EU via the Atlantic shores.
We propose the following measures to be taken by the EU:
Identification of all merchant companies responsible for operations in the free zones of the EU (Amberes and Rotterdam);
Exhaustive tracking of the ships used, and therefore also of the crews.
Some of these may have had involvement with previous contraband shipments.
Demonstration of the fact that the ships never arrive at the harbour of destination, or if they do arrive, they do so without the tobacco shipment.
Increased cooperation between the customs authorities investigating such shipments.
In Spain, customs have observed illegal introduction of tobacco by aerial observation and by following sea shipments.
To check the economic circuit in order to track payments made to those who provide illegal tobacco.
To encourage the EU Member States to increase the fight against this fraud. Large
O numbers of police officers must be assigned to the eradication of the problem, and they must be given all necessary technical and other resources.
To encourage the EU Member States to make laws and effective regulations to create sanctions against tobacco contraband, and to make the Courts of Justice aware of appropriate legislations and to enforce sanctions.
To work togethi r with private investigation agencies, if necessary, to put an end the contraband problem.
To maintain contact and sign agreements with tobacco manufacturers and the USA government.
To introduce immediately a labelling system for the transit documents by means of bar-coding.
This will prevent transit documents from being counterfeited.
Please find a report on this bar-coding system in annex III.
We understand that the contraband problem has been studied in depth by the authorities and customs police of each Member State.
However, it is necessary to dedicate more attention and energy to eradicate this problem and to achieve more judicial and police cooperation at the EU level.
Madrid, 12 January 1996
ANNEX II: MEASURES TO BE TAKEN BY THE EUROPEAN UNION IN ORDER TO ELIMINATE TOBACCO CONTRABAND
INTRODUCTION
As tobacco retailers, we can see how the illegal trade of tobacco is competing with the legal trade, creating large losses for legitimate industry and commerce.
In addition, tobacco contraband injures all the State sectors, because State is forgoing enormous amounts of tax revenues as a consequence smuggling.
Contraband has gone from being merely a local problem to being a European one.
It does not only affect the country of destination, because the networks which back this type of crime are by necessity widespread.
The only internal market that is really functioning is the market for organised crime.
The scale of the problem resulting from the large amounts moved by the contraband networks is huge.
Contraband tobacco represents 20% of consumption in Spain and Italy, 15% in Germany and between 5% - 10% in the rest of the European Union countries.
All these figures are increasing rapidly as a consequence of the consolidation and strengthening of the tobacco contraband networks within the European Union.
In total, the Union loses about 750 millions Ecu every year in taxes alone.
In addition to this amount, we must also consider the lost taxes from each country, as well as the "knock-on" effect of the fraud on related commercial sectors (jobs directly and indirectly related to the tobacco sector, such as tobacconists).
In Spain alone, the spread of contraband tobacco products resulted in the loss of the following amounts of tax revenue.
Tobacco contraband is now a problem of European dimensions.
It therefore needs a common solution at a European level.
It needs action from the main institutions of the European Union in order to eradicate this type of fraud.
MEASURES TO ERADICATE TOBACCO CONTRABAND IN EUROPE
EUROPEAN DIRECTIVE TO HOMOGENISE THE LEGISLATION AND REGULATIONS OF MEMBER STATES WITH REGARD TO THE REPRESSION OF CONTRABAND.
In this context, it is necessary to distinguish what different aspects of any regulation should fall within the scope of national legislation and what is required as a common European body of rules.
The creation of special judicial and fiscal offices for the pursuit of these kinds of crimes.
Human and technical resources should be made available for these organisations.
In some countries, like Spain, special regulations already exist for the creation of these kinds of judicial and fiscal offices to counter economic fraud and other corruption, although they are not yet operational.
Harsher penalties for the perpetrators of the contraband and application of the principle which presupposes that the financial resources of the person involved in the contraband has been attained by illegal means.
It would follow that any assets could be seized and frozen by the State until it was demonstrated otherwise.
It is especially important to freeze bank accounts thus paralysing the financing of this illegal business.
There are certain EU countries where the penal system already has harsh penalties for fraud.
In others, however, such as Spain, the modification of the legal system, such as raising of threshold between administrative infraction and actual crime to three million pesetas, has actually benefited those involved in contraband.
Real judicial cooperation between the different Courts of Justice in the European Union.
The contraband networks now have an international structure and territory.
International judicial cooperation is required in order to pursue the perpetrators of the contraband throughout thé territory of the European Union.
B) FISCAL MEASURES Development of a common European customs , endowed with enough technical and human resources to guarantee a legitimate intra-European trade.
We are all aware that nowadays those involved in contraband are taking advantage of the lack of coordination and resources of the old customs regime following the disappearance of internal borders within the EU.
Computerisation of the customs transit system.
This is vital to control the actual time it takes to transport goods.
The computer system is still not in place, due to a lack of budgetary resources.
However, it is vital to realise that the investment required to install the system is small in comparison with the amount of money which the EU is losing on a daily basis at its borders as a result of fraud caused by lack of effective control.
Apart from the figures for Spain quoted in the introduction, UCLAF's own figures indicate a loss of some 10,000 million Ecu in the EU caused by tobacco contraband.
UCLAF's report, published at the beginning of 1995, explained that the cost of financing a central EU computer investigation system at about 9.5 million ECU and about 13 million ECU for each member state.
These amounts are almost negligible when compared to the saving that this protection will produce, estimated by UCLAF at 1.200 million ECU for the first five years alone.
Other complementary advantages derived from the increased protection for the tobacco sectors must also be noted.
The European Union should finance the costs of the customs computerisation programme in the member states with budgetary difficulties so as not to delay the start-up of the customs' control system.
Such alarming figures require a serious economic effort on the part of the European Union in order to start this computer control system.
Action programme of the customs in the EU (Customs 2000).
We understand that the period for application and progressive adaptation is scheduled to run from January 1, 1996 to the year 2000.
We believe this is too long a period.
Due to the importance of the problem of fraud, we believe strict measures to counter this are needed more urgently.
The Customs 2000 programme must be implemented within a shorter time frame and with more concrete measures in view of quantifiable targets.
ADMINISTRATIVE REGULATION
It is necessary to reform international transport and transit conventions, particularly with regard to sensitive goods, such as tobacco, which are subject to frequent fraud and contraband activities within the European area.
Since the disappearance of the internal borders, tobacco contraband continues to exist and is even on the increase through abuse of the suspensive regime of transit merchandise in the EU territory.
Transport of tobacco in the Union's territory should be regulated in order to fight against this kind of generalised fraud by administrative methods.
New regulations should contain the following elements: a) Every transporter who carries tobacco, through the Union's territory, must obtain a special administrative authorisation or an authorisation title issued by the Special Taxes Institution or managing agency from the member state where its headquarters are registered.
In order to become an authorised transporter and to be entered into the Tobacco Transporters' Special Register, the companies must fulfil the following criteria:
They must have a minimum social capital of 2 million Ecu. The financial partners, administrators and staff of the transport company must have certifications showing they have no judicial or administrative track record in subjects relating to economic fraud or contraband which may have occurred in any Member State.
They must be required to deposit a global guarantee of 2 million Ecu with the authorised organism, against any breach or infringement of an EU or national regulation.
International transport companies authorised to transport tobacco should meet the following criteria:
An obligation to have a register book containing all contracts relating to the transport of tobacco in its registered headquarters.
In addition, there should also be a register of the staff employed by or related to the company.
This way, the national police will be capable of taking global action to control the circulation of transport of this nature.
CREATION OF A SPECIAL COMMUNITY POLICE FORCE TO COMBAT CONTRABAND
This would be a police force with its own executive and repressive capacity, free to act in any Member State.
It is constantly demonstrated that national police forces do not pay enough attention to the fight against fraud, as they have other priorities.
Currently, there is an embryonic organisation at the EU level, namely UCLAF.
It is necessary to give UCLAF more technical and personnel means to get results in the fight against contraband.
In addition, the UCLAF Task Forces should be reinforced in their fight against this kind of fraud, by the assignment of effective competences to EUROPOL.
Given the international character of this kind of criminal fraud, this police force should have the competences of a Judicial Police in order to investigate and stop the infractions perpetrated in any Member State.
EFFECTIVE SANCTIONS TO BE APPLIED TO MEMBER STATES WHO FAIL TO EFFECTIVELY FIGHT FRAUD AND CONTRABAND.
The Member States must send a trimestrial report to the European Union which states the measures taken in their fight against the fraud and indicating the following points:
Seizures of illegal merchandise in the territory of the Member State.
Police and security forces, and technical means used in the fight against the contraband.
Number of judicial and administrative proceedings started during the trimester against the contrabandists.
Police analysis of the more common contraband cases, with proposals for international collaboration measures for eradicating the contraband.
DRAFT REPORT ON THE INTRODUCTION OF BAR CODING IN TRANSIT DOCUMENTS
I.- STATEMENT
One of the biggest threats for collection of taxes by customs in the European Union is caused by contraband merchandise circulating in transit in the European territory.
The illegal activity is extremely well organised at an international level, with specialised groups dealing with the different operations, such as buying, creating documents, transport and distribution.
The fraud mechanism works in the following way.
Transit documents (TIR carnet, Tl) are legally created to protect the overland transport of merchandise under suspension of duties between two customs offices.
The receipt, confirming arrival of the goods (Copy nr. 5 of the Tl or the carnet TIR), is given back, meaning that the correct merchandise has indeed arrived, i.e. the transit document is cleared.
Over the last years, at least 2000 abuses of the transit regime have been noted.
The merchandise is diverted in a member state of the EU, usually a country though which the goods must pass in order to arrive at destination.
Stolen or invalis customs stamps have been used, as well as false declarations of destination or goods.
Genuine customs seals have been fraudulently used by bribed customs officials.
This fraud does not only relate to goods coming from outside the European Union, it aiso concerns goods coming from free warehouses inside the Community.
In its document number XXI 1202/94, the Bar Code Committee (Transit Section) has proposed the following interim measure: the introduction of an ad hoc procedure for the resending and ending of transit documents for sensitive goods by means of a specific identification system of such documents.
This report suggests a sure, quick and effective method to prevent fraud with transit documents.
In recent years the bar code control system has become a part of every day life.
Bar codes are not only used in shops and supermarkets, but is increasingly to be found in areas where a high level of security, effectiveness and control is needed, for example, banks, hospitals, etc...
The system is as simple and immediate as sticking on a label, and its worldwide spread is partly due to the fact that it is one of the more profitable identification methods.
The information is codified in a series of numbers, with data not understandable to humans.
A special reader is needed, which decodes and reads the bars automatically, through printers and special labels.
It would only be necessary to stick the label on the transit document (copy 5) as an indication of the presentation of the merchandise to the customs authorities.
In addition, the customs seal can be printed on the same document.
In this way, every transit document is individualised, making it more difficult to steal the customs seals which are used to certify to customs in the country of departure that the merchandise under transit has arrived at the customs in the country of destination.
When copy 5 is sent back to customs in the country of origin, it would only be necessary to check the authenticity or falseness of the document using the bar code reader, which can be done quickly and easily.
This method would only be really effective if there is total security against falsification of the labels.
In order to avoid this possibility, the labels should be sequentially numbered, with alphanumerical control characters obtained by a complex mathematical algorism, which can be modified from time to time.
The numbers are codified in the bar code, invisible to the human eye, rendering the labels readable only by a special bars code reader.
IV.- POSSIBLE IMPROVEMENTS
The advantages of this mechanism:
The surprise effect will contribute to disorientate the contraband organisations, obliging them to change their strategy.
This will take time and effort.
The people at customs in charge of clearing the transit documents must be made responsible, because the labels, perfectly numerated and identified, will be under their control and care.
The disappearance of labels will be relatively easy to check and they could be cancelled immediately.
If labels with bar codes are used, which are almost impossible to counterfeit, the current problem of falsified seals to certify the arrival of goods would be eliminated.
When copy 5 with the bar-coded label arrives at the Customs office of departure, officials will be able to verify immediately the authenticity of the label by means of the barcode reader.
This will reduce the time it takes to discover if fraud has occurred.
This will enable the customs authorities to take action immediately, such as blocking deposits or preventing further shipments with similar fraudulent labels.
This system provides the possibility of progressive and graded introduction.
In this way, the system can be introduced as a pilot or experimental project at certain customs offices in order to verify its efficiency at low cost.
A bar-coded label will also allow the storage of other useful information, such as the nature of the merchandise, the qualifications of the companies concerned or those of the transporter.
Bar codes could also be used in other customs documents where it is important to secure the authenticity of the contents.
For example, the bar codes could be used in Bail Certificates, where the Customs Guarantee seal must appear.
In conclusion, although this is a preliminary report, it can also be viewed as a first step in resolving the problems of the correct completion of transit of sensitive merchandise.
This report should be followed up with a wider and more detailed study of the possibilities that the bar coding system offers for the prevention of fraud.
THE ADVANTAGES OF THE SYSTEM
VIABILITY The bar code is independent of the person who introduces the data because it is an automatic method of recording data.
SECURITY The control digit is calculated by means of a mathematical formula specifically made for this application, so the interpretation of the data can only be made by a computer system.
If incorrect information is introduced, the system will block.
OPERATIONAL. The operation system is as immediate and simple as sticking a label.
ECONOMIC The bar code's use is almost universal because it is an affordable system of recording data which can be implemented quickly.
EXTENSIVE This system can be extended to different customs centres, depending on the need.
Each customs centre could have a different identification code.
CAPABLE OF BEING MODIFIED In order to make falsification of the labels more difficult, the algorithm used to calculate the control digit would be changed from time to time.
INVISIBILITY All the information will be codified in a bar code, which is incomprehensible to humans.
This code will be covered with a black ink that will conceal any visual signal.
AUTOMATED The codification and reading system will be automatic, being performed by means of printers and special labels, using special bar code readers.
OBJECTIVE: CONTROLLING IN REAL TIME, WITH NO MISTAKES, AVOIDING DUPLICATIONS AND FALSIFICATIONS IN THE CUSTOMS DOCUMENTS.
SPECIFICATIONS OF THE LABELS
The labels should be sequentially numbered, with a total of 13 characters in order to render the idenfication system both operational and impossible to falsify: for example:
"a a a" corresponds to the customs office that issues the document (maximum of 999 Customs); "χ χ χ χ χ χ χ x" are eight digits indicating the document number.
The documents are sequentially numbered from 1 to 99,999,999; "c c" corresponds to 2 alphanumerical characters attained by a mathematic algorithm.
These are control characters (similar to, for example, the Spanish system of Fiscal Identification Numbers).
In addition, these numbers will be codified in a bar code, invisible to the human eye, and readable only by a special bar code reader.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSPORT SYSTEM
Notice to members
Please find attached the contribution submitted by the Conseil National du Patronat Français (CNPF), Paris, to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transport System.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
