Please find enclosed a preliminary general document by the CNPF on fraud in Community transit.
Our adviser on customs matters, Mr Claude Merlin (Tel.
(closing formula and signature)
Enc.: 2 pages
DOC EN\CM\290\290954
Comments for the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into fraud in Community transit
I. General remarks
The customs procedure for Community transit is an important factor in the smooth flow of goods within the single market.
It is an essential complement to the policy of local customs clearance at the headquarters of import and export firms, which has been particularly highly developed in France and which is very popular with commerce, industry and haulage contractors.
It helps to maintain equality of administrative treatment between operators established in different Member States, leaving them free to choose the place of customs clearance, either at the frontier or inside the European Union, and also helps, among other things, to prevent an accentuation of the logistical advantages of bringing goods through the North Sea ports.
The CNPF is, of course, concerned at the crisis situation in which the Community transit procedure currently finds itself (as does that of TIR - international road transport) as a result of the increase in fraud, and is anxious to contribute to the search for solutions which are not only effective, but also in line with the needs of businesses.
Avenues to be explored
The present system of Community transit is based on administrative links based on 'paper' documents between the customs administrations of departure and arrival.
The computerization of the procedure which has been initiated by the Commission should help these communications to be made more rapidly and in greater security.
The CNPF can only encourage this development.
However, it must be ensured that the use of information technology for the essential purpose of combating the fraud which is a drain on the budgets of the Community and the Member States does not increase the financial and administrative burden which has to be borne by businesses.
With a view to ensuring better compliance with customs obligations, Community transit users could also be compelled to use modern anti-theft techniques providing constant surveillance of the movement of vehicles and goods.
The Community's internal borders have been abolished as far as movement of goods and economic transactions are concerned, but they continue to exist in the area of customs.
A (supranational) Community monitoring system could be designed which would allow specific measures to be taken in this area, where continuity of surveillance is particularly necessary.
There are discrepancies between Member States in the rules governing customs procedures and sanctions, which are intolerable within a single market.
It is unacceptable that operators in identical situations should be subject to different controls and sanctions within a procedure which should be regulated by the Community alone.
The European Union should place Community transit among its highest priorities for harmonization of customs powers and sanctions.
It should be easy to carry out an analysis in this area, which could be used as a basis for uniform practices.
DOC_EN\CM\290\290954
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
(translation)
Subject: Our letter of 29 February 1996 on the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Dear Mrs Wemheuer,
On 29 February 1996 we wrote to you with our views.
Unfortunately, we have since discovered that page 9 of the text was not printed in full.
Please find enclosed a complete version of our opinion; we should be grateful if the interests of industry using the transit system could be taken into account in further considerations.
(closing formula and signature)
The EC COMMUNITY TRANSIT Procedure
February 1996
Summary
Industry and the public administration rely on an effective and reliable transit procedure.
However, the basic principles of the EC transit procedure - which is biased towards the civil servants - cannot be maintained if, as a result of changing circumstances (in particular, damage resulting from criminal activities), these principles increasingly have a detrimental effect on industry alone, i.e. the principals elements in the transit procedure, thereby raising questions about its acceptability as a customs procedure.
Immediate action is required, mainly because of the increase in criminal activities: remission of duties following incidents of fraud in the past; secure procedures in future through new ruóles under customs law and more effective procedures.
If there is clear evidence of criminal activity on the part of third parties, it is unacceptable that action should be taken immediately and solely against the principals, thereby indirectly facilitating and encouraging crime.
The attractiveness of the transit procedure has already sunk dramatically: a failure to take the effective action which we have been calling for for years would have an adverse effect on the credibility of this customs procedure and hence on Community customs law in general.
The associations of forwarding agents have put forward numerous proposals in the past, and there is a willingness to take part in further talks.
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
Industry and the public administration rely on an effective and reliable transit procedure.
However, the basic principles of the EC transit procedure - which is biased towards the civil servants - cannot be maintained if, as a result of changing circumstances (in particular, damage resulting from criminal activities), these principles increasingly have a detrimental effect on industry alone, i.e. the principals elements in the transit procedure, thereby raising questions about its acceptability as a customs procedure.
Immediate action is required, mainly because of the increase in criminal activities: remission of duties following incidents of fraud in the past; secure procedures in future through new rules under customs law and more effective procedures.
If there is clear evidence of criminal activity on the part of third parties, it is unacceptable that action should be taken immediately and solely against the principals, thereby indirectly facilitating and encouraging crime.
The attractiveness of the transit procedure has already sunk dramatically: a failure to take the effective action which we have been calling for for years would have an adverse effect on the credibility of this customs procedure and hence on Community customs law in general.
B. Importance of the Community transit procedure and how it works
C. Shortcomings in the transit procedure
Preliminary remarks The return copy procedure Fraud by third parties Current forms of fraud Less frequent checks
D. Waiver of duties following incidents of fraud in the past
Rethink Changes in customs legislation a) b) c) d) e) f)
Changes to the way the transit procedure is organized a) b) c) d)
Computerizing the transit procedure Provision for risks within companies Changes in the declaration form New deadlines on completion of the procedure
F. Conclusions
A Introduction: Increasing risks in the international transport of goods
Reports of problems in goods transport within Europe and in the regions immediately adjacent to Europe have become more frequent over a number of years.
The transport industry is faced with almost insuperable problems.
Frequent occurrences of the following incidents have been noted in recent years: theft of lorries and cargo, with drivers often being threatened, mistreated and blackmailed; persons in disguise masquerading as officials ; forgery of certificates, stamps and customs receipts; deliberate and quite specific fraud in the Community transit procedure and the TIR system; offences at collection points, lorry parks and in the clearance procedures at customs posts; greater use of bribes, and larger sums involved.
These incidents are occurring so frequently as to constitute a threat, in themselves, to international transactions.
Another frequent aggravating fact is that in many countries crimebusting action is taken which, while laudable in itself, tends to put restrictions on goods transport in general.
The following are examples : direct or indirect pressure to use certain lorry parks or service stations where a fee is payable, and a reduction in the number of 'free' lorry parks and customs offices; administrative requirements, or insistence on additional securities for the transport of certain goods which are at risk of theft and subject to high customs duties or consumer taxes; timeconsuming setting up of long convoys of vehicles between fixed points, e.g. at the border.
Taken individually these measures are perfectly logical, and could prove effective in the fight against fraud, but taken as a whole they necessarily jeopardize and impose unacceptable constraints on international transactions.
Β Importance of the Community transit procedure and how it works
The advantages, and also the problems of the Community transit procedure, must be seen against this background.
The Community procedure and the TIR carnet are the best-known procedures of suspension of customs duties without which the smooth international transport of goods would not be possible.
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
Without this procedure every crossing of a border would necessarily involve customs clearance, e.g. a national transit procedure or clearance for home use.
As far as the customs authorities are concerned, this would involve an extreme concentration of staff at external frontiers.
Realistically, it would be impossible to create the infrastructure needed, bearing in mind the funding required and the problems which have been occurring for some time at borders. Fast and cost-effective transit clearance of goods transported internationally is inconceivable without the transit procedure.
This is the main reason why the industry is interested in it.
In broad terms the system operates as follows. At the beginning of the process the person making the customs declaration (the 'declarant' pursuant to Article 4, no.
18 of the Customs Code [CC] ) must declare the goods presented at the office of departure with a transit declaration (T1 or T2 form) and must deposit a security (in cash or absolute suretyship of a third person), the amount of which depends on the duty payable on the goods.
Once the formalities have been completed by the office of departure (i.e. checking and recording the declaration; setting the deadline for presentation at the office of destination, establishing the identity of the goods, return of completed sheets 2, 3 and 4 to the declarant), the goods are transported - possibly via customs offices en route at EU external frontiers - to the point of destination, where the Community transit procedure comes to an end (i.e. presentation of the goods, submission and checking of the transit document, inspection and identification of the goods).
The actual purpose of the transit procedure is, therefore, to shift the collection of duties and taxes from the external frontier to the interior. Hence the need to ensure that the imported goods are actually declared in the interior at customs, i.e. the office of destination.
According to Article 96 of the Code, the principal, as the holder of the procedure, is responsible for ensuring that the goods are actually represented at the office of destination.
If he fails to do so, a customs debt on importation is incurred pursuant to Article 203(1) of the Code.
The principal's risk
The risk - virtually incalculable - to the principal is that he guarantees to the customs authorities the due completion of a process over the course of which he has, in fact, no direct control.
The forwarding agent who assumes liability, as principal, for customs transit is usually only the person organizing the transport.
The actual transport to the office of destination is, by contrast, carried out by another person (in legal terms, the 'carrier'). Hence, during transport the goods are completely taken out of the principal's power.
The situation is different only if the forwarding agent performs the transport with his own vehicles and using his own employees (i.e. on his own account).
However, this is becoming increasingly rare because of the growing trend towards the division of labour in international transport.
If we accept the argument that all parties involved - businesses and customs authorities - need the transit procedure, the inescapable conclusion is that in contrast to the original intention the risk should not be borne by one party alone.
In Germany some 70% of all transit procedures are initiated by forwarding agents as principals, almost all of whom use - for the purpose of speeding up the transport of third party goods - simplified procedures permitted under customs legislation, and this takes considerable pressure off the administration.
This section deals with the most obvious shortcomings in the transit procedure.
However, both the serious shortcomings in the return copy procedure used within the customs administration and the increase in criminal action by third parties are increasingly jeopardizing the procedure, and hence the principle underlying the procedure.
The return copy procedure is, of course, the only procedure as proof of completion of the transit procedure and hence of discharging the principal of his responsibilities. The underlying principle that the customs authorities retain control over the procedure to absolve the principle of his responsibilities is comprehensible from the point of view of the customs administration.
However, the inadequacies that are repeatedly observed and the complaints that are repeatedly made to the authorities about the shortcomings in the behaviour of customs offices (which are clearly in violation of Community law) have not
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
resulted in any change in the law.
Organized crime is acting in an increasingly intelligent, but increasingly brazen, brutal and sophisticated way.
Making the principal bear full responsibility means that the administration has a claim on the principal under customs law, while the principal - who has himself been defrauded - is liable to pay the duties defrauded by third parties.
Customs law ignores the fact that the principal has also been defrauded, i.e, that it is the criminal behaviour of a third party which has forced the principal into the role of customs debtor. The principal - who has played no part in the fraud - is held no less responsible, with the result that the principle underlying the transit procedure is increasingly being questioned.
The statement by the EC Commission (answer dated 3 May 1994 to written question E-411/94 of 2 March 1994, OJ C 362, 19.12.1994, p.
Fraud is mainly concerned with not completing transit procedures started in good faith by the principal, or with faking due completion of the process.
stamping of transit documents by criminals in (customs) uniforms using stolen or forged official seals; customs officials stamping documents 'as a favour' under pressure from criminals or as active bribery.
Depending on what is involved it takes weeks - in some cases years - for the offence to be detected, with the result that the prescribed customs duties and taxes are then collected, which again sometimes takes years if the statutory deadlines are observed.
This changed in the late 1980s: the obligation to surrender the transit advice note at the EC's internal frontiers ceased to apply on 1 July 1990 following an EC Regulation dating from 22 February of that year; political revolution in the former Eastern Bloc countries, with an increase in the number of transit procedures within the EU; complete removal of all checks at the EU's internal frontiers on 1 January 1993; reduction in the number of customs posts in the EU and reduction in the staff employed by customs authorities.
In other words, less energy has to be expended to engage in criminal activities.
The situation is aggravated by the fact that in the same period there have been further deteriorations in the return copy procedure used internally by customs authorities as a major check on completion of procedures. The authorities have not been able to compensate for the more infrequent checks by actually improving the return copy procedure.
The conclusion is not that certain developments and decisions should be reversed, but that if developments occur which have not only advantages for the administration and the users but also disadvantages, these disadvantages should not be passed on solely to the users of the transit procedure, i.e. the principals. The procedure itself must be tailored to changing circumstances.
The principal becomes a customs debtor by virtue of the fact that he initiates a transit procedure in his name which is abused by criminal third parties and in the light of current customs legislation this abuse leads to him having to
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
pay duties. It is the frequent and increasing action of intelligent economic criminals which is harming the transit procedure.
Estimates and information supplied by individual firms indicate claims Community-wide of at least ECU 3 billion.
Initial results of a BSL survey have confirmed this.
In individual cases firms have been asked to pay more than DM 30 million.
Anyone who understands the problems that enforcing these claims involves for the firms and guarantors concerned (e.g. bankruptcy and job losses), must consider unconventional methods: more specifically, a general remission of duty payable as a result of fraud in the past.
This is, of course, a difficult decision for the authorities to take, but if the assumption is that a transit procedure has advantages not only for businesses but also for the authorities, it is quite reasonable to ask whether the disadvantages of the process should be borne solely by businesses.
Are not the customs authorities obliged to give some thought to the effect on the process of the conditions they lay down?
The conclusion from recent discussions is that there are three problem areas which make remission difficult: the concept of the declarant, the rules in the CC on customs debt and the nature of the duties.
But if the nature of the process has changed so much, is it possible to maintain today the principles which were laid down when the process was devised and which gave satisfaction to all concerned for so long, provided the shortcomings were minor, the frequency of checks effective and hence the incidence of fraud much lower than it has been since the beginning of the 1990s?
Are changes to customs law now necessary and justified?
It is important to remember that the effectiveness of a customs procedure depends in part on whether it is acceptable to the declarant.
Is it right that, in the wake of obvious and proven abuse of a customs procedure by third parties, the declarant - the victim of fraud - is forced through strict application of customs law into bankruptcy, which in turn will lead to a loss of tax and social insurance revenue and of jobs?
Can the administration afford to facilitate, indirectly or directly, the criminal behaviour of third parties so that by invoking valid customs law they claim compensation only from the principal, who has played no part in the fraud?
We are convinced that the way in which the community tackles fraud will also have an impact on the future activities of forwarding firms.
The idea of withdrawing from services involving transit procedures as principal - which is no longer a theoretical possibility but one which is already being discussed in many firms - because of the increasing and incalculable risk of fraud by third parties and because of the absence of any mechanism to protect the principal would bring cross-border goods transport - and hence Germany's external trade -to a halt.
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
E New rules for the future
Rethink
In view of the way the procedure operates and its advantages to the authorities and industry, the changes which have already been discussed should now lead to a rethink of the concept.
The original principles which operated for years to the satisfaction of all concerned can no longer be applied.
A rethink should involve: changes to customs legislation; changes to the way in which the procedure is organized.
Although the distinction between these two is often fluid, it will be maintained as a way of highlighting and solving the problems.
Changes in customs legislation
The process needs to be redefined to make it more secure and more predictable for users; the rights and obligations of all parties concerned, and the parties themselves, must also be redefined.
Measures:
Community customs legislation needs to be amended in certain respects. A number of examples are given below.
a) No demand for payment following offences committed by third parties
The customs authorities must give full or partial remission for incomplete transit procedures, if principals can prove that they have not acted negligently in making out the transit certificate but that they have been defrauded by a third party.
This is something which the European forwarding agents' associations have been calling for for more than twelve years.
Under no circumstances should enforcement of demands for duties lead to the liquidation of enterprises.
It would be aberrant to magnify the loss of import duties following abuse of transit procedures by a loss of revenue - and a loss of jobs - which the liquidation of enterprises would entail.
Article 900(1) of the Regulation implementing the Community CC should therefore be amended as follows:
Import duties shall be repaid or remitted where:
(p) enforcement of the claim would lead to insolvency of the party liable.' b) Reassessment of persons liable
Article 203(3) of the CC, which is the basis for claims against debtors in the event of failure to complete the transit procedure, gives a rank order of debtors.
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
It needs to be clearly spelled out that this rank order must be strictly complied with, i.e. that a debtor further down the chain can only be held liable if attempts to enforce claims against a debtor further up the chain have failed.
Paragraph 3 mentions in the first place the person who removes the goods from customs supervision. This makes sense.
It is in line with the traditions of administrative law and the general principle of justice, to make the first claim against the person who is responsible, through his actions, for a customs debt.
Applied to the transit procedure, this is the person actively committing fraud (or his backers) who fails to comply with his obligation to go to the office of destination to present the goods.
The principal is mentioned last in Article 203(3) and this, too, is logical.
Article 213 of the CC should therefore be amended to include the following:
A debtor lower down the list in the Code shall not be held liable unless enforcement against a debtor further up the list has proved fruitless.' c) Change of jurisdiction
Even though at first sight Article 203 of the CC establishes what appears to be a favourable ranking for the principal, this is of little use to him since the current legal situation and administrative practice are such that if the driver responsible, or any other customs debtor, is registered abroad the authorities take the line of least resistance and claim directly against the principal.
The justification for this lies in Article 213 of the CC and inadequacies in mutual administrative assistance when enforcing claims in other countries.
This approach takes too little account of the principal who is obliged to try to claim compensation abroad, in accordance with the legal procedure of the country in question, from the driver or other persons involved in the fraud.
Attempts by the principal to seek recourse through the courts in another country are usually financially pointless (in some countries the plaintiff has to pay his costs even if he wins his case) and extremely difficult for practical reasons (language problems, finding a specialist lawyer, translations etc.).
How much easier it would be for the European administration which would only have to establish a properly functioning system of administrative assistance.
As described above, difficulties are also involved in bringing a claim against a German principal by a foreign country before a foreign court.
A solution to the problem lies in changing the jurisdiction rules.
It would be sensible, and acceptable to all parties concerned, to shift jurisdiction to the customs authority of the Member State where the principal has his registered offices.
Article 378(1) of the Regulation implementing the CC should therefore be amended as follows:
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
'Where the consignment has not been presented at the point of destination, the offence shall be deemed to have been committed in the Member State where the principal has his registered offices.' d) Remission in the event of dereliction of duty
In effect the offsetting of the principal's claim against the administration's claim for duties results in remission for the principal.
Accordingly, there can be no doubt of the existence of a claim for state liability.
Article 900(1) of the Regulation implementing the Community CC should therefore be amended to include the following:
(q) officials or employees of the customs authorities, through violating their official duty, have contributed to making debtors out of principals acting in good faith'.
The principal should be allowed a similar claim if, as a result of negligence, the internal return copy procedure fails to operate in the due manner and the return copy arrives at the office of departure either too late or not at all (see C.2 above).
Absence of a return copy may indicate abuse by third parties to the detriment of the principal.
Officials of the office of destination are obliged, pursuant to Article 356(2) of the Regulation implementing the CC, to ensure correct handling of the return copy; this requirement is therefore an official duty towards the principal.
The principal should be compensated for the damage suffered as a result of violation of this duty, i.e. no duties should be levied.
Article 900(1) of the Regulation implementing the Community CC should therefore be amended to include the following:
(r) the customs authority has failed to comply with its obligations, in particular with regard to carrying out the return copy or inquiry procedures, and the principal has suffered damage as a result.'
DOC EN\CM\294\294888
The following letter c) should therefore be added to Article 380 of the Regulation implementing the CC:
'c) by the production of a receipt made out by the customs authorities.' f) Proof of regularity of a transit operation
The principal must therefore have the opportunity of furnishing proof - in the form of his own documents, e.g. commercial invoices, delivery notes, payment receipts etc. - of regular completion of specific processes.
(a) by the production of a document (five words deleted) establishing that the goods in question were presented at the office of destination or, where Article 406 applies, to the authorized consignee.
Changes to the way the transit procedure is organized
Procedures need to be introduced to enhance transparency between industry and the authorities, to make fraud difficult and to help detect instances of fraud more rapidly.
Improving communications, printed forms etc., e.g. by using modern technology; examples are given below.
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
a) Computerizing the transit procedure
Computerization of the procedure - handling the return copy form - has been under discussion since the early 1980s, but none of the plans has materialized.
Since 1994 there have been increasing signs that the EC Commission wants to computerize the entire procedure from providing the guarantee and application to final completion.
In addition, computer messages between the applicant, his guarantors, the office of departure and the office of destination etc. would replace the current paper procedures.
The stated aim, and the essential condition for industry's consent, is not only to speed up and simplify the procedure, but also to use computers in the fight against fraud in customs procedures.
Initial steps were made to inform industry some time ago; the exchange of information must be continued.
b) Provision for risks within companies
Companies themselves could also take action, of course.
For example, a better choice of transport firms to be used, additional securities for principals, customs and other parties concerned etc.
Refusal to issue a transit document is another possibility.
Measures like this have been practised by companies for a number of years.
For example, many companies have precise internal rules on the cases in which, and the conditions under which, transit procedures can be opened.
Provision for risks within companies is necessary and it will not be neglected in future; however, it is no longer sufficient as a means of providing effective protection from the consequences of criminal activity.
Internal measures by companies and action by the police are not in themselves sufficient to fight fraud; new legislation is needed, along with a rethink of the way the process is organized.
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
One obvious example would be to include on the form the name of the company taking over the goods and the transit document from the principal and the inclusion of an obligation on the part of that company.
The additional possibility of the administration granting remission to the principal (see E.2f above) could be insured by including in the printed form a sheet which could act as alternative proof pursuant to Article 380 of the Regulation implementing the CC.
d) New deadlines on completion of the procedure
The offices of destination and departure also have a major role to play in detecting irregularities.
If the return copy does not reach the office of departure no later than ten weeks after completion of the deadline for presentation, the office asks the principal (or his representative or any other person involved in the transit) for the whereabouts of the goods, with a request for a reply.
If the transit document cannot be completed after this period, the office of departure sends an inquiry notice to the office of destination about four months after the despatch note has been made out.
In many cases the ten-week period is not complied with, and the result is that transit procedures are initiated in good faith which would not be initiated if a request were made to the principal by the due date.
Because of the protective function of this period, it must be possible to reduce the ten weeks at the request of the principal.
The rules on carrying out the inquiry procedure - currently in the national service instructions - should be included as new rules in Title II, Chapter 4 of the Regulation implementing the CC. By way of clarification it should be pointed out that the periods in question also serve to protect the principal.
If non-compliance with the ten-week period causes the principal to suffer damage, no claims for duty may be made against him by virtue of violation of official duty (see E.2c above).
DOC_EN\CM\294\294888
However, if the conclusion is that customs authorities themselves increase the principal's risk because of shortcomings in the administration's internal return copy procedure, the principal's risk increases even further because of the greater incidence of fraud by third parties, the principal himself has no way of furnishing proof or of preventing fraud through any in-house measures, and the principal suffers damages from events beyond his control, the authorities must take account of these fundamental changes and there must be a willingness to change the legal rules for the customs procedure, the original intention of which, and the way it is implemented, are being questioned.
A major change in the conditions for a customs procedure must also lead to changes in the procedure itself.
This paper has set out, and provided reasons for, appropriate proposals.
If action is not taken soon, the criminals will have achieved their objective: irreparable damage to industry and the administration, coupled with a catastrophe for firms operating reasonably in the international exchange of goods.
To prevent this, everyone must cooperate in bringing about changes; the Bundesverband Spedition und Lagerei e.V. is, as always, prepared to play its part.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by The Danish Freight Forwarders Association (Dans Speditorforening), Copenhagen.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
The European Parliament Att.: Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
The Danish Freight Fonvarders Association Scandiagade 15 DK-2450 Copenhagen SV
At the request of Mr. Freddy Blak, MEP, and member of the Committee of Inquiry into the Com munity Transit System we hereby forward an account of the points of view of the Danish Freight forwarders Association concerning the problems recorded in connection with transit fraud within t'r.e EU under the current Community Transit Procedure.
The stipulations governing the procedure of community transit are found in Council Regulation No. S42 of IS March 1969 with subsequent amendments.
The regulation has.subsequently been elaborated by several regulations find decisions from the Commission, most recently by Commission Regulation (EEC) No. 2454/93 of 2 July 1993 on Certain Implementation Provisions to Council Regulation (EEC) No, 2913/92 on the Introduction of a Community Customs Code.
As an introduction we wish to state that it is the opinion of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association that access to carry out transportation under the current regulations is of decisive and Yital im portance not only for the transport and forwarding trade, but also for all exporting trades.
The replies given below relate to the written questions made by Mr. Freddy Blak during a meeting on 8 March 1996 for the purpose of the further deliberation on the problems by the committee.
Is it in tii3 experience of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association that there is a rise in the occurrence of fraud in connection with the transit procedure in recent years?
When?
What are the circumstances causing this rise in the opinion of the Danish Freight For warders Association?
Reply: In the opinion of the Danish· Freight Forwarders Association there has been a marked rise in the number of occurrences or' fraud in the recent two-three years.
In the opinion of the Association there is an exact connection between the start of the in ner market as of 1 January 1993 and the rise in the occurence of fraud.
The reason for this is that in connection with the establishment of the inner market there was simultaneously - and as a natural consequence of this - a discontinuation of the control measures and customs procedures at frontier posts previously in force.
In Denmark the Danish Freight Forwarders Association administers the current procedure for community transportation.
Through this administration the Association currently receives notifications under the pro cedure and to the Association it is clear that the majority of fraud incidents are caused by external economic crime.
In the opinion and experience of the Association in connection with the administration of the procedure it is quite obvious that the established fraud may quite essentially be as cribed to criminal elements who do not have any direct affiliation with the transport and forwarding trade.
What kind of persons have typically been involved in the incidents of fraud which the Danish Freight Forwarders Association has so far come across (e.g. transport firms, individual drivers, import/export firms, customs officers, organised crime...)?
Reply: As stated above in our reply to question 1 it is the obvious opinion of the Association that the essential majority of fraud with transportation can be ascribed to organised crime.
In connection with the organised crime it is a matter of course that this crime will, to some degree, include hauliers and their drivers to the extent that the criminal elements do not al ready dispose of the necessary means of transpon,
The Association is familiar only with one incident where an employee of a member of the Association contributed to the transportation fraud.
What methods are most often used to carry out the fraud in connection with the transit procedure?
What degree of judicial/technical forgery is necessary to be a "transit fraud ster"?
To Illustrate this circumstance we can mention that the final handling of the T-document in the majority of all cases does not take place until nearly one year after the actual com pletion of the shipments.
This slow administration of the transit procedure is quite obviously a reason for criminal elements to get the opportunity to commit the fraud.
Therefore a marked tightening should be made of the final handling of the T-documents by the authorities.
In the opinion of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association, how much profit is lost by the member countries and '.he EU because of the transit fraud?
Reply: It is not possible for the Danish Freight Forwarders Association to give an exact idea of the extent of the fraud.
According to the information received by the Association from the European Freight For warders Association CLECAT in Brussels the present estimate is that the claim for pay ment of customs and duties comes to an amount in excess of 2 billion ECU.
How often is fraud connected with community policies (e.g, export refund of agricultural goods, preferred trade agreements)?
Reply: The established fraud as a consequence of external organised crime is, in the opinion of the Association, a consequence of the establishment of the inn;r market.
The establishment of the inner market has resulted in a vast numbei of positive consequences fore the export trades and the transport and forwarding trade.
However, at the same time we have to record the occurrence of a number of unfortunate side effects.
Fraud with goods occurs within a wide range of commodities, and it will not be possible to state specifically what commodity groups are typically involved.
However, heavily taxed goods such as cigarenes, alcohol, perfumeries, and restitution goods-as butter, meet etc. would be typical of the kinds of articles Involved in fraud.
In the opinion of the Association, however, it is important that also the heavily taxed goods and restitution goods may in future be transported under the current procedure.
These commodity groups are a natural link in the traditional commercial and consumer patterns and a large number of trades would suffer Irreparable damage if these commodity groups were to be exempted from the transit procedure.
In the opinion of the Association it is also considered unworthy for a community founded on the rule of law if organised economic crime would lead to a situation where certain commodity groups cannot be transported within the Community.
Reply: As stated above in the reply to question 5 a vast number of goods are affected by fraud.
However, it is obvious that particularly heavily taxed goods and restitution goods are es pecially exposed to fraud.
The reason for this is that in case of fraud with these goods the profit is very high, and also these goods are typically easily marketed,
However, fraud has also been recorded with audiovisual equipment, radios, TVs, CD-players, chemical products, medical products, and computers and other IT equipment.
Which system is more exposed to fraud, the transit procedure or the TIR procedure?
Reply: In the opinion of the Association it is not possible to determine which cransport type is the more exposed to fraud.
On the part of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association, however, we have tried to limit the possibilities of fraud under the Community Transit Procedure as much as possible.
This has been done by warning the members of the Association of the increasing fraud and encourage users of the procedure to be particularly alert in cases where the transportation concerns sensitive goods.
Does the Danish Freight Forwarders Association find that the documentation system for administration of the transit system is suitable/sufficient?
Reply: In the opinion of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association the administration of the Community Transit Procedure is far from optimal.
This is first of all due to the fact that between national authorities and the organisations administering the procedure there is no mutual cooperation on the administration of the procedure.
Secondly, to the knowledge of the Association, there is no sufficiently close cooperation between authorities in the individual countries, including the Commission.
In the opinion of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association the problem can be solved only if cooperation is established between authorities, organisations and users of the pro cedure nationally and internationally.
To illustrate this we can state that a carriage from Denmark to any destination within Europe will on an average take two to four days, depending on the destination and on concrete circumstances.
On arrival to the country of destination the T-document must be stamped by the local authorities and copy No. 5 of the T-document must be returned to the customs office of departure in Denmark.
This procedure should not take more than 10-14 days.
However, it turns out that a period of 10-12 months often passes before the copy No. 5 of the T-document appears at the customs office of departure.
Such a procedure is quite unacceptable and is one or' the main reasons why fraud with transportation takes place.
Add to this the fact that between national authorities a formalised procedure of reminding has been established, according to which the customs office of departure will remind the customs office of arrival of the T-document after 4, 8 and 10 months, and it is quite obvious that the authorities do not trust each other and do not live by the customs code.
Is the sealing used to secure shipments sufficiently "fraud-proof"?
Reply: The Danish Freight Forwarders Association is not aware of any instances there the seal has been broken.
How often go European customs authorities effect physical control of goods in transit? (an approximate figure of percentage would be sufficient)
Reply: The Association does not have any concrete knowledge of the extent of physical control by the authorities of goods in transit, but if the Committee of Inquiry should so wish, we are prepared to make an investigation among our members.
How often are vehicles ordered to follow predetermined routes?
How, if applicable, is this controlled?
Reply: Only in extremely rare incidences will authorities order a vehicle to follow a pre determined route.
The only possibility of controlling whether a vehicle has actually followed the route is through satellite surveillance or by making a passage of controlled frontier posts.
How often do customs authorities insist that individual or flatrate guarantees be used in transit operations with heavily taxed goods?
Reply: In accordance with the current customs code guarantees may be made either in the form of individual guarantee, comprehensive guarantee, or flatrate guarantee.
The procedure administered by the Danish Freight Forwarders Association merely applies to flatrate guarantee in which connection the carrier hands out the required number of se curity documents of 7,000 ECU.
Under the implementation regulations Article 368(2) the authorities have to check in each individual case that the correct number of security documents be used to cover any claims for customs and taxes.
In the experience of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association the authorities are far too careless in checking that the correct number of documents is used.
To a large degree, authorities will accept the number of documents handed oyer to the customs office of departure without ex officio checking whether the number or" documents is sufficient.
Do Danish insurance companies show any disapproval of insuring carriers' guarantee obligations under the transit procedure?
Reply: Since Denmark entered the EEC on 1 January 1973 the Danish Freight Forwarders Association has administered the procedure in Denmark and covered the procedure in terms of Insurance with the same Insurance company over the entire period.
As of lately, the insurance company has raised doubts about a continuation of the procedure, particularly in so far as concerns sensitive goods.
In the opinion of the Association the consequences of a rejection of insurance cover would be that forwarding agents and carriers would have to discontinue the implementation of transport of special commodity groups.
In the opinion of the Association this would be a disaster for all exporting trades in the Community.
Therefore a procedure should be established with the EU which will secure national authorities and the EU against losses but at the same time provide the opportunity for trade and industry to export goods, including the implementation of transportation within the EU,
Have any Danish transport firms had to close down because they haye had to pay for their guarantee obligations?
Reply: On the face of it the Association is net aware of any Danish carriers or forwarding agents having wound up as a direct consequence of claims under the guarantee scheme.
Possibly, this is merely due to fortunate and fortuitous circumstances.
The transport trade is already hard hit financially, and it is therefore possible that claims may arise which may lead to firms winding up.
Therefore, as mentioned earlier, It is important that measures be taken very early by the EU so that a system may be established that will enable the implementation of transportation without forcing the individual undertaking to provide guarantees to the authorities in instances where fraud has taken place as a consequence of external economic crime.
How often does the guarantee fully cover customs and excise duties for a given shipment?
Reply: Through intensive consultation at meetings and in the form of circular letters from the Association the Danish Freight Forwarders Association has encouraged users of the pro cedure to ensure that in case of implementation of :he individual transportation the correct number of security documents be used.
The guarantee provided will therefore provide full cover in the cases known to the Association. ciation.
However, the Association has in fact recorded that the authorities do not ensure that the sufficient number of documents are used.
In the majority of cases where the guarantee did not cover the reason has been that the authorities just accepted the number of documents handed over by the user without checking whether the number was in accordance with the number of documents which de facto had to be used.
How many cases are known to the Danish Freight Forwarders Association where persons who have committed fraud have been revealed and where the outstanding duties have been paid?
Reply: The formulation of the present insurance scheme in Denmark implies that the insurance company will have an obligation to pay unless the user of the scheme has acted in gross negligence or intentionally.
Consequently, any outstanding amounts of customs and duties will as a main rule be paid in any event.
Are the customs authorities sufficiently careful in their examination of cases related to irregularities under the transit procedure?
Do they claim payment of the guarantee by the agent too early (i.e. when it is still possible to recover the customs amount by other means)?
How efficient is the cooperation between Denmark and the other member countries to the knowledge of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association?
Reply: As previously stated a closer cooperation, also between authorities, would be of invaluable importance.
However, it is the opinion of the Association that Danish customs authorities do not sufficiently cooperate in order to prevent fraud or for the subsequent solution of it.
Does the Danish Freight Forwarders Association have any suggestions for improvement of the transit procedure?
Reply: In the opinion of the Danish Freight Forwarders Association any improvement of the transit procedure would necessitate that the national authorities would ensure that the sufficient number of security documents be used, In addition to this it is vital for the system that feedback from customs offices of arrival will be considerably faster than what is presently the case.
The Association is aware that the Commission (DG XXI) is working on the establishment of an on-line system between national authorities.
Such a system would no doubt be ex tremely valuable, but would require that the authorities of each individual country have the necessary technological prerequisites for this to work.
Has the Danish Freight Forwarders Association noticed any other relevant areas in this connection, such as level of customs (both relative and absolute), the export refund system, etc.?
Reply: In the opinion of the Association the transit procedure with the EU is an integrated unit of the political and economic intercourse of the Community.
Any alteration of the transit procedure would therefore require that careful consideration be made of political and economic consequences In connection with any change of the present rules.
What are the advantages of the transit system?
Could we live without it?
Reply: The transit system is of vital and decisive importance to the entire Community.
In the opinion of the Association it would not be possible to close down the transit system unless the entire political foundation for the EU would be taken up for revision.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Freight Transport Association, United Kingdom.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
CONTRIBUTION BY THE FREIGHT TRANSPORT ASSOCIATION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMUTEE OF INQUIRY ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT
INTRODUCTION
The Community Transit and TIR regimes operated for many years without significant problems providing invaluable benefits facilitating International trade.
It is considered to be in the interests of governments, manufacturers, retailers, shippers and carriers alike that the systems be retained and strengthened.
However > in the past three years the number of irregularities resulting in claims has reached an unprecedented and unsustainable level. A large proportion of this increase can be attributed to deliberate fraud by organised crime but many claims arise from procedural and administrative failures in a paper based system which has not been sufficiently modernised or resourced to cope with changed and increased patterns of trade. 1.3
One immediate consequence of the upsurge in claims and lost revenues has been the erosion of the universal scope of the guarantee systems.
In November 1994 tobacco and alcohol products were excluded from the TIR system and there is the impending exclusion of a wide range of "sensitive" goods from the comprehensive guarantee facility of the CT scheme.
Perhaps far more serious is the distinct possibility of the irnminem collapse of both systems as a result of key legitimate contributors to the EU trade structure, ie. carriers, forwarders, insurers and guarantors being overwhelmed by a combination of accumulated claims, associated administrative burdens or being deterred from further participation in what has become a commercially high risk activity.
It is not just existence of conditions conduciveto errors in efficiency and fraud which must be a matter of concern.
The scale (some 18 million Tl and 2*4 million TIR movements occur annually) and complexity of the system makes it almost impossible to quantify accurately the loss of revenues and potential damage to existing businesses.
The Commission in its report on "Fraud in the Transit Procedures, Solutions Foreseen and Prospectives for the Future" states that fraud in transit, which involved organised crime, is known to have had a financial impact for the Community and Member States of a rnmimum of 750millionEcu, during the period 1990­1994.
The 1994 Court of Auditors Report estimates an annual loss of at least 200 million ECU.
These figures do not take into account claims pending from 1995.
Nor do they take into account the extent to which potential losses are averted by recovery, from legitimate Principals and Guarantors, of claims.
Where these arise through errors and inefficiencies in the system there is a high probability that the duties end up being paid twice.
CONTRIBimON TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT BY THE FREIGHT TRANSPORT ASSOCIATION
Although there are many parallels in the problems and solutions for both the CT and TIR regimes they are dealt with in separate sections in this paper in recognition of some fundamental differences.
COMMUNITY TRANSIT SCHEME
The Community Transit scheme is applicable to certain categories of goods moving within the EU or between the EU and designated countries belonging to the common transit system eg.
EFTA members.
From 1 July 1996 it will be extended to Poland, Hungary, the Czech Republic and the Slovak Republic.
Its allows the suspension of duties and other charges until destination irrespective of the number of borders crossed in transit.
Most movements take place under an EU wide, paper based "Τ l " document.
Use of this document obliges the Principal to ensure proper discharge of the goods at destination.
If the oorrect discharge procedure does not take place, or is not recorded as having taken place, the Principal has the liability to pay all Customs duties.
This liability is secured against an indemnity given by the Principal in person or through a guarantee given by a third party on behalf of the Principal.
Once the indemnities and/or guarantees are lodged with the Customs Authorities the Principal can issue Tl documents.
These accompany the goods and obviate the need for duties to be paid either in the country of departure or at borders.
The Tl document consists of several copies.
One copy is kept by the Customs Office of Departure, one by the Principal.
The carrier has three copies, two of which are required at the Customs Office of Destination.
Once procedures have been completed at destination copy number S is stamped by the Custom Authorities and sent back to the Customs Office of the country where the Tl was issued.
In some cases it goes to the actual Departure Office, in others to a Central Clearing Office.
Once the Customs Office of Departure has received verification that the goods have been presented at destination the movement is considered satisfactorily completed.
If the movement is not recorded as having been satisfactorily discharged the Principal is notified and has the onus of proving that procedures have been properly completed or meeting claims for duties and other charges.
If the Principal fails to meet these obligations Customs have recourse to the indemnity or to the Principal's guarantor.
The system does not provide any mechanism for notifying the Principal/Guarantor that the movement has been successfully completed.
Inevitably in a paper based system covering millions of transits the emphasis is on exception reporting.
Under the existing Regulations Customs at departure have 12 months from the date of the movement commencing to notify the Principal/Guarantor that satisfactory evidence of discharge has not been recorded.
Any consequential liability for payment of duties and taxes does not expire by affluxion of time until 3 years after the movement.
In practice, Customs often wait until some 9 months or more have elapsed before issuing
Carnets are also used for transportation between countries outside the EU which have no bilateral agreements.
Like the Τ system the TIR regime is a paper based system designed to allow the free movement of goods without the need for physical checks or the payment of duties at borders during transit.
The main document used is a TIR carnet which consists of a number ofpages, accompanies the goods on the journey and serves as a manifest, journey record and guarantee.
The TIR system is administered centrally by the IRU in Geneva and operates through a chain of Guaranteeing Associations, normally national trade associations representing freight movers and/or industry generally.
Most Associations have a dual function in that they guarantee to meet claims by their own national Customs Authorities where these arise from irregularities in the use of carnets irrespective of the nationality of the carrier or origin of the carnet.
The admission of carriers to the TIR scheme and the subsequent issue of carnets to such approved holders is undertaken by the national Guaranteeing Associations working to internationally agreed guidelines set and coordinated by IRU.
Once admitted to the scheme a carrier's continuing participation is dependent, among other criteria, on the return of used carnets within specified time limits and the absence of detectable irregularities in those carnets through checks carried out by the Association. Traditionally such checks have concentrated on determining the carnet contains a Customs stamp of the country of ultimate destination or of exit from last country within the TIR Convention.
Until recently, it was not deemed necessary to have a facility for cross-checking the
CONTRIBUTION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT BY THE FREIGHT TRANSPORT ASSOCIATION authenticity of the stamp or obtaining independent verification that the goods had been properly cleared.
Only through the emergence of evidence of widespread use of "false" Customs stamps has the need for independent verification provided by the Customs themselves been accepted by the Authorities.
The use of false Customs stamps and the long delays in the notification of irregularities has played a major role in facilitating repetitive frauds.
The detached sheet is itself then divided and the lower portion sent back to the previous Customs Office as evidence to show that the goods have now completed the next stage of thejourney.
For goods entering the EU under the TIR regime the only checks carried out are at the entry border and in the country of final destination.
Equally, for goods being loaded in the EU the only Community checks will be in the country of departure and at the point of exit.
TIR, like CT, suffers from being a paper based system where a Customs records of goods entering or leaving the country is only "cleared75 by the physical receipt of a stamped portion of the carnet which has been detached by the next Customs Authority along the route.
Without the receipt of this document the carnet is treated as not having been discharged and the Customs holding the "undischarged" record can present a claim provided they have issued a notification of non-discharge to the guarantor within 12 months from the date of the original entry.
The notification period is extended to 2 years where a discharge is found to have been obtained fraudulently.
In practice, Customs have frequently used the maximum period available before issuing such notices if only because they are taking into account the amount of time it normally takes for the detached counterfoil to be returned from the destination Customs.
CONCLUSIONS
This approach aligns with other proposals to raise the level of guarantees generally, for certain goods, to the full value of duties and taxes.
Such courses of action, if pursued, would it is true go some way to recouping lost revenues and would ensure that Customs Authorities have, in the future, the right to recover larger amounts in respect of each irregular shipment.
However, such measures taken alone cannot substantially reduce the vulnerability of the systems to determined and organised fraud.
When claims arise, whether through deliberate fraud or administrative inefficiency, the present structure of both transit systems are heavily biased against legitímate businesses.
In the CT system the burden falls most heavily on Freight Forwarders and their Guarantors, in the TTR system on carriers, IRU and the Guaranteeing Associations.
CONTRIBUTION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT BY THE FREIGHT TRANSPORT ASSOCIATION guarantee system on the basis that the principal and/or guarantor when confronted with a claim has no option but to undertake the work to prove discharge or make a financial settlement.
Nothing in the foregoing comments is intended to imply that the Customs Authorities are solely or predominantly responsible for the current crisis in the CT and TIR systems.
The creation of the Single Market led in many Member States to reductions in Customs manpower.
At the same time modernisation of Customs procedures through investment in technology has been given a low priority.
However, it is important to identify Customs Authorities as part of the problem if only in order to underline that it is impossible to achieve a viable solution without their full cooperation, There must also be recognition of the key role they can play, not only in collecting and protecting the revenues of the Member States, but in protecting and securing the interests of all involved in International Trade.
Recent recognition of the extent of the problems, their impact on National and Community revenues and the threat posed to legitimate trade has resulted in a number of initiatives, others remain to be taken.
CT Scheme - this is an existing EU project designed to remove reliance on the paper based system. Key elements are the data capture of consignment details at departure and transmission ofthat data to the Customs Office of Destination.
TIR System - the work undertaken by IRU and Customs Authorities as a result of the recommendation of UNECE WP30 has commenced the process of computerisation of the TIR system.
Tighter Controls on Access to Both Transit Systems, in the TIR system this should be based on the exchange of information between Customs Authorities and the Guaranteeing
CONTRIBUTION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMUTEE OF INQUIRY ON FRAUD IN TRANSICT BY THE FREIGHT TRANSPORT ASSOCIATION
Associations.
Improved coordination between Authorities to ensure harmonised application of the transit rules.
