DOC EN\CM\297\297608
Note to the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit
Contribution of the IRU
SAFETIR is based on the Recommendation and the Resolution referred to below.
RECOMMENDATION OF THE ADMINISTRATIVE COMMITTEE FOR THE TIR CONVENTION (20 OCTOBER 1995)
SAFETIR is the name given by the IRU to the computer system which comprises a number of items of software, a central database, a wide range of decentralized databases and an electronic network linking all the contracting parties to the TIR Convention.
Its main purpose is to enable the IRU and, above all, the issuing associations to compare the discharge stamps on the counterfoils of the TIR carnet (seals) with the information which the customs authorities have undertaken to supply to the associations concerning discharge operations in respect of TIR carnets registered on their territory.
The comparison of this evidence from two different sources enables the issuing associations, before issuing a new TIR carnet to a user, to establish with certainty that the previous carnet has been used in accordance with the provisions in force (Convention, IRU rules, etc.).
In legal terms, in a Recommendation adopted by the Administrative Committee for the TIR Convention on 20 October 1995 (ECE/UN, Geneva), the contracting parties have undertaken to supply the information on a daily basis to the national associations or the IRU (text of the Recommendation attached).
The main advantage of this system is that it is based on the existing computer or telecommunications (fax, telex) networks of each contracting party.
* Operation
The offices of discharge in each Member State forward to a central processing point in the customs administration information concerning all the TIR carnets discharged on their respective territories regardless of where the carnets were issued.
(a) If the information is sent on paper, a software application developed by the IRU (CUTE) makes it possible to key in this data and convert it into a format established by the IRU (this method is employed in Germany, Spain, Italy, Ireland, Denmark and Switzerland);
DOC EN\CM\297\297608
This information is transmitted to the IRU by computer.
The IRU allocates to each association the carnets it has issued, then returns the information via the computer network.
Once the TIR carnet used by the haulier has been returned, the issuing association will be able, with the aid of the 'ATIRS' software, to compare the information on the stamped counterfoil with the information forwarded to it by the IRU via the computer network.
The IRU has offered to bear all the hardware and software costs, including that of the central processing point in the customs administration and the cost of telecommunications between the national centres and the IRU.
* Timetable
April 1996 establishment of the network, installation of the 'ATIRS' software on the premises of the associations, installation of the 'CUTE' software on the premises of the customs administrations who wish to use it or of the IRU associations.
Mav 1996 initial exchanges
July 1996 access to the IRU database open to the customs administrations.
* Development
With the aid of SAFETIR, from summer 1996 onwards countries which wish to do so will also be able to process TIR carnets entering or leaving their territory, thereby enabling them to monitor national discharges of TIR carnets on a day-today basis.
It is clear that a programme on that scale may make certain adjustments essential.
That is why the IRU is looking to each customs administration to make available to it and the issuing association a hotline by means of which all the errors or day-to-day teething problems can be detected.
The IRU has a specific unit in Geneva, the BRIRU, responsible for the centralized management of all these problems.
In Turkey, which is the third-largest issuer of TIR carnets in the IRU (240 000 per year), three hardware units will be installed on the premises of the central
DOC_EN\CM\297\297608
customs administration.
II - RESOLUTION 49 OF 3 MARCH 1995
The current time-limit is between one and two years in the event of a fraudulent discharge.
It is now being ratified, and six European Union Member States have already done so. It seeks to reduce the time-limit for the notification of non-discharged operations to one month, taking into account the fact that the vouchers should be returned within five days.
Were this resolution to be ratified and put into practice by all the Member States, serial fraud could be prevented and fraudsters would be identified and exposed at the latest within one month after the fraud.
SAFETIR could be put into operation very quickly (three to four months) if the Member States were to make what is, in relative terms, a minimal investment, one out of all proportion to the losses and damage caused by fraud. Guarantees could then be reintroduced for all goods.
SAFETIR may evolve in such a way that all TIR carnet itineraries can be recorded electronically.
The IRU is quite prepared to provide the members of the Committee of Inquiry with all the requisite explanations and, if necessary, to give a presentation.
Annexes : 1 - Recommendation of the Administrative Committee 2 - Resolution 3 - Outline of SAFETIR
