EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Câmara dos Despachantes Oficiais, Lisbon.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Letter of 24 April 1996 from Mr R.M. Rosado Fernandes, Member of the European Parliament, to Mr John Tomlinson, chairman of the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Dear Mr Tomlinson,
Please find enclosed a contribution for our committee of inquiry submitted by the Câmara dos Despachantes Oficiais.
(closing formula and signature)
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
Letter of 17 April 1996 from Dr Fernando Carmo, President of the Câmara dos Despachantes Oficiais (on behalf of the Governing Council), to Mr Rosado Fernandes
CONTRIBUTION OF THE DESPACHANTES OFICIAIS IN COMBATING FRAUD
Please find attached the study drawn up by the Association, which has already been submitted to the State Secretary for Tax Affairs and UCLAF.
In addition to the social status of despachantes oficiais and the organization of their profession, we have focused on the disastrous impact and scale of tax fraud in the Community area, which we consider to be a matter of particular concern.
We have accordingly given the necessary attention to statistics, intraCommunity trade and the tax liabilities incurred therein, and the activities of tax representatives and guarantors.
Other key points to be dealt with are special taxes on consumption, the administration of non-customs tax warehouses, and the issue of accompanying administrative documents connected with consignments of goods.
We have proposed various measures relating to all of the above with a view to ensuring that the correct rates of tax can be calculated more readily and duly paid.
We feel compelled to stress that, if the Community area is to be properly and fairly regulated, as is the intention, it will be impossible to dispense with the technical expertise and dedication of the despachantes oficiais.
It is no mere coincidence that the virtual disappearance of the profession has coincided with an upsurge of confusion, tax fraud, and tax evasion.
Our contention has consistently been that the present situation is intolerable because it gives rise not only to particular offences, but also to highly reprehensible social crime that needs to be eradicated as a matter of urgency, employing every means available.
The Association and its members reaffirm their entire willingness to assist you and the European Parliament in all endeavours deemed necessary to achieve the above end.
(closing formula and signature)
STUDY ON REORGANIZATION OF THE ACTIVITIES OF DESPACHANTES OFICIAIS AND THE CDO
Since the beginning of recorded history, which in Portugal is considered to have occurred in the Middle Ages, there has been a gradual realization of the need for a specialized professional who, by completing the administrative procedure entailed in movements of goods from one place to another, would ensure proper compliance with the provisions governing collection of the dues or taxes incidental to such movements.
The forerunner of the despachante oficial (official forwarding agent or DO) thus came into being, and the first references in law appeared in the Decrees of 6 April, 16 May, and 20 July 1832.
These were followed by further, less important, legal texts (Decree of 17 December 1833, Act of 10 September 1861, Act of 10 June 1864, Order in Council 31.665 of 22 November 1941, Order in Council 46.311 of 27 May 1965, and Order in Council 513/F-1/79) aimed at regulating the profession in keeping with its continuing development.
The substance of the DOs ' tasks naturally kept pace with the developing relations between firms and the Government.
Having initially functioned as a channel of communication between the two parties, DOs came to occupy a key place in these relations.
They ceased to be customs experts in the narrow sense, assuming a role closer to that of tax experts, with the result that they were made responsible for collection of the taxes owed and, later, personally or jointly and severally liable for payment of the corresponding debts.
In addition to handling the various types of complicated arrangements for processing customs declarations, the activities of DOs were expanded in such a way as to imply liability for payment of the debts incurred.
Successful candidates were assigned to the vacancies announced by the Government and were subject to its disciplinary authority.
Even when it decided to computerize its departments, the Government, knowing full well that the exercise could not succeed without their cooperation, advisedly made use of the DOs and induced them to undertake substantial or indeed ruinous investments, for it was aware that these would coincide with the imminent completion of the Community internal market.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
Largely as a result of the costs incurred by the DOs, and the fact that the latter have been sacrificed, Portugal has managed to integrate fairly smoothly into the Community, at least where customs procedures and the related tax arrangements are concerned.
That was the position as regards the work of the DOs as at 31 December 1992, when there were 465 firms employing 590 DOs in all.
Without any expense to the public purse, the DOs in question collected tax revenue more punctually than had ever previously been the case and were thus able to supply some Esc 3 bn a month to the State Treasury.
The current situation arose after December 1992.
Out of the firms of DOs in existence on 31 December 1992, only 12% are today not on the point of bankruptcy or facing insolvency in the short term.
The other firms have either gone out of business or else operate on an occasional basis, without any form of organization.
Even the firms in the first category have managed to survive thus far only because they have switched to other markets and sectors of activity and drastically cut their work-force.
Many people are living in near-poverty, and, in two reported cases, DOs have been unable to endure the consequences of a situation brought about through no fault of their own.
Meanwhile, tax crime is taking hold.
Moreover, fictitious export transactions are being employed as an expedient with the aim either of obtaining unlawful refunds of VAT or of reducing imaginary stocks.
Trade within the Community is not immune to evasion.
Statistics, which are slow to be produced and of a poor standard, are based on late and incomplete information that is not processed in advance in the proper manner.
To help alleviate, if not resolve, many of these problems, which need to be remedied as a matter of urgency in order to protect the general interest, the CDO {Câmara dos Despachantes Oficiais = Association of Official Forwarding Agents) is therefore putting forward the various suggestions set out below.
For reasons of method, a different approach has to be adopted in each individual instance, although all of the proposals relate fundamentally to the same subject and must consequently be considered as a whole in order to be fully understood.
The development of what are termed modern societies has been such that it has come to be recognized that significant collective interests should be administered by specially trained professionals.
Vocational training courses corresponding to the various levels of education have thus been organized in order to provide a means of obtaining the relevant qualifications.
Aware of the essential nature of the system and hence of the necessity that given professions should be restricted to those who are properly qualified to exercise them, countries have even made it a criminal offence for persons to practise a profession when they do not hold the requisite qualification (in Portugal's case the stipulation to that effect is laid down in Section 358 of the Code of Criminal Law).
It was once the case in Portugal that DOs were required to hold a qualification (licence) in order to be recognized as such and entitled to practise the profession, and all DOs used to possess the necessary qualification.
At present, however, customs declarations may be processed by any agent, without professional qualifications or even, on occasion, minimum literacy skills!
Section 426 of the Customs Reform (RA)
According to the wording of the above provision as it stood prior to publication of Order in Council 89/92 of 21 May 1992, customs declarations could be made only by owners or recipients of goods, acting on their own behalf or represented by agents, employees of such owners or recipients (private forwarding agents), customs officers of railway companies or airlines, or DOs, whose responsibilities extended to all shipments of goods.
It follows that, under the law couched in these terms, owners or recipients of goods, and their agents, are entitled to deal only with shipments of goods belonging to them or companies owned and represented by them or which have instructed them to act on their behalf.
A similar restriction applies to the customs officers of railway companies and airlines.
Agents may not act unless they have a letter of attorney and may not represent more than one principal (RA, Section 433).
DOs are entitled to deal with any shipments, whether or not they produce a letter of attorney.
Consequently, under the legal arrangement in question, DOs are the only party empowered to handle the business of a principal not represented by an agent.
If the principal is specified in a letter of attorney, owners or recipients of goods have complete freedom of choice: they may elect to be represented by a DO, an agent, or even an employee.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
A further significant text, published after the above provisions entered into force, is Council Regulation (EEC) No 3632/85 of 12 December 1985.
Council Regulation (EEC) No 3632/85 of 12 December 1985
The recitals appear to imply that the Regulation is intended to lay down the conditions under which goods may be placed under a customs regime.
There is no mention of capacity or qualifications other than in Article 6(a), which plainly applies to DOs (in Portugal).
As regards the person to whom a written declaration refers, Article 3(1) of the Regulation maintains the distinction already established in Section 426 of the RA, discussed above.
Article 3(2) and (3) impose a restriction and provide for an option which may be summed up as follows: when a Member State permits declarations to be made in the name of one person but on behalf of another, the right to make declarations in the name and on behalf of another person or in the name of one person (i.e. the party making the declaration) on behalf of another may be confined to self-employed persons engaged in the occupation of making declarations.
This Regulation is entirely consistent with the national legislation outlined above, since it is permissible under Portuguese law for a person to make declarations in his own name on behalf of another (Section 426(4) of the Customs Reform, applicable to all declarations, and Article 2(1) of Order in Council 289/88 of 24 August 1988, relating to comprehensive security).
On that basis, and on the understanding that the condition regarding comprehensive security had to be satisfied, the right to make declarations in the above category applied solely to DOs.
This way of thinking, however, has not been accepted by the Court of Justice of the European Communities.
In the judgment handed down on 11 March 1992 the Court of Justice ruled that if the fundamental principles set out in Section 426 of the RA continued to hold good, the provisions of Regulation (EEC) No 3632/85 would be impossible to implement.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
Because of this Court ruling, an activity in the public interest and as such subject to the necessary regulation and control was turned into a complete free-for-all and even opened up to persons without any qualifications. The resulting consequences are becoming more and more apparent every day.
(EEC) No 2913/92 of
Community Customs Code (Council Regulation 12 October 1992)
Repealing Regulation (EEC) No 3632/85, Article 251 of the Community Customs Code lays down the conditions under which persons may act as agents vis-à-vis the customs authorities (Articles 5 and 64).
The Code marks a significant departure from the system established under the previous Regulation, on which the Court ruling discussed above was based.
Although it is still permissible for owners of goods to have customs declarations made on their behalf, the persons making the declarations must be qualified to do so, a condition which did not apply under the earlier arrangements.
It is therefore obvious that an agent without qualifications can never act as a representative in a professional capacity, since the system would otherwise be completely turned on its head.
In the light of the foregoing, the following measures should be proposed.
Measures proposed
(a) Given that the occupation of making declarations to the customs authorities is one involving considerations of public interest, it should be carried on only by those who are properly qualified to do so, for example DOs, the only group in Portugal to meet that requirement.
(b) There is nothing in Portuguese or Community law to prevent exclusive status as professional agents from being conferred on DOs.
On the contrary, such a right appears to be justified on account of Articles 5 and 64 of the Community Customs Code.
(c) Articles 1, 2. and 3 of Order in Council 89/92 of 21 May 1992. except for the new wording of Section 430 of the RA, should be repealed, as should Articles 1 and 2 of Order in Council 280/92 of
18 December 1992 (with regard to the latter article, this applies only to the amendments to Section 426 of the RA).
The legal effect of the laws in force prior to the two abovementioned acts should accordingly be restored.
The essential features of the system, known as Intrastat, are laid down in Council Regulation (EEC) No 3330/91 of 7 November 1991.
Article 21, relating to the 'statistical data medium to be transmitted to the competent departments', stipulates that 'goods shall be designated in such a way as to permit easy and precise classification in the finest relevant subdivision of the version of the combined nomenclature in force at the time'.
In Portugal, persons who fail to fulfil their obligation to supply information in the appropriate fashion at the right time may be fined (Article 21 of Law 6/89 of 15 April 1989).
Current situation
Firms are advised to simplify their statistical returns as far as possible and consequently omit many of the goods that should be mentioned.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
Not least because the combined nomenclature codes are complicated, the classification for duty purposes and the customs value at the frontier are not indicated correctly unless the return has been filled in by DOs.
To make returns easier to complete, the INE (National Statistical Institute) supplies firms with photocopies of individual chapters and not the full text of the combined nomenclature.
The laudable desire for simplicity thus has the somewhat unfortunate consequence of manifestly detracting from the reliability of the figures.
Moreover, every liberty is taken in the course of the procedure.
To ascertain that statistics are up to standard, it would suffice to check them against the accounts of the firm which supplied them.
DOs compile statistics in time, after carefully studying the documents supplied by firms, classify goods correctly, according to the combined nomenclature, and specify the appropriate customs values.
In so doing, they plainly help a great deal to provide data on which to base accurate and reliable statistics.
That was why, in the light of all the above considerations, and as it argued at the time, the CDO made a request to the Minister for Territorial Planning and Management calling for the arrangement whereby third parties may be instructed to provide information (Article 9 of Regulation (EEC) No 3330/91) to be made an exclusive preserve of DOs.
This request elicited an old-fashioned peremptory response from the INE, which, by conceding that the INE had to accommodate itself to the data supplied by companies and not the other way round, offered an excuse and incentive to take liberties and indirectly conferred respectability on the INE's work and statistics.
Since the role of the INE cannot and must not be understood in these terms, least of all in the light of the above Regulation, we are proposing the measures below.
(c) Firms making use of DOs as third parties responsible for providing statistical information should benefit under the arrangements for tax transparency.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
(d) The existing legislation on the quality of information to be provided for statistical purposes should be enforced and, where applicable, penalties imposed for failure to comply.
INTRA-COMMUNITY TRADE AND TAX LIABILITIES INCURRED THEREIN
Situation prior to the single market
Before the single Community market was completed, the customs authorities performed an important controlling role, since they used to coordinate supervisory activities in the economic, financial, health, statistical, and tax spheres.
In the economic sphere they ensured compliance with the formalities required by economic coordination bodies.
With regard to public health, the customs authorities would not release goods for consumption unless the relevant law had been observed, that is to say, unless the appropriate bodies had carried out the inspections and checks for which they were responsible.
As regards statistics, the data necessary for proper information were duly checked by the customs authorities against the documents supplied by firms, thus ensuring that the resulting figures to be passed on to the INE were totally accurate.
Finally, responsibility for tax, the principal task assigned to the customs authorities, was exercised in such a way as to guarantee that all dues payable in respect of incoming and outgoing goods (customs duties, levies, and indirect taxes) were collected almost without fail.
In addition, the customs authorities were involved in areas such as quality certification and control, standardization, ecology, and so forth.
All of the above tasks were performed virtually faultlessly, and it is only fair to point out that the private customs sector, consisting of the DOs and their staff, provided invaluable assistance in that connection.
Every month the State Treasury received over Esc 80 bn in VAT alone by means of an extremely simple, regular, and reliable procedure, namely comprehensive security for customs clearance, the whole of which system was placed under the responsibility of the DOs.
Furthermore, the first steps towards computerization of the customs service were taken in the offices of the DOs, who were compelled to make substantial investments.
On account of their wide-ranging involvement in international trade, the customs authorities were the linchpin of the Portuguese economy until 31 December 1992.
Situation after 1 January 1993
The general picture described above was completely obliterated when the single Community market entered into force.
The abolition of frontiers has had momentous consequences that were not properly weighed up beforehand, even though spokesmen in various quarters attempted to warn of the potential risks now recognized on all sides.
It was not necessary to close the borders once more, but rather to prevent imports moving within Portugal unless VAT had been paid on them.
Such goods consequently had to be monitored while they were carried on Portuguese roads.
Provision also had to be made for effective health inspections, since Portugal was, in many respects, the dustbin of Europe.
Tax evasion is also a crime from the social point of view because it impedes solidarity at the official level. The measures which we are proposing are set out below.
This proposal is concerned essentially with the difficulty of monitoring goods entering by road, since the safeguards existing in other cases, for example ships' lists, enable the cargoes being transported to be identified more readily.
The strategy set out in this study focuses on three stages: - identification of the means of transport; - accounting and responsibility of official accounts experts; - link with Intrastat.
This strategy aims not only to restore the prestige of the customs authorities and their ability to act, but also to make most effective
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
use of their technical and human resources, combined with similar resources to be marshalled in the private sector, including DOs, official accounts experts, forwarding agents, carriers, and so forth.
The leading role naturally falls to the customs authorities, which have to coordinate all operations on national territory.
First stage: identification of the means of transport
Every vehicle entering national territory must be weighed (the weighbridges will need to be installed).
At the same time as the vehicle is weighed, a remote-controlled toll meter or similar device will photograph its registration number.
The ticket will show the following:
— entry number (vehicles will be recorded in order of arrival),
— gross weight of the vehicle.
The ticket will remain in the possession of the driver, who will be required to hand over one of the copies, at the place of unloading, to the consignee, together with invoices or equivalent documents or other papers relating to the consignment carried.
A check of this type, which is also carried out in the United Kingdom in order to protect the roads, would, in addition, enable supervision for tax purposes to be exercised by the customs inspectorate while goods are in transit.
This stage hinges on authentication of the proof of arrival of goods by the TOC (official accounts expert), proceeding from the premiss that the TOC is an expert whose status derives from professional and ethical responsibilities recognized by the Government.
Having been dealt with by the above procedure, all invoices or equivalent documents relating to consignments delivered in a given month will be submitted in duplicate to the future supervisory body, together with the corresponding Intrastat declaration, the transport document, if any, and the ticket, which the vehicle driver will be required to produce not later than the tenth working day of the month following his arrival.
Third stage: checks on transport documents
After being submitted, the invoices on which an Intrastat declaration is based, will be returned, bearing the stamp of the supervisory body, by virtue of which they, and they alone, will be considered valid for the purposes of VAT deduction.
The ticket giving details of the carriage, which must be submitted at the same time, will be used by the supervisory body to check its data by computer and record the carriage in question in the system, once it has been ascertained that the goods have entered the territory and been handed over to the consignee and the documents concerned posted to the accounts.
The Intrastat declaration, submitted in duplicate, along with duplicates of the supporting documents, will be checked by the supervisory body.
After making the check, the supervisory body will send:
— the Intrastat declaration to the INE, guaranteeing, by so doing, that the information contained therein is correct and can thus be treated with confidence; and
Lastly, if any vehicles have not been accounted for in the light of the tickets submitted, the Tax Department will be notified so as to enable it to identify the offending vehicles and recipients of the goods concerned with a view to completing the appropriate tax procedure.
This, in outline, is the system which we are advocating for the purpose of monitoring intraCommunity trade.
Firms falling below the Intrastat statistical thresholds
Instead of the Intrastat declaration, these firms will submit a monthly return to the supervisory body covering invoices relating to intraCommunity trade, purely to allow checks to be carried out.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
Freight in bulk
Carriers will furnish consignees with a declaration identifying the means of transport, which will be considered equivalent to the ticket and submitted, together with the invoices or equivalent documents and the Intrastat declaration, to the supervisory body.
They will send the originals of the tickets to the supervisory body, along with lists specifying the freight carried in the vehicles concerned.
'Negative' Intrastat declarations - Idle periods
In this case, firms will merely submit the current negative declaration to the supervisory body.
Carriage of goods by sea, air, or rail
Since the documents supplied in the above cases suffice to identify the freight carried, the only change applying to these forms of carriage is that the entry ticket will be replaced by the bill of lading, the airway bill, or the document issued by the railway company.
In addition, carriers will send freight lists to the supervisory authority for back-checking.
We propose that the supervisory body be set up by conferring the necessary powers on the Câmara dos Despachantes Oficiais.
The supervisory body would be subordinate to the CDO but enjoy specific forms of autonomy to be laid down by means of appropriate legislation.
The body would be funded by an arrangement to be determined, for instance by imposing a very small levy, expressed in per mil, on the tax checked, bearing in mind that if, in recognition of its technical and professional expertise, a large number of jobs were provided for the customs sector, substantial resources would be released, since the State would no longer have to pay benefits or other subsidies, thus creating a system of communicating vessels.
When an Intrastat declaration is submitted by a DO, as the third party responsible for providing information, the following formalities should cease to be required:
— it should also no longer be necessary to submit supporting documents along with an Intrastat declaration, since, if the welltried practice were reinstated, statistical data could be presumed to have been processed correctly;
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
— as soon as possible, it should be permissible to transfer data, via a telematic link, directly from the office of a DO to the INE (the latter would be jointly responsible for the information, along with the party making the declaration).
APPLICABILITY OF THE SYSTEM TO DISPATCH AND THE ARRANGEMENTS FOR PAYMENT OF VAT AT SOURCE
The way of proceeding described above could be applied to and, subject to minor adjustments, is consistent with the projected arrangements for payment of VAT at source.
Similarly, the necessary adaptations to the system could easily be made forthwith so as to prevent fictitious dispatch operations, the sole purpose of which is to enable VAT to be deducted in respect of goods sold tax-free.
TAX REPRESENTATIVES AND GUARANTORS
It is a little difficult to understand, or perhaps not, given that tax problems are now assuming such dimensions as to become a matter of fundamental concern, that tax representatives are being neglected.
The role of tax representatives has not been properly defined, and they have been hampered on account of excessive initial red tape, the restriction whereby they may act only on behalf of persons not resident on national territory, and the confusion affecting the systems under which they operate and the procedures of which they make use.
The situation is similar with regard to tax guarantors, bearing in mind that it is impossible to take responsibility for guarantees without clear understanding of the underlying rules.
Worse still, legislators have failed to realize that one of the most effective ways of facilitating tax collection is to exercise proper control and supervision over operations at the earlier stages intended to ensure that payment is made in the proper transparent fashion.
Once the amount of a tax debt has been determined and the party liable to pay it identified, collection becomes far easier.
That being the case, as it undoubtedly is, it is difficult to understand why DOs are not put to use in the system or recognized as tax representatives, if only by virtue of their status.
Given that they are professionals who are entitled to place goods under any customs regime and familiar with the rules on the movement of goods and the related guarantees, DOs could and should play a decisive role up to the time of payment of a tax debt, if need be by guaranteeing the regularity of the payment.
DOC_EN\CM\298\298614
In view of their professional qualifications and the guarantees they can offer (comprehensive security), DOs could even improve the system.
It might thus be possible, and of obvious benefit to Portugal, to put a more speedy end to those fictitious transactions whereby goods whose place of final consumption is Portugal are allegedly shipped to other Member States in order to secure preferential tax treatment in the familiar way.
Portuguese ports and other terminals would be turned into interfaces for entry to Europe. The tax revenue generated by the goods which Portugal produces and the services it provides would remain on Portuguese national territory.
The same point applies to IECs (special taxes on consumption). As far as they are concerned, it is incomprehensible that DOs should not be recognized as tax representatives, if only as an automatic consequence of their status.
DOs, in their capacity as tax representatives, should be responsible for running (non-customs) tax warehouses and issue documents relating to movements of goods, providing the necessary guarantees in these respects; they would also settle accounts and ensure that stocks of goods and accounting records were in line.
This would ensure that warehouses were administered more conscientiously, tax returns and direct payment of tax debts handled more accurately, proper collection guaranteed more effectively, and tax fraud and tax evasion consequently reduced. The measures proposed to achieve the above ends are as follows:
The various laws applicable to tax representatives and guarantors should be harmonized. Furthermore,
Procedures connected with indirect taxes should be simplified.
By virtue of their professional expertise, DOs should automatically be recognized as tax representatives where the above taxes are concerned.
Since tax representatives act prior to collection of taxes, they should be involved in calculating tax debts and guarantee that they are paid.
Tax representatives/DOs should manage (non-customs) tax warehouses with regard to accounts and stocks of goods, the issue of documents relating to movements of goods and the relevant guarantees, and improvement of the system.
The warehouses should be obliged to use the services of a tax representative and may be permitted in return to benefit from the tax transparency arrangements (ROC scheme).
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
It was reasonable to predict that completion of the internal market, in the manner in which it was brought about on 1 January 1993, would spell near-ruin for DOs collectively.
Moreover, it was plain to see that there were insufficient resources to honour the commitments entered into before and after that date, in particular those arising from the fact that the vast majority of employees were doomed to lose their jobs.
Order in Council 25/93 of 5 February 1993 was issued, supposedly with a view to alleviating the consequences described above.
It is explicitly acknowledged in the preamble to this act that the activities of DOs have been palpably cut back, and, with the aim of reducing the adverse impact of the situation brought about, a number of special measures have been laid down, in particular for persons aged between 55 and 60, namely:
Since DOs who are partners in a firm work on a self-employed basis and are treated accordingly for the purposes of welfare benefits, they were not eligible to join the early-retirement scheme, or draw the lump sum allowance, or, of course, obtain compensation for dismissal.
As regards the one measure that does directly affect DOs, a State contribution covers a third of the compensation for dismissal which DOs are required to pay to their employees.
DOC_EN\CM\298\298614
In the light of all the above points, we propose the following measures, on the understanding that sufficient time must be allowed for the benefits to take effect.
(a) DOs aged 52 and above at the time of retirement or who have totalled 25 years' social security contributions should be entitled to retire.
(b) To complete the abovementioned contribution period, it should be permissible to pay voluntary contributions to cover the time remaining.
(c) The amount of retirement pension should be three and a half times the current minimum wage unless a higher pension is payable by virtue of the relevant laws or regulations.
(d) In cases of proven need, DOs who do not satisfy the requirements set out in point (a) and are unable to pay contributions to cover the full period specified therein should be entitled to draw a survivor's pension, the amount of which should be as laid down in point (c).
(e) Where a DO is in receipt of income below the minimum pension, the latter should be reduced to the extent required to ensure that total income from the two sources does not exceed the amount of the pension.
(f) DOs should not be entitled to retire under these arrangements or draw the minimum pension while they are employed in the customs sector, and the corresponding entitlements should be terminated in that event.
(g) Entitlement to either of the two abovementioned benefits should be deemed to exist whether or not a DO is carrying on his occupation, provided that any cessation of business has resulted from completion of the internal market.
(h) The amounts corroborated by evidence which DOs or firms thereof have disbursed in payment of compensation to their employees should be refunded in the form of a tax credit.
(i) Tax credits should be used solely to pay existing or future debts to the State or the social security scheme.
DOC_EN\CM\298\298614
(j) Refunds to former DOs or firms thereof not in debt to the State or the social security scheme should be made in cash.
(k) Persons satisfying the requisite conditions should not be entitled to benefit under the arrangements set out above unless they make the necessary application before the end of the current year.
Having regard to the specific public interests which it serves through its members, the CDO is an association governed by public law (Article 267(3) of the Portuguese Constitution).
(a) The CDO must be provided with the necessary funds to promote training courses for its members in areas connected with their business.
DOC EN\CM\298\298614
To our mind, the following conclusions can be drawn from this study :
One subject to be mentioned in the study is redefinition of the status of DOs along the same lines as in the world's leading economic blocs (the US, Japan, and Canada), thus securing a fitting position for professionals whose technical expertise Portugal cannot forgo and restoring the dignity and prestige of which the profession has unjustly been deprived.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
Letter dated 7 May 1996 from Mr Freddy Sandahl, Swedish Freight Forwarders' Association, to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Translation
The Swedish Freight Forwarders' Association and the guarantee voucher system
As from 1 January 1988, when the EFTA countries acceded to the Community's transit convention, Swedish haulage companies have been subject to EC requirements to furnish guarantees as security in respect of customs duties and other duties and taxes chargeable in the course of transit operations.
The Swedish Freight Forwarders' Association, which represents Swedish hauliers and freight forwarders, considered it only natural to look into the conditions for obtaining credit and guarantee cover for transit operations.
With the insurance companies, it developed a system involving the use of flatrate guarantee vouchers which suited the requirements of both the transit convention and the marketplace.
The guarantee vouchers protect the principal, i.e. the freight forwarder, against claims from the customs service for payment of taxes and duties when it is established that a transit operation has not been duly completed.
Given the nature of the business of members of Swedish Freight Forwarders' Association, the flatrate guarantee system has proved to be the most effective solution.
Since the system started up in 1988, the Association has applied firm principles as regards the issuing of guarantee vouchers.
Every request for guarantee vouchers is examined by an expert with extensive knowledge of the industry.
It goes without saying that, in this way, the guarantor is seeking to protect himself against claims from the customs service.
Only well-established, professional haulage companies and freight forwarders are allowed to use the Association's guarantee vouchers.
It is common for requests to be turned down if a firm is considered unsuitable.
The amount of guarantee vouchers used depends on the volume of transport operations.
For instance, one of the larger member companies used about 15 000 vouchers annually until Sweden joined the EU.
For practical reasons, therefore, guarantee vouchers must be issued in batches to cover the estimated requirements for a given period.
Obviously, it does not make sense to issue guarantee vouchers on a one-by-one basis or to wait until one transit operation has been completed before issuing the guarantee vouchers for the next operation.
From 1988 until the end of 1994, the Association issued about 800 000 guarantee vouchers with a total value of Skr 56 billion.
During that period, there were less than 20 cases of loss, resulting between them in the payment of Skr 629 000 in guarantees.
DOC EN\CM\291\291249
worked excellently until pressure was put on the transit system's Achilles heel, namely the shortcomings in the way the authorities' supervisory system works.
Measures introduced when fraud first became apparent
It was early in the summer of 1995 when the first information was obtained on transit fraud, mainly involving cigarettes, in Europe.
The decision was discussed with the Swedish Customs Administration, which asked for written notification about the ban so as to be able to discuss the matter in the Customs Code Committee in Brussels.
The Customs Administration also announced that it would, until further notice, also require the Association's vouchers to be endorsed with a stamp worded to the effect that the vouchers were not valid for any goods listed in Annex 2 to Article 44 of the transit convention.
The Association wrote to the clients to whom it supplied guarantee vouchers informing them that tobacco and alcohol had been excluded from the system.
The customs service then issued a circular to all customs offices in Sweden informing them that the Association's decision was not in accordance with the transit convention and that the guarantee vouchers would be valid as normal.
A far-sighted and precautionary measure introduced by the Association as a way of curbing possible economic crime thus met with a bureaucratic response which eventually resulted in the exploitation by criminals of the guarantee system and transit arrangements, with both the government and businesses incurring financial loss.
This was despite the fact that the customs service was by then fully aware, as a result of Case A (detailed below), of the risk of transit fraud involving high-duty goods.
Detailed below are six cases of transit fraud.
All of them have affected wellknown and well-established businesses, and the losses could not have been either foreseen or prevented by the guarantor.
In one case, the business changed hands after the vouchers had been supplied but before the irregular transit operations were carried out.
In March 1995, A, a small firm of freight forwarders in Stockholm, was asked to transport cigarettes of American origin from Sweden to Switzerland.
The cigarettes arrived in Sweden by rail from the Netherlands.
The firm furnished a flatrate guarantee for the transit operation in the form of a single voucher instead of the 150 or so which should rightly have been furnished.
It accepted the business because of the very lucrative remuneration that the Swiss principal was offering.
The lorry never arrived at its destination.
DOC_EN\CM\291\291249
discontinued owing to lack of evidence, and the owner was released.
The customs service charged the guarantor ECU 7000 (approximately Skr 70 000).
The total amount which should have been charged in respect of customs and excise duties and VAT was Skr 10-12 m.
On the basis of its investigations in this case, the customs service acquired a very good understanding of cigarette-smuggling activities in Europe.
In spring 1995, B, a road haulage company, was offered good money, via a contact in Malmö, to transport cigarettes in transit from Sweden to Spain.
The cigarettes were in storage in Stockholm freeport, where they had been taken by a Dutch haulage company.
There was also one lot in Malmö.
The following transport operations took place:
Inquiry
Latest date of arrival
Consignment Date of departure of
This consignment was despatched from Malmö.
It was a long time before inquiries were made, in one case more than two months.
In spite of the fact that high-duty goods were involved and the fact that customs by then were well aware of the risk of transit fraud, the inquiries and monitoring activities were carried out in a dilettante fashion.
On 11 August, customs were alerted to the fact that three transit operations had not been completed at the customs office of destination, but nevertheless allowed three more operations to commence from Stockholm, despite the fact that it ought to have been obvious that the consignments were involved in acts of fraud.
The Customs Investigation Service visited B's premises on 14 July, and the relevant report indicates that the consignments of cigarettes transported by the firm were obviously being handled by persons with criminal records and that it was quite obvious why the cigarettes were being sent from the Netherlands to Spain via Sweden.
That ought to have been sufficient for customs to take immediate action to halt further consignments of this kind.
Swedish customs could, for instance, have informed the guarantor of their suspicions or contacted Spanish customs by telephone or fax to verify whether the person stated to be the consignee for the cigarettes actually existed and was waiting for the consignments in question.
DOC EN\CM\291\291249 —
Only then did the Association become aware of the fraud. In a conversation between the Association's employee responsible for the guarantee vouchers and the Customs Investigation Service, the latter said that 'all transit operations involving cigarettes were the subject of economic crime'.
The Association immediately sought to stop Β from carrying out further transit operations on the basis of its guarantee vouchers.
It only succeeded in doing so when the head of Stockholm Region Customs Service intervened.
This did not, however, prevent customs from allowing a transit operation involving cigarettes to go ahead on the basis of vouchers from another guarantor on 21 August, even though they had been informed by Spanish customs that previous transit operations had not been duly completed.
Forged return copies had, for instance, been received in Stockholm in Spanish customs service envelopes.
The Association and its insurers reported the case to the police.
It was only then that the Association gained access to documents relating to the Customs Investigation Service's investigation, which had previously been stated to be classified.
Customs did not report the case to the police until the Association had complained several times that such action had not been taken.
3.3 Case C
In 1995, C, a firm of freight forwarders in Gothenburg, was asked by a trading company in the same city to transport cigarettes to Spain and Portugal.
The firm was aware of the requirement for an increased guarantee and made inquiries of the customs service as to the amount of guarantee required.
The reply they got was: seven guarantee vouchers per lorry-load.
Three transit operations started from Gothenburg in August 1995, all of them covered, with the customs service's approval, by seven guarantee vouchers per lorry-load.
The return copies subsequently presented turned out to be forged.
As the goods never arrived at the office of destination, the company was required by Swedish customs to pay the full amount of duties and taxes chargeable, which totalled Skr 15 m.
The company invoked Article 379(2) of the Transit Regulation, the customs service granted a three-month period of grace.
The freight forwarder argues that if he had received correct information from customs regarding the amount of the liability required, he would never have agreed to transport the goods.
Customs had not reported the case to the police, and it is not known whether the Customs Investigation Service has launched its own investigations into the case.
3.4 Case D
In autumn 1995, D, one of Sweden's largest freight forwarders, received an inquiry from a German freight forwarder regarding storage in Sweden of butter from the Czech Republic.
The storage operation went ahead and, during September and October, the butter was despatched in transit to Portugal.
DOC EN\CM\291\291249
Customs requested that 6+4 guarantee vouchers be furnished in respect of the transit operations.
This should rightly have been 6+6.
This has the effect of reducing the guarantor's liability and increasing the principal's.
3.5 Case E
During 1994, before Sweden became a member of the EU, company E sold a consignment of Swedish meat to a Belgian firm for delivery to Prague.
The transport was provided by a Belgian haulier, who collected the consignment in Gothenburg.
When the lorry arrived at the German-Czech frontier, the driver produced the transit document for endorsement, but the customs officer said he first wanted to have a look at the consignment.
The driver went away to open the vehicle.
When the customs officer came out, the lorry had vanished.
The meat was probably sold in Germany, where the purchaser, with the aid of export documents, attempted to obtain export subsidies.
The Belgian company has now gone bankrupt and ceased trading.
As the transit operation was never completed, E was required by German customs to pay taxes and duties amounting to DM 21 000, which was covered as to ECU 7000 by the guarantor.
Swedish customs had requested only one guarantee voucher in respect of the consignment, which was not enough.
3.6 Case F
In October 1995, the Stockholm office of a large international firm of freight forwarders shipped a consignment of cobalt to the Netherlands from a customs warehouse in Stockholm freeport.
The cobalt originated in Russia.
Cobalt is not one of the goods listed in Article 44, Annex 2, of the transit convention, in respect of which an increased guarantee is required, but customs in this case asked for 14 guarantee vouchers instead of one.
The company representative received the impression that so many vouchers were required owing to the high value of the goods and provided the required number of vouchers.
After the lorry had left Stockholm, it transpired that the reason why customs had asked for more vouchers was that, pursuant to Article 368(2), customs suspected a transit offence.
The Association alerted the company, which managed to trace the consignment and place it under special surveillance so as to ensure that the transit procedure was duly completed.
When the Association subsequently wrote to the customs service to complain that no information had been provided as to the real reason for the increased requirement for vouchers, the customs service invoked Chapter 9, paragraph 2, of the Swedish Official Secrets Act.
It is clear from the above account of various cases of transit irregularities that customs did not react as rapidly or as purposefully as they ought to have.
As early as October 1994, customs knew that a certain named person was looking for someone to transport cigarettes which were to be unloaded unlawfully at a location which was not their stated destination.
They did not, however, take any action.
In spring 1995, customs investigated the first known case of fraud involving cigarettes (Case A).
In mid-July 1995, company Β was suspected of effecting transit operations which were not completed at the location intended.
The goods were said to simply disappear after being transported.
Customs did not inform the guarantor of their suspicions.
Despite the fact that three transit operations were reported as not having been duly completed after 23 weeks and no reply had been received to inquiries, they allowed a further six transit operations, with a variety of guarantors, to be started.
When it subsequently became clear that the majority of cases were involved in transit offences and that the government was losing very large amounts of money, customs reacted in an astonishingly passive manner.
Certainly, they made inquiries with the office of destination, but did not conduct any more comprehensive or thorough investigations.
They did not make any attempt to find the owner, consignee or place where the goods were unloaded.
The reports from the office of destination that the goods had arrived turned out to be forgeries.
Circumstances existed which aroused suspicions that customs officers or other persons at the place of destination might be accessories to the fraud, but they were not investigated.
If a consignment has not been presented at the office of destination, and it is not possible to establish where the offence or irregularity occurred, the customs office of departure is to levy customs and other duties in accordance with the provisions which apply in the country of departure.
Once they established that the goods had not been presented at the office of destination, Swedish customs charged the customs and other duties to the principal, and, when he did not pay, to the guarantor.
This, however, is not a satisfactory reason for levying the duties in the country of departure.
This can be done only if it has not been possible to establish the place where the offence or irregularity occurred, and that would require customs to carry out more thorough investigations than they had done in this case.
It has emerged from the contacts which the Association has had with various customs offices that they apply the transit regulation in different ways and sometimes break the rules.
One might ask why the customs service has performed its functions so unsatisfactorily.
The Association believes one very important reason to be that customs officers' motivation has fallen since their terms of reference was restricted and made more complex following accession to the EU.
This has been made clear on many occasions, including within an EU context.
In addition, there is no great incentive to ensure that the government collects customs and other duties chargeable in respect of transit operations.
If goods disappear, the customs service merely deducts the customs and other duties from the guarantees.
This is not necessarily a deliberate action on the part of customs officers, but is. a typical reaction, conscious or otherwise, which is characteristic of the way in which they exercise their duties.
It is, however, highly unsatisfactory that offences, in this case of a serious and premeditated nature, are not prevented or their perpetrators prosecuted, even in cases where the government does not lose money.
DOC EN\CM\291\291249
To summarize, the sorry state of affairs in the transit system leads the Association to draw two fundamental conclusions:
As they stand, the rules facilitate large-scale premeditated international criminal activity.
The risk of punishment is low.
The action to be taken by customs must be further defined and made mandatory by being incorporated in the rules on the transit system.
As they stand, the rules on transit invite international economic crime, whilst the action taken by customs has not been as thorough as one might have hoped.
In view of this, serious consideration must be given to issuing a moratorium in respect of levying the duties chargeable, which could have devastating consequences for the firms affected.
In cases where customs have failed to take adequate action, the guarantor should not be required at all to bear financial liability.
It does not of course make sense to exclude the possibility of transporting goods in transit in the EU.
Instead, measures should be considered which help to prevent irregularities and increase the element of risk involved.
At the same time, consideration should be given to measures which make transit operations more secure, e.g. by drawing up clear rules on the processing of documents and for communications within customs services and between customs services and the business community.
It is very important in this connection that clear and binding rules be drawn up as regards the role of the customs services.
Firstly, action should be taken to ensure that investigations are launched as soon as possible when irregularities are suspected.
At the same time, a procedure should be laid down for establishing contact between customs and the principal/guarantor and between the customs authorities in the countries of departure and destination.
The Association therefore suggests that consideration be given to the following measures.
In order to achieve the desired effect, these measures should be incorporated clearly in a provision implementing the transit convention or a similar provision.
A. The office of departure should contact the principal and guarantor when a transit operation, which in the authority's experience, or for other reasons, appears to involve a risk, is to begin.
The reason for this is that the parties involved should have an opportunity to avoid entering into an obligation in respect of the transit operation.
Likewise, the office of departure ought in each case to inform the guarantor which companies are regarded as posing risks in relation to transit operations.
B. In accordance with Article 368(2), the office of departure must, when highrisk goods are being transported, require a guarantee to be furnished which exceeds the equivalent of ECU 7000.
This is to be furnished in multiples of ECU 7000.
Special rules exist in the case of specific goods (sensitive products).
Increased guarantees may be required in other cases, too, if particular
DOC EN\CM\291\291249
The reason is that the principal and guarantor should have an opportunity to withdraw from a forthcoming transit operation.
In order to ensure compliance with this provision, it is essential to lay down rules for the customs authorities of the various countries and ensure effective cooperation between them.
To achieve this objective, the Association considers it necessary to introduce binding rules in the form of an implementing regulation or similar provision.
The following rules should be considered for transit operations involving highrisk, sensitive products, and if the office of departure considers a forthcoming transit operation to involve significant risks.
(i) The office of departure must inform the office of destination that a transit operation has begun by faxing to it a copy of the T1 document.
(ii) If the consignment has not been produced at the office of destination shortly thereafter (e.g. within 35 days, depending on the distance involved), the office of destination must notify the office of departure accordingly by fax.
(iii) Investigations must then be launched by each of the customs offices in their own country. Such investigations must focus on the transport company, the owner, the consignee, the route taken, any customs or similar warehouse used and any other circumstances deemed to be of relevance in the case in question for establishing who is responsible and where the goods have been unloaded.
(iv) If the preliminary investigations do not yield the desired result, the office of departure, which is primarily responsible for establishing where the goods have been unloaded, must address specific questions to the office of destination, also by fax.
(v) If replies are not received to those questions, or if the replies are inadequate or incomplete, consideration must be given to establishing contact between the customs HQs of the countries concerned.
Such contact should also be established in cases where irregularities involving high-duty goods have occurred repeatedly.
If, in any EU Member State, it is felt that reasons of confidentiality prevent an exchange of information pursuant to the rules proposed above, it should be ascertained whether it is possible to waive the need for confidentiality when, as in this case, there is a need to combat large-scale organized international crime.
The Association takes the view that, in accordance with EU rules and bilateral customs agreements, opportunities should already exist for exchanging information in cases where fraud is suspected.
DOC EN\CM\291\291249
In point 9, UCLAF proposes that the current rules stipulating a maximum of 12 months as regards notification of the guarantor should be replaced by rules stipulating a three-year period.
The Association considers that it makes perfect sense for the time limit to remain unchanged and for the principal to be given a chance to investigate the matter himself.
In addition, the Association has the following proposals to make:
* It should no longer be possible to extend transit operations.
A transit operation must be completed at the stated office of destination.
If circumstances alter, a fresh transit operation must be commenced, from the existing office of destination to the new office of destination.
* Consideration must be given immediately to how guarantors' liability in respect of flatrate guarantees is to cover transit operations which end in the Visegrad states, as it cannot be assumed that guarantors will be prepared automatically to extend flatrate guarantee cover to those countries, as the risk of fraud is high in relation to transport operations.
The Association considers it appropriate for the European Parliament or the Commission to set up a committee as soon as possible to draw up proposals for more effective rules on transit.
The committee should include representatives of parties who have practical experience of transit, such as freight forwarders, freight transport companies, industrial and trading companies.
It is important that the problems be addressed at the highest political level and not be chewed
The Association feels that experience shows that, as they stand, the rules on transit constitute an invitation to fraud.
The acts of fraud committed are large-scale, serious, meticulously planned and carried out by international syndicates who make huge amounts of money whilst running very few risks.
Ultimately, it is the guarantors and insurance companies who have to bear the brunt and pay the customs and other duties which have been evaded. Such a situation is extremely dubious from a moral standpoint and must be stopped in cases where a claim is made against a guarantor because customs or police authorities have not done their jobs properly.
In Sweden, the situation is now such that no insurance company is willing to reinsure guarantees furnished pursuant to the transit convention.
The Union must now take firm action to combat the virtually risk-free exploitation by criminals of the Community transit system, a system which is designed to facilitate international trade and transport.
The whole system is now being put at risk and, along with it, one of the cornerstones of the EU, namely the frontier-free internal market and the free movement of goods etc. which this involves.
Confidence in the whole idea of the Union is at stake.
