EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the European Petroleum Industry Association, Brussels.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Chairman of the Cttee of inquiry info the Community Transit System rue Bernard 97­113 B­I047 Bruxelles
Brussels, 3 May 1996 Ret Ç06/TAC/110B/L
Following your letter of 7 March In which you ask for information, please find herewith my reply,
The supply position of European refineries Is such that in the main there is no need for substantial Import of product from outside the EU.
Even where those products are Imported they rarely enter into the community transit system as they are used in the member state of importation.
Therefore Community transit fraud does not Impact this area of activities of the mineral oil industry.
On the other hand we would like to mention that the Belgian Petroleum Federation and the Dutch Olie Contact Commissie have submitted information to their respective Governments of fraud by the use of false export documentation.
Before 1.1.93 export involved both trade with other member states and trade with third countries.
For trade with third countries we think the volume of such fraud has reduced since 1.1.93 as a result of the measures taken in respect of the single market.
However trade within the EU is affected by fraudulent practices in the sphere of transport and by the absence of physical controls , as we explain in the attached paper.
Yours sincerely.
J. Segreltes Chairman of the EUROPIA Tax Advisory Committee
AIL. ; Mineral oil fraud patterns (2 pages)
u de : 32 2 2849869
MjgfitQ] oil fraud patterns
At this moment the main types of fraud concern CA) fraud with products under excise duty suspension during transport and (B) fraud with excised products caused by the absence of physical frontier controls.
These fraud patterns do not only threaten the tax revenues of the Member States, but also give rise to trade distortions for the mineral oil sector.
As expressed during the Lisbon Conference in November 1995, EUROPIA considers this subject to be an important issue for further discussions with DG XXI of the European Commission.
Transport The transport fraud relates to diversion, whether or not within the limits of transportation losses as accepted by the Member States, of mineral oil products (mainly gasoline, gasoil and kerosene) during national and cross­border transport, under excise duty suspension arrangements.
In general transporters and recipients are not held liable for excises on losses and thus often do not Inform the sender (signatory),
Via organised crime (criminal networks) these products ore being sold via a number of unreliable traders.
An exact estimation of the financial losses concerned cannot be given , As regards The Netherlands the extent of this type of fraud is estimated at approximately Dutch guilders 50 million per year, although this amount may vary over the years.
In Belgium the yearly amount Is reckoned to be between 4 and 12 billion BEF.
Abuse Is made of deficiencies In metering during loading and unloading procedures and control guidelines.
Bunker fraud
Closely related to the above is the so called 'sea bunker fraud'. Gasoil.
MDF and fuel oils are delivered for own use on board of sea­going vessels.
In these cases less product is delivered than actually invoiced.
Any surplus (possibly after Illegal blending) 1$ sold on the market.
This fraud pattern corresponds to the aforementioned transport fraud.
Again, advantage is being taken of the lack of good metering facilities during loading and unloading procedures and control guidelines.
The financial magnitude of this fraud Is not· known.
The Dutch fiscal fraud squad, however, expects It to have at. least the same extent as the transport fraud.
Fraud within the loss­margin
With respect to losses we face serious problems due to diverging standards, as applied by each Member State, Also there exist uncertainty as to which fisc Is competent to impose the taxes and which national excise rates are applicable.
We notice that losses may regularly have a connection with fraud­situations.
I.e. theft within the loss­margin.
Therefore harmonised regulations and co­operation between governments and Industrial circles are required.
Art. 2 bis ­products
The use of AAD's for movements of 'article 2bls­products' (sensitive products) has led to a chaotic situation.
The obligation to use AAD's during the transport of 2bis­products cause an extra workload of several ten thousands of documents per year.
Only some bilateral agreements on a simplified regime for these products have been concluded and those that have been concluded are being Interpreted by each Member State in a different way.
This situation hinders the transport within the community and causes high extra cost.
Moreover, the importance of the excise amount Involved and the risk of fraud are small, but the time spent on the art, 2bis procedures cannot be used to avoid other fraud patterns.
EUROPIA does not want to create impression of no longer wishing to adhere to the requirements of this procedure, but it would like to emphasise the need for administrative streamlining under bilateral treaties.
Recommendations
If the loss shown In the D-measurlng Is above the margin, the transporter and/or (with him) the recipient of the goods should to be held liable.
Please note; at this moment In case of FOB-deliveries organised crime has "carte blanche" as the signatory of the AAD is not able to check the volumes during transport and unloading,
Short term exchange of Information regarding transport figures, flows of transport, and suchlike by electronic data interchange may speed up the identification of fraud.
Absence of physical controls
Due to the great differences between the different excise rates, excised volumes of gasoline and gasoil with origin Luxembourg and Belgium (faxed at relatively low rates) are sold in member states with higher rates via aforementioned network of unreliable traders, without paying the excise duties In the member state of destination.
The extent of this fraud pattern is not known, but according to the Dutch fiscal fraud squad this should amount to at least dozens of millions Dutch guilders per year,
Recommendations i.
Further more, 'suspicious' flows of transport and market-prices should be monitored by industry In consultation with the fiscs.
