COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by Dansk Speditørforening (The Danish Freight Forwarders' Association) Copenhagen.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
DOC EN\CM\291\291256
Danish Freight Forwarders Association Scandiagade 15 2450 Copenhagen SV
European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System Plateau de Kirchberg L-2929 Luxembourg
Introduction
By way of introduction and to put the Committee of Inquiry in the picture I would like to explain that since Denmark's accession on 1 January 1973 the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association has administered the EU transit system with regard to the flat rate guarantee in Denmark.
The way the system operates in Denmark is that the Danish Customs and Tax Administration, on the basis of Commission Regulation (EEC) 2454/93 and the implementing rules for Council Regulation 2913/92 (the EU's customs code) has delegated to the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association the business of administering the flatrate guarantee system in Denmark.
In accordance with this the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association has paid a limited surety deposit to the Danish Customs and Tax Administration whereby the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association assumes responsibility to the Danish Customs and Tax Administration for the payment of duties and charges for those businesses which avail themselves of the system and which, in the event of loss, would not be in a position to pay the duties or charges concerned.
The reason the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association's arrangement with the Danish Customs and Tax Administration is a limited surety deposit is that it was agreed between the parties that those availing themselves of the system could take up to a maximum of 20 surety documents of ECU 7000 per consignment, corresponding to about one million Danish crowns.
Consequently the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association concluded an agreement with the Tryg-Baltica Insurance Company to cover the possible risk assumed by the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association as provider of the surety deposit, and thus, in those cases where the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association is faced with a claim from the Danish Customs and Tax Administration, there can be immediate payment by the insurance company of the amount claimed.
In the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, these arrangements have been particularly satisfactory for the Danish Customs and Tax Administration, the insurance company and those using the system.
In addition to these, consignments are transported in Denmark under the provisions for Comprehensive Guarantees or Individual Guarantees, which together account for a similar amount, so that the total consignments under the EU transit system from Denmark to EU destinations amount to not less than ECU 700 000 000.
However, these figures must be viewed with caution, since there are no exact records in Denmark for transport operations covered by comprehensive or individual guarantees, and the real number could thus be much higher.
In the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association the EU's transit system is a cornerstone of cooperation between the individual EU countries and one of the reasons why it has been possible to expand trade within the EU significantly in the 80s and 90s, which in both political and business terms is of major importance for the realization of the EU's objective.
The transit system offers, in the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, many advantages for a community with a very complex economy and the Association believes that it is improbable that the EU system in general, and business in particular, could achieve the full benefits of an integrated single market without the EU transit system.
It is thus the Association's view that the system is of crucial importance for the establishment of the internal market and EEA.
For business, the EU transit system is also vital for the integration of the Central and Eastern European countries into the EU and for the trade flows which are the natural consequence of the new departure in the Eastern European countries.
In the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association there is no tenable alternative to the present EU transit system.
After the introduction of the single market on 1 January 1993 it is inconceivable that anyone could require payment of duties and charges on the basis of systematic monitoring of access to the Community, since monitoring of this kind would result in an accumulation at the external frontiers and in particular at the road transit points in Central Europe.
As mentioned above, there were no practical problems with the transit system in the years from 1973 to 1993.
DOC EN\CM\291\291256
However, we in Denmark have to admit that there were unfortunately, in the course of 1995, a number of irregularities in the carrying out of transport operations within the EU.
It is the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association's view that these irregularities were due, first and foremost, to the establishment of the EU's single market on 1 January 1993.
As a consequence of the introduction of the EU's internal market, there was a removal of the previous customs formalities at the national frontiers and a removal of the previous monitoring measures.
The lack of harmonization of customs duties and charges in the individual EU countries is a significant contributory factor to the fraud which has now been ascertained.
In the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association the main reason for the fraud which has been ascertained is, however, the lack of cooperation between the national customs authorities and the concomitant lack of practical implementation in practice of the rules which came into force after the creation of the single market.
The transit procedure following the establishment of the EU's internal market
As mentioned above, it is the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association that the main cause of the problems ascertained must be ascribed to the establishment of the single market, in connection with the related lack of the ability of the authorities to operate the rules which came into force on 1 January 1993.
This point of view is best documented by the fact that, before 1993, no problems were ascertained with transport consignments within the EU, and that these first arose after the removal of the previous customs formalities.
If we consider the fact that, at the same time, the establishment of the EU's single market was not accompanied by tighter administrative practices on the part of the authorities, the inevitable result is that criminal elements exploiting the lack of intervention by the authorities for their own gain.
In the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association we did not ascertain any problems with the system before 1995.
At present the Association has 10 cases of fraud involving transport operations, and of these 10, five involve alcohol or cigarettes and the other five concern the transport of 'refund' goods.
We in the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association have carried out an examination of the causes of the fraud.
DOC EN\CM\291\291256
In this connection it has to be noted that the goods in question were thus exported from Denmark and brought into free circulation in the Community without any customs duties or charges being paid on them.
In the majority of the cases, however, the cause of the fraud was that the goods were exported from Denmark and alleged to have arrived at their ultimate destination and duly stamped at the customs office of reception, whereupon copy 5 of the Τ document was returned to the Danish customs authorities.
However, it was ascertained at a very late stage that the customs stamps used were either forgeries or stolen.
These two forms of fraud are of course only the expression of a general trend and it is presumably only the lack of imagination which imposes any limits on other ways of carrying out fraud.
The Association's claim that the primary reason that fraud can occur at all is the fact that the authorities act too late should be seen in conjunction with of of of
Article 356(2) of the implementing provision which states that 'the office destination shall record on the copies of the T1 document the details controls carried out and shall without delay send a copy to the office departure and retain the other copy'.
Problem areas
The security systems are inadequate following the establishment of the EU single market.
There is a lack of cooperation between the EU, the national customs authorities and business.
Poor, slipshod processing by the authorities accumulation of unprocessed documents late return of copies to be returned the investigation procedure for undischarged consignments is started too late no order of priority given to investigation procedures lack of vigilance on the part of users of the system.
As mentioned above, since the introduction of the EU single market there have been no monitoring measures that can counteract the fraud ascertained.
In the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, the EU system must therefore include ways of carrying out ad hoc investigations of transport operations carried out, in such a way as to ascertain to what extent the transport is being carried out in accordance with what is stated on the transit
Adequate resources should be earmarked by the national authorities for immediate investigation and customs clearance of goods when they arrive at the customs office of destination.
One of the common problems, in the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, is that the establishment of the EU single market took place exclusively as a political process without regard for solving, at the same time as the establishment of the single market, a number of practical problems arising as a consequence of the introduction of the single market.
In the Association's view there was not enough knowledge of the problems resulting from the establishment of the single market, either on the part of the politicians or on the part of the team of officials in the Commission.
At national level, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association has on several occasions called on the Danish authorities to cooperate closely with a view to solving problems in the interest of all concerned.
However, we in Denmark have to admit that at no time have the authorities shown any form of willingness to discuss common problems: on the contrary the authorities have bureaucratically insisted that it is not a national matter, but a matter that can only be discussed in Brussels.
This has meant that any practical solution to the problems has been made impossible.
The Danish Freight Forwarders' Association is very surprised at the extent of the fraud considering the fact that both the central EU authorities and the national customs authorities were aware at an early stage of the trend towards considerable irregularities.
The authorities concerned should have acted much earlier by establishing a task force to investigate irregularities.
To illustrate this, it came to the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association's notice that the Danish customs and tax authorities had held a meeting in February 1995 with the EU Court of Auditors at which the discussion centred on the increased fraud.
In spite of this meeting the Danish authorities did not at any time inform the organizations that undertake transport operations under the EU transit system of the increased trend towards fraud in connection with transport operations.
DOC_EN\CM\291\291256
It is the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association's view that the different types of administration in the EU constitute one of the main problems in cooperation between the authorities and industry.
As mentioned above, it is clear from Article 356 of the implementing provisions that the authorities at the recipient's customs station are obliged to return copy 5 of the Τ document without delay.
Although it must be clear to everyone that the expression without delay must be seen as an unconditional requirement to return the form after reception at the customs station, we in Denmark have noted that a large number of demands are made 10-11 months after the departure of the transport from Denmark.
There is also the fact that the Danish customs and tax authorities, to the best knowledge of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, have introduced a practice whereby it is only after three months that the Danish customs authorities write to the principal or send a reminder to the office of destination and that this procedure is followed up by a further written reminder after four, six and then eight months: it must be clear from this that even the national customs authorities are incapable of respecting the implementing provision in Article 356(2) that the processed document should be returned to the customs station of departure without delay.
If the authorities have also introduced an administrative reminder procedure in breach of the provisions, this can be taken as a lack of confidence in the system and poor administration of the legislation in force.
In the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association's view it is evident that if the authorities had in fact respected the requirement to return the documents without delay, a significant proportion of the fraud which has now been ascertained would have been avoided since the time factor is of particularly decisive importance.
In the Association's view there is also a lack of necessary designation of priorities to the investigation procedures to be carried out in order to ascertain whether a transport operation has been properly concluded.
In the view of the Association there should be, both at the customs office of destination and at the customs office of departure, a selective assessment of which transport operations should be specially investigated, and here it should be noted that particularly sensitive consignments as described in Annex 52 to the implementing provisions should be given much higher priority than nonsensitive goods.
In those case where the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association has not received notification from the Danish tax authorities after about three months, the Association implements an immediate investigation into the reasons why a particular transport operation cannot be deemed concluded.
This investigation takes the form of an oral and written request to the company concerned to investigate and document the conclusion of the transport operation,
and that this has been done properly.
The Danish Freight Forwarders' Association also makes direct contact with the customs office of destination indicated, with a request for dispatch of a copy of the stamped copy 5.
In more than 98.5% of the cases in which the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association is told by the Danish authorities that a transport operation has not been concluded properly, we have nevertheless been able to ascertain that the shipment was properly concluded but that the administrative procedure between the national authorities was not functioning, as the document was either still at the customs station of destination or had not been registered at the customs station of departure.
As mentioned above, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association does not feel that we have received sufficient information in time from the Danish authorities about problems with the EU transit system.
Since the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association became aware of the problems of the system in summer 1995, the Association has continually mailed circulars to users of the system to tell them to display the necessary vigilance, with proposals for complying with the safety procedures and recommending acquaintance with the transporters used, knowledge and acquaintance of the recipients of the goods and immediate follow-up regarding the conclusion of the transport operation.
In this context a whole series of meeting has been held under the auspices of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association, at which the problems have been defined and those present have been called on to be careful about transport operations particularly of sensitive goods.
It is the view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association that this advice alone has caused a not insignificant drop in the number of cases of fraud involving goods dispatched from Denmark.
As mentioned at the outset, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association believes that there is no tenable alternative to the existing EU transit system.
The Commission has suggested that the transit problems that have arisen should be regarded as a business problem for the transport sector alone and that, in this case, it is up to the transport sector to ensure that transport operations can be carried out properly.
The view of the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association is that it is of crucial importance here to make it clear that the cases of fraud ascertained in Denmark can all be classed 'criminality originating in other countries'.
DOC EN\CM\291\291256
There is therefore nothing to support the claim that the fraud ascertained can be ascribed to a certain branch of industry: instead, it must be seen as a serious social problem.
However, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association doubts whether such a system could be introduced and implemented rapidly enough, since the latest indications from DG XXI are that this could only take place in 1998/99 at the earliest.
In negotiations with the Danish customs and tax authorities, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association has already proposed such a model: however, it was rejected on the grounds of lack of resources for the implementation of monitoring or cooperation, particularly with regard to the tightening up of transport operations involving sensitive goods.
B. Introduction of an on-line computer system
DOC_EN\CM\291\291256
C. Reduction of time needed to process cases
The Danish Freight Forwarders' Association believes that this is an expression of the lack of a desire on the part of the authorities to cooperate in solving the problems since it merely extends the time taken to process a case and is certainly not the expression of a wish for efficient, well-organized handling of the case.
DOC_EN\CM\291\291256
In addition to this, the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association considers it reasonable that ways should be considered and investigated of introducing rules to make it impossible to change the destination once a consignment has departed unless there is a new transit procedure with a new destination.
The Danish Freight Forwarders' Association believes that it would be particularly radical for the practical implementation of transport operations if binding itineraries were laid down.
We should, however, admit that it will be necessary to examine the extent to which the introduction of binding itineraries would be a possible way of proceeding.
F. Integrated cooperation between the parties involved
With a view to examining possible ways of establishing further measures to prevent the increased fraud and to counter new problems in the future it is proposed that formal cooperation should be established between a representative of the Commission and the parties involved to provide continuous assessment of the current rules and of the need to introduce new measures.
In conclusion the Danish Freight Forwarders' Association would submit that it is the Association's view that the present EU transit system is of vital importance not only for the transport industry but also for a large number of export companies, and that it has been impossible, in the Association's view, to find any tenable alternative to the present system.
However, it is important, in the Association's view, for the authorities to meet the Customs Code requirement of speedy and efficient processing of cases, since such processing is of crucial importance for preventing serious problems with transit transport in the future.
It is furthermore a characteristic of the problems which have now arisen that they are caused to a great extent by lack of cooperation and lack of information about problems.
It is therefore of the greatest importance that there should be formal cooperation between the parties concerned to ensure that possible irregularities and distortions are stopped at a very early stage.
Transport within the EU is of fundamental importance for the Community and must be regarded as one of the basic pillars of integrated trade.
It is therefore important that reasonable measures are taken rapidly to counter the increasing fraud, but that they should be such as to make it possible in practice to undertake transport operations under reasonable conditions.
Danish Freight Forwarders' Association
