DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
Letter dated 30 April 1996 from Mr P.J.N, van Os, Director of FENEX (Rotterdam), to the Temporary Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Further to the call for evidence by the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System (OJ C 60, 29.02.1996) with a view to investigating allegations of contraventions or maladministration under the Community Transit System, we enclose the following contribution.
We hope that our comments and remarks will contribute to your investigation.
We also hope that any proposals you may have to improve the Community Transit System will take account of our suggestions.
We should be grateful if we could discuss this with you in person.
(closing formula and signature)
Contribution from FENEX, Netherlands Organization for Forwarding and Logistics, on the Community Transit System for the European Parliament's Temporary Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
Further to the call for evidence in connection with the work of the European Parliament's Temporary Committee of Inquiry into the Community transit system (OJ C 60, 29.02.1996, p.
1), FENEX would like to make the following contribution.
Completing customs formalities for European goods transports is one of the fundamental tasks of forwarding agents acting on behalf of trade and industry to ensure that goods can reach their outward destination efficiently.
The majority of the Community transit declarations are made by forwarding agents on behalf of their clients in trade and industry.
In this connection it is important to point out that the Community transit system is in itself a very important facility for trade and industry.
An internal market without the possibility of the Community Transit System is inconceivable.
The transit system makes it possible to transport goods without having to pay duties and the levies on the spot.
Without the customs transit system goods would have to be imported directly on passing the external frontiers of the European Union.
The possibility of storing third country goods in a customs warehouse is also of considerable importance to trade and industry.
For a variety of trade policy reasons it may be more advantageous to defer imports. Furthermore, many thirdcountry goods are stored in a warehouse although they are ultimately intended to leave the EU or their destination is not yet known.
If Community transit were no longer possible, storage in a customs warehouse would be a thing of the past, with all that that entails.
As far as the customs authorities are concerned it would mean an enormous concentration of forces at the EU's external frontiers.
Hence, a solution to the problems connected with the Community transit system does not lie in abolishing this system, not least because of the major advantages it affords trade and industry.
However, the way in which the system works needs to be drastically improved.
The problems experienced in the Community transit system stem from a number of factors.
For example, it appears that a large proportion of the problems connected with the non-discharge of T-documents are structural in nature, caused by the fact that the certificate of discharge is either not returned by the customs administration of the Member State or only after some delay.
However, returning the return copy to the office of departure is of crucial importance, since it is only then that a customs transit document is regarded as formally completed.
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
In addition, forwarding agents are faced with fraud in the Community transit system perpetrated by dishonest elements in society, some of whom are organized gangs, who are able to evade the customs laws.
If it subsequently appears that the rules have not been respected, they receive a claim for recovery of the duties, levies and taxes due.
This happens even if the failure to respect the rules has nothing to do with them. It is particularly galling that if all the claims for fraud over the last few years were enforced this would lead to the bankruptcy of bona-fide forwarding agents with all the financial and social consequences that this would entail.
Possible causes of the problems in the Community transit system
From the economic point of view, the customs rules should as far as possible take account of the legitimate interests of trade and industry. The guiding principle should be that the transport of goods is impeded as little as possible.
However, the rules proceed from the premise that completion of the procedures can be satisfactorily monitored and checked by government bodies working closely together.
Yet nothing could be further from the truth.
In its communication on 'fraud in the transit procedure, solutions foreseen and prospectus for the future' (COM (95)108 final, 29.03.1995), the Commission itself says that the transit systems are in a state of crisis.
National administrations are responsible for the quality of administrative controls and the management of the system.
The quality of these controls has fallen progressively over the last few years.
This has resulted in an increasing number of complaints from economic operators concerning unacceptable delays in the system.
The Commission goes on to say that these delays are typically caused by belated return of the return copy, leading to the accumulation of uncleared documents.
Furthermore, inquiry procedures regarding outstanding movements are not initiated at a sufficiently early stage, and are not pursued with sufficient priority and vigour.
The result is that these operational problems have created an ideal breeding ground for fraudulent activities.
The Commission is also of the opinion that fraud is often not detected until a very late stage in the procedure because of unacceptable delays in the system and because some of the mechanisms used are not longer able to cope.
It is clear from the foregoing that the Commission has come to the same conclusion as FENEX and CLECAT with regard to the possible causes of the problems in the Community transit system.
It should be mentioned here that it is an inherent feature of the present system that these delays can occur, since 18 million T-documents in quadruplicate (some 75 million forms) have to pass through the hands of customs officials.
Furthermore, handling these documents is one of the tasks which the customs have to carry out.
It is not inconceivable that, given the other major tasks, administrative handling of the transit documents is not their first priority.
Furthermore, the customs rules themselves militate against supervision of the goods.
It is, after all, quite possible that an initial declaration states that the goods are intended for, or pass in transit through, a specific Member State, while it subsequently appears that they went to or via an entirely different Member State.
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
The following factors may also have contributed to a reduction in the frequency and efficiency of customs controls: the abolition in 1990 of the transit advice note for the EC's internal frontiers; the political change in the countries of the former Eastern bloc resulting in an increased volume of goods; the complete removal of controls at internal frontiers of the EU; the reduction in the number of customs offices within the EU and, consequently, a reduction in the number of customs officials.
Another factor of importance is that in recent years the return procedure between customs administrations has further deteriorated.
The various bodies involved were not in a position to compensate for the loss of a number of checks by improving the return procedure.
Fraud can never be entirely eliminated.
However, the rules and the way in which they are applied should not result in fraud being practised over a long period so that the duties payable mount up out of all proportion.
Measures to improve the system should be aimed at shortening the lines of communication and administrative procedures, to prevent this happening.
Computerizing the customs transit system is the solution to the problems that have been found in that system.
It would literally result in lines of communication becoming shorter and in delays becoming a thing of the past.
However, computerization has been under discussion for a number of years without any tangible results.
It is all the more welcome, then, that some time ago the Commission again made computerization of the system one of its priorities.
It is also of the utmost importance for a computerized system to work to the advantage of the principal.
For example, the system should take account of the system often used for principals in the EU of authorized consignor and authorized consignee.
Furthermore, a principal should also be in a position himself to check in the system: whether a recipient is also an authorized consignee for the customs transit; whether the goods and the document have arrived at the office of destination; whether a report has reached the office of departure from the office of destination with regard to due completion of the procedure.
As stated above, computerization of the customs transit system is regarded as the solution.
However, pending computerization in the short term changes must be made to the Community rules with a view to improving the way the system currently operates.
In view of the future existence of firms, it is of the utmost importance for the liability of the principal to be made clear.
On the basis of the Community rules, the principal has the possibility of proving regularity of a transit operation if the return copy has not been received by the office of departure.
In practice, however, it appears that this is very difficult. CLECAT has in the past made proposals on improving the Community transit system.
For example, it has proposed giving declarants better opportunities of proving that goods have been presented at the office of destination.
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
One positive feature in this connection is the recent amendment to Article 380 of the Commission Regulation for the implementation of the Community Customs Code .
This article says that the principal can prove regularity of the transit operation by means of a customs or trade document certified by the customs authorities which contains sufficient information to identify the goods.
Regrettably, it has not yet been decided in what way the regularity of the procedure is to be demonstrated. It should in any case be possible for the principal - for all types of goods - to have an additional fifth copy of the document stamped by the office of destination.
However, an obligation on behalf of the customs authorities to cooperate in this respect must be included in the Community rules. This could be included in Article 357(1).
The amendment to Article 380 does not go far enough. It should be possible, using all other possible documents, to prove regularity of the transit operation.
They could include ones which when combined permit an identification of the goods, for example invoices, freight letters, payments etc.
Furthermore, as far as presenting proof by the principal is concerned, it is an unnecessary obstacle for him to have to have the documents mentioned in Article 380 stamped by customs.
In this connection the rules on authorized consignee should also involve the latter being authorized to sign a special receipt which contains a complete identification of the goods or an additional copy of the fifth form.
This could be done in Article 406(3) and could also be deemed to be proof within the meaning of Article 380, without the need to have these documents stamped by the customs authority.
If the principal can prove regularity of the transit operation pursuant to the modified Article 380, the customs authority then has the obligation to prove irregularity of the operation, and if it is unable to do so it should not be able to claim compensation.
Given the fact that formal discharge of the procedure ultimately depends on the administrative processing by customs of the return copy, it should be more a task for the customs to demonstrate proof of irregularity of an operation.
The customs authorities have their own internal procedures for establishing irregularity of operations.
Declarants do not have such an option, except via the customs authorities.
Changes to the rules in connection with the return procedure
Furthermore, non-return or belated return of the return copy should resolve principals of liability in the sense that the customs administration must prove that any irregularities have occurred as a result of action on behalf of the principal.
To this end, a clause should be included in the Community Customs Code (CCC) so that the office of destination is obliged to return the return copy within two days, in the case of goods liable to fraud, and within 15 days in the case of other goods.
A clause should also be included to the effect that the office of departure must make immediate inquiries with the principal as to what has happened with fraud-sensitive goods, and within eight weeks in the case
Commission Regulation EEC No 2454/93
DOC_EN\CM\291\291258
of other goods. The inquiry request that in general is made after four months by the office of departure to the office of destination, should be regulated in the CCC.
The period in question should also be shortened.
Furthermore, the CCC should contain provisions to the effect of cooperation by the office of destination within a specific period.
The CCC should make it clear that these points are included to protect the principal and that if the offices of departure and destination do not comply with the abovementioned obligations, the result will be to absolve the principal of his liability in the first instance.
Principals cannot reasonably be expected to react promptly to such notifications if it subsequently appears that most documents are discharged because the return copy is received.
Further changes to Community rules and policy on supervision
The Community rules prescribe a number of time-limits for the customs services of the Member States which have to be respected.
Article 379 says that where the consignment has not been presented to the office of destination and the place where the offence or irregularity occurred cannot be established the office of departure shall notify the principal of this fact as soon as possible and in any case before the end of the eleventh month following the date of registration of the Community transit declaration.
To protect the principal acting in good faith this period should be foreshortened; for example, to a period of six months.
Non-receipt of notification concerning nonclearance affords only a pseudo-protection to the principal in these cases, since the duties etc. can be recovered at any time within a period of three years.
In this connection, with a view to improving customs checks CLECAT has proposed that customs offices should be obliged to state on the special certificate of receipt, or the additional copy No 5, and the regular return copy the number of the entry in the administration.
The customs office of departure should regularly, on a sample basis, check on the basis of the entry number with the office of destination whether the operation has been properly completed.
This approach would help to avoid the situation where principals learn, only after some length of time has elapsed, that irregularities have been found in their documents.
Member States and the Commission are already endeavouring to make improvements in the system through administrative agreements.
In this connection we would point to the introduction of the system of pre-notification of certain types of goods liable to fraud.
Furthermore, for the goods which are excluded from the system of comprehensive security at the request of Spain and Germany, a special procedure is being introduced with regard to an additional No 5 copy which the principal can have stamped by the office of destination and which has to be used by the office of departure to establish whether the goods have been presented at the office of destination.
It should be noted here that the system of preregistration and of checking presentation at the office of destination by the
office of departure must be seen as positive with regard to preventing and combating fraud.
Liability
It is unreasonable for demonstrably fraudulent activities on the part of third parties and the poor functioning of customs administration to be passed on in full to bona-fide principals.
In addition, it is also possible that persons other than the client and the forwarding agent are involved in the fraud.
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
Import duties shall be repaid or remitted where:
A clause should be added to this article that import duties will be repaid or remitted where:
Article 203(3) of the Community Customs Code lists persons who may be deemed to be debtors if goods are removed from customs supervision.
'Where several persons are liable for payment of one customs debt, they shall be jointly and severally liable for such debt. When claims are made against several debtors the sequence used shall be the sequence given in the relevant articles.
A debtor can only be held liable for the customs debt if there is clear proof that the debt cannot be paid by a person or persons higher up the list. '
A recent amendment of Article 356 of the Implementing Provisions states that, with regard to certain goods subject to fraud, it is not possible to change the office of destination or the route without the consent of the principal.
Article 378 of the Implementing Provisions specifies which Member State is entitled to collect the duties etc.
In practice principals are faced with major problems if collection is carried out by a Member State different to the one where the principal is registered.
The problems, including problems of languages and of communications, also weaken his position.
From this point of view it would be useful for the Member State where the principal has his registered offices to be the only one authorized to collect outstanding duties.
'If a consignment has not arrived at the office of destination, the offence or irregularity is deemed to have occurred in the Member State where the principal has his registered offices.'
DOC EN\CM\291\291258
(Closing formula and signature)
PROBLEMS INVOLVED IN THE EUROPEAN CUSTOMS TRANSIT SYSTEM
The European customs transit system is currently undergoing a serious crisis.
The fact that this crisis is affecting both customs transit systems, the Community Transit System and the TIR System, shows that this is a problem of substance, not a freak occurrence.
The sums allegedly involved in case of fraud are enormous.
At this level, the accuracy of the figures becomes secondary.
With particular regard to the Community Transit System, it is impossible, because of the number of uncleared transport documents, to distinguish between shortcomings in administrative formalities and actual cases of fraud.
The situation is all the more serious because it makes it difficult to take properly targeted measures.
How can we combat fraud when the limits of fraud are not defined and while fraudulent operations are totally lost among thousands of transit documents which have not been cleared because of administrative shortcomings?
For a long time, the two systems operated in a generally satisfactory manner
The TIR System goes back to 1949, in its initial form, and has been widely used since 1960.
The Community Transit System dates from 1970.
This system has been gradually deteriorating since 1980.
The TIR System has only been deteriorating since 1990, but that deterioration has been seen more sharply because of the logical reactions of the guarantor, the IRU, to the systematic claims against the system, whereas, in the case of the Community Transit System, the enormous scale of the trade in question and the large number of guarantors have entailed a certain amount of inertia on the part of the Administrations which have clearly recoiled in the face of the size of the task to be undertaken.
DOC EN\CM\299\299476
The deterioration is caused by several factors
The Community Transit System was conceived and put in place for a group of six countries, three of which (the Benelux countries) had already created a customs union 15 years previously.
Moreover, those countries formed a reasonably coherent geographical area and had similar legal and administrative systems.
Since then, the Community has grown to a point where it now has 15 members and is contemplating further enlargement.
The geographical and administrative outcome of all this is that the European Union is a heterogeneous area that is difficult to keep under control.
And, what is more, the number of trade transactions has increased massively.
By themselves, the quoted figures of 9 million Τ documents issued in one year by France and 11 million by Germany are enough to illustrate the insurmountable scale of the administrative tasks arising therefrom.
The TIR System, too, has undergone a quantitative increase, with particular regard to the number of contracting parties.
Once recognized by the United Nations, the new countries emerging from the collapse of the USSR have been able to become contracting parties to Conventions administered by the UN without having to furnish proof that they are capable of applying the provisions of such Conventions.
In the countries of Eastern Europe, which already existed as individual entities, the customs administrations have been weakened and disorganized.
Any restrictive measures are regarded as being throwbacks to the former regime, and this has also helped the growth in organized crime.
Faced by this situation, the reaction of the customs services has been weak.
Most of the time, as regards the TIR System, and contrary to the provisions of the TIR Convention, the customs services have done no more than submit claims to the guarantee chain, within the time-limit laid down of two years, systematically pushed to the limits, without trying to find the perpetrators of cases of fraud, although in many cases investigations would have been possible.
But the task of the customs service is to combat fraud, not to use administrative channels to claim money from the insurance bodies.
In the case of the Community Transit System, it is the sheer scale of the administrative formalities which has resulted in inertia.
We only need to look at the number of requests for information submitted over the last few years that have remained unanswered.
Reference has already been made to the lack of organization in the countries of Eastern Europe.
Elsewhere, the concept of progress in the European Union has generally been deemed to entail the disappearance of the customs services.
The abolition of internal frontiers has led to a reduction in the number of staff, in equipment and in funds.
The logical consequence of this has been the disarming of the customs services, both psychologically and materially.
DOC_EN\CM\299\299476
To that has been added a certain amount of confusion in the current rules.
In his daily work, the customs officer no longer knows whether he should carry out an inspection or not, whether or not the TIR carnet may be used and how.
The results are errors in entries in the accounts and forgotten clearances, as well as a very obvious tendency to close the files in administrative terms by claiming payments from the guaranteeing associations.
It is also characteristic to note that, during debates in the UN/ECE Working Party on Customs Questions affecting Transport (WP 30), which administers the TIR Convention, whenever a reference was made to the increase in the number of cases of fraud, the standard answer was to increase the amount of the guarantee, whereas what was needed was a means of finding the resources to improve the fight against fraud.
We must also take full account of the fact that a customs guarantee system actually based on the system of insurance cannot cope with the current scale of losses.
What is more, systematic payments by the guaranteeing associations would end up by encouraging fraud, because the perpetrators would be subject to no penalties whatsoever.
Transit under customs seal is and will continue to be indispensable, whether just one country is involved or a number of countries.
The possibility of transporting goods with taxes, duties and any other statutory measure being suspended is fundamental for a modern economy.
It will remain essential, even in the event of a fully federal Europe coming into being.
It is, then, all the more urgent to undertake an indepth reform of the systems involved, bearing in mind experience already acquired and the real reasons for the crisis.
From the outset, we must repeat that any customs transit scheme presupposes that its operation is fully and correctly applied by the customs administrations themselves.
A global European concept of transport of goods
The European Union must have accurate information concerning movements on its territory if it is to avoid becoming one enormous uncontrolled area.
It must maintain the same control over external movements, particularly with countries with which it is or will be linked by various agreements.
The EU must therefore retain the possibility of using the two transit systems, its own and the TIR System which links it to third countries.
DOC_EN\CM\299\299476
Principles of the reform
These include:
Thorough study of the customs transit requirements of the European Union and the definition of a global concept of European transit.
Definition of the role of the customs administrations, essentially, the correct application of procedures and fight against fraud.
Provision of adequate resources.
Precise definition of liabilities, with particular regard to the 'principal' in the Community Transit System.
In the Community Transit System, a study of the simplification of guarantees (the current range is too complex).
Investigation into a 'ticket' guarantee system for isolated operators.
In the TIR System, a revision of the conditions for claiming payment under the guarantee system so as to make the fight against fraud the priority.
Such investigation into modern inspection resources is indispensable.
It must not create the illusion of a transit system which would be automatically self-monitoring. That is not possible.
Priority remains with the activities of the customs officers responsible primarily for the implementation of customs procedures.
We must avoid a situation where an advanced system gives the illusion of security, since the imagination of the perpetrators of fraud knows no bounds.
Publication of a Europe customs transit manual.
This manual would be identical for all the Member States. By means of tables, chronograms and the like, it should define clearly and simply the cases when it was to be used and the action to be taken in the event of irregularities, incidents or accidents.
The manual would be issued to customs officers.
There might be a case for publishing a version for 'operators'.
It would, naturally, cover the two current systems.
DOC EN\CM\299\299476
CONCLUSION
Is there any need to stress the urgent nature of the measures to be taken?
The scale of fraud demonstrates that illegal transactions are no longer isolated cases.
They illustrate the gradual establishment of a 'grey' market involving also the laundering of profits from drug trafficking, the provision of funds for the drugs market and the progressive contamination of all commercial sectors.
The scale of the fraud explains, at least in part, the relatively sluggish speed of the economy at a time when all the conditions for economic revival are present.
Professional fraud fighters are ready and waiting.
A clear definition of their task and the provision of modern resources would enable them to succeed in concerted action at European Union level.
DOC EN\CM\299\299476
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Conseil National du Patronat Français, Paris.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
DOC EN\CM\301\301281
Dear Mr Shackleton,
Please find attached a second general document from the CNPF on fraud affecting the Community transit system.
(closing formula and signature)
DOC EN\CM\301\301281
28 May 1996 (rev)
Contribution by the CNPF to the parliamentary inquiry on the current operation of the Community customs transit system
The foundations of the system
The Community transit system has been in operation in the Community since 1 January 1970.
Since that time, its objective has been to prevent stops at external borders as a result of customs, tax or other regulations.
The bases of the system are simple:
A document describing the contents of the consignment in accordance with commercial practice was drawn up at the customs office of departure in a Member State by the person responsible for the transport who undertook to present the consignment again at the customs office of the country of destination.
One copy was kept at the office in the country of departure and two copies were given to the transporter.
The customs office of the Member State of destination sent one copy to the customs office of departure and kept the other.
(a) a frontier transit note was deposited which made it possible to check that the means of transport had entered a state (either a transit or the state of destination).
This formality, which was abolished many years ago, was intended to determine where necessary (e.g. if the means of transport had disappeared) the last country which it had entered and thus the country whose criminal, customs or tax law was to be used as a basis for prosecution, recovery and penalties.
(b) checks could be carried out at any point in the EEC.
A guarantee system worked out after consultation with European banks had been set up to enable it to be accessible to all and in line with the risks actually known.
The system was particularly flexible and was adaptable to different situations.
It enabled transport firms to increase their productivity and perceptibly
DOC EN\CM\301\301281
reduced the costs borne by exporting firms which were enabled to benefit from simplified procedures appropriate to their needs or even computerized.
It called for close collaboration between the customs administrations responsible for managing the system but it is clear that it was and still is difficult - in the absence of a body of Community officials - to obtain perfect results in this connection for the following reasons:
When a consignment leaves the territory of a country or is merely crossing it, the administration's reaction is to take less interest in it than if the goods were destined for the national market.
The system went astray in a number of cases, particularly when import/export agents unilaterally assumed the responsibility of signing transit declarations, thereby undertaking to present the goods again at the destination whereas this undertaking may only be entered into by the transporter.
By so doing, they were able to continue their activities, both at external borders and within the EEC, in collusion with the local customs services which saw it as a way of guaranteeing their own jobs.
In such cases, they did not, however, increase checks at the point of departure.
The existence of the deposit payment system was regarded as sufficient reason for not carrying out checks.
The same causes produced the same effects at the point of destination.
The discharge of documents led to difficulties in view of the large number of documents involved.
Central offices for dealing with all declarations were set up in the Member States, but their reliability varied from state to state.
When inquiry notices were sent out following failure to discharge documents, they were implemented late or even with a certain lack of interest.
A large number of allegedly undischarged transactions proved to be regular.
The system worked for 25 years in spite of these difficulties, even though frauds were committed.
It should be noted that the shortcomings noted above are identical under all transit systems and in particular have characterized the TIR scheme for a long time.
The current situation which led to setting up the parliamentary committee of inquiry shows, however, that it has reached the limits of tolerance, particularly since the creation of the large single market and the opening up of markets in Central and Eastern Europe.
DOC_EN\CM\301\301281
Proposals
To remedy this situation, a wide variety of complementary measures should be implemented.
An awareness-raising campaign should be launched.
Those involved must be reminded of the essential characteristic of a transit operation: The transporter must undertake to present again at the point of destination the goods contained in the means of transport for which he is responsible.
A way must be found of making the guarantees more appropriate.
The guarantee must be assessed in the light of the goods transported, complying with the standards laid down by Community rules on this subject.
Import/export agents must be more selective in their activities.
Import/export agents must be aware that in substituting themselves for their principal they are shouldering the transporter's commitments.
They should only act in this capacity with extreme caution and only for reliable customers.
On the other hand, they are always permitted, if the customs administrations of their country agree to it, to stand surety for the transporter, but there again on the understanding that they are committing themselves.
Greater responsibility on the part of the national customs services The function of customs administrations must be performed, and if gaps continue to appear in cooperation between administrations, a body of Community controllers of transit operations must be set up and deployed at external borders and principal crossing points.
(a) the registration number of each declaration must appear as follows:
Code number of office of destination
Code number of office of departure
Series number of office of departure
This presupposes a universally accepted coding, but the postcode of the locality could serve as a reference point.
DOC_EN\CM\301\301281
It is now possible to follow consignments by using modern methods available to operators, e.g. tagging of all lorries (major haulage firms already use this system for their own purposes).
Promotional measures could be undertaken with national or Community funding conditional on administrative action (adaptation of checks and deposit payments for example).
Further consideration should of course be given to all these points while, at the same time, investigating the possibility of a 'variable geometry' procedure to enable reliable firms to continue enjoying the benefits of the Community or other transit systems at the minimum administrative cost.
