A submission to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry on Fraud in the Community Transit System by Eric Ellen, Executive
Nations in 1919 to serve world business by promoting trade, investment and open markets for goods and services.
ICC-Commercial Crime Services (CCS) was created in 1982 as a specialised division of the ICC.
A major part of the work of ICC-CCS is dealing with worldwide problems of fraud, the forgery of documentation and the theft of cargoes.
Fraud is endemic in international trade. The means by which most frauds are committed is paper - letters of credit, bills of lading, invoices, certificates of origin, packing lists and numerous other documents which can be used to deceive either seller, buyer or bank acting as guarantor.
The checks to prevent fraud in international trade are best carried out before and during the actual business transaction.
Businesses which are at financial risk from fraud employ banks, inspection companies and other
The most common types of fraud in the Community Transit (CT) system are:-
The nominal victims of fraud are the Member States through loss of tax or duty.
Member States.
Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union. The International Road Transport Union
Routiers (TIR) system for these destinations.
As in the CT system the increase in traffic has resulted in an increase in fraud.
Action Taken By The European Commission
The European Commission has formulated short term solutions to the problems of fraud in the CT system: special procedures for high risk goods and a commitment to computerisation ( broadly similar to solutions proposed for fraud problems in the TIR system).
The introduction of an individual guarantee as part of the special procedures for high risk goods in the CT system appears to provide simply another opportunity for forgery.
The solutions in general, mean that enquiries will still be carried out retrospectively by Member States that are guaranteed their revenue.
The guarantors, transport operators and the freight forwarders will remain the victims.
The time scale for the computerisation of the CT system (all countries and all
Customs offices by the end of 1998) appears desirable but, according to various trade bodies, optimistic.
Customs service but will not reduce the incentive for fraud which is driven by the disparity in rates of tax and duty in the Member States.
The current problems of fraud in both the CT and TIR system are a mirror image of the problems of maritime trading which led to the creation of the ICC-Commercial
Crime Services. The CCS approach to the problem includes the maintenance of a large specialised database with information on fraudulent companies and persons involved in international trade.
Suspect documents such as bills of lading, letters of credit are routinely referred to the CCS for authentication.
The CCS conducts investigations, gives advice and publishes regular confidential bulletins to banks, insurance companies, traders and business in general to provide up to date information and highlight trends in fraudulent trading.
(The IRU approached the CCS in the Autumn of 1995 to explore the possibility of applying CCS methods to the TIR system.)
There is no equivalent of the CCS for businesses in the CT System yet the problems are the same.
Everyone involved in business
• Analysis should be carried out of the methods, types and extent of fraud; the common factors, people, companies and commodities involved.
• An education programme on the operation of the CT system for enforcement officials; and
• The active pursuit and prosecution of offenders by enforcement bodies.
