Thank you once again for the invitation to appear before the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry.
The vast majority of these procedures are completed in the proper manner.
The new situation took the authorities and the business community by surprise.
It makes no sense for astronomical customs payments to be demanded from principals when it is, in the final analysis, criminal third parties who have lined their pockets.
Before a future reform of the transit procedure legislation is considered in detail, we feel it is important to bear the following basic idea in mind:
Flourishing trade between the EU and third countries is inconceivable without transit procedures. All concerned - the customs administration, importers and exporters and forwarding agents - need the transit system.
In our opinion, it is a general principle of justice that the risks inherent in a procedure used by several parties should not be borne by one of them.
We therefore recommend once again that the keynote paper we have forwarded to you be made the subject of a proposal for the reform of the transit system.
We would add that we continue to regard the decision on such an extension as a political decision (which will be irreversible because of its geopolitical significance).
The decision will ultimately be based on the wide-ranging efforts to bring the Central and Eastern European countries closer to the Community; we refer in particular to the Council's report to the European Council meeting in Essen on 9 and 10 December 1994 (Annex IV to the Presidency conclusions).
