Dear M.Tomlinson,
As the President of CLECAT, M.Chabrerie and the undersigned had the opportunity to stress during the meeting wc had wittyyou CLECAT (s. enclosed synopsis "What about CLECAT") our Liaison Committee is the most representative body in the European Union both of the freight forwarding and customs agents industry.
We already had the opportunity to give you some answers to the first question.
As mentioned during our last dialogue we are at your entire disposal to examine enhancement of future reform perspectives.
Looking forward to meet you as soon as possible I remain
CLECAT represents the european freight forwarders and customs agents organizations with the European Institutions.
These Member Federations organize altogether about 20.000 companies employing altogether about 4QQ.Q00people.
The organization was founded in 1958, only very shortly afier the establishment of the Treaty of Rome.
Since that time it is regularly consulted by the European Institutions on all kinds of issues in theßeld of transport and logistics.
As organizers of the transport chain, freight forwarders provide comprehensive services to their customers, that is the European shipping industry. ín order to serve their clients (the European industry) in the best possible way, it is the freight forwarder's mission always to opt for the most efficient and most cost effective (door to door or point to point) transport solution.
As the organizers of lrunòport the European freight forwarders control about 60 to 65% of the cargo transported by road (most probably over 90% when you isolate the transport of small parcels), between 90 % and 95 % of the cargo transported by air, over 65 % of maritime cargo, They also play a major role both in containerized and non containerized transport by inland waterways, railways and combined transport.
There is no commodity freight, forwarders and customs agents do not deal with.
Freight forwarders and customs agents organize or support lhe transport of a variety of goods (general cargo, dangerous goods, cars, cereals, heavy goods etc.)
The organization of point to point or multimodal door to door transport requires ever more professional skills, know how and technical means both with respect to the physical handling of the goods (vehicles, handling resources), and with respect to information and documents flow.
CLECAT notes that Regulation 482/96, published in Official Jonrnal of the European Communities L70 on 20 March 1996, contains some improvements to the Community Transit Regime.
CLECAT reaffirms that the Community Transit System is vital to the smooth functioning of the economy of the European Union.
Indeed, in many cases, goods coming from third countries should be exempt from duties and toes, and exterior (rode rules should be suspended while they are moved between their pointa of entry to and exit from the EuropeAn Union and the bonded warehouses or zones within the territory of tlie European Union.
• responsibility and guarantee offered by the principals;
Regulation 482/96 of 19 March 1996 stipulates that users of the Community Transit System must be established in the country of origin of the operation in question and that they must have been active within the appropriate sector for a period of six months.
In addition, the principals must fulfill their responsibilities by offering:
Proof of unqualified delivery to an approved consignee would constitute settlement of a transit operation nnd release the principal from any further obligations.
It also insists that efforts be made to find ways of improving the circulation of paper documents, e.g. by using computerized information exchange facilities (EDI), bar codes or microchips.
Please find enclosed the opinion of our organization on the interim report No 2 of the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System.
OPINION OF THE 'CÂMARA DOS DESPACHANTES OFICIAIS' ON THE INTERIM REPORT No 2 OF THE COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
This report is based on a sound approach to the problems of transit in the Community.
The proposed outline for the final report suggests that it will cover all the situations relevant to a responsible analysis and draw the necessary conclusions.
Nonetheless, without wishing to question the contents of the report, we consider that the following basic points need emphasizing:
A. THE VITAL ROLE OF TRANSIT IN THE COMMUNITY
Transit within the Community is essential to the harmonious development of the Community and its Member States.
It is unthinkable that any problems or abuses relating to the transit regime could be used as a pretext for supporting, or even suggesting, its abolition.
Such an act would merely create a situation of unfair competition and unquantifiable income losses for economic operators in the Community, with similar knock-on effects for all those indirectly linked to them.
The death of the patient does not mean that his illness was cured.
The disappearance of the Community transit regime would, beyond all doubt, encourage and create developmental asymmetries which would be far more pernicious and intractable than the problems now under discussion.
B. THE POLITICAL ASPECT
We consider that the problems currently facing the Community transit regime are largely the result of a lack of political will (unwillingness to change the present situation) or of a political desire to maintain the status quo.
This error of omission or commission by the Community and the Member States is reflected in the following areas:
Thanks to the completion of the internal market and the major changes which have occurred in their traditional sphere of action, the customs services are still extremely unsure what their exact powers are.
With this appearing to be the new dominant attitude, they have all but universally downgraded their basic, if not exclusive 'priority' functions of control and information in favour of cooperation actions with economic operators.
The lack of articulation of the customs services with other bodies and institutions is more than visible; as a result, their expertise and technical capacities are not being used to the full.
In this area, especially with respect to possible specific actions, in connection, for instance, with the 'administrative procedures' discussed in section IV.A of the report, attention should be drawn to the proposals made by our organization in its study (already distributed to various Community and national authorities) and to the key role which private operators should play in checks under the regime.
THE POLICE AUTHORITIES
In the wake of reorganization, some Member States do not at this moment have a fiscal police division to deal with crime in this sector.
In the absence of monitoring, checking or preventive measures, the few cases detected are passed to the usual legal bodies, which are themselves, obviously, not properly qualified to carry out types of analysis and action which require substantial specialized knowledge.
THE NEED FOR STANDARD PROCEDURES
With the aim of encouraging use of the services of economic operators in their territory, some Member States have permitted an interpretation of the procedures to be used for transit operations which manifestly contradicts the regulations for the sector and constitutes an incentive to their infringement.
The introduction of standard procedures, along with monitoring, would obviously have prevented many of the problems now arising.
C. EXTENSION OF THE REGIME
In view of the above, and in line with the position expressed in the report, we consider that there is no case for extending the Community transit regime to the Visegrad countries until the necessary reforms have been made.
Lisbon, 26 June 1985
