DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Introduction
On the 1st Januaiy 1993, frontiers arid border controls in the EL' w:r; removed to create a single European Market.
New regulations were implemented to allow Petroleum products to continue to he transponed across Europe on a duty­derened/duu­ircc basis.
Guarantees or Bonds are frequently required to cover the dut)· deferment.
The effects on the barge market, which underlie;, IPE's Gas Oil Contract, were both profound and continuing.
Border controls were replaced with a new system of documentary conçois whereby a form (The Accompanying Admimsü­ative Document ­ "A \0':- or ILS equivalent) travels with the barge between Terminals and a copy is then stamped b > Customs at the Receiving Terminal and returned to the Loadme Terminal. 1.3
The novelo, of the system coupled with a senes of missive claims arising out of a major Customs fraud m 1991 (before the 1993 changes), led to the oil majors (and hence everyone else) imposing a requirement that buyers should secure payment of Excise Duty by the issue of a standby letter of credit or bank guarantee for an amount sufficient to cover both the Excise Duty and any penalties due.
The cost of meeting tins requirement, which amounted to up to 40üv > oi the value of a cargo, had a severely detrimental effect on the physical market, and the participation of smaller players does not appear to have every fully recovered from it. 1.5
Flagship Agencies Ltd is an introducing, agenc in the London insurance market.
The principal, Chad Sarll, has very considerable experience to. this field and in 1993/4 approached ΓΡΕ to establish whether there WÜ a potential role for the insurance market in the provision of an insurance­backed Bond to secure the payment or Excise Duty/ related penalties.
In the intervening period, and with the encouragement of both IPE and LCH. Mr Sarll has further developed the concept to the extent that he has:
(c) obtained the. support of Oil Majors and sigmiicam Oil Traders for the concept; and id) investigated operational issues with Dutch tanking installations and others.
Following two seminars attended by key participants io Holland it appears clear that the provision or'a cost effective insurance­backed Bond is viable but dependent upon a satisfactory monitoring system for barge movements between participating Terminals.
The Underwriter's Role
LCH and TPE are considering the introduction of contract rules requiring the provision of security for payment of Excise Duty, whetiier by an approved Bond (such as the Flagship Rond) or alternative security.
Those 1PE Members and customers who wish to utilise the Flagship Bond will provide details to the Underwriter.
The Underwriter will hold an initial and confidential credit assessment meeting with each market participant to establish a working credit relationship and to determine the number of Bonds to be made available and outstanding at any time during the. year.
Flagship will be notified of the limits.
There would be credit reviews between Underwriter and Client ar regular intervals to ensure close understanding by the Underwriter of the Client's business and intentions.
These reviews would be che opportunity for both sides to ensure mac appropriate credit limits were available and that reporting and compliance perfonmmce standards were being met.
Flagship non­speciñe historical data would be made available to Underwriters for this purpose.
Responsibility for maintaining and updating the Flagship Bond Credit rating of each Client \v:ll rest with the Underwriter at all tiir.es. based upon information provided by Flagship and by the Client Since the underlying relationship is one of Insurer and Insured, the interests of all parties are best served if the principle of 'Utmost Good Faith" is observed, and good information flov.· should help to keep premiums low.
The Monitoring System
For the purpose of this description, the Trading company that freights the product v\ili be called the "Trader".
All companies in the "Chain" between the Trader and the Loading Installation will be protected b\ the Bond issued in connection with die individual loading, provided that each such company has notified Flagship of its inclusion tu the Cham.
Unce initial Credit assessment has been agreed with the Underwriter, Lhe Trader will be given a simple (E­Mail) Bond requisition forni together with an identification number.
The Trader will contact Flagship by E­Mail for a Bond reference number, giving the Customs clearance details diat v. ill be required by the Loading Terminal for Issuance of die AAD.
