but would naturally be collateralized. Without a monitoring system the leading Bank's guarantee is fully exposed to loss.
Conclusions
The "Rotterdam" market and Tank. Terminal companies appear tv) fa\ our a single monitoring system which Flagship and the IPE/LCH are capable of supplying.
A cost effective bond requires an effective monitoring system.
It is unlikely that a duplicate system would be supported by market participants, thereby giving Flagship the pre­eminent position.
The Customs authorities in Holland are fully supportive of this swem and will assist with Customs Verification when requested by Flagship.
The Ministry of Finance in the Plague has offered connections tor Flagship with other minisuies in Germany and Belgium.
The Flagship Bond effectively provides a "level playing field" for traders, and can be expected thereby to increase participation by smaller participants.
The Traders can buy and sell across borders more easily once they can guarantee liabilities.
Flagship provides a "walk tall" factor for ihese compames.
Once the Flagship system becomes operational the larger oil companies will buy Flagship Bonds when trading to comply with this single system of security.
Flagship has been invited to advise and assist the Freight Forward Market in Europe­There is a vety large scope for expansion for Flagship in Europe and beyond and it is hoped to complete the first stage of the development (oil market) su that broader markets can be developed using the same system.
Bonds for Industrial Alcohol are already being issued, monitored by the tank Terminals until Flagship is in place.
The Dutch Government adopted main of the Flagship principles in structuring this legislation which provides a strong indication of Flagship's future role in European Trade.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the International Road Transport Union.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Is SAFETIR applicable to the Community or Common Transit System (CT)?
Objectives of SAFETIR
SAFETIR is the control system on the use of TIR Carnets developed by the IRU as requested by the UN WP.30 based on the UN/ECE Recommendation of 20.10.1995.
The objective of SAFETIR is to render TIR Carnet issue safer by strengthening the control system and rapidly eliminating defraudeis.
This objective will be reached, once the system is fully operational. by enabling the party issuing TIR Carnets to only issue further Carnets on the following two conditions:
• The Customs Authority confirms the discharge of used TIR Carnets, if possible on a daily basis, using a parallel network of information interchange.
• Transpon operators return previously used TIR Carnets duly discharged by Customs Authorities as attested by relevant Customs stamps.
SAFETIR enables the guarantor (issuing TIR Carnets) to obtain without delay from Customs Authorities all relevant information concerning the discharge of a TIR transit operation.
A comparison of this information with the stamps affixed on the TIR Carnet proves to the guarantor beyond any doubt that Customs Authorities have effectively discharged a transit operation.
This information is transmitted through a computer network linking all points in Europe.
SAFETIR benefits Customs Authorities by assuring them that only transport operators (or forwarders) having correctly completed a transit operanon are entitled to use such transit documents.
SAFETIR also enables Customs to rapidly identify defrauderà and those using stolen or counterfeit documents.
This simple system, with an extremely low investment cost to Customs Administrations, is based on the following elements:
Politically A willingness on the part of Customs Authorities to transmit, on a daily basis, confirmation of the discharge of TIR transit operations for all operations; the quality of such information should be 99% accurate.
Technically A package of software or interfaces, a computer network» national computer networks and a discharge message providing 7 elements.
Development Whether one favours an amendment of the CT or the alternative suggested below - albeit less ambitious than the current project for a global computerisation of the CT - once experience has made it possible to roaster these procedures, the concept might be extended to: 1 ) the taking in charge of operations (departure, exit or entry), 2) other transit elements (goods, value, HS code, guarantee amount involved, etc.), 3) any other element requested by Member States.
Decisions to be taken
Applying SAFETIR to the CT does not require any new politic*! decision; indeed, the principles already adopted (UN/ECE Recommendation of 20.10.95 and UN/ECE Resolution no.
49 WP.30/RJ64) in particular by the 15 + 6 could well apply lo the CT by analogy.
Main advantage The concept does not require a prior harmonisation of all software currently used by Contracting Party States to the CT (21 countries at present).
The concept mastered by the IRU does not require any major political decision.
It is inexpensive, transparent, and can be audited by the competent bodies of CCC, of the Commission in Brussels, or any other body commissioned by the competent authorities.
Alternative solution for the CT As requested by some transport operators or forwarders, an amendment of the regulations of the Community Transit System (an.
5) which bans the use of TIR Carnets for strictly intraCommunity goods transport (contrary to the "Carnet de passage" and contrary to Common Transit), might be envisaged.
This exclusion (dating back to 1969 or 1970) is no longer justified, since both Systems have their own advantages which are up to the user to appraise.
If such an alternative were adopted, forwarders themselves could become TIR Carnet holders, while remaining subject to a strict monitoring of their use.
1) Customs Authorities commit themselves to providing data on a daily basis to the national central collection point for the computer network.
(Existing national computer networks might be used rationally to this effect).
2) The Τ document is printed in an unforgeable way (ditto TIR Carnet).
3) An additional copy of the T5 is handed to the transport operator (since this document duly stamped has to be compared with discharge confirmation).
4) All Tl documents have a single identification.
5) Identification numbers, once printed, are only attributed to companies affiliated with the computer network, and can be identified by a code (principal debtor).
These companies might then issue such documents to transport operators under certain conditions.
EUROPEAN
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Commission of the European Union.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
SYNOPSIS FOR EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT TEMPORARY COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE TRANSIT SYSTEM
BACKGROUND:
The current Community/Common Transit system is manually based, using paper copies of the Single Administrative Document (SAD) to support the movement of consignments through the European Union and EFTA countries.
Security for the duties and taxes which are suspended during the transit movement is afforded by the provision of financial guarantees.
The provisions of the Convention on Common Transit were extended to Poland, Hungary, Czech Republic and Slovakia (the V­4 countries) with effect from 1 July 1996.
Economic operators are required to submit transit declarations to the customs administration at the Office of Departure.
After performing any necessary control, the customs administration retains Copy 1 of the SAD and returns Copies 4 & 5 to the operator for onward travel with the goods, The operator presents the goods and SAD documents to the relevant customs Office of Destination. After exercising any required control, the customs administration retains Copy 4 of the SAD and returns Copy 5 to the Office of Departure where the document is compared with the original Copy 1. If all controls have been satisfactorily completed the transit movement is written offend the guarantee is discharged. 1.3
The current system has proved to be time­consuming, resource intensive and open to fraudulent manipulation.
The Transit Computerisation Project aims to introduce a new modern way for management of the transit system.
Therefore, the Joint EC/EFTA Committee on Common Transit confirmed their commitment to develop a New Computerised Transit .
PARTICIPATING COUNTRIES
There are currently 21 countries actively participating in the development of the New Computerised Transit System.
These comprise the 15 EU Member States plus Norway, Switzerland, Poland, Hungary, Czech Republic and Slovakia.
In addition, Iceland, Liechtenstein (represented by Switzerland) and San Marino wilt be involved later.
It is likely that the NCTS will be extended to other Eastern European countries as they accede to the Convention on Common Transit,
MANAGEMENT STRUCTURE
The project is owned by the Joint EC/EFTA Committee on Common Transit which has appointed a Steering Committee at Deputies level to control the project at the strategic level; set business objectives, priorities and milestones; and approve terms of reference and executive documents.
At the working level, participating countries send IT and procedural representatives to the Transit Computerisation Working Party (TCWP).
The project is managed by the Central Project Team who also co­ordinate the national project teams.
SYSTEM OUTLINE
The New Computerised Transit System comprises three separate areas - the Common Domain, the National Domain and the External Domain.
The Commission Project Team is responsible for the Common Domain, system specification and coordination and arranging for the provision of electronic interfaces for connection to national transit systems and permit the interchange of data between administrations.
It will also develop the Standard Transit Application software to be used in the National Domain by those countries who do not want to, or cannot, build their own transit application.
The electronic gateways will be developed and provided by the CCN/CSI (Common Communications Network/Common Systems Interface) Project being progressed by DGXXÍ in the Commission to provide a generic network for the Customs & Indirect Taxation sector. 4.2
The participating administrations are responsible for the development of the national transit systems in the National Domain, together with the interlaces to economic operators (trade users) in the External Domain.
Administrations will capture data relating to each transit movement from the economic operator (either manually or electronically) at the customs Office of Departure.
Electronic checks will be made to verify the validity of guarantees presented as security for each transit movement.
The administration will then transmit an electronic message to the customs Office of Destination containing data relating to the specific goods being moved and allocating an estimated journey time.
All this will be done in real time and before the goods have left the Office of Departure.
This electronic message will be used by customs staff at the destination office to assess the relevant risk and allocate resources for control purposes prior to the arrival of the goods.
Control results will be returned electronically by the customs destination office to enable the transit movement to be discharged at the Office of Departure and guarantee controls to be completed more quickly.
Automated enquiry procedures will be initiated on expiration of the allocated journey time in the event of non arrival of control results.
Each national administration will maintain databases for movement data, guarantees issued, list of approved customs offices, authorised business partners etc.
This new system will, therefore, provide additional improved facilities.
Data will be transmitted directly between administrations, replacing the current paper documents carried by the transit operator and providing advance notice of the movement.
The Customs authorities will have less administrative work, whilst obtaining greater control over the transit procedure.
The economic operators will gain from the provision of greater security in the system, increased speed of transit movements, harmonisation of work practices, more rapid clearance and quicker discharge of guarantees, tracking facilities and the ability to check the authenticity of authorised consignees etc.
TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE
The architecture will be required to support a distributed system linking disparate national systems.
Each of these autonomous national systems will inter­act with each other using a common communications infrastructure.
The details of the technical architecture are still being discussed and have yet to be agreed or approved by the participating countries.
However, the system will be based as far as possible on existing national infrastructures, with specific national transit applications being developed for each administration.
Standard interfaces will be provided between the Standard Transit Application and Nationally Developed Transit Application modules and other customs systems.
These interfaces will make use of international communications standards and promote the harmonisation of transit systems amongst the participating countries.
The intention is avoid technical disruption to national systems as far as possible while at the same time ensuring that some 3,000 customs offices throughout Europe are computerised.
Wherever practical the proposed architecture will use commercially available software solutions rather than the development of bespoke software.
FINANCIAL REQUIREMENTS
The expenditure of Community funds seems limited to central costs (e.g. network, gateways, and common specifications) but it is possible that national cost­benefit analyses will give an indication of areas in which Community funds might usefully be used to support national activities of common interest.
One area that has already been identified has been the central development of a standard application.
Currently, the funding for central development has been provided mainly from the IDA (Interchange of Data between Administrations) Programme.
The financing of this programme (line B5­7210) was greatly reduced during the course of the 1996 budgetary procedure.
Of the 50 MECU asked for by the Commission, only 30 MECU has been allocated of which 7.5 MECU has been reserved by the Parliament.
Pg: P.4/4 the supporting national infrastructure.
Many of the countries involved have complex . budgetary and financial machinery.
In some, administrations future expenditure has yet to be planned and bids have to be made several years in advance of system development and implementation.
This presents problems for implementation of the operational system within the 1998 timetable.
Financial provision for development of national transit systems will be made against demands for the funding of other national systems.
It is essential, therefore, that administrations are committed to the Transit Computerisation Project and allocate a high priority to providing sufficient finance.
This would accord with the Internal Market Council resolution adopted at its meeting on 23 November 1995 urging the Commission and Member States to give priority to the provision of the necessary financial and human resources in order to have an operational computerised transit system in 19*98.
It is envisaged that participating administrations from the EFTA and V­4 countries will make a contribution to central costs in addition to the funding of their national transit systems and internal infrastructure.
These costs will include development of standard software, telecommunications layers and any necessary hardware required for gateway installations.
The V­4 countries should be encouraged to seek financial assistance from the EC PHARE Programme.
IMPLEMENTATION TIMETABLE
The Transit Computerisation Project is being progressed in a number of phases.
Phase 0 (Feasibility Study) was completed in November 1994.
The current Phase 1 (Development of System Specifications) was authorised by the Joint EC/EFTA Committee in December 1994 and is due to be completed within the next few months.
The time schedule for the future phases of the project is shown in the following paragraphs.
Phase 2 (Construction and Pilot Implementation) is planned to commence in 1997 and be completed in 1998.
Consideration is being given to requesting the Steering Committee to authorise commencement of Phase 2 to run in parallel with Phase 1 to minimise project delay.
During the Construction and Pilot Implementation phase the organisation, software, hardware, and communications required to support a pilot transit phase will be constructed, tested and integrated.
The New Computerised Transit System will be implemented and operated in a limited number of pilot sites in parallel with the existing paper­based system, for a period of 4 to 6 months.
Phase 3 (Implementation and Extension) will commence in 1998 and continue into 1999.
During this phase the pilot network infrastructure will be expanded to the operational network infrastructure.
The transition to and the running­in of the operational service in the pilot countries including the extension of the service to all national Transit Offices will be performed.
In addition, the operational service will be extended via national pilots to the other countries and the required legislative, organisational, and procedural framework will be implemented.
Phase 4 (Operation and Maintenance) will be effected from 1999 onwards and consist of the on­going activities required for the operational service.
The responsibilities for operational activities will require definition and approval by the participants.
Service level agreements will be developed to ensure that the Commission, national administrations and economic operators are both aware οζ and fully accept their respèuive responsibilities.
I. FREEDOM AND CONTROL
This desire to pursue two opposing priorities simultaneously tended, in the initial years of operation of the Single Market, to favour trade freedom and expeditiousness at the expense of control operations. The priority was (and still is) to emphasize the beneficial effect on economic growth and European integration of the elimination of controls.
Advantage is taken of the transit system which is now being extensively used as a means of avoiding customs checks.
AN EXAMPLE OF FRAUD: TOBACCO SMUGGLING
Background
The entry into force of the Maastricht Treaty, which Spain was authorized to ratify by means of Organic Law 10/1992 of 28 December 1992, brought about the suspension of customs duties, quantitative restrictions on the import and export of goods and all other measures of equivalent effect through the enactment of the Royal Decree of 29 December 1992, when customs duties on trade with the European Community were abolished in preparation for the fact that Spain would become a full member of that Community with effect from 1 January 1993.
In the light of the abovementioned regulations and the Community rules governing the free movement of goods within the customs territory of the Community, the belief began to spread within certain sectors of the population that, with effect from the date on which Spain had become a full member of the European Community, tobacco imports had been liberalized and tobacco was no longer to be regarded as an item to which the government's sales monopoly applied.
Such beliefs arose to a considerable extent as a result of the readiness with which certain interested parties began to supply such information to the media which, in certain cases, even covered their front pages with large headlines announcing that tobacco smuggling had been decriminalized and that anyone could now import the substance.
In turn, once such information began to spread, the lawyers responsible for defending those accused of tobacco-smuggling offences began to quote the Maastricht Treaty to the appropriate legal bodies and stressed the fact that the Treaty constituted directly applicable law which overrode internal law.
On these grounds the practice developed of requesting acquittals in presumed cases of tobacco smuggling which had not yet been settled.
However, there began to be greater cause for concern when this line of defence started to be accepted by certain public prosecutors who, even though they are a minority group, withdrew the accusations which they had previously made since they also thought that, in effect, all activities involving the unauthorized import of tobacco into Spain had been decriminalized following the entry into force of the Maastricht Treaty.
In view of the highly accusatory nature of criminal proceedings in the Spanish legal system, certain judges, on hearing of the withdrawal of the accusation by the prosecutor's department, began to issue acquittals in which it was stated that tobacco had ceased to be a product under government monopoly and that import operations should no longer be punished, even if they were unauthorized.
On the basis of this interpretation of the rules and of the many facilities assisting the import of goods into Spain thanks to the abolition of controls at land borders, there also came about a change in the strategy used by bodies involved in the illegal import of tobacco into Spain.
Thus there were the beginnings of a change to the traditional system of illegal imports by sea, which had used major networks and numerous large vessels and also a set of organizations within Spain which were in turn connected to some other international superstructure.
From 1993 onwards there was a notable decrease in the sea-based activities of such organizations as soon as they realised that the costs and risks involved in transporting tobacco by road were less than those associated with transport by sea.
Once they had realised that it was easier to get through customs and that, by using road transport, they could disguise loads more effectively and avoid checks and inspections, they switched fully to crossing land borders using heavy
Hence the latter became the new system used, in place of the traditional method of smuggling by sea which is more vulnerable to detection.
The most common way in which organizations involved in tobacco smuggling operated was by pretending to use a customs transit operation with a fictitious destination from a Community country to some other third country, normally a North African country, with the goods leaving EC territory through a Spanish customs office such as Algeciras, Málaga or Almería.
In reality, the tobacco did not leave the Community through any of those customs offices but was diverted for consumption somewhere within the European Union.
The ringleaders of smugglers' networks usually establish their operating base in one or other northern European city (often Antwerp, Rotterdam, Le Havre, Dordrecht, etc), in which they acquire tobacco in large amounts, usually consignments present within the European Union's customs territory under suspension-of-duty arrangements after being imported through a free zone, a free port or a duty-free store.
In many cases the goods come from substandard consignments which can usually be purchased at a cut-price rate, either because they are old stock or because they have failed health or quality checks, which means that a significant mark-up can be added to their cost price and that substantial profits can thus be made.
In the abovementioned ports the smugglers succeed in obtaining a T1 customs transit document, which covers the transport of non-Community goods between free zones, free ports or duty-free stores in EU countries.
The document must be accompanied by a TIR carnet in cases where the final destination of a consignment is a non-Community country.
The most significant features of the scheme are that the company transporting the goods must present, in the country of origin, the TIR vehicle (which must provide adequate fiscal security) and the TIR carnet. Following any checks which are considered appropriate and having made sure that both documents are duly completed, the customs officer seals the vehicle.
The documents must contain details of the customs offices of entry and exit, the itinerary to be followed, the deadline for the delivery of the goods (normally 8 days), the load list, the consignor, the customs office of destination, the consignee and the transport company. The TIR carnet can only be used for vehicles belonging to the company which holds the carnet and bearing the certificate 'Agrément'.
TIR carnets contain several leaves, each of which has two counterfoils. The first counterfoil is kept at the customs office of departure in the originating country and the second one is kept at the customs office through which the goods leave that country.
When the vehicle arrives at the Spanish customs office of destination, which in turn is the office of departure from the Community territory, a customs officer records the carnet in the appropriate register, checks that the seals have not been forced and may examine the goods.
He then stamps and signs the carnet and immediately returns the second counterfoil direct to the customs office of origin for discharge. It is also at this time that the guarantee which had to be lodged before the start of the transit operation is released.
However, the transit operation then proves to be entirely fictitious since, in the mind of the organization, there was never any intention other than that of diverting the tobacco for consumption within the Community and thus making it
DOC EN\CM\307\307439
possible to evade the import tariff duties which would be charged on a legal import operation, assuming that the required authorization had been obtained.
Amongst the range of irregularities which are usually committed in order to divert tobacco, particular mention should be made of the following: falsification of the customs stamp, both that of the customs office of origin and that of the office through which the goods leave Spain, which is responsible for returning the stamped forms direct to the customs office of origin; this makes it possible for the transit form to be discharged and the corresponding guarantee to be released illegally.
On other occasions the vehicle carrying the goods does indeed arrive at the customs office through which the goods are due to leave the Community, but only after the tobacco has been unloaded by means of some skilful method which allows this to be done without breaking the seals, such as removable doors, false bottoms which allow access to the main receptacle and through the communication vents of which the goods are extracted, etc.
On other occasions, even when the smugglers break the seals and unload the goods, they need only omit to return the arrival counterfoil to the customs office of departure, since the customs services do not always carry out exhaustive checks into discharges in view of the large number of transit operations which take place.
Other irregularities involve entering the names of fictitious companies as consignees on customs documents or using the names of genuine companies without their consent and without their having any commercial relationship with the operation: their name is simply used by the smugglers to obtain apparent legal cover to enable them to achieve their fraudulent aims.
Such operations are financed by individuals who normally have a history of convictions for tobacco smuggling but, to avoid raising suspicion, payments are made through intermediaries who have no criminal record and with whom the smugglers connive.
TIR carnets are also used which are not endorsed with the word TABAC, which is mandatory and should be printed diagonally across the carnet.
The vehicles used for carriage do not always belong to the company which holds the customs document and such vehicles are usually accompanied by others which act as 'pilot ships' and are equipped with mobile telephones or radiotelephony communications equipment for the purpose of providing the driver with information as to whether or not he is under observation or regarding the detours he must make if road checks are being carried out.
On other occasions the goods manifesto is not sealed and yet the TIR carnet counterfoils bear the stamps of the customs officers of origin and departure, which have usually been falsified.
C. The legal situation of the tobacco monopoly in Spain
As previously mentioned the liberalization of the market and the free movement of goods between the EU Member States since 1 January 1993 have been interpreted by some interested parties as meaning that, with effect from that date, any natural or legal person may import tobacco into Spain.
However, the legal situation of the tobacco monopoly pursuant to Law 38/1985 of 22 November 1985 is that imports by wholesalers of tobacco originating, or released for free circulation, in the EC Member States have been liberalized, although the statutory requirements must be fulfilled.
In the case of natural or legal persons who have been duly authorized to make wholesale imports of Community tobacco, the latter may not be regarded as goods to which the state monopoly applies and, therefore, such conduct may not be legally classified as smuggling.
On the other hand, as regards imports from non-Community countries, retail sales and distribution (even involving tobacco from Community countries) and tobacco manufacture in Spain, the State continues to hold a legal monopoly over such activities, by virtue of which such goods are regarded as 'monopolized' and such activity may therefore be deemed criminal behaviour under the current Organic Law on Smuggling when the amount involved equals or exceeds 1 000 000 pesetas.
DOC EN\CM\307\307439
These days in legal circles there is no longer any doubt about the fact that tobacco is still a 'monopoly' product when it does not originate in the European Union or has not been released for free circulation therein, and contraventions of this principle have in recent times been punished accordingly.
Furthermore, to remedy the shortcomings which had become apparent the transit scheme in respect of sensitive products (including, inter alia, tobacco) has been tightened up, in the sense that there is now a ban on the use of an comprehensive guarantee (an earlier scheme which allowed operators to carry out transit operations under a guarantee based solely on the volume of operations in the previous financial year) and a guarantee covering the total amount of the taxes and duties suspended is now required for each operation.
Tobacco transit is also subject to prior notification, a scheme under which the customs office of departure immediately informs the country of destination via the Scent computer system, which enables the country of destination to bring monitoring procedures into operation and to react immediately if the consignment is not discharged in the normal fashion.
The combination of all these factors means that fraud relating to the transit of tobacco and other sensitive products has declined over the past year and there are signs of a return to traditional tobacco smuggling by sea, at much greater risk and cost to the smugglers.
For this reason, claims to the effect that 25% of tobacco sold in Spain is smuggled may be regarded as pure fantasy.
It should be borne in mind that Virginia tobacco is the only kind which is smuggled, for which reason, if dark tobacco and Virginia tobacco each account for 50% of consumption, approximately half of the Virginia tobacco sold would have to have been smuggled for this level of fraud to be achieved.
This would be difficult in the case of such a product which is bought on impulse whenever the existence of a sales outlet coincides with the moment at which the need arises.
Spain is particularly affected by the problem of transit fraud and especially cigarette smuggling, which is estimated to account for 70% of the irregularities detected.
This is particularly worrying on account of the impossibility of taking pre emptive action to counter the 'legal' use of the transit scheme for various purposes, for example importing massive amounts of tobacco from tax-free areas of other Member States into Gibraltar and using Spanish customs offices as points of departure from the Community customs territory (where administrative interference is unlikely for fear of accusations concerning restrictions on the freedom of trade), the outcome being the illegal entry into Spain of vast amounts of tobacco.
This is not strictly an instance of irregularity or illegality in the use of the transit scheme but, rather, an illustration of the damage which can be done if the rules are contravened and if advantage is taken of Spain's geographical situation as an external Community border and of shortcomings in cooperation and in the understanding of the problem by other Member States.
Spain's concern has led the country to adopt, inter alia, the following measures :
Spain was the first EU Member State to request (on 4 April 1995) application of the measures provided for in Regulation (EC) 2454/93 in respect of cigarette consignments: a ban on the use of comprehensive
DOC_EN\CM\307\307439
guarantees and the requirement for an individual guarantee fully covering the duties and other charges payable.
Creation, within the Secretariat-General for Inspection and Investigation of the Department of Customs and Special Taxes, of a unit specializing in investigating and combatting infringements of the transit scheme.
This unit has begun work in the following areas:
Discussions with the National Coin and Stamp Factory concerning the design and production of new stamps which are more difficult to falsify.
Greater security in the use of such stamps through the introduction of strict procedures to restrict access thereto.
Improvements to the prior notification system in respect of transit operations so that the customs authorities of the individual Member States can be alerted rapidly (by fax or electronic mail) regarding transit operations within Spain.
Improved management of the transit scheme within the section of the Department responsible for customs management, so as to standardize criteria and harmonize customs procedures in order to make them more flexible and effective in the detection of irregularities and the statistical monitoring of the system.
Three procedural handbooks relating to transit have recently been drawn up to assist the customs service by rationalizing the internal procedure in an attempt to increase the speed and effectiveness of the response to any irregularities detected.
Discussions have been initiated with the countries on Spain's borders (France and Portugal) with a view to undertaking joint transit monitoring operations.
DOC_EN\CM\307\307439
Any suspected irregularities are notified to the inspection and ancillary services of the customs administration and appropriate preventive action is taken.
The introduction of computerization at national level, which began in 1995, has already enabled false discharges to be detected and made it possible to implement investigation and distraint procedures designed to prevent irregularities.
At a port such as Bilbao or in any busy customs office the detection of irregularities in the case of consignments which are not declared as sensitive will be a more complex matter.
The only alternatives are comprehensive checks (which, as has been pointed out, are in incompatible with the political desire to see the Single Market operating effectively), or investigation and the provision of information, based on the flexibility of all the Community customs offices concerned, which would make it possible to carry out selective checks more intensively.
In this connection it is frequently the case that repeated requests from the Spanish customs administration for information concerning transit are systematically ignored by certain foreign customs offices where the consignments originated.
In view of the fact that the system has not yet been fully computerized, the figures contained in the Statistical Annex are estimates.
Tables 1 and 2 of section A of the Annex are concerned with quantifying the volume of transit operations recorded as having been initiated during the period from 1992 to 1995.
Tables 3, 4 and 5 of the same section refer, for 1995, to the number of operations completed within Spain and also the number of inquiry notices (the TC 20 document which is issued in cases where, once the statutory period has expired, there is no evidence that the transit form has been discharged in the normal fashion) which originated in Spain and received in Spain or were received in Spam fror, other EU countries.
It should be pcinted out that the number of investigations initiated by Spain alone is extrenely similar to the number of inquiries requested by all the other Member States together.
Lastly, section Β of the Annex is concerned with quantifying the level of fraud within the transit scheme, although it should be stressed that the figures quoted are only estimates, given the nature of the activity.
Table 1 shows the fall in estimated fraud in 1995.
This signals a break in the growing trend seen in previous years and is the result of the recent implementation of the measures previously described (prior notification system, refusal to allow a comprehensive guarantee, better legal description of fraudulent operations, etc.
Table 3 expresses fraud and other irregularities as a percentage of the total volume of transit operations recorded.
The last table in this section - Table 4 - illustrates the number of transit operations recorded by the prior notification system between January and June 1996, broken down by category of product and quantifying the irregularities recorded.
The starting point for this section should be the fact that the Community transit scheme was designed for a Community with six Member States, of which three already formed a customs union (Benelux), and covered external trade figures which are negligible compared with those currently generated in a customs union with 15 members and a much larger geographical area.
In other words the original transit scheme has to be applied in very different and much more cimpl-:' cz "cujistances.
Moreover, the original system which operated reasonably well for a few years is no longer the one which is currently applied since certain checks which these days would be extremely useful in preventing fraud and in identifying the country in which the fraud occurs have now been abandoned.
This isa specific reference to the transit advice note, which was a simple document which the transport company had to hand over at the customs office of entry into each country through which the consignment passed.
Moreover, the transit system has been liberalized to such an extent that proper management of the documents which it generates and of the checks to be carried out no longer depends solely on the customs administration but also on satisfactory compliance by economic operators with the obligations by which they are bound.
The manual handling of transit documents between the various customs administrations had led to an untenable situation, which demonstrates one of the main limitations of the system since the available resources are unable to handle efficiently the more than 18 million transit forms and 3 million TIR carnets which are issued each y«;ar in the European Union.
It should be borne in mind that each transit operation requires at least three document dispatches : from the customs office of departure to the central customs office of the individual Member State, from the latter to the central customs office of departure and from the latter to the individual customs office of departure.
In future, action to prevent transit irregularities cannot be based solely on the willingness of the Member States to carry out checks.
The Community must take a firmer line on this matter and, in order to protect its financial interests, it will have to establish the limits and the nature of the checks which the Single Market is capable of accommodating and it will have to insist that the Member States carry them out in a uniform fashion.
If this does not happen, customs control will gradually become standardized throughout Europe at the level of the most flexible countries, which will promote their traditional capacity as focal points for trade with the rest of Europe and the world in general, and this will bring renewed protests from those who are responsible for protecting the EC's financial interests and from domestic companies which will call for Community production to be protected by the customs service as the body responsible for implementing the Union's trade and fiscal policy.
In this context it may be pointed out that improvements should be introduced in the following areas : the conditions for the use of transit guarantees; establishment of a guarantee system for transport companies as well ;
DOC EN\CM\307\307439
In addition to these and other technical and procedural improvements, priority must be given to greater cooperation and mutual assistance amongst the various EU customs administrations, involving joint investigation and preventive operations at a uniform level of intensity throughout the customs territory.
Improvements solely to the schemes within the individual Member States will be pointless if the Community as a whole does not tackle the problem on the basis of common minimum standards and shared objectives.
In pursuit of this form of cooperation, Spain is in contact with the French and Portuguese customs administrations (i.e. those of its neighbours) in a coordination effort which will make it possible to achieve greater effectiveness in the investigation and prevention of fraud based on the illegal use of the customs transit scheme.
In addition, Spain is now equipped with a sophisticated computer system which makes rapid response possible, but it cannot be held responsible for any failure to respond on the part of others.
All 82 of Spain's customs offices are connected to a central information system known as Automated Customs Management.
The infrastructure which has been installed allows for:
(a) real-time connection of all customs offices to a central management system;
(b) the possibility for economic operators of applying for import and export clearance by computer link at any customs office.
Currently, 350 operators make customs declarations on a daily basis throughout Spain by using three interconnected added-value networks and 17 types of message of international standard.
This makes it possible for every stage of the procedure, from the submission of a provisional summary declaration of the arrival of the goods to the granting of clearance by the customs authorities, to be carried out by computer link.
DOC_EN\CM\307\307439
The centralized reception by computer of summary declarations, either from consignees or organizations with which the customs authorities have established agreements, such as state-run ports or airports.
Currently, all summary declarations submitted by consignees at state-run ports are received by customs via the computer network.
This also includes traffic across the Strait of Gibraltar.
The ability for customs offices to send and receive any kind of message to or from any consignee within Spain and of access to international networks including XIES.
To conclude, the Spanish customs information system allows for the immediate establishment of the infrastructure required for the transit system, including the processing of EDIFACT messages in real time.
The Spanish customs service is in a position, if it so wishes, to set up, within a very short period of time, a pilot scheme involving the places of origin and destination which generate the greatest volume of traffic.
It considers that current planning, which should include the need to computerize customs offices, is inadequate since there is insufficient financial commitment and the matter is a technologically complex one.
The uncertainty and the length of the timescale threaten the viability of the project, which is considered to be of the greatest importance.
A successful pilot scheme, even one of limited geographical scope but carried out over a short period of time and with a significant volume of traffic, would undoubtedly increase the level of the participants' commitment, for which reason such a scheme should be introduced without delay.
DOC EN\CM\307\307439
