SWU - Background
Coming from a diverse background in engineering, marketing and publishing, in among others, the oil industry, Joop Noppen designed the original technology for security of mobile objects in 1992.
The main characteristics of this technology are that it uses a combination of GPS, mobile communications, and DOS.
CarAngel was founded.
The technology was first exploited with fleets of trucks in Italy.
Some 500 trucks are still running successfully using the Car Angel system.
VBMO was founded to exploit a completely new concept and technology called EuroSafe 2000 for car safety.
A complete concept for car safety was created in association with insurance companies.
EuroSafe 2000 is now being successfully commercially exploited in the European market.
Safe Wheels Unlimited, was founded in 1996 as a research and development company, based on the experience and know-how developed through the Car Angel and VBMO.enterprises.
Safe ..Wheels Unlimited's first project is.to devise and implement the S.A.F.E. (Secure Accurate Forwarding in Europe) concept.
A concept specifically devised for the security of road, rail and inland waterway transportation throughout Europe.
The S.A.F.E. technology due to its unique combination of proactive security management, wireless digital telecommunications, its large backup battery autonomy, has many other applications.
It can be applied to transportation of oil and gas through pipelines, VIP and capital transportation The S.A.F.E. technology is also attractive to self-supporting installations such as generators, booster stations and other mechanical equipment.
Introduction
Fraud in transit of goods through the European Union
Incidents of fraud in transit have dramatically increased in the last few years.
This has directly impacted on the premiums requested by insurance companies for the transit of goods.
The direct victims of this situation are the honest freight forwarders.
Indirectly, the European tax­payer is paying for the cost of fraudulent activities as a consequence of considerable loss of revenue in taxes collected by customs authorities.
Forwarders are being faced with increasing, and sometimes, commercially unrealistic, if not impossible, demands from insurance companies.
This situation is particularly dramatic for those companies that deal in sensitive goods.
Freight operators are having to look increasingly for alternative means of transport.
In our opinion, it is through the application of dedicated electronic technology that the solution to this problem can be found.
After a period of research we have come to the following conclusions and created the technology needed to implement these solutions.
— modified mobile communication system route intelligence (black box) positioning i^modified mobile alarm system ^'­'^ international monitoring centre
The S.A.F.E. (Secure Accurate Forwarding through Europe) project has been developed to implement just such intelligent,, highly sensitive, permanent, proactive surveillance for the transportation of goods.
The technology behind the S.A.F.E. project has been developed by SWU since 1992.
It is already being successfully commercially exploited for car security systems.
Since 1993, more than 500 trucks have been using an earlier system.
INSTITUTE POR DESIGN AND DEVELOPMENT f OR SAFETY AND PROTECTION OF MOBILE OBJECTS
'Reactive versus proactive'
The application of electronic intelligence and telematica transferred out of the vehicle are 'proactive' in terms of risk management.
A brief description of the technology
An ITCU (Integrated Truck Communication System), is installed in the vehicle.
All the data that is required to check and control the truck; its consignment and its journey are stored in the ITCU.
This information can be automatically accessed and/or 'controlled by the Basé Control Station (BCS) of European-Customs Control Centre (ECCC)at any .time duough a special Secure Circuit.
GSM Network and is called up. by a secret ID code.
Alarms generated by the vehicle'àre transferred via the GSM network and received in the space of a few seconds bv the BCS/ECCC. ^ ^ ^ ^- > At the'same time'thé exact geographical position is automatically located, with an accuracy of a few metres. Date, time, speed direction and cargo -information about the vehicle are transferred to the monitoring centre. Remote control monitoring is available throughout Europe. Fleet operators can, if they wish, have the option of independent control of the logistical management from their own remote terminals.
The BCS/ECCC takes all further initiatives and automatically checks the programmed routes, area, distance, time and speed limitation.
The BCS/ECCC is on permanent stand-by, has a professional crew and authorisation to contact the customs authorities, police and international security organisations.
