COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by EVO, Ondernemersorganisatie voor logistiek en transport, Zoetermeer, The Netherlands.
The EVO represents 36.500 Dutch member companies in industry, trade and agriculture, in the field of logistics and transport.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
To mr. J. Tomlinson Chairman of the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System of the European Parliament RueBel_BAR_iard97­1l3 B­1047 Brussels BELGIUM
30 September 1996
Re.: EVO note on community transit system and fraud
EVO represents the interests of 36.500 Dutch member companies in industry, trade and agriculture, in the field of logistics and transport.
A large number of these companies is in­volved in international business.
EVO is very much interested in the inquiry of your committee with regard to the causes and possible ways to reduce fraud.
We underline that an effective and balanced anti fraud policy is of great importance to industry, trade and agriculture.
EVO however strongly objects to rules and regulations obstructing international trade and considers these measures contra productive.
Against this background a group of experts in customs and international trade, members of EVO, has discussed the problems and developed a view on this subject as laid down in the attached note on community transit and fraud.
A summary of the note is also enclosed.
We herewith trust to contribute to an effective and balanced anti fraud system in which the interest of "bona fide" companies is respected.
Yours faithfully.
End. (s): 2
Summary Community Transit Sytem and Fraud
The European Parliament has set up an Investigation Committee which was asked to inquire into the present functioning of the Community transit system.
This was induced by strong rumours about abuse of the system.
The attached note hopes to make a contribution to this discussion.
In order to formulate the problem in the clearest possible terms, the note starts with a discussion of Fraud as a concept and of the shapes it comes in.
The deliberateness is stressed of the action in order to pay no or less taxes.
Several ways exist to classify fraud: based on the damage it causes, on the types of offenders, and on the costs of investigation.
An overview of the legislative Customs system is given.
Some of the concepts discussed are: 'amount of customs duties', 'liability', 'guarantees' and 'establishment of the amount duty to be paid after the event'.
The European Customs system has served its purposes for a long time, but has not changed enough with the passing of time.
There is hardly any attention for the owner of the goods.
The Customs authorities have little competence for apprehending the real offenders.
The note observes the total lack of harmonisation of civil law, and of criminal law in particular, in the EU.
Since the note describes the system as 'obsolete', additional legal measures and audit measures have to be taken on European level.
The prime goal should be to prevent the offenders from entering into the market.
In order to realise this the offenders have to be treated as the first liable parties in legislative terms.
Further should buyer c.q. sellers be involved in fraude prevention by legislation.
In compensation the note suggests the extension of exoneration.
It should be possible to clear one's good name with more evidence than is currently allowed.
The additional measures should take the interests of bona fide businesses sufficiently into account.
In particular the note warns against the danger of 'overkill'.
The protection of people's privacy and similar general constitutional notions, should not be violated.
Distortion of competition should be guarded against.
Additional measures should be implemented as harmonised as possible in the whole of the EU.
The note suggests the following measures: (i) more physical control at the places were Customs goods are being stored, (ii) more precise tracking of vehicles and of Customs goods during transportation, (iii) the speeding up of the execution of Customs procedures, and (iv) the introduction of trial procedures, which could be useful in striking the right balance between the running down of fraud and the damage that may be caused by it.
For the longer term an effective combat against fraud requires more than this.
The note suggests (i) an intensification of the use of automation in the communication between Customs authorities and the communication between Customs and businesses, (ii) the systematic use of risk analysis and administrative control, (iii) the easing of the stipulations with regard to additional proof and exoneration, (iv) a closer cooperation between the various European authorities and (v) lastly, the reduction of rates, the rigorous harmonisation of civil and penal law the EU and the simplification of the Customs rules.
HAV/AYB 96.60e 30 september 1996
Community Transit System and Fraud
On January 16, 1996, the European Parliament set up an Investigation Committee which was asked to inquire into the functioning of the Community Transit System.
In a meeting of May 23,1996, the EVO Advisory Group customs and International Trade decided to establish an ad hoc group to explore this problem and to make proposals for change.
This decision was based on the importance of the topic for the members of the EVO and was also induced by the precedent effect which the EP has created by using its inquiry right for the first time.
These remarks are its result.
The Inquiry Committee uses the general term 'fraud' in its brief.
We believe that this is of vital importance, because the functioning of the Community Transit System can only be improved if the problem is stated in the broadest possible terms.
In general terms fraud may be characterised as: "deliberately and purposely providing wrong or incomplete information or not providing information to Taxation Authorities, in order to pay no taxes or less taxes than would have to be paid if the relevant regulations were obeyed."
When discussing the Community Transit System another dimension is added because of great importance of the physical aspect of the goods.
The general characterisation may be extended by adding that fraud is also understood to include: "the deliberately and purposely withdrawing goods from the supervision of Customs Authorities, where this is lawfully required, in order to pay no or less taxes."
In an explanatory note it may be said that an essential element of fraud is the deliberate, purposive and premeditated distortion of facts.
So, for instance theft is fraud.
Mistakes or differences of opinions are not fraud.
Neither are the loss or disappearance of goods - be it by way of loss during transport, or evaporation of liquids, or resulting from sampling, or any other form.
What are known as tax-saving constructions may result in practically the same outcome, i.e. the loss of tax revenue, which causes higher rates for other taxpayers, without being offset by productive investments.
However, these cannot be categorised as fraud.
In the first place, fraud is breaking of the law.
Several ways exist to classify fraud.
In general it can be stated that the more parties that suffer damage, the bigger the fraud is.
3.2 offender
Community Transit System and Fraud
Levying taxes is only possible within a legislative framework which determines the responsibilities and competencies of the parties involved.
For European Customs law this is to be found in the Community Customs Code (CCC).
The determination of the amount of Customs duties to be paid is governed by Articles 201 - 216 CCC.
The main rule is that the size of the amount to be paid and the time of payment are dependent on the goods being introduced into free circulation. The debtor is the person who introduces the goods into free circulation.
4.2 liability
The person who has ended the Customs supervision of the goods is the debtor.
If more debtors thai one are held responsible for the amount of Customs duties to be paid - which is the normal case -each of them is severally liable to pay, in accordance with Article 213 CCC.
In this way an additional safeguard is established for the amount of Customs duties to be paid, because it creates the possibility for the Customs authorities to hold somebody else liable, for example when a debtor is untraceable or has gone bankrupt.
In the majority of cases the person to end the Customs supervision of the goods is not the owner of the goods.
The owner will normally leave the required formalities to an agent, a Customs agent or a haulier.
The second way in which an additional safeguard is established for the amount of Customs duties to be paid is the security or guarantee.
Article 189 CCC obliges the debtor to provide security.
Further, Article 78 CCC is important in that it allows the Customs authorities to conduct investigation after the event.
In addition to this, Article 221 CCC gives the possibility of establishing the amount oi Customs duty to be paid after the event.
Article 91 CCC contains rules on the external Customs transport; Article 163 CCC on the internal Customs transport.
These rules open the possibility to send goods within the EU from one destination to another, without them having to be cleared through Customs.
5 Deficiencies in the legal system
Community Transit System and Fraud
In the above list of the relevant stipulations the owner of the goods is missing.
The Customs system is totally focused on the goods and the physical movements of goods.
Indirectly, however, the owner is present when he can be grouped under the heading of "persons who provide the necessary information for the completion of the declaration" (Article 201, section 3, CCC).
It has to be concluded that this system prevents Customs from seeking close cooperation with the person who has prime responsibility for the -,oods and who should act accordingly.
The system allows the Customs too little scope to apprehend fraud offenders and law breakers.
As a result, non-offending parties or even innocent bystanders can and will be held liable for other people's misdeeds.
This appears to be a comfortable position to be in, both for the offenders and the taxation authorities; on the other hand it is undeniably becoming less and less comfortable.
This results from a continually increasing social and public pressure for a fairer distribution of the burden between all market parties.
Equally, the legislative system does not sufficiently differentiate between types of offenders.
While the customs law and its implementation are harmonised and while the cooperation between Customs authorities has started, not even an initial attempt at harmonisation has been made for civil law, and criminal law in particular.
This seems to be, in our view, a precondition for progress in this area.
In our view the need for action is evident.
We agree with the Investigation Committee's terminology where it uses the phrase "the crisis in Customs transit transport."
Some facts to illustrate this are:
The scope of certain types of modern fraud forces us to consider additional measures.
This has to d( with the highness of the rates for certain sensitive goods.
Next to this, the political map of Europe ha changed drastically during the last few years and this has resulted in enormous East-West and West-East movements of goods.
7 Required Measures
If the need for action is clear, the goal and types of the measures required need to be a-reed upon.
Additional measures can be divided into legal measures and audit measures (implementation). We think that both aspects have to be realised together and simultaneously.
The fact that we have described the system as 'obsolete' indicates that the system cannot be saved merely by some adaptations but that a general overhaul is required.
Only with the help of legal measures may the system begin to function in an optimal way again.
In addition to legislative measures, implementation measures are required.
Below, in paragraphs 9 and 10 we will see that both types of measures may also be divided in another way: in short term and long term measures.
The prime goal of the additional legal measures should be to prevent the offenders from entering into international trade.
This makes extra supervision unavoidable.
Fraud and the chance of getting caught happen to be linked together like communicating vessels.
7.3 buyer / seller
This implies involvement of buyers and sellers in the prevention of fraud by defining their legal positions.
This is in the first place a matter of redefining the liability of the owner of goods which are in Customs transit.
This implies modification of the CCC rules on liability as meant in paragraph 4.1.
7.4 offender
The authorities' grip on the offender should be strengthened.
For people like that a separate -exceptional - legal approach should be created.
Here it is also a matter of redefining the amount to be paid on the basis of a person's responsibility for his own actions.
In particular the main concern should be about the heavier offenders.
A legal framework will have to be created in which a ranking of liabilities is established, based on types of offenders.
7.6 exoneration
For reasons of principle the possibility of exoneration should be created or extended.
The taxpayer would thus be given the opportunity to clear his good name on the basis of papers provided by himself, even if this meant that the tax liability was not paid.
This goes beyond 'additional proof.'
Community Transit System and Fraud
As the primacy rests with the legal programme, separate criteria need to be agreed upon, so the additional measures can be tested.
In paragraph 7 above we discussed the increase of the competence of the authorities. The avoidance of 'overkill' in this respect is an important second criterion.
The first consideration should be proper and separate briefs for both fiscal authorities and penal authorities.
The concept that one cannot be regarded as an offender after this is established by an appropriate procedure, should be upheld.
Finally, the additional measures should be implemented in the most harmonised form.
Especially in this area of Customs transport the business community is interested in thorough and complete harmonisation, if not unification, of the legal framework.
Additional legal and implementation measures may be divided into (i) those which relate to loading and discharging places, warehouses, free zones and other locations where bonded goods are stored on the one hand, and (ii) those which relate to the transport of bonded goods.
9.1 physical control
For goods stored at loading or discharging places and other locations where bonded goods are to be found, the extent of the effective physical control Is decisive on whether there is much or little risk of fraud.
The increase of physical control may be the price that has to be paid.
If the legitimate rights of the bona fide businesses are sufficiently taken into account, it is undoubtedly possible to steer an even course.
In particular the Customs authorities might develop an accommodating approach by introducing trial measures.
In this way certain facilities are given to selected groups of taxpayers, subject to periodical evaluation.
With regard to the transport of bonded goods the speed (or rather the slowness) of the execution of Customs procedures is decisive.
We think that possible ways of improving this are:
Some experiments are already under way with systems where notifications are used to track lorry movements.
An example can be found in the port of Rotterdam.
The immediate reporting of the arrival or non-arrival of goods using electronic means.
Wherever possible, existing systems should be used.
Especially we think that the option of passing on transpor: data via Internet - in a confidential way - should be explored.
9.2.3 passage of transit goods
Also in this area of transport of Customs goods room should be created for experiments and trial procedures.
The greater involvement of the owners of goods should be offset by the granting of concessions by the authorities to alleviate the administrative burden on a intiividual hasis.
10 Long Term Measures
In the longer term - which in fact starts today - several possible improvements are available.
10.1 automation
The increased use of automation in the Customs area is a matter which in the first instance concerns only the authorities, but business is influenced by it, too. A major concern in implementing automate·: systems is the administrative burden it places on the businesses.
In principle, however, it may be assumed that the burden on businesses can be reduced.
In the first place it must be possible to introduce a Community-wide warning system (or more care fully phrased: a monitoring system), which checks which goods have departed from where, with which destination and at what time they are expected to arrive.
Further the communication between Customs and businesses may be improved by electronic systems.
In many publications - for instance in the Commission's 'Customs 2000 Action Programme' - it is stressed that the Customs regulations need to play a proactive role in the development and promotion of foreign trade.
Naturally it is not useful or desirable for Customs to have unlimited access to all commercial data, but links with existing systems should be developed.
10.2 risk analysis
Increasing the use of risk analysis is the next area that requires attention. The systematic charting of declaration agents, licensees, importers, exporters, etc. is very likely to be an effective means of controlling the parties in international trade.
Examples of the measures we have in mind are: the opening of files on the goods they are trading in, both in physical and financial terms, on their fiscal record, on the usage they make of and their wishes for Customs regulations.
10.3 administrative control
Administrative control seems to be one of the more effective ways in which the Customs authorities can supervise the parties in international trade.
Audit based customs control ABCC in World Trade Organisation terminology ­ can provide an in­depth and comprehensive overview of the patterns of the movements of goods.
Also, it may have a cost­reducing effect. The former results from the requirement that medium­sized and big companies should at all times have detailed and up­to­date knowledge available on products, suppliers and customers.
The latter results from the replacements time consuming and expensive (physical) checks prior to and during the transport of bonded goods.
Currently the use of additional proof is severely curtailed.
The demonstration of the absence of irregularities totally depends on the required Customs documents being completed and processed correctly.
However, we think that the monitoring of goods and destinations can be done in several ways and through various channels.
It is our opinion that in this modem age it is no longer acceptable to consider the required documents, to the exclusion of all other evidence.
This type of formalism belongs in an age in which few people knew how to read and write.
For the meaning of 'additionai proof please refer to paragraph 7.6 on exoneration, where we give a broader than usual definition of this term,
10.5 penal law
Together with the need to harmonise penal law in the EU. we may group together the following things that need to be done urgently but on the longer term: χ closer cooperation between police and Customs, χ a less rigid demarcation of territorial competencies, and * a systematic implementation of experimental procedures, thus creating the scope for the introduction of made­to­measure regulations on the basis of trial and error processes.
All these items are so closely linked that they have to be addressed collectively.
Community Transit System and Fraud
Of course the best results can be expected if these measures are combined.
It is hardly possible to consider ABCC of businesses, if they have not been charted with risk analysis.
And risk analysis is hardly imaginable without faster and closer cooperation between Customs authorities. Etcetera.
