DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
TRADE TRANSIT INFORMA TION CONTROL SYSTEM
Discussion Document
Introduction.
In seeking a solution to the current prob fernsfacing the Community have look to their own resources in the first instance.
The varying levels of competence and commitment with regard to the control of the transit systems by Customs Administrations in the Member States, EFTA and the Visegrad countries, means that trade should not look to this source to provide a solution.
The European Commission's "New Computerised Transit System" (NCTS) will help, but full implementation of the system is not due before 2003 and that does not allow for any slippage which can be anticipated bearing in mind the varying technical abilities of the Customs Administrations involved.
In addressing the transit probL·ms, trade have basically two options:- 1) do nothing or 2) develop their own solution.
The first option will result in a continuing decline of the CT system and possibly to its withdrawal, no doubt to be replaced by a system based on a "cash in advance" which would only be repayable on production of proof of satisfactory termination of the transit movement
The second option therefore seems to offer the only way forward, but how to proceed ?
There may be a number of potential solutions, but the following, which has been based on discussions with trade, DG XXI (NCTS) and SGS's own experience in the field, is put forward as a discussion document and as a framework for arriving at a definitive solution.
The problems facing trade are well knoivn and can best be summarised asfollows:-
* missing copy 5's of the SAD, result in excessive claims from
* around 90% of claims are resolved satisfactorily and are largely raised as a result of the inefficient management and operation of the system by Customs
* although documents are invariably lost or missing within or between Customs Administrations, responsibility of accounting for proof of correct discharge of a transit is passed to trade
* the cost to trade of this "paper chase" in both time and resources, is becoming commercially unacceptable
* additional costs are incurred by trade in respect of associated guarantees and securities having to be kept open in the accounts for far longer periods than are actually necessary.
Trade look to Customs to control and administer CT effectively and efficiently, but it takes only one Customs authority to fail in this to undermine the integrity of the system.
Unfortunately, and for a number of reasons, there arc more than one Customs Administration failing to fulfil its responsibilities in respect of transit movements and this has contributed significantly to many of the current problems.
If trade cannot expect help from Customs before 2003 and a change in Customs attitude towards CT is unlikely to take place in the interim, then trade must look for a solution which solves the problems referred to above. copyright sas .V.l.. 1996
Potential Solution
Trade need a fast and reliable advice to confirm that a transit has been correctly terminated and that the appropriate guarantee/security can be released, or is not at risk anymore.
Only Customs have the authority to give thL· release, but in the absence of their capability to meet the fast and reliable criteria, trade need to provide themselves with a facility which will reduce its risk exposure.
Taking into account the levels of fraud, irregularities, inefficiencies, inexperience etc, endemic in the present system, the first stage of the proposed solution does not start by addressing the trading community as a whole.
The first step is therefore for selected traders to create a group (aho referred to as the "Club" in thL·paper) which can set terms and conditions of membership and apply higher standards of control and procedures to their CT transactions and in so doing, reverse the trend of declining standards.
The need to have rapid and reliable confirmation that a CT transaction has been successfully completed L· paramount and an automated control and reporting system based on a secure and reliable Wide Area Network (WAN), supported by a control and auditfunction, L· required.
As a group, the facility to reduce guarantee/security and insurance costs exists, through the securing of a "Club" comprehensive insurance and which is not restricted in respect of the nature of goods transported.
The "Club " would monitor the performance and claims record of members through the computerised system and the control & audit f unction, and would have the power to withdraw "Club" membership f rom any member not meeting the performance standards as laid down by the "Club". Additionally, the "Club" would provide the Customs Authorities with the guarantee of settlement of claims, thus avoiding the current problems faced by Customs in respect of unsettled claims and outstanding revenues.
At present there L· no Unique Consignment Reference Number (UCR), and group members wishing to transact any transit movement under the "Club 's" insurance policy, would be required to apply for a UCR.
The structure of the UCR not only uniquely identifies the group member, but also which office in which country is using the number.
In addition, the ÜGR can uniquely identify consolidation and consignment splits and instances where more than one vehicle or trailer L· used to carry the consignment
Em p has L· L· placed on system security which applies at various stages f rom application for "Club" membership, through UCR request/ allocation and user security and status vetting, to time monitoring and reporting of UCR activity through to termination of the transit transaction.
Summary of "Club", Security, Control & Audit Elements of the System. * "Approved" Club Members allocated a unique PIN Reference prefixed by country code and urique code identifier
* Security file holds unique PIN plus user's own specified identifiers
* Club Members must nominate their "approved" agent/s (e.g. can be their own subsidiaries, appointed agent etc.) for each country they trade with.
