* Following receipt of the TCM, the system will generate a message to the originator of the DA T, providing all data necessary to respond to any claims by Customs concerning that particular transit transaction (thL· message can be made available to Customs and has formed part of the discussions with DG XXI)
* Where the check is not satisfactory, the originator of the DA Τ message will be advised and a marker will be set in the system preventing any further access to the "approved agent" until such time as the matter has been satisfactorily resolved (this information can be made available to Customs - see section 6).
The system will keep a historic record of transactions and also detaiL· of user performance
To date, SGS has met with one or two companies who have introduced their own internal system of transit completion messages and although there may be a delay before Customs officially confirm the satisfactory completion of the transit, nevertheless, these companies remove the guarantee or security from the provision in their accounts.
It L· interesting to note that these companies have had to pay few, if any, claims to Customs since the introduction of their control system, but nevertheless are interested in the wider scope of secure business that the "Club" would provide them with.
They are ato interested in the potential savings on insurance and guarantee cosL· that a larger group such as the "Club" can achieve
Interfacing With Customs.
The trade system L· designed primarily to provide "Club" members with a secure trading environment audited and controlled by an independent third party and in which fast and reliable information on the sat^factory completion of a transit transaction L· available to them.
ThL· data not only enables "Club" members to deal easily and effectively with any associated claim, thus reducing the costs associated with processing claims under the present system, but ato enables the guarantee or security to be removed from any provmon in the accounts.
The system L· ato designed to operate in the present "copy 5" system, but can readily interface with Customs national computerised systems/NCTS, or where such systems do not exist, provide Customs with a free on-line access to the system.
