DOC_EN\CM\307\307143
3 October 1996
NOTE ON GENERAL DEVELOPMENTS IN THE TRANSPORT OF GOODS IN EUROPE AND THEIR TRANSIT UNDER CUSTOMS CONTROL
GENERAL BACKGROUND
Explosive growth combined with increasing complexity
Intra-European trade has expanded enormously since the Community came into being (1959), undoubtedly as a result of the progress achieved with integration among the Member States, but also as a result of general economic growth.
It is worth pointing out, in this connection, that the former EFTA countries have experienced growth in this area on a par with that achieved by the EEC, although the means used have been different.
At the same time, the development of transport techniques and those used for sending and processing information has increased the complexity of operations and their spread between Member States, between European countries and around the world.
Widespread liberalization ...
This growth in economic interdependence, which has outstripped national economic growth and remained unaffected by the 'crisis', has been accompanied by a virtually complete worldwide liberalization of trade, although it is not possible to discern clearly a relationship of cause and effect.
... the organization of which remains vague ...
Except for the elimination of tariff barriers in the early years, what has actually happened has not been structured liberalization but the continuous removal of obstacles and the smoothing-out of differences.
Consequently, we have seen the creation of a huge, vaguely-defined territory (as a result of the multiplicity and variety of agreements) which has no real internal organization, akin to a huge road network without a highway code.
... with extremely negative consequences
The internal borders between Member States were eliminated in 1993, and new association agreements (the latest with Turkey) and accessions have taken place since then.
The return of the countries of Eastern Europe to economic liberalism has increased the size of this vague territory, while enhancing its fluid nature as a result of their economic and administrative chaos and uncontrolled privatization, and this has given free rein to irregular activities.
In this shifting scenario, the basic rules (essentially those concerning origin and customs value), which had been stipulated in minute detail by the GATT since 1947, were replaced at Marrakesh by those resulting from the Uruguay Round.
These new rules, which are based on global liberalism, are consequently contributing to the trend described above.
In addition, although they form the basis of the attitude of the signatory governments, their implementing provisions are still imprecise, which adds further to the general uncertainty.
DOC_EN\CM\307\307143
In these circumstances customs controls have disappeared (the Community's internal borders), are disorganized (borders with the Eastern European countries) or have become substantially weaker.
Within the Community, we must be honest and acknowledge that liberalization has taken place at the expense of the customs authorities, whose opportunities for action and resources in terms of equipment and staff are shrinking everywhere.
In the countries of Central and Eastern Europe, economic liberalism is presented as a corollary of political liberalism, which makes any regulatory measures suspect and thereby facilitates all kinds of abuses.
In addition, the various trade areas are reciprocally 'contaminated', particularly by the various forms of transit; these are essentially Community transit and the TIR system.
Fraud, irregularities and administrative shortcomings have expanded to form a phenomenon on an impressive scale.
The volume of undischarged Community transit documents is estimated to amount to the equivalent of a year's trade, although it is impossible to say whether fraud or administrative delays are to blame.
TIR-related customs queries still run at the rate of 200 carnets every day, and customs officials generally limit themselves to demanding that the guarantee be drawn upon, without pursuing the fraudsters.
It is also difficult for them to base their claims on a clear procedure, because transit documents (under all procedures) are frequently filled out very badly, or not at all, since customs officials are no longer sure what they may or may not demand regarding goods in transit.
A significant example is provided by the finding reported at the Luxembourg Statistical Seminar in March 1996 concerning what are known as 'mirror' statistics, drawn up by two countries for their reciprocal trade.
The mirror statistics for Germany and Italy reveal variances of 35% in each direction, i.e. 70% in all.
The highest figures, of course, relate to exports from Germany to Italy, which qualify for exemption from VAT; the lowest are for imports into Italy, on which VAT has to be paid.
The overall picture is that European trade is no longer under control.
The term 'European', rather than 'Community', is used deliberately on account of the Community's extension, in various guises, to most European countries and also because the situation is no better in the countries of Central and Eastern Europe.
THE NEED TO BRING TRADE UNDER CONTROL
I do not intend to give a lengthy economic disquisition on the need to bring trade under control, because it is so obvious.
Suffice it to say that such control is a determining factor for any economic policy measures, that its absence poses a threat to national budgets and the Community budget and that the present situation conceals massive and steadily increasing fraud, which amounts to the rapid development of a 'grey market' throughout Europe's economy; this explains the sluggishness of the official economy which, being subject to regulated operating conditions, is prohibited from fighting it out with the parallel economy.
DOC EN\CM\307\307143
lead to restrictive measures which may seriously damage the relations between peoples, thus having an effect totally at variance with the ideals of the United Nations.
THE URGENT NEED TO DRAW UP A PLAN TO CONTROL TRADE FLOWS THROUGHOUT EUROPE
General approach
The need for urgent action does not need to be demonstrated further.
The seriousness of the situation is such that significant damage has already been done.
An economic explosion would cause serious political damage.
The problem goes far beyond questions of how customs procedures operate.
We have to bring the European market under control by adopting a complex approach at several levels.
Action must be taken at pan-European level, and it is at this level that measures taken by the various relevant players must be coordinated.
These are led, of course, by the Community and its specific customs transit procedure, which is currently being extended to the Central and Eastern European countries (the four Visegrad countries, with effect from 1 July 1996), and by the UN Economic Commission for Europe, the custodian of the TIR procedure, which is particularly suitable for full lorry-loads.
The TIR procedure is an essential link between Community Member States and third countries.
The Community should also consider the possibility of reintroducing it within Community territory, specifically for full lorry-loads of goods in external transit (currently under T1 ) .
The TIR procedure has only been undermined by the almost universal relaxation of controls, and can be improved quickly.
Finally, the involvement of the ECMT (European Conference of Ministers of Transport) could be envisaged.
This body does not manage any transit procedure, but its economic and statistical expertise would be a valuable contribution to the work that needs to be done.
In addition, the involvement of the Ministers of Transport is indispensable for the reform which is envisaged.
Development of the concept of 'transit under customs control'
'Transit under customs control' has two elements: first, a transfer of specified goods from point A to point B, and secondly the situation of those goods, i.e. their customs status, since the regulatory measures to which they are normally subject are suspended during the transfer operation.
The two operations are similar in many respects. The customs authorities, like the person who has booked the transport, wants to find the goods at their destination in good condition, intact and within a reasonable time.
Transport contracts and transit declarations thus have similarities which are not sufficiently exploited where the organization and checking of the performance thereof is concerned, since this takes place in one go.
DOC EN\CM\307\307143
control and prohibition measures.
It may be followed, on completion, by the full range of customs procedures, just as it itself may commence following any customs procedure, with the exception of release for consumption, which frees goods from all customs requirements.
Their circulation is then only subject to any rules which may apply to the circulation of domestic goods.
The importance of customs duties is declining as a result of international agreements and the progress achieved by the Community.
The amounts involved often remain substantial, on account of the level of excise duty (alcohol, tobacco) and VAT, both of which are also suspended by a transit operation.
At the same time, however, transit operations concern goods which have a status, and the importance of that status in transactions is increasing.
Goods are either in free circulation in the Community or are not; they are eligible, or ineligible, to benefit under this association agreement or that Uruguay Round rule; they come under, or do not come under, such and such a common organization of the market, etc.
The concepts of the status of goods are thus increasingly important, because they determine the nature and possibility of the commercial operations envisaged.
Transit under customs control, in the broad sense, thus covers a very complex reality and is far from disappearing.
It is, and will remain, a favoured instrument for controlling European trade flows, because the economy has a permanent need to move goods under a total or partial suspension of the financial or regulatory rules applicable to them.
The area concerned is Europe and its neighbours.
Given the negotiation of new agreements, the limits are imprecise and the political situation is in flux.
The definition of the scope of the work to be undertaken also varies according to the transit procedures used.
For obvious reasons of prudence, it would be necessary to limit it initially to the Community and its Associate Members, where Community transit is concerned, and to the current Contracting Parties where the TIR system is concerned1.
The poor functioning of customs procedures has made statistics imprecise.
What is needed here is not detailed economic analyses, but a definition of the volumes to be dealt with and the geographical flows covering their transport.
1 It should be noted that as soon as a 'new member' is admitted to the United Nations, it may accept all the conventions adopted by that body, including the TIR system, without having to prove that it is capable of applying their provisions.
DOC_EN\CM\307\307143
The Community transit procedure is primarily a victim of its own size, while the TIR system suffers from the poor application of its procedures by customs administrations and the vagueness surrounding the conditions of its application in the Community.
What are needed are steps to slim down and speed up the purely physical administrative tasks and to enhance the self-checking which is built into procedures.
This goal is to be sought by:
- computerizing operations, following parallel rules for the two procedures and using the same network wherever possible.
Care must be taken not to create 'passive' information, which would give customs services a feeling of false security, whereby they would feel exempt from exercising any scrutiny or checks ;
- speeding up operations and stepping up cooperation, thereby making controls more effective and enabling straightforward incidents to be resolved quickly;
- speeding up exploitation of the similarity between the customs procedure operation and the transport operation: an analysis of the two processes shows many similarities which are not exploited.
Procedures should be based on commercial procedures (transport contract) to a greater extent;
- reducing the huge volume of Community transit operations by seeking technical solutions, by sector, based on the commercial tools noted above.
While maintaining the basic principles governing the procedure, the conditions of their application should be adjusted to the mode of transport or to the procedure used commercially.
Transit regimes which take account of the specific features of the transfer of goods (mode of transport, procedure, etc.) will therefore be sought.
A basis for this does exist in the current rules, but it has not been adequately exploited.
This point deserves detailed study.
With the same aim in mind, steps will be taken to seek the introduction of continuous contractual transit procedures, to cover frequent regular movements of goods between many European companies, whether they belong to the same group or not.
Such procedures should be used for the operation in its entirety.
The use of the TIR system in the Community should be investigated without prejudice.
It has demonstrated its value when the procedure is properly applied.
It is particularly suitable for full lorry-loads and it would be a simple matter to use the TIR 1 and TIR 2 procedures.
DOC EN\CM\307\307143
Special resources should be devoted to this essential task; a working party should be set up comprising customs officials, operators, insurers and bankers.
The proposal is to carry out research into the way in which the existence of traffic routes might be used to bring transit operations under more effective control.
In principle, such routes make it easier to carry out monitoring on the ground.
The idea is not to suggest the creation of compulsory itineraries, but rather preferred routes, the use of which would go hand in hand with increased certainty for everyone, greater speed and/or less stringent guarantee requirements (to be investigated).
These routes could have a 'varying customs presence' according to the countries concerned.
For instance, they would make it possible to improve the situation of the Eastern European countries without having to wait for the entire administrative apparatus of a country to reach a satisfactory level.
HOW THE WORK SHOULD PROCEED
The first stage is to define a common policy for the whole of Europe and its neighbours.
To this end, it is proposed that there should be a meeting between the Executive Secretary of the UN ECE and the Director-General of DG XXI.
Participation by the ECMT should be considered, but seems desirable.
Each organization would then determine, in conjunction with its members, what work needed to be undertaken in order to achieve the implementation of that policy, i.e. its translation into a plan for overhauling the existing transit procedures.
Periodic meetings (with preparation beforehand) between the same parties would review the progress made and decide on the requisite proposals.
CONCLUSION
In conclusion, I shall content myself with pointing out that, in both Geneva and Brussels, work on customs transit procedures is not given sufficient thought as an element of European transport policy.
Customs regulations, which are highly technical by their very nature, are left to experts in the field who are, however, not always in a position to take account of the specific situations affecting transport and even less to turn the commercial procedures (contracts, etc.) under which transport operations take place to good account. The present crisis must provide the impetus to improve this situation.
Translation
As requested I enclose a copy of the memorandum by European insurers on contraventions under the Community transit system, for the hearing on 4 November 1996 at the European Parliament.
* Mr Enrico Orlando, Chairman of the CEA's Marine, Aviation and Transport Plenary Committee, and Deputy Director of Lavoro & Sicurtà — l'Italica, Italy, * Mr Nigel Jenkins, Chairman of the Institute of London Underwriters, UK, * Mr Annibale Rossi, Deputy Director of La Neuchâteloise, Switzerland, and three observers: Mr Birger Joensen, Chairman of the Legal Committee, Association of Danish Marine Underwriters, Mr Jacques Léglu, Deputy Secretary-General and Mrs Cornelia Valus, Research Assistant of the CEA.
To ensure they are fully informed, I should be grateful if you would kindly let me have details of the time and place of the hearing we have been asked to attend.
AI 046A (10/96) 14 October 1996
Parliament to look into the problems associated with the Operation of the Community Transit System (CTS).
For several years, it has been recognised by those involved in the CTS and TIR schemes that both, systems are experiencing serious difficulties.
It was probably in 1994 that insurers and their clients first became concerned about the serious back­log of unresolved problems and paperwork.
The experience of recent years would suggest that the symptoms of such a breakdown, are all too apparent.
Recent experience shows that fraudsters have been targeting highly taxed goods, such as tobacco and alcohol, and so European insurers have become increasingly reluctant to grant cover for these items.
The insurance cover itself may take the form of differing types of insurance policy, including haulier's liability insurance (CMR insurance, which includes customs duty), freight forwarder's liability insurance (which may, if separately agreed as a supplement, include cover for the forwarder in respect of his liability associated with the issuing of Tl forms), insurance for cargo extended to cover duty, and guarantee bonds offered by banks or trade associations and often backed by insurance cover (a fuller explanation of the different types of insurance cover available may be found in Annex I).
Whether demands for duty payments arise from CTS or TIR sendings, the impact often falls on the same insureds.
The accumulation of demands for alleged unpaid duties threatens to drive many of these parties into liquidation.
In general, it is the innocent companies that tend to be facing these demands as the fraudulent players have long since wound up their operations and disappeared by the time the requests for the unpaid duty arc received.
To date, the authorities have not been conspicuous in their success in bringing to account the major fraudsters.
For the majority of European marine insurers, the most serious claims in respect of non-payment of duty have arisen under haulier's and freight forwarder's liability policies.
Most covers contain a limit under the policy for recovery any one loss and an aggregate limit for any one year.
The frequency of claims, many being advised by customs authorities at a very late stage, has meant that policyholders have found their protections exhausted.
At the same time parties protected by guarantee bonds are being pursued for recovery of duty, Guarantors are only liable for claims advised within 12 months, whilst principals can be pursued until the normal statutory period of limitation in the country concerned.
If the system were operating normally, 12 months would be more than adequate - in practice, administration is poor and delays endemic
While claims have aggregated to freight forwarders and hauliers, marine insurers have also been faced with, an increasing incidence of crime in many forms.
Thefts or hijacks (often involving extortion) of entire loads - once a problem in only one or two areas - have become a problem throughout the European Community.
No Member State is free from these crimes which are displaying signs of increasingly sophisticated organisation.
It is not easy to say how far these cases could be reduced by better adininistratiori of the CTS and TIR systems, but the failure to properly apply the rules of carriage breeds a contempt for the rule of law and an environment in which fraud can flourish.
WHY THESE
The creation of the European Single Market in 1993 led to changes in the use of * T » forms.
In particular the system was changed from one where the presumption of liability for duty was assumed to one where the presumption was one of free circulation unless accompanied by a Tl form.
This speeded up border passage and appears to have led to reductions in customs staff.
The change of emphasis seems to have extended to customs authorities, and proper completion of Tl forms has not always been the rule.
The number of undischarged forms hae grown rapidly and authorities have been slow in returning TS forms to originating customs.
This meant that evidence of proper discharge was lost pr difficult to reconcile, and that any fraud was undiscovered and could be repeated by a criminal party with little fear of detection.
Insurers were likewise unaware of problems since cither goods had arrived at actual destination and cargo insurers had therefore not received a claim for the loss of the goods, or fraud had been perpetrated against customs authorities only (and owing to the delays outlined in 2.3 above, insurers had not yet received claims in respect of the non-payment of duty).
2.B. Whilst no National Insurance Associations have been able to provide detailed figures as to losses or their source (i.e. rnaladniinistration or fraud), several markets have indicated that there is reason to believe that by number, the major proportion of CTS claims have been due to maladministration, with only a small percentage resulting from fraud.
This suggests that in the majority of cases duties havej in effect, been paid twice.
However it is also believed that the small number of claims attributed to fraud, in fact, actually represent a higher proportion of the claims in terms of value, since fraud is generally attempted on higher valued sendings.
It is also felt that by number, instances of fraud have been more prevalent in the TIR system, whilst rnaladniinistration has been a bigger problem vrithin CTS.
Since 1993 insurers have also become concerned that customs authorities arc increasingly placing the responsibility on insurers' clients to prove proper discharge of « Τ » forms rather than themselves proving non-discharge.
This practice is unacceptable, as insurers' clients do not have the necessary means at their disposal to do this.
In particular, they do not have the same access as the customs authorities to the documentation that is required to prove their case.
OF THE
PROBLEM
Annex II shows some typical examples of fraudulent claims received and met by insurers.
In many instances it is hard to categorise losses as having been caused by rnaladrninistration or fraud.
Whilst no figures exist for the level of claims, some details are available for thefts of trailer loads (see Annex ΠΓ).
These do not necessarily represent fraud against customs or insurers, but are nevertheless criminal acts and indicative of a rising problem.
Several members have advised of transits where insurance cover was granted on sendings known, to be fraudulent by customs and other authorities prior to transit.
Insurers themselves were not aware of the situation and whilst sendings were monitored at the time by the authorities, claims arc now being made against insurers for lost duty.
3.4 Certain members have also received claims for duty, where a client correctly complied with the necessary customs clearance procedures, but the customs clearance stamp on the T5 document later proved not to be genuine (either forged or stolen).
All members have reported increasing notifications of non-discharge. notifications make it impossible for underwriters to properly assess risks,
ACTION
Over recent years, insurers have become more and more selective in their choice of clients in an attempt to reduce the extent of moral hazard (and thus fraud) among insureds.
In many markets, steps have also been taken to exercise greater control over the insurance cover offered in respect of Community transits - for example, ■ consignments of goods such as tobacco and alcohol wbich attract the high duty payments, are now generally declared to insurers on a case by case basis, rather than simply accumulating under the previous open cover facilities.
Insurers are keenly aware of the problems faced by their clients and would like to assist where poesible.
Not only do they seek customer satisfaction but insurers also see a genuine opportunity to expand business in the area of Community transits.
However two uncertainties remain.
Firstly, there is the question of the magnitude of the current and past problem.
The Court of Auditors Report suggests annual revenue losses of ECU 200 million and the Commission's own report suggests losses of ECU 750 million between 1990 - 1994, Underwriters need clarification on the impact of these old years.
Secondly, whilst some steps have been taken to improve adniinistration, much needs to be done.
Insurers, as risk takers, are not looking for perfection and realise and understand that no system can be entirely free from fraud - they do, however, expect the relevant authorities to take every possible measure to combat it.
Insurers need to know that systems are reliable and that instances of non-discharge are not paper losses (maladministration), but genuine loss or fraud, Then, and only then, can both insureds' and insurers' aspirations be met.
The only certain step to prevent the breakdown of the CTS and TIR systems is to grant an immediate amnesty for all prior claims for duty, except against known perpetrators of fraud, This will ensure clarity for insurers but also for their insureds, who will otherwise be Hable for uninsured losses.
Unless such an amnesty is granted, there is a very real danger that bankruptcies will become more widespread amongst freight forwarders and hauliers.
This in turn will make it more difficult to obtain and complete Tl forms, which will then seriously impede the free circulation of goods within the Community,
It is essential that the problems identified in the existing system are addressed now before the « Τ » system is extended to include further new territories,
The marine insurance industry has led the way forward for other insurers and played a major part in the growth of international trade.
Insurers can and wish to respond to the future needs of the CTS, but will only be able to do eo with confidence when clarity return e.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the European Confederation of Tobacco Retailers (CEDT), Brussels.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
CONTRIBUTION TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT INQUIRY COMMITTEE ON FRAUD IN TRANSIT 18 NOVEMBER 1996
Tobacco fraud
European Confederation of Tobacco Retailers (CEDT)
Introduction
CEDT appreciates to have been invited to give evidence to the Committee concerning the specific problems of transit fraud with tobacco products.
CEDT has previously contributed to the Committee hearings identifying the special problems relating to fraud with tobacco.
CEDT's written contribution from that session has already been distributed to the Committee members.
The arguments and proposals for solutions in our previous contribution are naturally still valid.
CEDT is extremely concerned about the fraud development in the transit regimes, and the direct impact fraud has on the tobacco retail industry.
Tobacco retailers are contributing largely to the fiscal revenue in the member states by functioning as one of the largest excise duty collectors and furthermore tobacco retailers have a controlling role as to the sale of tobacco on behalf of society.
Consequently, when tobacco products and other sorts of tobacco are brought illegally into circulation it not only harms the tobacco retailers but to an even larger extent it harms the member state finances and also to a certain extent the EU financial resources.
Furthermore, society's control on tobacco sales is being undermined.
Even though authorities during recent years have taken action to fight tobacco contraband.
CEDT feels that the transit regimes to a large extent still represents an open gate for contraband to operate.
This is mainly due to a lack of control of transit
CEDT, EP Inquiry Comminee on fraud in transit
In order for the tobacco retailing industry to be able to survive, immediate measures must be taken in order to fight the tobacco fraud taking place in the transit regimes.
We believe that effective control measures could reduce effectively the tobacco fraud and consequently contribute to an overall benefit for society.
Obviously, reinforcement of controls in other well known areas fraud is a precondition for a reconstitution of the legal tobacco market.
CEDT welcomes the initiative taken by the European Parliament to examine the problems in the transit regimes and we hope to be able to contribute to at least a part of the solutions.
Background
The tobacco fraud has re-flourished during recent years mainly due to an extensive increase in taxation of tobacco products.
Consequently, the market for non taxed illegally consumed tobacco products has grown.
The contraband problem is to our knowledge largest in some of the southern member states, as a result of the large consumption of tobacco products in these countries and the high taxation.
Nevertheless, fraud is expected also to be extensive in other high taxation countries; i.e. the Scandinavia, the Benelux, UK and Germany.
Certain events taking place at the same time as the increase in the taxation of tobacco products have according to our opinion facilitated the fraud, these events being:
• Creation of the single market and abolition of borders • Extension of the transit regimes to more and more countries • Simplified customs procedures
Finally, we note, that excise duties on tobacco products are varying a lot between member states even after the creation of the single market.
Methods of bringing tobacco products illegally into circulation
The main mechanisms of introducing illegal tobacco products to EU have changed slightly during the last couples of years.
The main mechanisms are the following:
= > A couple of years ago tobacco products legally entered the Community from major ports as Rotterdam or Antwerp or departed legally from EU based producers under cover of the transit systems, Τ or TIR, for destinations outside the Community. CEDT, EP Inquiry Committee on fraud in transit
However, the tobacco products never left the Community, but were fraudulently brought into free circulation without taxes and duties being paid.
Due to the growing awareness of the misuse of the transit systems, stricter regulations for carrying tobacco products under cover of the transit systems have been introduced (i.e. increased guarantees for transit operations involving tobacco products). Consequently, the number of registered tobacco transports under cover of the transit systems and fraud cases in connection with these have been reduced drastically.
Private smuggling of tobacco products from countries with low taxes and duties to neighbouring countries with high taxes is creating an increasing problem.
Problems have especially occurred in Germany's Eastern border regions, in Austria, Italy, the Spanish border areas to Gibraltar and Andorra and in the French border areas to Andorra as well.
• Sea transport.
Large shipments of tobacco products are loaded onto vessels or coasters that are popularly known as "wet nurses".
Once the vessels are in international waters the tobacco consignments are broken into smaller cargoes and brought illegally into member states by high powered speed boats.
To our knowledge the largest volume of tobacco products, that brought into illegal circulation are deriving from transit operations, where the goods have been wrongly declared.
Revenue effects
In annex you will find a country by country status report of the tobacco contraband situation in 8 of the EU member states.
The information is provided by our member associations on the basis of public available information and information provided by the national authorities.
Shortly summarised, the following revenue facts can be deducted from the report:
CEDT, EP Inquiry Committee on fraud in transit
CEDT proposals for solutions
According to our opinion the following measures could help to reduce the tobacco fraud in the transit regimes:
Continuation of the strict customs and transit procedures for tobacco consignments
Increased effective controls at ports, customs offices of entry and departure.
Controls should be based on the use of technology like scanners for containers and lorries identifying the cargo securing efficient and quick operations.
Increased cooperation with customs authorities of departure in third countries concerning pre-announcement of tobacco consignments, especially when arriving per ship, in order to identify tobacco shipments.
Increased cooperation with customs authorities of destination in third countries concerning the arrival of the tobacco consignments from EU, especially when arriving by ship, in order to secure the proper expon from EU.
Direct necessary financial means to a supra national investigation body for investigation into customs fraud and contraband.
CEDT will continue to cooperate with all the parties in order to eliminate the dangers for our industry being undermined by contraband.
The European Parliament Inquiry Committee on fraud in transit can count on our full support and cooperation.
CEDT, EP Inquiry Committee on fraud in transit
CEDT survey on Contraband 30 October 1996
