EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by Rothmans of Pall Mall (International) Limited, Aylesbury, UK.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
STATEMENT TO THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM BY ROTHMANS OF PALL MALL (INTERNATIONAL) LIMITED
Rothmans International Is a major manufacturer of cigarette» and tobacco products in the European Union (EU) with factories in Belgium, Ireland, The Netherlands and the United Kingdom (UK).
Product from these factories Is supplied to all EU domestic markets and for export from the EU.
Under the Rothmans Group structure the factories in the UK are operated by the Group's export region, Rothmans of Pall Mall (International) Limited and the other European factories operated by the Group's Europe domestic market region, Rothmans International Europe.
The Group's activities sustain significant employment and economic activity in the European Union.
The Community Transit System
Rothmans has complied with the requirements for Internal and external Community Transit of product manufactured by Group companies within the EU Member States in accordance with the appropriate regulations:
Council Regulation (EEC) No. 222/77 Council Regulation (EEC) No. 2728/90 Council Regulation (EEC) No. 2454/93
Within the definition of the Regulations, Rothmans has acted as a "Principar in respect of T1 declarations.
Historically, the incidence of problem T1 shipments prior to 1992 was rare.
On the few occasions problems did arise discharge of the T1 document was always achieved by providing acceptable secondary evidence to the Authorities.
The development and expansion in trade with Eastern Europe and the non­availability of TIR Carnets Increased the demand on the use of T1 documentation.
Notification of the discharge of a T1 shipment failed to happen on a timely basis and the system operated on a "no news is good news" basis in that the Authorities failed to provide positive confirmation of documentation discharge or problem identification.
In common with many other T1 users, Rothmans has had to respond to the belated (and aggressive) challenge and demands of Authorities in respect of the 1992 period.
We have had to address such Issues as false stamps or misuse of official stamps, forged documentation, etc.
In support of the T1 requirements, Rothmans negotiated on a formal basis with the Member State Authorities in accordance with stated procedures.
The cost Implications of funding the Comprehensive Guarantee amounted to no more than (say) £30,000 for a £20-25 million guarantee facility.
No T1 Issued under the Comprehensive Guarantee facility has failed to discharge.
No Member State Authority has sought to "call-in" the guarantee in respect of a T1 shipment
The Company's costs of doing export business were raised significantly when, with effect from 1 February 1996, the use of Comprehensive Guarantees was suspended.
The Initial suspension was described as temporary and was for a period of six months.
The suspension has subsequently been extended until 31 December 1996, and we have it on good authority that measures are In hand for a further period of suspension -the temporary suspension is assuming an air of permanency.
The cost implications of operating Single Guarantee facilities can be measured in terms of being 30 times more than the cost of operating the Comprehensive Guarantee.
At the same time the Comprehensive Guarantee has still been maintained to cover T1 shipments not yet discharged by the Authorities (nine months additional costsl).
As a major exporter, Rothmans feels strongly that the suspension of Comprehensive CT Guarantees has placed exports from the EU at a competitive disadvantage on the world market Although the suspension of the Comprehensive Guarantees would seem to have been intended to address the question of fraud in the transit system relating to cigarettes originating outside the EU, Its true effect has been to raise costs of EU-based exports.
This financial penalty imposed on operators such as Rothmans has done nothing to facilitate the workings of the transit system or the fight against fraud.
It has achieved a transfer of funds from ourselves to the banking sector and placed the Company at a commercial disadvantage to non-EU based manufacturers.
The true effect of the Single Guarantee procedure is to impose an additional "internal tax" on operators.
Rothmans is committed to developing and maintaining transit systems to safeguard the highest practical level of commercial security which will be consistent with the provision of fiscal security.
The Commtttae is considering evidence of alleged fraud and organised crime on ■ significant scale together with many examples of shortcomings In procedures end maladministration - we are victims of both.
The approach of Risk Analysis has not been fully and consistently applied and implemented throughout the EU. It Is therefore not possible for the Authorities to make proper allowance for those organisations who have demonstrated a firm resolve to fulfil their responsibilities for revenue compliance.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Swiss authorities.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
During the present inquiry into the Community transit system, Switzerland has occasionally been accused of being a centre of contraband goods.
Switzerland is aware that certain operators take advantage of its status as a non-Member State of the European Union and its geographical position to violate the EUs customs legislation.
These practices harm not only the European Union, but also the Swiss economy.
Switzerland has no interest in such operations being organized from its territory.
It already takes vigorous action to combat all violations of customs rules, and the scale of violations committed in Switzerland - compared to those committed in the EU itself- must not be exaggerated.
Switzerland cooperates closely with the Member States of the European Union to combat fraud, for example by immediately notifying the customs administrations of the Member States and the Commission of the European Union of shipments of certain products (for example, cigarettes) leaving Switzerland.
Switzerland is prepared further to strengthen this cooperation: by joining the EU's 'early warning system' (EWS/SCENT), if the EU so desires; by finalizing negotiations on an administrative mutual aid agreement in the customs field; by facilitating administrative aid in penal matters.
DOC EN\CM\313\313390
Fraud in the context of Community transit
It is well know that certain types of merchandise, such as cigarettes, which attract high taxation, are shipped from Switzerland to the European Union (EU) under the Community transit system, and freely marketed there - illegally - on the basis of forged or false dispatch notes.
This practice has meant a heavy loss of revenue for the EU.
In order to avoid violations of this kind, the Swiss customs administration has, since 27 October 1993, notified the customs administrations of the transit countries and those in whose jurisdiction the customs post of destination is located and the Commission of the European Union (UCLAF) of all shipments of cigarettes leaving Switzerland under the Community transit system.
This procedure is based on an administrative agreement between the Commission and the EFTA countries.
In this way both the Commission and the customs administrations of the Member States of the European Union receive the information they require to order appropriate surveillance, control or other measures to combat fraud.
This does not, however, totally prevent violations of the EU's customs legislation.
Specific measures by the customs bodies of the Member States of the European Union are also needed if this is to be achieved.
However, the customs administrations of these States are clearly not in a position to monitor all shipments.
Switzerland cannot be held responsible for this, since this is a problem linked to the completion of the internal market and the opening-up of borders with the countries of Central and Eastern Europe.
The early warning system referred to above must be improved.
By letter of 7 November 1996, the Swiss customs administration notified the Commission that it was interested in the EU's early warning system, SCENT (Early Warning System - EWS) and that it wished to join. The new electronic information interchange assisted solution will replace the present fax-based early warning system and will contribute markedly to an even more rapid dissemination of the information necessary to combat fraud.
Since the entry into force of Decision 1/96 of the EC/EFTA Joint Committee of 1 August 1996, shipments of cigarettes are only permitted on presentation of an individual guarantee (for example, security or cash deposits).
This new formula is strictly applied by the Swiss customs and administration.
Given the increasingly international background of violations of customs legislation and other laws and regulations enforced by customs administrations, a transfrontier exchange of information and close collaboration between the customs administrations of the various states involved are necessary.
For this reason Switzerland will endeavour to bring to a conclusion, probably before the end of this year, negotiations with the European Union on an administrative aid agreement in the customs sphere which broadly corresponds to Protocol No 11 of the Agreement on theTEuropean Economic Area.
This will allow a marked improvement in cooperation in the customs sphere between Switzerland and the European Union, since administrative aid in this area will no longer be limited to certain individual procedures, but will be more generalized (for example, covering customs clearance in transit under the TIR carnet).
DOC_EN\CM\313\313390
This Swiss customs administration is aware that Switzerland, by virtue of its geographical position and its status as a non-member state of the European Union, is still being misused as a basis for operations and a country of origin for violations of EU customs legislation.
This harms not only the European Union, but also the Swiss economy.
Switzerland therefore has no interest in allowing such activities to be organized from its territory.
In order to back investigations undertaken in the various Member States of the European Union, the Swiss customs administration has provided administrative assistance in numerous cases and complied with requests to take measures against offenders on Swiss territory, ordering searches, questioning and the confiscation of documents.
These procedures are generally very time-consuming, since the persons implicated in fraud have numerous legal channels open to them.
Owing to the revision of Swiss federal law on administrative assistance in penal matters which tightened up and accelerated the procedure, Switzerland will be better equipped to fulfil its obligations in terms of international cooperation.
DOC EN\CM\313\313390
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the European Parliament Committee Of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Scotch Whisky Association, Edinburgh.
DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
20th November 1996 Ref: IS/JGH/CE­5
PROBLEMS IN THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT (CTI SYSTEM A Scotch Whisky Industry Perspective on the Current Situation
May I commend to you, in your capacity as one of the two UK representatives on the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the CT System (as well, of course, as being its Chairman), the enclosed submission to the Committee by The Scotch Whisky Association.
Anything you may be able to do in support of the Association's position will be much appreciated.
I am also writing in similar terms to Dr W M Ewing MEP.
Please do not hesitate to contact me if I can be of any further assistance.
(JHedley) Director, Operational & Technical Affairs Enclosure
IUOIÍTSSED IN SCOTLAND NO: 35148
PROBLEMS IN THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT fCT) SYSTEMA Scotch Whisky Industry Perspective on the Current Situation
INTRODUCTION
The Scotch Whisky industry is a massive export earner for the European Union (EU).
In 1995, the value of exports of Scotch Whisky to countries outside the EU was over £1.4 billion.
It has been estimated that the industry accounts for some 70,000 direct and indirect jobs in the UK. The jobs of those employed directly in Scottish production are primarily in rural areas, . where there are few alternative means of employment, or in urban areas with high unemployment.
In essence, the external CT procedure allows the controlled movement within the EU of EU­produced goods destined for export to third countries, without payment during transit of EU duties and taxes.
An effective CT system which does not impose disproportionate burdens on exporters is essential if Scotch Whisky is to continue its export success.
Scotch Whisky producers use the external CT system for certain exports to countries outwith the EU.
Collectively, they initiate approximately 250 such CT movements per month.
Several of the markets concerned, for example those in Central and Eastern Europe, have developed into important onesJor Scotch Whisky.
Crucially, they are regarded as among the markets with the greatest opportunity for
However, to provide the most fertile environment for growth, stable conditions are a prerequisite, and these must include a secure and effective customs framework for exports, within which routine procedures can be applied with the maximum simplicity and the minimum cost for business.
Only such a trading environment will allow EU­based companies to remain competitive and succeed in a tough external market­place.
1. τ;β: '" As concluded in the European Commission's Communication of March 1995, Fraud in the Transit Procedure, Solutions Foreseen and Perspectives for the Future, 'the existence of the transit systems is indispensable' as part of this trading environment.
The Scotch Whisky industry is therefore seriously concerned at the problems which currently afflict the CT system and at the possibility that it will be further undermined.
LONDON OFFICE; 14 Cos?
From the industry's point of view, further disintegration would be catastrophic and trade in certain markets would probably become unviable : the integrity of the CT system must instead be maintained and improved.
The aim of this paper is to analyse the problems from the particular perspective of the Scotch Whisky industry, and to put forward the industry's views on the measures by which they may best be tackled.
BACKGROUND
There have been increasing operational problems in administering the CT system.
These have helped to provide opportunities for fraud and abuse of the system, which have in turn added to the operational difficulties, and so on.
A vicious circle appears to have developed.
Operational Problems .
Among the operational problems cited by the Commission and others, one frequently mentioned by Scotch Whisky exporters is delay in processing documentation.
Belated return of the return copy by the customs offices of exit leads to movements being undischarged for too long a period.
There have been occasional instances of the official return copy never being returned at all, even when it is known that the movement completed its transit safely.
In the industry's view, requests for the return of outstanding documentation are not being pursued with sufficient speed or thoroughness.
Unless this situation can be remedied, consideration should be given where possible to alternative commercial evidence of exit from the EU, of which there is often a wide variety available.
Exporters are not satisfied without adequate commercial proof of ultimate delivery, and it is felt that such proof should be equally acceptable to customs authorities";
It is not considered reasonable that exporters can be exposed to a potential duty liability because of a failure within the customs system to discharge CT documentation efficiently.
Of particular concern to the Scotch Whisky industry is the fact that the customs offices of exit from the EU seem lax in their processing and returning of the control copy T5, which is essential for distillers claiming an export refund under the Common Agricultural Policy.
They experience considerable frustration at not having any controLaver. the IS!s. return within laid­down timescales.
The control copy T5s must be processed and returned more efficiently.
Goods subject to high taxation, for example
Pg: 5 P.5/11 cigarettes and spirits, are particularly at risk because of the incentive to evade that taxation.
When illegal activity of this nature occurs, the legitimate trade suffers from unfair and illicit competition.
The viciousness of some of the organized criminals involved is widely acknowledged.
Governments have a duty in these circumstances to protect legitimate trade and the public; and to pursue fraudsters and tackle the root causes of fraud.
It has been questioned whether they are putting sufficient resources into tackling the problem and acting with sufficient vigour.
The measures taken so far have been described as 'papering over the cracks'.
Where fraud or irregularities do occur in CT movements, customs authorities tend to fall back too readily on the guarantee principal for recovery of. duties . and taxes, rather than pursuing those who are profiting from the fraud.
An attitude by the customs authorities that 'It's not our problem, it's up to commercial interests to ensure proper control and security of consignments, and to insure against the risks accordingly' is one which reflects an abnegation of those authorities' responsibilities to play their due part.
Alternatively, the authorities tend to react by imposing 'blanket' measures and restrictions, which are not based upon a proper risk assessment and . thus unfairly penalize low-risk legitimate traders.
As far as the Scotch Whisky trade is concerned, members of The Scotch Whisky Association report only negligible losses and irregularities occurring in CT export movements.
For almost all consignments, CT documentation can be validly discharged.
This may in part be because distillers generally employ highly reputable and experienced hauliers for CT movements.
If there is any fraudulent diversion of Scotch Whisky during a CT movement, it is more likely to occur during movements by parties at several removes down thë~cnstributioh chain, over whom the producers have very little or no control, or indeed even knowledge.
Prudent, legitimate Scotch Whisky exporters are alarmed by the possibility that the use of CT may be impaired or taken away due to frauds over which they have no control, and which are unconnected with direct Scotch Whisky exports.
There is an obvious need to make it more difficult for forgeries of the present multiplicity of different official stamps of national customs authorities to succeed.
The number and type of fiscal stamps should be reviewed, and systems which verify whether a given stamp is genuine should be developed and promoted.
Reduced and harmonized rates of duty on alcoholic drinks would reduce the incentives to commit fraud.
Many large Scotch Whisky producers do not act as the principal in obtaining the (comprehensive) guarantee necessary to cover their CT exports.
Most use the haulier's guarantee, which costs a premium of several thousand pounds per annum for a typical large company.
The haulier thus provides a comprehensive service, and will recover the costs of the guarantee through his freight tariffs.
It is thought to be a good principle that the direct financial burden, and potential liability, should go with the responsibility for the care and control of the goods during transit.
Scotch Whisky exports were not affected by the recent withdrawal of the comprehensive guarantee facility for certain sensitive products (although many members of the Association experienced the effect on their trade in other spirits imported from outwith the EU).
One company with actual experience of obtaining individual guarantees for rum shipments gives a real example of the 'nuisance value' and administrative complexity involved :
Calculate liability using a complex formula involving the value of each consignment, the industrial ECU exchange rate (liable to fluctuate ­ see note below), customs duty, excise duty and VAT Forward liability calculation to guarantor Individual guarantee certificate back to consigning warehouse Warehouse issues 8­part CT document ­ see second note below Warehouse documents plus guarantee to C & E C & E authenticate that guarantee is acceptable C & E return documents, to warehouse Despatch now possible.
The above exchanges of documents often have to be carried out by ■·· " messenger as thetimescale does not allow for the uncertainties of­the­postal service.
Meanwhile, the customer was waiting in frustration for delivery of his order!
[Note on sub-para, (a) above : It is worth adding that a failure has been reported in advising traders in sufficient Ume when amendments are made to the ECU exchange rate añer the rate for the month has been set.
This can result in new documentation having to be prepared and new single guarantees obtained from the bank, both incurring additional costs.
As explained, the introduction of individual movement guarantees or a 100% comprehensive guarantee would add to the costs of trading in particular markets.
If costs have to be passed on to customers, reduced sales and reduced viability of trade might result, with the possibility of a consequent knock­on effect on employment levels.
The Scotch Whisky industry agrees that it is paper documentation which creates so much inefficiency, and scope for abuse and fraud, in the CT system.
It therefore also agrees that automation by computerisation is the sensible way forward.
This project deserves the very highest­priority and support from the Member States.
So concerned were the Association's members about the current problems • in thè CT system that they invited an independent body, SGS (Société Générale de Surveillance SA), to make a presentation, of its proposed commercial method of computerised control within the CT system.
Pg: P.9/11 system which is used for intra­EU movements of excisable products.
The Association considers that the problems with CT, TIR and AAD movements are in reality all different facets of the same problem, and that action, particularly computerised controls, should be co­ordinated accordingly.
Prior to 30th November 1994, consignments of Scotch Whisky in road transit to countries beyond the EU could be covered by a 'High Value' (Tobacco/Alcohol) TIR Carnet with a guarantee ceiling of US $200,000.
However, because of the burgeoning incidence of theft and fraud, mainly in respect of tobacco products rather than alcohol, the 'High Value' Carnet scheme was terminated on that date.
This led to a longer lead time to meet customer requirements; and trade in some areas, particularly those for which a number of countries had to be transited en route, became unviable.
CT was used to the limit of its geographical coverage, but the arrangements to continue into affected markets included, for example, the hire of armed escorts in Hungary, Russia and former Yugoslavia at a cost of several hundred pounds per consignment.
In the Czech Republic, bonds had to be established with the relevant authorities at specific border crossings ­restricting not only whom the exporters use, but also the route of export.
To some extent, the extension of CT to the Visegrad countries alleviates the sjtuatjon caused by the non­availability of the 'High_Value!JQR_C.arnet, but there remain markets for which no transit regime is currently available.
In additionrthere appears to be some uncertainty in theScotclrWhisky industry as to whether it is in fact yet possible to use external CT for consignments travelling to or through the Visegrad countries.
The Association has therefore called for the early re­introduction of the 'High.
Valuei Carnet In its representations, it has made­the­following points, many of which apply equally to the CT system :
- Hauliers should provide a comprehensive service, and include any necessary guarantee costs in their freight tariffs.
In view of the similarities, the Association considers that there is a case for designing the proposed computer network for CT in such a way that TIR procedures can be linked in.
For intra-EU movements of Scotch Whisky, AADs (Administrative Accompanying Documents) are used to accompany and control each movement. It has been estimated that collectively, Scotch Whisky producers initiate approximately 2,000 AAD-covered movements per month.
In particular, there are delays in receiving return-copies of AADs; and fraud and diversion are increasing, partly because of the ease of falsifying evidence of receipt. Such frauds cause significant losses to Member States' excise revenues.
There appears to be an equally strong case for computerisation of AAD procedures, and for this to be linked to computerisation of CT and TIR.
A UK excise official has expressed the view that not to do this would be a 'wasted opportunity'.
There is therefore concern that, because customs issues and excise issues tend to be treated separately, no overview is being taken of the related problems in the two fields.
Scotch Whisky producers also question why they cannot take out a single guarantee to cover their potential liabilities for all these related types of irregularity : this would reduce the burdens on trie legitimate trade.
The track records of these large companies are known, their history of losses is apparent, their systems and procedures are all open to audit, and so on. For such substantial and established companies, it is feltthat a straightforward corporate guarantee/undertaking should suffice.
It has been too easy for too long for those trading illegitimately to get away -with fraud.
This appears to have been compounded by-an-attitude in several quarters of 'It's not my problem'. The simple way for the authorities has
Pg: 11 P.11/11 been to fall back on the guarantee principals, i.e. the legitimate trade, to sustain revenues.
It is legitimate traders who have been suffering from the deficiencies in the CT system.
It is totally unreasonable to expect them to subsidise maladministration and fraud, and to be exposed not only to direct financial liability for the costs and penalties of the irregularities occurring, but also indirectly to unfair competition from those who exploit the deficiencies.
There is considerable concern that certain features of the CT system are being undermined to such an extent that they are in danger of collapsing.
If, for example, consignments of spirits were to be excluded from making use of the CT system, this would be not so much a solution as an admission of failure.
The solution is that Governments, through the customs authorities, should fulfil their obligation to protect and promote legitimate trade by upholding and strengthening the CT system, and must meet their responsibilities to tackle fraud.
This does not need.to be done in a way which adds burdens and costs to bona fide businesses. Measures and resources should be targeted and proportionate, based upon an assessment of risk.
This period of uncertainty about the workings of the CT system must come to an end.
Businesses require clear, timely explanations of what complex procedural and regulatory changes mear for them in practice.
More could be done centrally to disseminate information on current problems, trends etc, with simple advice on the practical implications.
The Scotch Whisky Association, having consulted its members' views, very much hopes that the above points will provide ä useful contribution to the debate.
SWA 5th November 1996
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Please find attached a contribution to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by Dr S. ARENDT, expert in legal cases relating to transit, Bildungszentrum des Bundesfinanzministeriums, Münster.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
This law is therefore in line with the objectives of the planned European Community agreement on the protection of its financial interests in that it defines what constitutes.
The range of penalties available to the judge are fines, up to DM 3.6 m., and imprisonment for up to five years. Sentencing is carried out by the judge alone, but the tax authority can exert a powerful influence since, in its application for the issue of a summary sentence (judgment without an oral hearing), it must specify a particular penalty.
In the event of regular professional misconduct, the penalty ranges from three months to five years' imprisonment (Section 373 of the Tax Law).
DOC_EN\CM\314\314277
The above crimes can be punished only if it can be proved that the perpetrator acted deliberately, i.e. that he committed an offence in full knowledge and intentionally.
The fact that a crime was committed does not in itself imply that it was deliberate; intention must be proved by the court.
With regard to the transit procedure, if there is evidence that tax laws have been violated, but not that the crime was deliberate, the tax authorities can punish the offence as an administrative offence and/or an offence against public order.
For example, if the perpetrator behaved negligently, i.e. did not exercise the care which it was his duty to exercise, in the light of the particular circumstances of the case and his personal abilities and knowledge, although he should have been aware that this would lead to tax evasion, the tax authorities, through the administrative authorities, may impose a fine of up to DM 100 000.
Fines of up to DM 10 000 may also be imposed for infringements of various customs regulations.
If a case involves both a criminal act and an administrative offence, criminal law takes precedence, i.e. the court alone decides the penalty Therefore the cases cited by the German authorities are almost exclusively criminal offence; exceptionally the court will punish the offence against public order as well as the criminal offence, if there is a connection.
The conclusion to be drawn from the above is that German criminal law has sufficient provisions to enable the courts to impose appropriate penalties in cases of infringements of the transit procedure.
