DIRECTORATE GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
To the European Communities
John Tomlinson Esq MEP Chairman Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System European Parliament RueBelliard97-113 B-1047 Brussels
I am enclosing a note prepared by Customs which deals in some detail with the problems encountered during this case.
CUSTOMS AND EXCISE ν GfflSELLI AND OTHERS
The background
This case involved the fraudulent diversion of 7 lorry loads of cigarettes (5 into Italy and 2 into Spain) by taking them out of bonds in the UK and Switzerland using TIR Carnets which asserted that the goods were in transit to a destination outside the EC.
The TIR Carnets were obtained in the UK from Freight Transport Association (FTA).
The loss to the Italian and Spanish revenue from the consequent likely sale there on the black-market is estimated to be £8 million sterling equivalent.
This is a brief summary of the problems encountered and how they might be avoided in future.
Count 3 on the Indictment (see Annex) was quashed at the outset by the trial judge as he ruled that the investigation of cigarette smuggling in Italy was noi an assigned matter as defined in section 6 of the Customs and Excise Management Act 1979 0 assigned matters are duties lard to Customs by the law).
In retrospect a better argument by the prosecution would have been that the integrity of the i IK Carnet System was an assigned matter and putting false consignment details on a TIR Carnet in the UK was an offence.
A count of conspiracy to defraud by putting the FTA at financial risk on their guarantee would have been a better or alternative count.
The Section 167(1) CEMA count was on the Indictment because we could not prove that cigarette smuggling was "a serious offence" in Italy in 1993-1994 (we were unclear about the Italian law at that time).
Counts 2 and 3
These counts related to 2 separate lorry loads of cigarettes being transported to Spain and diverted there.
The trial judge excluded the certificate of Spanish law from evidence under Section 78 PACE as it was not clear.
He refused to allow further evidence to be called to clarify or to rectify any omissions.
On first reading the certificate appeared to "make sense".
The letter requesting the certificate specifies what the certificate needs to prove.
At trial a number of objections to it were raised.
These are detailed below with the assessment by Customs lawyers of their significance and the means, where necessary, to avoid them in future cases.
it cites two possible criminal offences one under the Penal Code - general tax evasion ie "cheating the Revenue", the other under the Anti-Smuggling Law - more specific behaviour.
Admitted.
However, the behaviour would be an offence under both just as you could be charged under Section 170 CEMA or cheating the Revenue.
We would only use anti-smuggling law in future.
it cites an offence of obtaining a repayment or relief from taxation Article 350 of the Penal Code which is irrelevant.
Admitted.
This is irrelevant rather than misleading in any way.
We would ensure that we received the text of each statute.
it mentions civil penalties.
Admitted.
This surely clarifies what behaviour is criminal smuggling and what is merely "an administrative infringement".
We do not believe thai a certificate would fail on these grounds alone.
the Penal Code was shortly to be repealed in May 1996 and another Penal Code enacted.
Irrelevant in view of Section 7 l(3)(b) CJA 1993 which states that the law to be applied is the law in force at the time of the offence.
Will not be a factor in future cases.
the Anti-Smuggling law of 1982 was repealed in December 1995
Irrelevant in view of Section 71(3)(b) CJA 1993.
Will not be a factor in future cases.
The minimum penalty of 12 months.
The offence committed in Spain must be a serious offence.
It must be punishable by "a maximum of 12 months or more" Section 71 (2) CJA 1993.
The drafting confused the trial judge when faced with the fact that a person convicted of smuggling in Spain must receive a prison sentence of 6 months imprisonment ('prison menor') but could receive up to 6 years.
Mandatory sentencing is rare in England and Wales except for murder convictions and drink-driving.
Unfortunately the translation, although footnoted, did not do justice to the concept of'prison menor' and we did not choose to provide further clarification.
Clearly we should do so in any future case.
Other Evidence Excluded
The trial judge had previously excluded French motorway toll receipts found on a driver as evidence, as there was no certificate stating that the apparently computerised machine was working properly.
Section 69 and Schedule 3 to PACE 1984 demand this.
The receipts "proved" a vehicle's passage through France to the Spanish border.
Lessons
THE QUEEN V FABIO GfflSELLI, FABIO MARCUS GHISELLI, GARY RICHARDSON, KENNETH RICHARD STEVENS.
STUART ALLAN MacTAGGART, MARK ANTHONY BENNETT and PETER ALAN LISSENBURGH.
FABIO GHISELLI, FABIO MARCUS GHISELLI, GARY RICHARDSON, KENNETH RICHARD STEVENS, STUART ALLAN MacTAGGART, MARK ANTHONY BENNETT and PETER ALAN LISSENBURGH are charged as follows:
Statement of Offence
Assisting in or inducing conduct outside the Untied Kingdom which involves the commission of a serious offence against the law of another member state, contrary to section 71 of the Criminal Justice Act 1993.
Particulars of Offence
FABIO GHISELLI, FABIO MARCUS GHISELLI, GARY RICHARDSON, KENNETH RICHARD STEVENS, STUART ALLAN MacTAGGART, MARK ANTHONY BENNETT and PETER ALAN LISSENBURGH between the 26th day of September 1993 and the 10th day of May 1994, by: a) arranging for use of bonded warehouse facilities in Bristol, b) arranging for delivery of 1180 cartons of duty-free cigarettes to that Bond, c) arranging for the sending of those 1180 cartons of cigarettes, in bond, from Bristol, bound for Morocco, d) arranging and providing transport for those cigarettes, in bond, from Bristol to Dover and onto a ferry for France, e) obtaining and providing travel documents, namely an Invoice 9403/093, Carnet 10084584, C88 02/052 and CMR for those cigarettes, f) obtaining a false stamp and stamping Souche No 2 page 2 on Carnet 10084584, showing arrival of the lorry and cigarettes at Algeciras on 22/2/94, g) returning the falsely stamped Carnet 10084584 to the FTA on 31/3/94, assisted in or induced conduct, namely the importation of dutiable goods (1155 cartons of duty-free cigarettes, valued in excess of 1,000,000 pesetas) into Spain without paying tax and their disposal in Spain without paying tax, contrary to the provisions of Spanish Law, namely Articles 1.1, 12, 349 & 350 of the Spanish Penal Code. 305
Conspiracy to contravene Section 167(1) of the Customs and Excise Management Act 1979, contrary to section 1 of the Criminal Law Act 1977.
Particulars of Offence
FABIO GHISELLI, FABIO MARCUS GHISELLI, GARY RICHARDSON, KENNETH RICHARD STEVENS, STUART ALLAN MacTAGGART, MARK ANTHONY BENNETT and PETER ALAN LISSENBURGH between the 1st day of October 1993 and the 10th day of March 1994, together and with other persons unknown, knowingly or recklessly, conspired to cause to be made documents, made for the purpose of assigned matters, which were untrue in a material particular, namely Carnets with false delivery particulars, invoices with false purchaser details, C88/Travel documents with false delivery details and false customs stamps.
Members will find enclosed a contribution to the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Confederation of European Community Cigarette Manufacturers Limited, Brussels.
THE CONFEDERATION OF EUROPEAN COMMUNITY CIGARETTE MANUFACTURERS LIMITED.
Statement to the European Parliament Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System
The reason for CECCM's request to represent its members before the Committee is that it is the very task of CECCM to do so, it being the focal point as well in the contacts with the Commission on the issue.
The problems regarding fraud associated with the transit of cigarettes have been discussed for a number of years.
There can be no doubt that the root of the problem lies in the very high taxes our products have to bear: three out of every four ECU's of the retail price are tax, which shows clearly the potential profit for those that find ways to avoid paying tax.
For practical reasons it was decided that, while fraud in transit issues clearly has a Community dimension, the fight against smuggling has to be dealt with at national level by the national manufacturers' associations in close cooperation with the authorities of the Member States.
CECCM, itself, is involved in the discussions regarding the Community Transit System.
Because of the consequences for our industry of the suspension of the comprehensive community guarantee for cigarettes, we have been in contact with the European Commission Commissioner Monti wrote to us saying that the suspension of the comprehensive guarantee for cigarettes, since February of this year, represents "measures against fraud only insofar as they limit fraudulent activities based on the use of forwarding companies which disappear after having initiated several transit operations concerning sensitive products".
Meanwhile the cost to industry of financing the present "temporary" system of single guarantees has increased to between twenty and thirty times the cost of the equivalent comprehensive guarantee arrangements, not to mention the cumbersome bureaucratic procedures that have to be completed.
In spite of this burden the industry was not consulted when the suspension measure was taken.
We are still not fully aware of the concerns that led to the measure and to what extent these could be met by the suspension of the comprehensive community guarantee, other than "to protect the financial interests of the Member States and the Community", as Commissioner Monti stated Even on the matter of the prolongation of the suspension until February next year, the industry was not consulted.
Unlike the situation with other products, the industry bears the full risk of the transit of cigarettes.
When irregularities occur, or shipments get stolen, the industry pays the full amount, or sometimes much more as is shown in the case of a stolen truckload of cigarettes in Germany, destined for Denmark Both Member States claim the full amount of taxes, Denmark because Danish banderoles were attached and Germany because the cigarettes were presumed to have been consumed there. .
A solution to avoid this double taxation has not yet been found
The following example shows how valuable such a truckload is because of the high tax involved: inq
A truckload containing 10 million cigarettes manufactured in Germany with a total invoice value of DM 200,000 has to be covered by a guarantee of DM 1,698,700, representing excise taxes, duties and VAT due on the cigarettes when being shipped under Tl since the suspension of the comprehensive guarantee effected on 1 February 1996.
Not astonishingly, such high value can attract the attention of criminals, if they can steal the truckload at the expense of the manufacturer or the freight for warder, who generally stands as a guarantor.
This value and the low penalties involved (and often the lack of enthusiasm by the authorities to prosecute because tax payment is ensured by the industry) make criminal involvement highly probable and even more profitable and less risky than drugs!
This phenomenon has encouraged the industry to take measures to protect and control their own shipments and with success: thefts only rarely occur.
On the other hand, the industry cannot be made responsible for possible irregularities occurring after the sale of their products.
It is in the same position as the manufacturers of all other products.
The close cooperation with the local authorities controlling the industry's own shipments shows that no problems occur at this stage.
It is clear that the cigarette industry has become the victim of inadequacies of the transit system and poor law enforcement.
It bears the risks but cannot control the whole process, although it does more than anyone to protect its products.
The absence, in most cases, ofinformation or feedback from the competent authorities does not improve the situation.
We are, of course, more than willing to cooperate in finding more adequate ways of controlling the transit system.
Any solution should result in a system which minimises the current excessive costs of operating single guarantees.
Together with the Commission (DG XXI) we will look for solutions.
A first workshop has already been planned.
CECCM is open to all ideas that can solve the current problems without creating others!
