EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to members
Please find attached a contribution to the European Parliament's Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System submitted by the Swiss authorities.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Document explainine the functioning and status of free warehouses in Switzerland
Storage in a bonded warehouse allows for the postponement of final customs clearance for goods imported from abroad.
It properly follows initial customs clearance under transit arrangements, clearance which is essential if the goods are to be bought from the customs border to the customs warehouse.
The storage of goods in bonded warehouses allows for the initial interim customs charges to be paid.
A fresh customs procedure must be started if the goods are to be released.
This fresh customs clearance (customs procedure) may be final (customs clearance for import, possibly under duty-free arrangements) or interim (provisional customs clearance for import, customs clearance under transit or carnet arrangements).
Since the previous customs charges have been paid prior to the goods entering the warehouse, a fresh customs procedure must be started when they are removed.
Under Swiss customs law, customs warehouse traffic means storage in warehouses in free districts (in particular free ports), federal warehouses and open customs warehouses.
In contrast, the EU Customs Code draws a clear distinction between customs warehouses (Articles 95-110), on the one hand, and free zones and warehouses (Articles 164-179), on the other.
The federal warehouse is the oldest form of storage for goods which have not received customs clearance.
The stored goods are under customs surveillance, i.e. the customs office checks goods entering and leaving the warehouse and that the warehouse is sealed for customs purposes.
Handling operations are authorized only where they are essential for the conservation of the goods.
In customs terms, the goods are deemed to be on Swiss customs territory.
Goods may be stored for no more than five years, a period which may be extended for a further five years.
The introduction of new customs procedures in connection with processing traffic and the transformation into customs duties, as part of the transposition of the GATT/WTO rules, of the protectionist measures applied at the border until the end of June 1995 necessitated the establishment of customs warehouses on firms' own premises.
Importers, forwarding agents, haulage companies and firms which process goods on their own premises may store highly taxed goods governed by quotas for unlimited periods and on a duty-free basis.
This is the purpose for which the 'open' customs warehouses were set up They are deemed to be Swiss customs territory; however, it is the calculation bases, trade policy stipulations and the rates of duty in force when the goods leave the warehouse which are decisive.
In the case of goods brought to the warehouse under the transit procedure, an inventory must be drawn up, generally using electronic information processing, of the goods concerned following their release by the competent customs office.
All the information required for the proper implementation of the procedure must appear on that inventory.
By way of a security, the warehouser must lodge a guarantee, the amount of which depends on the nature of the goods to be stored.
In general, goods which have not cleared customs must be stored separately from Swiss goods and goods which have received customs clearance.
'Mixed' storage may be authorized in warehouses equipped with state-of-the-art technical equipment, e.g. electronically controlled verticil storage During unlimited storage, all handling operations designed to conserve the goods mav be carried out In addition, the goods may be examined,
sampled. The Directorate-General for Customs may exclude certain types of goods from storage in open customs warehouses.
At present, cigarettes may not be stored in customs warehouses.
Open customs warehouses comply to a substantial extent with the standards laid down for 'private warehouses' (type D) as defined in Article 99 of the EU Customs Code.
The free ports are used in international intermediate trade and for the storage of goods whose final destination on Swiss territory is not yet known or in respect of which customs clearance must be postponed.
They are regarded as foreign customs territory.
Goods may be stored for unlimited periods.
The warehoused goods are not under the individual supervision of the customs administration.
Customs law does not stipulate than an inventory must be kept of goods entering and leaving the free port.
Goods stored in free ports may be unpacked and repacked, and may also be examined, analysed, divided up, sorted, mixed and sampled.
By contrast, working or processing which changes the nature of or the customs tariff applicable to the goods may be carried out only with the explicit authorization of the Directorate-General for Customs.
Certain sensitive goods subject to special licensing arrangements may be stored only if the appropriate authorization has been granted.
The warehouses and free ports are operated by railway undertakings or private warehousing firms.
Operating licenses are always granted subject to the condition that the warehouse should be public, i.e. available to all interested parties on the same terms.
The warehouse administration itself must not carry out commercial transactions involving the stored goods.
The Swiss free ports are not fundamentally different from the free zones and free warehouses as defined in Articles 166 et seq. of the EU Customs Code.
The owner of the stored goods may sell them without informing the customs administration.
Relations between the warehouse administration and users are governed exclusively by private law (warehousing contract pursuant to Articles 482 et seq. of the Code of Obligations).
The customs surveillance carried out in federal warehouses and free ports is designed solely to prevent unauthorized handling and the removal of goods without customs processing.
As regards the storage of Community goods in the three types of customs warehouse, additional conditions (separate storage, limited storage period, requirements regarding the handling operations authorized in respect of the packaging and goods) must be met so that the Community nature of the goods (what is termed its T2 status) can be reported when the goods are re-exported into the European Union.
EUROPEAN
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice from Members No. 11 lembers will find enclosed a notice from Mr John Tomlinson, Mr Edward Kellett-îowman, Mr Jan Mulder, Mr Bartho Pronk and Mrs Maj Britt Theorin on the lelegation visit to Geneva and Bern on 1 and 2 July 1996.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Monday, 1 July and Tuesday, 2 July 1996 **********************
Composition of the delegation
Mr Edward KELLETT-BOWMAN, Rapporteur Mr Jan MULDER Mr Bartho PRONK Mrs Maj Britt THEORIN
The delegation was accompanied by Michael Shackleton, Head of the Secretariat.
Outline of the visit
The visit was composed of three parts.
The delegation spent the whole of Monday, 1 July at the headquarters of the International Road Transport Union (IRU).
IRU had come to the committee on 21 March to explain the operation of the TIR carnet system, which it has the task of operating.
This visit enabled IRU to discuss at greater length why the system has been in difficulty, what it is doing to reform it and what the obstacles to reform are.
The members expressed their particular thanks to Mr Martin MARMY, Secretary General of IRU, for his assistance in organising the visit.
On the. morning of the Tuesday the delegation was invited to the headquarters of the Société de Surveillance (SGS).
This company has been involved since 1878 in the monitoring of the quality of goods and employs 35,000 people in over 140 countries.
On the basis of this reputation it was invited by IRU in the autumn of 1995 to introduce a system designed to improve the security of the TIR system.
IRU was invited by national customs authorities to abrogate the agreement with SGS in October 1995.
Representatives of SGS had explained to the Chairman and the rapporteur the nature of the system they had devised at an informal meeting held in March in Brussels.
This meeting provided an opportunity to discuss the system in more detail and to consider what further initiatives are being considered in the private sector.
Both IRU and SGS provided substantial background material which is available in the Secretariat for those who wish to consult it.
After this visit the delegation travelled by train to Bern where it met members of the Swiss Customs administration, including Mr Rudloph DIETRICH, Director of Swiss Customs.
The Swiss mission in Brussels has been following the work of the Committee of Inquiry very closely and indicated that the Swiss administration would be willing to discuss with the delegation the difficulties for a country involved in the common but not the Community transit system, the particular problems involved in the transport of cigarettes and the attitude of the Swiss on the prospects for reform of the transit system.
On this last point the Swiss authorities provided a critical written evaluation of the computerisation project of the Commission which is attached to this note.
Themes of the visit
The visit gave members the opportunity to expand their knowledge on a number of issues that have arisen in the course of the committee's work.
The following provides an overview of some the most salient of these issues.
The IRU as the central manager of the TIR system
One significant difference between the Tl and the TIR systems is that the latter is much more centralised.
The IRU is the single body with responsibility for managing the TIR system: no equivalent body exists for the Tl system.
The IRU: issues carnets to national associations who in turn issue them to their members: since 1949 it has issued 30 million carnets, at around 400 to 500,000 per year before 1991/2, now around 2.3 million; only issues new carnets to an association once 60% of the previous carnets have cleared.
controls the system of guarantees that all those entering the system are obliged to take out, deciding itself to exclude alcohol and tobacco from the coverage of the system in the face of strong opposition from customs authorities; can and does exclude companies that fail to follow the rules of the system: over 1000 operators have been excluded in recent years; had the job of finding alternative insurance cover when the original insurers broke their contract at the end of 1994.
Within the limits of its responsibilities the IRU also enjoys a considerable measure of autonomy.
As a non-govemmental organisation it is not dependent on governments for its financing but rather has to persuade its members that the finance they provide should be increased or used for specific purposes.
Thus it has succeeded in varying the level of the cost of a carnet, recently raising it from 40 to 60 Swiss francs, to take account notably of the higher costs of insurance.
It has increased its expenditure to meet specific problems: in the last 12 to 15 months it has spent 90 million Swiss francs with a view to meeting the specific problems that the TIR system is facing.
It has also increased the number of staff to take account of the major increase in the volume of outstanding claims, now standing at 5000 as compared with 300 in the past: those working in the TIR section are now 51 as compared with 8, six years ago.
The role and function of Working Party 30 of the United Nations
WP 30 is a committee of the United Nations composed of representatives of the customs services which meets two or three times a year to discuss inter alia the operation of the TIR system.
It was described by one speaker as a "self-contained Empire with a life of its own".
There was a general view expressed that it is essentially an inward looking body whose members receive only a minimal mandate, if any at all, from political authorities in the Member States.
It has tended to react rather than take initiatives in the face of the opening of markets.
IRU pointed to three particular aspects of WP 30 which it felt were not satisfactory: that the committee works on the classical intergovernmental system of consensus where the outcomes of the discussions are either limited to recommendations or resolutions geared to the lowest common denominator.
"In order to facilitate the detection of fraudulently discharged TIR Carnets and to accelerate the discharge procedure, Contracting Parties may wish to create, as far as possible and in line with national requirements, centralised offices or procedures for the administration of TIR Carnets"; that the participants have become more junior in rank: originally the Directors General of Customs came, but over time there has been a tendency for less senior officials to be sent from national capitals and from the Commission; and that the decision-making process is dominated by a small number of members of the committee with the EU members states in particular tending to leave the talking to relatively junior officials of the Commission.
This has led to a number of specific suggestions of the IRU, such as the use of bar codes on the TIR forms, being rejected for reasons which IRU did not find convincing.
The relationship between the TIR and the Tl systems
Although the two systems are strictly separate, IRU felt that the opening of the frontiers within the Union and the increase of movements to the East should have been accompanied by more extensive regulation at Community level.
From a TIR perspective the EU became a single country already on 1 January 1992 and it was at this point that the problem of the return of documents to the point where goods entered the Community began.
As it was, only one EU regulation concerning the TIR system (719/91 of 21 March 1991) was adopted.
IRU anticipated that there would be at least a set of accompanying measures, including a list of the 7000 customs offices in the Union.
Relations between IRU and SGS
The delegation specifically avoided becoming involved in the dispute between the two parties.
It took note of the following points that were made during the visit: that the search for the services of SGS was prompted by the insistence of the new insurers found by IRU that the system be made more secure; that as a result of this pressure IRU may not have been able to consult with the customs authorities or indeed their national associations as much as would have been desirable; that SGS was able to process a very large percentage of the carnets issued (they said 90%) by the time the experiment was fully underway towards the end of 1995; that SGS was in a position to give IRU in Geneva three diskettes at 9 in the morning based on information received from 38 countries by midnight the previous day; that the ending of the contract with SGS was fundamentally a decision taken by the national customs authorities, taken within the administrative committee of WP 30;
Public versus private in the transit sector
The tension between the public and private sectors in the transit sector became clearer during the visit.
On the one hand, there are customs services with longer traditions than virtually all other departments of state and very firm views about how things should be done.
Two public sector solutions were discussed, both far from easy to implement.
One could envisage a centralised system such as that which exists in the United States, where duties are normally collected at the point of entry.
This implies a single customs authority and a single fiscal authority, both of which are some way from being achieved in the Union.
Alternatively, there can be extended customs cooperation with all countries willing to devote much greater resources to this aim than they do at present.
The experience of IRU so far in its attempts to get a closer grip on the TIR Carnet system suggests that much remains to be done in this domain.
On the other hand, there is a private sector which is interested in ensuring maximum security for goods in transit and does not find the existing system satisfactory.
SGS showed that they could obtain information on the ground very quickly, establishing for example how and in what form TIR carnets can be forged.
The delegation was shown examples of exceptionally good forgeries and told how much they cost to obtain.
In this context, SGS pointed to the possible development of a new system designed to permit it to put to good use the 2.5 million Swiss Francs that it invested in the IRU experiment.
It pointed to the idea of a private "Club" of operators who would set up their own system geared to their own needs which would provide for clearance of goods parallel to that provided by customs but executed more rapidly.
Operators would be admitted to the Club on the basis of the volume of goods that they transport and would be expected to meet certain stringent entry requirements, such as obligatory in-cab communications.
Such a system would pose severe difficulties for small operators unless they went into a cooperative arrangement with others.
It would also challenge the role of the IRU and the operation of the TIR system.
Community and common transit
The Swiss authorities made plain that from their point of view the advantages of having a veto under the common transit system are more apparent than real.
It is true that they did not have to accept the proposal to eliminate global guarantees for sensitive goods from 1 April.
However, Swiss transporters were faced with the possibility that they would be obliged to produce an individual guarantee as soon as they crossed the border into the Union.
Hence all the EFTA states were faced with a difficult choice, which left them with little room for negotiation.
The leverage to obtain consent is, in their view, on the side of the Union.
In the end, the EFTAns accepted to incorporate the Community rules into the common system in a deal that was completed in the week before the common transit system was extended to the Visegrad states.
The Swiss felt similarly unable to make their concerns heard in relation to computerisation.
As the attached document makes clear, they are highly critical of the proposed project of the Commission.
They have consistently argued for a much smaller scale project, concentrating on the return of copy 5 of the Tl form.
As for the deadline of 1998, in their view, it is totally unfeasible, given the complexity of the project.
Cigarettes and their passage through Switzerland
This issue prompted rather different responses.
At IRU it was argued that there were 400 to 500 claims outstanding concerning shipments of cigarettes that started in Switzerland.
It was noted that cigarette production had increased by 30% even though consumption was stagnant.
The level of cooperation between Swiss and EU authorities was not seen as ideal.
The Swiss authorities took a very different view.
They recognised that the nature of the privacy laws in Switzerland imposes certain constraints on the level of investigation that they can undertake.
However, they have had no prosecutions on cigarette shipments within the country.
In their view the difficulties begin inside the EU rather than in the free zones in Switzerland through which many shipments pass.
They argued that they were very cooperative with other countries.
They operate an early warning system for cigarettes, indicating to all countries through which a shipment is due to pass what is going to arrive and giving the same information to UCLAF in Brussels, information that was not in their knowledge provided by all Member States of the Union.
Since October 1993 they have announced 3000 such movements of which half are going to Belgium and the Netherlands.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members No 38
Visit by a delegation from the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System to Vienna, Nickelsdorf and Berg, on 9 and 10 September 1996
Please find attached a summary record of the above visit.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
17 September 1996
DOC_EN\CM\308\308210
The following Members took part in the delegation's visit to Vienna, Nickelsdorf and Berg:
Mr Stefano De Luca (vice-chairman); Mr Edward Kellett-Bowman (rapporteur); Mrs Eva Kjer-Hansen, Mr Marcel Schlechter, Mr Otto Bardong, and Mr Roy James Perry.
The delegation was accompanied by the Austrian Members Mr Herbert Bosch and Mr Friedrich König.
9 September 1996
The delegation was welcomed at 8.30 a.m. by Head of the Customs Service in the Finance Ministry, Dr Fuchs, and by Dr Heller.
Mr Kellett-Bowman expressed the committee's thanks for the invitation.
The vice-chairman, Mr De Luca, took the chair at approximately 8.4 5 a.m.
Dr Fuchs outlined the intended programme and answered questions by Mr Kellett-Bowman, Mr Perry, Mr König and Mr Bosch
Mr Fuchs pointed out that it was not possible at present to provide exact figures for the costs of computerizing the transit procedure in Austria, since the computerization and networking of customs administration was not confined to the transit system alone.
In his opinion the transit system was not being deliberately targeted for the purpose of committing fraud.
The distinction had to be made between fiscal and economic losses.
A relatively minor loss of customs and tax revenue, for example, could result in significant consequential economic losses if operators who played by the rules went out business by being put at an unfair price disadvantage.
The extension of the transit syste4m to include the Visegrad States as at 1 July 1996 had as yet been slow to make an impact.
That was probably because the Hungarian and Slovak banks still had relatively little experience of issuing guarantees.
In addition, border clearance operators were concerned not to lose any share of business.
Despite putting extra staff on border duty, stepping up physical checks and modernizing technical equipment, it had still not been possible for fraud to be stamped out completely.
As a first step towards reducing the incidence of fraud the deadline for returning copy No 5 of the T1 transit document should be shortened.
The best solution would be to replace postal return notification by electronic notification .
In conclusions, Dr Fuchs stated that it was still too soon to offer any assessment of the extension of the transit procedure to the Visegrad States since it had only be applicable in those states for two months.
At 10.30 a.m. the delegation arrived at the Nickelsdorf customs post on the Austro-Hungarian border.
It was welcomed by Mr Deimel, head of the customs office.
Mr Deimel produced statistics showing the increase in border traffic between 1994 and 1996.
DOC EN\CM\308\308210
Mr Deimel went on to point out that since the opening of the border to the East, offences had increased by 300%.
Most of these were however accounted for by smuggling in private cars.
Although Nickelsdorf was the biggest border crossing point, a good many carriers were prepared to make detours round it.
In reply to questions by Mrs Kjer-Hansen, Mr Bardong, Mr De Luca, Mr Kellett Bowman, Mr Perry, Mr König and Mr Bosch, the following information was provided:
There existed between Austria and Hungary a bilateral agreement under which the each country notified consignments of sensitive goods to the other.
With the introduction of individual guaranty for sensitive goods the transit system had become more secure, although conventional smuggling was on the increase.
If physical checks were to be effective, more staff would be required.
He also considered it urgently necessary for the procedure for returning copy No 5 of the T1 transit document to be speeded up.
The TIR carnet procedure was more secure in that failure to present consignments at the reception point meant that no new TIR carnet would be issued.
Since the extension of the transit procedure to the Visegrad States he had not detected any significant changes.
That was, in his opinion, because it was difficult for Hungarian import-export agents to secure adequate bank guarantees.
The ratio of TIR carnet to T1 document was 20 to 1 .
Questions were then put to the representatives of import-export agents and customs officials present.
The latter took the view that it was wrong for fraud in the transit system to be at the expense of import-export agents.
There were reliability problems in operating with agents from the Visegrad states since the risk of non-presentation of consignments at the reception point was higher.
In the agents' view, only qualified specialist firms with a solid capital base should operate under the transit system.
Some agents did not draw up transit documents until they had completed the transport operation from beginning to end, since that meant taking a calculable risk.
At approximately 11.15 a.m. the delegation met the Deputy Director-General of the Hungarian Customs Service, Dr Karoly Soos.
Dr Soos stated that the transit system had been in place for two months and was functionaing.
The preparations for the transit system had been effective. At present, there was a preference for using the TIR carnet system for exports from Eastern Europe.
But the use of the T1 system could be expected to increase in the future.
DOC EN\CM\308\308210
T1 No 5 copies were being returned on time.
Of the 3 612 transit procedures opened since 1 July 1996, 2 826 had been concluded.
In some 800 instances the forms had still not been returned.
The inspection of the Nickelsdorf customs post was concluded at approximately 12 15 p.m.
At approximately 2 55 p.m. the delegation arrived at the customs post at Berg on the Austrian-Slovakian border, where it was welcomed by the head of the customs office, Mr Drescher. Mr Drescher, stated that the representative of the Slovakian customs administration, Mr Ljubomir Martincak, was unable to take part in the talks owing to sudden illness.
Mr Drescher pointed out that little had changed since 1 July 1996.
It was still the case that most transit procedures started and finished at the Berg customs post and not in Slovakia.
Only two T1 procedures per day finished somewhere other than in Berg.
As with Hungary, there was also a bilateral agreement with Slovakia under which consignments with sensitive goods were notified by each country to the other.
In reply to questions by Mr Kellett-Bowman, Mr Schlechter, Mr Bardong, Mr König, Mr De Luca, Mr Perry and Mrs Kjer-Hansen, the following information was provided by Mr Drescher and the import-export agents present:
The salary of a Slovakian customs official was between 2 000 and 2 500 Austrian schilling per month. That of an Austrian counterpart was about 20 000 to 25 000 schilling.
Whether or not this earnings discrepancy created a susceptibility to corruption he was unable ~o judge.
More effective checks would only be possible with extra staff and computerization of the system.
With the ending of border controls with Italy and Germany, staff had of course been released. It was however difficult for social reasons to relocate staff from Austria's western to its eastern borders.
The import-export agents stated that the extension of the transit system to the Visegrad States had to all intents and purposes still not happened.
Subsequent to the discussion, the processing of private motor vehicles in the border customs post was demonstrated to Members.
The customs post inspection was concluded at 4.45 p.m.
10 September 1996
The delegation was received at 8.30 a.m. by the Austrian Finance Minister, Mr Victor Klima.
The minister began by outlining the main components of Austrian finance policy in terms of complying with the criteria for economic and monetary union.
Just as savings had to be made at national level, it was equally important that they be secured at the level of the European Union.
The committee could contribute by helping to stamp out loss of revenue due to fraud.
DOC_EN\CM\308\308210
progressive relocation of staff from Austria's old internal borders to its external borders, with due regard to the attendant social problems
Mr Klima also pointed out that petty smuggling of cigarettes and alcohol was a more serious problem than transit system fraud.
Smuggling was responsible for an annual shortfall in Austria of 1 billion schilling in sales of cigarettes and alcoholic beverages.
At 9.45 a.m. Mr De Luca opened the meeting with representatives of the Austrian Chamber of Commerce and import-export agents.
The Chamber's director for customs legislation, Dr Heher, said that under the composite guarantee scheme there had been no instances of losses since 1973.
The extension of the transit system to the Visegrad States had, in practice, not really started, since the necessary implementing provisions did not yet exist.
In that connection, one of the export-import agents present stated that he had been in Hungary on business on 1 July 1996 and had established that the agent he was visiting had no knowledge of the extension of the transit system.
In reply to questions by Mr Kellett-Bowman, Mr Bosch, Mrs Kjer-Hansen and Mr Perry, the representatives of the import-export agents present stated that some fraud had been perpetrated by or through corrupt customs officials.
In this case, it had been impossible to lodge a claim against the principal party liable.
Dr Fuchs took issue with that view, and pointed out that where there was bribery, at least two persons always had to be responsible.
The agents also indicated that they tended to minimize the risk of fraud by dealing only with serious business partners.
Problems only arose if they acted at the borders as customs agents pure and simple and issued transit documents for third-party carriers.
The best arrangement was for consignments to be taken in hand from start to finish by a single agent.
Fixed transit routes and satellite monitoring were not the answer, since they would restrict carriers' freedom to operate.
Free movement of goods and customs checks had to be maintained in a healthy relationship with each other.
They specifically proposed that every border crossing should be recorded on the T1 document and the deadlines for returning copy No 5 of the T1 document should be shortened.
Mr De Luca thanked the representatives of the Austrian Chamber of Commerce and declared the meeting closed at approximately 11.00 a.m.
DOC_EN\CM\308\308210
EUROPEAN
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to Members no. 61
Subject:
Visit by a delegation from the Committee of Inquiry into the Community Transit System to Rome and Naples on 26 and 27 September 1996.
Please find attached a summary record of the above visit.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
3 December 1996
Composition of delegation
The following members made up the delegation:
Mr John Tomlinson (Chairman)
The delegation was accompanied by the Italian members Mr Stefano De Luca, vicechairman and Mr Gianni Tamino.
Programme of Visit
The visit took place on 26 and 27 September 1996 in Rome and Naples according to the following programme.
Thursday 26 September 1996
Visit to the Rome II customs district (Leonardo da Vinci Airport, Fiumicino).
Working lunch hosted by customs service.
Meeting at the Ministry of Finance, with Mr Giovanni Marongiu, Minister of State and Mr Renato Spetrino, General Director of Customs.
Visit to the national headquarters of the Guardia di Finanza. Meeting with General Niccolò Pollari, Capo di Stato Maggiore.
Friday 27 September 1996
Visit to port of Naples.
Meetings with Heads of Naples regional and district customs services, port authorities and port users.
Visit to the Naples headquarters of the Guardia di Finanza.
Presentation
Rather than taking a chronological approach to the various meetings undertaken by the delegation, this report adopts an issue-based approach.
The following sections therefore constitute broad themes which repeatedly arose during the visit.
The Guardia di Finanza is also under the direct responsibility of the Ministry of Finance, but is a military corps with a general responsibility for the protection of the financial interests of the state and therefore provides a service complementary to that of customs.
Its activities include the investigation of financial crime and tax evasion, the enforcement of fiscal legislation and the control of borders.
It has wide-ranging powers to carry out on-the-spot inspections, stop and check vehicles, make arrests and so on.
As part of these functions, it maintains a presence in ports, at border crossings and airports alongside the customs service.
The two services work in close cooperation within the bounds of their respective powers and competences.
Customs "Culture"
One of the most interesting points to emerge from the point of view of the delegation was the customs "culture" it encountered.
During previous visits in which members of the Committee had participated, customs had often expressed a keen awareness of their role in ensuring the smooth flow of trade, and consequently gave high priority to minimising the obstacles placed in the way of the movement of goods.
While aware of such interests, the delegation's interlocutors in both Rome and Naples stressed that their priority, within the limits of available resources, was in the prevention of fraud, notwithstanding a clear consciousness that this more "repressive" approach unquestionably created a competitive disadvantage for Italian businesses.
This issue arose in all the delegation's meetings with the customs service.
In Fiumicino airport, it was indicated that the customs were aware that strictness in controls had a negative impact on Alitalia's competitive position, with the clear suggestion being made that Northern European airports with less stringent regimes benefitted as a result.
In the meeting with Minister Marongiu, it was the minister who suggested that it was in the face of pressure from the tourism lobby that the Ministry continued to insist on a high level of controls along Italy's coastline.
Finally, the delegation heard a plea from the representative of the association of port operators in Naples that customs procedures be harmonised throughout the EU, as there was a widespread feeling that Italian customs were stricter than their counterparts elsewhere in the Union.
The general comments heard by the delegation on this theme were backed up by some of the details quoted.
Almost uniquely in Europe, for example, the Italian customs service has not seen its total personnel reduced since the introduction of the single market, with figures stable slightly over 10,000 officials (in addition to which the Guardia di Finanza can provide customs-related assistance).
On the question of physical checks, in Fiumicino airport, all incoming goods were checked on a documentary basis, with physical checks reaching 6-7% on "sensitive" goods, and averaging 5% in total.
Of these, between 7-10% were further subject to "anti-fraud" checks (carried out by a specialised department) and as many as 5% were said to lead to legal proceedings.
In Naples, even greater rigour was described: 15% of goods were checked, with all declared sensitive goods being subject to physical checks, backed up by, on the one hand, completely random checks of other goods and, on the other, targeted checks arising from risk analysis.
In general, 1% of goods checked proved fraudulent in one way or another.
It should be noted that, owing to an oft-cited lack of financial resources, all this activity was largely done without the benefit of special technological equipment.
The main lesson drawn by the delegation's interlocutors in this area was that customs procedures should be harmonised in order to eliminate the competitive disparities created by different approaches to customs control. Furthermore,
to make the fight against contraband effective following the liberalisation associated with the Single Market, they went further, suggesting that harmonisation should be "up" to the Italian level rather than "down" to the level of some other EU Member States.
One statistic adduced in favour of this argument was that some 40-50% of drugs discovered at Fiumicino airport arrived on internal Community flights.
Experience of transit
Although the specific locations visited by the delegation are not the largest in Italy in terms of transit volumes (land borders in the north play this role), the country as a whole is one of the most concerned with transit trade, with outgoing operations peaking at nearly two million in 1994 (1.4 million in 1995) and the number of incoming operations also high.
Italian customs is therefore one of the services most involved with its management and control.
By way of example, even in Naples, which is relatively peripheral in terms of pan-European commerce, 30% of total trade through the port was in the form of transit.
Return times for copy 5 of the T1 document
It was pointed out to the delegation that the transit system was the one substantial aspect of the Italian customs service's work not to be computerised.
The delegation asked frequently about the difficulties encountered with the return of copy 5 of the T1 document.
While it was generally said that the Italian authorities managed to return copy 5 within a maximum of one month (with copies leaving individual customs offices generally within 10 days and accelerated procedures for sensitive goods), there were problems with the return of documents from other Member States.
There seemed to be a consensus that most other authorities generally achieved a 50-60 day deadline but, on more than one occasion, it was said that other Member States, in particular Greece, took a great deal longer to return documentation.
In many instances, moreover, documentation was not returned at all and had to be chased up.
In the meeting at Customs Head Office, strong support was expressed on behalf of the Italian authorities for the Commission's proposed computerisation system as a way of resolving the problems described above.
Nevertheless, some reticence was expressed as to the amount of detail which it would be possible to include in the computerised system, in particular concerning the coverage provided by the comprehensive guarantee (see below) and the statistics the system would provide.
Moreover, it was indicated that, while all Member States publicly expressed support for the computerisation project, some hesitancy had been perceived within meetings of the computerisation working group on the part of other Member States on the question of funding and timing.
This led the delegations' interlocutors to conclude that the political will necessary to implement computerisation on schedule could not be taken for granted.
In accordance with the "strict" culture described above, the Italian authorities expressed concern as to the practical functioning of the comprehensive guarantee.
In their view, the amount of the comprehensive guarantee should be sufficient to cover all the outstanding customs liabilities of a transit principal at any one time.
In other words, a system was needed whereby the customs service could verify that potential liability on all uncleared transit operations guaranteed by a certain principal did not exceed the amount of the comprehensive guarantee.
At present, this was not possible and in any case, the amount of the guarantee was generally set too low to ensure proper coverage of
debt. Italy would therefore support an increase in the level of the comprehensive guarantee.
In relation to future computerisation, other than the suggestion that the new system could help calculate the true cover offered by a principal's comprehensive guarantee, it was also suggested that computerisation could be linked to reductions in the amount of the guarantee required from a trader.
The logic of this suggestion was that a trader would only be linked to the computerised system on the basis of a proven track record and evidence of financial probity and solidity.
This in turn would allow the customs to offer a guarantee "discount" in return for the company (i) removing some administrative burden from customs, and (ii) allowing customs direct access to its systems.
Common Transi t
Italy had a particular interest in the workings of common transit given that it has a long border with Switzerland, over which a great deal of transit traffic passes.
The delegation was given to understand that cooperation with the Swiss authorities had presented the Italians with some considerable problems, especially when it came to investigating apparent irregularities or fraud.
Within this context, the Italians had been seeking and had obtained a greater degree of bilateral contact with the Swiss in order to attempt to resolve some of the problems arising.
Experience of Transit Fraud
As elsewhere, it proved very difficult in Italy to arrive at any clear estimate of how much fraud there was within the transit system.
Nevertheless, it was agreed that the amount concerned was substantial, most of all in the field of sensitive products.
The Italian authorities described four basic mechanisms for defrauding the Community Transit System:
Non-presentation of goods at destination (no return copy 5) Falsification of documents or customs stamps (forged return copy 5) Invalid guarantee (e.g. forged guarantee certificates) Insufficient guarantee (wilfully exceeding guarantee limits)
It was pointed out that the transit system in its current state provided little defence against any of these kinds of fraud, primarily because of its paperbased nature whose inherent delays and errors hindered the early detection and investigation of fraud.
The Effect of The 100% Guarantee
The Italian authorities stated unequivocally that there existed a proven link between the level of the guarantee and the incidence of fraud.
In essence, the higher the guarantee, the lower the level of fraud.
The recent ban on the use of the comprehensive guarantee for sensitive goods had therefore been an extremely useful move which had significantly reduced fraud within the transit system.
However, while not contesting this interpretation of the effects of the 100% guarantee within the transit system, it was made clear to the delegation, above all by representatives of the Guardia di Finanza, that the overall effect of
Within the wider context of fighting fraud in general therefore, the 100% guarantee had in effect merely displaced the problem rather than eliminated it.
The Perpetrators of Fraud
It was made clear to the delegation by all interlocutors that transit fraud (and indeed conventional smuggling) was dominated by organised crime.
Much of this was of course of local origin, but, increasingly, the organisations concerned were based in former Eastern bloc countries or taking on a wider international nature.
It was moreover emphasised that the problem of black market goods could no longer be viewed as a national problem but was spreading to involve all countries of the European Union.
Moreover, its importance in connection with other forms of crime (drugs smuggling, arms smuggling, illegal immigration and even terrorism) should not be disregarded.
A confidential meeting was held at the headquarters of the Guardia di Finanza at which cigarette smuggling and its relationship to the Transit System was discussed in detail.
In order that the Committee as a whole could be appraised of the important information provided by the Guardia di Finanza in this meeting, the Chairman invited its representatives to attend a Committee meeting in Brussels.1
Documentation
The Italian authorities provided voluminous documentation during the visit to Italy, in addition to the substantial amounts provided prior to the visit in response to the Committee's various questionnaires, enquiries, etc.
Most substantive information provided has been distributed to Members in all languages.
Other statistical information, in Italian only, is being incorporated within the wider statistical analyses carried out by the Committee and is available on request from the secretariat.
Acknowledgements
The members of the delegation express their warm thanks to all those they met during the visit to Italy for the helpfulness shown and the value and quality of the information provided.
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
TEMPORARY COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
Notice to members No 63
Visit by a delegation from the committee to the Hamm Central Transit Office on 2 December 1996
Enclosed please find a summary record of the visit referred to above.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
4 December 1996
DOC_EN\CM\314\314289
Participants
The following members took part in the delegation's visit to Hamm:
Mr John Tomlinson (chairman); Mr Edward Kellett-Bowman (rapporteur).
The Federal Financial Administration was represented in the discussions by the following: Dr Sievert (President of the Regional Finance Office, Münster); Mr Sohn (head of subdivision in the Customs Department of the Federal Finance Ministry); Mr Esser (principal administrative officer in the Customs Department of the Federal Finance Ministry); Mr Hüttemann (head of the Main Customs Office, Paderborn); Mr Bade (head of the Customs Office, Münster).
Mrs Sievert, President of the Regional Finance Office, Münster, welcomed the committee delegation on behalf of the Federal Financial Administration and explained the structure of the German customs administration.
Mr Tomlinson thanked the representatives of the Federal Financial Administration for arranging the visit and answering the questionnaire.
Topics discussed
The work of the Central Transit Office and general aspects of the transit system were discussed during the visit.
Work of the Central Office
Mr Bade, head of the Customs Office, Münster, explained how the transit system was handled at the Central Office and referred to a memorandum on the subject, which is attached to this notice.
He said that the Central Transit Office in Hamm was responsible only for forwarding return copies (copy 5 of the Tl document) to German customs offices of departure.
Return copies were forwarded to foreign customs offices of departure via the central office at Helmstedt.
Thirteen people were employed at the central office, six or seven of them checking return copies.
In 1996 19 793 documents had been processed per day.
A clerk could check between 3500 and 4000 return copies a day, which was equivalent to about seven seconds per document.
DOC EN\CM\314\314289
A close check of return copies was made not in Hamm, however, but at the customs office of departure or the customs crime investigation office.
The Hamm central office was thus essentially concerned with ensuring that the acknowledgement procedure functioned efficiently.
At the central office incoming return copies were merely checked for formal errors, sorted and then forwarded to the customs offices of departure.
They were forwarded to the large customs offices of departure every day and to the small ones once a week.
Return copies relating to sensitive goods were forwarded not later than one day after their arrival.
Forwarding was done through normal postal channels.
Even if return copies were lost in the post, the transit procedure could be subsequently checked, since the customs offices of destination kept copy 4 of the Tl document.
One of the main aims of the central office was to save postage when forwarding return copies.
The committee delegation objected that the return copies often came from foreign customs offices of destination and so they alone had postage to pay.
General problems relating to the transit system
During the discussion that followed the tour of inspection general problems relating to the transit procedure were considered, the following being the main points made:
- The representatives of the German Financial Administration were opposed to across-the-board cancellation of past customs debts.
Thought should be given to the future possibility of particularly reliable principals being granted rebates or reductions in guarantees.
- It was pointed out that there were only two guaranteeing associations in the TIR system in Germany, whereas there were about 8500 guarantors in the Community transit system.
- The introduction of the 100% guarantee for 'sensitive goods' had made the transit system more secure.
As the import of bananas had shown, however, it had not resulted in imports being shifted to non-Community ports.
- The transit system was gradually being extended to include the Visegrad countries.
In September, for example, some 1000 acknowledgments a week had been received from Poland, Hungary and the Czech Republic and 250 from Slovakia.
The figures were slowly rising.
- The representatives of the Federal Financial Administration were opposed to compulsory routes for transport operations, since they would overly restrict carriers' freedom of action.
It also had to be clarified whether there were to be transit corridors or accurately defined transit routes.
Ultimately, it would also have to be decided what sanctions should be imposed on carriers who departed from the fixed route.
- Germany was opposed to an increase in the comprehensive guarantee to 100% of weekly turnover.
The representatives of the Federal Financial Administration also confirmed that they had opposed
DOC EN\CM\314\314289
- The German delegation pointed out that the current provisions already included rules on maximum transport periods.
Finally, the chairman and the rapporteur raised the question of the computerization of the transit system.
The representatives of the Federal Financial Administration said the first task must be to replace the postal notification of completion with electronic notification.
Germany would be participating in a Commission pilot project in 1997.
However, even an electronic transit system would have to be backed up and safeguarded by documents.
Carriers should, in particular, have with them during transport operations a document that could be checked.
The German representatives were especially sceptical in this context about the advisability of smart cards, which the chairman had referred to as a possible option.
Until the electronic system had been introduced, a heading for the name of the carrier should be added to the Tl form.
DOC EN\CM\314\314289
EUROPEAN
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT
Notice to Members No. 65
Members will find enclosed the summary record of the visit of the Committee of Inquiry to Antwerp on 5 December 1996.
DIRECTORATE-GENERAL FOR COMMITTEES AND DELEGATIONS
Purpose of mission
The visit to the port of Antwerp was arranged in addition to the delegation visits of the Committee of Inquiry; it was possible to carry out the visit in the space of one day by bus from Brussels.
The purpose of the visit was to investigate customs and transit procedures at the port of Antwerp, especially in regard to sensitive goods such as cigarettes passing through to other destinations.
This note provides a summary of the main points raised.
A list of those individuals whom the Committee met is appended and further documentation on the port and the rôle of customs is available on request from the secretariat.
Participants
The Members of the Committee who participated in the Delegation were:
Edward Kellett­Bowman, rapporteur Freddy Blak Maj Britt Theorin Rosemarie Wemheuer
The members would particularly like to thank Mr Jaak Vandemeulebroucke, Member of the Committee, who was unable to attend, and Mr Bart Staes of the ARE Group secretariat who organised the visit, in collaboration with the Port of Antwerp Promotion Association.
Programme of the visit
In a long and productive day, the members of the delegation had the following meetings:
Harbour House (Havenhuis): Introduction to the port by public sector representatives of the Port of Antwerp Authority
Tower Building, Customs and Excise: Review of operations of the departments and of computerisation ­ visit to office responsible for clearance of Τ documents
Visit of harbour installations by launch: presentation on board in regard to cigarette fraud
Visit of container terminal: presentation on modern container transport procedures
Visit of customs house at the terminal: presentation of procedures for check­in and control of containers
Visits to 2 cigarette warehouses
CEPA building: meeting with representatives of freight forwarders and shipping agents
Court of Justice (Gerechtshof) : meetings with deputy Public Prosecutor and examining magistrates
Dinner at the Royal Yacht Club hosted by the Port of Antwerp Promotion Association (private sector).
Activities of the Port of Antwerp
Although Rotterdam is considerably larger in terms of tonnage, Antwerp is a port of major significance for the trade of the European Union, with a dominant position in the trade in certain products, in particular, chemicals, fruit and tobacco.
The port stretches for 20 kilometres along the river Scheldt, reaching as far as the Dutch border.
25% of the seagoing traffic of the port is associated with industries located in the area of the port itself, especially petrochemicals.
The port is currently owned by the municipality of Antwerp, but from 1997 will have an autonomous status as an enterprise wholly owned by the city.
More than half the places on the board of directors are held by members of Antwerp city council.
The private sector has no direct participation in port authority, but a consultative council exists and commercial joint ventures will now be possible.
The port users have established a Port of Antwerp Promotion Association.
Port infrastructure is owned by the city but leased for long periods to private operators.
The port's income is therefore comprised of income from port dues, leases and the provision of certain services.
Cargo handling charges are entirely a matter of private contract.
In 1995 the port's business is shared in 4 roughly equal portions between conventional general cargo, containers, dry bulk and liquids (especially oil).
It is of particular importance for cars (500 000 units in 1995), steel (10 million tonnes), fruit (1.6 million tonnes) and paper (4 million tonnes).
The volume and proportion of containerised cargo is still rising, but the volume of other types is static.
Only 29% of hinterland traffic is by road: barges and railways remain very important, with 50 000 barges in particular assuring about half of the total volume of long-distance traffic.
Antwerp also has a notably large amount of covered storage space (4.2 million square meters).
The harbourmaster is responsible for security, in particular for dangerous cargos.
But the port also provided a special controlled area for high value, sensitive products to reduce theft.
Unfortunately this has been under-used because of the charges imposed.
The port provides employment for 60 000 people of who half work in industrial enterprises, 15% in the public sector and 35% in private port services.
Antwerp port activities constitute 4% of the GNP of Belgium.
[For comparison, Rotterdam contributes 12% of the GNP of the Netherlands.]
Customs and Excise
There is currently no possibility of direct inquiry.
A separate, more rapid, procedure exists for sensitive goods, which requires an extra Tl form to be filled in and for a system of exchanges of information by fax.
During the tour of the harbour, the delegation heard examples of cases involving cigarette frauds which had started from the port of Antwerp from a specialist of the customs department.
The role of the duty-free warehouses in Antwerp and elsewhere was discussed and in particular the degree of permitted processing and packaging inside such warehouses.
The delegation visited a container terminal operated by the firm Hessenatie, specialists in stevedoring.
It heard about the highly automated nature of the terminal, which had about 9000 containers on its premises on each day, and was able to witness the rapid movements of containers from lorries or trains to docks to ships and vice-versa, using swift machines called "straddle-carriers" linked by on-board computers.
It also heard of plans to introduce camera inspections of containers passing at 60 kph through a gateway, to check on their physical state and on sealing.
Computers already control the allocation of space to containers, their loading and unloading and their position on board ship (to ensure proper weight distribution).
At the customs office for the container terminal the delegation witnessed the procedure for registering containers on arrival and for verification of seals.
Cigarette warehouses
The delegation visited two such warehouses run by the firms Belgian Pakhoed NV and by Rhenus, respectively for PJ Reynolds and for Philip Morris.
Each firm worked exclusively for one cigarette producer and accepted instructions for onward distribution only from this firm.
The goods were all - with the exception of goods sent to duty-free stores - for reexport outside the EU (with Russia being a favourite destination); each case had identification in regard to shipment and storage as well as the nature of the product and sometimes the intended market.
On average, goods were estimated to stay in the warehouses for one and a half months.
Repacking was undertaken only for damaged containers and shipments were usually sent on in airtight bags when loaded., with seals from both the manufacturer and the customs.
Theft of cigarettes from the premises had occurred (rarely) and always resulted in the warehouse operator having to pay the full value of taxes and excise duty owing.
Change of ownership of the goods took place after the cigarettes had left the warehouse.
Freight forwarders and shipping agents
This meeting with representatives of the private sector of the port of Antwerp was held in the city.
A list of indicative questions prepared by the secretariat had been circulated in advance and the meeting began with oral replies to these questions from a speaker on behalf of all port users:
* from 40 to 42% of port business involves Common Transit
The major problems in regard to Community customs fraud as seen by the operators were then presented to the Committee (see attached letter).
In particular, the potential for fraud was increased by the requirement that customs controls be carried out on the premises of the exporter and not at the port itself.
2) List of Antwerp representatives
'•rank BOOGAERTS Manager.
MAJOR PROBLEMS FOR ANTWERP-BASED FREIGHT FORWARDERS ARE :
