Indeed, fraude and theft can already occur on the route from the exporting company and the port.
We feel also that the Commission does not consider in a proper way the possibilities offered by art.
161, para 5 of the Custom Code, ("or" - "or"...)
Theoretically this problem should be solved by the recently taken decisions (alternative means of proof).
However, in practice this is not always the case, since it still happens that for some reason the required document is not stamped or is not returned, or does not arrive at the office of departure.
In some cases stamps are falsified ones, or have been stolen.
How can the freight forwarder check and be held responsible ?
Full automatisation is the only adequate solution, otherwise, the freight forwarder, having signed the document will still be kept responsible (see Enel..
Tt will undoubtedly have a positive impact, but docs not give a solution to the basis of the problem : not the fraudulous people/companies are attached, but these companies that still are willing/have the financial capacity to take such a high responsibility.
Furthermore, at the end the consumer will have to pay the high costs involved in these huge guarantees.
The ideas as put forward in the Report 3 of the European Parliament is therefore very interesting and will prove to be adequate, i.e. co-responsibility of the e.g. truckers/road hauliers (page 5 - 2 a.i.i.).
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMMITTEE ON BUDGETARY CONTROL
REPORT to the Committee on Budgetary Control by the members of the delegation of the Committee on their visit to the Port of Rotterdam of 11-12 April 1996
Members of the delegation:
Purpose of Mission
The first of the two visits was to the French port of Le Havre, and took place in July 1995 .
The second, to Rotterdam, is the subject of this report.
The purpose of the visit was to examine the means by which customs services control and monitor freight traffic and the mechanisms by which the Community and the Member States collect customs duties, excise and VAT on goods entering the Community at Rotterdam.
More generally it wished to explore the role of customs within the operation of the port as a whole.
In an intensive two-day programme, the members of the delegation had the following meetings
Visit to Delta Terminal site.
Presentation by Mr J.R.M. Nijman of Europe Combined Terminals (ECT) on the operation the container port.
Presentation by Mr J.P. Everwijn of Customs District Rotterdam on customs procedures in Rotterdam port.
Presentation by Mr H.M. Huisman of the Customs Information Centre on the targeting of physical checks.
Presentation by Mr H.R. Rodenburg of the Fiscal Intelligence and Investigation Service (FIOD) on the operation of this service.
Presentation by Mr C.A. De Beaufort, Public Prosecutor specialising in EU fraud, on the legal means for dealing with fraud.
Visit to container physical control site.
Visit to Customs House Oliphantweg.
Presentation by Mr J.M. Dekkers of the Rotterdam Port Industries Association on the demands of port operators on the functioning of the customs/control system
Presentation by Mr M.S.B. Duin of the Rotterdam Association of Shipbrokers and Agents on the need for a "level playing field" in customs controls.
Presentation by Mr P.J.N, van Os of the Netherlands Association of Forwarding Agents and Logistics on the relationship between the activities of forwarders and customs.
Visit to Van Ommeren Tank Terminals (import, storage and forwarding of liquid products).
Presentation by Mr H. van Westenbrugge of the impact of customs/transit regulations on Van Ommeren1s business.
Visit to Ribro International Transport (textile forwarding agents).
Presentation by Mr C. van den Broek on customs clearance procedures.
Presentation by Mr M.J. van Broekhoven of the Chamber of Commerce for Rotterdam and the Lower Maas on certificates of origin.
Visit to Royal Maas Yacht Club.
Welcome by Mr J.T. Bernard, commodore.
Presentation by Mr P.M.A. van den Berg of the National Inspection Service for Livestock and Meat on veterinary controls.
Concluding comments by Mr H.W.H. Welters, Rotterdam Port Industries Association.
This can be made available to members wishing for greater detail on individual meetings.
The following report is therefore not a resumé of the meetings which were held, but a distillation of the important issues and problems which were laid before the delegation, with some preliminary observations of the members themselves.
The following subject areas will therefore be covered: a) b) c) d) e)
Activities of the Port of Rotterdam
Europe ' s
Measured by the total amount of traffic it handles, Rotterdam is the biggest port in the world, though this position is under challenge from some Far Eastern ports.
More crucially, it is indisputably Europe's dominant port, and a substantial part of the Netherlands economy.
The following figures for 1994, issued by the Port of Rotterdam, illustrate the scale of the enterprise.
Total goods transshipped Seagoing ships in/out Market share in Hamburg - Le Havre range Container throughput Employment : direct indirect Contribution to Netherlands GNP Area
The dominance of Rotterdam in European terms is likely to be consolidated or increased over coming years, as the size of ships steadily grows, limiting in practical terms the number of ports capable of handling them and encouraging shippers to look for a restricted number of "continental" ports which can act as hubs for on-shipment.
Rotterdam's geographical position and its existing (and planned) facilities make it the most likely to be able to fulfil this role.
The scale of operations in Rotterdam have made fast and efficient cargo handling and on-transport indispensable.
The most striking example of this encountered during the visit was the Delta Terminal, which handles the bulk of Rotterdam's container traffic.
This huge, almost completely automated 24-hour facility illustrated clearly the sheer volume of goods flowing through the port and the rapidity with which goods could be transferred from a cargo vessel onto the back of a lorry on the motorway.
The prime concern of any operation of this sort is smooth and rapid transit of goods, is it is this which gives its a competitive edge and repays the enormous investment in infrastructure.
Implications for the collection of own resources
A flow of goods on this scale clearly gives rise to substantial amounts of VAT and duty, payable both to the Community and the Member States, either immediately at the port or at the conclusion of a transit procedure beginning at the port.
The latter area is particularly important in Rotterdam, with, the delegation was informed, nearly half (44%) of goods passing through Rotterdam being forwarded under the Community transit regime.
The Dutch customs in Rotterdam therefore has a disproportionately large task on its hands in controlling the flow of goods and ensuring either the payment of the appropriate taxes and duties in situ or correct completion of the transit procedures.
The manner in which it achieves this formed the central issue explored by the delegation
The activities of the customs service
The place of customs in the operation of Rotterdam port
It was said to the delegation on several occasions, not least by customs officials themselves, that it was incumbent on the customs service to carry out its job with as little interference in the flow of goods as possible.
In other terms, the customs service should put minimum bureaucratic and administrative obstacles in the way of importers.
There are clear practical reasons for this (the queues could quickly become astronomical and the quantities of goods overwhelm customs officials), but the reasoning was also commercial - namely that Rotterdam trades on its reputation for speed and efficiency.
(This is obviously a potentially sensitive issue and will be examined in greater detail below.)
Customs therefore clearly saw itself as an integral part of the Rotterdam "machine" and a contributor to its success.
A number of implications for the working methods of customs emerge.
The delegation had the opportunity to see, both in customs offices and in private importers' offices, a quick and efficient computer system for the clearance of customs. In particular, the so-called "SAGITTA" centralised computer system, set up by Dutch customs, allowed importers to process their own customs declarations for goods entering free circulation.
The main day-to-day administrative problems arise from the Community transit system, which the delegation was given to understand to be of antediluvian primitiveness, requiring vast amounts of paperwork and relying on the outmoded technology of a customs stamp to confirm clearance.
Leaving aside for a moment the risk of fraud involved in the system (one more than familiar to the European Parliament already) the mere task of administering it was extremely burdensome and prone to error.
Controlling fraud - physical checks
No customs service has yet come up with a means of controlling goods in passage which eliminates the need for physical checks.
Sheer logistics in Rotterdam mean however that such checks are very limited in number relative to the amount of traffic passing through.
The figure quoted to the delegation for the number of checks carried out was on average of between 0.2% and 0.5% of total traffic passing through the port.
On the day the delegation visited the Delta Terminal, the result of this was that 14 containers were inspected, of which 6 were found to contain some kind of irregularity, four of these resulting in a loss of revenue.
Given the small number of physical checks possible, some sort of targeting of those checks is clearly indispensable.
In fact, the delegation found the Dutch customs quite insistent that even a huge increase in the number of physical checks would be quite useless unless backed up by effective risk analysis.
For this reason the Customs Intelligence Centre (a "horizontal" service within Dutch customs) placed considerable emphasis on developing risk analysis, based in particular on "memoranda of understanding" with shipping agents and importers providing for advance information on the nature of goods on their way to Rotterdam.
With this information, combined with other factors (such as new legislation, industrial sector and product information, laboratory reports, information from other customs, etc.) physical checks could be carried out on specifically targeted loads or containers.
The effectiveness of such targeting was, in the view of customs officials, demonstrated by the high "hit" rate among the 14 containers checked in the presence of the delegation.
Technological aids to physical inspection were also the subject of discussion.
The delegation was shown one of the Dutch customs' two mobile Xray units through which individual packages were passed for examination.
Though an effective form of examination (akin to similar scanners for hand luggage in airports) the capacity of these devices is clearly limited.
As a result, Dutch customs has requested from its government three Xray loading bays, i.e. scanners capable of checking an entire container at once.
This new technology, which the Committee has already seen in action at Le Havre, would clearly represent a major increase in the amount of physical control possible, though, at an estimated throughput of 130,000 containers a year, is still a
Organisation of Dutch customs
Apart from its 9 district offices, of which Rotterdam is the largest with fully 30% of total staff, the Dutch customs service has two "horizontal" services aimed at combatting fraud which made presentations to the delegation.
The DIC - Customs Intelligence Centre - is the "upstream" service responsible for gathering and collating information and in particular for the risk analysis outlined above.
The FIOD - Fiscal Information and Investigation Service - is responsible for the prevention and investigation of tax evasion and customs-related offences.
This body worked on the basis of information received from a variety of contacts, which were indicatively enumerated for the delegation: tip-offs (about one per day), communications on the customs mutual assistance system (40-50 per year), irregularities in tax/customs declarations (80-90 per year) as well as providing information to other services in the Netherlands and customs services abroad (the requests from the latter totalling 115 in the first quarter of 1996).
The FIOD also explained its working method, which involved dividing its investigative resources between different problem areas, such as drugs, tax fraud, customs fraud, dealing with inward requests for information, etc.
The balance struck between these areas depended however to a large degree on circumstance, and could be affected for example by a particularly large problem in one area or perhaps an abnormal number of requests from abroad for information.
Only one presentation was made to the delegation on this particular subject - by a public prosecutor specialising in EU fraud.
The comments made were familiar to members of the Committee on Budgetary Control but particularly striking in coming from a person involved on a day-to-day practical basis in prosecuting EU fraud.
They provide some food for thought.
Is criminal prosecution always the most appropriate response to fraud?
It is not compulsory in the Netherlands to prosecute in all cases where fraud is indicated, and it was suggested that criminal action might not always be the most appropriate way of dealing with a specific case (although obviously preferable in cases where detention was a likely outcome).
The underlying problem was to chose the right cases to take to court given the extremely lengthy procedures required and the complexity of the evidence to be submitted.
It should furthermore be borne in mind that the police, prosecution service and courts are all overburdened, leading to severe practical difficulties in dealing with a mass of evidence and information and adding extra delays on top of those already built into the system.
In short, only the most serious cases should, in practical terms, be prosecuted. Consequently, and again with reference to the real rather than the ideal world, administrative sanctions often provided a simpler and cost-effective response to fraud, while, if used with sufficient rigour, providing an adequate deterrent effect.
While cooperation between law enforcement agencies in different countries does exist, it is slow, formalistic and not responsive to the needs of the prosecution.
The latter point is particularly important, and arises from the fact, say, that the Dutch prosecution service has no authority over investigative services elsewhere in the Union, and can consequently not oblige them to give priority to a matter which may be of pressing urgency in the Netherlands.
Role of the EU institutions
In this area the Commission, and in particular UCLAF, can be of great help.
However, the relationship between the EU and the Dutch public prosecution "works strangely", with contacts taking place, but without any official basis for these contacts.
UCLAF had proved helpful in organising informal meetings and coordination between the various services of different Member States and in general in trying to "push things along".
As a result of its limited brief, however, it could not follow through in any more concrete capacity and inevitably reached a point at which it had to stand back from specific cases.
In the Netherlands, it had been decided to allow UCLAF to report information on possible offences direct to the public prosecution service, without the intermediary step of the Permanent Representation.
Though useful in accelerating procedures, this had been done purely at the discretion of the Dutch authorities and was limited to cases where suspected fraud affected direct subsidies managed by the Commission - clearly only a small part of a wider problem.
One necessary conclusion presented to the delegation was therefore that contacts between UCLAF and national prosecutors should be "formalised" in such a way as to make them useful in specific prosecutions.
The "level playing field"
One point that almost all interlocutors were keen to make was that the activities of customs, representing as it were the financial interests of the "state" (national authorities, the EU, taxpayers and citizens at large), had to be balanced against other, at times overlapping, interests.
In its simplest form, the argument was that the customs should not "interfere" unduly with the efficiency of trade through the port.
Underlying this idea however are a number of specific complaints and issues which require careful consideration.
Rotterdam in the European Union
Great insistence was placed in all quarters on the prime importance of ensuring that customs controls are of the same rigour everywhere - especially in those ports in direct competition with each other.
The abstract point is clear enough: if the activities of customs (paperwork, physical checks, requirements for information, etc.) are more onerous in one port than in another, then this represents a competitive advantage for the port with the more relaxed approach (all other conditions being equal).
It should be stressed that there is no evidence that this downward spiral has occurred, but that the pressure clearly exists.
Moreover, a perception can easily arise that one's competitor is playing less strictly by the book than oneself and a belief that undoubtedly different cultures of public administration give rise to different standards of control.
It is vital therefore that the European Community, which in effect writes the rule-book for customs, ensure objectively common standards throughout the Union.
The EU in the wider world
The problem of competitive advantage through the action of public administrations is not one which is limited to the EU Member States.
Following the changes in the countries of central and eastern Europe, competition to ports such as Rotterdam for trade into and out of the European continent is provided increasingly by non-EU ports.
Here the question is more linked with underlying customs legislation and the incentive it might provide to use ports outside the EU.
As a general issue, the importance of this problem is still relatively limited insofar as non-EU ports cannot at present compete across the board in terms of capacity, efficiency and so on.
The issue is currently felt at the level of single businesses.
Individual operators within
Although many interlocutors made the same point, one example will serve to illustrate a specific complaint made about the Community transit system and its effect on the competitiveness (and profitability) of particular operators.
As members are aware, under the Community Transit system, taxes and duties are paid not at the point of entry into the EU, but at the point at which they goods are to be placed on the market.
Goods travel from the point of entry to the point of customs clearance under the transit system, with the "principal" (i.e the freight forwarder) giving a guarantee to the customs authorities to cover the tax/duty liability until the goods are cleared through customs.
This guarantee is at the heart of many complaints the delegation heard in Rotterdam.
As from 1 April 1996 a 100% guarantee is required for the transport of certain "sensitive" (i.e. high-duty) goods under the transit system.
One of the visits paid by the delegation was to Van Ommeren NV, whose businesses include trade in ethanol.
This product, basic value 1.20 guilders per litre, has an all-duty-paid value of 40 guilders per litre and is therefore classified as "sensitive".
The company reports that, following the introduction of the 100%-guarantee on 1 April 1996, what was a rapidly-expanding business has ceased totally.
The explanation given was that the potential liability was too high for both Van Ommeren, which relies on a banking facility to cover its guarantee obligations as "principal" and for its customers which are contractually bound to Van Ommeren.
Even where traders were willing to use Rotterdam, the ceiling effectively placed on Van Ommeren by its banking limits would have the consequence of limiting possible traffic to less than an eighth of its pre-April level.
The hypothesis put to the delegation was that the trade was switching to eastern European ports where such guarantee requirements do not exist.
It is clear that Van Ommeren's presentation of its problems were not of an entirely disinterested nature, and that the taxpayer at large has a legitimate interest in making the transit system as secure as possible.
However, the members of the delegation would invite reflection on the fact that there is a balance to be struck between the interests of the taxpayer (who is eventually required to make up lost revenue) and the interests of the trader (who, in case of fraud, pays the lost revenue whether or not responsible and in any case takes on a large contingent liability in the balance sheet at a significant direct financial cost).
In other terms, there is a cost benefit equation to be made, between costs to the taxpayer and costs to the economy.
Customs procedures - a users' wish list
During a meeting with the representatives of Rotterdam Port Operators the delegation was presented with a list of ten "demands".
These are an interesting reflection of the viewpoint of the users of the system and of Rotterdam Port, which is at some variance with the usual institutional viewpoint encountered by the Committee.
They are reproduced verbatim below, not because the delegation members wish necessarily to support them, but because they reflect concisely much of what they heard in Rotterdam.
(Clarifications are added in italics as necessary.) a) "Level playing field" (see above) b) "Tailor-made, client-oriented solutions" (i.e. control procedures tailored to the circumstances of individual businesses) c) "Pragmatism/flexibility" (including in the interpretation of legislation) d) "Incentives for good behaviour" (i.e. customs to take account of operator's track record in deciding level of control to apply) e) "Simplification of administrative procedures to create more capacity for 'intelligence'" (i.e. eliminate useless paperwork) f) "EDI/tracking and tracing" (i.e. use of satellite tracking technology to monitor vehicle under transit regime) g) "Cooperation between customs of EU ports" h) "Advanced notice of and consultation with private port operators" i) "Preservation of transit trade" j ) "Cooperation between customs and private port operators to cope with fraud"
Conclusions
The delegation found its visit to Rotterdam very interesting and useful, predominantly for the reason that it provided an insight into the role customs, and thereby the collection of own resources, can play in the commercial life of a port like Rotterdam.
Above all the members of the delegation were able to appreciate the difficult equilibrium to be struck between operators' freedom to trade and customs' duty to collect income on behalf of the public.
This report does not wish to draw firm conclusions as to how this balance should be achieved, but
Mr Herbert Bosch Mr Piet Dankert Mr Stefano De Luca Mr Edward Kellett-Bowman Mr Jan Mulder Mrs Rosemarie Wemheuer attended as members from the Member State concerned
EUROPEAN
COMMITTEE OF INQUIRY INTO THE COMMUNITY TRANSIT SYSTEM
The following is a brief account of the principal points arising during a workshop held by the European Conference of Ministers of Transport (ECMT) on 4 December 1996 on the subject of "Crime in Transport"1.
The ECMT is an intergovernmental organisation established by a Convention signed in Brussels in 1953.
It now comprises the Ministers of Transport of 30 Member States, plus eight associate and observer states and 25 non-governmental organisations and professional associations.
It meets in plenary session once a year and has as its objective (i) to aim "to achieve, at general or regional level, the maximum use and most rational development of European inland transport of international importance", and (ii) " to coordinate and promote the activities of international organisations concerned with European inland transport, taking into account the work of supranational authorities in this field"2.
The purpose of the workshop
Following an increasing international awareness of the serious levels of crime involving the transport sector, the current French presidency of the ECMT decided to prioritise the subject at the next full meeting of the Conference.
The aim of this conference was to gather a wide range of experts and concerned parties to discuss the key issues connected with crime in transport and to formulate recommendations on the action to be taken by the Ministers of Transport.
The meeting was attended by a wide range of participants from all over Europe, including representatives of national transport ministries, international organisations, customs, police, transport sector organisations, freight forwarders, trade unions, etc.
Though entitled generically 'Crime in Transport", the meeting tended to focus on fraud in connection with the Community and Common Transit systems and the TIR Convention, though there was some discussion of other (often connected) issues such as vehicle theft, hijacking, etc.
The meeting was chaired by the Deputy of the French Minister of Transport, Mr Jean Guillot, and was structured around a series of presentations by nominated experts, on the basis of written submissions.
Held at OECD Headquarters in Paris
"Transport related crime" The German Delegation
"The General Trend in Goods Transport in Europe and in Customs Transit" Mr Jean DUQUESNE, former Director of the ECE/UN Transport Division1
"The Theft of Goods and Vehicles in Transit - French Law and the CMR" Professor B. MERCADAL, Institute of International Transport Law, Rouen
"The Future of Transit Systems" European Commission, DG XXI (represented by Mr Jean-Michel GRAVE)
"Figures relating to lorry load and warehouse thefts" Mr Rodney WALKER, Detective constable, International Lorry Load Desk, UK
"IRU Contribution to 'Crime in Transport' workshop" Mr Jean ACRI, Deputy Head, TIR Department, IRU, Geneva
"Contribution by FreightForward Europe to 'Crime in Transport' workshop" Mr Poul HANSEN, Manager FFE2
The speaker from Freight Forward Europe in particular stressed the enormous costs to business of transit fraud arising from the principal's guarantee.
FFE representatives appeared as witnesses in the Committee of Inquiry: 25 April 1996
Appeared as a witness in the Committee of Inquiry for the Comité Européen des Assurances: 4 November 1996
Notwithstanding the general agreement that the crisis existed, it was extremely difficult to establish how much transit fraud was taking place, or even what amounts by freight forwarders and other principals to the various national customs services.
Only Mr Hansen of FFE attempted an estimate, pointing to about $350 million of accumulated debt to customs services and anything between Ecu 3.5 billion and Ecu 20 billion of debt outstanding under the Community transit system.
All speakers deplored the inability of either national or international public authorities to provide proper statistics in this field, thus placing a severe handicap in the way of any effective treatment of the problem.
Responsibilities and Liabilities
Who are the criminals?
There was general consensus that crime within the transit systems was perpetrated by international criminal organisations, some of them within Western Europe but increasingly based in the countries of Central and Eastern Europe and the former Soviet Union.
It was also observed, whoever the perpetrators, that crime was a problem above all in "core" Western European countries rather than in "exotic" members of the TIR Convention, such as Kazakhstan, Afghanistan, etc.
Mho bears the responsibility for failings of the system?
Though clearly controversial, the question of responsibility saw a consensus emerging, with the finger pointing at the political world in general and the customs services in particular for failing to react to changing circumstances.
Mr Duquesne indicated that it was irresponsible to create a system without possessing the instruments necessary to manage it.
Several speakers, including Mr Acri, pointed out that governmental complacency arose in part from the misuse of the system solely to secure government revenue and that transit systems had slowly been transformed into guarantee systems for money rather than transit systems for goods.
Mr Ehmcke openly acknowledged that the German customs service saw its primary role as the collection of revenue, and that as long as the guarantee system worked in these terms he would be satisfied.
He nevertheless acknowledged that any transit regime "is an invitation to fraud".
Responding, Mr Duquesne demurred from this point of view, affirming that the first duty of customs is to prevent, detect and punish fraud.
The effect of the guarantee was to make the legitimate economy pay for fraud rather than the fraudster.
Who pays the bills?
Trade representatives expressed their concern that the failings of transit were visited almost exclusively on freight forwarders and transporters.
Much criticism of customs' failure adequately to investigate and punish fraud was therefore voiced.
In a similar vein, the introduction of a 100% guarantee for sensitive goods was attacked as an unjustified additional cost on business.
Invited to make proposals for action, a number of delegates mentioned areas where measures could be introduced.
The following are examples of the suggestions made:
Better collection and precision in statistics relating to the transit system; Better collection and distribution of intelligence concerning transit fraud between national authorities and traders; Application of information technology (computerisation of transit system); Allow transporters to provide alternative forms of proof of discharge of a transit operation; Ministries of Transport to cooperate more effectively with Ministries of Finance to defend the interests of transport operators; More rigorous policing of external borders; Closer examination of possible fraud within non-road forms of transport; 'White listing'; A more equitable share of financial responsibility under the transit system; Application of other technologies (e.g. satellite tracking); Customs checks to reach a dissuasive level; Principals guarantees to correspond more accurately to genuine risk; More effective cooperation between police and customs services; International control/investigation authorities (leading to single customs service); Reinforcement of customs services.
OTHER TRANSPORT CRIME
The meeting also addressed briefly other forms of transport crime, though at considerably less length.
As this is of less interest to the Committee of Inquiry, it is not dealt with in detail here.
Nevertheless a few statistics may be of interest.
The following were provided by a British police officer concerning vehicle theft in the United Kingdom.
Annual loss to business owing to vehicle theft: £1.6 billion; Seven out of ten transport companies have suffered serious truck related crime; Annual value of trucks stolen in the UK: £190 million; Recovery rate for stolen trucks: 10%; Recovery rate for stolen trailers: 5%; Ten years ago, 8 out of 10 stolen loads were sold on UK market within 2 weeks; now only 2 out of 10 appear on the domestic market, with the remainder exported;
Representatives stressed the importance of vehicle marking and police/customs cooperation in dealing with the increasing crime problem.
