OFFICE FOR OFFICIAL PUBLICATIONS OF THE EUROPEAN COMMUNITIES
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
DEVELOPMENT OF EUROPEAN LEGISLATION impact of the European Parliament through the codecision procedure r :S l c
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT he Treaty on European Union, signed at Maastricht in 1992, conferred upon the European Parliament the power of codecision in legislative matters.
This institutional reform represented real progress for democracy within the European Union.
The European Parliament is, in fact, the only institution of the Union directly elected by all the citizens of Europe.
Through the introduction of the codecision procedure in 1993, the European Parliament's influence over EU legislation has been substantially increased.
At the Amsterdam European Council in June 1997, codecision took a further step forward.
Introduction by Mr José María Gil-Robles, President of the European Parliament
European Union which is more democratic, more transparent and chser to their concerns. They , yêé'/ í/wí í^í Union should not ^^^T
_^«Ρ*\/ > ■.. ørø/y speak with a single voice on the international stage and coordinate economic and social policies in order to boost employment but should aho be able to influence their everyday lives.
The codecision procedure, involving the European Parliament and the Council ofMinisters, is intended to meet this expectation.
It represents a step forward for democracy and makes decision-making more transparent.
The procedure may appear complex but it has worked well.
The directly elected European Parliament represents all the citizens of the Union, while the position of the Member States is expressed in the Council.
The representatives of both these sources of kgitimacy have karned, as a result of the Maastricht Treaty, to work together in a number of areas.
The examples given below show that teamwork between the two institutions can give a genuine European character to Communitypolicies. policies.
Ckarer labelling, recycling of waste, information campaigns on cancer, AIDS and drug dependence, more student exchanges, effective protection against abuses of new sales techniques or comparative advertising and steps to safeguard the right of everyone to watch major sporting events on televmon — all these are areas of everyday life on which the European Parliament has left its mark.
Through the codecision procedure, Parliament and the Council are also preparing together for the future of the European Union.
Taking advantage of the Community dimension, they are developing transport, telecommunications and energy networks.
Also, as we stand on the threshold of the 21st century, promoting the European dimension in research is one of their foremost concerns.
Once the Treaty of Amsterdam, which will significantly expand the scope of this procedure, has been ratified, Parliament and the Council will have to work together on important areas such as social policy, combating fraud and the introduction of a genuine European citizenship.
As far as Parliament is concerned, the success of codecision so far only strengthens our wish to have this procedure used for all European Union legislation.
It is only right to call for equal powers for the two arms of the legislative authority — Parliament and Council — and we are making this demand not merely to strengthen our own position but because we are convinced that codecision is one of the best means of safeguarding the interests of the public we represent.
José María Gil-Robles President of the European Parliament
From the right to be consulted to the role ofco-legislator co-legislator
Consultation procedure (1957)
For a long time Parliament played only a secondary role in the adoption of texts: the final decision was always taken by the Council of Ministers.
The Treaty of Rome, adopted in 1957, gave Parliament only the power to deliver an opinion on a text proposed by the Commission, under the consultation procedure.
The Council was not obliged to take account of Parliament's opinion when taking a decision.
The Single European Act of 1986 gave Parliament more weight by introducing the cooperation procedure.
This new and more complex legislative procedure gave Members of the European Parliament (MEPs) more say in the drafting of Community legislation.
Nevertheless, the earlier situation remained basically unchanged in that the Council still had the final word in all cases and so in the end could impose its own wishes.
The cooperation procedure introduced under the Single Act worked in a very satisfactory way, showing that an extension of Parliament's legislative powers did not cause the decision-making process to seize up as some had feared.
After the Amsterdam Treaty, which will come into force when the ratification procedure is completed, the cooperation procedure will only be used for matters relating to economic and monetary union (EMU).
The Maastricht Treaty, ratified in 1993, introduced the codecision procedure.
This is fundamentally different from the consultation and cooperation procedures.
It involves the adoption of joint acts by the Council and the European Parliament.
Under the codecision procedure, many more parliamentary amendments have been included in Community acts.
The two institutions are on an equal footing. No text can be adopted under this procedure without the formal agreement of the European Parliament and the Council of the European Union.
Under the Amsterdam Treaty, the codecision procedure is simplified and its sphere of application extended to new provisions of the Treaty as well as to areas which previously came under the assent, consultation or cooperation procedure.
Codecision in practice
Codecision means dialogue and compromise.
A codecision culture has gradually emerged and has proved very successful.
Very often the Council and Parliament reach agreement at the second reading. In other cases dialogue continues in the Conciliation Committee.
The two institutions work on an equal footing to produce a joint text.
The codecision procedure may appear complex but practice has shown that it does not slow down legislative activity.
Indeed it enriches it by giving it a European and democratic character.
The examples below show that codecision can often be responsible for improvements in people's daily lives.
It means that Parliament now has considerable influence on European legislation.
This is all the more important in that codecision has a real impact on everyday problems.
Nevertheless, some proposals have resulted in tough negotiations between Parliament and the Council.
In these cases it has been necessary to convene the Conciliation Committee.
Each institution then has to compromise, while trying to uphold the points that it considers essential.
By 1 July 1997, of 103 common positions examined under the codecision procedure, 56 did not require the intervention of the Conciliation Committee.
This means that either no amendments were proposed by Parliament (33 cases) or the Council incorporated Parliament's amendments into its common position (23 cases).
This shows that the Parliament and the Council's joint work has been bearing fruit since the beginning of the procedure.
As a result of dialogue, an increasing number of Parliament's amendments are being included without the Conciliation Committee being involved.
Three examples will give an idea of the contribution made by Parliament.
In the field of road safety, the Commission proposed an intermediate stage before imposing more rigorous tests for front- and side-impact resistance.
In the interests of immediate safety improvements, Parliament did not want the transition phase. The Council accepted this approach.
Many workers are posted to another Member State by their employers.
Parliament's amendments to the directive governing their conditions of work ensured a clearer definition of employer obligations guaranteed by the Community (no discrimination of any kind, equal treatment in respect of maximum periods of work, pay thresholds, etc.).
Digital television, a major technological development, will make possible a considerable increase in the number of pay-TV channels.
Parliament did not want the consumer to have to pay for a series of expensive, incompatible decoders that would rapidly become obsolete.
The ideal technical solution — a single decoder used with different access cards — is not yet on the market Parliament therefore favoured a compromise, accepted by the Council, whereby access to encryption systems is offered to all broadcasters under fair, reasonable and non-discriminatory conditions.
Fields in which codecision applies
Freedom of movement for workers (Article 49)
Freedom of establishment (Article 54; mutual recognition of qualifications, Article 57(1); measures concerning the self-employed, Article 57(2))
Freedom to provide services (Article 66)
Approximation of the laws of the Member States to establish the internal market (Articles 100a and 100b)
Education (support measures, Article 126)
Health (support measures, Article 129)
Consumer protection (Article 129a)
Trans-European networks (guidelines, Article 129d)
Research (multiannual framework programme, Article 130i(l))
Environment (in part: general action programmes, Article 130s(3))
Ban on discrimination on grounds of nationality (Article 6)
Citizenship (right of citizens to move and reside freely within the Member States, Article 8a(2))
Transparency (general principles and limits to right of access to documents, Article 191a)
Aid-fraud measures (Article 209a)
Customs cooperation (statistics, Article 213a)
Data protection (establishment of an independent supervisory body, Article 213b)
Employment (incentive measures, Article 5)
Social policy (equal opportunities and treatment, Article 119; health and safety of workers, working conditions, information and consultation, equality between men and women, integration of persons excluded from the labour market — new articles resulting from incorporation into the Treaty of the Social Protocol; Social Fund: implementing decisions, Article 125; vocational training: measures to contribute to the achievement of objectives, Article 127(4))
Free movement of workers (social security of migrant workers within the Community, Article 51)
Freedom of establishment (special treatment for foreign nationals, Article 56(2); taking up and pursuit of activities as self-employed persons and training and conditions of access to the professions, Article 57(2))
Health (minimum requirements regarding quality and safety of organs and substances of human origin, blood and blood derivatives and measures in the veterinary and phytosanitary fields designed to protect public health, Article 129)
Transport (common rules for international transport, operation of transport services within a Member State by non-resident carriers, safety improvements, Article 75(1); sea and air transport, Article 84); Trans-European networks (Article 129d)
Research (implementation of programmes, Article 130o, in part)
Environment (measures and implementation of programmes, Article 130s(l))
The European Parliament and everyday life
As the direct representative of the citizen, the European Parliament is committed to dealing with the problems of everyday life.
More than any other, the codecision procedure enables Parliament to bring its full democratic influence to bear on the drafting of directives and regulations which will apply throughout the Union.
Parliament's influence on the texts ultimately adopted is greater than it has ever been.
The European Parliament has taken the opportunity offered by the codecision procedure to achieve one of its objectives: improving the quality of life of European citizens.
This is a two-pronged objective: on the one hand, the improvement must benefit all citizens, and, on the other, it must be aimed specifically at certain social groups.
For example, during the negotiations on a technical directive on the safety of lifts, the European Parliament insisted that, wherever technically possible, lifts should be fitted out in such a way as to facilitate access to and use of them by disabled people (size, positioning of the control buttons, etc.).
European citizens and the environment
Novel foods
Detailed labelling offers the best protection
Regulation on novel foods and novel food ingredients, such as genetically modified soya beans, sugar beet and tomatoes.
Genetic manipulation is used, for example, to influence the taste, flavour or storage period of products or to protect such products against insects or pesticides.
The aim of this proposal for a regulation was to establish standard European marketing procedures so as to ensure that disparities between national laws did not pose an obstacle to the free movement of these foodstuffs and hamper the development of the internal market.
The European Parliament insisted that these novel foods and novel food ingredients should be edible without posing a risk to health, should be environmentally sound and should be correctly labelled.
That objective was achieved by means of European Parliament/Council negotiations in the Conciliation Committee.
Under the terms of the agreement, the Council agreed to incorporate a number of European Parliament amendments into the proposal for a regulation.
• the regulation should enter into force much earlier than it had proposed in its common position: 90 days rather than 12 months after its publication in the Official Journal;
• novel foods suitable for placing on the market without delay — i.e. foods in respect of which a more detailed assessment is not required — must nevertheless meet the labelling requirements laid down in the regulation.
Consumers will thus be able to identify these products.
Compromises were reached in respect of other amendments, with the result that the provisions of the regulation will be more favourable to consumers than those originally put forward by the Council:
• all foodstuff labels must inform the consumer of the characteristics or properties which, as demonstrated by a scientific assessment, render a novel food or novel food ingredient different from a conventional product.
The label must also indicate the presence of genetically modified organisms.
Packaging waste
Two priorities: prevention and recycling
The directive on the reduction and recovery of packaging waste
This text, which seeks to remedy the problem of the ever increasing volume of waste and the overloading of waste dumps, was substantially influenced by the European Parliament.
Many of its amendments were adopted, the most important involving:
• the need to establish a hierarchy of priorities in packaging policy, i.e. top priority for prevention, followed by reuse (recycling), incineration in order to recover energy, incineration without energy recovery and, finally, elimination by dumping;
• the acknowledgement that landfill cannot be regarded as a form of organic recycling;
• the recognition of the 'polluter-pays' principle.
European citizens and health
The Cancer programme
Many points to which Parliament attached great importance were incorporated in the texts ultimately adopted:
• the development of complementary health education measures aimed at specific groups, in particular children;
• the promotion of information campaigns intended to encourage a healthy lifestyle, a diet rich in fruit and vegetables, and consumer advice;
• active efforts to combat the adverse impact on young people of tobacco advertising;
• the selection at European level of the best methods of giving up smoking, publicising them and assessing their impact;
• the launch of a media campaign against passive smoking involving opinion leaders;
The AIDS programme
Better understanding and more education needed
• encouraging the availability of, access to and use of high-quality condoms supplied with instructions for use with a view to combating sexually transmitted diseases;
• promoting information campaigns in the Member States concerning t proper use of condoms as a means of preventing the transmission of the AIDS virus;
Drug dependence
Medical treatment and dealing with the problem in a social context
The' text adopted largely reflects Parliaments standpoint, which sees drug dependence as a 'health' problem which needs to be dealt with in a social context.
The European Parliament also persuaded the Council to acknowledge the need for an 'open' approach to the issue.
Thus, the agreement reached in the Conciliation Committee states that drug dependence constitutes a health problem and that the measures taken under this initial 1996-2000 programme, with its budget of ECU 27 million, must give priority to the prevention of drug dependence and the rehabilitation of drug addicts.
Prevention must cover both hard and soft drugs, i.e. all types of drug wherever they are used.
Hence the decision, among other measures, to take action in prisons and also in places where previous measures have not reached certain at-risk groups.
This is why the programme will support the campaign 'on the ground' so as to reach drug users on the streets.
Taking this approach one step further, and after very long discussions with the European Parliament, the Council finally acknowledged the role played by drug addicts' families and friends, people who may be able to benefit from the experience gained from the programme and the resulting exchanges ofinformation.
An effective prevention policy calls for high-quality, targeted information.
Accordingly, the text stipulates that measures must be launched with a view to reaching young people and other potential users in places they frequent so that a dialogue can be initiated with them.
'Wherever possible', these measures must involve parents and other individuals and groups concerned.
The two delegations concluded that the scourge of drug dependence could h overcome only by mobilising all forces in society.
They therefore decided that public and private bodies, such as non-governmental organisations (NGOs), should be involved in implementing the programme.
European citizens and education
The Socrates and 'Youth for Europe' programmes
Need to encourage exchanges and increase funding
The European Parliament had a substantial impact on the Socrates and 'Youth for Europe'programmes adopted under the codecision procedure.
The Socrates programme is a new initiative, covering the period 1995-99, which brings together and strengthens the existing programmes dealing with higher education (Erasmus), school education and horizontal activities in the sphere of language learning, open and distance learning and information (including Lingua and Eurydice).
The 'Youth for Europe' programme seeks to foster exchanges between young people and their mobility in the European Union by breaking away from the traditional framework of education or vocational training.
The European Parliament also secured an appreciable increase in the budget for the two programmes.
Its aim is to enable the maximum number of students to benefit from them.
The respective budgets are ECU 850 million for Socrates (the Council having originally proposed ECU 760 million) and ECU 126 million for 'Youth for Europe' (the Council originally having proposed ECU 105 million).
In addition to the budget changes, the Council agreed to many other requests made by Parliament, making some concessions even before the conciliation procedure started.
As regards Socrates, the Council acknowledged Parliament's political determination to encourage intercultural school education and the education of the children of migrant workers or those of people who go from place to place in the course of their work, travellers and gypsies.
It also acknowledged that financial support could be granted to international projects which introduce intercultural teaching practices.
As regards the partnership between educational establishments, the Council agreed that one of the priorities governing eligibility for Community support should be the promotion of equal opportunities for boys and girls.
The Council agreed to incorporate into the directive an amendment which points out that the objective is to ensure that some 10% of students in the European Union follow a university course organised by universities in more than one Member State.
As regards 'Youth for Europe', the Council accepted the amendments which:
• in the specific context of the education of young people, encourage the recognition by young people of their European citizenship and the importance of democracy;
• in the case of equal opportunities, encourage women to lead an active life in all sectors of society; guarantee that young people taking patt in these exchanges or activities do not forfeit their rights, including those relating to welfare protection.
The conciliation procedure enabled Parliament to win over the Council on points still under discussion:
• the opening-up of participation in the programmes to the countries of central Europe, Cyprus and Malta;
• easier access to the programmes for persons from the least favoured groups in society with a view to combating social exclusion.
Thus, with regard to 'Youth for Europe', the Council acknowledged the importance of implementing measures to assist young people of secondgeneration immigrant stock in order to enable them to gain an insight into their culture of origin without prejudice to the objective of integration.
European citizens and consumer affairs
Distance contracts
Under the terms of the directive on distance contracts, private individuals will be protected against the improper use of sales techniques which enable salesmen to market goods or offer services to clients using means of distance communication such as unsolicited advertising, electronic mail, fax machines, automatic calling machines (without human intervention), radio, videophone and television (teleshopping).
At present, with laws varying from one country to another, it is easy for firms to circumvent the restrictive rules applicable in one Member State simply by contacting consumers, for example by telephone, from a Member State where the legislation is less strict.
Under the terms of the agreement concluded in conciliation, the Council agreed to incorporate into its common position significant Parliament amendments on the following issues:
• telephone calls: the identity of the supplier and the commercial purpose of the call must be stated at the beginning of any conversation with the consumer, who will thus be protected against unsolicited calls and will have the requisite information at his or her disposal right from the start of the conversation and will be in a position to decide whether or not to hang up;
• where suppliers employ distance selling techniques requiring an advance payment, they will be required to give not only their names but also their addresses;
• right of withdrawal: consumers will have a period of at least seven working days in which to terminate the contract without penalty and without giving any reason.
They will only be required to meet the cost of returning the goods, i.e. the postal charges;
• 'clear and comprehensible' information, i.e. not buried in the small print: the consumer must be given readily legible confirmation that the contract which he or she is being asked to sign authorises the supplier to replace the goods or services ordered by products of equivalent price and quality (e.g. black shirts as opposed to shirts with a flower pattern);
• failure by suppliers to meet obligations inherent in their contracts: in such cases, the consumer must be reimbursed without delay.
Contracts for supplies, foodstuffs, beverages and other consumer products delivered to the consumer's place of residence or place of work are covered by this provision of the directive.
The same applies to housing, transport and catering contracts to be honoured at a later date, since otherwise travel agencies and hoteliers would no doubt have exploited this loophole.
Making decisions based on reliable information
Although comparative advertising can provide consumer information, Parliament would like to see certain safeguards so there is no risk of degeneration into uncontrolled commercial warfare.
Parliament has therefore insisted that the advertiser should provide proof of accurate data when comparing different products so the consumer can make a decision based on reliable information.
Parliament also wants to protect undertakings by banning advertising that makes comparisons between imitations or reproductions on the one hand and originals bearing a protected trade mark or trade name on the other.
Parliament has also urged that comparative advertising should not be used to discredit or malign a competitor's trade mark, goods, activities, etc.
Bank deposits and cross-border credit transfers
With regard to deposits, and before the conciliation procedure had even begun, the European Parliament had already convinced the Council to offer consumers better protection by incorporating into its common position an amendment which Parliament regarded as fundamental: the increase in the amount of the guarantee from ECU 15 000 to ECU 20 000 pet depositor should deposits not be honoured.
During the conciliation procedure, the two institutions agreed to incorporate into the directive arrangements whereby consumers can seek legal redress and a clause specifying that the guaranteed amount should be revised every five years.
As regards cross-border credit transfers, the European Parliament insisted that they should be rapid, reliable and cheap.
The conciliation procedure led to the scope of the directive being extended to cover all transfers involving sums of less than ECU 50 000 (as against the figure of ECU 30 000 originally proposed by the Council).
In addition, the level of compulsory repayment in the event of default was increased to ECU 12 500 from the ECU 10 00C originally proposed by the Council.
Giving consumers the means to fight
For example, the right of the purchaser to withdraw from a contract during the 10 days following its signing and an increase in the number of items of information (nature of the charges, timeshare rules, etc.) which must be included in a contract, failing which the purchaser would have a longer period in which to decide whether or not to withdraw from a contract.
European citizens and television
Television without frontiers
Major events: protecting the public's rights Protection of minors: facilitating parental control
During the conciliation procedure forming part of the negotiations on the directive on television broadcasting activities, the European Parliament was at pains to defend two key points: the right of viewers to watch events in unencoded form and the protection of minors.
The broadcasting of major sporting events is increasingly becoming a key issue for television channels.
Given the growing number of pay-TV channels, a trend which the development of digital television can only accentuate, the European Parliament was keen to safeguard the right of viewers to watch major sporting events in unencoded form.
The compromise stipulates that the Member States may draw up lists of national or other events which may not be exclusively broadcast in encoded form.
This covers both events such as the Olympic Games or the football World Cup and events which the Member States regard as important for the public.
Under the terms of this provision, for example, the United Kingdom might not include a rugby match forming part of the Five Nations Tournament on its list, but France would.
As a result, an encoded British channel could purchase the rights to broadcast that event, but nothing could prevent public or private French channels from broadcasting the event in unencoded form.
The Conciliation Committee took the view that the protection of minors should be improved and that parental control over certain broadcasts should therefore be facilitated.
With that aim in view, within the next year the Commission will carry out, in conjunction with the competent authorities in the Member States, an investigation into the advantages and drawbacks o: measures designed to facilitate such parental control.
This study should amongst other things, consider the introduction of filter systems, the encouragement of family viewing policies and other educational and awareness-raising measures.
The European Parliament and the future of the European Union
The European Union is developing a number of major projects which may seem far removed from the concerns of the average citizen.
Nevertheless, it is through major Community projects of this kind that citizens form their impression of Europe.
The European Parliament is aware of this and thus attaches great importance to these projects.
The trans-European networks
Transport, telecommunications, energy, exploiting the EU dimension
In these areas, the paramount aim must be to exploit the EU dimension for the benefit of both producers and consumers.
Increased competition must work to the benefit of the consumer.
The European Union's measures seek to encourage the interconnection and interoperability of the national networks and access to them.
For example, a train must be able to use another company's network and must not be delayed at a frontier by a difference in gauge.
In telecommunications, it must be possible to reach a mobile telephone subscriber from a telephone using a fixed network.
In the energy sphere, alternators and other items of equipment must be compatible throughout Europe and a Portuguese producer who wishes to sell electricity in France must be able to transit via the Spanish network.
The European Parliament regards it as essential to make the trans-European networks more effective
The European Parliament laid particular stress on strengthening the Community nature of the projects covered by the trans-European networks Hence the agreement reached in the Conciliation Committee stipulating that, in the three areas, priorities should be set as effectively as possible.
From now on, all projects of common interest must be finalised using the codecision procedure.
In addition, Parliament insisted that the environmental and soci impact of such projects should be taken into account when they are drawn up
In the transport sphere, for example, the European Parliament secured a agreement to carry out impact studies across the whole of the Europea: network.
Particular attention should be paid to multimodal transport and traffic management.
Special care should be taken to facilitate links between island, isolated or outlying regions and the centre.
As regards the telecommunications networks, it was important to create the conditions for the development of a genuine information society.
By insisting on the incorporation of satellite communications into the draft texts, the European Parliament was seeking to pursue one of its main interests: ensuring that isolated regions also have access to these networks.
In addition, if infrastructure is important, due note must also be taken of the impact of these networks.
Agreement was therefore reached, for example, to carry out an assessment of the social impact of teleworking.
A further European Parliament concern is reflected in the directive: EU citizens, whatever their language, must be able to benefit from innovations brought about by these networks.
The 'Customs 2000' programme
A conciliation procedure between the European Parliament and the Council produced a very satisfactory agreement on the important 'Customs 2000' programme.
Supplementing the measures taken by the Member States in the customs sphere, this European Union programme seeks to develop and modernise customs administrations by improving the effectiveness of procedures and checks at the Union's external border.
This will make for the optimum development and management of the four freedoms guaranteed by the internal market: the free movement of persons, services, goods and capital.
Throughout the legislative process, the European Parliament has pursued a dual objective: on the one hand, making the concept of a European customs service more visible to citizens, and, on the other, improving the effectiveness of checks.
Its commitment to these two principles has borne fruit, since the final agreement very clearly reflects its two main concerns:
• the effectiveness of checks will be ensured by harmonising them at European Union level, thereby eliminating weak links in the European customs chain;
• the greater visibility of the service will be reflected by the appearance of the European 12-star symbol on the uniforms of the customs officials and die development of the concept of the internal market as a domestic market in order to show that it is entirely comparable to a national market.
With the same aim in view, the agreement includes an undertaking to examine the possibility of exchanges of customs officials among Member States, joint training and the development of computer systems to facilitate exchanges of information.
Finally, the programme provides for the computerisation of Community transit between now and 1998 and the stepping-up of cooperation with third countries, in particular the associated countries in central and eastern Europe.
Research The fourth RTD framework programme
Need for rationalisation and adequate funding
The European Parliament made substantial changes to the fourth framework programme of research and technological development adopted under the codecision procedure in April 1994:
• an increase in the overall budget from the ECU 12 billion advocated by the Council to ECU 12.3 billion;
• an operating budget of ECU 600 million for the Joint Research Centre, whereas the Council had earmarked only ECU 575 million;
• the more rigorous and methodical assessment of programmes. The Commission will monitor the stage reached in implementing the programmes on a systematic and ongoing basis with assistance from independent experts;
• a genuine concern for the protection of workers and the environment: new methods and techniques will be developed with a view to protecting the environment and improving the quality of life and working conditions, particularly as regards the health and safety of workers; at the request of the European Parliament, the Commission has undertaken to set aside for renewable energy sources 60 % of the funding earmarked for non-nuclear energy.
Conclusion of the three Vice-Presidents who are permanent members of the Conciliation Committee:
The Commission submits a proposal to the European Parliament and the Council which it may modify as long as it has not been adopted.
Parliament gives its opinion and forwards it to the Council, with or without amendments.
The Council may adopt the proposed act by a qualified majority if it approves all Parliament's amendments or if Parliament has proposed no amendments.
The Commission submits a proposal to Parliament and Council.
An explanation of this kind is particularly useful now that the Amsterdam Treaty has significantly increased the number of policy areas which will in future fall within the codecision procedure.
Parliament gives its opinion and forwards it to the Council, with or without amendments.
After receiving the opinion, the Council adopts a 'common position'.
This position is communicated to Parliament for a second reading.
h is noticeable that compromises are already being found on a wide range of issues even before the conciliation.
Within three months, Parliament: • does not take a decision • approves the common position • amends the common position
Parliament may also indicate its intention to reject the common position.
Parliament may reject the Council's position by an absolute majority of its Members.
After a few teething troubles, with occasional confrontations between Council and Parliament, this procedure has moved gradually closer to the spirit of the Maastricht Treaty, reflecting an attempt to reach a 'common accord' with the assistance of the Commission.
Otherwise, the Council has three months to approve all the amendments and adopt the act definitively.
The Commission submits a proposal.
Parliament gives its opinion and forwards it to the Council, with or without amendments.
After receiving the opinion, the Council adopts a 'common position'.
This position is communicated to Parliament for a second reading.
The expanded codecision procedure, which is more efficient, quicker and more transparent, will help achieve this goal.
Within three months, Parliament: • does not take a decision • approves the common position • amends the common position
Parliament may also indicate its intention to reject the common position.
Parliament may reject the Council's position by an absolute majority of its Members.
If, within three months, the Council has not approved all Parliament's amendments, the Conciliation Committee is convened as quickly as possible, within six weeks.
The Conciliation Committee is composed of members of the Council and an equal number of representatives of Parliament.
Its task is to reach agreement on a joint text, on the bash of the Council's common position and Parliament's amendments.
If the Committee reaches agreement within six weeks, the 'joint text' may be adopted by Parliament, by a simple majority, and by the Council, by a qualified majority.
If the Committee does not reach agreement, the act is not adopted.
If the 'joint text' is not approved by one of these two institutions, the act is not adopted.
However, the Council may confirm its position within six weeks.
Parliament can then reject the text only by an absolute majority of its Members.
This 'confirmation'option, known as the 'third reading', was abolished by the Amsterdam Treaty.
