Taking nature into account
;ing nature into account also means taking account of everything involving people, whether at work or during their leisure time, in terms of the use of land, air and water, particularly energy and natural resources.
Development should be sustainable.
In 1994, the European Parliament called for a rethink, when countries were estimating their gross domestic product (GDP), so as to take into consideration the environmental costs of economic and industrial activities.
It stressed the need to estimate the 'non-environment costs' for the European Union.
The system of national accounts, which was devised in 1947 and is still used to calculate GDP, should be brought up to date to include external environmental costs (European Parliament resolutions: O) C 77, 14.3.1994)
'Taking nature into account makes it clear to everyone how much he has to pay, when and for what purpose.'
'We know today that we can consume nature free of charge, but not without consequences.
Today nature is presenting us with its own bills: floods and droughts, landslips and avalanches, barren land and decimated habitats for humans and animals have a hidden, almost incalculable cost. '
