1 he Joint Assembly has actively contributed to the imple­mentation and strengthening of successive ACP-EU conventions.
Its influence has been decisive in a number of fields:
• Maintaining the ACP preference and aid for the least developed countries;
• Taking into account differing degrees of development and the local economy;
JOINT
• Devehping human resources; commercial
• Promoting regional, political and cooperation;
• Integrating environmental projects;
ASSEMBLY
• Improving measures to combat epidemics and improve health and hygiene services; dimension
• Emphasising the importance of the cultural in North-South cooperation;
• Accelerating aid procedures and increasing funds earmarked for refugees and, for the first time, displaced persons;
• Establishing a conflict-prevention system;
• Strengthening the commitment to the respect for and the defence of human rights and human dignity;
• Upgrading the role of women in the development process;
• Closer cooperation with non-govern­mental organisations active in the development field;
• Introducing annual meetings betiveen the economic and social partners.
ACP-EU JOINT ASSEMBLY
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
COMPOSITION AND FUNCTION
ACP-EU JOINT ASSEMBLY the various developing countries that are members of the Lomé Convention.
1 he representatives of the 71 ACP States, most of whom (80 % as against 50 % 10 years ago) are elected, meet in plenary session for one week twice a year with 71 Members of the European Parbament.
The Joint Assembly meets alternately in an ACP country and an EU country.
It is governed by rules of procedure based on parity and democracy.
1 he Joint Assembly's work, therefore, goes far beyond matters of purely economic interest and deals with more fundamental development aims and peaceful relations between peoples.
.Dorn out of a joint desire to bring together the directly elected representatives of the European Community, Members of the European Parbament, and the representatives and elected members of the African, Caribbean and Pacific States ('ACP countries') that are signatories to the Lomé Convention, the ACP-EU Joint Assembly has deve loped into an institution unique in the world.
It is the only international assembly where the representatives of different countries regularly gather together with the aim of promoting North-South interdependence.
An irreplaceable instrument for democratic exchanges, debates and deliberation, the Joint Assembly devotes a major part of its work to promoting human rights and democracy and the common values of humanity.
1 he proceedings are chaired by two co-presidents elected by the assembly.
Twenty-four vice-presidents (12 European, 12 ACP), also elected by the assembly, together with the two co-presidents, form the Joint Assembly's Bureau.
The bureau meets several times a year to provide continuity for the Joint Assembly's work and prepares new initiatives aimed particularly at strengthening and improving cooperation.
It also discusses topical pobtical questions and adopts positions on all matters connected with respect for human rights and democracy in the 86 signatory countries to the Lomé Convention.
and defend the democratic process with a view to gua ranteeing the rights of peoples to determine for them selves their development objectives and the methods of achieving them.
