United in democracy
decision with the Council in a number of important areas; • to adopt the EU budget, after shaping it with the Council; • to exercise democratic control over the work of the European Union institutions.
Know your Europe
The Court of Justice is the institution which interprets the legal side of European Union business, particularly with regard to the way the Treaties and Union legislation are applied.
The Court is currently made up of 15 judges and sits in Luxembourg.
Following the Single European Act, a preliminary chamber, the Court of First Instance, was set up.
Court of Auditors
Parliament's budgetary powers are undoubtedly its most extensive.
The 15-member Court of Auditors was set up in 1975 to oversee the management of die European Communitys budget.
Parliament may of its own accord broach any European issue by means of what is called a European Parliament 'own-initiative' report.
All of these contain Members from various countries, and each group dierefore represents European rather than exclusively national interests.
MEPs or parties that do not want to form part of any political group sit in Parliament as 'non-attached' Members.
The political groups organize their work during the week before Parliament meets in plenary session, in order to achieve a consensus amongst the viewpoints of the national delegations within each group so as to present a united front in the debates.
Parliament's standing committees are the arteries that carry Parliament's lifeblood.
They discuss the wotking documents that will become the reports voted on subsequently in plenary.
The number of committees has increased as the Union's activity has been extended to new fields.
Temporary committees to deal with specific subjects may also be set up.
For example, a temporary committee on employment was established at the beginning of the current legislature.
In addition to these institutions, the European Union includes bodies such as the Economic and Social Committee, the European Investment Bank, the European Monetary Institute and the Committee of the Regions.
PARLIAMENT'S COMPLEX MACHINERY
A PARLIAMENT FOR EUROPE
Committee members are elected by the political groups on a proportional basis, so as to ensure that all groups are represented.
The meetings are normally open, and public hearings with experts or national civil servants can be otganized.
Ministers, Commissioners and other officials may appear before these committees to give their opinions and make proposals.
THE EUROPEAN INSTITUTIONS
In the sphere of foreign relations, Parliament exercises a genuine power ofratification, giving its assent to accession and association agreements.
The Treaty on European Union has also extended this procedure to cover the unifotm electoral law, the Structural Funds and European citizenship.
This undoubtedly strengthens Parliament's powets.
Parliament has, for example, appointed a European Ombudsman to whom the citizens of the Union have the right to appeal.
Citizens also enjoy the right to appeal directly to Patliament through its Committee on Petitions.
The European Parliament has three places of work: Luxembourg, Brussels and Strasbourg.
Parliament's permanent seat has been fixed in Strasbourg, where it meets each month in ordinary plenary sessions for one week.
Additional plenary sessions are held in Brussels.
The committees and political groups meet in Brussels.
Parliament's Secretariat is based in Luxembourg.
Country by country, Parliament's make-up today is as follows:
Members 21 25
Parliament's wotk is organized by the Bureau - the President of Parliament and the 14 Vice-Presidents elected by the MEPs - in collaboration with the chaitmen of the politico groups.
All wotking documents and debates are translated and interpreted into the official languages of the Union.
Since 1979 the Members of the European Parliament have been elected by direct universal suffrage for a fiveyear term.
The Parliament is now in its fourth legislative term (1994-99), and conists of 626 Members.
It exercises political control, helps to frame of European Union laws and possesses budgetary powers.
In other words, it exercises democratic control over the general running of the Union.
The European Union is currently made up of 15 democratic countries committed to a policy of unification.
They form the largest voluntary and peaceful bloc in the world - 370 million European citizens facing together the challenges of our time.
The principal means whereby Parliament exercises democratic control are in its powers to 'invest' and to censure the Commission.
Othet forms of control exist, such as written and oral questions to the Commission and Council, debates on the programmes and results of each Presidency of the Council of the Union, and the power to set up committees of inquiry.
Finally, as the democratic assembly of the European Union, Parliament may appeal to the Court against the Council or the Commission on grounds of their failure to act.
The goals of the European Union include:
THE POLITICAL GROUPS
Council
By voting in the European elections, the citizens of Europe take a direct part in the running of the European Union, which plays an ever-increasing role in our daily lives.
MEPs represent almost 100 European political parties. At European Union level, however, this vast specttum of political opinion is condensed into the following large 'political groups' which, on the basis of ideological affinities between their members, represent the major divisions in European politics.
POWERS AND RESPONSIBILITIES
• the defence of freedom, democracy, respect for human rights, fundamental freedoms and the rule of law, • the creation of an economic and monetary union, including a stable, single currency, • solidarity between the Union's peoples, • the promotion of social and economic progress, • the establishment of a common citizenship, • the development of a common foreign and security policy, • cooperation in the fields of justice and home affairs.
The Member States' government ministers for each specific area (agriculture, finance, transport, etc.) make up the Council of Ministers of the European Union.
They normally meet in Brussels.
The Council of Ministers has the final say on whether or not Union legislation is adopted.
The European Council is the summit - held at least twice a year - at which the Heads of State or Covernment meet to discuss what political steps are to be taken in connection with major European Union issues, and to establish guidelines for economic and political action.
The thtee fundamental powers of the European Parliament are: • to take part in the EU's legislative activity, by virtue of its right to be consulted and to deliver opinions.
The consultative role assigned to Parliament by the Treaty of Rome was extended by the Single European Act in July 1987, which introduced the legislative cooperation procedure with the Council.
This entides Patliament to introduce amendments to the directives establishing the single market.
The Treaty on European Union confers on Parliament a power of legislative co-
The Commission is the Union's executive institution.
It currently has 20 Members who are appointed for five years.
The Commission is run on a collegiate basis, independently of the Membet States.
In addition to the committees themselves, there are also subcommittees - on Security and Disarmament, Human Rights and Monetary Affairs.
Finally, with a view to fostering interparliamentary relations, the European Parliament also has interparliamentary delegations to parliaments throughout the world. Parliament also takes part in the ACP-EU Joint Assembly, an institution set up under the Lomé Convention.
In order to achieve its goals, the European Union has at its disposal the following institutions: European Parliament Council of Ministers of the European Union European Commission Court of Justice European Court ofAuditors. Auditors.
