The European Parliament is your voice in Europe
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT AND THE DEFENCE OF HUMAN RIGHTS
The European Union is founded on the principles of liberty, democracy, respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms and the rule of law, principles which are common to the Member States.
The Union shall respect fundamental rights, as guaranteed by the European Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms signed in Rome on 4 November 1950 and as they result from the con stitutional traditions common to the Member States, as general principles of Community law.
(Article 6, Treaty on European Union)
The Council, meeting in the composition of the Heads of State or Government and acting by unanimity on a proposal by one third of the Member States or by the Commission and after obtaining the assent of the European Parliament, may determine the existence of a serious and persistent breach by a Member State of principles mentioned in Article 6(1), after inviting the government of the Member State in question to submit its observations.
(Article 7, Treaty on European Union)
The Union shall define and implement a common foreign and security policy covering all areas of foreign and security policy, the objectives of which shall be: - to safeguard the common values, fundamental interests, indepen dence and integrity of the Union in conformity with the principles of the United Nations Charter; - to strengthen the security of the Union in all ways; - to preserve peace and strengthen international security, in accor dance with the principles of the United Nations Charter, as well as the principles of the Helsinki Final Act and the objectives of the Paris Charter, including those on external borders; - to promote international cooperation; - to develop and consolidate democracy and the rule of law, and respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.
(Article 11, Treaty on European Union) m m
ROLE OF THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT
Some of these provisions in the revised Treaty on European Union are new.
The European Parliament's commitment to promote respect for human rights is not.
Although the founding EC Treaties made little explicit reference to human rights, the Treaty revisions of the Single European Act, Maastricht and most recently Amsterdam, have made the promotion of human rights and democracy a major objective of the European Union.
Parliament can take much of the credit for this.
It is the institution which has taken the leading role in pressing for the development of EU human rights policies and in keeping human rights at the forefront of the EU agenda.
The EP's concern for human rights stretches back a long way.
Even in its early years, it debated the implications for EC law of the European Convention on Human Rights.
By the 1970s, it was already breaking the 'rules' of parliamentary diplomacy by raising human rights problems with delegations from third countries and by adopting resolutions denouncing human rights abuses.
Prior to the direct elections of 1979, the Soviet psychiatric dissident Vladimir Bukovsky attended a plenary session of Parliament to thank the institution publicly for its support in helping to bring about his release into exile in Britain.
But it was after the first direct elections that the European Parliament began to prioritise human rights issues - largely in response to appeals from citizens both within and outside the EU
and from the rapidly growing number of non-governmental organisations dealing with human rights throughout the world.
In 1980, Parliament's Political Affairs Committee established a Human Rights Working Group which in 1984 became the Human Plights Subcommittee.
The provisions on home affairs and justice in the Maastricht Treaty led Parliament in 1992 to establish a Committee on Civil Liberties and Internal Affairs with a remit for human rights issues within the EU.
Rome, November 1950 : signature of the European Convention on Human Rights
HUMAN RIGHTS IN THE EU
One of the most significant developments of the Treaty of Amsterdam has been for certain matters in the field of home affairs and justice previously decided intergovernmentally by the EU to be moved to Community competence.
This will give the EP in some cases, after a transitional period, the power of co-decision with the Council on certain significant issues affecting the individual rights of EU citizens.
These are issues which transcend national borders; for example, how best to regulate new technologies to ensure that they do not undermine respect for human rights.
The decisions being taken will have an impact on us all.
In social policy matters, the Amsterdam Treaty and other develop ments have opened up the prospects for various policies designed to promote economic and social human rights within the Union.
These are currently central'concerns for the EP, as are how to respond to issues such as racism and xenophobia, religious intolerance and the treatment of refugees and immigrant workers.
EUROPEAN CONVENTION ON HUMAN RIGHTS AND FUNDAMENTAL FREEDOMS
AU EU Member States are bound by the European Convention on Human Rights adopted in 1950 under the aegis of the Council of Europe, which predated the European Community.
The Convention is the main text of reference in international human rights law for the EU and its Member States, together with the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the UN human rights covenants and conventions which have followed.
The EP and the Commission have called for the European Community as such to adhere formally to the European Convention on Human Rights - a proposal which is still under discussion.
The EP has gone further: in 1989, it called for the enactment of a 'Bill of Rights' for citizens of the European Union, on the basis of a Declaration of Fundamental Rights and Freedoms which had been drawn up by Parliament.
This proposal also remains on the table.
STRAIGHT TALK
In its debates, parliamentary questions and resolutions, the EP frequently raises human rights in a direct and forceful manner.
Responses from governments indicate that there is a very definite sensitivity to the EP's criticisms.
On occasion, EP resolutions are used as a basis for a démarche by the Council of Ministers.
These resolutions can have a direct effect.
In 1996, a citizen of Pakistan attributed his release from detention to the adoption of an EP resolution.
More often, EP actions involve cases where clearly the EP is just one of many voices publicly pleading the cause of a particular individual or group.
Examples of cases where pressure by the international community and the EU have had an effect in recent times are the release of Chinese dissident Wei Jingsheng, the 'Ogoni 19' in Nigeria and the Filipino maid, Sara Balabagan, in the United Arab Emirates.
SILENT DIPLOMACY
In other instances, the EP acts more discreetly.
Three releases of long-term political prisoners in a North African country were attributed by Amnesty International directly to EP intervention.
Two other releases of prominent human rights advocates were secured
by EP pressure in another African country.
In Afghanistan, a member of an EP fact-finding delegation negotiated the release of two Soviet army deserters being held by the Mujehaddin in appalling conditions and brought them back to safety in Europe.
As a result of EP pressure, a non-EU country decided not to send back to Yugoslavia an army deserter who had refused to commit a war crime, thus setting a precedent for similar pending cases.
In earlier years, interventions by presidents of Parliament helped to resolve a number of East-West family reunification cases.
Personal contacts in Moscow by a chairman of the Human Rights Subcommittee secured the release into exile of a number of Jewish refuseniks; also resolved in Russia were some cases of conscientious objectors.
Often EP delegations hand over lists of political prisoners in meetings with their counterparts in other countries.
This can sometimes lead to a positive result.
The dissident Anna Chertkova was released from a psychiatric hospital in the USSR after her case had been raised by a delegation of MEPs in a meeting with Soviet representatives.
The raising of two cases of political detainees in Angola during a meeting of the ACP-EU Joint Assembly brought about their swift release.
An EP delegation to Sudan managed to secure the release of a number of prisoners of conscience, though some were later re arrested.
Some time later, one of those former prisoners fled to Europe where his status as a political refugee was called into question.
He had the good fortune to find that an MEP who had been with the EP delegation in Sudan lived in his area and was in a position to vouch for him.
Cases can also be raised by individual MEPs or by political groups.
The release to the West of Irina Grivnina, for instance, followed a sustained campaign by Parliament's Liberal Group, backed by a resolution of Parliament calling on the Soviet authorities to allow her and her family to emigrate.
SANCTIONS
It is largely as a result of Parliament's pressure that most new EU agreements with third countries now contain a standard human rights clause as an 'essential element', which can lead to the suspension of the agreement in the event of gross violations of human rights.
Parliament has also urged and supported sanctions against countries which have persistently abused human rights.
In recent years, the EU has applied sanctions of different kinds against, among others, Nigeria, Sudan, Togo, Burundi, Equatorial Guinea and the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia.
Parliament has strongly supported action against the systematic use of forced labour in Burma and successfully urged the Commission to act on complaints lodged by trade union organisations to bring about the suspension of preferential trading agreements.
Political dialogue is increasingly important in the EU's relations with all its partners, where most agreements with other countries have gone beyond mere trade and aid.
Respect for human rights, consolidation of democracy, respect for the rule of law, good governance, participation, fair development centred on the reduction of poverty and conflict prevention, all lie at the heart of the dialogue between the EU and the African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) countries.
Indeed, they are among the most vigorously debated issues when Parliament meets with its counterparts from the ACP countries in their main political forum, the ACP-EU Joint Assembly.
It is also thought to be no coincidence that in the period before Parliament was required to ratify the EU Customs Agreement with Turkey in 1995, there was some liberalisation of the penal code and the early release of a large number of political detainees.
One of those individuals in Turkey whose prison term was shortened was the former mayor of Diyarbakir, Mehdi Zana, who had been sentenced for testifying about the situation of the Kurds in south­east Turkey to the EP's Human Rights Subcommittee.
PUBLIC HEARINGS
The EP has often provided a platform for human rights advocates to state their case while at the same time inviting the governments they criticise to put their position.
The EP was the first parliament in Europe to enable the Dalai Lama to address an official meeting on Tibet, despite strong protests from the government of China.
At a public hearing on East Timor, the EP heard not only Dr Ramos Horta, the political spokesman of the independence movement and subsequent winner of the Nobel Peace Prize, but also representatives of the Indonesian government.
A public hearing on human rights in Romania led directly to the suspension of negotiations for the renewal of the EC agreement with that country.
A public hearing on human rights in Algeria heard both official and Opposition' views and led to the start of a 'breakthrough' political dialogue at parliamentary level both in Algeria and in Strasbourg.
More recently, delegations representing the two main opposing political currents among Kosovo Albanians were persuaded to meet together in public at the EP to debate their differences openly.
POSITIVE MEASURES
In general, however, the EP has laid less emphasis on sanctions than on positive measures to encourage the development of civil society, the promotion of human rights and the consolidation of the rule of law.
The EP has provided funding in the EU budget for a wide range of programmes such as the rehabilitation of street children, prevention of violence against women, reform of the judiciary, training of security and poÜce services, civic education and the development of indepen dent media.
One such programme arose from an EP anti-torture initiative which led to EU support for an international network dedicated to the rehabilitation of torture victims around the world.
EU financial support for this initiative is in fact greater than the UN voluntary fund for victims of torture.
The EU provides the largest source of funding for human rights and democracy projects in Europe, supported from a separate chapter in the budget which was specifically created by the EP.
In 1995, Parliament successfully reversed a Council proposal to cut expenditure on human rights and democracy by half.
This intervention ensured the continuation of many programmes designed to support the under privileged in some of the world's poorest countries and regions.
The EP has laid much stress on helping - often with small grants, 'seed money' - grassroots organisations which can provide a springboard for programmes which sometimes develop into major initiatives.
At Parliament's initiative, specific democracy programmes have been established for these regions.
In helping to consolidate the emerging democracies of eastern and central Europe, Parliament has paid particular attention to the situation of minorities.
In seeking to promote democracy, the EP attaches particular importance to observing and monitoring elections, on occasion bringing about a change in EU policy if an election is found to be flawed.
SPECIFIC INITIATIVES
The EP has long been formally opposed to the death penalty.
It has called for the abolition of the death penalty to be a criterion for accession to the EU.
The current international campaign for a UN moratorium on the death penalty can be traced back to an initiative by the European Parliament.
The EP was an early supporter of the creation of an international criminal court, adopting a resolution to that effect in April 1994.
It welcomed the eventual adoption of a statute setting up the International Criminal Court in Rome in July 1998.
At the same time, it called for steps to ensure that the Court could act decisively.
Despite the reservations of some EU Member States, it also succeeded in helping to fund the International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia in The Hague in the early stages when the Tribunal's resources were scarce.
Women's rights have increasingly become an integral part of Parliament's human rights concerns.
Largely as a result of efforts by Parliament's Committee on Women's Rights, there have been moves to bring gender issues into the mainstream of EU policies.
One of the European Parliament's most important initiatives is an EU-wide campaign to combat violence against women.
Awarenessraising and practical projects to help victims are at the centre of this campaign.
The Commission has proposed funding €25 million for this initiative in its Daphne action programme for the period 2000-04.
One major target of the campaign is trafficking in human beings, where the victims include children and young ,:ş»^S people as well as women.
Another Parliament initiative sought to highlight the plight of women in Afghanistan, which led to International Women's Day in 1998 being celebrated with the slogan of 'A flower for the women of Kabul'.
The EP also has appointed a mediator who has helped to resolve 'tug­of­love' cases of children who have been abducted across fron­tiers.
This appointment dates back to the successful mediation by a former president of Parliament in a number of cases involving European women married to Algerian men.
As a result of this action, conducted in cooperation with the Algerian and French authorities but bypassing normal diplomatic channels, a number of children were reunited with their mothers.
Each year, the EP honours an individual or organisation with its human rights prize for freedom of thought.
The prize is named after Andrei Sakharov, the Soviet dissident, nuclear physicist and Nobel Peace Prize winner who gave his blessing to an EP prize bearing his name in clandestine contacts with Parliament in the 1980s.
At that time, he was living in internal exile in Gorky.
The prize has been awarded to: Nelson Mandela and Anatoli Marchenko (1988); Alexander Dubcek (1989); Aung San Suu Kyi (1990); Adem Demaçi (1991); the mothers of the Plaza de Mayo (1992); Oslobodjenje (1993);TaslimaNasreen (1994); Leyla Zana (1995); Wei Jingsheng (1996); Salima Ghezali (1997); and Ibrahim Rugova (1998).
Sadly Aung San Suu Kyi has still to regain her freedom as a political leader despite her landslide election victory in Burma in 1990 and Leyla Zana has recently seen her prison sentence extended by the Turkish authorities.
TOWARDS THE NEW MILLENNIUM
The EP is currently setting its priorities for the new century.
One of these is to combat the exploitation of children, whether as a source of cheap labour, as prostitutes or as child soldiers.
The EP also wants the EU and its Member States to coordinate their individual efforts more closely.
This will require more systematic human rights reporting by EU and Member State diplomats, and better ways of assessing the impact of human rights initiatives.
Parliament has called for more transparency and accountability in the European Union's expenditure on human rights.
In 1998, the EP and the international community marked the 50th anniversary of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, reaffirming its commitment to the indivisibility of human dignity.
At Parliament's plenary session to mark this anniversary, the President of Parliament called for a 'Millennium Amnesty' for prisoners of conscience before the year 2000.
This call was taken up by Parliament as a whole in an adopted resolution.
It is a plea that may fall on deaf ears but the EP has always felt that if its actions result in just one release or in improved conditions for even one political prisoner, its efforts will have been worthwhile.
