FOR
EUROPE pages 4/5
Women in Europe pages 6/7
An opportunity for women: the European Parliament pages 8/9
Women's rights recognised by the European Union pages 10/11 The European institutions demonstrate their commitment pages 12/13 Working together to achieve equal opportunities pages 14/15 Action through Community programmes pages 16/17 Publicising and asserting women's rights
WOMEN
More than half the 375 million citizens of the European Union are women and since the 1920s, when most European countries gave women the vote, their role in political and economic life has grown considerably.
More women than ever before in the EU are engaged in paid work and gaining increasing social recognition and personal independence.
Daily life, however, does not always reflect this situation.
Despite the fact that equal treatment is one of the fundamental principles of democracy, women do not yet occupy a position of complete equality with men in areas such as employment, pay, in-service training, work organisation, access to positions of responsibility, respect for dignity and representation in public life.
Many women encounter the same types of discrimination: unemployment for example, particularly long-term unemployment, affects them more than it does men.
At work, they very often occupy inferior positions although on average they do better than men at school.
Through their commitment, the women of Europe can help to change such situations for the better.
Pages 4/5
FOR WOMEN:
EUROPEAN
AN OPPORTUNITY PARLIAMENT
The European Parliament is the only European institution elected by direct universal suffrage every five years.
Women, as voters or as elected Members of the European Parliament, play an essential part in building a fairer society, based on equal opportunities and rights.
The 626 elected representatives scrutinise, change and approve draft European directives.
The European Parliament therefore matters to all women, whatever their circumstances, lifestyle or job.
It opens up new ways of dealing with everyday problems in such areas as food, informative labelling on consumer products, consumer protection, the safety of children's toys, air and water quality, combating pollution and reducing and recycling industrial and household waste.
But it has a broader commitment as well: fighting all forms of sex-based discrimination at work and in social life.
The European Parliament is the voice of Europe's citizens: each election gives women the opportunity to make their voices heard.
Pages 6/7
RECOGNISED
BY THE
RIGHTS UNION
The EU has taken women's aspirations into account ever since its inception and has done much to ensure that the principle of equal treatment is recognised.
Even though women's rights still meet with obstacles in practice, the EU's founding treaties have gradually enshrined them in European law.
In 1957, the Treaty of Rome obliged each
In 1976, a European directive prohibited more generally any form of sex-based discrimination.
In 1982, the first action programme on equal opportunities for women was launched.
In 1992, the directive on the protection of pregnant women at work was adopted.
Thus the equal rights principle, which started as a simple provision in the Treaty of Rome aiming to establish the concept of equal pay for equal work, has widened and developed into a substantial programme of equal opportunities in every area.
The Amsterdam Treaty has laid down a new legal basis for women's rights.
The EU now has the task of eliminating inequality, combating the obstacles which prevent women from playing their full part as citizens and promoting measures which create true equality: equal treatment and pay, training and education, recruitment conditions and employment, new forms of work organisation, health protection, combating unemployment, parental leave, better access to positions of responsibility, establishing more compatibility between working and home life, and respect for every individual's image and dignity.
Finally, after the Treaties of Rome, Maastricht and Amsterdam and the adoption of directives on equal opportunities, the Court of Justice of the European Union has on several occasions delivered judgments on specific cases, thereby guaranteeing recognition and respect for these rights.
Pages 8/9
THE EUROPEAN INSTITUTIONS THEIR COMMITMENT
The Members of the European Parliament have consistently played an active role to ensure recognition for women's rights and enshrine them in the Union's fundamental legal principles.
Since 1979, the European Parliament has been elected by universal suffrage; over 20 years, the number of women members has greatly increased, from 71 to 167, almost 27 % of the total number of MEPs.
This demonstrates women's growing interest in political life and an increasing political awareness in the search for a new balance.
In 1984, Parliament set up a Committee on Women's Rights.
This committee considers proposals for directives affecting women's work and also promotes equal rights and opportunities in all aspects of life, so that all forms of discrimination can be eliminated.
In 1998, Parliament adopted a report entitled '1999: Year of zero tolerance of violence against women' which resulted in an action programme carried out with help from a network of associations and non-governmental organisations.
Parliament votes on the budget of the Union which funds, for example, programmes for continuous vocational training or grants for studying in other countries. Through its financial procedure, it ensures that equal opportunities are systematically taken into account in every
EU measure, thus clearly putting its political guidelines into practice.
The Commission and the European Parliament have also promoted respect for the principle of equality between women and men in their reform of the EU Structural Funds, which are among the essential tools of economic and social justice.
Projects financed by these funds (European
Regional Development Fund, European Social Fund and European Agricultural Fund) must state how the measures involved have contributed to developing equal opportunities.
Thanks to this new approach, the European Social Fund has been able to finance programmes for women without vocational qualifications or who have lost their jobs, or for long-term unemployed women.
The Commission has set up structures to deal with specific areas: the Women's information
Section disseminates information about EU policies and activities of benefit to women. The
Equal Opportunities Unit is responsible for monitoring legislation in this area and implementing the fourth Community action programme on equal opportunities for men and women (1996-2000).
WORKING TOGETHER TO ACHIEVE EQUAL
OPPORTUNITIES
The Commission cooperates with Parliament on a programme designed gradually to integrate the principle of equal treatment into every aspect of Community legislation.
The Commission reports periodically to Parliament on the progress made in the area of equal opportunities and on the obstacles encountered and solutions proposed.
Parliament also participated in the official delegation representing the EU at the United
Nations Fourth International Conference in Beijing on the advancement of women and what steps should be taken to bring this about.
A more integrated approach opens up new avenues for resolving social, political and cultural problems.
This is why promoting equal opportunities has been an acknowledged priority of the EU and its
To sum up, a body of European law has been gradually developed and specific structures put in place.
At the European Parliament, the Committee on Women's Rights and the Committee on Employment and Social Affairs occupy a central position.
However, achieving equal opportunities through the Community action programme concerns everyone.
The key to the programme's success is partnership among all those involved, be they individuals or organisations.
Pages 12/13
THROUGH
COMMUNITY
The fourth Community action programme was proposed and carried out by the Commission after consultation with Parliament.
The programme aims not only to adopt measures to assist women in certain areas but also, in a wider context, to tackle issues so as to take account of the situation of women in all
Community programmes and policies by incorporating equal opportunities into all aspects of society: this is known as mainstreaming.
This new approach affects, for example, the organisation of work, social security, the funding of care for the elderly and dependants and school opening hours.
The main objectives of the medium-term action programme are:
• to mobilise all those involved in economic and social life to promote equal opportunities;
• to promote equal opportunities in specific areas such as education, vocational training and access to the job market;
• to enable men and women to combine the demands of family life and work;
• to create social and cultural conditions conducive to equal treatment;
• to ensure balanced representation of women in the decision-making process.
The fourth Community action programme covers many areas: education and vocational training, access to employment and working conditions, equal pay, the organisation and flexibility of working life, the working environment and sexual harassment, encouraging entrepreneurship and disseminating information on equal opportunities.
For example, the Daphne programme aims to combat violence against women, adolescents and children by working together with a wide network of associations and organisations; similarly, the Stop programme aims to raise awareness and promote training among the police and judiciary.
With regard to education and training, there have been many initiatives: the Socrates programme gives priority to educational projects which take account of equal opportunities for men and women.
The Leonardo da Vinci programme promotes training programmes to encourage women to participate in areas where they are traditionally under-represented.
The EU's commitment to equality is demonstrated by its funding of programmes specifically for women and of NGOs, in particular women's associations and the two sides of industry.
Pages 14/15
AND ASSERTING
WOMEN'S
The principle of equality has been recognised in the EU's legal texts and then extended to other aspects of equal treatment.
Equality between women and men is a fundamental human right which cannot be fully exercised without balanced participation in all areas of life.
Being a female EU citizen brings with it the right to education, employment, social security, health at work, social protection during maternity leave and other benefits.
If, for example, you think that your rights to equal pay and treatment, equal social security benefits or parental leave have not been respected, you can take steps to ensure that they are, at both national and European level.
You can complain to the European Commission, send a petition to the European Parliament or contact a Member of the European Parliament who can put questions to the Commission and Council.
Building a Europe for women is a substantial democratic investment.
The EU is aware of their aspirations: women need Europe.
The European institutions have worked hard to ensure that women's rights are recognised and to set up structures to put them into practice in everyday life.
As a representative body, the European Parliament is an essential means of progress towards equality and social justice.
The European Parliament is your voice in Europe; make your voice heard by casting your vote.
Pages 16/17
