Incompatible status and dual mandates
Members of the European Parliament are prohibited from carrying on certain professional activities or holding certain other offices (for example judge, government minister, or director of a public corporation).
They also have to comply with national laws restricting or prohibiting dual mandates.
Members of the European Parliament
How do you become a Member of the European Parliament?
Common democratic rules apply everywhere, especially the right to vote at the age of 18, equality between men and women and a secret ballot.
In some Member States, such as Belgium, Luxembourg and Greece, voting is compulsory.
A common statute
Members of the European Parliament receive the same parliamentary salary as members of their national parliament.
The salary is paid by each Member State.
MEPs are also eligible for allowances to cover the costs incurred in the performance of their duties and the recruitment of assistants.
These allowances are paid by the European Parliament and may be cut if members do not take part regularly in the work of the plenary session.
At Parliament's request, provision was made in the Amsterdam Treaty for a common statute for all Members of the European Parliament.
Composition of the European Parliament
The number of Members per State is laid down by the Treaties (the UK has 85, Ireland 15).
In the chamber, Members sit in political groups, not in national delegations.
Parliament currently has eight political groups, plus some 'non-attached' Members.
These political groups include members from over 100 national political parties.
Chairs of the political groups
Heidi Hautala and Paul Lannoye
Group of the Greens/European Free Alliance (Greens/EFA)
Gianfranco Dell'Alba (chairman) Francesco Speroni and Charles de Gaulle (co-chairs) Technical Group of Independent Members - Mixed Group (TDI)
Croup of the Party of European Socialists (PES)
Confederal Group of the European Jens-Peter Bonde (acting), United Left/Nordic Green Left Jean Saint-Josse and (EUL/NGL) Hans Blokland (co-chairs)
Pat Cox Group of the European Liberal, Democrat and Reformist Party (ELDR)
In 1979, women made up 16.5 % of the European Parliament.
This figure has grown steadily over successive parliamentary terms, climbing to 27.5 % on 1 January 1996 and almost 30 % following the 1999 elections.
Source: DC I, 23.3.2000.
The work of the Members of the European Parliament
Members of the European Parliament meet in plenary session for one week a month in Strasbourg where the European Parliament has its seat.
The parliamentary committees generally meet for two weeks a month in Brussels, for ease of contact with the European Commission and Council.
The third week is set aside for meetings of the political groups and the fourth for the plenary sitting in Strasbourg.
Parliament also holds additional plenary sittings in Brussels.
The secretariat is located in Luxembourg.
Simultaneous interpretation of all parliamentary and committee debates is provided in the Union's 11 official languages: Danish, Dutch, English, Finnish^ French, German, Greek, Italian, Portuguese, Spanish and Swedish.
Similarly, all official parliamentary documents are translated into and published in these 11 languages.
Although ¡ts seat is in Strasbourg, the European Parliament, for historical reasons, has three places of work.
The European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC) was established in Luxembourg in 1 952, while the European Atomic Energy Community (EAEC), often called Euratom, and the European Economic Community (EEC) were established mainly in Brussels in 1958.
Strasbourg first became the seat of the Council of Europe, and then in 1 952, the Common Assembly of the ECSC met there to symbolise Franco-German reconciliation.
The governments of the Member States meeting in Edinburgh (1992) and the Amsterdam Treaty (1997) formalised the location of the institutions in three places.
This is the procedure for the adoption of legislation, which may require two readings, as in the case of the codecision procedure (see the glossary).
