THE PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEES
Know your Europe
The Court of justice is the institution which interprets the legal side of European Union business, particularly with regard to the way the Treaties and Union legislation are applied.
The Court is currently made up of 15 judges and sits in Luxembourg.
Following the Single European Act, a preliminary chamber, the Court of First Instance, was set up.
Parliament's budgetary powers are undoubtedly its most extensive.
Parliament may amend the draft budget; it has the last word on what is called non­compulsory expenditure; it approves the Commission's management of the budget; it rejects or adopts the budget itself, and the signature of the President of Parliament is required for the adopted budget to come into force.
Court of Auditors
The 15 Member Court of Auditors was set up in 1975 to verify management of the European Community's budget.
Parliament may of its own accotd broach any European issue by means of what is called a European Parliament 'own­initiative' report.
Nearly all of these contain Members from various countries, and each group therefore represents European rather than exclusively national interests.
MEPs or parties that do not want to form pan of any political group participate in Parliament as 'non­attached' Members.
The political groups organize theit work during the week before Parliament meets in plenary session, in order to achieve a consensus amongst the view­pints of the national delegations within each group so as to ptesent a united front in the debates.
Parliament's standing committees are the arteries that carry Parliament's lifeblood. They discuss the working documents that will become the reports voted on subsequently in plenary.
The number of committees has increased as the Union's activity has been extended to new fields.
Over and above these institutions, the European Union includes bodies such as the Economic and Social Committee, the European Investment Bank, the European Monetary Institute and the Committee of the Regions.
