On the wider front of world affairs, the European Parliament makes its opinions known by adopting resolutions on problems of international politics and condemning human rights violations.
Parliament acts as both the voice and the conscience of Europe, and seeks to use diplomatic contacts to find solutions to conflicts and to combat injustice.
Since 1979 the Members of the European Parliament have been elected by direct universal suffrage for a five­year term.
The Parliament is now in its fourth legislative term (1994­99), and con:ists of 626 Members.
It exercises political control, cJces part in the framing of European Union laws and possesses budgetary powers.
In other words, it exercises democratic control over the general running of the Union.
The European Union is currently made up of 15 democratic countries committed to a policy of unification.
They form the largest voluntary and peaceful bloc in the world ­ 370 million European citizens facing together the challenges of our time.
The principal means whereby Parliament exercises democratic control are in its powers to 'invest' and to censure the Commission.
Othet forms of control exist, such as written and otal questions to the Commission and Council, debates on the programmes and results of each Presidency of the Council of the Union, and the power to set up committees of inquiry.
