THE PARLIAMENTARY COMMITTEES
Know your Europe
The Court of Justice is the institution which interprets the legal side of European Union business, particularly with regard to the way the Treaties and Union legislation are applied.
The Court is currently made up of 15 judges and sits in Luxembourg.
Following the Single European Act, a preliminary chamber, the Court of First Instance, was set up.
Parliament's budgetary powers are undoubtedly its most extensive.
Parliament may amend the draft budget; it has the last word on what is called non-compulsory expenditure; it approves the Commission's management of the budget; it rejects or adopts the budget itself, and the signature of the President of Parliament is required for the adopted budget to come into force.
Parliament's standing committees are the arteries that carry Parliament's lifeblood.
They discuss the working documents that will become the reports voted on subsequently in plenary.
The number of committees has increased as the Union's activity has been extended to new fields.
There are now 20 permanent committees.
Temporary committees to deal with specific subjects may also be set up.
For example, a tempotary committee on employment was established at the beginning of the current legislature.
In addition to these institutions, the European Union includes bodies such as the Economic and Social Committee, the European Investment Bank, the European Monetary Institute and the Committee of the Regions.
A PARLIAMENT FOR EUROPE
Committee members are elected by the political groups on a proportional basis, so as to ensure that all groups are represented.
The meetings are normally open, and public hearings with experts ot national civil servants can be organized.
Ministers, Commissioners and other officials may appear before these committees to give their opinions and make proposals.
In the sphere of foreign relations, Parliament exercises a genuine power of ratification, giving its assent to accession and association agreements.
The Treaty on European Union has also extended this procedure to cover the uniform electoral law, the Structural Funds and European citizenship.
This undoubtedly strengthens Parliament's powers.
Parliament has, for example, appointed a European Ombudsman to whom the citizens of the Union have the right to appeal.
THE EUROPEAN INSTITUTIONS
The European Parliament has three places of work: Luxembourg, Brussels and Strasbourg.
Parliament's permanent seat has been fixed in Sttasbourg, where it meets each month in ordinary plenary sessions for one week.
Additional plenary sessions are held in Brussels.
The committees and political groups meet in Brussels.
The European Union is made up of democratic States.
A democracy without a parliament is inconceivable; this is why the European Parliament was created. Country by country, Parliament's make-up today is as follows:
On the wider front of world affairs, the European Parliament makes its opinions known by adopting resolutions on problems of international politics and condemning human rights violations.
POLITICS AND ECONOMICS
Parliament's work is organized by the Bureau - the President of Parliament and the 14 Vice-Presidents elected by the MEPs - in collaboration with the chairmen of the political groups.
All working documents and debates are translated and interpreted into the official languages of the Union.
Since 1979 the Members of the European Parliament have been elected by direct universal suffrage for a fiveyear term.
The Parliament is now in its fourth legislative term (1994-99), and consists of 626 Members.
It exercises political control, helps to frame of European Union laws and possesses budgetary powers.
MEPs, Commissioners, Ministers from whichever country holds the presidency and, on occasion, EU
The European Union is currently made up of 15 democratic countries committed to a policy of unification.
They form the largest voluntary and peaceful bloc in the world - 370 million European citizens facing together the challenges of out time.
The principal means whereby Parliament exercises democratic control are in its powers to 'invest' and to censure the Commission.
Other forms of control exist, such as written and oral questions to the Commission and Council, debates on the programmes and results of each Presidency of the Council of the Union, and the power to set up committees of inquiry.
Finally, as the democratic assembly of the European Union, Parliament may appeal to the Court against the Council or the Commission on grounds of their failure to aa.
The European Union exercises powers in areas where its Member States have gradually ttansfetred them, not only in the economic sphere but also, since the Treaty on European Union, in the political sphere. The goals of the European Union include:
