If we want to reopen the whole subject, we shall probably have to go through a procedure which will take a couple of years longer to complete.
In compliance with toe principle of cooperation with staff, we shall have to enter into fresh consultations on this whole subject. The Commission needs to think
') Agenda for next sitting: see Minutes.
Debates of the European Parliament
I. Questions to toe Council
Question No 23 by Birgitta Ahlqvist (H-0031/97) Subject: Events in Belarus
The events which took place in Belarus in November 1996 showed that President Lukashenka was not prepared to compromise with the parliamentary opposition.
He effectively limited toe freedom of opinion.
His total control over the media gave him a monopoly over political news in rural areas, and he was thus able to rally the voters to his support.
On 1 November, I put a question to toe Council concerning its view of developments in Belarus.
The answer I received was that it would wait and see what the referendum at the end of the month brought.
Now we see the result: the President was not interested in compromise with the opposition and opted for an authoritarian solution.
How has the Council signalled its concern at the evolution of authoritarian presidential powers in Belarus?
How does it intend to proceed as regards, its relations with Belarus?
Answer
The evolution of the political situation in Belarus has brought the EU to use a number of diplomatic steps (demarches and EU statements) in the run-up to toe referendum.
After the referendum on a new Constitution carried out on 24 November 1996 the EU Troika, headed by Prime Minister Bruton, has conveyed the Union's grave concerns about toe evolution in Belarus in a meeting with President Lukashenko in the margins of the OSCE Summit of Lisbon.
The Union has at this occasion mainly stressed the necessity of respecting procedures foreseen in toe Belarus Constitution for modifying it and notably to respect the constitutional rights of the Belarusian Parliament; furthermore, the means of preparing and organising the referendum, like access to the media and full information for voters, as well as several provisions of toe Constitution submitted to the referendum, were put into question.
The Council pressed its proposal to send a fact finding mission to Belarus in order to obtain an objective understanding of the developments.
President Lukashenko eventually acceded to this request.
A delegation headed by a former Justice Minister of the Netherlands as Presidency and comprising constitutional experts and diplomats elected by the Presidency, Commission, OECD and Council of Europe visited Minsk from January 26-31.
It conducted some 60 interviews with government, opposition, media and other figures, and was received by President Lukashenko.
The mission's findings have been toe subject of two COEST Working Group meetings and will form toe basis for deliberations of toe General Affairs Council on February 24.
Furthermore, it must be stressed that since November 1996 contacts with opposition leaders and the representatives of the 'shadow' Parliament and its speaker as well as other leaders of the civil society have been constantly maintained.
Question No 24 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0038/97) Subject: Findings by a German court concerning Mrs Ciller's involvement in drugs trafficking
Article K.l of the Treaty on European Union states that toe responsibilities of toe Council of Ministers shall include cooperation '...for the purposes of preventing and combating...unlawful drug trafficking and other serious forms of international crime, including if necessary certain aspects of customs cooperation'.
Article K.5 provides that Member States shall defend the common positions adopted in these matters in international organizations and international conferences.
What measures does the Council intend to take in respect of the Turkish Government, in view of toe pubUc accusations made by the President and Judge of a Frankfurt court, in the wake of a major trial involving heroin trafficking by Turkish gangs, that the latter were operating under the protection of members of the Turkish Government, and in particular toe Turkish Foreign Minister, Tansu Ciller?
What specific measures does the Council intend to take to ensure that the Customs
Debates of the European Parliament
Union with Turkey does not lead to an increase in toe trafficking of drags from that country, particularly in the light of the involvement of members of toe Turkish Government in this trafficking?
Answer
The points mentioned by toe Honourable Member of Parliament have not been discussed in the framework of the Council.
Trafficking on Balkan routes is a major concern for the Council.
As an implementing measure of the Drugs plan adopted by the meeting of the European Council of Madrid a Joint Action was agreed under Irish Presidency concerning participation by the Member States of the European Union in the strategic operation planned by the Customs Cooperation Council (CCC) to combat drug smuggling on toe Balkan route. -
Moreover the Council at its meeting of 28 / 29 November 1996 took note of the report of thé European Drugs Unit on drug trafficking activities by Turkish criminal organisations from an EU perspective.
The operational followup to recommendations in this report is being discussed in competent bodies of the Council.
Question No 25 by Claude Desama (H-0039/97)
Subject: Use of regional aid for relocation purposes
The Belgian undertaking Boston Scientific Europe which is located in an Objective 2 area is to be transferred to an Objective 1 area in heland.
This case of relocation clearly reveals serious flaws in the European system of aid which encourages competition between regions in decline.
It also shows the urgent need for a fiscal harmonization policy in Europe.
How does toe Dutch Presidency intend to address this problem?
Answer
As the Honourable Member knows, aid under the Structural Funds is governed by toe regulations adopted by the Council in July 1993.
By toe terms of these same regulations, toe CouncU entrusted the management of toe Structural Funds to the Commission. Consequently, it is no longer appropriate for the Council to comment on the way the Funds are implemented or the consequences.
In regard to a fiscal harmonization policy as mentioned by the Honourable Member, I would remind him that the Dublin European Summit took due note of the Commission's report on developments in the European Union's tax systems, stressing toat work should continue in this area.
The summit welcomed the Commission's intention to continue discussions within a working party on fiscal policy.
Question No 26 by Sören Wibe (H-0042/97)
Subject: Egg industry aid which is distorting competition
Sweden's egg producers have been hard hit since the country joined the EU, toe reason being that Finnish egg producers receive aid (so far authorized for the period 1 January 1995 to 31 December 1999) which is paid directly to them from the Finnish state.
The aid was actually intended to help cut production and eliminate Finland;s egg surplus.
This means toat Finnish packers are able to buy eggs from producers at low prices and sell them more cheaply than Swedish packers can.
Swedish packers have to pay the whole production cost to Swedish egg producers, who do not receive any production aid.
In other words, the 'advantageous' price of Finnish eggs is the result of distorted competition.
This has resulted in heavy losses for Swedish egg producers, some of whom have gone bankrupt.
If nothing is done to tackle this problem, the Swedish egg industry -could very quickly be wiped out.
Does the CouncU intend to take any steps to deal with the problem?
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
Finland's Accession Treaty to the European Union included special measures in the field of state aid, in particular for egg producers.
These measures were intended to take account of toe need to bring Finnish prices into line with Community prices from the very first day of membership and, at the same time, to improve the structural situation of this sector in Finland.
These measures are laid down in Article 138 of the Treaty of Accession and also in Article 140 of Annex XIV to this same Treaty.
Article 138 states toat Finland may grant subject to authorisation from the Commission and for a transitional period of five years from the date of membership, transitional and progressively reduced state aid to producers of basic agricultural produce which is subject to the Common Agricultural Policy.
Eggs come into this category. This same article also lays down the conditions for the authorisation of this aid by the Commission, for the determining .of the amount of aid, and for any adaptations which may be necessary as a result of developments in the Common Agricultural Policy and in Community price levels.
Article 140, together with Annex XIV, stipulates toat for a transitional period of five years from the date of accession, the Commission authorises Finland to grant transitional state aid in the form of investment aid, subject to the following conditions: this aid must not result in an increase in global production capacities and must respect the individual production Umits laid down by the Commission according to the procedures laid down in Article 29 of Regulation 4253/88/EEC.
The Accession Treaty also gives the Commission the option of adopting protective measures at toe request of the Member State concerned in the event of serious disturbances resulting from trade in the area of agriculture between one or more of the new Member States and the Community as it was composed on 31 December 1994, or between toe new Member States themselves.
It is therefore toe Commission's responsibility to ensure that the provisions of Articles 138 and 140 of the Treaty of Accession are correctly applied and also to check that the conditions justifying toe application of toe protective clause are met.
Question No 27 by Hans Lindqvist (H-0046/97)
An intense debate is currently in progress in Sweden concerning the country's possible participation in economic and monetary union (EMU).
The Swedish government takes the view that it is the Riksdag which in autumn 1997 will decide whether or not Sweden participates in EMU.
Sweden's Prime Minister, Göran Persson, has expressed fears that EMU will result in a common fiscal and financial policy.
What would toe consequences for Sweden be if the Riksdag says 'no' in autumn 1997 to Swedish entry into EMU?
Is there a desire to establish a common fiscal and financial policy for the EU?
Answer
In reply to the first part of the Honourable Member's question, the Council points out that Article 109 J of the Treaty on European Union states that if, at the end of 1997, the date for entry to third stage of EMU has not been set, as will no doubt be the case, it will then start on 1 January 1999.
Before 1 July 1998 toe Council, meeting in the composition of the Heads of State or Government, must confirm, in accordance with the procedure laid down in Article 109 J, which Member States fulfil the necessary conditions for the adoption of toe single currency.
In accordance with Article 109 K of the Treaty, the Council must also, acting on a Commission recommendation, decide whether any Member States shall have derogation because they do not yet fulfil the conditions for the adoption of a single currency.
As to the second part of the question, I would like to stress that the Council cannot pronounce on toe consequences of a possible decision by the Swedish Parliament which, furthermore, is a hypothetical question.
At the present stage, toe Treaty does not include any obligation for a rigorously uniform fiscal and financial policy.
Debates of the European ParUament
Question No 28 by Luciano Pettinari (H-0050/97) Subject: 'Colonization' of Famagusto
The Turkish terrorist organization 'Grey Wolves' which was responsible for the murder of two Greek Cypriots last summer has threatened, both in declarations made by the head of the organization in Cyprus and in a communiqué published by Turkish-Cypriot newspapers, to establish settlers in toe occupied City of Famagusta.
Answer
Statements like the one referred to by the Honourable Member and recently taken up toe Turkish-Cypriot leader Mr R Denktosh himself, together with the tragic events of last summer in Cyprus, have again highlighted the urgent need to intensify efforts towards promoting a comprehensive political settlement in Cyprus, under the aegis of the United Nations.
Thé Council has called for restraint and avoidance by all concerned of any actions liable to raise tension and to hinder or compUcate talks between the parties promoted by toe international community for later this year.
The Council has reiterated its full support for the work of toe UN Secretary-General and his Special Representative for Cyprus, Professor Han Sung-Joo, and has caUed on all parties to step up their efforts to make a decisive move towards a settlement on toe basis of a bi-communal and bi-zonal Federation in accordance with the relevant United Nations Security Council Resolutions and High Level Agreements of 1977 and 1979.
The prospect of membership of the European Union could be used constructively to make progress towards a just and viable solution.
It is vital toat this opportunity be grasped.
The Netherlands Presidency has invited Ambassador Heaslip to continue in his task as Presidency Representative for Cyprus.
He wUl keep in close contact with the parties in toe region and other international players, in particular the UN.
The Council wiU continue to urge Turkey to contribute to a solution in Cyprus in accordance with the UN Security Council resolutions and the High Level Agreements of 1977 and 1979.
Question No 29 by Arthur Newens (H-0054/97) Subject: Aid and human rights in relation to toe Republic of Yemen
Since 1988 toe Yemeni Government has repeatedly stated that it would be in favour of negotiating a new Agreement. During the 7th EC-Yemen Joint Cooperation Committee meeting in Sanaa on 19 September 1996, toe Community agreed with the Yemeni side to start talks on a new cooperation agreement as soon as possible.
') Minutes of 24.10.1996, p.57
Debates of the European Parliament
Therefore, the Commission has proposed to toe Council to negotiate a new agreement with Yemen (replacing the 1984 Cooperation Agreement) which would be modelled in accordance with toe latest generation of toe EC's cooperation agreements with Asian developing countries (i.e. Bangladesh, Cambodia, Laos, Pakistan).
The CouncU is currently examining toe Draft Directives with a view to adopt them on 24 February 1997.
Of course, under the information procedure governing toe relations with toe Parliament in toe field of toe association and trade agreements, the relevant parliamentary committees wUl be duly informed on the content of toe mandate as soon as it has been adopted by the CouncU.
The principle of the EC-Yemen Cooperation Agreement would be to enhance and develop the cooperation between the Contracting Parties in the areas which fall within toe bounds of their respective competence.
Therefore, toe draft mandate for the Agreement would include develop ment trade, economic and cultural cooperation, environmental protection and sustainable manage ment of natural resources as well as human rights and democracy.
Ù In particular, the development cooperation will remain an important component of overall cooperation between the Parties.
In order to emphasize toe importance of human rights and democracy, the proposed Cooperation Agreement should accordingly include articles on human rights and democracy and thé need for human resource development.
The standard clause stating respect for human rights and democratic principles, wiU be the basis for toe cooperation between the Parties and for the provisions of the agreement thus constituting an essential element of the Agreements.
Since the start of EC-Yemen cooperation in 1978, 57,7 MECU have been committed for economic and development projects in Yemen.
The Commission has committed more from 27 MECU since Yemen unification in 1990 for 15 economic or development cooperation projects and project studies.
In addition, approximately 10 MECU were spent on food aid, humanitarian aid and decentralised cooperation projects.
The Government of Yemen has invited the EU Member States and the European Parliament to participate in the monitoring of the 27 April general elections.
Member States have responded positively and the Presidency will ensure coordination of their task.
Question No 30 by Kirsten Jensen (H-0063/97) Subject: Procedures for authorizing the marketing of genetically engineered maize
On 18 December 1996, the Commission decided to authorize the marketing of genetically engineered maize produced by Ciba-Geigy, in accordance with the provisions of Directive 90/220/EC (') and on the basis of reports by three scientific committees (on food, animal nutrition and pesticides).
The decision was taken despite opposition from a large majority of Member States (13 out of 15) and the European Parliament.
The Commissioner responsible for toe environment told Parliament's environment committee on 21 January 1997 that toe above procedure was 'unsatisfactory'.
WUl the CouncU act to ensure that the IGC abolishes this remarkable procedure which leaves an awful lot to be deshed regards democracy?
If this matter does not concern the IGC but is of an interinstitutional or similar nature, will the Council take the necessary action?
Answer
Although it is not for the CouncU to comment on statements made by a member of the Commission, and the way the question is formulated does not permit it to identify the procedure to which the Honourable Member is alluding, the Council would point out that:
Decision 97/98/EC, adopted by the Commission on 23 January 1997 and concerning toe marketing of geneticaUy engineered maize, was taken in accordance with Directive 90/220/EEC (adopted by toe Council in cooperation with the European Parliament) and Article 13 in particular;
Directive 90/220/EEC, and in particular Articles 13 and 21, is categoric regarding toe division of decisionmaking powers between toe Council and the Commission depending on the various stages in the process leading to a decision to authorize marketing.
Debates of the European ParUament
In any event, the Council has no competence to intervene in the activities of the IGC.
If, however, toe Commission presented a proposal to amend Directive 90/220/EEC, the Council would most certainly look at this as soon as possible.
Question No 31 by Konstadinos Klironomos (H-0067/97)
Subject: Recommendation issued by UK authorities to British tourists intending to visit Greece
The Foreign Office recently issued a 'recommendation' to British tourists to avoid Greece as a tourist destination, owing to strike action in that country.
Given the risk that this recommendation may discourage British tourists from spending their holidays in Greece, and that Greece is not toe only country affected by strikes (quite apart from the fact toat terrorist attacks occur in other Member States of toe European Union on an almost daily basis), wUl the CouncU say whether it considers that this 'recommendation', which wUl have a particularly damaging effect on Greece's ailing tourist sector, is justified or exaggerated, bearing in mind that this is a particularly critical period, since most bookings for the summer of 1997 will be made from now on, and what measures it intends to take to ensure that Greece does not suffer toe consequences of this 'recommendation', i.e. a drop in the number of UK tourists to Greece?
Answer
The Council has no knowledge of a recommendation by the UK Foreign Office concerning British tourists intending to travel to Greece.
Clearly any recommendation of this kind issued by an individual Member State is issued on its own behalf and in no way commits the CouncU which is not authorised to comment on statements made by Member States outside toe context of its meetings.
Question No 32 by Richard Howitt (H-0068/97)
Subject: Oil exploration by European companies in Colombia
What active steps has the CouncU token following Parliament's 24 October 1996 resolution (') and, the Colombian Attorney-General's Official Inquiry into the activities of British Petroleum in the Casanare region of Colombia?
Bearing in mind its answer to Question No 32 H-1018/96, (2) will the Council now commit itself to making a positive intervention to pursue the aims of toe resolution?
Answer
The Council has continued to follow closely the situation in Colombia.
It is very concerned about toe continuing high level of violence, which has resulted in death and injury of civilians.
It will continue to make known its concerns in relation to human rights, democracy and the rule of law on every possible occasion, as it has done in the past.
We urge the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights to accelerate the estabUshment of toe office, so that it can do its job.
On the question of the fight against drags, the Honourable Member will be aware that the Dublin European CouncU endorsed the implementation of the agreed followup to toe report of the expert mission to Latin-american.
This will continue to be the subject of contacts with Latin American countries, including Colombia.
2) Annex to Verbatim Report of Proceedings, 11.12.1996, p. 44
Debates of the European Parliament
What steps does the Netherlands Presidency intend to take to counteract toe current deceleration and incipient decline of Euro-Mediterranean policy?
Answer
The Council has on a number of occasions reiterated the importance it attaches to relations between the European Union and its mediterranean partners, a view which is, we know, fully shared by toe European Parliament.
In this context, toe Euro-Mediterranean Conference which took place in Barcelona in November 1995 marked a turning point in these relations, establishing a partnership covering three areas: strengthened political dialogue; the development of economic and financial cooperation, and greater emphasis on the social, cultural and human dimension.
Since the Barcelona Conference, a large number of activities have taken place which have helped to consolidate the development of this partnership.
In particular meetings at ministerial level have been held covering such issues as Industry, Culture, Information Society, Fisheries and Water.
Under the political and security partnership, work on implementation of the principles in the Barcelona Declaration has progressed, notably through agreement on a number of confidence and security building measures, with a view to the conclusion of a Euromediterranean Charter/Pact as an overall framework.
The Council considers that the number and range of activities which have taken place throughout last year constitute substantial progress in the development of the partnership.
The Council has welcomed the plans to consolidate this further through toe programme of work over the coming months.
The Dutch Presidency, with the full involvement of all member states, will work hard to ensure that the momentum achieved since the Barcelona meeting is maintained and developed.
In this context, the next meeting of Foreign Ministers in Malta on 15-16 April will be a priority for the Presidency and for the Council as a whole.
It will provide the opportunity for ministers to assess the progress achieved so far, and to target priorities for the future.
The multilateral element of the partnership is complemented by toe network of bilateral agreements currently being established.
Agreements with Tunisia, Israel and Morocco have already ben signed, and negotiations are being pursued actively with Egypt, Jordan and Lebanon.
An agreement with the Palestinians is due to be signed shortly.
Negotiations with Algeria are expected to start soon, and it has been agreed that exploratory conversations will be held with Syria.
The Dutch Presidency will do all it can to ensure that the outstanding negotiations are concluded as soon as possible in order to make further progress towards the completion of the network of new agreements.
Question No 34 by Bernd Posselt (H-0071/97)
Subject: EU office in Kosovo
The European Parliament has called for an EU office to be opened in Kosovo, the idea being that such European representation could prevent a dangerous conflict in advance.
Has the Council aheady initiated preparations for this, or what else does it intend to do to prevent conflict in Kosovo?
Answer
As the Honourable Member will be aware, the Council has repeatedly expressed its concern about the situation in Kosovo.
At its sessions of 1 and 28 October 1996, it reaffirmed its conviction that an increased international presence in Kosovo would have a moderating effect on toe two parties and could thus contribute to reducing tensions.
An international presence would certainly constitute another positive element in finding an acceptable solution to the question of the status of Kosovo within the FRY.
To that end, the Council pursues the aim of establishing an EU presence in Kosovo and continues to insist on that point with the authorities of toe FRY.
With regard to the second part of the question, the European Union has made clear that the opening of an EU office in Kosovo and the accreditation of an Ambassador of the FRY to the European Union are being dealt with in parallel.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 35 by Fiorella Ghilardotti (H-0073/97)
Subject: The Council's property policy
Shortly after moving into new premises large enough to house virtually all its staff, the CouncU Secretariat realized toat the accession of new Member States would necessitate building an extension on the adjacent plot of land on Chaussée d'Etterbeek.
This plot, previously toe property of the Régie des bâtiments (State buildings authority), had recently been sold to a developer.
Why, in the interests of sound financial management did toe Council not acquire this land in advance, thus leaving itself free to build without having to go through toe intermediary of a developer?
Answer
The CouncU Secretariat informed toe Belgian authorities back in 1994 of its interest in toe plot of land on the Chaussée d'Etterbeek in Brussels, with a view to extending the Justus Lipsius buUding.
The Council Secretariat then discovered in a press article toat the plot of land was going to be sold.
The Council has asked the Secretariat to provide a full report on this subject and will soon be discussing the matter.
Question No 36 by Anita Pollack (H-0075/97)
Subject: Bodies of schoolgirls found in sea off Sri Lanka
Is toe Council prepared to call for an impartial investigation into the finding of bodies of schoolgirls inside bags dumped in the sea off the coast of Mullaitivu, on toe Jaffna peninsula of Sri Lanka?
Answer
It would be inappropriate for the Council to involve itself in criminal cases in any or every part of the world unless they raised policy issues for the European Union.
Question No 37 by Karin Riis-jørgensen (H-0080/97)
Having regard to ParUament's resolution of 10 October 1995 (B4-1204/95) on the transparency of CouncU decisions and the Community's legislative procedures (') and following toe adoption of the Code of Conduct of 2 October 1995 on toe pubhshing of Council minutes and statements in the minutes, in which particular cases has the CouncU continued its practice of making unilateral statements on pending legislation in its minutes, for what reasons, have they made accessible to the public in accordance with the Code of Conduct and has it always obtained the consent of Parliament in advance in the case of declarations made under the codecision procedure, or, at least informed Parliament as co-legislator?
Answer
The Council adopted 301 legislative acts between 2 October 1995 - toe date on which the Council adopted toe Code of Conduct on the publishing of minutes and statements in the minutes of toe Council acting as legislator - and 31 December 1996.
These acts gave rise to 537 statements in the minutes.
Debates of the European Parliament.
Of toe 301 legislative acts, 152 were not the subject of statements in the minutes.
We can therefore judge toat toe Council made a moderate use of toe instrument of statements in the minutes.
The Council decided to publish 534 of the 537 statements in the minutes, using the aforementioned Code of Conduct.
As stated in toe Council's written reply to written question P-2385/96 which Mr Bonde asked on this same subject, only three statements made when adopting legislative texts have not been published in the framework of the Code of Conduct for the period indicated.
This is because these statements, all three of which concerned the agreement on shipbuilding in the framework of toe OECD, did not relate to legislative acts as such but rather to toe conclusion of an international agreement.
Moreover, one of these three statements - that by the Commission when adopting Council Regulation 3094/95 on aid to shipbuilding - was submitted to the Honourable Member by the Council following his request of 23 September 1996, pursuant to Council Decision 93/731/EC on public access to Council documents.
Would toe Honourable Member please note that the Code of Conduct concerns the final adoption of legislative acts, in toe sense given to this term in the annex to toe Council's Rules of Procedure.
Statements in toe minutes made when the Council adopts joint positions, in the sense of Article 189 Β and 189 C of toe EC Treaty, are not made accessible to the public. They are only made accessible at the time of toe final adoption of the legislative act.
Question No 38 by Christine Oddy (H-0082/97)
Subject: Nicaragua
Is the Council aware that toe new government of toe Liberal Alliance in Nicaragua has rejected the Minimum Agenda which was signed by all political parties, except the Liberal AlUance, and by many popular organizations before the election?
It pledged them to support and respect toe minimum international standards on rights enshrined in international treaties.
Will the Council press toe new government to adhere to international minimum rights?
Answer
The Council has not discussed toe rejection by the new Government of Nicaragua of the Minimum Agenda referred to by the honourable Member.
Irrespective of whether the party of toe new Government of Nicaragua has signed the Minimum Agenda or not, it is one of toe duties of a government to honour its undertakings with respect to international treaties and covenants, to which it is a party.
In toat context, the Union is sure that the new Government of Nicaragua wiU abide by its international obligations.
Question No 39 by Francisco Sanz Fernandez (H-0089/97)
Subject: 'European cities of culture' after toe year 2000
The Council has asked the Commission to draw up and submit a proposal for establishing a procedure for the designation of 'European cities of culture' after the year 2000.
However, at the most recent formal meeting of the Council of Culture Ministers, on 16 December 1996, France and Italy put forward Lille and Genoa respectively as candidates for 2001, and Austria proposed Graz for 2003.
Can the Council state what cities apart from the above have to date been officially proposed by the national delegations as candidates for 'European city of culture', and for which years from 2001 on?
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The following candidacies have to date been officiaUy submitted for designation as 'European city of culture' after the year 2000:
2001: Basle and Riga (submitted as candidates in 1995) 2003: Graz (submitted as candidate in December 1996)
It is not for the Council to intervene in decisions taken by the authorities in toe Member States, especiaUy äs, while awaiting the new regulations to be adopted on toe basis of a Commission proposal, toe field in question continues to be governed by the procedure applied to date.
Question No 40 by Gerard Collins (H-0090/97) Subject: Ah safety agency
Has any Member State proposed that the new ah safety agency discussed at the Transport Council on 12-13 December 1996 be established on its territory and would toe Council view favourably toe location of this new agency in Shannon in Ireland?
Answer
The Commission submitted to toe Council on 16 December 1996 a recommendation for a CouncU Decision authorizing the Commission to start negotiations with a view to establishing a European organization responsible for civil aviation safety.
The idea is to create an authority in Europe, which should be able to adopt safety rules and to check conformity of products, organization and personnel with these rales.
The recommendation by the Commission is being examined by the CouncU bodies and efforts wUl be made to maintain or enhance toe high level of safety standards in aviation prevailing in Europe.
In toe introduction to its recommendation, toe Commission recalled that, since it would not be appropriate in the wider European context to give a Community agency the power to issue its own safety rules, toe best way was to create a new international organization with appropriate powers to enable it to fulfil its tasks.
This new organization should be based on toe present Joint Aviation Authorities (JAA).
Until toe adoption of a Decision by toe CouncU on this issue, in particular on the statutory framework of toe European organization responsible for civil aviation safety, a discussion on toe location of its headquarters should be considered as premature.
However, no Member State proposed toat such a new organization should be estabUshed on its own territory or in any specific location.
Question No 41 by Pat Gallagher (H-0095/97) Subject: Single currency and overseas investment
Has the Council considered toe implications of a single currency involving some, but not all, EU countries for the future pattern of investment by overseas multinational companies into jobcreating projects in EU countries, as highlighted recently by toe President of Toyota concerning investment in Britain, and does the Council consider toat overseas companies are likely to concentrate their EU investments inside or outside a single currency zone?
Answer
According to Article 3a(2) of the Treaty, toe introduction of a single currency and the definition and toe conduct of a single monetary poUcy and exchange rate policy has as a primary objective to maintain price stability and to support the general economic policies in the Community, in accordance with the principle of an open market economy with free competition.
Debates of the European ParUament
Question No 42 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0099/97) Subject: Implementing shipping safety rales
For example, there is toe case of toe Greek Registration Organisation: following privatisation in 1992, 13 vessels with seaworthiness certificates sank with the loss of 49 lives.
Serious questions should also be raised about toe reliabUity of modern methods of transmitting SOS signals and the abolishment of toe post of wireless operator from crews.
Will the Council say what initiatives it intends to take to improve shipping safety rules in order to ensure toat proper controls are carried out on vessels before seaworthiness certificates are issued, particularly where these are issued by private individuals, and to ensure toat these individuals are also monitored, and finally whether it intends to reintroduce toe post of wireless operator in crews on a mandatory basis, at least until toe new safety and communications systems have proved their reliability?
Answer
Regarding the Council's intentions for action in toe field of maritime safety, the Council would like to indicate from toe outset that it has adopted several resolutions containing action programmes for toe prevention of maritime casualties off the European coasts and of incidents of marine pollution.
Numerous directives and regulations have implemented these action programmes and more measures are to come.
Concerning the three sub-questions made in toe parUamentory question, toe Council would like to indicate the foUowing:
The CouncU tried to improve toe quality of navigabUity certificates and the construction of ships by adopting Directive 94/57/EC on common rules and standards for ship inspection and survey organizations and for toe relevant activities of maritime administrations.
Under this Directive, each ship must be constructed and maintained in accordance with toe requirements of a classification organization; those organizations must meet high standards of professional competence and shall be under toe strict control of the national authorities and of the Commission.
The issue of toe possible deficiencies in the modern techniques for broadcasting distress messages, which were introduced by the Global Maritime Distress and Safety System (GMDSS), has never been discussed by the Council.
The Council also understands from the Commission's reply to Question P-597/96 put by Mr EPHREMIDIS, that the Commission does not intend to propose changing toe GMDSS system.
The Council also cannot not take up positions on the opportuneness of the aboUshment of the function of the radio operator.
The Council has never discussed this matter and induces from the aheady mentioned Commission reply that this Institution has no intention of proposing to reinstate toe function of radio-officer.
As aheady indicated in its reply to Question E-0435/95, put by Mr EPHREMIDIS, GMDSS provided toat the function of radio-electric officer could be token up by any qualified deck officer; Directive 94/58/EC on toe minimum level of training of seafarers tries to contribute to the efficiency of toat system.
This directive, which copies into Community law toe International Convention of 1977 on Standards for Safety, Training, Certification and Watchkeeping (STCW), requires every master, chief mate and deck officer to be trained in morse, signalling, radio communication procedures and use of radio equipment.
Debates of the European ParUament
Branner, a former SS officer, who has been on the wanted Ust since 1945 and is sought by the German authorities, was sentenced to death in France for the extermination of 25 000 Jews in Drancy.
Interpol has issued a warrant for his arrest, but so far without success.
Syria denies toat Branner is on its territory, and Germany is hesitating to call for his extradition.
What measures has toe CouncU taken so far in toe Branner case, and what does it intend to do, within toe framework of its powers, to clear up this particular affair and secure justice for toe thousands of victims?
Bearing in mind toat 1997 has been designated European Year against Racism, Anti-Semitism and Xenophobia, and that toe last few years have seen an alarming upsurge of racism and anti-Semitism frequently culminating in attempts to deny that toe holocaust took place, what measures has toe CouncU taken - or does it intend to take in future - to ensure that the EU and its Member States react vigorously to phenomena of this kind?
Answer
The Council is aware of toe problems mentioned by the Honourable Member of toe European ParUament.
Concerning toe European Union and its Member States, the Florence European CouncU on 21 and 22 June 1996 reaffirmed toe Union's determination to combat racism and xenophobia with the utmost resolve.
The Council adopted, on 15 July 1996, a Joint Action on the basis of Article K.3 of toe Treaty on European Union, concerning action to combat racism and xenophobia.
This Joint Action sets out to facilitate international judicial cooperation in combating racism and xenophobia with provision either for certain racist and xenophobic behaviour to be made a criminal offence or, failing that and pending any adoption of toe necessary legislation, for toe principle of double criminality to be waived.
At its meeting in Dublin on 13 and 14 December 1996, toe European Council welcomed the progress made for toe establishment of a European Monitoring Centre on Racism and.Xenophobia.
It called on the Council to complete its work with a view to toe speedy establishment of this Centre which wiU have to work in close cooperation with the Council of Europe.
The establishment of toe Centre will be an important element to counter racist and xenophobic phenomena and wUl mark the importance of 1997 as toe European Year against racism and xenophobia.
For the problems relating to extradition, two Conventions have been signed to simplify extradition procedures between the Member States of the European Union: one Convention was singed on 10 March 1995, the other was signed on 27 September 1996.
These Conventions will faciütate the measures to combat racism and xenophobia within the European Union.
Question No 62 by Bernd Posselt (H-0072/97) Subject: EU research policy
It seems that research cooperation within the EU is in serious decline because of financial constraints.
What new priorities wiU the Commission adopt in 1997 to counteract this?
Answer
It is true toat public research is subject to strict budgetary constraints in toe Union Member States.
These activities are attracting an increasing number of researchers and are resulting in toe creation of fast-growing cooperation networks. The decision for a 4th RTD Framework Programme makes it possible to guarantee the continuity of Community action through to 1998.
Debates of the European ParUament
The Commission has defined toe future framework programme's principal areas of activity, within which cooperative research activities wiU be maintained.
Community action wUl concentrate on six priority topics and wUl be structured as follows: three thematic programmes and three horizontal programmes.
It seems that toe content of these programmes will be more clearly defined and take more account of citizens' expectations.
Each of toe three thematic programmes wiU be organized at two levels: a limited number of key actions designed to stimulate research around important social and economic objectives for toe Community, and general activities for the development of generic fundamental research technologies in order to maintain the development of knowledge and skills.
On 12 February 1997 the Commission adopted a working document to this effect and plans to hold a wideranging debate with Parliament and other Community bodies from 28 February to 1 March next.
Subject: Scientific cooperation with toe Maghreb countries
What opportunities will be afforded for associated Maghreb countries to become involved in research, technological development and innovation policies?
Answer
Research cooperation is included in toe cooperation agreements concluded in 1992 between toe European Community and the Maghreb countries, and also in toe new Euro-Mediterranean agreements aheady signed or being negotiated.
No specific scientific and technological cooperation agreements with these countries are therefore being planned at the present time.
The Maghreb countries have been involved in toe Community's research activities since 1983, initially through toe 'Development sciences and technologies programme', subsequently through a specific cooperation initiative with toe Mediterranean countries (toe Avicenne Programme) and, more recently, in the framework of the· 4th Framework Programme's specific international cooperation programme.
Cooperation with toe Maghreb countries takes the form of joint research projects, cooperation networks and scientific conferences.
Maghreb researchers have participated in all these activities.
This cooperation should significantly expand in the context of toe Euro-Mediterranean partnership.
The working document on the 5th Framework Programme, adopted by the Commission on 12 February, stresses toe priority which should be awarded to international technological and scientific cooperation, and cooperation with Central European and Mediterranean countries.
The three principal areas for this increased cooperation should be toe carrying out of joint research activities, the reinforcement of local research capacities and technology transfer.
The Commission wiU be providing detoils of the aims and means of Euro-Mediterranean technological and scientific cooperation in a special communication on the subject which it will be submitting in the course of the coming weeks.
Question No 64 by Hugh Kerr (H-0102/97)
Subject: Arts education and training (SOCRATES programme)
Will arts education and training continue to be supported under the SOCRATES programme?
Culture is most certainly an area which knows no borders and which lends itself to European development.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 68 by Heidi Hautala (H-0019/97)
Subject: State of toe EU's forestry industry
The European Parliament has recently debated the EU's forestry strategy in connection with toe Thomas report.
In its opinion appended to toat report, the Committee on Economic and Monetary Affairs and Industrial Policy asks toe Commission 'to present to the European ParUament a communication on forestry industry which examines toe current state and competitiveness of this important industry based on toe criteria of sustainable forest management'
Answer
This situation has reflected toe traditional status quo, prior to toe most recent enlargement.
However, as the Commission is well aware, that event radically altered affairs, such that forest resources doubled (in area), timber production increased by half, pulp production increased three-fold and paper and board production by over 50 %.
Whilst there were no radical changes in the trade and consumption patterns of forest products as a result of the enlargement toe Community has nonetheless become a major producer and consumer of these items, the World's second after the USA.
Thus, as sought by toe Thomas report in general and by the Committee's opinion in particular, it is a suitable time for toe Commission to review its relevant poUcies affecting this sector.
Accordingly, for forest resources, it is foreseen to review toe existing provisions in toe light of experience so far and toe changed circumstances following enlargement.
In toe meantime toe Commission is contributing fully to toe preparation of the Community position at the InterGovernmental Panel on forests, whose 4th inter alia SFM (Sustainable Forest Management), and trade and environment aspects of forest products such as eco-certification and toe possibility of a legally-binding instrument to encompass all economic, social and environmental aspects of forests.
Question No 69 by Glenys Kinnock (H-0024/97)
Subject: Child seats and airbag safety
In view of the growing concern about the risks of serious injury or death to children in rear-facing child restraints used in toe front seats of cars equipped with passenger-side airbags, and toe tragic evidence of such accidents occurring in toe United States, will the Commission take the lead and encourage car manufacturers, consumer and motoring organizations to launch a major Europeanwide public information campaign to inform parents of toe danger of combining child seats with airbags?
Answer
The Commission is well aware of the serious risks for chUdren transported in rear-facing chUd restraints when such devices are fitted on front seats equipped with airbags.
Therefore, the Commission has adopted on 17 June 1996 a directive which obliges car manufacturers to affix a warning label to such seating positions in all new cars offered for sale alter 1 January 1997.
With such a regulatory requirement the Commission deems to have efficiently covered what can be done on toe cars themselves.
Of course, consumer and motoring organisations are free to launch complementary information campaigns to make parents understand toe dangers of fitting child seats to car seats with airbags.
* Question No 70 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0048/97)
Subject: Production and marketing of bogus pharmaceutical products in the European Union
Is toe Commission aware of information concerning toe existence of illegal laboratories producing bogus drugs which, according to press reports, are principally active in Spain, Italy and Greece and which market their products principally in the Third World, but also in Europe?
What measures is toe Commission taking to smash these illegal rings so as to safeguard public health and to protect the pharmaceuticals industry from competition from toe iUegal production and marketing of bogus pharmaceutical products?
Answer
Production and putting on toe market of medicinal products in the Member States are subject to strict Community and national rales and controls.
The aim of these measures being in particular toe protection of public health and the defense of legitimate commercial interests.
The European Commission does not dispose of any particular information - except for a widely worded article in the daily newspaper 'Le Monde' of 26 October 1996 - concerning recent cases of production or placing on the market of counterfeit medicinal products.
Following toe newspaper article cited above and toe oral question on this issue, toe European Commission is, however, planning to attract the attention of Member States to possible illegal activities in this field in upcoming pharmaceutical committee meetings.
Question No 71 by Ludivina Garcia Arias (H-0049/97) Subject: Directive on the transmission of television signals (situation in Spain)
Does toe Commissioner responsible for industrial policy not consider toat this is contrary to the provisions of Directive 95/47/EC (') on the use of standards for toe transmission of television signals?
Does toe Commission not consider that this initiative runs counter to the principles of free competition in Europe?
Question No 72 by Joan Colom i Naval (H-0079/97) Subject: The standardization of decoders
Directive 95/47/EC (2) regulates the standardization of decoders in the EU.
Debates of the European Parliament
In this context, can the Commission say: what the legal effect of this provision adopted after toe deadline set in the Directive is?
whether the Royal Decree-Law complies with Community legislation as regards both the standardization of decoders and respect for free competition?
Joint Answer
Directive 95/47/EC of toe European Parliament and of the Council on toe use of standards for the transmission of television signals was adopted unanimously, on 24 October 1995, by the Council, having received a large majority in toe European ParUament.
In relation to conditional access to digital Pay-TV service the directive also requires:
The incorporation of a descrambler conforming to toe common European scrambhng system in aU relevant consumer equipment; and that conditional access providers offer their services to broadcasters on fair, reasonable and nondiscriminatory terms.
The directive sets out toe general principles which must be followed by Member States into national law, where the necessary detailed provisions should be set out.
The directive requires that such transposition be carried out before 23 August 1996.
Question No 73 by Jonas Sjöstedt (H-0052/97) Subject: Manufacturers' liability for car exhaust systems
In a case currently before the European Court of Justice, the Commission has supported VAG Svenska AB, a car importer which wants to see Swedish requirements for manufacturers to accept liability for car exhaust systems to be scrapped.
The manufacturers' liability scheme was introduced on environmental grounds, and its scrapping would weaken controls on emissions from traffic.
What is more, the Commission has proposed that a similar scheme be introduced at EU level.
There are currently no common rales on manufacturers' liability in the EU.
The Commission's actions are therefore not only hostile to the environment but also a trifle inconsistent.
How does toe EU reconcüe its call for a lowering of Swedish environmental standards with its own international commitments to reducing emissions into the atmosphere?
Does toe Commission consider toat Sweden could invoke Article 100a(4) of toe Treaty in order to maintain its own rules?
Answer
The European Commission strongly supports measures intending to reduce ah pollution by car emissions.
But toe Commission also has to take care of toe unity and functioning of the single market.
This does not mean that the single market and economic considerations predominate environmental aspects. It means that Member States, which want to proceed in the protection of the environment have to promote European initiatives with a view to strengthening existing European legislation.
With regard to the issue of in-service testing of vehicles, toe Commission has, as the honourable Member points out in his oral question, recently put forward a proposed revision to Directive 70/220/EEC which includes harmonized provisions for in-service testing and possible recall procedures.
Debates of the European Parliament
Subject: The European defence industry
The meeting of the Committee on Foreign Affairs on 19 September 1996 was attended by Commissioners Bangemann and Van den Broek.
At that meeting Gary Titley's working document on toe Commission communication on toe challenges facing toe European defence-related industry (COM(96)0010) was discussed.
Directly after the meeting I sent an open letter to the Commission ers to which no reply has yet been received.
I should therefore like answers to a number of the questions I put in that letter.
Why is toe Commission disregarding fundamental market economic principles where the defence industries are concerned, while other industries are aUowed to go under regardless of unemployment or social problems?
Why is the Commission not seeking global cooperation for disarmament and arms export control but instead seeking to become as successful an arms dealer as toe USA?
Answer
I. The defence industry is not very different from other industrial branches.
That is why, in its communication of January 1996, toe Commission gave priority to measures based on existing Community instruments for toe single market.
However, there are certain special features in the defence industry.
The following are some examples:
The defence industry is a strategic branch.
Governments are its only cUents and in many cases they are also the main owners of defence undertakings.
This branch has an enormous longterm technological value.
Some specifically defenceoriented technologies can have both military and civiUan applications.
All exports of defence technology are subject to government authorization.
Moreover, late last year the POLARM group (inter-disciplinary ad hoc working party of the Council on 'arms policy') adopted a document on toe special features of toe defence industry.
In ito analysis of the defence industry the Commission emphasized its desire for increased competition and for this sector to be more market-oriented (taking account of the above special features).
This was because of toe need not to lose any further ground in the competition with toe US defence industry.
That is particularly important for three reasons:
Major technological and industrial bases are at stake, which can also be used for civilian purposes (dual purpose); they also involve a large number of jobs in the EU.
There is a risk of a poUtical imbalance between Europe and toe USA.
If the EU does not have a competitive defence technology and toe related industrial basis, it will find it hard successfully to develop a European identity in the field of security and defence.
With regard to disarmament and export controls, toe European Commission is not looking towards increasing exports of armaments.
The Commission takes the view toat a more efficient European defence with a larger number of common projects and a less fragmented market will be far less dependent on arms exports.
Moreover, it seems unhealthy in terms of toe international political situation for the USA to have a monopoly of advanced weapons systems.
A few examples of EU activities in toe field of disarmament and exports:
In June 1991 the European Council adopted a declaration on toe non-proliferation and non-export of weapons.
It was based on eight criteria (see annex) for arms export policy.
In 1995 the EU adopted a joint measure to resolve the anti-personnel mines (APM) problem.
Among others, this measure included a ban on exports of mines from the Union.
Question No 75 by Elly Plooij-van Gorsel (H-0087/97)
Directives 70/156/EEC (
) and 70/220/EEC (
), cover the application of a new in-car technology for permanent emission control known as on-board diagnostics (OBD).
WiU this OBD system, and toe information necessary for its repair, be made accessible to all car repairers, i.e. irrespective of whether or not they are official dealers?
If not, how does the Commission propose to guarantee competition on the internal market?
Answer
The Commission fully recognises that access should be given to enable the quahfied personnel of franchised dealers, non-franchised garages and roadside organisations to diagnose faults and repair vehicles that are equipped with on-board diagnostic (OBD) systems.
This is essential to fully utiUse toe benefits of OBD such that faulty vehicles are repaired as effectively as possible and pollutant emissions are kept under control.
OBD systems, the codes that indicate faults and the scan tools used to interrogate OBD systems will be based on standards pubUshed by the International Standards Organisation (ISO) and toe Society of Automotive Engineers (SAE).
These standards are referenced in toe proposals by toe Commission.
WhUe OBD will indicate toat a component is faulty, a repairer will still need information (e.g. service manuals) to show where that component is installed on toe vehicle and what steps to take to repair or replace it.
The conditions of the Block Exemption Regulation on selective distribution requires vehicle manufacturers to make available toe information necessary to repair vehicles to parties outside their normal distribution network.
The Commission is actively considering the suitability of these regulations and the format of any additional legislation that may be necessary to ensure that such OBD repair information is commerciaUy available in toe market place to the appropriate interested parties, thereby preserving the competitive nature of the vehicle repair business in line with the internal market.
Question No 76 by Niall Andrews (H-0096/97)
WUl the Commission consider banning the use of 'bull bars' on motor vehicles, because they are frequently responsible for more severe accidents to pedestrians, particularly young chUdren, than would be the case if 'bull bars' had not been fitted?
Has the Commission examined this problem and if not, wtil it now do so with a view to presenting proposals for controUing the use of 'buU bars' at the earliest opportunity?
Debates of toe European Parliament
On the basis of the results of a current study aimed at providing an objective opinion on the cost-benefit aspect of such a regulation, the Commission will take up toe already existing draft for toe Directive concerned and finalise it in the appropriate manner.
The Commission hopes that this work can be completed before thè end of this year so that the proposal can be discussed in toe Council and toe Parliament from the beginning of 1998.
In the meantime there is scope within national competence for Member States to take action against the use of 'bull bars' which are not part of the original equipment of cars and, hence, not covered by type-approval.
This is, to toe Commission's information, toe case of toe overwhelming majority of such devices which can currently be found on vehicles in service.
Question No 77by Fernando Pérez Royo (H-1031/96) Subject: Strategic competitiveness plan of the Astilleros Españoles group
What is toe Commission's position as regards implementation of the agreement authorizing Ptos 90 bn in state aid to fund toe strategic competitiveness plan of the Astilleros Españoles group?
Answer
The Commission assumes toat the honourable Member is referring to the 90 billion pesetas balance of the 180 bülion pesetas aids notified by the Spanish authorities that has still to be approved by the Commission, 89.103 biUion pesetas in toe form of previously approved unpaid loss compensation aid plus interest having been approved by the Commission in December 1995.
The Commission has decided to open a formal investigation procedure into the outstanding 90 billion pesetas (which comprises social aids and investment aids) in order to assess the compatibility of these measures with the shipbuilding aid directive, having regard also to the OECD agreement respecting normal competitive conditions in commercial shipbuilding and ship repair.
In addition, various other measures of public financial assistance are to be covered in toe investigation, including past interest payments of 62.028 billion pesetas during 1988-1994 due to delays in payment of previously approved aid; past extraordinary payments by the state shareholder of 7.355 billion pesetas; and continued financing of yards to enable them to maintain activities despite growing losses.
This investigation extends another investigation procedure already opened in respect of proposed future tax credits of 48 bUlion pesetas.
Question No 78 by Irini Lambraki (H-1035/96)
Is the COM in cotton not facing toe same transparency problems as the other COMs, and if it is, wiU the Commission also implement a system for granting subsidies directly to producers in the COM in cotton?
The Commission wiU be submitting a report on the operation of toe subsidy system for cotton by toe end of the 1999-2000 marketing year at toe latest.
Subject: Initiative on inter-regional cooperation to combat forest fires in the Mediterranean Member States
Forest fires are a real scourge for the Mediterranean Member States, especially during toe summer, and cause considerable damage, mainly to the environment but also to other sectors of their aheady weak economies.
That being so, what is the Commission's attitude towards toe idea of launching an initiative to tackle such fires - an initiative based on cooperation between toe various regions of toe Mediterranean and including measures such as exchanges of information and know-how, information and awareness campaigns, common fire-prevention programmes, etc.?
How could the funding for such an initiative be found?
Answer
As toe Honourable Member points out Ares can cause major damage in almost one half of Europe's wooded.areas, and the Commission is very much aware of this problem.
Community action to protect forests against fires - the extension of which was approved by Parliament at the January partsession - makes it possible to finance preventive measures implemented in toe framework of »coherent protection programmes.
These measures include information and awareness campaigns, together with the carrying out of pilot projects concerning new methods and technologies designed to make toe action more effective.
This action has also made it possible to set up a Community information system on forest fires which is an excellent tool for evaluation and toe exchange of information.
These measures are funded by means of agricultural loans independently of toe EAGGF.
The Commission also reminds toe Honourable Member that forestry actions are included in toe various planning documents approved under Objectives 1 and 5b in many Mediterranean regions.
These actions include fire prevention, the protection and development of forests, and technical assistance.
Subject: 'Euro-info' publication
In toe Commission's own words, SME's are urged to 'play their considerable role on the European market'.
To this end, DG ΧΧΠΙ publishes the 'EURO-info' bulletin which, amongst other things, keeps SME's abreast of upcoming conferences, workshops, etc.
However, businesses in my constituency are informing me toat toe publication is consistently out-of-date simply because it never arrives in time.
The situation has not improved, despite letters to the Commissioner bringing this to his attention.
What steps does the Commission intend to take to rectify this situation which is only serving to frustrate SMEs instead of encouraging them to take part in Europeanwide events to further inter-SME participation?
Answer
The Commission thanks the Honourable Member for his interest particularly as regards toe EURO INFO bulletin.
Debates of toe European Parliament
FinaUy, a detoUed questionnaire appeared or wUl appear in the December 1996 and January and February 1997 editions. This questionnaire will not only enable toe pubtishers to update the Ust of subscribers, but above aU to tailor toe contents of EURO INFO more effectively to the needs of SMEs.
The various measures outlined above will aheady help to bring about an appreciable improvement in the quality of the information supplied to SMEs.
The Commission wUl continue to monitor toe situation very closely.
Should the improvements faU short of expectations, other solutions might be considered.
Question No 81 by Ahn Gillis (H-1044/96)
Subject: Civil war in Sudan
Given toe ongoing civil war in Sudan, what steps is the Commission prepared to take to ensure the installation of a monitoring procedure in order to bring this tragedy to an end and to ensure adequate humanitarian aid to the people of Sudan who are the victims in this conflict?
Answer
The European Commission is foUowing toe situation in the Sudan very closely.
Development aid was suspended in March 1990 with toe agreement of toe Member States due to the prevailing lack of human rights, lack of both democracy or moves towards it and a lack of meaningful efforts to negotiate a peaceful end to the civil war in toe south of the country.
AU these issues remain on toe agenda of toe 'frank and crucial dialogue' toe Community initiated with toe Sudan in order to bring over to the Sudan its concern in these matters and to help overcome toe problems with a view to work towards normalization of toe relations between the Community and toe Sudan.
Despite these efforts, the Sudan has not responded positively, and toe problems raised have not been addressed by the government in Khartoum.
Thus, development aid remains suspended, though humanitarian aid still continues, principally to the population affected by the civU war in toe south of toe country.
Meanwhile, the Government of toe Sudan maintains that it is above reproach.
This view is not shared by the international community, but it does make it extremely difficult to see how the Sudan would agree to have independent monitors.
Then there is toe question of guaranteeing an acceptable level of security for monitors.
This would be almost impossible in toe present circumstances.
As the war continued, the displaced and war-affected population were - and still are - in dire need of substantial humanitarian relief.
The European Community has been able to rise to the challenge and has, since 1994, provided humanitarian aid amounting to over ECU 61m through toe European Community Humanitarian Office (ECHO) together with some ECU 35.2 of food aid and is still continuing to provide such aid.
Question No 82 by Arlene McCarthy (H-1047/96)
Subject: Value added tax on toe transportation of human remains within the EU
Given that in the single European market, there are inevitably an increasing number of cases of citizens moving away from the place where they would eventuaUy like to be buried or cremated, does the Commission not feel that it is both callous and inappropriate that a bereaved family, under the EC Sixth Directive, should be charged VAT on the transportation of a relative's remains, a process surely expensive enough without this additional cost?
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4-495/207
Although some Member States exempt this activity from VAT on the basis of a temporary derogation, the principle of its taxation is laid down in Community law, which does, however, authorize toe imposition of a lower rate of VAT.
The transport of corpses generally forms part of toe service offered by funeral directors and is covered by the relevant tax arrangements.
If, however, toe transport takes place separately, it is subject to toe general rules governing transport, i.e. it is taxed in full in toe Member State.
The Commission does not plan to propose any changes to toe current system.
In particular, it does not plan to exempt the transport of corpses from VAT.
Such a measure would deprive the transporting firm of its right to deduct toe tax for this type of service, thereby compücating the administration of its activities.
Question No 83 by Mark Killilea (H-1049/96) Subject: Continued payment of headage payments
Could the Commission inform me if a statement recently made by toe Irish Department of Agriculture is true, i.e. that it was deshed by the Commission to change the headage payment system, because, claimed the Government, toe Commission's attitude was toat the funds allocated to this measure represented too. high a percentage of toe overall allocation to the Structural Improvement and Rural Development element of the Operation Programme?
If that be the truth and as those payments are being made to disadvantaged areas, the weakest agricultural sections in our country, what proposals is the Commission suggesting that the Government should implement as an alternative, understanding as we do that the headage payments were a limited support and a very good one at that?
Answer
The major role played by compensatory allowances in helping maintain farm incomes in toe less-favoured areas is expressly recognised in the Community support framework for Ireland and the operational programme for agriculture, rural development and forestry 1994-1999.
However, there must be a proper balance between this measure and others in toe operational programme to ensure toat farming can become more self-reliant and integrated into toe rural economy.
According to a commitment in toe Community support framework, a special examination of the system of compensatory aUowances with a view to amending and better targeting the measure was carried out.
Subsequently, toe opinion of toe Monitoring Committee was requested in September 1996 on proposals which would make some economies in the operation of the measure.
The Commission is currently discussing this matter with toe Irish authorities.
Question No 84 by Bryan Cassidy (H-1052/96) Subject: Economic impact of Commission proposals
In Official Journal C 359 of 28 November 1996, DG XXIV issued an open invitation to tender for a 'Study on toe economic impact of the directive proposal on toe sale and guarantees of consumer goods' (96/C 359/19).
In Official Journal C 361 of 30 November 1996, DG XXIV issued another open invitation to tender for a 'Study on thè economic impact on small and mediumsized retati businesses of the implementation of the European Parliament and Council Directive on consumer protection in toe indication of toe prices of products offered to consumers' (96/C 361/09).
Why did toe Commission not carry out these studies before adopting the two draft directives?
Debates of the European Parliament
Furthermore this study will prepare toe report on toe application of the provision in the draft Directive concerning the smaU retail business (amendment 22, adopted by the European ParUament in toe first reading, and Article 13 of toe Council's common position).
Therefore this study responds to a request by the Council and especially toe European Parliament.
The study on toe economic impact of the directive proposal on the sale and guarantees of consumer goods is intended to broaden the considerations of the Commission in this field.
The Commission's assessment of toe economic impact had been criticised after toe presentation of the proposal.
This assessment was based on toe fact that toe gains in consumers satisfaction would outweigh toe costs.
The study has ben commissioned to confirm these considerations.
Question No 85*by Lone Dybkjær (H-1053/96) Subject: Environmental considerations and public procurement
Public procurement accounts for a very large proportion of overall consumption.
It is therefore essential for the environment toat environmental considerations be given priority in public procurement transactions.
This wiU not only benefit the environment in specific cases but will also help to set higher standards for the market generally.
Does toe Commission not take toe view that it would be appropriate to improve the legal situation in this sector by introducing clearer rules on toe right to impose environmental requirements?
Has it thought how the scope for environmental requirements might be extended to include the environmental assessment of a product's entire Ufe cycle?
Answer
The Commission recognizes the importance of environmental protection poUcy and the need, in accordance with Article 130 R of the Treaty, to take full account of environmental protection requirements when drawing up and implementing other Community policy.
Last November toe Commission adopted a Green Paper entitled 'PubUc procurement in toe European Union: areas of study for the future'.
This was intended to provide a framework for a wideranging debate on pubUc procurement policy and was addressed, among others, to toe European Parliament.
For example, toe directives make it possible to exclude any entrepreneurs guilty of an offence or serious misconduct resulting from the violation of legislation designed to promote environmental interests; to make toe performance of contracts awarded conditional upon the respect of environmental obligations; to include environmental aspects in toe technical conditions governing the characteristics of the works, supplies or services which are toe subject of contracts.
In this respect the purchasing bodies can encourage companies to adopt an active environmental approach by not rejecting offers of products containing reconditioned parts or recycled materials, when toe technical performances of these products meet toe requirements laid down in toe general conditions.
Subject to certain conditions, the directives also make it possible to include toe environmental protection objective in the selection criteria for contract candidates and in the criteria for awarding contracts, in order to identify the most economically beneficial tender.
The problem of unemployment in Europe is unanimously regarded as the issue of greatest importance to the future of toe European Union since it affects everday Ufe and toe social and professional future of millions of people.
The Commission President Mr Santer, has on various occasions expressed bis concern at toe employment situation in the Union and has launched a
Debates of the European ParUament
No 4-495/209 proposal for an employment 'confidence pact'.
Parliament has more than once expressed its views on the matter, for example by means of toe Rocard report on a reduction in working time.
During this rather difficult period, Italy is witnessing a confrontation between toe trade unions, the industrialists' association and toe Italian Government concerning toe renewal of toe national employment contract for metal and mechanical workers.
In an attempt at mediation toe government has proposed a monthly wage increase of Lhe 200 000, a proposal which the industrialists have rejected, thus causing the unions to call a national strike.
With due respect for national policies and toe freedom of the Member States' governments to take decisions, does toe Commission not consider that it should issue a call to toe Italian Government and toe opposing sides in toe dispute, in particular Federmeccanica, toe national industrialists' association, in order to ensure toat toe matter is satisfactorily resolved as soon as possible through the acceptance, of toe proposal put forward by toe government?
Answer
The Commission is trying to develop dialogue between toe social partners at European level. But it does not intervene at national level in this area.
Question No 87 by Hadar Cars (H-0004/97) Subject: EU support for toe former Yugoslavia
In its opinion adopted on 17 July on Union support for toe former Yugoslavia, toe European Parliament called for procurement under this project also to be open to those countries in Central and Eastern Europe which had applied for membership of toe Union.
The Council went some way towards meeting Parliament's wishes and allowed the countries of Central and Eastern Europe to submit some tenders under toe aid programme.
When, a few months ago, I asked toe representative of toe Council, Mr Mitchell, about toe further development of these arrangements, he advised me to contact the Commission.
What has been achieved since July?
How much of the sums earmarked has been paid out?
When wiU the reconstruction budget have been fully allocated?
How far have the Phare countries been aUowed to submit tenders and, of toe tenders received from these countries, how many were successful and what was their value?
Answer
All 1996 budgets have aheady been allocated and committed (Reconstruction, Refugees, Europe for Sarajevo, Phare national programme and essential aid Phare - EAP, including its reallocation).
In regard to opening up these programmes, and toe Phare programme in particular, to toe Central and Eastern European countries which are eligible for Phare programmes (toe two latest entrants being Bosnia-Herzegovina and Macedonia), toe normal conditions governing their participation in invitations to tender, consultations or private contracts were applied in accordance with the procedures.
Efforts were also made to interest these countries in projects, including publication on the Internet (which some delegations consult), toe presence of toe Commission in Sarajevo and Brussels, in order to meet and inform delegations from these countries, and encouraging these countries to contribute their skUls, within the project terms of reference.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 88 by Concepció Ferrer (H-0059/97) Subject: Humanitarian aid to the former Yugoslavia
In its report on humanitarian aid to the former Yugoslavia (A4-0174/96) ('), the European Parliament stressed toe need to provide funding for peace education programmes to help foster tolerance among peoples.
Has the Commission adopted any such programme?
Does it have plans to carry out joint actions with other international organizations in this or any other sphere? \
Answer
In implementing its programmes of humanitarian aid for former Yugoslavia, toe Commission has given the broadest possible interpretation to the possibilities offered by toe Humanitarian Aid Regulation.
This is in line with the resolution of toe European Parliament on Humanitarian Aid to Former Yugoslavia.
In this spirit the Commission financed programmes covering not only traditional humanitarian aid (food, medical supplies and shelter), but also restoration of basic services and psycho-social assistance.
At this time the psycho-social programmes carried out not only in Bosnia and Herzegovina but also in Croatia and toe Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, are rather better designed to relieve immediate post war traumas than to start a programme of active social integration.
Nevertheless, at least in one programme in Bosnia and Herzegovina, implemented through Children's Aid Direct specific provision is made for teachers training in the hope toat their work may then act as a catalyst for the younger generations to understand toe need to restore a genuine multiethnic society.
The Commission is also working closely with UNHCR and the High Representative to create enhanced conditions favouring the return of refugees.
In doing so, it is examining the possibility of using such bodies as the Bosnian Women's Initiative.
This grouping while newly established, does provide a model showing how different ethnic groupings can work towards common objectives.
Question No 89 by Thomas Megahy (H-0006/97) Subject: Age limits in recruitment to EU institutions: advertisements in Ireland
The use of age limits has been one tool in managing a high number of applicants.
Debates of toe European ParUament
No 4-495/211
The competitions of toe new Member States, where women participate to a great deal in working life, have proved that the age limits have not prevented women to apply.
Furthermore, the results have shown that an age is an important factor to mobUity.
At toe lowest grades, 51 % of the recruited officials were women, whereas, at the middle management grades, the correspondent figure was 41 %.
In addition, toe Commission has set annual objectives for female recruitment which has also given positive results.
Question No 90 by Kenneth Coates (H-0009/97) Subject: Unemployed citizens: freedom of movement
Preparations are under way for a number of marches by unemployed citizens to converge on Amsterdam for the Intergovernmental Conference.
Many unemployed people would like to participate and show their concerns about social exclusion.
However, in some countries such action may lead to toe loss of then benefits.
In such cases, toe loss of employment wiU imply toe loss of civU rights as well.
What action does the Commission propose to uphold the rights of every citizen to lobby toe European institutions and influence social poUcy?
Answer
The Community provisions on toe coordination of the national social security schemes for workers moving within the Community (Regulation 1408/71) guarantee that unemployed people can retain their unemployment benefits for a period of three months if they go to seek work in another Member State and register with toe employment services in toat Member State.
There is no Community provision on toe retention of unemployment benefits when an unemployed person moves within toe Community for other reasons - such as famtiy visits, holidays or to take parts in events.
In these cases the retention of benefits depends solely on the provision in the national legislation.
In so far as influencing social policy at Community level is concerned, the Commission is anxious to involve citizens - and organizations close to the citizen - in toe poUcy-making process in all feasible ways.
The 1994 White Paper on social poUcy was produced on the basis of a consultative process open to citizens.
The Commission intends that 1997 should see a strengthening of the dialogue between toe Community Institutions and the nongovernmental organisations concerned with social policy at Community level.
The new budget line (B3-4101) to aid charitable associations in toe spirit of Declaration 23 attached to toe EC Treaty will be deployed in support of this objective.
Question No 91 by Freddy Blak (H-0010/97)
Subject: EU aid for tobacco production
The Danish anti-smoking campaign has just established that smoking is the most frequent cause of death in Danish society, causing more deaths each year than road accidents and drowning, AIDS, alcohol, drugs, suicide and murder.
The EU actively supports European tobacco production and thus contributes to toe spread of diseases such as lung cancer, thrombosis, cerebral haemorrhages, osteoporosis and arteriosclerosis.
What political objectives justify such consequences?
When will toe Commission rectify toe disparity between its health policy and its agricultural poUcy?
Does it consider toat such paradoxes are good for toe EU's reputation?
Answer
The Commission considers tobacco control to be a key Community policy area and this is reflected in the priorities for action under the Third Cancer Action Programme which was adopted by this House and the CouncU on 29 March last year.
Moreover, in December 1996 toe Commission adopted a Communication on tobacco control poUcy outiining measures adopted at Community level to date to combat smoking, and suggesting further action which could be taken at Community level in toe future.
The purpose of this Communication is to promote a wideranging debate on toe policy to be foUowed in reducing smoking.
On toe same occasion, toe Commission adopted a Report into toe organisation of the tobacco market which proposes further reforms to the tobacco sector, following on from those made in 1992.
Changes proposed include increasing from 1 % to 2 % toe levy on producers' subsidies.
The sums raised would be put towards toe Community Fund for Research and Information on Tobacco, to be used to finance both projects on the dangers of smoking, and research into crop conversion and less damaging varieties of tobacco.
The adoption in paraUel of a Communication on smoking prevention, and the report on reforms to the raw tobacco market iUustrates the Commission's will to reduce the disparities to which toe Honourable Member refers.
Subject: Sanctions for failure to implement the Third Multiannual Guidance Programme
At a time when the Fourth Multiannual Guidance Programme is about to enter into force, certain Member States have not yet met the objectives set in the Third MGP and have even in some cases increased their fleet capacity.
How does toe Commission view this state of affairs?
Does toe Commission intend to apply sanctions? If so, what kind of sanctions?
Answer
The final results of toe Third Multiannual Guidance Programmes, or MGP III, will not be known in detaü until toe end of April 1997.
1. ­ Member States which have not met the targets set by their programme must, pursuant to the provisions of Article 10 of Regulation 3699/93, suspend any system of public aid to fleet modernization leading to increased fishing.
2. ­ The Commission can also introduce an infringement procedure under Article 169 of toe Treaty for failure to respect Community regulations.
3. ­ Finally, for the future, the Commission's position is clear and well known: the general fishing reductions decided under MGP IV must be applied to toe targets laid down under MGP III.
This method of calculation carries over to MGP IV toe share of reductions not achieved under MGP ΠΙ and can be considered as an additional sanction to the two previous ones.
On toe other hand, Member States which implement measures which go beyond the targets laid down in MGP III wiU receive a target credit at the start of MGP IV.
Question No 93 by Robert Evans (H­0013/97)
Subject: Children's books and images of ethnic minorities
Is the Commission aware of accusations that British publishers are deliberately excluding images of those from ethnic minorities from their books in order to sell them abroad ­ including in other Member States (The Observer of 27 October 1996)?
Would the Commission not agree toat this, if found to be of substance, is a shocking and unacceptable practice, toat cannot be tolerated?
In this, the European Year Against Racism, what action is toe Commission willing to take to investigate and combat such offensive acts which should have no place in our multicultural European society?
Answer
The Commission is not aware of the allegations raised by toe Honourable Parliamentarian, but it considers such practices, if found to be of substance, to be quite unwarranted.
Debates of the European Parliament
The Commission has no powers to investigate such practices.
It has, however, recently proposed toe creation of a European Monitoring Centre for racism and xenophobia ('), with a mandate to supply toe institutions of the European Community and the Member States with objective, reliable and comparative data on racism, xenophobia and anti-Semitism.
Moreover, during toe European Year against Racism a particular emphasis is being placed on highlighting the positive contribution toat ethnic minorities make to all Member States.
Question No 94 by Luciano Vecchi (H-0016/97) Subject: European Senior Citizens' ParUament
In 1993 a 'European Senior Citizens' ParUament' met in Luxembourg, and the venture, organized on the initiative of the European Parliament, proved to be a great success.
It has been proposed that toe experience be repeated in 1998, on the eve of UN 'International Year of the Elderly'.
Would toe Commission be willing to give poUtical and financial support to such a move?
Does toe Commission consider that the European Senior Citizens' Parliament could be a useful means both of encouraging old people to involve themselves in European pohtics and of raising awareness within the Community institutions of issues concerning the elderly?
Answer
The Commission warmly welcomed the eariier Senior Parliament and will do the same again if the Parliament chooses to go ahead with the current proposal.
The message coming from these and similar events - that society should listen to the voice of toe older generation - is fully in line with the Commission's efforts in this area in recent years.
The 1993 European Senior Parliament was successful in toat toe core message of participation by older people did get transmitted clearly - to the media and to concerned organizations across Europe.
A Senior Parliament taking place in 1998 would serve as a bridgehead for European participation with regard to the United Nations year for older people in 1999.
A new Senior Parliament will require careful planning and preparation, and the Commission is prepared to be involved at these stages if this should be requested by toe Parliament.
With regard to the question of financial support, the expenditure incurred by the 1993 European Senior Parliament was provided out of toe Parliament's own budget.
Therefore, the financial aspects of a new event needs to be carefully assessed by the ParUament itself.
Question No 95 by Philippe Monfils (H-0017/97) Subject: Relocation of a Belgian firm from the Walloon region to Ireland
Boston Scientific, a surgical equipment manufacturer, has recently decided to close its factory in Battice in Belgium and transfer its production centre to heland, leading to toe loss of 230 jobs in toe WaUoon Region, for toe simple reason toat it will obtain a significant level of European aid, since heland is an Objective 1 region.
Given toat toe philosophy behind regional aid is the convergence of toe economies of the various regions and toe growth of employment does not the Commission consider toat, in terms of economic ethics, there is a misuse of European subsidies when a company relocates - with disastrous social consequences and even though the company is in no way in difficulties - solely in order to obtain more European aid without having toe least impact on toe total level of jobs?
Answer
In conformity with Article 130A of the EC Treaty, toe Community develops and pursues actions leading to toe strengthening of its economic and social cohesion.
Aid from the Structural Funds is normally provided via the cofinancing of programmes, for example for industrial development.
19.2.97 national aid schemes lies with the competent national authorities, which select the individual projects benefiting from the scheme.
It should also be recalled toat all Member States provide assistance to foreign direct investments and have schemes similar to the one in heland supported by the Industrial Development Agency (IDA).
The Community contributes 51.1 MECU to the IDA Scheme for fixed asset support, with a global envelope of public support for the years 1994-1999 of 373.6 MECU.
This is around 13 %.
It should be emphasized that aid from toe Structural Funds for industrial development must be granted on toe basis of State aid schemes approved by the Commission beforehand.
As for national or regional authorities, they have toe option of making aid conditional on the maintenance for a certain period of jobs created.
With regard to State aids, heland is an eligible region under Article 92(3)(a) of the EC Treaty, which allows alti to promote the economic development of areas where toe standard of living is abnormally low or where there is serious underemployment.
Studies undertaken by toe European ParUament regarding the location or relocation of enterprises have shown that aid for investment is only one of many factors considered in reaching such a decision.
In toe Irish context, it would seem that other elements such as salary costs, tax regime and availability of skiUs influenced Boston Scientific's decision to increase its aheady existing activities in Ireland.
Question No 96 by Jan Andersson (H-0021/97)
Subject: Employment in the maritime transport sector
A study carried out for the Swedish National Maritime Administration shows that, in Swedish waters alone, 2500 jobs would disappear if taxfree sales ended.
Certain regions would be particularly hard hit.
Some 18 million EU citizens are currently unemployed.
Every effort must be made to preserve existing jobs and create new ones.
In view of this, does toe Commission not consider that toe current taxfree sales arrangements should continue within the European Union?
Answer
The Commission has aheady had occasion to stress its position regarding taxfree intraCommunity sales.
It believes toat these sales are incompatible with toe principles of completing toe internal market.
It reminds you that the Council, together with Parliament, adopted such a position back in 1991.
Finally, it stresses that toe Council has, nevertheless, granted toe sectors concerned a substantial period (more than 7 years) in which to adapt to the new situation, while at the same time deciding to put an end once and for all to taxfree sales inside toe European Community on 30/06/99.
The study mentioned by the Honourable Member has not been brought to toe Commission's attention.
It would nevertheless Uke to point out that toe removal of the tax advantage granted to a sector of activity does not necessarily mean toe end of this activity and it would be appropriate for the sector in question to employ this transitional period in order to develop a strategy which will enable it to adapt.
Question No 97 by David Morris (H-0026/97)
There are areas within the EU, such as Less Favoured Areas etc, where an agricultural subsidy is necessary for employment, environmental and social reasons.
However, at present toe largest proportion of EU support for agriculture goes to farmer and agri-businesses not faced with these problems.
With toe increasing pressure for open competition in agricultural production, what action does toe Commission intend to take to ensure that in future, subsidies are directed to where they are needed and where they wiU return positive benefits?
Answer
The less-favoured areas scheme is only one of a large range of measures in the Community agricultural and ruraldevelopment policy aiming at environmental, social and economic objectives.
Thus in addition to benefiting from specific compensation for their natural handicaps, farmers in less-favoured areas profit from other measures under Structural Funds Objective 5a for speeding up the adjustment of agricultural structures.
Furthermore, these farmers may benefit from support under Objectives 1, 5b and 6 for the development of certain rural regions.
The Common Market Organizations, which apply throughout the Community, have been reformed since 1992 in view of increasing competition, deregulation and globalization in agriculture.
The accompanying measures of the 1992 reforms were specifically designed to promote positive effects from farming.
In order to address future challenges to the agricultural sector and rural areas, the Commission is persisting in its efforts to better integrate, simplify and evaluate its ruraldevelopment and market policies under the Common Agricultural PoUcy, and thus direct subsidies to where they are needed and where they wiU return positive benefits.
Question No 98 by Alex Smith (H-0029/97) Subject: Nuclear shipments
On 13 January 1997 a ship arrived at the Scrabster port in the North of Scotland, delivering a cargo of nuclear fuel from Germany to the Dounreay nuclear plant; on the same day toe 'Pacific Teal' cargo ship left the port of Cherbourg in France with a cargo of 20 tonnes of high level nuclear waste from the COGEMA plant at La Hague destined for Japan after reprocessing.
WUl toe Commission examine both the prior notification regulations covering the sea shipment of nuclear materials, including radioactive wastes, and the safety provisions in place for the nuclear transports in toe light of the scientific and technical criticisms contained in toe report: 'The sea transport of vitrified highlevel radioactive wastes: unresolved safety issues', published by the Nuclear Control Institute in Washington D.C. in December 1996?
Answer
The safety of transport of radioactive materials, including the prior notification of shipments, is regulated by toe Member States on the basis of the IAEA Safety Standards for the Safe Treatment of Radioactive Material (Safety Series No 6-1995 edition - amended in 1990).
The modal aspects of safe transport of radioactive materials by sea are regulated by toe implementation of toe International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code (IMDG-Code) and the Code of Practice for the Safe Carriage of Irradiated Nuclear Fuel, Plutonium and High Level Radioactive Waste in Flasks on Board Ships.
Specific provisions are provided for by the CouncU Directive 92/3/EURATOM (') on the supervision and control of shipments of radioactive waste between Member States and into and out of the Community.
The corresponding standard document was established by the Commission Decision of 1/10/93 (2).
The procedure aims essentially at ensuring toat shipments of radioactive waste take place only after toe prior informed consent of all the States involved.
With regard to toe findings, published in toe NCI study of December 1996 on 'The Sea Transport of Vitrified HighLevel Radioactive Waste', it is noted that they were drawn to the attention of toe IAEA, the forum in which the international transport regulations are regularly reviewed and updated in order to take stock of toe newest level of knowledge in science and technology.
Question No 99 by Christine Oddy (H-0083/97) Subject: Transportation of nuclear waste
Is the Commission aware toat trains transporting nuclear waste pass through my constituency and that in January, in clear contravention of safety rules, the train was parked immediately next to homes and factories for 5 hours to allow staff to investigate a problem on one of toe flasks containing radioactive waste?
As this is one of three such incidents involving this train in recent years, what steps will the Commission take to press for greater security and safety measures in transporting radioactive waste?
Answer
The event mentioned by the honourable Member of Parliament is related to toe shipment of irradiated fuel on 4 January 1997 from the Dungeness B power station to Sellafield in Cumbria. • During a stop at Rugby for inspection of the three wagons carrying the irradiated nuclear fuel casks it was discovered that their weather covers were not properly padlocked.
These covers are not intended to provide either safety or security and their use is optional under the power stations operating procedures.
The covers serve to keep toe weather off the fuel flasks, thus obviating toe need to clean the flasks prior to unloading.
The event has received the necessary interest but according to the Commission's information has not revealed any radiological consequences since toe radiation level did not exceed the normal, low levels associated with fuel flasks.
Nevertheless the power stations operators were asked by toe competent authorities to take the necessary remedial measures to avoid reoccurrence.
On a regular basis during toe meetings of the Commission's Standing Working Group of national experts for the transport of radioactive material, events in the Member States are discussed to share information and learn lessons from transport practices in order to verify the adequacy of toe transport regulations and toe way these are implemented.
Question No 100 by Hugh Mcmahon (H-0035/97) Subject: Working time directive - excluded sectors
In toe original working time directive 93/104/EC (') transport was one of the original excluded sectors; however, toe Commission has, on several occasions, faithfully promised to introduce legislation in this area.
Answer
As a first step, the Commission requested each of the Joint Committees in the excluded sectors ,concerned to provide joint descriptions of the existing situation with regard to working time arrangements in their sector and to give their recommendations as to how, taking account of the specific nature of the work in the sector, the principles of the Working Time Directive might be applied.
The current situation following this request may be summarised as foUows.
In the Joint Committee on Rail Transport, agreement has been reached that the provisions of the Directive should be extended to all workers in toe industry, subject to the condition toat similar provisions be applied to other transport sectors at the same time.
In ah transport, there is agreement that the provisions of the Directive should similarly be extended to cover all ground staff (some 80 % of personnel).
Within the sea transport sector, toe International Labour Organization adopted Convention No 180 on seafarers' hours of work and the manning of ships in October 1996.
This successful outcome was achieved largely as a result of toe tireless efforts of both employer and trade union members of the Joint Committee on maritime transport, supported by toe Commission.
At present, there is no comparable progress to report in respect of road transport, inland waterway transport or sea fishing.
Furthermore, toe absence of Joint Committee or Social Partner structures in toe other excluded sectors makes progress difficult.
The Commission is currently preparing the White Paper on the sectors and activities excluded from the Working Time Directive, the aim of which will be to explore toe best ways of ensuring, with regard to working time, toe protection of the health and safety of the workers concerned as well as health and safety generaUy, with due respect for the principles of subsidiarity and proportionality.
The White Paper will analyze toe situation in respect of each of these sectors and activities and set out the Commission's views on toe possible options to take this matter forward.
The factual part of toe White Paper will be based on the work which has been undertaken by the social partners in toe Joint Sectoral Committees in each of the five transport sectors and toe sea fishing sector and on the studies which the Commission arranged to be undertaken in respect of doctors in training and 'other work at sea'.
It is expected that the White Paper in question wUl be issued in toe first half of this year.
Question No 102 by Ivar Virgin (H-0041/97)
In Sweden, the use of meat meal in animal feedingstuffs has been severely restricted and its processing and manufacture subject to very stringent requirements since 1988. Owing to the cases of BSE discovered in Germany, similar measures have now been caUed for there, too.
In view of this and of toe considerable risks which the use of meat meal in animal feedingstuffs entails, does the Commission intend to bring in restrictions and tougher rules on toe manufacture and use of meat meal?
Answer
The Commission has a three part policy with respect to meat and bonemeal use in animal feed. 1.
Part 3 has been in force throughout the Community since July 1994 with toe prohibition on feeding mammalian protein to ruminants.
The final stage of part 2 comes into force on 1 April 1997 with the compulsory use of the most vigorous rendering process for all mammalian waste material.
Several Member States have implemented part 1 on a national basis but, to date, the Commission has not been able to persuade the majority of Member States of the need for safe sourcing.
The Commission beUeves toat these three tiers of protection would give toe necessary health protection.
Question No 103 by Julio Añoveros Trias de Bes (H-0051/97)
The rales on automobüe distribution give independent garages the right to know toe technical specifications of automobües in order to carry out repairs efficiently and safely.
However, automobile manufacturers are supplying these specifications in software programmes toat are covered by toe provisions on toe protection of intellectual property.
What does the Commission intend to do about this practice which is contrary to the principle of freedom of movement?
What steps can independent garages take to defend theh interests?
Answer
Article 6, paragraph 1 of Commission Regulation 1475/95 of 28 June 1995 states that the exemption per category of automobile distribution agreements from the ban laid down in Article 85, paragraph 1 of the EC Treaty does not apply in certain cases.
In particular, under No. 12 of this provision, toe condition to which toe Honourable Member refers states that: 'Exemption does not apply when:
12) the manufacturer refuses to make accessible, possibly against payment, to repairers who are not part of its distribution network, the necessary techniques to repair or service contractual products or equivalent products or to implement environmental protection standards, unless this
By means of this provision toe Commission sought to give independent garages the widest possible access to the technical information necessary for repairing motor vehicles, while taking into account the rights attached to industrial property.
The inclusion of this clause in Article 6 of the Regulation should allow the competent national courts to check that the exemption conditions provided for by the Regulation are met and, if not, to identify the possibly anti-competitive nature of the practice in question in order to, in the given circumstances, award damages to compensate for toe alleged prejudice.
If, for example, automobile manufacturers, by using software programmes protected by intellectual property rights, try to prevent independent garages from gaining access to the technical information necessary for toe repair and maintenance of vehicles, the above provision appties.
Interested partieS who find themselves wronged by such actions can appeal directly to the national courts which, according to the Commission, must appraise in each individual case whether or not abuse has taken place and what indemnity should be taken into consideration.
National courts can also ask the Commission for advice and elements of fact and of law useful to toe procedure.
Question No 104 by Manuel Medina Ortega (H-0053/97) Subject: Fisheries meeting in Casablanca
What will be the objectives and substance of the fisheries meeting to be held in Casablanca in May 1997 and what impact will it have on the current fisheries agreement between the European Union and Morocco?
Answer
Casablanca 97 partnership - Fishing industries is a joint initiative by the European Commission and the Moroccan Ministry of Fisheries and toe Merchant Navy intended to encourage lasting partnerships between fishing sector industries in the Community and Morocco.
Around one hundred Moroccan projects were selected and theh particulars included in a catalogue distributed in the Member States with a view to stimulating technical, commercial and financial cooperation.
Interested European companies will have the opportunity to discuss the projects with theh potential Moroccan partners at contact days to be held on 26 and 27 May in Casablanca.
In regard to Spain, toe FROM is responsible for distributing this catalogue and identifying potential Spanish companies.
This initiative helps to effectively implement the measures introduced under Article 3 of the cooperation agreement for sea fishing between the Community and the Kingdom of Morocco.
Question No 105 by David Bowe (H-0055/97) Subject: Civil liability and the use of biotechnological products
Does the Commission think that liberalization in the contained use of genetically modified organisms should be accompanied by a provision ensuring strict UabUity in case of accidental escape or toe misuse of biotechnological products?
If not, why not?
Answer
The proposed amendment of Directive 90/21/EEC (') with its harmonized approach to risk assessment of activities and selection of containment measures, defined standards of containment facilities and relating administrative oversight to toe risk of the work being undertaken, will better safeguard the appropriate safety standards for human health and the environment.
As the Commission Proposal will result in a clarification and better definition of the containment measures to be applied to the contained use of genetically modified micro-organisms, the safety
No 4-495/219 standards applied to toe contained use of geneticaUy modified microorganisms wUl not be lowered. The proposed modification of Directive 90/219/EEC will not affect toe provisions to be applied to biotechnological products containing genetically modified organisms, which fall under toe scope of Directive 90/220/EEC (') on the deliberate release into the environment of genetically modified organisms.
Therefore, toe Commission does not perceive the need to include provisions on liability in toe proposed amendment of Directive 90/219/EEC.
The Commission is aware of the need to ensure that the issue of environmental liability is correctly dealt with at the Community level and decided on 29 January 1997 to prepare a White Paper on an EC liability regime.
Question No 106 by Astrid Thors (H-0058/97) Subject: Commission plans for financing Baltic cooperation
The Commission launched its Baltic initiative in 1996, but in practice cooperation in toe region is currently an extremely complex matter.
For instance, cofinancing between toe Interreg II C programme and the Phare and Tacis programmes is, in its present form, a very slow process, if at all possible.
Is the Commission prepared to introduce a new common financing instrument (own budget line) for the cofinancing of joint projects between Member States and Tacis and Phare countries in the Baltic region?
Answer
The Commission's Baltic Sea Region Initiative sets out how the Community supports cooperation among toe Baltic Sea States.
The Initiative mentions that it will be implemented with existing instruments and financial resources.
The Commission has developed mechanisms to encourage joint PHARE, TACIS and INTERREG projects.
The 1996 PHARE Baltic Sea Region Cross-Border Cooperation Programme and toe 1996 TACIS Cross-Border Cooperation Programme provide funds for the partner countries' participation in INTERREG activities.
The first projects are under preparation.
In cooperation with the different partners, the Commission is examining ways of overcoming practical difficulties in using PHARE, TACIS and structural funds as complementary sources.
The creation of a new budget line covering support to the Baltic Sea Region would not be compatible, however, with toe structure of the Community budget.
Question No 107 by Carole Tongue (H-0060/97) Subject: Implementation of European broadcasting quotas
In accordance with Article 4(3) of the 'Television Without Frontiers' (TVWF) Directive 89/552/EEC (2), toe UK Government has submitted information to the Commission concerning the implementation of Articles 4 and 5 of the said directive.
This wording, which is the result of a
Debates of toe European Parliament
19.2.97 difficult compromise, departs from toe Commission's initial proposal and from the wishes expressed by Parliament at that time.
It introduces an element of flexibUity indicating that attainment of toe majority proportion objectives can be overridden by technical constraints or economic imperatives.
The provisions amount to an obligation for Member States to behave in a certain manner, while allowing the discretion in choosing toe means of attaining toe desired result.
Being unable to agree on a more binding text, the Council nevertheless wished the flexibility of the legal instrument to be counterbalanced by toe monitoring role assigned to toe Commission.
Since toe entry into force of the Directive, the Commisssion has published two monitoring reports, covering respectively the periods 1991/1992 and 1993/1994.
These show that toe four broadcasters in the United Kingdom which have by far the largest audience share (90 %) transmit well in excess of the majority proportion of European works (60 % on average).
Certain thematic or recently created channels failed, however, to meet the objective.
Consequently, toe Commission has used the means at its disposal to persuade the UK government to do its utmost to ensure, by appropriate means and where practicable, that toe targets are met by aU the channels to which they apply.
The UK Secretary oT State for National Heritage has assured the Commission that the UK government places the highest priority on ensuring that the UK meets its Community obligations and that it will not hesitate to use, if necessary, toe legislative means at it s disposal.
At the same time, interim information covering 1995 and early 1996 was supplied to the Commission showing that the progress was being made with respect to toe channels that had failed to attain the majority proportion.
The Commission is continuing to monitor the situation and expects to receive further information from the UK authorities in the framework of the 1995/1996 monitoring exercise with a view to publishing its 3rd monitoring report later this year (1997).
As regards toe system itself, toe Commission, while acknowledging the need for an appropriate level of flexibility, has made it clear that it has never been happy with toe lack of legal security introduced by the phrase 'where practicable'.
This is why, in toe framework of its 1995 proposal to amend toe 'television with frontiers' Directive, toe Commission proposed to delete this phrase and to replace, for thematic channels, toe transmission time obUgations with investment obligation, toe latter being better adapted to the nature of such channels.
The Council, however, in its Common Position, decided to retain the 1989 provisions.
The European Parliament did not amend toe Council's position in its second reading last November.
The Commission wUl therefore have to continue to implement toe 1989 system.
In this respect, the Commission considers that the setting up of a Contract Committee, which is foreseen in the new Directive now entering the conciliation phase of the codecision procedure, is an important improvement on toe 1989 system.
Question No 108 by Ilona Graenitz (H-0061/97)
Subject: Food agency
Does the Commission believe it to be sensible for the task of dealing with the food sector to be split between certain departments in DG III (Industry) and other Directorates-General?
Would it not make more sense to set up a separate Directorate-General for this key area in which all responsibilities, in particular for protecting public health, could be grouped together and which would operate along the lines of the American FDA?
The Honourable Member is invited to refer to the Commission's decision on this matter on 12th February 1997, communicated to toe Parliament the same day.
Question No 109 by Doeke Eisma (H-0062/97)
Subject: Energy supply in Ukraine
At the beginning of September 1996 the Commission and toe EBRD set up an independent panel of experts to carry out a study into the possible construction of two 1000 MW nuclear power stations (Khmelnitskiy 2 and Rovno 4) in Ukraine.
Under its terms of reference, the panel was to examine the question of whether the construction of these two stations in the year 2000, following the closure of Chernobyl, was the most economical way of meeting Ukraine's energy require ments.
The report was to be submitted by the panel on 29 November 1996.
To preserve the independence of toe panel, there would be no interim reports.
Has toe panel aheady deUvered a final report?
If so, can toe Commission send a copy to Parliament?
If not, why not?
Will the Commission undertake to abide by the conclusions of toe report drawn up by independent scientists?
Answer
The Panel of experts had delivered its report (Volume 1) on 4 February 1997 to toe Commission, toe EBRD and to the US government which have contributed to its financing.
The second part of the report (Volume 2) is still under peer review.
It is expected that this part of toe report will be delivered early march.
At present the Commission, is examining the first part of the report. This includes, among other things, an assessment of toe methodology used and the assumptions made.
After completion of this analysis, toe Commission will make toe report together with comments available to the other institutions, interested persons and organisations.
Question No 110 by Antoinette Spaak (H-0065/97) Subject: Right to vote in municipal elections - failure of toe Kingdom of Belgium to comply
The Member States were to bring into force toe laws, regulations and administrative provisions to implement toe requirements of Directive 94/80/EC (·) before 1 January 1996.
Although that date is now long past, the Kingdom of Belgium has still not done so, blatantly flouting European law.
The Commission stated in its answer to my question H-0942/96 (2) that it had initiated toe procedure provided for in Article 169 of the EC Treaty.
When was the procedure initiated?
What deadline did toe Commission set for the Kingdom of Belgium in its reasoned opinion?
Will the Commission take account of toe constitutional reform needed for toe Kingdom of Belgium to comply with its obligations and the length of toe procedure when assessing the advisabiUty of bringing an action before toe Court of Justice?
Answer
As the Honourable Member correctly points out, Council Directive 94/80/EC of 19 December 1994, based on Article 8 Β (2) of the EC Treaty, determined toe procedures for exercising the right to vote in municipal elections for Union citizens residing in a Member State of which they are not nationals.
2) Annex to the Verbatim report of proceedings of 10 December 1996, p. 15
Debates of the European Parliament
With toe opening up of the Internal Market in 1993 and Finland's accession to the EU in 1995, the Finnish and European confectionery industry suffered exceptional restrictions on trade in the EU as a result of the confectionery tax payable in Finland.
Although, in the Internal Market, border formalities should have disappeared altogether, the product tax on confectionery proved impossible to collect in practice without some kind of control at Finland's borders.
This is an unnecessary obstacle to trade for Finnish and European confectionery manufacturers, particularly since all the other EU countries with the exception of Denmark have abolished theh confectionery taxes.
Does the Commission agree that toe confectionery tax causes problems with trade on the Internal Market and does it propose to take measures, in accordance with the powers accorded to it under Article 99 of toe Treaty of Rome, to abolish completely the confectionery tax in Finland?
Answer
Current Community excise legislation only allows Member States to retain national excise duties on mineral oils', alcoholic beverages and manufactured tobacco products and only on condition toat the introduction or retention of these national duties does not give rise, in trade between Member States, to controls when crossing a border.
Following question 3657/95 by Mr Toivonen, the Commission undertook an evaluation of the procedures for collecting toe tax on confectionery as applied in Finland.
In order to allow Finland to provide further details on this matter, it was sent an administrative letter at toe end of 1996.
Given the present situation, toe Commission does not consider it appropriate to submit harmonization proposals on toe subject of taxes on confectionery.
Question No 112 by Richard Hewitt (H-0069/97)
What consideration or discussions took place in or with toe Commission concerning the imposition of a windfall tax on banks in the United Kingdom by the UK Government in 1981, and its compatibility with the then European Community law?
Can the Commission identify toe relevant legislation and confirm toat none passed by 1981 or subsequently could prevent the use of a windfall tax by any Member State, as long as it does not discriminate unfairly between companies?
Answer
In the United Kingdom, Section 134 and Schedule 17 of the Finance Act 1981 introduced a one-off windfall levy on banks.
The levy did not give rise to any consideration or discussions in or with toe Commission concerning its compatibility with EC law.
As to toe second part of the question, it is not possible for the Commission to take a view on toe compatibility of tax legislation with Community law without seeing the particular legislation in question.
Question No 113 by Bernie Malone (H-0078/97)
Is the Commission aware that the project to build a tunnel unking Dublin Port with the national motorway network is being delayed due to concerns about structural damage to houses if the 'new Austrian tunnelling method' is used and the probable increase in pollution from exhaust emissions at the Northern Portal?
Will toe Commission confirm that no funding will be provided for the tunnel's construction until toe Irish Government submits an application and toe Commission is satisfied, on the basis of the information supplied, that there will be no harmful effect on the environment, especially in relation to the two concerns mentioned above?
Will the project receive the full 75 % funding from the Cohesion Fund once contracts are entered into before 31 December 1999, even if the work is not finished until after 31 December 2000?
Answer
The European Commission's financial participation in the Dublin port access project to date has been limited to a grant of 5.8 MECU from toe Cohesion fund for part of toe planning and design work for the project.
The assistance provided does not cover any element of land acquisition or construction.
The intention of providing this aid is to allow the authorities to thoroughly investigate all economic and environment aspects of toe proposed project.
The granting of assistance from the Cohesion Fund for one phase of project does not legally commit toe European Commisssion to grant aiding any subsequent phase of a project.
Therefore, a further separate application for assistance will have to be submitted before funding for the construction phase of the project can be granted.
Any such application will be judged on the merits of the economic and environment arguments advanced by the Irish authorities.
The agreement of toe Commission will be required before a decision is taken to grant assistance.
The Commission understands that a decision has been taken in principle to toll toe Dublin port access route.
Assuming toat this is toe case and assuming toat any application for Cohesion Fund assistance is acceptable on economic and environmental grounds, toe commission's practice in such cases would be to grant aid toe cost less the revenue generated by tolling.
In these circumstances, it would be unlikely toat the rate of assistance would be toe maximum possible (85 %).
In toe absence of an appUcation for assistance and toe information required to determine toe cost of toe project and toe revenue likely to be generated, it is impossible to say at this stage what toe aid rate would be.
Under the Community's budgetary system, Cohesion Fund assistance which is committed to a project from the budget of one year may be drawn down in the form of payments to toe beneficiary member state in subsequent years.
Other than an initial advance, periodic payments related to the project would be made only on condition that toe member state demonstrates toat toe financial and physical progress of toe project is proceeding satisfactorily towards completion.
Under toe current financial perspectives, commitment appropriations for toe Cohesion Fund are available only until the end of 1999.
There are at present a large number of competing demands for toe limited amount of Cohesion Fund assistance for heland which remains uncommitted.
Question No 114 by Karin Riis-jørgensen (H-0081/97)
The internal market rules on the purchase and sale of shares across borders also, according to toe EU Treaty, applies to the electricity industry.
The Danish Government has just tabled draft legislation which includes rules for the sale of shares by electricity companies.
The remarks appended to the bill include toe words 'toe aim of the legislation is to reduce the special incentive to local authorities to sell electricity supply holdings to companies not covered by the price rales laid down in the electricity supply act'.
Does not the Commission consider that this remark seeks to discriminate against foreign undertakings in theh ability to buy shares in Danish electricity companies if the legislation is adopted by toe Danish Folketing?
Answer
The Commission is not aware of any specific provisions within the provisions of the proposed legislation which would discriminate against the participation of Community undertakings in the share capital of Danish electricity companies.
Were such a restriction to exist in the draft legal text and be subsequently adopted, the question of its compatibility with EC Treaty provisions on establishment (Art. 52 ff) and capital movements (Article 73b ff) would be examined, in the context of toe exceptions to the freedoms in the relevant articles.
On the basis of the results of this examination, were the Commission to consider that toe measure was not in accordance with toe Treaty provisions, appropriate action would be taken.
Debates of toe European Parliament
Question No 115 by Gerard Collins (H-0091/97) Subject: Objective 1 regions
When does the Commission intend to table its initial proposals listing the regions (or parts of regions) which it considers should continue to quahfy for Objective 1 status, thereby qualifying for maximum Structural Fund grant aid levels, after 1999?
Answer
The list of less-developed regions eligible for assistance under Objective 1 of the Structural Funds was drawn up by the Council in July 1993 on the basis of à Commission proposal.
That list wUl remain valid until the end of 1999.
As regards the period after 1999, the Commission will propose revised regulations for the Structural Funds and a new Ust of less-developed regions in the 15 Member States as soon as possible after the European Council has laid down toe financial perspective for toat period.
The financial perspective and guidelines for toe future cohesion policy will be based on toe relevant proposals (SANTER package) which the Commission intends to adopt as soon as the Intergovernmental Conference has ended.
Question No 116 by James Fitzsimons (H-0092/97) Subject: Severe effect of soaring European paper prices on large users of paper in heland
Is toe Commission aware that European wood pulp manufacturers and suppliers of raw paper have increased theh prices significantly in recent months, and that the price of carbonless paper, copier paper and woodfree offset paper have gone up by as much as 20 % in the last three months, aU of which is having a severe effect on large users of paper in heland such as manufacturers, printing companies and SMEs, and will the Commission, as a matter of urgency, re-investigate possible paper price fixing among European paper manufacturers and will it outline what measures it will take to halt this rise?
Answer
The Commission is closely following price developments and the competitive situation in the paper and paper pulp sector.
The prices of products in this sector rise and fall constantly in response to a wide variety of factors, such as shortterm internal Community demand and toe pressure exerted by external demand.
Thus, in 1996, certain sources report toat paper pulp producers were forced to abandon a number of moves to raise prices because of a substantial drop in internal consumption (almost 3 %).
However, in this connection, the Commission is closely monitoring the possibility of producers employing concerted practices which might constitute breaches of the relevant provisions of Community law.
This is why the Commission is actively pursuing its investigations in the newsprint sector, although it is not possible at this stage to forecast precisely when a decision may be taken.
However, the Commission has noted the price increases for toe categories of paper referred to by the Honourable Member (carbonless paper, copier paper and woodfree offset paper), but as yet it has received no complaints concerning the employment of concerted practices among the producers concerned with a view to fixing or maintaining prices at an artificially high level.
The Commission will certainly examine very closely any information it receives which points to a breach of the competition rales laid down by the Treaty in these various sectors.
Question No 117 by liam Hyland (H-0093/97) Subject: Decrease in Irish projects under toe EU's LIFE programme for environmental projects
Can the Commission explain why in 1996 only one project from Ireland secured funding from toe EU's LIFE programme for environmental projects and why in 1997 only two Irish projects are to be cofinanced from this programme?
Answer
Each year, LIFE proposals are evaluated on merit three times: by panels of independent evaluators, by the Member state administrations and toe Commission.
Only the best succeed to become fuU projects with Community co-funding.
The linkage between Member states and success rate is not assured.
The relationship between number of proposals and success is more consistent and statistically credible - toe higher the number of proposals, toe higher probability of good proposals.
For 1995 (under LIFE I) less than 20 proposals were submitted to toe Irish Authority for transmission to toe Commission.
Only one was judged to be good enough (start date 1996).
For 1996 (under the new LIFE II) only nine proposals were submitted.
Two were judged on merit to be good enough.
At the beginning of February 1997 neither has been able to signal acceptance of toe conditions.
Question No 118 by Peter Sichrovsky (H-0105/97) Subject: Year against Racism - special programmes for young people
The European Union has devoted this year to toe fight against racism.
Violence motivated by racism and xenophobia is steadily growing, especially among young people.
What practical educational programmes and activities will the Commission be launching with a view to preventing or eliminating racial prejudices among young people by means of information campaigns and intercultural events?
What financial support will be provided for these activities?
Young people unemployed and uncertain about the purpose of life are focusing theh prejudices on religious as well as ethnic minorities.
How is toe Commission planning to improve young people's understanding of religious minorities as part of an anti-racism campaign in the widest sense?
With the prospect of the EU's eastward enlargement future Member States should aheady be involved in a campaign against racism, extremism and xenophobia among young people.
To what extent are there plans for cooperation with former Eastern Bloc countries in toe fight against racism (in toe widest sense)?
Answer
The European Year against Racism wUl be an exercise in partnership and communication: the Commission aims to involve aU possible actors in the struggle against racism, xenophobia and anti-semitism.
The Year will try to mobilise societies as a whole; there will be no European level information campaigns or intercultural events specifically aimed at young people.
Discrimination against religious minorities, such as anti-semitism, is encompassed in the general framework of the Year and toe subject will be dealt with appropriately.
The limitations both in time and in budget made it impossible to enlarge toe European Year against Racism to states which have applied for membership.
However, cooperation with the Council of Europe has been established and toe Year aims to build on the experiences of the Council of Europe's Youth Campaign against Racism.
Member States might have special events or campaigns aimed at the young and financial support at national level depends on the national budgets avaUable for toe Year.
Question No 119 by Anne Mcintosh (H-0855/96) Subject: Measures to prevent coastal erosion in the Naze area of North Essex
Could the Commission explain further the objectives of a European spatial development programme and detail the ways in which it is hoped these can be accompUshed?
Could the Commisison detail toe number and location of the projects that have been set up under the three year demonstration programme, further to its information document of February 1996, and toe reasons for theh choice?
Answer
The Commission does not intend to propose specific measures concerning coastal erosion in the Naze area of Essex.
The aim of toe European Spatial Development Perspective Programmes is, on the basis of a coherent vision of the main strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats characterising the European territory, to set up an integrated, multi-sectoral and indicative strategy for its develop ment.
This strategy is to ensure consistency and complementarity between the Member State's territorial policies and to coordinate toe territorial aspects of the Community policies.
As with any strategic document, toe ESDP is not however, merely intended to set out a number of objectives.
It is also a tool for identifying the measures and actions likely to contribute to these objectives.
With toe adoption of a third strand of INTERREG II on transnational cooperation in toe field of spatial planning, the Community has for toe first time the opportunity to demonstrate how toe principles and objectives of the ESDP can be translated into practical cooperation programmes as well as the benefits, notably in terms of cost-efficiency in the longer ran, to be derived from applying a spatial planning approach.
Seven such Programmes concerning respectively the Baltic Sea Region, the North Sea, toe North Western Metropolitan Area, toe Atlantic Space, South Eastern Europe and the 'Adrianube' (Central Europe, Adriatic, Danube) are expected to be submitted to the Commission by March the 10th 1997.
Moreover, five pilot actions under article 10 of toe ERDF, also geared to spatial planning have aheady been announced and wiU reach the Commission by the same date.
These pilot-actions are targeting respectively the Northern periphery, the Eastern Alps, the South Eastern Mediterranean, the Rhine-Lotharingia area and Mediterranean Doors (Portugal, Spain and Morocco).
A first draft of the European Spatial Development Perspective will be presented to the Ministers responsible for Spatial Planning meeting in Noordwijk early June.
The Perspective wUl be subject to a wide public debate to take place in the second half of this year.
Twenty- six project areas have been retained so far to constitute the basis for the demonstration programme on integrated management of coastal zones.
Twelve projects have been selected under the LIFE Programme.
Four networks, composed of a total of twelve areas have been pre-selected under the TERRA Programme.
Provided the negotiations regarding the contents and the eligibility of the projects have been completed successfully the final selection of the projects is expected to take place before the summer of 1997.
Two projects funded under toe PHARE programme have expressed interest to participate in toe demonstration programme.
Details of the project areas are freely available but are too long to be presented here.
The choice of the projects was based on the criteria announced in the tender documentation of the LiFE Instrument (XI/660/94) and toe TERRA Programme.
Question No 120 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0997/96)
Subject: Failure to announce a competition for toe project for the completion of the Athens Music Centre
On 12 July 1996 building Ucence No. 702 was granted for the completion of the Athens Music Centre.
However, on 18 October 1996 toe Panhellenic Association of Architects lodged an appeal to have this licence invalidated, and a decision is still pending.
This project which includes an underground garage and an opera or cultural or conference centre has an overall budget in excess of Drs. 10 biUion which will be paid for entirely out of the public purse and Community funds.
Given toat, for projects with a budget in excess of Drs. 1.5 billion (ECU 5 million) undertaken with public monies, a competition must be announced and published in accordance with the
No 4-495/227 provisions of Directive 93/37/EEC ('), wUl the Commission say whether the mandatory competition for the execution of the project has been announced and pubüshed and, if not, what measures it intends to take?
Answer
The Commission informs the Honourable Member that the announcement of a competition for the completion of the Athens Music Centre was pubüshed in toe Official Journal of the European Communities of 8 January 1997. (2)
