IN THE CHAIR: MR ANASTASSOPOULOS Vice-President of Gil-Robles.
I have the impression, Mr President, that some wicked virus crept into the attendance list yesterday.
(The sitting was opened at 9.00 a.m.)
Opening of annual session
Martens (PPE). - (FR) Mr President, I am now called 'Marcet s'.
President. - I declare the 1997-1998 session of the European Parliament open.
McGowan (PSE). - Mr President, it is simply that I do not find my name anywhere, despite the fact that I signed in.
You had better check the lists as well, please.
President - The Minutes of the last sitting have been distributed.
Are there any comments?
Poettering (PPE). - (DE) Mr President, this business is even more bizarre.
I have just seen in Wilfried Martens' Minutes, i.e., in the Dutch edition, that my name is listed, although spelt rather oddly, but it is listed.
Let me state quite explicitly that I was not present yesterday, did not put my name down on any list and am not claiming the daily allowance!
Pasty (UPE). - (FR) Mr President, I would like to point out mat I was present yesterday.
I am not on the attend ance register.
I probably forgot to sign in but I took part in the roll-call vote, which you can check.
Fontaine (PPE). - (FR) Mr President, just to say that unlike Mr Poettering I am not listed, yet I am absolutely sure I signed in and took part in the debate.
Medina Ortega (PSE). - (ES) Mr President, Mr Pasty is not on the attendence list for the same reason that that my name and the names of at least fifty people are not on the list today.
I have discovered that I appear under a different name: Mayera Ortega.
But that applies to most Members.
For example my good friend Mr Falconer appears as Falconerò; my colleague from our party Mr Dührkop Dührkop is down as Donnerp Dührkop.
Pompidou (UPE). - (FR) Mr President, my name was not misspelt, for the simple reason that it does not appear in the Minutes even though I was not in two places at once and was sure I was there yesterday.
