Bjerregaard, Member of the Commission. - (DA) Mr President, I thank the rapporteur, Mr TrakateUis, and the rapporteur for an opinion of the Committee on Research, Technological Development and Energy, Mr Pompidou, for the thorough work that has gone into this report.
As we all know and as has been stressed in the debate here today, the regulation of the GMO area is a difficult matter, and it is high on the poUtical agenda.
Directive No 90/219 is a horizontal directive, and it buüds on the precautionary principle.
It is intended to ensure that the development of the technology does not endanger human health and the environment.
The Directive thus regulates the rules of play or the safety precautions that must be observed by laboratories conducting research and carrying out work on GMOs.
This Directive covers a highly technical area, which is in a process of rapid development.
It is a field which also has considerable implications both for employ ment in the EU and for industry.
Experience with the Directive hitherto has shown some weaknesses.
Let me name them; they are: unsatisfactory classification of GMMs, inadequate guidelines on risk assessment and containment measures, lack of flexibility as regards adaptation to technical development, lack of consistency be tween notification and administration requirements on the one hand and the risk inherent in the activities in question on the other hand, and excessive administration in connection with low-risk activities.
As a result they may be obUged to weaken their containment and control measures in order to comply with this amendment, and that is of course not desirable.
The Commission's proposal observes the precautionary principle, while at the same time setting out to solve the
