I wish, however, that we could get the Council and the Commission to build us into the procedure in a more effective way so we could reaUy feel part of that process.
for revenue. Nevertheless, within the agreement there are some new developments which show exciting possibilities for using these agreements towards the development end of the spectrum.
We often talk about the dilemma of whether these agreements should be trade agreements or development instruments.
Parliament has repeatedly said we should move more and more towards seeing them as development instruments.
Jöns (PSE), draftswoman of the opinion of the Committee on Budgets. - (DE) Mr President, Commissioner, ladies and gentlemen, I am speaking here on behalf of the Committee on Budgets, and I should like to emphasize the following points.
As the previous speaker has already said, we are deaüng here with agreements that were initialled in December 1995 and May 1996.
So it is good to see that the Guinean agreement begins to move in that direction.
For example, there are for the first time specific grants for scientific research and for study and training built into the agreement as well as the amounts of aid offered for general revenue, some of which will find its way - this is always the defence of this kind of system - through the priorities of the relevant govern ment to schools, clinics and development of that nature.
So we should not rule out that aspect at all.
I hope, in any case, that the kind of evasive tactics demonstrated by part of the budgetary authority will now be a thing of the past. The code of conduct that we adopted along with the 1997 budget was intended to ensure that this would be the case.
Not to be consulted until five (and in the other case four) months after the initialling of the agreement is bad enough!
However, the potential for these agreements is enormous.
A country like Guinea, which is one of the poorer countries of Africa, has a great potential if it can achieve stability, which I think it has now achieved despite the instabUity in the region, if it can exploit the economic zone which it possesses.
This is a problem because it faces invasion by foreign boats.
I am not talking about EU boats - at least we have an agreement which regulates that - but a number of fleets from elsewhere, particularly from Asia which have been found in violation of the territorial waters of the Republic of Guinea.
Guinea is, at the moment, very vulnerable.
We really have to look, as the EU, at that problem and look towards a regional solution right along the West African coast.
This, I know, would be welcomed by most of the countries in Africa with which we have agreements.
In other words, the Commission, as it generally has done in recent years, took the Council's decision on provisional implementation as a sufficient legal basis for paying out the first tranche.
One might almost be forgiven for thinking that our opinion counts for nothing any more!
This is what we agreed jointly - and I emphasize the word jointly - in the code of conduct.
In 1958 Guinea became famous in Africa because when De Gaulle said to the French colonies 'Do you accept the new French Union of all the colonies?', they all said 'oui' but Guinea said 'non'.
And the famous 'non' of Sekou Touré was met with a whole lot of reprisals from the French.
Guinea suffered quite severely for standing up for its principles and becoming independent.
IronicaUy, two years later, all the other colonies which had said 'oui' were also being given their independence, no questions asked, and a lot of aid.
I have a lot of admiration for the Guineans for standing up for independence but they have a backlog from that period to cope with.
So, as we approach countries like Guinea, we should be generous in our interpretation.
The code of conduct also lays down that the CouncU, the Commission and the Parliament should do everything possible to ensure that the procedures involved in the legislative process are carried out as rapidly as possible.
But this also means - and I would ask the Commission to take particular notice of this - that the Commission should begin its negotiations with hon-member countries in good time, so as to leave enough time for the rest of the process to proceed properly.
It would be nice if these requests did not have to be repeated, rather like a prayerwheel, every two years!
In conclusion, I should Uke to say a few words about the comments from the other committees of Parliament.
The Committee on Budgets is absolutely right on its procedural points.
Every time this Parliament makes the same point saying: 'we want to be concerned and consulted before the agreement takes effect', and every time it is ignored.
Mrs Jöns has made the point very precisely and I would support aU her arguments.
Likewise the Committee on Development and Cooperation, as I mentioned, made absolutely the right point in saying that these agreements should be seen as tools of development. These agreements are important.
We have to ask ourselves why that is the case.
One of the reasons for that overcapitalization is the vast sums spent by national governments on subsidizing longdistance fishing industries. That in itself means that there is a race to fish in order to make enough money
Debates of the European ParUament
Teverson these developing countries.
Our fishing agreements ought to be coherent with our development policy.
This is the theme, or one of the major themes, of the report I shall produce for this Parliament in May.
That is exactly what international fishing agree ments, as concluded by the European Union, are all about.
It is that subsidy that has caused the overcapacity that has led to the depletion of stocks.
It is a cycle that has to end in order to protect fish stocks for fishermen for the longterm future.
I have to stress this. We have a responsibility aU of us, particularly to the developing world, and particularly to countries which were our colonies in the past to whom we have not shown the respect they deserve.
We need to help them develop their own fishing industries so that in the long run - and I emphasize the long run because it will be the long run - they are able to export their own fishing products and get the added value of their fishing industries for their own development.
Dührkop Dührkop (PSE). - (ES) Mr President, I welcome the renewal of the fisheries agreements with Angola and Guinea, because they benefit European fishermen and consumers and also help Angola and Guinea who thus receive a significant source of income.
That is why the Liberal Group in this Parliament is the only one that is actually facing reality and saying that the public subsidization of fishing agreements has to stop.
That is why within both these agreements we are tabling amendments that say that the fishing fleets, the vessels and the owners themselves who benefit from these agreements should pay the fuU cost of these agreements, and that they should no longer rest on the taxpayers of the EU - not because that is bad in itself but because through subsidy we have overcapitalization and continued depletion of stocks.
I ask this Parliament to support those amendments to ensure that we safeguard our fish stocks and fishing communities for the future.
The mutual benefit is assured by making fishing by local and European fleets complementary activities.
This, it seems to me, is a question of a few balanced and fair protocols entailing a cost for the European Union which is reasonable.
The ACP countries must also be helped of course with monitoring measures in their waters in order to conserve stocks and also to drive out those factory ships fishing Ulegally.
Against this background, it is no surprise that the European Parliament should be less and less happy with international fishing agreements.
This is an institutional problem but it is also a procedural one, a methodical problem, given that the Commission system atically confronts the European Parliament with a fait accompli.
We must deplore, as we so often need to, the fact that the Commission and the Council relegate the Parliament to a very marginal role beneath its station in budgetary mat ters.
The information submitted to us by the Commission is normaUy very scant and consultation of Parliament -which is one of the budgetary organs - scarcely takes place; everything arrives late, wrong and when the agree ments have already come into force.
It is important that this should not penalise fishermen and other people connected with the industry, who depend on this vital aspect of fishing policy: international fisheries agreements.
An approach to these Agreements which is solely based on cost benefit is reductive, limiting, equivocal; what it is aimed at is ending the socalled international fisheries agreements.
Against such a tide, I should say that this is a good Agreement - I am talking about the Agreement with Angola, for both the European Union and for Angola itself.
Let us hope that the shipowners and Community fishermen using it can benefit in full from the fisheries opportunities which have already been negotiated. ated.
I think the European Parliament should act, but extremely carefully if it wants to kick the CouncU and the Commission into action, for fear of accidentally kicking our own fishermen in the process.
And we must stamp out the image which some people with a bad conscience have of these fisheries agreements with the ACP countries; they are not agreements which harm the population nor do they threaten the local fisheries sector.
The European Union pays a fair amount of financial compensation and the agreements are beneficial for the development of those countries and at the same time keep our fishermen in employment.
Crampton (PSE). - Mr President, I agree with a lot that has been said about the lack of consultation with this ParUament and 1 hope that the Commissioner present will take that back to the Commission.
We need to be fully involved in the presentation of these fishing agreements right from the start.
Like Mr Mccartney, I went to the Angolan Fishing Ministry and talked to them and they were dreadfully concerned.
They were going to sign the agreement be cause they needed the money. I do not think that these developing countries, especially countries that have gone through such a tragedy as Angola, should be forced into that position.
But they need the money and they were saying quite clearly they do not think they will be able to monitor this agreement. They wUl not be able to monitor 1, 500 km of coastline with seven outdated boats and no airplanes.
So we ought to help them. We ought to help
Bjerregaard, Member of the Commission. - (DA) Mr President, the Commission would firstly like to thank the rapporteurs for these two exceUent reports concerning fisheries agreements.
As regards the new protocol to the fisheries agreement with Angola, it should be pointed out that this creates increased scope for a variety of fishing activities.
Also, the fishing opportunities for shrimps and prawns are maintained and a new type of fishery is included - pelagic fishing - which opens up the agree ment to other EU countries such as Germany and the Netherlands. It should be emphasized that the agreement's period of validity has been extended from two to three
Debates of the European Parliament
Bjerregaard years, and this longer rimescale will provided more stable conditions for Community shipowners.
For the first time, the annex to the protocol allows the Angolan authorities to introduce a biological recovery period.
The financial compensation has been increased to take account of the new fishing opportunities created by the agreement.
which are specific to the European Development Fund.
It must also be considered that even if the outcomes which the Commission is aiming at in the negotiations on fisheries agreements are of a commercial nature, in the sense that a balance is being sought in the concessions made on each side, these outcomes respect and are in keeping with the measures being applied as part of development policy. ,
On Amendment No 4, the Commission would point out that the protocol does not impose an obligation on Guinea to submit a report on the detaUed use of the sum referred to in Article 6.
However, the Commission will make enquiries of the Guinean authorities as to how this sum is used, and will then pass on the information to the European Parliament in an appropriate way as part of the current information procedures.
As regards Amendments Nos 1 and 3, the Commission agrees with the sentiment they express, but is unable to endorse them.
The Commission already ensures that information is provided in an appropriate way on implementation of the agreements, in particular at the meetings of the Committee on Fisheries and prior to each meeting.
Nor can the Commission accept Amendment No 2, since when the interinstitutional agreement was negotiated, only the Mediterranean financial protocols were included in the classification as non-compulsory expenditure.
The other agreements were excluded, such as those on fisheries.
In conclusion, I should like to say that I will of course ensure that the Commissioner responsible, Mrs Bonino, is made aware of the fact that Parliament wishes to be involved in the process at an earlier stage.
The Commission cannot approve Amendment No 4, since the Council lays down negotiating directives individually for each agreement, but not when every protocol is renewed.
When the Commission negotiates these protocol renewals, it ensures that the directives it was given by the Council are respected.
Cunha (PPE), rapporteur. - (PT) Mr President, ladies and gentlemen, I apologise for the late hour, but I felt that I should tell the Commissioner that I am deeply disappointed with the response that she has given to our very modest proposals for amendments.
I must say here, before the European Parliament, that the Commission has not given the slightest signal of changing a perfectly ridiculous situation which is insulting to the European Parliament when it comes to Fisheries Agreements.
As far as Amendment No 3 is concerned, calling on the Commission to present us with a progress report in the last year of an Agreement's duration, the Commission does not even seem wUling to approve such a suggestion.
Quite frankly, we are being messed around!
Turning to the fisheries agreement with Guinea, the Commission believes that the new conditions for fishing by the Community fleet in Guinean waters can ensure that the agreement is implemented more effectively, while observing the fundamental requirement that fishery resources should be conserved.
Through this new protocol, the Commission and the Guinean authorities have sought to encourage EU vessels to return to Guinean waters, a return which was started under the previous protocol, and to provide Guinea with resources that will enable the country to improve the supervision of its fishing zone and combat illegal fishing.
The technical and financial provisions of the protocol have this dual aim, and this applies in particular to the provision concerning the special financial contribution for fisheries surveillance bodies.
As for the amendments which have been tabled, the Commission cannot endorse the four amendments, since the first three fail to make a clear distinction between the aims, the thinking and the procedures which are specific to the fisheries agreements with the ACP countries and those
Debates of the European Parliament
I. Question to the Council
Question No 27 by Anne Mcintosh (H-0001/97) Subject: Approximation of excise duties across the European Union
Does the CouncU hope to prioritize in 1997 the approximation of excise duty rates, so that no Member State is placed at a competitive disadvantage within the Single Market?
Has the CouncU drawn up a timetable for such action, or does it have plans to do so during 1997?
Would the Council agree that failure to approximate excise duties distorts the intraCommunity trade between Member States, to the detriment of both the individual consumer and the national exchequers?
Answer
In reply to Mrs Mcintosh's very interesting question, I must point out that Directives 92/79/EEC, 92/82/EEC and 92/84/EEC on minimum excise duty rates provide that the Council shall review these rates every two years on the basis of a report by the Commission.
When undertaking this review, the Council is required to attach particular importance to the potential risks of distortion of competition and deflection of trade caused by differences in the rates of excise duty applied in the various Member States.
However, any amendment by the Council of the existing legislation on excise duty rates requires a proposal from the Commission.
But in the first report it submitted to the Council in 1995 in connection with the review referred to above, the Commission refrained from making any proposal regarding an adjustment of the rates.
This was because the Commission felt that this matter should be the subject of consultation between the national governments, business circles and other interest groups with a view to a more in-depth analysis of all the factors and the consequences of possible adjustments in rates that might result.
Questions to the Commission
Question No 37 by Maren Günther (H-0136/97) Subject: Return of Bosnian refugees and the reconstruction of Bosnia
What steps does the Community intend to take to assist in the reconstruction of Bosnia and the return of the refugees in 1997?
Which Commission services are responsible for which programmes, and what financial resources are available for projects to assist the return of refugees from Germany in 1997?
Answer
The Commission attaches a very high priority to helping refugees return to Bosnia and Herzegovina.
It is working with those Member States which are hosting refugees, and in particular Germany which has the largest number.
The Commission is also working closely with international agencies such as UNHCR, as part of a coordinated approach to the problem.
FoUowing the decision of the London Peace Implementation Conference, a working group, consisting of the High Representative Office, the UNHCR and the Commission, was set up to examine ways of improving conditions for refugee return.
The Commission participates actively in this group.
Among the issues which affect the rate of refugee return are security conditions, political agreement of local authorities and the material conditions in the areas of return whUe recognising that the greatest number of refugees are currently in Germany.
All programmes are open to help the return from anywhere in the Community.
The Commission makes funds available for projects once lists of identified refugees have been delivered by the German
Debates of the European Parliament
In 1996, the Commission devoted almost ECU 50m from various budget lines for former Yugoslavia to refugee return.
This money was used to finance UNHCR operations, as well as a series of NGO housing repair projects in the Una Sana canton and in areas such as Sarajevo, north of Mostar, Posavina and the Anvil area.
The total ECHO funds in 1996 for Bosnia and Herzegovina were ECU 142.5m, of which ECU 38m was for UNHCR.
In 1997, the Commission proposes to allocate ECU 122m for reconstruction and refugee return out of a total budget for Bosnia of ÉCU 206m, drawn from various budget Unes for former Yugoslavia.
This money wUl be used to provide water, housing and economic activities in a number of regions where large numbers of refugees are expected to return.
This integrated approach wiU be complemented by further assistance from ECHO which, in addition to providing humanitarian aid in the form of food, medical supplies and psycho-social assistance, will also be funding medical centres and schools.
For 1997, ECHO has a budget of ECU 98m of which approximately 80 % is expected to be spent in Bosnia and Herzegovina for reconstruction and refugee return.
Question No 38 by Birgitta Ahlqvist (H-0149/97)
Subject: Tacis crossborder cooperation
The Commission has taken a welcome decision to allocate ECU 8.7 m under Tacis crossborder cooperation to the development of border-crossing posts between Russia and Finland.
One of the four projects covered by the decision is the Salla route between Apatity in Russia and Rovaniemi in Finland.
Financial support is needed in order to man the customs post between Finland and Russia on a permanent basis.
If this is not done, it will be necessary to make an approximately 500 km detour via Akujärvi and Murmansk.
What progress has been made as regards development of the border-crossing post on the Salla route?
Can the Commission say when the SaUa route will be opened for normal traffic on a regular basis?
Answer
The Commission welcomes Mrs Ahlqvist's oral question and her support for the decision to fund the SaUa border crossing under the Tacis Crossborder Cooperation Programme.
The development of an international border crossing post on the Barents Corridor is crucial for the development of the region.
Work has already started in this area.
The Murmansk regional authorities have allocated considerable resources to develop the road connections and the Finnish Government has allocated funds to build a new Finnish border post.
The Tacis project wiU fund development of the SaUa border station in Russia.
This wiU include both technical assistance, such as customs training, and the provision of smaU-scale infrastructure, which is new territory for Tacis.
The Commission is in the process of developing detaUed plans for the project which wiU be subject to the Finnish and Russian authorities reaching agreement on its international status;
The Commission trusts that the official international status of the crossing wUl be recognized soon in order for work to proceed.
This will be discussed at the Barents Corridor Business Development Seminar on 19 March 1997 and the Commission looks forward to hearing of the latest developments.
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The EU no longer has the same central role in administration of Mostar as until the end of 1996.
But it is supporting closely the Office of High Representative in its effort to implement the Dayton agreements as they apply to Mostar.
The OHR is leading the investigation of the disgraceful events in Mostar on 10 February.
The International PoUce Task Force has completed its investigation report with commendable thoroughness and speed.
The EU fully endorses the report's recommendations and js putting its weight behind pressing those responsible to implement them.
One step which it is actively pursuing is to ban entry to EU countries by those named in the IPTF report as having taken part in the shootings.
More generally, the EU will need to have in mind the attitude shown by the responsible authorities, as it pursues the approach to countries in the region which was agreed at the General Affairs Council of 28 October 1996.
Question No 40 by Niels Sindal (H-0164/97) Subject: New Member States
Has the Commission already ruled out some of the countries that have applied for membership, in the light of the evaluation reports?
What are the Baltic States' chances of inclusion in the first round?
Answer
The Commission treats all applicant countries on an equal basis in preparing its opinions on their applications for membership.
Each is being assessed on its own merits, against the background of the criteria established by the 1993 Copenhagen European CouncU. .
On the basis of the Commission opinions and the other reports it is preparing, the European Council will determine when the negotiations should begin and with which of the applicants.
Question No 41 by Hubert Pirker (H-0166/97) Subject: Eastward enlargement and security of external borders under the Schengen Convention
Austria's accession to the Schengen Convention wUl take effect from 27 October 1997.
Securing the EU's external borders with the applicant countries Slovenia, Hungary, the Czech Republic and Slovakia has been achieved at great expense.
Austria is therefore particularly concerned to know how long these external borders will exist as such; this depends in turn on the enlargement of the EU to the East.
What does the Commission regard as a realistic timetable for the individual appUcant countries wishing to join the EU?
Answer
Enlargement to include the candidate countries of Central and Eastern Europe is an historic challenge.
The Commission hopes that enlargement will be realised as soon as possible.
However, the timetable for enlargement wiU depend mainly on the progress made by individual countries in their preparation for membership.
The political and economic conditions for membership were laid down by the European Council in Copenhagen in June 1993.
If, as planned, the Inter-Governmental Conference (IGC) concludes in June, negotiations for membership with the candidate countries of Central and Eastern Europe could open early in 1998.
On the basis of the opinions and the reports which the Commission will submit following the IGC,
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 42 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0189/97)
Subject: Amnesty International report on human rights in Turkey
In its report on human rights in Turkey, Amnesty International recaUs that the European Parliament endorsed the Customs Union between Turkey and the EU, which entered into force on 1 January 1996, only on condition that the human rights situation in Turkey improved.
However, the report then goes on to describe as deplorable the human rights situation in Turkey, particularly given the wave of torture and abuse accompanied by disappearances and summary executions from the early 1990s onwards, constituting new forms of human rights violations in Turkey.
Does the Commission consider that the conditions laid down by the European Parliament for approving the ratification of the Customs Union with Türkey have been fulfilled and what steps will it take to bring Turkey into tine with international agreements on respect for human rights particularly in view of the forthcoming meeting of the UN Commission on Human Rights on 10 March in Geneva?
Answer
In its report of 9 October 1996 on the development of relations with Turkey since customs union came into force, the Commission described the human rights situation and the democratization process in Turkey.
In doing so, the Commission regretted that the reforms initiated in 1995 were not pursued in 1996, and that the Turkish Government has not pursued with sufficient determination its action against various policies complained of in the report.
In all its contacts with the Turkish authorities, the Commission stresses the importance of restarting the process of democratic reforms interrupted in 1996.
This message was repeated again recently to the Turkish Undersecretary for Foreign Affairs, Mr Oymen.
The matter is even more important at a time when Turkey is making numerous diplomatic efforts to re-apply for accession to the Community.
It should also be emphasized that, out of the five financial packages which the Community had undertaken to extend to Turkey on 6 March 1995, only one is functioning, and that ParUament's resolution of 19 September 1996 imposed severe restrictions upon it.
It should also be recalled that, as it stands at present, customs union operates virtuaUy entirely in favour of Community enterprises.
The Commission can also confirm that, in the implementation of the 1997 MEDA programme, it intends to comply with the European Parliament's resolution by concentrating its projects in the areas of human rights defence and the promotion of civil society, and that it also intends to furnish aid to the most deprived population groups affected by the events in southeastern Turkey.
The Commission will have the opportunity to deal with this question at the joint working group with the European Parliament.
With regard to the 53rd session of the United Nations Commission on Human Rights, which opened in Geneva on 10 March 1997, the Dutch presidency will speak on behalf of the European Union and wtil review the situation in various countries.
As far as Turkey is concerned, the Union's common position will be arrived at by the usual methods.
Question No 43 by Bernd Posselt (H-0192/97)
What is the current state of relations between the EU and the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, and how have those relations been affected by the Kosovo question and/or the fact that the EU would like to open an office in Kosovo?
Answer
The position of the EU on relations with the FRY is set out in the conclusions of the General Affairs Council of 28 October 1996.
At that meeting the Council adopted a regional approach, proposed by the Commission, which aims to develop contractual relations with certain countries of southeastern Europe, among them the FRY, provided certain conditions are met including full compUance with international obligations such as the Dayton/Paris Peace Accords.
In November 1996 there were local elections in Serbia.
Initially the government refused to recognise the results in a number of municipalities won by the opposition.
Following international pressure, including from the EU, the results have now been recognised.
But the EU still seeks progress from the government on other aspects of democratization, such as the opening of a dialogue with the opposition, access to the media and reform of the electoral law.
The EU has also made clear that it sees progress on Kosovo as essential for normalization of its relations with the FRY.
It has consistently pressed the authorities in Belgrade to show a more forthcoming attitude, in particular towards granting the people of Kosovo a large degree of autonomy within the FRY.
The EU will also continue to press its request to establish an EU presence in Pristina.
It will continue to press the FRY authorities to allow a visit to Kosovo by Mr van der Stoel (Special Representative of the OSCE Chairman-in-Office) and Ambassador Lutz (Chairman of the Working Group on Ethnic and National Communities and Minorities).
Question No 44 by Jan Andersson (H-0194/97)
Subject: Enlargement of the EU to include countries in Eastern and Central Europe
The debate on enlargement of the EU to include countries in Eastern and Central Europe generally focuses on the economy. However, other issues, such as health and safety at work and workers' rights, are at least equally important.
What is the Commission doing to ensure that the negotiations with the applicant countries also address those issues, so as to avert the risk of social dumping in the future?
Answer
Employment and social affairs form an important part of the Community acquis which new members will have to accept when they join, including rules on health and safety at work and the rights of employees.
In the Opinions, the Commission Will assess the ability of candidate countries to take on the obligations of the acquis.
In the perspective of enlargement, they need to continue progress in aligning their rules with Community standards.
The Community has established a pre-accession strategy to prepare candidates for membership, including financial support from Phare to encourage social sector adaptations.
Applicants must not only adopt Community rules but also implement them effectively.
The Commission encourages the applicants to pursue efforts to improve their social systems and more generaUy, their living standards.
Question No 49 by Ulla Sandbæk (H-0139/97) Subject: Consultation arrangements and Berne Convention
Can the Commission provide information on the form of consultation arrangements between the Commission and Member States with non-governmental organizations prior to and during the standing committee meetings of the Berne Convention for each of the last five years?
Question No 50 by Maartje van Putten (H-0157/97) Subject: Cooperation with NGOs and the Berne Convention
NGOs have completely free access to the meetings of the Berne Convention Standing Committee.
The preparatory meetings of the Member States and the Commission at which agreement is reached on the EU's position are, however, entirely closed to NGOs.
They are not even allowed to attend these meetings as observers.
How does the Commission explain this difference in the treatment of NGOs?
How, in the Commission's view, can the exclusion of NGOs be reconciled with the emphasis placed on closer cooperation with them in the Fifth Environmental Action Programme?
Joint Answer
There are no specific arrangements for formal consultation with non-governmental organisations with regard to meetings in the framework of international conventions.
Therefore, no formal consultations with non-governmental organisations have taken place prior to or during the Standing Committee meetings of the Bern Convention during the last five years.
However, the Commission is in regular, informal contact with non-governmental organisations in the field of nature conservation, in particular as far as EC nature directives are concerned.
Non-governmental organisations are now invited as observers to the Scientific Working Croup of the Habitats Committee.
The Commission intends also to suggest, to Member States a similar involvement for non-governmental organisations during the next ORNIS Committee meeting.
Official coordination meetings of Member States and the Commission are of a different nature.
The 15 member States and the Community, represented by the Commission, are Contracting Parities to the Bern Convention.
During international convention sessions, formal coordination meetings of the Member States and the Commission are held on the spot, in order to identify common positions on the agenda's points, whenever possible.
These meetings replace the meetings of the same kind which take place in Brussels and are a regular practice during international events.
This does not prevent, of course, the Member States and the Commission from having information meetings and exchange of ideas with NGOs or other bodies outside such Community coordination meetings.
The coordination meetings with the Member States are following the internal rules of the Council.
The Commission cannot decide on the participation of NGOs to these meetings.
Question No 51 by José Apolinário (H-0130/97) Subject: Lisbon-Almada rail link
Following the recent report in the Portuguese newspaper 'O Independente' regarding the Commission's position on the Lisbon-Almada rail Unk, will the Commission, in its talks with the Portuguese Government, give due consideration to the benefits of this link for the population on both sides of the Tagus, taking into account the thousands of people who need to commute to Lisbon to work, while obviously respecting environmental protection requirements and the relevant environmental legislation?
Answer
The technical meetings that took place between the Commission and the Portuguese authorities regarding the Chelas-Coina rati link were intended to ensure the correct appücation of the appropriate environmental legislation, especially the provisions of Directive 85/337/EEC (environmental impact).
In this context, the Commission shares the honourable Member's concern to attach the utmost importance to considering the impact of this project on the population groups concerned, especially as regards their right to be consulted on the project before work begins.
Question No 52 by Sören Wibe (H-0134/97) Subject: EU environmental standards in relation to the three new Member States
In certain areas, the countries which became Members of'the EU on 1 January 1995 have a higher level of environmental protection than exists within the Community as a whole.
It was agreed during the negotiations on accession that the new Member States would be able to retain their higher environmental standards for a transitional period of four years.
Parliament called on the Commission to pubUsh the initial results of the overhaul of Community environmental legislation and discuss them, inter alia with the Environment Council, before 1 April 1997.
The Commission has also been caUed on to submit a binding timetable for the overhaul.
Can it be assumed that the environmental standards that apply in the EU when the overhaul is completed will have to afford the same high level of protection as the standards that apply in Finland, Sweden and Austria, or will those three countries have to give up their more stringent rules after the transitional period?
Answer
The Commission is reviewing the provisions of the Treaty of accession which allow Austria, Sweden and Finland to keep their higher standards.
A followup to Resolutions B4-0834, 0835, 0836, 0837, 0859 and 0860/96 of the European Parliament on the Review Process has been prepared by the Commission.
In order to intensify the dialogue between the Member States and the Commission, a first coordination meeting was held on 20 June 1996 and a second one on 12 November 1996.
On 28 April 1997, another High Level meeting with representatives from the three new Member States wiU take place to take stock of the progress made.
The Commission is currently working on the publication of the interim results of the Review Process.
The Commission's efforts in the Review Process aim at a high level of environmental protection.
But the Review Process depends not only on the Commission but also on the support and the contribution of the three new Member States and on the agreement amongst all the Member States.
Question No 53 by Anita Pollack (H-0135/97) Subject: Wild-caught primates
Will the Commission review the use and import of wild-caught primates covered by Directive 86/609 (') so that it is fully implemented by placing the burden of proof on potential importers of wild-caught primates to show that they cannot use captive bred animals; and how does the Commission intend to alter the collection of statistics to obtain accurate data from each Member State on the number of animals used in experiments?
Answer
The use of primates is essential for biomedical research.
The number of primates needed for this research in the Community cannot be provided by the European breeding centres, despite the setting up of the European Primates Resources Network, which has received support from the Commission.
Import of primates from third countries remains thus essential to satisfy the needs of European biomedical research.
According to Directive 86/609/EEC on the protection of laboratory animals, only purpose-bred animals may be used in experiments.
Animal welfare organizations have repeatedly expressed concern that import of animals from third countries casts uncertainty as to their origin, in particular that some animals could be wild caught.
The Commission will address this concern with the competent authorities of the Member States once the question of statistics of animals used for experimental purposes is resolved.
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The Commission reserved an amount of ECU 200, 000 in the administrative part of the 1996 budget.
This amount has been spent on 'green' reviews of the Commission's administration as a followup to the adoption of a Strategy Statement in the field of Green Housekeeping in December 1995.
The reviews performed in the latter half of 1996 focused on three priority areas, namely Office Activities, Mobility and Housing.
The recommendations resulting from these reviews have been incorporated into a draft action plan to be approved by the Commission.
Hopefully, that will happen in March 1997.
These recommendations vary from substitution of paper by E-mail to energy audit of Commission buildings.
The action plan wiU have no budgetary implications for 1997.
For the following year the amounts wUl be fixed through the budgetary procedure.
The objective of this action plan wiU be to introduce environmental considerations in a number of daUy activities of the Commission, in such a way that this Commission by the end of its mandate will be applying the highest standards of environmental care across the whole range of its administration and be seen to be taking a lead on this.
Question No 55 by Hugh Mcmahon (H-0146/97) Subject: Infringement Procedure A92/4132 (Pilmuir Quarry)
What is the current state of play with regard to the infringement procedure being carried out against the UK Government in relation to Pilmuir Quarry?
In particular, can the Commissioner responsible for the environment inform the House of action to be taken by the Commission in response to the UK Government's denial of breach of Directive
Answer
Infringement procedure A92/4132 concerns the development of the former Pilmuir Quarry in Renfrewshire, Scotland as a waste landfill site.
The Commission has corresponded with the UK authorities concerning the aUeged breaches of Directives 85/337/EEC on environmental impact, 75/442/EEC and 78/319/EEC on waste and 80/68/EEC on groundwater.
In response, the UK authorities have indicated that as development consent in the form of planning permissions was appUed for in December 1987 and granted in April 1991, this is a transitional project to which the provisions of Directive 85/337/EEC do not
Debates of the European ParUament
Question No 56 by Karin Riis-jørgensen (H-0186/97)
Subject: Distortion of competition under the European eco-labelling scheme
The EU eco-labeUing scheme for paints and varnishes covers only water-based paints and varnishes, thereby excluding a number of linseed oU-based products which are at least as envtionment-friendly as water-based products.
How will the Commission prevent distortions of competition when certain environment-friendly products may carry the eco-label and others may not?
How was the scope of the eco-labelling scheme for paints and varnishes determined?
Answer
The eco-label can be awarded to aU products which meet the criteria adopted on 15 December 1995 (Commission Decision of 15 December 1995 estabtishing the ecological criteria for the award of the Community eco-label to indoor paints and varnishes) and published on 6 January 1996 (96/13/EC).
The eco-label for paints and varnishes is not exclusive to paints and varnishes based on water.
Products based on flax oil or water are not excluded as long as they meet the environmental criteria.
From this point of view there is neither a limitation of the scope of products nor a distortion of competition amongst paints and varnishes based on different raw materials.
Question No 60 by Alan Donnelly (H-0101/97)
According to recent reports in the press (see FT 19 January 1997, page 1), ship losses increased by more than 10 % in 1996 due to the world's ageing shipping fleet - with a resultant substantial increase in loss of life and environmental damage.
What is the Commission currently doing to encourage owners of ageing, unsafe or substandard ships to break up their older vessels and to invest in new ones?
Does the Commission envisage taking further steps in the future to strengthen current measures?
Answer
The Commission shares the strong concern expressed by the honourable Member and others about the ageing world shipping fleet and beüeves that this is a contributory factor to the persistence of substandard shipping.
The Commission consequently considers that the most immediate and effective response to those conditions is to make every effort to try to ensure that ships that are substandard because of ageing are not allowed to operate.
Amongst the many safety measures that have been adopted within the Community the most important tool for eliminating substandard shipping from European waters is the Port State Control Directive which has been in force since July 1996. (')
That Directive stipulates that ships of certain categories, after a certain age, will have to be subject to an expanded inspection which may lead to detention if international safety requirements are not met.
It must be stressed that the age of a ship is not, in itself, an indication that such a ship is substandard since many other factors such as manning, operation, maintenance and the overall condition of the ship, also have to be taken into consideration.
Taking into account the particular high loss ratio and the age distribution of the bulk carrier fleet, the Commission has initiated a study with the purpose of estabUshing the factors that contribute to this particular problem and of establishing a basis for taking decisions on any further actions that are required to strengthen the current measures.
Clearly these activities wUl continue, and the Commission looks forward to the positive consequences for shipping standards.
Question No 61 by John Cushnahan (H-0107/97)
Subject: Mullaghmore Interpretive Centre
Is it true that the Commission intends to penalize the Irish Government because of its decision to abort the construction of the Mullaghmore Interpretive Centre?
Answer
The construction of an interpretative centre at Mullaghmore in the Burren, County Clare was one of the projects included in the Tourism Operational Programme for Ireland for the period 1989-1993.
Since work on this project was abandoned in November 1993 at an early stage, the Commission, in consultation with the Irish authorities, is considering making a corresponding reduction to its financial contribution to the programme.
Any such reduction should not be regarded as a sanction but as a financial correction.
Question No 62 by Brian Crowley (H-0109/97)
Is the Commission aware of the negative effect on the operation of the LEADER Programme in Ireland, caused by the inordinate delay of the Irish Department of Agriculture in not appointing a secretariat to the Irish LEADER network and will it indicate what action it will take to ensure this problem is overcome immediately?
Answer
The arrangements for the estabUshment of the national networks under Leader II in each of the Member States were discussed with them at a number of meetings between September 1995 and February 1996.
Among the requirements was that the operation of the network was to be aUocated to external providers on the basis of an invitation to tender to be published in the Official Journal.
The Irish proposal 'was approved by the Commission in June 1996 and an external provider has now been chosen by the Irish authorities.
Three Member States have established national networks to date and the Commission is encouraging the remaining Member States to finalize their arrangements without delay.
In general, the Commission has noted the concern expressed by various local and national agencies about the delays in establishing these networks.
Question No 63 by Heidi Hautala (H-0121/97) Subject: Groupeuro
In the context of the Information Programme for European Citizens (Prince), the Commission has established a network of independent EMU experts called Groupeuro.
The Commission demands that they sign a statement whereby they agree that 'they may speak in a personal capacity for a part of their lecture, provided that they state expücitly that they are going to do so and do not express any views contrary to those of the Commission.'
Does the Commission think that this declaration is in accordance with the ethnics normally expected of scientists and with democratic, pluralist values?
Can the Commission pubtish all the relevant decisions and documents concerning Groupeuro including the lists of experts who have signed the agreement?
In which other contexts does the Commission demand simUar declarations?
Answer
The 'Prince' priority information action programme encompasses Groupeuro, which was created as part of the action: 'Euro: one currency for Europe' in order to meet the increasing requests which the Commission receives for officials to speak at conferences and seminars on EMU.
In an attempt to meet these requests without always sending an official, a number of specialists in the Member States were asked if they would be interested in becoming members of Groupeuro and to speak on EMU on occasions when the Commission had been asked to send an official to give its point of view.
Following two information seminars, where the Agreement was discussed at length, 170 persons decided to sign an Agreement with the Commission.
It is clear that in aU other situations members of Groupeuro are free to express their own views.
However, when replacing the Commission at these events, the members would normally give the Commission's point of view, as this is the very least which is expected of them by the organisers.
That is why members should not express views contrary to those of the Commission when a Commission official is not available.
Moreover, Groupeuro members are frequently included in panels with other speakers opposed or sceptic to EMU.
For this reason the Commission does not consider this Agreement to be contrary to the ethos normally expected of scientists nor contrary to democratic pluralist values.
In similar circumstances for other areas of the Community's policies, the Commission might require a likewise declaration.
Members are invited to speak at a particular conference or seminar directly by the Commission in cases where a Commission official cannot intervene and it is therefore not possible for conference organisers to invite a member directly.
Documents relating to Groupeuro will be sent directly to the honourable Member.
Question No 64 by Petrus Cornelissen (H-0123/97) Subject: Swine fever: EU aid for farmers
Can it be assumed that in the near future the Commission will take a decision guaranteeing an EU contribution of 70 % towards the costs of a purchasing scheme for pig farmers with farms within the areas where there is a ban on transport?
What scope does the Commission see for compensation for pig farmers with farms situated within the area where there is a ban on exports but outside the area where there is a ban on transport or a purchasing scheme?
Answer
The Commission presented to the Management Committee on 17 February 1997 a draft regulation laying down exceptional measures for supporting the pigmeat market in the Netherlands.
This regulation, which was published on 4 March 1997 and applies retroactively with effect from 18 February 1997, specifically provides for:
- the granting of aid to the Dutch authorities for the supply of pigs for fattening and piglets originating in protection and surveillance areas;
- - the processing of these animals in knackers' yards; 70 % financing from the Community budget of the expenditure associated with this aid.
The regulation lays down the total number of eligible animals at 350,000 pigs for fattening and 450,000 piglets originating from the 'Venhorst' and 'Best' regions of the province of North Brabant.
With regard to possible Community support for pigmeat producers outside the areas of protection and surveillance, the Commission cannot intervene, because the legal base for such measures is extremely restrictive and the budgetary resources are limited.
Question No 65 by Otto von Habsburg (H-0131/97) Subject: The Commission's human rights commitment
At the European Parliament's sitting of 20 February Commissioner de SUguy spoke with some feeling about the problem of Mordechai Vanunu, the nuclear spy imprisoned in Israel, and made it clear the Union advocates respect for human rights without regard for the country or individual concerned.
What is the EU's position on the solitary confinement of the former President of Panama, Noriega, under inhuman conditions in the United States after a trial that cannot be described as flawless in a court that was anything but competent to try him?
Answer
In its Communication to the Council and the European ParUament of November 1995 'The European Union and the external dimension of human rights policy: from Rome to Maastricht and beyond', the Commission set out in detail its principles, activities and instruments on human rights issues in the international context.
This basic position remains unchanged.
Question No 66 by David Morris (H-0137/97) Subject: Subsidized EU exports of beef to South Africa
Subsidized EU exports of beef to South Africa have increased signficantly in the last three years.
This has damaged the meat industries of neighbouring countries, especiaUy Botswana and Namibia, who traditionally suppUed beef to South Africa.
When this problem was raised at the November CouncU of Agriculture Ministers, Commissioner Fischler agreed 'to keep a watchful eye on developments'.
Has he now seen enough to recognize the damage that subsidized EU export of beef is inflicting on these Southern African countries?
What action does the Commission intend to take to rectify the present unfair situation?
Answer
In August 1996, the South African authorities wrote to the European Commission alleging that subsidised imports of beef from the Community were posing a threat to the South African meat industry and were also affecting other developing countries in the Southern African region that export to the South African meat market.
In its response, the Commission highUghted the commitments undertaken by those who subscribed to the conclusions of the Uruguay Round and emphasized that it had no wish to undercut local prices and requested that South Africa supply detailed information to support its claim so that the Commission could act accordingly.
Unfortunately, the South African authorities did not reply to this request.
Nevertheless, in an effort to improve the situation concerning the meat trade within the 'Southern Africa development Community' the Commission decided with effect from 1 February 1997 to regroup together the African countries within the same geographical zone.
The new measures mean a reduction of approximately 8.5 % in the level of export refunds for certain cuts of boneless meat, exported from the Community to the regions of Eastern and Southern Africa.
This reduction is additional to the 10 % global reduction in the level of refunds in the meat sector, for exports to all destinations, which took effect from 15 January 1997.
Trichloroethylene is banned outright in Sweden for health and safety reasons.
The Commission has called for the ban to be Ufted.
Does it not consider the use of trichloroethylene to pose health risks?
On what legal grounds does it intend to get rid of this important Swedish environmental provision?
Answer
Trichloroethylene, a solvent mainly used for metal degreasing by professional users, is classified as carcinogen category 3.
Category 3, which is the lowest category, means that there is a cause for concern for man owing to possible carcinogenic effects but in respect of which the available information is not adequate for making a satisfactory assessment.
The Commission does therefore see a certain risk and considers that the exposure of workers to trichloroethylene, as for many other chemicals used by industry, needs to be controUed.
There is however a wide range of options available to control exposures in the work place.
Studies of exposure to trichloroethylene in Finland and Sweden indicate relatively low levels of exposure.
The Commission considers that a ban on trichloroethylene is excessive in relation to the risks.
Trichloroethylene is on the Commission's priority list for the development of Occupational Exposure Limits (OEL) for chemical agents at work.
In the course of this work all currently available data concerning risks to workers' health wiU be evaluated.
The Commission may subsequently propose appropriate legislative measures within the framework of the existing legislation on occupational safety and health.
A risk assessment is being carried out under Regulation 793/93 on Existing substances.
In the draft presented by the UK it is indicated that certain occupational exposure situations may pose risks in poorly controlled work places.
However, the conclusion cannot yet be drawn that industrial uses of trichloroethylene should be restricted at Community level.
The Swedish measures to prohibit the sale, transfer and use of methylene chloride and trichloro ethylene for industrial purposes fall within the scope of Article 30 of the EC Treaty which prohibits aU measures having equivalent effect to quantitative restrictions on imports.
A Member State may in certain cases rely on Article 36 of the EC Treaty or mandatory requirements recognized by the Court of Justice, inter alia, public health or the environment, to justify derogations from the principle of free movement of goods.
The Court of Justice has consistently held that it is for the national authorities that invoke Article 36 or any mandatory requirement to prove in each specific case that a measure that hinders intraCommunity trade is necessary for the effective protection of such a requirement and that the import of the product concerned would constitute a serious risk.
Under the principle of proportionality, the national measures have to be proportionate to the Objective to be attained, i.e. to public health or the environment.
Question No 68 by Hans Lindqvist (H-0144/97)
Subject: Anonymous committees
A report by Parliament reveals there to be at least 424 committees of officials set up by the Commission as well as possibly a thousand or so committees of experts.
The report which Parliament has now called for illustrates clearly why discoveries such as mad cow disease are able to continue year after year without action being taken.
When neither the agendas nor the bases on which decisions are taken nor the minutes of meetings are made public, anything whatsoever can be swept under the carpet and serious problems and differences of opinion can be kept from the public for years.
Next time, antibiotics, salmonella or some geneticaUy engineered food could be the source of a new disaster and a new crisis.
This must be prevented at aU costs.
EU secretiveness has caused enough problems, suffering and economic damage.
11.3.97 really necessary? If not, what is the Commission prepared to do to reduce the profusion of committees?
Answer
The Commission has taken numerous steps intended to create greater transparency within committees.
On 21 December 1995 it informed the European ParUament, pursuant to the Wynn resolution of 26 October 1995, of the activities it proposed to undertake in this respect.
Thus, for example, in June 1996 it forwarded to Parliament, for the first time, an annual report on the functioning of the committees over the previous year.
It takes steps to ensure strict compUance with the agreements under which the European Parliament is to be informed on the activities of committees (Plumb-Delors, Klepsch-MiUan, 'modus vivendi') and is follow ing up the new commitments on the transparency of the proceedings of the committees which it gave to the Committee on Budgets in September 1996.
There are today some 400 committees at the Commission.
Only about one-fifth of these committees have been set up by the Commission to facUitate consultations prior to its initiatives.
Most of the others were instituted by the legislative authority to assist the Commission in the exercise of the enforcement powers granted to it.
In this context, it should be stressed that the Commission always endeavours to avoid creating new committees when use can be made of existing committees, and that it called for a simplification of committee procedures in its memorandum in the current Intergovernmental Conference.
As for the crucial question of health, to which the honourable Member refers and which is directly linked to the functioning of the ten or so existing scientific committees, I should recall the first series of measures which the Commission laid down and which the president of the Commission explained at the February part-session of the European Parliament (separation between, on the one hand, legislative responsibiUties and those of scientific consultation and, on the other hand, those of control; increase in transparency and diffusion throughout the decision process and control activities).
Question No 69 by Maria Berger (H-0145/97)
Subject: EUR-1 for cargoes
What action does the Commission intend to take to deal with the problem arising from the fact that EUR-1 import certificates may not be issued for parts of a vessel's cargo from'non-Community countries? (eg problem of Israeli citrus fruit: EU - Austria - Kopar - Italy?).
Austrian companies are at a severe competitive disadvantage under the present rules, as Austria has no seaports and therefore can often take only part-cargoes.
Might a possible solution be for the EU certificate to be lodged at the border and the part-cargoes recorded there?
What other solutions are under consideration?
Answer
The question of the honourable Member of Parliament concerns goods imported under preferential treatment into Austria.
The goods are imported from a country with which the European Community has concluded a free trade agreement (e.g. Egypt, Israel, Morocco) or a country which gets preferential treatment under an autonomous regime (e.g. a country benefiting from the scheme of generalized tariff preferences).
All preferential trade arrangements provide for direct transport.
The direct transport rule states that preferential treatment only applies to products which are transported dtiectly between the Community and the country party to the agreement.
Products constituting a single consignment may be transported through other territories or temporarily warehoused in other territories provided that they remain under the surveillance of the customs authorities in the country of transit or warehousing and do not undergo operations other than unloading, reloading or any operation designed to preserve them in good condition.
The dtiect transport rule does not allow the spütting of consignments in a country of transit as this could create, and facilitate, fraud.
As soon as the goods have arrived in the Community, either directly or through a transit country, the customs authorities can issue replacement certificates for each portion of the split consignment.
Question No 70 by Robert Evans (H-0147/97)
Subject: Nonrecognition by the Italian Government of divorce in another Member State
Is the Commission aware of the Italian Government's refusal to recognize certain judicial pronouncements made in other European Union states?
One of my constituents, originally from Italy, married a British citizen from whom she was later divorced.
She subsequently married another British citizen who has unfortunately died.
Now my constituent wishes to return to Italy and is experiencing difficulties with her pension and passport, as she is still registered with her first married name.
She has been told that she must go through a lengthy and expensive legal process in Italy to have her divorce recognized.
Does the Commission not agree that it is unacceptable for EU citizens to have to duplicate judicial pronouncements for them to be recognized in another Member State?
Answer
The Commission is aware of the problems faced by EU citizens confronted with the nonrecognition of their divorce.
At the present stage, two international instruments deal with recognition of decisions on matrimonial status, The Hague Convention of 1.6.1970 and the Luxembourg Convention of 8.9.1967, established by the Commission internationale de l'Etat civil.
None has been ratified by aU the Member States, and none deals with the determination of jurisdiction.
After Maastricht, the need to deal with the issue on the model of the Brussels Convention was recognized.
The Council now aims at establishing a Convention on jurisdiction, recognition and enforcement of judgments in matrimonial matters - the 'Brussels Π Convention'.
It will give clear rules on direct jurisdiction and enforcement procedures for foreign decisions on matrimonial matters, i.e. on divorces, separations and annulments of marriage.
In particular custody of children anciUary to a divorce poses problems.
With the signature in The Hague on 19.10.1996 of the Convention on jurisdiction, applicable law, recognition and enforcement in respect of protection of children, the works at Council level have received a fresh impetus.
The Commission is contributing actively to these works.
The aim of such an EU Convention, is that a divorce judgment pronounced in one Member State will be recognized in all other Member States without any additional procedure.
It will be a major step forward in improving the daily life of citizens.
Subject: Environmental damage caused by reform of the COM in oUve-oil
Is the Commission aware that the projected reform of the COM in olive-oil is environmentally harmful?
Does it realize that the land planted with olive trees cannot be utilized for other purposes, because of its sloping position and marginal role, and that olive growing consequently serves to prevent erosion and desertification, enabling real woodlands to be preserved in more remote areas?
Has the Commission assessed, the damaging impact of the reform in Andalusia?
Does it understand that olive groves are the lung of Andalusia and cover land not suitable for other uses?
How will the Member of the Commission responsible for the environment seek to forestall this assault on the Mediterranean economy and environment?
Answer
In response to Parliament's resolution of 25 October 1996, on 12 February 1997, the Commission adopted its paper on the Olive OU Common Market Organization.
The paper analyses the current CMO and suggests ways to improve it.
These range from keeping the current system, with modifications so that it can respond more to the need of the market and be more controllable, to introducing a substantially new system based on a unit aid per tree, modulated in the Member States according to regional and other conditions.
The Commission is looking forward to hearing from ParUament and the CouncU of Ministers on this subject.
When the EU institutions and interested parties operating in the sector have had an opportunity to make their view known, the Commission wiU make a concrete proposal which should be decided upon in time for the 1998/99 Olive Oil marketing year.
The Commission recognizes the importance of olive tree cultivation in marginal areas and, particularly, its role in the fight against desertification.
The Commission is also aware of the mono-cultural nature of oUve production in large parts of Andalucía and its impact on both the environmental and local employment.
Whichever of the options put forward in the Commission document is eventually chosen, it is clear that the system proposed wUl be such as to safeguard the production of oUve oU in the European Union as well as employment in the producer areas, while respecting the existing Community environment legislation.
Question No 72 by Clive Needle (H-0155/97)
Third country agreements concluded by the EU require developing countries and countries in transition to secure 'good governance' (sound management of aU available resources for their development) and, in particular, to curb corruption.
Meanwhile, however, bribing by European firms of these countries' officials is not considered to be a criminal offence in the EU and is even encouraged in several Member States where bribes are tax-deductible. ·
Does the Commission recognize the flagrant incoherence of the EU's position?
If so, what initiatives will it propose to ensure that the EU curbs - rather than encourages - corrupt practices by any of tis citizens wherever they may be operating?
Answer
The Commission is committed to combatting corruption both within the European Union and on a more global scale.
It is in favour of the criminalization of the bribery of foreign officials and against tax deductibUity for bribes.
It should be pointed out that there have been a number of recent developments on the issues raised by the honourable Member both within the EU and in international fora.
As a first step agreement has been reached within the EU that bribery of officials of other Member States should be made a criminal offence.
Negotiations are under way in both the Council of Europe and the OECD for the criminalization of the bribery of foreign officials generaUy.
As regards tax deductibility for bribes, the Council of the OECD recommended in April 1996 that 'those countries which do not disallow the deductibUity of bribes to foreign pubUc officials re-examine such treatment with the intention of denying this deductibility'.
The implementation of this recommendation is currently under review. ·
The honourable Member will be aware that all the Member States participate in the OECD and the Council of Europe and the Commission is also active in these fora.
FinaUy the Commission is of the view that a coherent and comprehensive European Union policy against corruption is reqwred, addressing all areas relevant to the fight against corruption.
To this end a draft document is being prepared within the Commission on the matter.
Question No 73 by Michael Elliott (H-0156/97) Subject: Meat imports
Can the Commission confirm that it has made compensatory payments to those countries whose meat or meat products have been banned from entry into the European Union because they contain hormones the use of which is not permitted in the EU?
If so, which countries have received payments, what amounts have been paid, and how long has this practice been going on?
Furthermore, since the Commission is involved in a legal case with the USA on the import of meat containing hormones, will it seek reimbursement of the amounts paid if the case is decided in the EU's favour.
Answer
The European Commission has not made compensatory or any other type of payments to any third country in connection with EC legislation prohibiting the use of hormones in livestock farming.
Question No 74 by Fernando Pérez Royo (H-0158/97) Subject: Freezing of aid to the fisheries fleet
Answer
The third-generation Multiannual Guidance Programme (MAGP) expired on 31 December 1996.
The Multiannual Guidance Programme is an essential element in the programming of structural aid; in the absence of a Multiannual Guidance Programme, the regulatory conditions no longer exist.
All aid to the fleet (inseparably, the whole of Title II of Regulation 3699/93 (·): aid to modernization/construction and aid to the adjustment of the fisheries effort) must take place within the framework of a programme.
At the Council meeting of 20 December 1996, the Council was unable to take a decision relating to the objectives designed to restructure the Community fisheries sector for the period from 1 January 1997 to 31 December 2002.
The Commission draws the foUowing conclusions from this situation:
- the Member States are still subject to the obligation of guaranteeing that their fleets' fishing effort does not exceed the final objectives of MAGP III; this obligation derives, in particular, from Article 1 of the Council Decision of 20 December 1993 (94/15/EC) (2) and from Article 5 of the Treaty,
- the Community can no longer finance the measures envisaged in Title II of the Regulation (EC) No 3699/93. .
Consequently, the arrangements for co-financing the measures referred to in Articles 7 to 10 of Regulation (EC) No 3699/93 are suspended with effect from 1 January 1997 for aU Member States and with respect to all measures applied to the fleet.
The suspension of the co-financing of aid does not imply the suspension of national aid to the fleet, provided that this comes within the framework of a government aid scheme which has been approved by the Commission (Article 92 of the Treaty).
The financing of construction and modernization is authorized only if the objectives of Multiannual Guidance Programme ΙΠ have been achieved as at 31 December 1996.
Nevertheless, the Council notes that, in the event that it should adopt, before 30 April 1997, a decision on MAGP IV with effect from 1 January 1997, the measures that Member States may
■Question No 75 by Marie­Paule Kestelijn­sierens (H­0159/97)
Subject: Ban on the export of Belgian beef to France
Various Belgian slaughterhouses and/or meat­processing undertakings wanting to export beef products to France are facing a ban on the import of Belgian beef into France as a result of BSE.
Every infringement of this ban is to be subject to a fine of FF 50 000.
Can the Commission indicate whether this information is correct?
If so, is the import ban currently operated by France permissible?
If it conflicts with Community legislation, what measures will the Commission take to ensure that it is halted as quickly as possible?
Answer
France, like other Member States, has adopted detailed legislation concerning BSE.
This legislation has been notified to the Commission.
The Commission is not aware that anything contained in the French legislation could operate as a general restriction on the free circulation in France of the meat of Belgian'bovine animals slaughtered in Belgium.
Furthermore, the Commission has not received any complaints concerning any specific incident involving prohibition of the circulation of Belgian meat in France imposed by the French authorities or any fines levied in this connection.
If the honourable Member could supply full details of the alleged restrictions the Commission would of course examine the matter further.
On what date, and in which language, were the first editions published? How much time elapsed between the date of publication of the documents and the subsequent translation thereof into Spanish?
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 77 by Christine Oddy (H-0161/97) Subject: Asbestos in the workplace
What steps will the Commission take to protect employees from the effects of working in factories built with asbestos (as it can often take 40 years for the disease to develop after exposure) and can the Commission reassure workers that their fears over asbestos are being taken seriously?
Answer
The Commission has been informed of the studies conducted in the United Kingdom and in other Member States which apparently predict a large and increasing number of cases of mesothelioma resulting from exposure to asbestos at work or outside work.
Since 1983, workers who are exposed to asbestos in the workplace have been covered by a CouncU Directive (93/477/EEC (»), as amended by 91/382/EEC (2)).
It requires every employer to evaluate the hazard created by any activity which may involve a risk of exposure to asbestos.
These dangers also affect workers who handle these products and those who may occasionally be exposed to asbestos present in buildings (or products), and also those involved in demoütion work, for example electricians, plumbers and other service providers.
The Commission has recently submitted a communication on this directive.
(4).The Commission is analysing the implementation of the Directive, in consultation with the Member States, and concludes that the measures laid down under existing Community legislation can still be shown to be valid in the overaU context of protecting the health of workers exposed to asbestos.
The Commission points out in its communication that, owing to the long latency period (25 years, on average), between exposure to asbestos and the appearance of a mesotheUoma), these diseases were, in a number of cases, caused by exposure to asbestos before the implementation of the 1983 Directive, at a time when exposure levels were significantly higher.
Nonetheless, given the severity of certain diseases caused by asbestos and the large number of groups of workers concerned, the Commission intends to continue its efforts to increase protection still further.
Regarding the placing on the market of products containing asbestos, the Commission has been examining the relevant Community provisions since 1996, in collaboration with the Member States.
Once it has analysed the latest scientific data, the Commission intends to submit a draft proposal for a Council and Parliament Directive banning asbestos, with some exemptions.
Question No 78 by Wilfried Telkämper (H-0171/97)
Subject: Environmental protection and human rights in cooperation between Canada and the EU under the Canada-EU action plan
The European Union is the third largest export market for Canadian wood and paper products.
Most of the feUing carried out by the major timber companies in Canada continues to be on the basis of clear-felling. This practice frequently involves infringement of the land rights of Indian
') OJ L 263 of 24 September 1983.
2) OJL 206 of 29 July 1991.
4) COM(96)426 final of 5 September 1996.
Debates of the European Parliament
11.3.97 peoples, as in the case of the Lubicon Cree in Alberta and the Nuxalk in British Columbia, or Canadian treaty obligations with the James Bay Cree and the Algonquin (Barrier Lake) in Quebec.
In view of the considerable imports of Canadian timber products into the European Union, how does the Commission intend to encourage the shift from clear-felting methods to ecosystem based forestry and to urge compliance with the treaty obligations of the Canadian Federal Government, the Canadian provinces and the Canadian constitution with regard to the rights of Indian peoples?
Question No 79 by Horst Schnellhardt (H-0172/97) Subject: Environmental protection and human rights in the Canada-EU action plan
At the meeting of the Intergovernmental Panel on Forests on indigenous forest peoples the EU Commission gave its support to using traditional forestry knowledge and experience in the development and implementation of sustainable forestry.
Under the EU-Canada action plan how does the Commission intend, with regard to the planned cooperation in the UN Commission for Sustainable Development, to support the principles of ecosystem based forestry and the utilization of the traditional knowledge of indigenous peoples in forestry, in particular with regard to the obUgations of the Canadian government with regard to the James Bay and Northern Quebec agreement?
The recently adopted Community Canada Joint Action Plan also includes a number of references to the preservation of the environment and forestry.
In Chapter ΠΙ ('Transnational Issues') of the Canada Joint Action Plan, the Community and Canada commit themselves to exchanging information and reports on forest issues.
The Action Plan, furthermore, prescribes that the Community and Canada 'wiU work together at the UN Commission on Sustainable Development (CSD) and will continue to work on the successful conclusion of the CSDs work on the sustainable management of forests'.
There is thus a clear commitment in the Action Plan to continue and further develop cooperation and dialogue with regard to forestry and the environment.
In this context it should be recalled, however, that the Action Plan is not considered to be a legally binding agreement.
Rather, it is a statement of intentions and political will to cooperate on a wide range of issues.
An opportunity to raise forest related issues with Canada on a bilateral level already exists through the socalled Ad Hoc Working Group on Forests, which is part of the Community-Canada Annual High Level Consultations on the Environment.
As regards the question of indigenous people, the European Commission is deeply attached to promoting sustainable development practices, which take due account of indigenous peoples' human rights, including land tenure and the preservation of traditional life style.
The Community has raised its concerns on these issues repeatedly in relevant contexts such as the Convention on Biological Diversity and, in line with the conclusions of the Rio Conference of 1992 - in which the concept of environmental rights was developed -, it has always emphasized the link between the promotion of human rights and respect for the environment.
Question No 80 by Gerard Collins (H-0175/97) Subject: Irish butter voucher scheme
Last December, the CouncU of Ministers decided to halve the quantity of butter distributed at reduced prices to people on social assistance in Ireland, thereby reducing one of the few benefits
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4-497/105 which the European Union brought to the 850000 people who received butter under the EU-sponsored butter voucher scheme and through reduced demand increasing the annual surplus of butter in the EU.
In view of the foregoing and bearing in mind that butter has held a stable market share in Ireland since 1990, will the Commission now undertake a review of the butter voucher scheme with a view to putting a proposal to the Council of Ministers to restore to the original level the quantity of butter being made available under this scheme?
The Council has, on the basis of a Commission report (*), decided to prolong for a two year period ending 31.12.1998 the scheme concerning sales of butter at reduced prices to persons receiving social assistance.
The reduction in the monthly maximum quantity per beneficiary from 1 kg to 0.5 kg reflects the development in the average butter consumption in Ireland, which has dropped from more than 0.9 kg/month in 1984 (where the monthly maximum of 1 kg was initially fixed under the scheme) to around 0.3 kg/month in 1996.
Considering this development, the Commission has recommended to adjust the maximum yearly quantity eügible per beneficiary under the social assistance scheme to a level which is still above the average butter consumption in Ireland and does not, therefore, at present find it appropriate to review the scheme and forward a new proposal to the Council.
Subject: Funding under the 1994-1999 CSF for Ireland
Can the Commission indicate the cut-off date for construction work on projects which have been allocated Structural Fund assistance under the 1994 to 1999 CSF, so that those projects wiU be able to draw down funding from this Community Support Framework?
Answer
Operational programmes under the Community Support Framework for Ireland specify the cut-off date for commitments and payments.
Legally binding commitments by the national authorities must be made no later than 31 December 1999.
As a general rule, payments may be made up to 31 December 2000.
With a view to ensuring that the Community respects linguistic and cultural diversity, the Commission intends to submit a proposal for a decision establishing a multiannual programme to promote regional and minority languages to the CouncU and Parliament during the second half of 1997.
