Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 83 by Liam Hyland (H-O179/97) Subject: Sporting events for the disabled
One million ECUs have been set aside for pilot projects and sports events in favour of integrating and developing sport for the disabled in 1997.
Will the Commission indicate how organizations interested in applying for financial support should present their appUcations, what the deadlines are and how similar aid in previous years has benefited Ireland?
Answer
Application kit is available on request from the sport sector of the Commission.
The kit contains the guidelines and procedures as weU as the relevant format in which the request should be made.
Applications should be submitted to the Commission's sport sector by the first of September of the year prior to which the project is planned to take place, and a copy sent to the National Committee in the Member State in which the project wiU take place.
Tel: 1-8338241 - fax: 1-8333873.
Since 1993 the following projects have been supported in Ireland:
European Wheelchair Snooker Championships European Waterski Championships Sports Training School for People with Learning Impairments European Athletics Championships for the Blind International Chess Championships for the Blind.
In addition the International Festival of Blind Sports that is due to be held in Dublin between 2 and 3 May 1997 is scheduled to receive support.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 85 by Pat Gallagher (H-0182/97) Subject: Demands by insurance companies for genetic history of new clients
Following reports that in the UK certain insurance companies making it a requirement that new clients who have undergone genetic tests in the past should provide detaUs of their genetic history, a move whch has been opposed by medical researchers, wUl the Commission ensure that such requirements are banned at European level, taking into account the fact that such demands could result in prohibitive insurance rates and could exclude cover for specific, named Ulnesses?
Answer
The honourable Member raises a problem which has various aspects, such as analysis of the insurance risk by insurance companies, the protection of privacy and ethical questions.
The Community has already acted in the area of privacy and data protection, the Council and the European Parliament adopting a framework Directive (95/46/EC) in October 1995, which must be implemented by Member States before October 1998.
This directive sets down a series of basic principles including provisions on the processing of special categories of data which include data relating to health.
Health data are deemed to include genetic data.
The processing of such data is in principle prohibited except where the subject of the information (and with genetic data there may be several data subjects because the same data will relate in part to all members of the same genetic line) has given his/her explicit consent (and even in such circumstances a Member State may choose not to lift the prohibition) or where another of the limited number of narrow exceptions applies.
In other cases, it would appear that insurers are bound by professional codes not to take genetic information into account in assessing risks.
Question No 86 by Robin Teverson (H-0185/97) Subject: Proposed sixth amendment to the EU directive on cosmetics
Debates of the European Parliament
11.3.97 tested on animals, with effect from 1 January 1998 in order to meet the requirements of the Directive.
However, the Commission may propose that this provision be postponed until a sufficient period has elapsed if it considers that the conditions set out in the second subparagraph of Article 4(i) have not been met.
The Commission notes that, in the absence of scientificaUy validated alternative methods of experimentation and of OECD guidelines on the subject, these conditions have not been met where ingredients and combinations of ingredients are concerned.
In addition, the Commission is closely examining the provisions of Article 4(l)(i) in the light of the Community's obügations under the rules of international trade.
The Commission could, without delay, propose an amendment to the Directive on cosmetic products to take these international obligations into account and deal with the ban on testing finished products with effect from 1 January 1998.
Question No 87 by NiMaos Papakyriazis (H-0187/97) Subject: Anti-Semitism - extradition of Alois Brunner
The hunt for Brunner, an SS officer, has been going on since 1945.
He is wanted by the German authorities and has received two death-sentences in France for the extermination of 25 000 Jews at Drancy.
Interpol has also issued a warrant for his arrest, which has not yet been put into effect.
What steps has the Commission taken to date with regard to Alois Brunner and what measures wiU it take to resolve this situation?
In view of the fact that 1997 is European Year Against Racism and Xenophobia, and in view of the alarming surge of racism and anti-Semitism, frequently culminating in denials that the holocaust ever took place, what steps can the Commission take and what measures wiU it adopt to ensure that the European Union and its Member States firmly oppose such developments?
In particular, what steps have been taken by the European Union to implement the agreements of 10 March 1995 and 27 September 1996 seeking to facilitate the adoption of measures to combat racism and xenophobia within the territory of the European Union?
Anti-Semitism is encompassed in the general framework of the Year and the subject will be dealt with appropriately.
Whether this includes actions, seminars or other events against denials of the holocaust, also depends on the agenda of the Member States for the Year and on the national budgets available for the Year.
Debates of the European Parliament
On 15 July 1996 the Council adopted a joint action (') concerning action to combat racism and xenophobia with a view to improving judicial cooperation between the Member States in this area.
The EU conventions of 10 March 1995 and 27 September 1996 referred to by the Honourable Member both deal with extradition but are not yet in force. These instruments will facilitate extradition between the Member States, including cases of racism and xenophobia.
Question No 88 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0188/97) Subject: Conservation of the historic Plaka bridge in Epirus
One of the largest single-arched stone bridges in Greece, the Plaka bridge, which is also one of the most important monuments in Epirus, is in danger of being destroyed as a result of a new hydroelectric project by the Greek PubUc Power Corporation (DEH) on the river Arachthos. This bridge, together with the Arta bridge, form an integral part of the local history, culturé and landscape.
In addition to the bridge, the artificial lake created by the dam across the river Arachthos wUl also cover the customs building historically associated with the national resistance movement where the agreement between the ELAS (National Popular Liberation Army) and the EDES (Greek Democratic National Army) was signed.
What steps will the Commission take to save these architectural and historical monuments and what representations wUl it make to the Greek Government and the Greek Public Power Corporation with a view to having them seek and adopt alternative solutions in order to avoid their destruction?
The Commission shares the sentiments of the Honourable Member about the protection of Europe's architectural heritage in general and about the historic bridge of Plaka and the Customs House in Epirus in particular.
With the support of the European Parliament, the Commission does indeed demonstrate its concern in the field of cultural heritage through its pilot actions, which encourage pooting of efforts and cooperation at European level for dealing with the important questions of how to protect, preserve and enhance the rich and diverse architectural heritage throughout Europe.
However in conformity with Article 128 of the EC Treaty and the principle of subsidiarity the Commission cannot intervene on the matter in question as the responsibility for the protection of national, regional and local cultural heritage lies exclusively within the competence of the national, regional and local authorities.
Question No 89 by Anne Mcintosh (H-0719/96) Subject: Review of BSE strategy
Does the Commission now accept that the proposed slaughter programme will involve a level of culling disproportionate to the anticipated reduction in the incidence of BSE?
Will the Commission consider alternative approaches to the problem of BSE, notably a reduction in the numbers of animals involved in the slaughter programme?
The Commission recognizes the continued reduction of the BSE epidemic in the UK. However, this does not directly affect the eradication programme already agreed, although the UK has the right to propose amendments.
Such amendments would require the agreement of the Commission and the Member States.
Debates of the European Parliament
It is generally agreed that the cohort slaughter scheme laid down in the eradication programme is the best means of further reducing the epidemic.
It should be noted that up to one third of the animals originally destined for slaughter under the programme have already been slaughtered in the over-thirty-month scheme. The over-ihirtymonth scheme provides for the slaughter with payment foUowed by destruction of bovines at the end of their normal working lives.
On the other hand the final results of the maternal transmission study are awaited and it is possible that these could indicate other high-risk animals which should be removed.
Question No 90 by José Girão Pereira (H-0012/97) Subject: EU-Greenland fisheries agreement
Does the Commission agree that, as the fishing quotas under the EU-Greenland agreement are not fully utitized, other interested Community fleets may fish in these waters?
Answer
However, this is easier said than done because of certain factual and legal constraints which have to be taken into account in the specific context of the EC-Greenland Fisheries Agreement.
On the factual side, it should be borne in mind that the reasons why Member States catch less than their full quotas are manifold and rather complex.
In Greenland waters, the low utilization rate is mainly due to coUapse of the cod stock and, therefore, the corresponding quotas do not reflect the actual quantities capable of being caught.
On the legal side, it must be stressed that quotas in Greenland waters are aUocated between Member States in accordance with the principle of 'relative stability'.
The underlying distribution keys cannot be changed at random.
Furthermore, the Court of Justice held in this respect that under-utilization of fishing quotas alone would offer no valid reason for re-allocating the quotas concerned to other interested Member States.
As far as more immediate solutions are concerned, Member States should be induced to make more frequent use of already existing mechanisms such as the possibility of quota exchanges. These mechanisms are particularly flexible because they do not require fuU reciprocity in terms of fish.
Question No 91 by Konstantinos Hatzidakis (H-0027/97)
Subject: Greek Government's procedure for selecting an advertising company to publicise and promote the Greek CSF
Did the competent Greek authority notify the Commission in due time of the appointment of its representative to the committee set up to evaluate the proposals submitted and select the company to be awarded the contract and, if so, which meetings of that committee did its representative attend or, if he/she did riot attend, what were the reasons for the failure to do so?
Does the Commission consider that, overall, the procedures for selecting the advertising company awarded the contract were duly conducted in accordance with the law?
In a letter dated 7 November 1996, the Greek authorities invited the Commission to send a representative to the national committee for the selection of a private company to be made responsible for providing information and publicity on the 1994-1999 Community Support Framework for Greece.
Debates of the European Parliament
The Commission, therefore, did not participate in the procedure for selecting the company.
At this stage, it is not in possession of any information to suggest that the procedure adopted was irregular.
Question No 92 by Per Stenmarck (H-0045/97)
Subject: Commission proposal for a new maritime strategy
The committees to which the proposal has been referred, however, maintain that it takes little account of environmental considerations and regard this as a major problem.
Discharges from ferries are a serious environmental problem in many places in the EU.
Is the Commission prepared to come back with proposals for fiscal or other incentives aimed at solving the environmental problems currently caused by many ferries operating between EU Member States?
Answer
The Commission shares the opinion that environmental considerations should not be neglected in the establishment of EC shipping policy.
Indeed, legislative initiatives presented by the Commission in the framework of its maritime safety policy aim, in addition to enhancing safety at sea, also to improve protection of the maritime environment. Any reduction of shipping accidents will limit consequential poUution and the Commission is convinced that its proposals in the Maritime Strategy document contribute to this.
In addition, specific measures initiated by the Commission have focused directly on the maritime environment, either by providing incentives as in the Council regulation on segregated ballast oil tankers (*) or by ensuring adequate logistic infrastructure as envisaged by the initiative on shore reception facilities announced in the Commission's Common Transport Policy Action programme 1995-2000 (2).
The Commission bases its specific safety and environmental measures, to the largest extent possible, upon existing international rules.
In connection with this latter point, the Commission, responding to the request by the Environment Council of December 1995, wiU present to the Council and Parliament a draft communication on combating acidification.
This will include a chapter devoted to activities concerned with emissions from ships.
Given the above and the fact that all ferries are subject to the MARPOL Convention, the Commission has no plans for proposing additional, specific measures concerning environmental problems caused by ferries.
However, the need for such measures is kept constantly under review.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 93 by Mark Watts (H-0074/97) Subject: Export of live cattle to third countries
Further to Mr Fischler's answer to Written Question No 93 P-4015/96 given on behalf of the Commission on 20 January 1997, and bearing in mind that the most serious animal welfare problems take place not in the European Union but on unloading at third country ports and at third country slaughterhouses, wiU the Commission answer the following question:
Does the Commission not agree that the export of live cattle from the EU to the Middle East, North Africa and Turkey should be brought to an end and replaced by meat exports?
Answer
Exports of live bovine animals is a traditional trade and as internal prices are above the third country prices, an export refund can be payable.
Export refunds have been paid out since the beginning of the market organizations for beef in 1968.
In certain third countries there is a specific demand for imports of live animals.
If the Community does not offer live animals for sale, other countries will do so and the Community wiU lose that market.
Discussions have started with Member States concerning the necessary provisions for the withdrawal of the export refund from exporters, where it is shown that they have not fuUy respected the rules on the welfare of animals during transport.
Such a measure would require an amendment of CouncU Regulation No 805/68 and also some modalities of implementation.
So, the Community policy is directed to improving the enforcement of current rules and improving the effectiveness of those rules where necessary rather than imposing a ban of these exports.
Question No 94 by Maj Theorin (H-0076/97) Subject: EMU
WiU the convergence criteria laid down in the Maastricht Treaty be interpreted in the same way for the new Member States following the forthcoming enlargement as they are when EMU starts in 1999?
Has the Commission analysed the 11 potential Member States' economies in the light of. those criteria and on that basis calculated a timetable for their participation in EMU?
Answer
By the time the candidate States enter the Community, the third stage of EMU will have begun.
Since these new Member States will not be able to adopt the euro immediately, they will have the status of exempt Member States.
The principle of equality of treatment will be applied to these new Member States as to any other exempt States.
The Commission believes it is difficult to examine the situation of States that are candidates for accession in the light of the convergence criteria.
This is because some concepts, such as the longterm interest rates, the pubUc administration sector and government deficits, do not yet have the same significance in transitional economies that they do in the Community.
It is also important that candidate States introduce all the macroeconomic and structural reforms which wUl enable them to stabilize their economies in the long term.
The capacity of the candidate States to carry out these reforms wiU be an important factor in considering whether or not they may join the Community.
Furthermore, various initiatives have been set up under the pre-accession strategy with a view to famUiarizing candidate States with the acquis communautaire and preparing for their integration into the single market and, eventually, the euro zone.
Among these initiatives, the following may be mentioned: the participation of the finance ministers of the associated States in the structured dialogue meetings with the ministers of the 'Ecofin' council and the organization of the biannual meetings for the exchange of information on the economic situation in the Community and in the candidate States (Association Subcommittee on Economic Affairs).
