Wolf (V), in writing. - (DE) The equivocation demonstrated by many Members here on the cloning issue evidently stems from two (historically linked) fallacies:
It should also be emphasized that both medical assistance with procreation and cloning methods confirm that the fertilized egg is indeed a human being.
Contrary to the conclusions which some people have seen fit to draw from the spontaneous segmentation of the egg which produces identical twins, the uniqueness of the original egg which then gives rise to the existence of two human beings does not prove that that egg is not a human being.
It is a human 'strain', which then gives rise to two human beings, each with a different personality though with the same genetic material.
The first of these is the ontologicai fallacy, which reduces reality to objects; these are then contrasted with subjects, which are regarded as something quite different and inexplicable.
This in turn makes the idea of identity a virtual impossibility, reducing it to the objective category of sameness. Leibniz's metaphysical principle of the identitas indiscernibilium paves the way for an indifference observable in the domain of engineering science, an attitude of 'What I cannot teU apart, I have no hesitation in calling identical.'
But the difference between identical twins resulting from spontaneous natural segmentation and identical twins resulting from artificiaUy induced segmentation is precisely that the former are indeterminate whUe the latter are under human control.
The second is the ethical faUacy of absolving us humans from any type of responsibility for our fellow creatures, which are regarded as mere 'things' or indeed 'machines', from any bonds of sympathy we might feel towards them.
That engenders an attitude of systematic denial of responsibüity for anything extraneous to ourselves, an egocentric withdrawal from the fuU implications of our own practice.
The frontier which separates these two events is the same frontier that separates the sphere of human freedom from that of total slavery.
The prohibition of cloning must, in any human society, be as fundamental a prohibition as the prohibition of incest.
That also throws the door wide open to misanthropic practices.
Anyone who artificially defines us humans as something entirely detached from the community of living beings, of 'sentient beings', to quote the succinct EngUsh expression, is capable of making other, no less artificial distinctions, for instance between mere humans and 'superhumans' or between 'subhumans' and 'proper' people... ple...
