Debates of the European Parliament
IN THE CHAIR: MR GIL-ROBLES GIL-DELGADO President
(The formal sitting was opened at 9.25 a.m.)
40th anniversary of the Treaty of Rome
President - Ladies and gentlemen, to a large extent the history of the European Community is the history of this Parliament, the Community institution whose evolution has been most closely linked to progress in the construction of the Community from the time the three Communities were created up to the present day.
In fact, aU the major changes introduced over the 46 years that have gone by since the Treaty of Paris estabUshed the European Coal and Steel Community, and over the 40 years since this Parliament was established as a parliament common to the three Communities, have gone hand in hand with the growth in the European Parliament's powers and its role in the interplay of the Community institutions.
That evolution has been important.
InitiaUy the European ParUament did not even have that name, because the ECSC Treaty and the two foUowing treaties estabUshed an assembly with almost entirely consultative status.
The first triumph of that assembly was to preserve its existence under the Treaties of Rome and constantly insist on matching that existence with the name of ParUament.
On 20 March 1958, barely three months after the Treaty of Rome came into force, the members of the Assembly took the decision to change its name by adding two adjectives which clearly indicated a programme: Parliamentary and European.
Four years later, on 30 March 1962, the parliamentary reference was converted from an adjective into a noun.
The European Parliament was born, and the name was bold indeed considering the slim powers the institution had at that time.
Since then the European Parliament has advanced in leaps and bounds under its own momentum, almost always ahead of the reforms and improvements introduced in the treaties, and that is stiU happening today.
The trend accelerated after June 1979, when Members of the European Parliament were elected by universal suffrage for the first time, and the mandate of the people conferred a legitimacy on Members of Parliament which strengthened their claims.
The first European Parliament to emerge from the ballot box instantly made its central priority a drive for institutional reform to increase the powers of Parliament, in a new decisionmaking framework more in tine with the classic systems in force in aU the Member States.
I am not going to speU out each and every advance because you are well aware of them.
But I want to highlight the fact that we are now commemorating 40 years since an Intergovernmental Conference was in progress and - as I do not want to draw out my remarks, but leave it to the groups to express the political positions of this Parliament - I would just Uke to say to the current negotiators who are carrying forward the project the negotiators of the Treaty of Rome set out, that it is a bold project, a project to find the paths for continuing to build day after day in the best interests of Europeans.
It is a project founded not on what each can obtain at any given time from a few treaties, but on how we can aU build peace and freedom together day by day.
Patijn, President-in-Office of the Council. - (NL) I am very grateful for this opportunity to address you today on the significant anniversary we are celebrating this year, namely the fortieth anniversary of the treaty of Rome.
Mr President, ladies and gentlemen, European history is not a collection of isolated events.
The course of history is often capricious; things sometimes move forwards, sometimes backwards, and now and again nothing moves and there is stagnation.
The pattern can only be traced afterwards when we look back at past events in an attempt to explain the present and sometimes, rashly, the future too.
Looking back from our comfortable position at the end of the 20th century what strikes one most is the hiccup in the middle of that century, World War Two. In our minds the periods before it and after it are drifting ever father apart, Uke two pieces of an ice floe.
The period before the break is marked by increasing nationaüsm, militarism and intolerance.
Debates of the European ParUament
No 4-497/199 elevated this to the status of a retigion.
In the post-war period the East disappeared behind a curtain, whilst Western Europe went down a completely different road, that of cooperation.
After the war it was Winston ChurchiU who first publicly voiced the idea of European unification in his 1946 speech when he talked of the need for 'a kind of united states of Europe', with 'a partnership between Germany and France' as the first step.
The motor of Franco-German cooperation was first started up at this point.
The movement away from the dangers of pre-war nationaUsm was launched and began to gather momentum.
A major difference in mentality between the pre- and post-war periods was that the victors no longer sought to ensure their own security by minimizing the economic and political power of the vanquished party on a permanent basis.
No, states were no longer to be kept within the straitjacket of their own national borders and Umitations.
The national economic and political power of states was now to develop within the dynamic of European unification which would be beneficial to aU.
This greatest vision, held by Schuman - and I mention his name in this House with respect - led in 1951 to the signing of the ESCS Treaty by the six original countries, as we know, as the first step towards a series of markets which would ultimately culminate in economic unification.
Straight away it seemed that things would go wrong.
Political integration ground to a halt in 1954 with the failure of the European Defence Community.
Fortunately we thought about things for two years, and at the Messina conference the thread was taken up again.
The photo exhibition which I looked at with great interest pays respectful tribute to that Messina summit. It led, on 25 March 1957, after nine months of negotiation, to the signing of the Treaty of Rome.
The negotiations were led by the Belgian minister Spaak, whose name I also mention with awe.
And now, forty years on, we appreciate the immensity of these events.
With the EC Treaty six countries laid the foundations of a process of European integration which had a twofold basis, economic and political.
This formula is based on a combination of the economic benefits of free movement and the political objective of peace and stability.
Looking back to Rome we see clearly the milestones along the road to European integration which Unk the present with the past.
Just as Rome was not built in a day, the process of European unification is a lengthy one.
So far our journey has brought us via the Single European Act of 1986 and the Maastricht Treaty to today's IGC.
Along the way another nine Member States have joined the original six.
European unification has from the start been characterized not only by a clear deepening but also by an unprecedented geographical widening.
Completion of the Intergovernmental Conference, the start of phase three of Monetary Union and the further enlargement of the Union will be the next qualitative and quantitative stages in the integration process.
The first stage, our current Intergovernmental Conference, will take us towards a Union which is more effective and ready for enlargement towards the East and the Mediterranean.
The second stage, the start of Economic and Monetary Union, wiU give the finishing touch to the single market.
The third and final stage will lead to the inclusion of countries for which World War Two did not effectively end in 1945.
They will consolidate their democratic and economic development within a European Home which meets the challenges of the future.
In all these great undertakings, but also in the day-to-day business of the European Union those who have made Europe their life's work - and that means aU of us here today - must reaüze that the aim of all our endeavours is to create and sustain a Europe in which the citizen can live in peace and prosperity.
Human beings are our yardstick.
The citizen himself knows best what is good for him, so he must have a say in how Europe develops.
He must be able to make his voice heard through Parliament, the European Parliament and his own national one.
Members of parUament can only do a good job if the democratic content of the European decisionmaking process is enlarged.
The issues then are those of parliamentary access to information and parUamentary powers.
At the start of my brief speech I said that the course of European history is capricious and that sometimes things stagnate.
But I believe we have made more progress than was imaginable forty years ago.
A Union which has economic and increasingly political weight on the world stage is more than many people had expected and some had hoped.
Of course there is still much to be done, but this formal celebration offers us a chance to count our blessings so that tomorrow we can return to the task of building Europe.
My hope is that in the year 2007 we shall be celebrating the 50thanniversary of the treaty of Rome and that by then we shall be able to say that we have completed the next stage of the road pointed out to us by visionaries Uke Schuman and Monnet and that in both Western and Eastern Europe we can say that life in the European Home is both good and safe.
Santer, President of the Commission. - (FR) Mr President, Mr President-in-Office, ladies and gentlemen, forty years have passed since the ceremony in Rome at which the treaties establishing toe European Economic Community and the European Atomic Energy Community were signed.
Debates of the European Parliament
On this occasion a decision was taken to abandon any imperialistic desires and the ever dubious balance of power.
The six founding nations committed themselves to resolving their differences peacefully in the future.
They furnished themselves with common institutions, with real powers.
Cooperation in matters covered by national sovereignty seemed impossible to contemplate at that time, so their intentions were based on a foundation of economic integration.
Mr President, ten years ago, when we were celebrating the 30th anniversary of the Treaties of Rome, the Single-European Act had just been signed.
The European Community of that time was preparing for a period of institutional development and exceptional expansion.
Western Europe had regained confidence in its capacity to shape the future, and that confidence reached its peak with the reunification of Germany and the collapse of the Soviet bloc.
The liberation of our feUow men in the east from the communist yoke vindicated those who, in 1957, had staked their faith in European Unity.
The Community system stood on the brink of its most dazzling period.
As we celebrate this fortieth anniversary, the omens are more modest.
Persistent high unemployment is burdening our democratic societies, and at the same time weakening confidence in the European project.
Public mistrust of the politicians in the Member States has, moreover, been extended by the ratification of the Maastricht Treaty to those who are working to bring about the Union, and to the chosen method.
At the same time, too, we are having to prepare for enlargement, the institutional and financial implications of which we are only just beginning to grasp.
As we look forward to these events, the time is right to ask ourselves two questions about our method.
Have we drawn aU the logical conclusions from the choice of functional method made in Rome on 25 March 1957?
And wUl that method be sufficient to ensure that the European Union develops in accordance with the legitimate aspirations of its peoples?
Descartes wrote his 'Discourse on Method', and now the time has come to rewrite it.
In forty years, our Member States have learned to work together on a day-to-day basis.
And, as the consequences of the functional logic have become apparent, so the scope of this cooperation has broadened.
Policies have proliferated, procedures have been revised - and they have become more complex, too.
Admittedly, these forty years have been punctuated by crises, and those crises form part of our peoples' common history.
Yet none of them has been so severe as to cast doubt on the initial intention.
The days when a contentious diplomatic telegram was sufficient to unleash a war between two European States are over.
Even though enthusiasm sometimes ebbs, the grand design remains, and we are indeed committed to the peaceful settlement of our differences and, also, to the quest for a common response to the aspirations of our peoples, which must be satisfied jointly.
That is what makes the European Union so attractive.
Ten years ago, the Six had already become the Twelve.
Today, the Fifteen are once again preparing to add to their number.
Hitherto, the economy has been the area of choice for the appUcation of this close cooperation.
The level of integration we have achieved is such that we are preparing to perfect it with a functional method.
In a single century, States which had twice been at war have reached the point of adopting a single currency.
But are we really sure that we have completely covered the field of the functional method?
May we not have reached the end of the road only to find that we have failed to note certain important landmarks?
The speed with which the single market has come into existence has led to various omissions, sometimes with serious consequences.
An economic Europe demands a social Europe.
Today, that social Europe is still work in progress.
And that is certainly another area, alongside health, where European citizens expect Europe to do more.
We have to draw aU the logical political conclusions from an economic Europe.
But we also have to move forward along the road opened up by Maastricht.
Cooperation on legal matters, the establishment of a common foreign and security poUcy - these are the key issues of the Intergovernmental Conference.
And we are also going to have to ask ourselves about the limitations of the method, because we need a joint plan.
Mr President, I said in my inaugural address that the Monnet method undoubtedly deserved adaptation.
Jean Monnet himself must have been aware of that, because he wrote in 1956, 'I pressed the creation of common institutions in the belief that the institutions would bring men closer together.'
That is true.
'That must be the ultimate objective, ' he acknowledged.
'But this is not the method, ' he added immediately, 'because institutions for coal and steel become institutions for coal and steel and not institutions for men.'
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4-497/201 identification than from any democratic shortfaU.
To this end, the European Commission is asking that the European Parliament should take decisions on an equal footing with the Council in legislative areas.
It is also declaring itself in favour of a uniform ballot in all States of the Union.
That ballot wUl be the best possible guarantee of electoral representativity.
Finally, we must rise above considerations of method.
The overall design must be more clearly apparent to Europeans.
It is no longer enough just to say 'Europe' to be sure of gaining the support of Europeans.
Europe has unmistakably come into the spotUght of public debate.
So let us clear away the wild imaginings on the one side and the ideological constructions on toe other.
The European Union will not be a European superstate.
It wiU be, and will remain, an entirely original form of cooperation and integration between nations and peoples.
This grand design wUl achieve its full impetus with the support of the peoples of Europe, and we shaU have that support once our pubUc discussions cease to focus on the instruments.
Foreign policy and the single currency are only instruments.
The answers that Europeans want are answers about the actual purpose of European unification.
Today, it really is the duty of the nations and peoples of historic Europe, all of Europe, at peace with itself, once again to adopt a common design for the years ahead.
Green (PSE). - Mr President, above aU else the signatories of the Treaty of Rome wanted sustained unbroken peace on the continent of Europe.
The Common Market was not in its inception an economic club.
It was based on political decisions, buUt on the ashes of fascism, on the ashes of the Holocaust, a political decision that Europe should never again live through the trauma of wholesale war.
On the strength of that peace the founding fathers believed they could develop a self-sufficient, confident, dynamic, successful, prosperous and forwardlooking Europe.
The countries within the European Union have been at peace with each other for the last forty years, and every famUy in the European Union benefits from that peace.
We forget at our peril that abiding gift of peace which the European process has brought to us all; and those who promulgate the politics of hate, who encourage nationalism and xenophobia, would do weU to remember the model of conflict resolution on which the European Union is predicated and which sttil provides an inspiration to us and to others.
Now, as we approach the end of the century, we have forty years of successful European integration behind us; we face a greater challenge than at any time in Europe's coUective history.
The challenge is not whether we can adapt the institutional structure of the Union through some grotesque agreement based on last-minute horsetrading by our prime ministers in Amsterdam in June.
The real issue is whether we can give the workers of Vilvoorde and their fantilies a real future; whether we can put the 18 rrtillion unemployed Europeans into jobs; whether we can manage the environment; whether our food is clean and safe; whether our young people can be inspired through education and the prospect of a longterm future rather than sink into the everpresent drug culture with all its implications; whether we can afford pensions for the elderly, benefits for the sick and disabled; whether Ufe is going to be fair and just, or degenerate into an unseemly scramble for our daUy existence.
In essence, can we meet the aspirations, the needs and the hopes of our people?
That Europe now has this challenge firmly in its sights was brought home to me last night by a presentation made to my group by the workers of Vilvoorde coming directly from their demonstration in Paris.
Their description of the Europe they see contained the seeds of destruction of the Union; and yet paradoxicaUy it demonstrated to me as weU the potential for continuing the inspirational work of the founding fathers.
On the one hand they argued that there is no social Europe and that workers and people of the Union were growing more disUlusioned as the result of closures, relocations and redundancies.
On the other hand they described how the Works Council Directive had enhanced the concept of European integration in a very real way by bringing together the Renault workers in a single body since 1993.
It had actually provided the forum in which workers from different countries had learnt to know, trust and like one another.
This was the experience which had led them in the past two weeks to develop an international plan of action in which workers from many countries had taken part in solidarity with the workers of VUvoorde.
Mr President, today the European governments face a daunting task.
They have to decide whether to take the difficult or the easy option.
The difficult option is to recommit ourselves to the concept of the founding fathers, to European integration based on the fundamental principles of solidarity, cooperation, unity and cohesion, to a European Union where Member States are equal partners, respected and heeded whether they are big or small, prosperous or not so prosperous, founder members or the newest Member States. And that is difficult, because it requires immense patience
Debates of the European Parliament
12.3.97 and goodwill to secure decisions and make progress.
The easy option is to find an escape route, to secure a position which will allow a handful of States to forge ahead on their own whilst the rest remain in a generaUzed, static and secondary free-trade area.
This would deny the fundamental principles on which the last forty years of European success have been built.
It would constitute a major breach with the concept of the founding fathers.
More than anything, it would recreate a hierarchy within Europe, with all the consequent tensions and strains which that would generate.
Given the potential which now exists for suspicion and mistrust in an era of rapid economic change, social dysfunction and growing political disillusionment, it would reopen the question of peace and stability in Europe.
In celebration of forty years of Europe we should raise our glasses to the positives.
We should acknowledge that integration may not come as quickly as some want, but we should be clear that we want a Europe which holds together because its people want it to do so; a Europe which holds together because Europe adds something to the lives of our people'and subtracts nothing; a Europe of which we can aU be proud.
Martens (PPE). - (NL) Mr President, ladies and gentlemen, the authors of the Treaty of Rome were driven by a poUtical vision.
The spectacular development of the common market created real' sotidarity amongst the peoples of Europe.
This was only possible because it was buUt right from the start on reconciliation and peace.
Why was it so successful?
The Treaty of Rome created institutions which were strong and balanced.
Under the institutional system of the Community national and Community interests complemented each other.
A real transfer of sovereignty enabled the Community to resist aU impulses of national egoism and shortterm self-interest.
Th governments were also able to defuse a major threat to the Community."Under the OECD there were plans to absorb the European Economic Community into a big free trade area, purely commercial in nature, with no institutions of its own, no common poUcy, no harmonization of laws or rules of competition, no structural funds which could correct regional imbalances and make the machinery of solidarity work.
The battle was hard but it was won thanks to the resolute conviction of the Six.
They were accused at the time of wanting to be a small and exclusive club.
But they stood firm and consistently implemented the Treaty of Rome.
But looking to the past only makes sense if we learn useful lessons from it for the future.
The message of the Treaty of Rome remains as powerful as ever.
The chaUenges have changed and new imperatives have arisen.
They are largely political, because they have to meet the expectations of the peoples of Central and Eastern Europe, who were for a long time divorced from the momentum of Western Europe and who now seek to join their destiny to ours.
But if we wish to present ourselves as founders of the new Europe we also have a common duty to meet the needs of European society and its citizens; we must reduce the enormous level of unemployment and, I address myself to the Commission President here, we must ensure that those hit by the decisions, of Renault, which we discussed yesterday, are given the chance of a meaningful solution.
Moreover, ladies and gentlemen, enlargement of the European Union from 15 to 25 Member States may jeopardize the very survival of the Union unless we strengthen its abitity to decide and act.
The threat will be mortal if we do not base European integration on a moral view of humanity and on the desire for a meaningful future for European society.
Globalization of the economy comes as a real culture shock.
It may lead to more nationalistic reactions and the dangerous result that nations turn in upon themselves.
If we confine ourselves to being just a big economic area, governed only by the laws of the free market, we shall not be able to satisfy the demands of Europe's peoples for security, safety and solidarity.
If we faU to operate a true foreign and security poUcy, Europe wUl be condemned to play a marginal role on the world stage.
Ladies and gentlemen, what will the verdict of future generations be if we cannot now decide on a structured, federal, strong and democratic Europe, if we cannot bring peace and stabUity to our continent?
If we cannot make our feUow-citizens free members of a prosperous whole?
The current Intergovernmental Conference is certainly not the end of it.
But let us not forget what this Conference is meant to do.
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4­497/203
Azzolini (UPE). ­ (FT) Mr President, on 25 March 1957 in Italy, in Rome, in the Campidoglio, in the Sala degU Orazi e Curiazi, the founding fathers guided by Alcide De Gasperi signed the Treaties that became the main pillar of the entire process of unification, Treaties that were revised and adapted by the European Single Act and the Maastricht Treaty.
The ambition of the founding fathers in Rome was rightly a lofty one, because at that time it was indeed very necessary finaUy to embark on the road towards building Europe and achieving a European integration that was not confined merely to the removal of customs barriers or to a purely economic vision.
It was an ambitious political project and the determination to pursue it, however slowly, with a view to creating a large European common market, while at the same time creating democratic Community institutions by harmonizing and renewing the various policies, gradually led to the estabUshment of socio­political­institutional whole that is today's European Union.
On 25 March, 40 years ago, the founding fathers in Rome pointed the way, with courageous foresight, to a political project that has guaranteed peace, freedom and prosperity to those who participated in it.
I beUeve that when we celebrate this 40th anniversary we must reflect and ask ourselves whether we can complete the project began by the founding fathers with the same political foresight and in particular whether we are doing all we can to enable the citizens of Europe to know, participate in, and 'live' the European Union.
History teaches us that economic factors, monetary events or individual interests cannot, in themselves, give rise to or provide the impetus for entire cycles of civilization.
It is poUtical projects ­ poUtical spelled with a large Ρ ­ that can change history or seize good opportunities as they arise.
However, in a democracy, we cannot achieve major political and institutional changes unless the people are adequately informed and prepared for these changes.
As Alcide De Gasperi and Jean Monnet emphasized on several occasions, the building of Europe must be founded not just on economics but also and above all on culture.
Forty years of peace and prosperity seem to have dimmed the memory of aU those fratricide wars that afflicted our countries, while also promoting a kind of socio­cultural laxity that has certainly weakened those responsible so that, for example, civic education is almost totally disregarded by educational institutions and the media.
It is essential to help the citizens of Europe to discover the common cultural identity that lies hidden in every aspect of social life beneath the diversity òf languages, tastes and artistic styles, that common inheritance that is the very root of the civilization of our continent.
To become aware of the cultural identity that is expressed within the diversity of the social patterns of the various European regions also means rediscovering the fundamental reference points that form the basis of this identity, which are universally vatid principles such as poUtical freedom and freedom of thought, the secular state, religious tolerance, the autonomy of scientific research, the importance of the famUy and of individual initiative, the right to social equality, which are the basis and foundation of the fundamental right to peace.
The citizens must come to realise that unity in diversity, based on common roots, is certainly a rich source of the stimuli and creativity that make the European Union such a splendid cultural, economic and socio­poUtical venture.
Now that the status of 'foreigner', a symbol of division, has been modified within the framework of the European Community to become the status of European citizen, a symbol of unification, we must finally become aware as Europeans of these common roots, of this European cultural identity, so that we can complete the great project that began 40 years ago and finaUy perform our role, in a world context, of promoting civil coexistence, respect for the fundamental rights of man and of peoples and therefore the right to peace.
Mr President, ladies and gentlemen, let us therefore return with renewed vigour to the European spirit of our founding fathers and work together to build Europe and, within Europe, the future of our chüdren.
De Vries (ELDR). ­ (NL) Mr President, I shall use this debate not to look back but to look forward.
The Community has a glorious past.
But will its future be equally glorious?
The fall of the Berlin Wall marked the end of an era in Europe.
Does that mean that the European Union, founded during the cold war, has outtived its time?
Is the pendulum of history swinging back to a Europe of nation states?
Such a development is not impossible. But it carries a twofold danger.
Debates of the European Parliament
If the European Union is to resist the centrifugal force of nationalism it needs a new remit.
That remit is clear. It is to integrate the peoples east of the Oder.
Since the Middle Ages, Eastern and Western Europe have developed along separate lines.
Different traditions of feudaUsm, the spüt between orthodox and catholic Christendom and the centuries-old dominance of the Ottoman Empire over the countries of the Balkans and the Danube basin have left deep marks.
The two revolutions experienced by Western Europe in the last 200 years did not affect the East till much later.
I refer to the French Revolution and the industrial revolution.
Today, however, after the fall of communism we have for the first time in centuries a real chance to make Europe whole and undivided.
The historic duty of our generation is thus as foUows: to do for Europe as a whole what the previous generation did for France and Germany.
To create a single home, a common framework in which power is subordinate to the rule of law.
But a Union of 25 countries wUl have to answer three vital questions: what will be the role of the national states, the place of the citizen and the rights of cultural and ethnic minorities?
'In a Union of 25 countries there is the danger that the big Member States wiU permanently dominate the small ones.
That could be fatal to the legitimacy of the Union.
So in each of the institutions the citizens of the smaUer Member States must continue to have enough of a voice.
The powers of the Union must also be separated more clearly from those of the Member States.
If a Union of 25 is not to become unmanageable, it must concentrate on a number of principal duties, namely pressing crossborder issues.
For all other matters the chief responsibility must remain with Member States.
Where there is power there has to be accountabüity.
Where the Union exercises power, this must be subjected to democratic scrutiny.
This process is not yet strong enough.
Whilst Europe's economy is becoming more« international, democracy is still organized largely at national level.
Power and scrutiny are part of the same thing in Europe.
At national level there is scrutiny, but no poUtical power.
At European level there is political power, but no scrutiny.
In the long term this trend is a threat not only to the legitimacy of the Union, but to that of the national states as weU.
Urgent consideration should thus be given to new forms of democratic scrutiny in Europe, such as a single European electoral system for the European elections and a closer link between the national parliaments and the European parliament.
The more Member States there are, the greater the diversity within the Union.
Within each Member State too, there is an increasing realization that Europe is a mosaic of cultures.
How to handle this diversity is the biggest and oldest problem which Europe has to face: Hungarians in Romania and Slovakia, Germans in Poland and the Czech Republic, but also Catalans and Basques, Scots or Tyroleans.
Europe is more than its national states.
Add to that the presence in Europe of miltions of migrants from nonEuropean countries, including many Muslims.
Increasing diversity in Europe awakens xenophobic feelings.
The other person is perceived not as an ally but as a threat.
The main chaUenge to our peoples and states is thus to deepen our idea of our identity.
Can we shape our own identity, side by side with that of others and not in opposition to it?
Can we regard ourselves as members of the human race firstly and only secondly as citizens of a state?
Are we prepared to acknowledge that our identity as Europeans rests not on whether we are Germans, Spaniards or Poles, but on the fundamental values of freedom, tolerance, responsibUity and justice?
The battle against nationalism and xenophobia is an everpresent one.
Just as important for today's generation as it was for yesterday's.
Precisely at the time when Europeans are rising to heights never before achieved, precisely then they are most fiercely loyal to themselves and their culture.
The demons of nationaUsm are old.
The wiU of our peoples, embodied in this House, and the other institutions of the Union, must be to keep those demons in check and create a single home for all the peoples of Europe during the next 40 years.
Puerta (GUE/NGL). - (ES) Mr President, in commemorating 40 years since the signing of the Treaty of Rome, which began the process of European integration, we can either indulge in self-satisfaction and a string of tributes to the significance of this historic moment and the long road travelled, or, with more humility, we can take stock and look forward, just as people reaching 40 tend to think over the meaning of their lives, what they have achieved and what they stiU hope to do.
Taking a balanced view halfway between commemoration and reflection, we can confirm that these 40 years have seen a consolidation of the founding principles of the European Community.
Debates of the European Parliament
The consolidation of democracy in countries like Greece, Portugal, and my own Spain, is a fine example of the positive effect of the European Community.
But we must also recognize, with sorrow and uneasiness, that the prosperity achieved is not shared by all the citizens.
Over 20 miUion unemployed and 50 miUion poor are signs of serious structural problems in our economies and a great social injustice which will eventoaUy become intolerable and have undesirable effects on coexistence and the economy.
In addition, nationaUsm is reappearing and racial and xenophobic attitudes that we thought had disappeared for good are on the rise.
In the historic European theatre of the Balkans, there are bloody interethnic and intercultural wars in former Yugoslavia and Albania is falling apart, while we are incapable of developing a common foreign and security policy which would prevent conflicts, or at least resolve them within a reasonable time.
Since 1989, when the reunification of Germany signalled the end of the cold war, we have been unable to meet the challenge of building a political, economic and social area on a continental scale and in harmony with the CIS countries that have emerged from the former Soviet Union.
At this moment in Europe's history, when the conference of the 15 governments is working on the revision of the treaties and the design of European institutions fit for the enlargement, the European Parliament must be the political conscience and the engine of the construction of Europe, estabUshing a political union with common democratic institutions and policies to preserve and improve the European social model, currently being battered by ultra-Uberal policies and strategies in the framework of economic globalization aimed only at boosting profits on capital, at the cost of dispensing with social rights and indeed with the workers themselves.
In the face of this reality, my group stands for building real European democracy.
Building European democracy means Ocrystallizing political union against the free trade area option.
It means developing a constitutional framework for the Europe of the States and the Peoples and also for the Europe of the citizens, recognizing the political, nature of European citizenship and the social rights that go with it.
Building European democracy means that economic and monetary union must take more account of the true situation in society and the economy — an economy that respects thé environment - than is provided for by the nominal convergence indicators adopted in the Treaty of Maastricht.
The move towards the single currency must be bound by and subordinate to economic objectives where employment and the better distribution of wealth and income are priorities.
Economic and social cohesion, the fundamental principle of the socalled 'marriage contract' between the Member States, must be strengthened, as must worker participation within businesses, trade unions and European works councils, the right to information and joint responsibUity for decision-making.
This would prevent the recurrence of undesirable situations like the Renault VUvoorde case and the many similar cases which might occur.
Building European democracy means affirming the identity of the European Union at the international level with one voice and a common foreign and security policy promoting world peace and the rise of a new international order of democracy and solidarity.
These are the challenges and aims of our time and their defence constitutes one of the principal missions of the European Parliament, which must not be inward-looking; it must look outward to society and the other European institutions.
Mr President, on this highly symbolic anniversary of the European Union, we naturally drink to its future, but we do so in a spirit of constructive criticism which we regard as a positive contribution to the task of finally buUding the common home of all Europeans, a European democracy where the peoples and citizens of the Europe of the 21st Century can live in peace, freedom and welfare.
Roth (V). - (DE) Mr President, Mr Santer, honourable representative of the Commission, ladies and gentlemen: my dear Europe, as befits this formal commemorative sitting, I should like to dedicate to you a birthday poem, which is a counterpiece to Brecht's Von der Freundlichkeit der Welt: 'So does this mean we must forego our dreaming and say 'That's how it is and wUl remain'; that we, though gUmpsing cups with nectar teeming, must reach for those that are already drained?
It is intended to remind you of your beginnings, of the hopes and expectations that were placed in
Debates of the European Parliament
After forty years you look like a tree which, though planted in the soil, has never really taken root properly and does not quite have what it takes to make a fine tree.
It may have three branches, but it has no leaves, no canopy, and it yields precious Utile fruit.
Many are the dreams that have been shattered; somehow, dear Europe, your magic has gone.
What has become of the idea of the common area of peace, freedom, justice and democracy?
In former times, when Europe had yet to take shape but the European ideal was a noble vision, Europe stood for precisely that and for a future without frontiers.
Today the situation has changed completely.
Europe without frontiers represents insecurity.
Europe was a blessing, but today national governments are portraying it as a threat.
The opening of borders is perceived as one thing: dangerous!
In this way, there has been a concerted effort to awaken anxieties about Europe, to portray further unification as playing with fire, for European awareness has gradually given way to anti­European sentiment.
Stand up, birthday girl, to the ever more prevalent attempts to »nationalize instead of Europeanizing policies, to misuse the European Parliament for the purpose of pursuing and promoting national poUcies and to corrupt the European Union into an overblown nation state.
You are not the Europe of the intergovernmental conferences in which heads of state and ministers stage their exclusive show with no transparency, no pubUcity and no democratic control.
Maastricht has aggravated the democratic deficit.
The so­called second and third pUlars are jerry­buUt.
But an antidemocratic Europe is not our Europe.
That is why Maastricht Π must be a large­scale project against the absolutism of Brussels.
That is my wish for your birthday.
You are not the Europe of the fat cats, in which old­style capitalism has donned the mask of globaUzation and is on the march once more.
Europe must defend and develop the welfare state and the rule of law against the law of the jungle.
Europe is a reform project based not on neoliberatism but on ecological solidarity.
Europe is there to prevent the ruthless 'me first' society from gaining even more ground, marginalizing increasing numbers of people and imbuing them with the devastating feeling that they are to blame for their own fate, be they refugees, homeless, welfare recipients, unemployed, disabled or elderly, or be they at the present time miners in the Saar, Rhineland and Ruhr coalfields or car workers in Vilvoorde.
Europe must reflect upon its roots and reject the coordinated irresponsibility of industrialists, scientists and politicians who drive people and animals mad and whose obsession with progress makçs them see opportunities in the cloning of living creatures.
Europe is not the self­seeking model of parochialism, nor does it erect any new walls to the east or to the south.
Above aU, it does not define itself by excluding others but through its openness, pluralism, democracy and rich diversity.
One thing is sure about our Europe. It is not the sanctuary of Western Christendom, Mr Martens.
In this Europe atheists, Christians, Jews and Muslims are all equaUy at home.
ReUgion must not be a constituent factor, as demanded by those who would avenge Byzantium.
Only then wtil Europe be a civiUzation.
But above all, Europe, you belong to your citizens, to everyone who Uves here.
You are the Europe of men and women ­ of women who wUl not simply accept their fate, who are fighting so that they do not only inherit half of the places in heaven but can also secure them here on earth.
But who, for goodness' sake, is responsible for drawing up the guest list for your birthday party?
Seventy­three dignitaries have been invited to Rome, of whom two are women.
Dear Europe, you have done nothing to deserve such manifestations of outdated patriarchaUsm!
We wish you more ­not more profit, but more democracy, more solidarity and more ecological responsibUity.
My birthday vision, then, is one of more Europe and more democracy. But it is not a vision; it is a recipe for survival.
My own wish, Mr President, is a new PA system for the European Parliament, because, as the former manager of a rock­and­róll band, I must say ­ and this is relevant to Europe too ­ that the sound output reaUy was very scratchy.
There are better systems!
President. ­ Thank you very much, Mrs Roth. You are entirely right: the anthem sounded terrible and finished worse.
Debates of the European Parliament
I should simply like to suggest that we think about the lessons handed down to us by the founding fathers of the Treaty of Rome, and about what we have to do today and tomorrow if we want to keep faith with the spirit that inspired them.
They were revolutionaries, in a way, in that they conceived and realized innovations which werp to revolutionize our society.
There was creative innovation in their desire to unite former enemies, and especially to bring in Germany as a full Member of that first group of six founder Members of the Community.
There was radical change in the abandonment of the intergovernmental method in favour of a mode of integration leading towards a completely new kind of federalism.
There was creative imagination in the establishment of an original institutional mechanism based on three bodies: a CouncU of Ministers, an executive Commission and an Assembly which was to become the European Parliament.
This list of innovations could be longer.
The founding fathers had courage, political vision and institutional imagination.
Since that time, huge advances have been achieved: the creation of an internal market moving towards unification, a monetary system on its way to becoming an economic and monetary union, a parliament elected by universal suffrage and vested with budgetary powers, in particular, etc.
Today, though, we have undoubtedly reached a turning point that the Union will find it difficult to negotiate.
There is no certainty as yet that the present generation will have the same courage and vision as the authors of the Treaty of Rome.
Among the vast reforms that we need to accomplish, I will mention three.
In 40 years, everything has been changed.
The European Union has become a living, powerful entity, but it has to confront two major challenges: the opening-up to the East, to accommodate the states of Central and Eastern Europe, and resistance to the West in dealings with the friendly but very heavy hand of the United States of America, and in dealing with globalization.
These are major strategic issues where success is extremely difficult to come by.
The second reform is an institutional one, and is particularly topical: the extremely important task faced by the Intergovernmental Conference.
I shall not dweU on that. I shaU however dwell on the third reform, which must also be a revolution, at least in the mind.
It involves bringing into the European domain certain matters which had initially been left to the competence of the Member States and then, very slowly, little by Uttle, brought into the sphere of action of the Community and the Union: social poUcy, which is still very inadequate, economic poUcy, foreign policy, and policies relating to human rights, democracy and matters of internal security.
For a wide variety of reasons, our Member States have slowed down the extension of the Union's powers into these areas.
Parliament itself treads cautiously on this ground, which threatens to open up differences between us.
Today, public opinion is no longer content with a single market opened up to free trade and competition.
PubUc opinion wants to know what Europe's poUtical choices are, and it wants to assess the appropriateness of those choices and ask us for explanations.
Mad cow disease and the Renault crisis bear witness to this.
In 1957, public opinion was very remote from the European scene.
Now, it wants to know what use Europe is, and above all who benefits.
Times have changed, but, as in 1957, we need clear-sightedness, intelUgence, determination and that spark of revolutionary courage without which nothing really new is ever achieved.
Bonde (I-EDN). - (DA) Mr President, 'forty, fat and finished' is what they say in Denmark on your fortieth birthday, affectionately, and mostly in fun.
The Treaty of Rome is now 40, and with its 21 392 regulations, directives and other EU rules it is much too fat, but unfortunately not finished.
How ought the treaty to look, if we were to start on a new one?
In the first place, it should be a treaty for all the nations of Europe, and not just the 15 which now belong to the EU.
It should be flexible and elastic, instead of forcing everyone to choose between all or nothing.
It should be built on a foundation of common minimum rules to regulate the common market, and apart from these should only have common rules on international problems, for example sea and air pollution.
The new treaty should be democratically founded on elected parliaments.
Laws should not be adopted by officials.
The cooperation which takes place should be accessible to the public, and it should be possible to debate it openly before the laws are adopted.
If laws work badly, it should be possible for them to be changed again by the electors and those they have elected, without waiting for a majority decision in the Commission and 62 out of 87 votes in the Union's Council of Ministers.
A new treaty should give precedence to democracy in the Member States, if an international provision conflicts with a national law.
A new treaty should be a legal blueprint for binding cooperation between free nations, but not an actual constitution for a new state.
Between member countries there could be moderate and amicable borders, easy to cross for goods and people with legitimate business, but it should also be possible to protect a nation from environmentally damaging products, drugs and international crime.
Debates of the European Parliament
Our group is caUed the Group of Independents for a Europe of Nations.
That is what we are, and what we want to have - a Europe for all the European nations, instead of a European superstate with its own currency, police and armed forces.
That does not make us worse Europeans, but brings us closer to the public feeling in our countries.
Everyone knows what most people in our countries would answer if they had the chance to state their views on the crucial question: should the authorities in Brussels adopt most of our laws, or only deal with matters which we cannot regulate effectively ourselves?
There may be disagreement on where to draw the line and on details, but today the crucial dividing line unfortunately runs between the voters and Brussels.
Most people want freedom and more democracy.
There is no one demonstrating in favour of having more decisions transferred from open parliaments to the closed rooms of officials, but that is exactly what is happening with the Maastricht Π treaty.
As if this were not enough, there is then an obligation for the Member States to comply with the rules and principles of the treaty when they adopt their 'own laws.
I quote: 'Member States are still required in their action to comply with the general rules laid down in Article 5 of the Treaty, by taking all appropriate measures to ensure fulfilment of their obligations under the Treaty and by abstaining from any measure which could jeopardize the attainment of the objectives of the Treaty'.
When the EU introduced the subsidiarity principle in 1992, there were 16 027 common provisions.
The legislation was then meant to be simptified, but the figure has grown to 21 392 in only three years.
With Maastricht Π, not a single one of these provisions is being returned to the voters and their elected representatives in local authorities or national parliaments.
On the other hand, with the proposal for more majority decisions, it is certain that the EU will experience even more growing pains and become even fatter.
Let us put the EU on a reducing treatment, let us put the treaty on a diet instead of building up more Union.
The EU should deal with certain things less, but do them better.
Just think if instead of 'forty, fat and finished', we could say 'forty, free and flexible'.
Muscardini (NI). - (FT) Mr President, today we are celebrating the 40th anniversary of the Treaties of Rome.
During those 40 years, the political situation in Europe has changed greatly, as has the political balance of forces.
In March 1957, to take Italy as an example, all the poUtical parties, from the Christian Democrats to the Italian Social Movement, enthusiastically voted for ratification of the Treaty.
They all did so, except the Communist Party representatives, who voted firmly against the Treaty establishing the EEC, and the Socialists who abstained and instead voted for the Euratom Treaty.
So it is clear that even 40 years ago there were pro- and a anti-Europe factions.
As representative of the National AUiance, a movement born of the ItaUan Social Movement and its merging with certain components of civil society and of the Uberai CathoUc movement, I proudly defend the choice we made at that time in favour of European integration, a choice that not everybody in this Chamber can say they made at the right moment.
Forty years ago, in a world in which the economic and social situation was still very critical, we believed we had opted for a democratic and liberal West, we believed, as we do today, that building a poUtical Europe was the only way to prevent conflict, promote development, guarantee the growth and protection of human rights and safeguard democracy.
Forty years ago, Europe was divided into two super-blocs: the Western bloc and the Soviet bloc.
Now that this empire has coUapse, the communists, who at the time saw the Soviet COMECON system as their main point of reference, today affirm that they have become Europeans.
We want to believe this, but to be European does not just mean professing to be so in words but actoaUy means cooperating in bringing about political integration: a union which must take priority in relation to economics, just as fighting unemployment must take priority in relation to the forthcoming enlargement.
On this solemn occasion, we have to admit that Altiero SpineUi, a man from the left and one of the great founders of political union, would feel very uneasy today in this Europe that only seems capable of looking on impotently at the tragedy of Yugoslavia, followed by the present one in Albania. I am calling this Europe impotent because it has no common foreign poUcy and is therefore not able to act as an official interlocutor for anyone.
Debates of the European ParUament
In 1954, the idea of a European defence community collapsed, because of various national sentiments and feelings of rancour clearly linked to the recent conflicts.
Similarly today, for reasons of national interest and unjustified pride, and the excessive desire of certain countries to expand their own power and economic strength in relation to that of other Union countries, we seem to be accepting without protest the demise of poUtical union.
Note well: economic union and the single currency could be achieved and would produce benefits only if we first achieve political integration.
Note well: over-hasty enlargement without a consolidated and solidary poUtical union will end in the tragedy of increased unemployment and condemn Europe to a long and sombre period of recession and social conflict.
I wonder what De Gasperi and Schuman would think today if they saw the 20 miUion European unemployed, a situation that does not seem of great concern to many heads of state compared with the Moloch of the single currency.
And, looking at those 20 rrtiltion unemployed in Europe, I remember saying in this Chamber, some years ago, that Delors' White Paper on unemployment was a book of dreams and not of concrete and viable projects.
I called it a book of dreams not because the proposals were not valid ones but because even then I had realised that the Union did not have an effective political will, i.e., that the Member States were not resolved to seek common solutions.
So that the celebration of the Treaties of Rome and of 40 years of proceeding along a laborious road are reflected not in a ceremony but in a concrete commitment, our Parliament must abandon the system of preconstituted majorities, controlled by this or that government, and must become the genuine expression of the wiU of the people, who want work for their young people, understanding for their old people, peace and social balance.
A Parliament of that kind, a democratic interlocutor that is representative of the people, will be more able to dialectically oppose the governments of our countries, so that Europe can assure future generations of a more secure future.
The past 40 years must not just lead to rhetorical celebrations or to a Europe of unemployment; they must be remembered as a positive experience that offers hope and also guarantees the future.
(The formal sitting was closed at 10.40 a.m.)
