Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 12 by Hugh McMahon (H-0331/97) Subject: Fisheries Council 14/15 April 1997
Can the Council inform Parliament of the outcome of the Fisheries Council meeting of 14/15 April 1997 and in particular publish details of the proposals agreed with respect to the multiannual guidance programme Pop IV?
To what extent do the agreed proposals differ from Parliament's position agreed in the Kofoed report and does the Council intend to reconsult Parliament on these proposals?
Answer
In reply to Mr McMahon's question I shall be glad to forward to him a copy of the information he requests concerning the state of progress in the Council on the proposal for a Council Decision on the objectives and detailed rules for Multiannual Guidance Programmes IV, restructuring the Community fisheries sector for the period from 1 January 1997 to 31 December 2002, with a view to achieving a balance on a suitable basis between resources and their exploitation (MAGPs IV).
I can now inform the Honourable Member that at its meeting on 14 and 15 April 1997 the Council reached a political agreement on the basis of a compromise put forward by the Presidency concerning the above programme.
I must, however, stress that an agreement of this kind entails a qualified majority.
The delegations of the United Kingdom and France did in fact announce their intention of voting against the compromise reached when it comes up for formal adoption.
Since this matter concerns the European Parliament's opinion of 13 December 1996,1 can assure the Honourable Member that the letter and spirit of that opinion were in evidence throughout the negotiations which made it possible to reach the political agreement mentioned above.
But as I have just mentioned, the political agreement on the MAGP IV programme was possible only at the price of a compromise in which the delegations inevitably did have to give up part of their demands and which naturally reflects the highest possible common denominator.
As regards the extent to. which the elements of the Presidency's compromise differ from the position expressed by Parliament in its opinion, it is not my task here to make a comparative exegesis of the two texts.
I would repeat that this involved a compromise and that each and every one had to make concessions to be able to reach it.
Question No 13 by Marie-Paule Kestelijn-Sierens (H-0335/97) Subject: Statute for the European Company
The proposal for a regulation for a Statute for the European Company has been bogged down for more than twenty five years.
The European Commission has repeatedly submitted a proposal.
The main purpose of the regulation is to create the possibility for companies established in different Member States of being allowed to merge or establish holdings or joint subsidiary undertakings without being encumbered by the sometimes insuperable difficulties resulting from the discrepancies between legal positions in the different countries.
It is also intended to make it easier for businesses to operate transnatiónally.
Will the Council state whether any progress has been registered, and will it indicate which states are standing in the way of the regulation and for what reasons?
In an attempt to overcome that problem of the role for workers in this context, the Commission issued a communication in 1995 on information for and consultation of workers and at the same time set up a highlevel Group of Experts under the chairmanship of Mr Davignon to put forward possible solutions.
That Group of Experts is expected to present its conclusions in the second half of May.
In the same context the Commission has ananged two meetings within the framework of social dialogue the last of which is to be held on 6 June.
The Council is therefore awaiting the Commission's conclusions on the debate under way on the above-mentioned initiatives.
Moreover, the Council has placed this item on the agenda for its meeting on employment and social affairs to be held on 12 June.
For its part the Council is fully aware of the need for a Statute for the European Company in the Community context.
It therefore hopes that the broad debate under way produces satisfactory solutions for the various interests at issue here.
The Presidency can assure Honourable Members that it will leave no stone unturned and will do its utmost to mediate to bring the dossier to an appropriate conclusion.
Question No 14 by Christine Oddy (H-0336/97) Subject: Disappearances in Sri Lanka
Amnesty International has found that more than 600 people have 'disappeared' in Sri Lanka between late 1995 and early 1996 - the highest number since 1990.
In one incident on 19 July 1996, at least 40 young men are reported to have 'disappeared' after they were rounded up by army personnel in Kaithady.
There are fears they may have been massacred.
What moves will the Council take to press the Sri Lankan government to take more vigorous action to end these human rights violations?
Answer
It is clear, as previous questions by other Honourable Members have shown, that the situation in Sri Lanka, is complex and that atrocities have been committed against innocent communities inespective of their origin.
All acts of violence are to be condemned, particularly against innocent civilians.
Rather than trying to apportion blame to one party or another, the Union believes that the only way to find lasting peace and stability is through dialogue.
On 17 April 1997, the European Union published a declaration welcoming 'the exchange of letters between the President of Sri Lanka and the leader of the opposition on a bipartisan approach to the ethnic conflict in that country.
The European Union believes that this exchange offers a constructive approach to the problems facing Sri Lanka and a promise of consistency for the future, regardless of changes in government.
The EU hopes that this exchange will be implemented in good faith and will contribute to a building of confidence between the different parties and to an ending of confrontational politics in Sri Lanka.
The European Union also hopes that the exchange will contribute to a national agreement to ensure peace and a lasting and fair settlement to the conflict, which has impeded the country's development and taken such a heavy toll of lives and resources among Sri Länkans of every origin.
The European Union shall assist, where possible, reconstruction and rehabilitation efforts in the north and east, in particular Jaffna, taking into account the prevailing conditions for successful implementation of reconstruction efforts in the peninsula.'
Although I perfectly understand the worries expressed in his question by the R.H. Member, to this end, I would like to remind the intervention on this subject made by the European Union during the last session of the Commission on the United Nations Human Rights.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 15 by Hans Lindqvist (H-0338/97) Subject: The European Parliament's new buildings in Brussels and Strasbourg
What is the total investment cost of those buildings, including their planning and construction, the final inspection on completion and removals?
How high are their (estimated) annual running costs?
Is their construction and the associated expenditure really compatible with the sound management of the Member States', the EU's and, ultimately, the public's and taxpayers' money?
Answer
Mr Lindqvist's question to the Council deals with the cost, planning, construction, handing over and equipping of the European Parliament's new buildings in Brussels and Strasbourg.
At the risk of disappointing the Honourable Member, I can only tell him that the Council has neither the jurisdiction nor the information required to provide an answer to the very specific questions he asks about the European Parliament's new buildings in Brussels and Strasbourg.
Indeed, if he wants to obtain reliable answers, having regard to the latest developments on the European Parliament buildings scene, he would certainly be well advised to put his questions direct to the Parliamentary authorities.
Question No 16 by James Nicholson (H-0341/97)
Subject: Special Support Programme for Peace and Reconciliation in Northern Ireland and the border countries of Ireland 1995-1999
What action is proposed to counter the reduction in available funds to District Partnerships occasioned by the prevailing exchange rate between the ECU and £ Sterling and can additional funds be made avaüable to restore the original value of the fund to those partnerships?
Answer
Mr Nicholson's question refers to the Special Support Programme for Peace and Reconciliation in Northern Ireland.
In this regard the Honourable Member's attention should first be drawn to the fact that the programme he mentions, the principle of which was approved by the Essen European Council in December 1994, forms part of a Community initiative and is therefore governed by the rules of the Structural Funds.
Under those rules the Commission is responsible for implementing and managing the Programme.
The Honourable Member must therefore put his question to the Commission.
Question No 17 by José Valverde López (H-0348/97) Subject: The European Union's strategy vis-à-vis imports of agricultural products
The Agriculture Council adopted a European Union strategy for trade in floricultural products in which it outlined a policy for protecting European agriculture from imports from the outside world, setting out various basic criteria to be met in negotiations with third countries.
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The Honourable Member's question concerns the European Union's future strategy for the importation of agricultural products.
It is conect, as the Honourable Member mentions in his question, that the 'Agriculture' Council, at its meeting on 17/19 March, examined the Commission communication on trade in floricultural products and approved the outline of the strategy proposed in the floriculture sector, whilst noting however that the delegations' request to bring the strategy developed for the ACP and SPG countries into line with the eight strategic axes suggested for the other countries.
The Honourable Member's attention is also drawn to the fact that the Council on the same occasion reiterated, in more general terms, the need to have regard, when defining the Community's position in negotiations with the third countries, to the various aspects of the agricultural market and the repercussions of any concessions envisaged on that market as well as to the need to develop a global and coherent strategy in the matter.
As regards the negotiations with the Meditenañean countries, the Community takes as a basis the principle that concessions already granted under former agreements must be maintained, including within the agriculture sector.
For new concessions within the field of agriculture, the, Community takes the criterion of traditional commercial channels for proposing a zero-rated tariff quota.
The size of the quota is calculated on the basis of the average for the past three years for which statistics are available.
Question No 18 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0349/97) Subject: Rights of succession of Greek citizens in Istanbul
In its written reply of 5 March 1996 to Petition No 305/94 (PE 211.841/rev.) by Mrs Elpida Frangopoulou on the question of property ownership by Greeks in Istanbul, the Commission replied that it intended 'to make every effort to ensure that this point (was) brought up at the next EC-Turkey Council meeting'.
In the light of this, the European Parliament's Committee on Petitions decided to hold over discussion of the matter until the Association Council met.
Has the Council taken the necessary measures to ensure that the matter is discussed at the forthcoming meeting of the EC-Turkey Association Council?
Answer
This question put by Mr Alavanos concerns the rights of succession of Greek citizens in Istanbul and, more specifically, the inclusion of this problem on the agenda of the EU-Türkey Association Council held in Luxembourg on 29 April.
My reply can scarcely be much different basically from that given by my Italian predecessor to an identical question put by Mrs Daskalaki during the sitting on 13 March 1996.
That reply mentioned in particular that the Council of the European Union, whilst it deplored the situation described in the question, was not in a position to formulate a view on a problem which came within the field of the implementation of private law (in this case the rights of succession).
It should in fact be stressed on this subject that, legally speaking, the Council is not empowered to give any decision on problems bound up with the implementation of legal rules forming part of the national law of a sovereign State, which is undoubtedly the case with Turkish law on the rights of succession.
I can already tell the Honourable Member, moreover, that there was no item on the rights of succession of Greek citizens in Istanbul on the agenda for the recent meeting of the EU-Turkey Association Council.
I have also just learned the ins and outs of this affair through this question which reached us in Greek on 23 April 1997 and in a working language on the 28th, that is the day before the Association Council.
The Council would also like to stress in this regard that it does not feel bound by undertakings entered into by other institutions which go against the Parliament or its branches.
Having said that, I can nevertheless assure the Honourable Member that the Presidency will not fail to explore the ways and means likely to clarify this situation and to pinpoint existing possible courses for action to reach a satisfactory solution to this problem.
Question No 19 by Bernd Posselt (H-0352/97) Subject: Refugees from international and civil wars
What steps has the Council taken during the Dutch Presidency, and what steps is it intending to take (perhaps along the lines of the relevant submission by Commissioner Gradin) to cope with the streams of refugees which appear with little warning during international and civil wars and in so doing to ensure that the burden falling on the EU is equitably shared?
Answer
Mr Posselt's question concerns the problems bound up with taking in refugees from war or civil war.
On 21 March 1997 the Commission sent to the Council its proposal for a joint action funded under Article K.3, 2(b) of the Treaty on European Union dealing with the protection of displaced persons.
According to our information, Mrs Gradin is going to present this important initiative personally to the Ministers at the next 'JAI Council.
The Commission's proposal already being examined and it will be considered further during the Netherlands Presidency.
However, before taking a decision in the matter, the Council would like to have the European Parliament's opinion within the framework of the consultation procedure provided for in Article K.6.
Within the field covered by the question the Council also adopted on 25 September 1995 a resolution on sharing the burden of taking in displaced persons for a temporary stay and on 4 March 1996 it took the decision on a warning and emergency procedure for sharing the burden.
Furthermore, on the basis of a Danish initiative some initial consideration is being given to certain types of ancillary protection for persons who cannot be regarded as refugees as defined in the Geneva Convention but who nevertheless need a certain type of international protection.
I should like finally to draw attention to one aspect - the taking in of unaccompanied minors who are arriving ever more frequently in our States and for whom, of course, we need to provide special protection because of their vulnerability, whilst not however encouraging this phenomenon too much.
There is in fact a strong chance that the meeting of the Council on 26 and 27 May will approve a joint action in this matter.
Question No 20 by Peter Truscott (H-0355/97) Subject: Restriction of freedom of movement by the Belgian Government
Following a recent ruling against the Belgian Government by the European Court of Justice regarding illegal restrictions on the freedom of movement of EU citizens, the Belgian authorities appear to be placing unacceptable restrictions on the travel of thirdcountry nationals.
Will the Council investigate and comment on reports that the Belgian Embassy in London is refusing to issue visas to thirdcountry nationals in a reasonable period, using red-tape as an excuse to effectively eliminate cross-EU travel for thirdcountry tourists and business people using the UK as a base?
Could the Council condemn the increasing intolerance and cavalier attitude of the Belgian authorities responsible for issuing travel and work permits for EU citizens and thirdcountry nationals?
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4-500/171
In this regard it is most important to stress the fact that where compliance with Community law is at issue, it is the task of the Commission to ensure that the law is properly applied in accordance with, in particular, Articles 155 and 169 of the Treaty.
Since we are dealing here with the Court's judgment of 20 February 1997 in Case C 344/95, to which the Honourable Member refers, it is appropriate to point out that the judgment concerns the application by Belgium of Community rales on the free movement of workers who are citizens of the European Union.
It is now a matter for Belgium to take the measures required to comply with the judgment of the Court of Justice, in accordance with Article 171 of the Treaty, and for the Commission to ensure that such measures are effectively carried out.
In view of the above remarks it is clear that the question put by the Honourable Member does not fall within the terms of reference of the Council but concerns the Commission, to which it should logically have been addressed.
Question No 21 by Michl Ebner (H-0358/97) Subject: Protection of ethnic minorities
Unfortunately, the European Union itself has so far not introduced any specific provisions on the protection of ethnic minorities, yet it wants future Member States in Eastern Europe to do so.
Answer
In reply to this question by Mr Ebner on the protection of ethnic minorities I would assure the .
Honourable Member that the Council is fully aware how important it is to-preserve specific ethnic, regional and local features which serve to make our Community a multi-faceted kaleidoscope in which diversity provides all the richness.
It goes without saying, moreover, that to the Council's way of thinking all individuals who have the nationality of one of the Member States of the Union must enjoy the same rights and freedoms.
In other words there should be no discrimination for any reason against anyone at all just because they belong to a national minority.
The Council in fact considers that it is the duty of each of the governments of the Member States to see that outbreaks of xenophobia do not trigger discrimination within the national communities which is incompatible with the foundations on which the Union rests - freedom and democracy.
Having said that, it is nevertheless appropriate to point out to the Honourable Member that the Council, as an institution is not involved in thé Intergovernmental Conference responsible for amending the treaties, is not empowered to express a view on the suggestions which his questions contain.
I must also tell the Honourable Member that the Council has not in the meantime received any Commission proposal on the protection of ethnic minorities.
The Council will of course give all due careful consideration to any proposals which the Commission might submit to it hi this field.
Question No 22 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0360/97)
Subject: Attempts to falsify social and political history in an article of the draft constitution of the Republic of Poland
In accordance with a decision taken by the Constitutional Affairs Committee of the national assembly of the Republic of Poland, the final draft of the constitution of the Republic of Poland includes an article which bans political parties which adopt the methods and practices of Nazism, fascism and communism.
Will the Council say whether it intends to take the necessary steps to prevent the emergence of authoritarianism in European countries, the falsification of social and political history in Europe and the prohibition of the free functioning of parties which have combated - and continue to
Will it also condemn the attempts being made to equate and identify the progressive movement of workers with the inhuman theories and practices of Nazism and fascism which once again presage the abolishment of basic rights and civil liberties, a threat which is today directed against communists, but will be directed tomonow, as in the past, against every progressive social, political and trade union organization?
Answer
In reply to this question by Mr Ephremidis on the adoption of the final draft of the Constitution of the Republic of Poland, I have to admit that the Council has never had occasion to discuss it. For a very good reason!
This is a problem which falls exclusively within the field of Poland's domestic law.
In this context the Council wishes to point out that it attaches the highest value to safeguarding the ideals of liberty and democracy as recognised by all Member countries of the Union.
But even so the Council, as a Union institution, is not authorized to intervene in a debate which falls solely within the competence of elected representatives whose legitimacy springs from a democratically expressed popular vote.
Question No 23 by Ulf Holm (H-0365/97) Subject: Motion of censure on an individual member of the Commission
The resolution of the Temporary Committee of Inquiry into BSE adopted by the European Parliament on 19 February 1997 included a call for Parliament to be able to table a motion of censure on an individual member of the Commission, whereas, at present, it can table a motion of censure only on the Commission as a whole.
This would require an amendment to the Treaty.
Has the Council considered such an amendment during the cunent IGC?
If so, will it be included in the final proposal for a new Treaty?
If not, why not?
Answer
Mr Holm's question on the revision of the treaties and in particular on introducing into those treaties a provision on the tabling of a motion of censure on an individual Member of the Commission calls for a three-fold clarification from the Council.
First it is appropriate to point out that the Council, as a Community institution, is not taking part in the Intergovernmental Conference responsible for revision of the treaties.
In fact the parties involved in that Conference, at least as regards the presentation of proposed amendments, are the national representatives of the Member States and the Commission.
The Council therefore takes no part in the Intergovernmental Conference and, a fortiori, has no influence over its work.
That applies in the case of your second question also.
Finally, for the same reasons as set out above, the Council does not need to explain its position since it is not an institution involved in the amendment of the treaties.
Question No 24 by Ioannis Theonas (H-0367/97) Subject: Failure of the Council to take specific measures in favour of SMUs
The proposal for a programme aimed at guaranteeing loans granted to small and medium-sized undertakings in respect of investments which create jobs (Elise) is continuing to be blocked in the Council.
The relevant appropriations are also being blocked in the Community budget, owing to the absence of a legal basis.
Furthermore, the failure to take specific measures to create a favourable climate for the smallest SMUs is making thousands of SMUs bankrupt and leading to the loss of even more jobs.
No 4-500/173 competition policy as well as measures to facilitate the access of SMUs .to sources of external funding on accessible tenns, within the framework of a more general policy aimed at supporting productive investments, averting the loss of yet more jobs and reinforcing the productive base in peripheral and remote regions?
Answer
The Council examined at its meeting on 11 November 1996 the Commission proposal to guarantee loans for investment by SMEs (Elise).
During this discussion it became apparent that under the present circumstances the required unanimous support for the scheme did not exist.
As the Honourable Member of Parliament is aware, the European Council of Dublin concluded that the fight against unemployment remains the prime responsibility of the Member States.
Additionally, the European Council considered this issue as a top priority for the Union.
Building òn the Essen strategy to combat unemployment, the European Council adopted the Dublin Declaration on Employment that stresses the need for a continuation of the macroeconomic strategy for economic growth and employment increased efforts to modernize the markets and exploit new sources of employment, a focusing on labour market efficiency and on investment in human resources, making taxation and social protection systems more employment-friendly and strengthening the interplay between macroeconomic and structural policies in the Member States' Multiannual Employment Programmes.
Most actions in the context of the above guidelines are particularly beneficial to the SMEs.
The same European Council stressed once again the essential role of the Internal Market in promoting growth and employment in Europe and the crucial role to this effect from the enhancement of the competitiveness of SMEs.
SMEs will also greatly benefit from the extension of the Simpler Legislation for the Internal Market (SLIM) initiative.
The implementation of this policy should make a substantial contribution towards creating the economic conditions for the development of European SMEs.
Furthermore, recognizing the particular importance of SMEs for the European economy, the Union has already provided a framework for financial support through the EIB, the EIF and the structural funds.
Question No 25 by Alex Smith (H-0369/97)
Subject: IGC review of Euratom Stämtes and powers of oversight by the European Parliament
What considerations have been given in. the Dutch Presidency's draft on the proposals of the Inter-Governmental Conference for a revision of the European Atomic Energy Agency (EURATOM) statutes and the inclusion of extended powers of oversight by the European Parliament over EURATOM's activities and priorities?
Answer
In answer to Mr Smith's question on the review of the Treaty establishing the European Atomic Energy Community, I must once again stress two aspects.
First, I would point out that I am appearing before this House in my capacity as President-in-Office of the Council to answer questions which are addressed in principle to the Council and not in my capacity as a representative of the Netherlands Government.
Secondly, it is important in this regard to draw attention once again to the fact that the Council is not a part of the Intergovernmental Conference entrusted with the task of revising the Treaties.
In view of the above observations I am accordingly unable to answer the question put by the Honourable Member.
Question No 26 by Inger Schörling (H-0372/97)
Subject: Transport of live animals for slaughter
In recent weeks, people in Sweden have been demonstrating on the streets to express their disgust at the way in which live animals for slaughter are transported.
The directives on the protection during transport of live animals for slaughter is not being complied with.
They are insufficient to ensure that the animals do not suffer during transport.
Pursuant to Directive 95/29/EC (l), the duration of transport may not exceed eight hours, after which a 24-hour rest must be provided.
Exceptions to this rule are possible if the vehicles used comply with very specific conditions.
Such conditions were to be laid down in a separate directive.
As this has not yet happened, anyone can take advantage of the exception.
The limits laid own in Directive 95/29/EC are therefore meaningless.
It is a well known fact that animals for slaughter do not fare well during transport, that they may become stressed, be injured and become sick, all of which also has an effect on the quality of their meat.
Does the Council consider there to be any justification at all for transporting live animals for slaughter over long distances?
Does it intend to promote a system under which animals are slaughtered as close as possible to the place in which they were reared? If so, how?
Will the Council take steps to end the granting of EU economic aid in respect o the transport of live animals for slaughter.
Answer
In reply to Mrs Schörling's question I must stress that the Council adopted on 29 June 1995 Directive 95/29/EEC with the aim of complementing the existing provisions on the protection of animals during transportation as laid down in Directive 91/628/EEC.
That Directive, which was due to be implemented by the Member States before 31 December 1996, lays down provisions which the Council regarded as necessary at that stage to see that animals were protected and to promote the welfare of all animals during transportation, inespective of their species or category, so that each animal should have the same conditions as regards welfare. The directive provides:
(b) in Article 5 that the competent authorities in the various Member States shall be obliged to grant the transporters a valid authorization for the transportation of animals subject to compliance with the requirements laid down in the legislation.
It is therefore in the first instance the responsibility of the Member States to see that these requirements are respected and to impose penalties in the event of any breach in accordance with the provisions of Article 18 of the Directive.
Moreover, in order to complement the existing provisions, Article 13 of the Directive provides:
(a) in the first paragraph that the Council should, on the basis of a proposal from the Commission, set further standards to be met by the means of transport.
A Commission proposal to that effect is expected in the next few weeks;
The Presidency expects to be able to put everything in train to obtain a decision on the matter before the end of the first half of 1997.
Moreover, a proposal for a regulation has just been submitted to the Council aimed at amending Regulation (EEC) No 805/68 on the common organization of the market in the beef and veal sector in order to establish a link between the granting of export refunds and respect for the provisions on welfare.
The Council is due to decide upon this proposal in the very near future.
The Council has not so far received any other Commission proposals on the subject of welfare.
Question No 27 by Hervé Fabre-Aubrespy (H-0374/97)
Subject: European Community expenditure on information, communication and education
As budgetary authority responsible for authorizing information measures funded by the Community budget, how can the Council ensure that information campaigns conducted by the European Communities, or with their resources, do not interfere with national laws, especially in the context of national election campaigns?
How can it ensure that such measures do not consist in unilaterally disseminating a particular view on a controversial issue, that they are conducted in close association with all partners and that they respect the principle of financial transparency?
Answer
In reply to this question by Mr Fabre-Aubespy on European Community expenditure on information, communication and education, I must confirm that the European Parliament and the Council are the institutions which draw up the Community budget.
However, pursuant to Article 205 of the EEC Treaty the Commission is empowered to implement on its own responsibility and within the limits of the appropriations without Council interference.
Moreover, as regards the Commission's activities in the field of information or other fields, the Treaty provides for various means to be made available to the European Parliament to exercise political control over that institution.
Question No 28 by Anne Mcintosh (H-0202/97)
Subject: European Union measures to combat paedophilia
Will the Council provide an update on the progress of the multiannual programme (1996-2000) to combat the problem of paedophilia? Is action taking place to facilitate the exchange of information between Member States on known offenders?
Specifically, what action has been taken to ensure that the propagation of material related to the sexual exploitation of children is not accessible on the Internet?
In what ways can the Council envisage increased cooperation between Member States, either judicial or practical, to help prevent such material appearing on the Internet?
Answer
On 29 November 1996, the Council adopted a Joint Action establishing an incentive and exchange programme for persons responsible for combating trade in human beings and sexual exploitation of children.
It is the Commission which is responsible for the implementation of this programme and it shall report annually to the European Parliament and the Council on its implementation.
On 16 December 1996, the Council adopted a Joint Action extending the mandate given to the Europol Drags Unit.
When the Joint Action entered into force, the facilities of the EDU became available to the Member States.
This includes exchange of information, support of operations and analytical support.
The EDU has already dealt with several requests from Member States in this field.
The Member States so far have little or no experience of offences perpetrated through the Internet but it is clear that material which is illegal outside the Internet should also be illegal when the material is on the Internet.
Therefore, the problem of the Internet cannot be seen in isolation.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 29 by Jan Andersson (H-0249/97)
Subject: Nickel-free euro coins
Nickel causes allergic reactions in many people in Europe.
It is to be found inter alia in coins.
Now that the Union is to have a common cunency, it is important that the new coins do not cause allergic reactions.
How does the Netherlands Presidency intend to ensure that the new euro coins are nickel-free?
Answer
In answer to Mr Andersson's very interesting question, I would like to point out that the Council has examined the issue of nickel sensitivity very carefully.
As a result, the Council has adopted a Directive (94/27/EC) that limits the use of nickel in products that are in direct and prolonged contact with the skin.
The mint directors have presented a proposal where at least four of the eight coins are nickel free.
Alternative nickel free materials are being considered also for the remaining coins.
However, there might be technical problems with the bi-coloured one euro and two euro coins and in these cases further testing will be required.
The Council has not yet decided upon the issue which will be resolved in the framework of a formal proposal by the Commission based on Article 105a(2) of the Treaty.
At the ECOFIN Council last Monday, the Council was informed by Commissioner de Silguy of the state of play concerning the preparation of the Euro coins in particular with regard to the outstanding questions relating to their technical characteristics and design: the nickel content of certain coins (10 and 20 euro cents) and the shape of 20 euro cents coins.
Commissioner de Silguy indicated that, after finalizing consultations with associations of consumers and of the blind as well as with vending machines producers, his Institution - taking into account the outcome of these consultations and based on the results of the design competition - will present to the next ECOFIN Council in June a formal proposal for the Euro coins.
Questions to the Commission
Question No 39 by Glenys Kinnock (H-0323/97)
Is the Commission aware that President Arnaldo Alemán and the Liberal Alliance in Nicaragua have introduced a bill which is intended to dissolve the Institute of Women and which will create a Ministry of the Family?
Is the Commission also aware that, if this bill becomes law, it will represent a serious attack on the rights of women and children in Nicaragua?
In view of the Commission's and Council's stated position on gender rights, will effective and immediate action be taken to reflect the concern which is justifiably felt about this proposed legislation in Nicaragua?
Answer
The European Commission cannot enter into a debate about internal administrative and political decisions taken by a sovereign government.
Nevertheless, the Commission is concerned about the future of INIM as an autonomous national women's institute, which up till now has had its own legal personality and financial independence.
Debates of the European Parliament
The Commission has been informed that representatives of the UNPD, the Netherlands and Sweden are to have a meeting with the Nicaraguan Minister of Foreign Affairs.
During this meeting the representatives will transmit their concerns about the possible loss of momentum of the INIM as an independent institution that has, until now, promoted women's rights in Nicaragua and they will also underline their attachment to the Beijing principles in the framework of their cooperation.
The Commission fully supports this political initiative.
Question No 40 by Bernd Posselt (H-0353/97) Subject: Negotiations with Egypt
How have relations between the EU.and Egypt in particular the negotiations on the association agreement, been developing in the light of the Meditenañean conference and how does the Commission assess the political situation in this important partner country?
Answer
A new Association Agreement requires major economic legislative and administrative adjustments in the partner countries.
In the case of Egypt the scale of economic reforms to be undertaken and the issue of agriculture, which presents problems different from those encountered with the Maghreb countries and with Israel, also explain the duration of the negotiations.
As concerns the specific chapter of agriculture, Egypt in the last months has revised to a great extent its original requests for concessions in agricultural trade.
However, because of the high level of the original demands, the revised figures remain still distant from what the Community could grant taking into account the present market difficulties for the main products concerned (potatoes, rice, oranges, cut flowers).
Differences also exist in other chapters such as re-admission of illegal immigrants and social benefits for workers.
However, they are not unbridgeable gaps.
Both sides agree on the strategic, long-term importance of the agreement and are committed to reach conclusions as soon as possible.
The Commission will spare no effort to achieve a balanced agreement in the interest of both parties, hopefully within the first semester of this year.
Question No 41 by Marie-Paule Kestelijn-Sierens (H-0362/97) Subject: Demarcation of indigenous area Raposa/Sena do Sol in Brazil
1775) of non-Indian people occupying land and the government of Roraima to the FUNAI's original proposals for the demarcation of the indigenous area of Raposa/Tena do Sol, in late 1996 the Brazilian government submitted a new proposal for demarcation which would reduce the indigenous area by one fifth (or 300 000 hectares).
Is the Commission aware of this controversial proposal on the part of the Brazilian government?
What action will the Commission take to make known its displeasure at this proposal?
Is it not possible for the Commission to demand withdrawal of this unacceptable proposal as a condition for financial aid for the demarcation of indigenous areas in the framework of the model projects in Brazil?
No 4-500/178
Normandia) which were sunounded in the initial project and to respect the rights of the indigenous population for whom a per capita area of over one thousand hectares is reserved.
The Commission is following this delicate question closely.
It fervently hopes that the indigenous people will have the benefit, as we are beginning to see, of dignified living conditions on properly demarcated territory.
It supports the efforts launched but cannot interfere directly in the demarcation operations to discuss the rights and wrongs of various measures.
Moreover, the Commission is assisting the indigenous peoples by participating in various projects and programmes and by ensuring that favourable conditions are created for utilizing the land with a view to sustainable and durable development.
Question No 49 by Jan Andersson. (H-0318/97)
Subject: Competition in the European shipbuilding industry
Europe's shipbuilding industry has to compete for markets against international competition.
In a number of Member States, the shipbuilding industry receives government aid, whilst in other it does not, and this situation distorts competition.
What steps will the Commission take to prevent aid to shipbuilding from distorting competition?
Answer
State aid to shipbuilding is subject to special rules (cunently the 7th Directive) which ensure equity and transparency throughout the Community.
The validity of the Directive ends on 31 December 1997.
An international agreement commonly known as the OECD agreement, has been signed by the Community, the USA, Japan, Korea and Norway, respecting normal competitive conditions in the commercial shipbuilding and ship repair industry.
All parties to the Agreement have ratified it with the exception of the USA.
Without their ratification the Agreement caimot enter into force.
For the future, the preference of the Community would be the entry into force of the Agreement.
However, failing this and while the Community continues to await the eventual ratification of the Agreement by the USA, in its meeting on 24 April 1997, the Council has acknowledged the need for a 'specific new regime' for the shipbuilding sector together with the extension of the Seventh Directive until 31 December 1998 and requested the Commission to present a proposal in this sense by this summer.
An amendment to the cunent rales which will provide a legal basis for aid to be allowed for the restructuring of yards in Germany, Greece and Spain has also received the political approval from the Council on 24 April.
These restructuring programmes are the same as had already been initiated and approved earlier by the Commission and the Council but unforeseeable circumstances made it necessary to increase the amounts of public funding.
However, extra reductions in capacity are offered and a tight monitoring procedure will be put into place.
There is also by the respective governments a commitment that this is the last time these yards will receive restructuring aid.
Question No 50 by Hubert Pirker (H-0363/97)
In many regions of Europe small and medium-sized enterprises are of crucial importance for economic development and employment.
In order to make them more competitive with large enterprises and in view of the negligible effect of their behaviour on the Common Market, they should be largely exempt from EC competition law.
What action is the Commission intending to take to extend the existing exemptions for small and medium-sized enterprises?
Answer
For small and medium-sized enterprises a competitive economic environment is very important.
It is clearly in their interest for competition not to be restricted or distorted by private action such as the creation of market dominance through mergers or by governmental measures such as state aid to big companies.
In this respect, the Commission as the European competition authority is a 'natural ally' of small and medium-sized enterprises.
In maintaining effective competition the Commission indirectly protects them.
Community competition rales rarely affect small and medium-sized enterprises directly.
For instance, they normally fall short of Community merger control due to the relatively high turnover thresholds.
This will not change after the amendment as principally agreed upon in the Council on 34 April.
With respect to Articles 85 and 86 the Commission has published a draft de minimis notice.
De minimis defines the thresholds up to which the Commission does not assume an appreciable restriction of competition.
This is of particular interest for small and medium-sized enterprises.
At the moment, in order to benefit, parties must have both a combined market share of less than 5 % and a turnover of less than ECU 300 million.
The new draft applies a higher market share for vertical agreements (10 %) and has dropped the turnover threshold altogether.
The draft includes a specific reference to small and medium-sized enterprises (')· It says that cooperation between them would rarely appreciably affect trade between Member States.
With regard to State aid, the Commission's general policy towards aid to promote SMEs has always been positive.
Although State aid for SMEs is entirely subject to the State aid rules of Article 92-93, the Commission's appreciation of the compatibility of aid with the Common Market will take into account the characteristics of SMEs and the specific problems they face.
The Commission therefore published guidelines on State aid for SMEs (OJ 1996 C 213) spelling out the concrete criteria that aid to SMEs must fulfil in order to be allowed under the exemption of Article 92(3)c.
Generally speaking, it is required that the aid has an incentive character, is proportionate to the handicaps SMEs are confronted with and is necessary to achieve objectives in the Community interest which market forces alone would not secure.
The guidelines translate these conditions into concrete admissible aid purposes and aid intensities.
Besides, several frameworks and guidelines regarding other types of aid (e.g. research and development aid, employment aid, environmental aid) contain more favourable rules for the assessment of compatibility if the aid is given to SMEs.
Finally, the Commission's de minimis rale for State aid (OJ 1996 C 68) is of particular interest to SMEs.
According to this rule, aid not exceeding ECU 100 000 over a three-year period is deemed not to have a perceptible impact on trade and competition between Member States and is therefore not subject to prior notification to the Commission.
Question No 51 by Astrid Thors (H-0364/97) Subject: Deregulation of the telecommunications sector
The Commission has drawn up a number of provisions concerning the application of the general rules of competition in the telecommunications sector, including timetables for the measures which should be taken in order that free competition may be implemented, as already decided, on 1 January 1998.
A number of Member States have reportedly requested extensions to the deadlines for some of the measures that are required to deregulate the sector, and implementation of the relevant legislation is proceeding slowly in those countries.
What steps will the Commission take to ensure that Member States comply with the rules and keep to the agreed timetable?
Debates of the European Parliament
Ireland, Portugal, Luxembourg, Spain and Greece have requested such additional implementation periods to Ireland (27 November 1996) and Portugal (12 November 1996).
The Commission will adopt formal decisions on the other requests during Spring 1997.
The Commission will monitor that the timetable is respected by these Member States.
In cases of nonimplementation the Commission will initiate infringement proceedings as provided by Article 169 of the EC Treaty.
The Commission will also monitor closely the implementation of all other aspects of the liberalization and harmonization package adopted by the Community in the field of telecommunications and initiate proceedings in the case of failure to implement.
Since the Commission Directive as implemented by the relevant Decisions have direct effect, individuals have the right to bring action against Member States in national courts for breach of Community law if any of these Member States would fail to conectly transpose the Directive.
Moreover, according to the Case law of the Court of Justice, Member States would be liable for any damages caused to individuals by breaches of Community law attributable to the State (·).
Question No 52 by Karin Riis-Jørgensen (H-0371/97) Subject: Future shipbuilding policy
In view of its draft paper of April 1997, how does the Commission see the future of the shipbuilding industry?
When will a stop be put to state aid to shipyards?
How does the aid which has just been approved (COM(97)132) square with the recent report on state aid?
When does the Commission expect to be able to create equal market conditions for the European shipbuilding industry?
When does it anticipate implementing in full the new non-repeat aid policy, and when does it expect finally to put a stop to exceptional aid packages?
How can it ensure that the monitoring of mix-shipyards is sufficient to prevent public money being transfened, which is already causing problems for the EU?
Answer
Entry into force of the OECD agreement would be the best way of eliminating subsidies and other distortions to.competition in the shipbuilding sector.
That is why the Commission continues to hope that the US will ratify the agreement.
Nevertheless the Community clearly needs to reflect on what approach should be adopted in the event that the OECD agreement does not come into effect.
This was the purpose of the Commission working paper to which the Honourable Member refers.
At its meeting on 24 April the Industry Council decided that if the OECD agreement has still not yet entered into force the cunent Community shipbuilding aid directive (which is due to expire at the end of this year) should be extended if necessary until the end of 1998 pending decisions on a specific new regime, on which the Commission will put forward proposals later this year.
The extended directive would lapse automatically as soon as the OECD Agreement is ratified by the USA or a new regime on shipbuilding aid policy is adopted.
The Commission would expect any such new regime to take into account many of the concerns expressed by the Honourable Member, although the Commission needs to be balanced and realistic at the same time if the overall goal of securing a competitive industry is to be achieved.
For example the complex issue of mixed yards needs to be addressed, but it may be difficult to find solutions.
The Commission would also seek to enshrine the 'one time last time' principle for restructuring aid to shipyards, in line with the approach followed in the specific recent cases covered in document COM (97) 132, to which the Honourable Member also refers, where the governments concerned have undertaken that no more such aids will be granted to the yards involved.
') Cases C-6/90 and C-9/90 Francovich and Others (1991) ECR 1-5357 and Cases C-46(93 and C-48/93 Brasserie du Pécheur SA and Others (1996) ÉCR I.
Debates of the European Parliament
Spain to saturation point, where they are unable to allow access to other companies, despite the liberalization of the air transport market.
Recently, these two companies, together with Iberia (for its promotional flights), simultaneously increased their fares by 20 %.
No action was taken in response to this by the Spanish Government, which should have been aware of the development given that Iberia is a public company.
This would suggest that the three companies have reached a price-fixing agreement, in breach of the rules of free competition and of Article 85(1) of the Treaty.
What measures will the Commission take in response to this alleged agreement?
Question No 54 by Pedro Aparicio Sánchez (H-0377/97) Subject: Agreement between Iberia, Spanair and Air Europa in breach of Article 85 of the Treaty
The- airlines Spanair, Ah Europa and Iberia (for its promotional flights) have simultaneously increased their fares by between 15 and 20 %, in what appears to be an agreement to restrict competition in breach of Article 85(l)(a) of the Treaty (directly or indirectly fixing purchase or selling prices) and Article 86 (abuse of a dominant position).
Will the Commission investigate these cases of suspected infringement of the principles of free competition, in accordance with Article 89 of the Treaty?
Joint Answer
With a view to preventing duplicated monitoring procedures and within the framework of the necessary cooperation between national competition authorities and the Commission, the Commission considers it desirable that national competition authorities enforce Articles 85 and 86 of the EC Treaty, potentially in combination with their respective national competition rales, in those cases where certain conditions are fulfilled.
In principle, a national competition authority should be responsible for handling those cases where the alleged restrictive practice or agreement bears its effects primarily in the territory of the relevant Member State and where, at first sight, the alleged restrictive practice or agreement does not appear to be entitled to an exemption pursuant to Article 85(3) of the EC Treaty.
The Commission reserves, however, the right to intervene in these cases if it judges it necessary.
In the case referred to in the Honourable Member's question, the Spanish competition authority, upon complaint from two regional governments and several consumers' associations, has already initiated a formal investigation procedure with respect to the alleged agreement between Spanair, Air Europa and Iberia to increase their respective tariffs on national flights at the same time.
The characteristics of this case show that the parties to the alleged infringement are established in a sole Member State, Spain; that the effects of the alleged illegal practice are essentially produced within the tenitory of this Member State, because the tariffs increase basically concerns national routes; and that the alleged infringement, a price fixing, does not appear to be exemptible under Article 85(3)EC.
This case, therefore, should in principle be dealt with by the Spanish competition authorities.
The Commission intends to publish, in the near future, a Communication where it will clearly define the principles and procedures to be applied in the cooperation between the Commission and the competition authorities of the Member States.
A draft of this Communication was published on 10.9.1996 (OJ C 262) in order to organize a consultation with the Member States and the parties concerned which has been very fruitful.
It is hoped that the Communication itself will be adopted by the Commission and published in the Official Journal before the summer break.
Question No 55 by Wayne David (H-0291/97) Subject: Reduced Earnings Allowance
In March 1966, the British Government introduced the Social Security (Inudstrial Injuries and Diseases) (Miscellaneous Amendment) Regulations.
These regulations have harsh consequences for those people in receipt of Reduced Earnings Allowance ('REA').
The regulations may have a discriminatory effect between men and women and be in breach of the provisions of the EC Treaty on equal pay for work of equal value.
As the guardian of the Treaties, what steps is the Commission taking to ensure that those provisions apply to the regulations?
Answer
On the basis of thè information provided by the Honourable Member of the European Parliament, the Commission cannot determine whether there is any infringement of the existing Community law in the area of equal treatment.
The Commission will make inquiries to the 'British Government in order to obtain further information on the new legislation in force.
Question Ño 56 by Irini Lambraki (H-0292/97)
Following the recent revelations in the Greek press of suspected connections between intermediary companies and high-ranking officials of Directorates-General V and XX in connection with the controversial issue of funding for vocational training centres, from the European Social Fund, will the Commission say what measures it will take to set up mechanisms for transparency within Directorates-General V and XX in order to prevent any illicit dealings and ensure effective use of Community resources?
Answer
The Commission made it clear, through its office in Athens on 19/03/97, that it rejected the allegations contained in the articles refened to by the Honourable Member and reserved the right to take the appropriate legal action in respect of unfounded allegations against its officials.
In this context the Commission is examining thoroughly all elements relevant to this case.
With regard to.the transparency and effectiveness of ESF financed actions, the European Commission in the framework of partnership, operates in close collaboration with the responsible Greek authorities, in order to ensure sound management monitoring, evaluation and control of all actions financed through the Community Support Framework 1994-99 for Greece.
Subject: Cohesion Fund
What is the Commission's view of the continuity of the Cohesion Fund post-1999?
.Answer
Article 130D of the EC Treaty provides the legal basis for establishing a Cohesion Fund.
The Commission is at present preparing its proposals for the future anangements concerning the Cohesion Fund and the Structural Funds and will, as already announced, make these proposals known after the conclusion of the Inter-Governmental Conference.
Question No 58 by Patrick Cox (H-0295/97)
Subject Council Directive 93/104 (working time directive) and its application to the defence forces
Is Council Directive 93/104/EC (l) concerning certain aspects of the organization of working time intended to include members of the defence forces?
Does the Commission consider that Section 3 of the Organization of Working Time Bill (Ireland), which is cunently being considered by the Irish Parliament and which precludes members of the defence forces, is compatible with Council Directive 93/104/EC?
Answer
The scope of Directive 93/104/EC is defined by Article 2 of Directive 89/931/EEC on the introduction of measures to encourage improvements in the safety and health of workers.
Article 2(2) of Directive 89/931 states that
'This Directive shall not be applicable where characteristics peculiar to certain specific public service activities, such as the armed forces or the police, or to certain specific activities in the civil protection services inevitably conflict with it.'
Each Member State is clearly in a better position than the Commission to assess, in the light of the anangements in that Member State, the circumstances and extent to which activities of the armed forces are likely to conflict with the provisions of the Working Tune Directive.
Question No 60 by Alfred Lomas (H-0300/97) Subject: Gibraltar - passports
Will the Commission confirm that the passports issued in Gibraltar to British Dependent Territory Citizens and British Citizens who are EU nationals, and which carry the name Gibraltar on the cover, comply with the Intergovernmental Resolution on the common format of passports, passed in 1981?
Answer
As the Honourable Member points out, in 1981 the representatives of the governments of the Member States of the European Communities meeting in the Council adopted a resolution on the common format for passports.
That resolution was supported by further resolutions in •1982, 1986 and 1995, to take account in particular of enlargements of the Community.
Those resolutions provide for a model European passport with common features such as the format colour and some of the information contained therein.
Other aspects, however, such as the period of validity of the passport its security specifications or even its cost, are not covered by the resolutions.
Given that the resolutions in question were adopted by the representatives of the governments of Member States meeting in the Council, the legal framework in which they were created does not fall within the European Treaties.
For that reason the Commission is not empowered to interpret the resolutions, nor to give a ruling on the conformity of a specific passport with the provisions laid down therein.
The Member States are also required to admit Union citizens to their territory and to recognize their right to leave it 'on simple presentation of an identity card or a valid passport'.
The Member States must therefore ensure that Union citizens can obtain an identity document within the meaning of Community law in order to exercise their right to freedom of movement.
If necessary, will it compensate local people who
Debates of the European Parliament
14.5.97 benefit financially from killing these animals?
The Commission should encourage European zoos in their efforts to coordinate the preservation of zoo tigers and to develop methods through which tigers may be reintroduced into the wild.
Is public interest in the campaign, perhaps through commercial sponsorship by a company with a widely recognized product, going to be promoted?
Guidelines for the protection of other endangered, but less prominent, species must be formulated by the Commission as well.
Answer
Yes, The Commission shares the concerns about the threats to the survival of tiger populations in the wild and - together with Member States - has actively participated in discussions and the formulation of recommendations on the issue in the context of the Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species of wild fauna and flora (CITES).
Tiger conservation will again be a major issue at the 10th meeting of the Conference of the Parties to CITES, to be held in Harare, Zimbabwe, from 9 to 20 June 1997.
The Commission is of the opinion that in situ conservation and enforcement of protective legislation in both range states and consumer countries is essential in order to eradicate poaching and illegal international trade.
Breeding of tigers in zoos, however, would appear to be a problem rather than a solution; in fact zoos are taking measures to reduce breeding.
The reintroduction of captive bred tigers implies serious genetical risks and would only be useful if in situ protection is guaranteed.
The issue of trade in wild fauna and flora has been dealt with in Community legislation since 1984 (Council Regulation (EEC) No 3626/82; OJ L 384 of 31.1.1982).
On 1 June 1997, this legislation will be replaced by Council Regulation (EC) No 338/97; OJ L 61 of 03.03.1997.
Question No 62 by Per Stenmarck (H-0314/97) Subject: Competition in ah transport The most recent step towards increased competition in civil aviation was taken on 1 April this year.
Is the Commission prepared to go still further in facilitating competition by taking action to bring about a redistribution of slots?
Answer
While the Commission's assessment of aviation liberalisation in Europe is positive with regard to licensing of ah carriers, market access and fares, the Commission does recognise that capacity limitations are having an impact at the major airports with capacity problems.
The increasing difficulty in obtaining suitable slots to expand ah services is creating barriers to market entry.
This restricts competition and protects incumbent carriers.
This distortion of the market prevents the full benefits of liberalisation of ah transport being achieved.
It is therefore necessary to improve the allocation mechanism to facilitate the possibility for slots to change hands in order to improve the efficiency of the ah services market and to avoid a static and inflexible situation developing at congested airports which prevents airlines entering the market or increasing frequencies to meet consumer demand.
The Commission is considering different options concerning the necessary revision of the existing slot allocation Regulation.
The Commission has studied the situation in detail and consulted all the interested parties in depth.
It intends to submit a proposal soon to amend the existing Regulation.
Debates of the European Parliament
The Speaker instead replied that it was not only the right but also the duty of Members of the Swedish Parliament to form opinion on various matters of public concern.
Does Linda Steneberg's attempt to muzzle Members of the Swedish Parliament reflect the Commission's?
Does Linda Steneberg have the Commission's foil confidence?
Answer
Following the publicity in Sweden on the statutes and salaries of Community officials, some Swedish Members of Parliament made statements.
A bomb threat was also received in the Commission Office in Stockholm.
Under such serious circumstances, the Head of the Commission's Representation in Sweden considered that it was her duty to react in order to protect the staff and folly clarify this issue.
She therefore wrote to the Speaker of the Swedish Parliament on her own initiative to point out that working conditions, salaries and taxes of Community officials are fixed by Community law.
This regime had been accepted by Sweden when entering the Community.
She also expressed the insult felt by her and the staff when being called a threat against democracy.
When it became clear that the fact of writing this letter led to misinterpretations, the Head of Representation publicly regretted this and felt sorry that her intentions had been misread.
The Commission has foil confidence in the way its Head of Representation in Sweden canied out her duties.
Question No 64 by Nikitas Kaklamanis (H-0317/97)
According to constant reports, some of which refer to official documents drawn up by the Council and the Commission's Directorate-General I, textile products of Asian origin (China, Pakistan etc.) are being imported into Turkish free zones and, although subject to transit regulations, are subsequently exported to the EU as products of Turkish origin.
This is clearly an infringement of the Customs Union agreement between Turkey and the EU, creating a major problem of unfair competition with products from many EU Member States.
What view does the Commission take of this?
What are the latest relevant facts at its disposal and what measures will it take if the above information proves to be true?
Answer
The Commission is aware of complaints over the alleged inadequacy of legislation, or implementation of this legislation, on imports of textiles and clothing products from Turkish free zones, which would make it possible to smuggle out of them duty and quota-free goods of third country origin, which would then be declared of Turkish origin.
However, the Commission is satisfied that legislation on the Turkish free zones is adequate to cover the operations which are at present performed there (essentially customs warehousing and not processing) and is implemented conectly.
This is confirmed by the findings of a mission composed of Commission and Member State representatives which took place from 21 to 25 October 1996 and included visits'to several free zones as well as meetings with the customs department headquarters in Ankara in order to improve the understanding of the way free zones function with respect to Turkey's commercial policy and customs union obligations.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 65 by Birgitta Ahlqvist (H-0319/97)
Subject: Development aid to Belarus
Reports last year about the political situation in Belarus were very alarming, with the President assuming increasingly authoritarian powers and the freedom of speech, of the press and of association gradually being eroded.
It is now vital to support the development of democratic parties, grassroots movements and free trade unions in Belarus.
The President, however, is doing his utmost to stifle democratic debate in the country.
Has the Commission, in the tight of the suppression of the political opposition, made adjustments to the development aid granted to Belarus?
How can the EU use such aid to counter the rise of an authoritarian state in Belarus?
Answer
The European Union is gravely concerned about the political situation in Belarus, which seriously impairs the prospects for the necessary democratic and economic reform.
For this reason, the European Union has adopted a dual approach towards Belarus.
This position adopted by the Council on February 24, 1997 is, on the one hand, no conclusion on the Interim Agreement no ratification of the Partnership and Cooperation Agreement, no Joint Committee meeting, no support for Council of Europe membership, no public récognition of the new Constitution and Parliament; but, on the other hand, continue bilateral dialogue and provide assistance with experts in constitutional matters, freedom of media, human rights and economic reform.
In the EU statement on Belarus adopted by the Council on April 28, 1997, the EU is open to establish a framework of dialogue with Belarus in cooperation with the OSCE and the Council of Europe to regularly review progress in democratic reform.
For the further bilateral dialogue in Minsk; the Commission is ready to contribute to the third Kosto mission, the content of which was proposed in the EU statement on Belarus: i) assisting both the Belarussian authorities and all the deputies of the elected 13th Supreme Soviet in setting up a formal consultation mechanism to discuss proposals for amending the 1994 Constitution, ii) monitoring and facilitating the consultations in coordination with the OSCE and the Council of Europe, iii) reporting to the GAC on the progress made in those consultations.
The Commission would be ready to use Tacis as the main instrument to provide the necessary expertise in the main areas identified by the Council's conclusions.
Question No 66 by John McCartin (H-0320/97)
Is the Commission aware of the activities of factory ships fishing for mackerel in the Atlantic waters off heland and the UK and has it investigated allegations that in their efforts to obtain uniformed sized mackerel, these ships are discarding as much as three times their landings?
Answer
The main fleets of Community fishing vessels catching mackerel in Western waters off the coasts of the United Kingdom and heland are purse seiners and freezer trawlers.
In accordance with Article 10(16) of Council Regulation (EEC) No. 3094/86, freezer trawlers may use automatic sorting equipment onboard if its sole function is commercial grading of all the fish caught and intended for freezing.
Debates of the European Parliament
Nevertheless, the Commission continues to be attentive to the problem of potential misuse of automatic sorting equipment for discarding onboard such vessels and will carry out additional missions on this subject.
Question No 67 by Ivar Virgin (H-0324/97)
The Swedish media has reported that animals are still being transported from the EU to third countries in conditions which in no way meet the requirements for them to be treated decently.
Cunent rules are such that they promote the transport of live animals, regardless of the suffering caused to them.
What steps will the Commission take to guarantee that animals are transported from the EU to third countries in acceptable conditions which meet high animal welfare standards?
Answer
The Commission has become increasingly concerned about the welfare situation of animals traded between the Community and third countries and vice versa following complaints received from animal welfare interests.
Although Directive 91/628/EEC contains provisions to protect transported animals both within Community territory an also where part of the journey involves the tenitory of third countries, it cannot be denied that problems of inadequate enforcement by the responsible authorities and inadequate respect of the rules of commercial operators have occuned.
Particular difficulties are involved in trade with third countries as, of course, Community directives do not directly apply as law within those countries.
Directive 91/628/EEC, as amended by Directive 95/29/EC however, contains provisions designed to ensure the respect by transport operators transporting animals from or to Community territory of adequate welfare conditions for the animals.
For example, the authorities of the exporting Member State must ensure that no animal shall be transported unless it is fit for the particular journey involved and suitable provisions have been made for its care during the journey and in case of exports on arrival at the place of destination.
Under these provisions the authorities of a Member State should normally be able to ensure, that the operator carrying out the export or import will be obliged to respect the provisions of the directive throughout the journey and carry out the transport in lorries and vessels which are adequate for the purpose.
In view of the recent complaints which have been made, the Commission intends to intervene further with the authorities of the Member States concerned (Germany and Italy).
If a Member State consistently fails to adequately enforce the respect of a directive the Commission may of course open the infringements proceedings provided for in Article 169 of the Treaty.
The Commission will consider the opening of this procedure in the cases refened to in the present question if on the examination of those cases it appears that the incidents have occuned owing to inadequate enforcement of the directive by the authorities of the Member States involved.
As far as the Commission's controls are concerned, both within and outside the Community, the Commission's product quality control and audit office has been integrated into the Directorate General for Consumer Policy and Health Protection and will be transfened from Brussels to Dublin in the course of this year.
Animal welfare will be included in this new structure.
However, the Commission has to point out the limits of the Community competence to impose Community legislation and the practical control of it outside the Community.
In order to increase the degree of protection for animals transported to third countries, the Council received recently a proposal for a Council regulation concerning the connection between granting of export refunds for live animals and the compliance with provisions established in Community legislation.
At a technical level, discussions have already started with experts from Member States to discuss the implementation modalities of such a proposal.
Question No 68 by José Torres Couto (H-0328/97) Subject: Transport and infrastructure
The Portuguese Government has decided to modify the positions of the previous government with regard to transport accessibility and infrastructure, and in the context of links with the rest of Europe it is now opting for intermodal corridors.
What is the Commission's assessment of this change of position?
Answer
The list of 14 TEN priority projects was endorsed, as the honourable Member will recall, by the European Council in Essen in December 1994 and project No. 8 on that list related to a 'Lisbon-Valladolid Motorway'.
The Portuguese Government has since altered its priorities in transport policy and is now seeking to put more emphasis on transport modes such as railways, seaports and airports.
In agreement with the Spanish Government, it consequently proposed to the European Council that the priority project be changed.
The European Council at Dublin concluded that 'It agrees with the proposal by the. Portuguese and Spanish Governments that priority project No. 8 in List 1 from the Essen European Council will become the Multimodal link Portugal/Spain with the rest of Europe'.
The Commission considers that the planned change to a 'multimodal' project is in line with the objectives of the common transport policy of the Community.
The project has also been examined by the Commission in the light of the specific criteria given in the Decision on the TEN Guidelines.
The revised project includes several railway, maritime and ah schemes and, can be considered as similar to the Nordic Triangle Project (Project No 12 on the list) which is also, by definition, multimodal.
The Commission is of the view that the components of the project as a whole comply with the objectives and criteria set out in the Guidelines.
In particular the various elements are included in the maps of Annex I and conespond to the criteria and specifications in Annex II.
As the European Council's decision implies that the description of project No. 8 in Annex III of the Guidelines should be modified, the Commission will therefore include this alteration amongst a number of amendments to the Guidelines which it intends to propose in the course of this year.
Any modification to Annex III and Article 19 of the Decision would of course be made under the codecision procedure and in line with the understanding reached in the context of the conciliation procedure on the indicative and political character of Annex III.
Question No 69 by Elly Plooij-van Gorsel (H-0329/97) Subject: Health risk to coeliacs through the use of wheat gluten in surface coatings for cheese
Is the Commission aware that the Netherlands dairy industry intends to replace plastic coatings for cheese by a wheat gluten coating which is environmentally friendlier and cheaper?
Is the Commission aware that the various national coeliacs societies, which have joined forces in the Association of European Coeliacs Societies, are very alarmed at this proposal, since there is a risk that gluten from the coating will get into the cheese, for example when it is cut further reducing the range of foods which can be eaten by coeliacs (people allergic to gluten)?
Is the novel foods regulation, and, in particular, the provisions on the labelling of food products, applicable in this specific case?
If not, what scope does the Commission see for measures to protect the health of coeliacs?
This question was discussed with the Member States during the subsequent meeting of the Standing Committee on Foodstuffs held on 15 April.
There is also some suggestion that this problem arises also in respect of other foodstuffs, such as coating apples with starch containing gluten.
Within the framework of the general debate cunently under way on the labelling of ingredients which might cause allergies or intolerance, some consideration should be given to the need to provide special labelling to inform consumers of the fact that a particular food has been in contact with products containing gluten.
Question No 70 by Christine Oddy (H-0337/97) Subject: Working Time directive and employment of lecturers
Is the Commission aware that many part-time lecturers employed by further education colleges in the UK are having their employment passed onto a separate body, the ELS (Education Lecturing Services), so that they are nò longer regarded as employed by the colleges, in order to circumvent their employment rights?
What moves will the Commission take to ensure that lecturers' rights to annual paid leave under the Working Time directive will not be circumvented by this action?
Answer
The Commission shares the Honourable Member's concerns regarding the situation of foreignlanguage lecturers in the United Kingdom.
However, it would like to point out that, in accordance with the case-law of the Court of Justice concerning the European rales on the safeguarding of employees' rights in the event of transfers of undertakings, businesses or parts of businesses (·), the rights and obligations arising out of a contract of employment or an employment relationship may, in the context of a transfer, be changed in respect of the transferee to the same extent as they could have been in respect of the transferor, on the understanding that the transfer of the business may on no account itself constitute the reason for the change.
The same principles apply to the right to annual holidays, including the rights laid down in the directive on the organization of working time (2).
The Commission would like to stress that once the British authorities have transposed Directive 77/187/EEC into national law, it is incumbent on the competent national institutions to check that the national measures implementing that directive are met.
In addition, the Commission for its part ensures that the Member States conectly transpose the measures contained in secondary legislation.
Only in cases where evidence emerged of delayed transposition, as with the directive on the organization of working time in the United Kingdom, or the inconect application of the national transposition measures, might the Commission decide, if appropriate, to initiate infringement proceedings pursuant to Article 169 of the EEC Treaty.
Question No 71 by James Nicholson (H-0342/97)
Subject: Special Support Programme for Peace and Reconciliation in Northern heland and the border countries of heland 1995-1999
What action is proposed to counter the reduction in available funds to District Partnerships occasioned by the prevailing exchange rate between the ECU and £ Sterling and can additional funds be made available to restore the original value of the fond to those partnerships?
The amount of assistance to be provided by the Structural Funds to an Operational Programme is fixed in ECUs in the Commission Decision which approves the Programme.
») Council Directive 77/187/EEC of 14 February 1977, OJ L 61 of 5 March 1977.
2) Council Directive 93/104/EEC of 23 November 1993, OJ L 307 of 13 December 1993, page 18.
Debates of the European Parliament
It is not therefore proposed to increase the Structural Funds' allocation to the Special Support Programme for Peace and Reconciliation in Northern heland and the Border Counties of Ireland (1994-1999), under which District Partnerships are funded, in order to compensate for exchange rate movements.
Question No 72 by Edgar Schiedermeier (H-0343/97)
What conclusion were drawn at the European Cohesion Forum organized by the Commission and held in Brussels from 28 to 30 April 1997?
Did the Commission present a proposal for the forthcoming reform of the Structural Funds at this conference?
Answer
The Cohesion Forum was organised by the Commission as an opportunity for all concerned by Community cohesion policies - national, regional and local authorities, social partners, representative organisations, research institutes and experts - to give their assessment of achievements up to the present and contribute ideas for the future.
While, in view of its nature, there were no formal conclusions or proposals arising from the Forum, the member of the Commission in charge of regional policies in the closing session, outlined the main messages which had emerged.
The Commissioner said that the Forum had recognised the key role of cohesion policy as a basic component of the European model of society.
Much progress had been achieved in the policies introduced since 1989, but more needed to be done to help the weakest regions, as well as other regions undergoing restructuring, and to improve the quality of Europe's human resources and reduce social exclusion.
There was room for improvement in Community cohesion policies, more concentration of resources, with transitional help for areas no longer eligible, improved integration of town and country in order to promote the balanced development of rural regions and a greater focus on the particular problem of urban areas.
The member of the Commission also pointed to the many possibilities for the simplification of the delivery system for Community cohesion policies in order to improve their effectiveness: fewer objectives and fewer Community Initiatives, fewer programmes, quicker procedures, more incentives to promote the efficient use of Community resources, tighter financial controls, wider participation in the partnerships.
The Commission will present its formal proposal for the future financing of Community policies soon after the conclusion of the Inter-Governmental Conference which is expected to take place in June 1997.
This will include guidelines for the future of structural policies taking into account the views expressed at the Cohesion Forum.
Following agreement on future financing, the Commission will propose new regulations for the Structural Funds probably in the first half of 1998.
Question No 73 by Ulla Sandbæk (H-0344/97) Subject: EU support to Community-based natural resource management in Africa
Meeting in Brussels on 17-20 March 1997, the ACP-EU Joint Parliamentary Assembly passed two important resolutions on Southern Africa supporting the region's approach to biodiversity conservation and the rural economic development of its poorest areas.
One of the resolutions acknowledges the success that community-based natural management programmes are having in the region and the ability of such community conservation processes to provide real and tangible benefits to communities through the sustainable use of wild species, while the other requests the European Union to explicitly support African community-based natural resource management programmes aiming at conserving natural resources through rural communities' empowerment and sustainable use.
As requested by the ACP-EU Assembly, will the European Commission effectively support community-based natural resource management programmes such as Zimbabwe's CAMPHRE, both financially and institutionally?
Answer
The European Commission fully supports the general principles of Community-based wildlife management in Africa.
Therefore, both resolutions passed during March 17-29 1997 by the ACP-EU Joint Parliamentary Assembly are coherent with the general orientations.
Moreover, the recently adopted Council Regulation on 'Environmental Measures in developing countries in the context of sustainable development' and the Council Regulation on 'Actions to promote tropical forests in developing countries' both indicate the preservation of biological diversity as an area for intervention, and further underline that particular attention will be given to projects from local populations.
The European Commission is cunently funding an integrated programme on Community-based wildlife management in Kenya, Uganda and Tanzania.
There is also an ongoing field research in Africa on the socioeconomic impacts of Community-based wildlife management.
A recent EU MS' Experts' Meeting on protected areas will consider further initiatives in this area.
The European Commission could consider supporting Community-based wildlife management programmes by any eligible organisation as per above-mentioned Council Regulations.
Question No 74 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0350/97) Subject: Protection of the archaeological site at Vouthroto in Albania
The distressing events following the crisis in Albania have resulted in the looting of the archaeological site at Vouthroto, which UNESCO designated in 1992 as part of the world cultural heritage.
Many of the priceless objects found, which witnesses say are now in the hands of persons unknown, date from the early Bronze Age and include typical pieces from every period of European history - a wealth of sculptures, vases, statuettes, engravings etc.
The archive material unearthed by the Greek archaeological expedition carried out under the intergovernmental protocol on scientific and technological cooperation between Greece and Albania is also exposed to similar risks.
Answer
The Commission has heard about the looting of the archaeological site at Vouthroto, which UNESCO designated as part of the world cultural heritage. This has happened during the cunent crisis in Albania.
It is important to bear in mind that the region in which the site is situated is still outside the control of the central government.
The multinational defence force in Albania is rather concentrated on the urban areas and the main fines of communication and can scarcely therefore protect the site.
Nor has it any mandate to act as a police force (only to provide assistance for humanitarian aid and defence of the international consultative mission).
There is therefore little possibility of taking action in the immediate future.
However, as part of its contacts with the new reconciliation government, the Commission intends to question the government on this matter and on the measures to be taken.
One other possible course of action would be to swiftly reinstate the customs services and thus set up checks on any crossborder movements of the goods in question in this specific case.
Since customs duties represent an important resource for Albania, action taken in this field - apart from humanitarian aid already given - are in any case one of the priority activities.
Following an initial general evaluation mission in Albania last week with a view to getting the PHARE programme restarted, the Commission will draw up a plan of action for the customs in cooperation with the Albanian authorities.
Moreover, as soon as conditions allow, the Commission is intending also to re-launch activities within the specific 'cross-border' (Greece/Albania/Italy) PHARE programme.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 75 by Eva Kjer Hansen (H-0351/97)
The Danish Association of persons letting summer cottages has for a number of years been seeking to have the authorized letting period extended and spread out.
Cunent Danish rules on letting are being interpreted by the Danish Ministry of the Environment as no more than 26 plus 4 weeks.
The Ministry's argument has been that extending and spreading the letting period would create doubt over the Danish protocol in the Treaty and that lengthening the period would make the letting out of second homes a commercial activity.
To clarify the matter, in March 1997 the Commission replied to questions from the Association.
Can the Commission confirm that the matter of any lengthening of letting periods for second homes is an internal Danish one, which does not include the acquisition of second homes, and that a change of this kind would have no effect on the Danish protocol in the Treaty on the acquisition of second homes in Denmark?
Will the Commission give its views on whether any extension of the letting period would mean that second homes acquired commercial status, and whether this would have any effect on the Danish protocol in the Treaty?
Answer
The purchase of real estate in another Member State is governed by Treaty provisions on the free movement of capital.
Apart from transitional periods for new Member States and the provisions in specific protocols on the Aland Islands and Denmark, this right is guaranteed throughout the Community.
As with the other freedoms, it is conditioned by the right of Member States to take measures which are justified on grounds of public policy or public security.
Other Treaty rights which should be borne in mind in any consideration of this topic are the free movement of persons and the freedom of establishment and to provide services, since these can also have implications for the acquisition of property.
In the Danish case, the right for other EU citizens to acquire property, generally a capital movement but sometimes a domestic operation (e.g. an EU citizen working or doing business from Denmark) is conditioned by the Protocol to the Treaty on the acquisition of property in Denmark which states that 'notwithstanding the provisions of this Treaty, Denmark may maintain the existing legislation on the acquisition of second homes'.
This was the formulation requested and obtained by the Danish authorities in the revision to the Treaty.
The Commission has never contested the right of the Danish authorities to apply this legislation.
A separate issue is the use of second homes.
In Denmark, as in other Member States, there are specific national rules governing the rights and duties of real estate owners.
The Commission, in accordance with the principle of subsidiarity, regards such matters as primarily within the domain of national regulation where such rules bear equally on nationals and other EU citizens.
The Commission met and conesponded with representatives of Danish summer house owners recently and expressed the view that the case of the maximum winter letting period for second homes was a matter for internal Danish legislation.
This legislation governs the use, rather than the conditions of acquisition, of these homes and, as such, would not, in the Commission's view, represent a change in the 'existing legislation on acquisition' refened to in the protocol.
On the question of an extension to the winter letting possibilities implying that this then became a commercial activity, they are not entirely clear why letting for 52 weeks should be any different from letting for 30 weeks per year, and how such an extension would automatically give a right to ownership.
However, the Danish authorities would appear to hold a different view.
This view may be based on the possibilities given in 'existing legislation' for operation in real estate linked to Treaty freedoms, other than those on capital movements.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 76 by Konstantinos Hatzidakis (H-0354/97)
Subject: Low take-up rate for the 'Kleisthenis' programme in the Greek Community Support Framework
The 'Kleisthenis' programme forms an important part of the Second Community Support Programme for Greece: it is intended to modernize Greek public administration which constitutes one of the main priorities in Greek attempts to promote development and convergence with the other Community countries.
However, various sources indicate that for over three years there have been serious delays in the take-up of the relevant.Community funds, so that the programme has been implemented very slowly.
Will the Commission say precisely how long these delays have been, and what measures it intends to take to ensure that the programme can be implemented more rapidly?
Answer
The absorption situation of the Operational Programme for the 'Modernisation of Public Administration' (best known as 'Klisthenis') is as follows: subprogramme 1: 86 % of 1994-96 total cost - 37 % of 1994-99 total cost; subprogramme 2: 31 % of 1994-96 total cost - 6 % of 1994-99 total cost; subprogramme 3: 47 % of 1994-96 total cost - 12 % of 1994-99 total cost; subprogramme 4: 51 % of 1994-96 total cost - 9 % of 1994-99 total cost.
Thus subprogramme 1, which is related purely to training, presents a more or less normal rate of absorption.
Subprogramme 3, related purely to infrastructure (mainly acquiring and installation of equipment), presents a lower rate.
This is explained by the major and lengthy organisational and administrative changes (requiring in certain cases complete legislative procedures) required before beginning actual implementation of the relevant projects.
Normally, absorption performance of this subprogramme will have decisively improved by the first half of 1998.
Subprogramme 2 presents an even lower rate of absorption.
However, low absorption perform ance of measures 2.1 and 2.3 of this sub-programme, concerning infrastructure, is justified by the same reasons as for subprogramme 3.
On the other hand, measures 2.2 concerning modernisation studies and 2.4 concerning the national initiative 'Kapodistrias', launched only recently by the Greek Government, need closer attention and concentration of efforts in order to absorb funds in an efficient and productive way.
The same remark applies to subprogramme 4 which concerns technical assistance.
The Commission expects to be informed on progress here at the next meeting of the Monitoring Committee later this month.
It is also possible that by the end of 1997 the Monitoring Committee of the programme will decide on any conective adjustments necessary for obtaining the best results from 'Klisthenis'.
Question No 77 by Ludivina Garcia Arias (H-0356/97) Subject: Use of standards for the transmission of television channels
When will the Commission give its opinion on this matter?
Question No 78 by Joan Colom i Naval (H-0357/97) Subject: Legislation on television decoders
In its reply to questions H-0049/97 and H-0079/97 (2) the Commission summarized the main features of European legislation governing the transmission of television signals and in particular digital television services.
Debates of the European Parliament
On 17 April 1997 the Spanish Congress of Deputies completed the passage into law of Decree-Law 1/97, which was the subject of my previous question.
Can the Commission now say whether the text of that Spanish law, including the transitional anangements, complies with Directive 95/47/EC (') and with Community legislation on free competition?
Joint answer
Members will recall that Directive 95/47/EC requhes the use of standardised transmission systems for digital television, and the incorporation of a descrambler conforming to the Common European Scrambling System in all relevant consumer equipment.
The Directive also requhes that for pay-television services, conditional access providers offer their services to broadcasters on fair, reasonable and non-discriminatory terms.
The monitoring of transposition into national law of Community directives is a formal process involving technical and legal studies carried out by the Commission of the relevant legislation.
The process has already started in connection with the Television Standards Directive in the case of a number of Member States, including Spain.
Upon completion of these studies, the Commission will make known its position on the Spanish legislation.
Question No 79 by Heidi Hautala (H-0359/97) Subject: The impact of xylitol on public health
The findings of a number of international research projects indicate that xylitol prevents tooth decay.
The Commission has nonetheless decided that tax relief on xylitol in Finland violates the rules on the internal market.
In order to demonstrate the soundness of the science involved, the EU's advisory scientific committee on foodstuffs ought to deliver an opinion on the effects of xylitol in promoting oral hygiene.
Previous research - the opinions delivered by the EU scientific committee in 1984 and the 1996 evaluation by the American Food and Drug Administration - did not examine the health-promoting characteristics of xylitol or other polyols.
Will the Commission investigate the public-health properties of xylitol?
Does the Commission wish to bolster the public-health aspect in parallel with free competition?
Answer
As noted in its earlier reply to Questions No P-0002/97 and E-0022/97, the Commission has sent a letter of formal notice regarding a fiscal measure in favour of Xylitol products to the Finnish Government.
Accordingly, the Finnish authorities have a certain period of time to prepare a response to the Commission notice.
The Commission is abstaining from any further comments until the Finnish authorities have sent their reply.
Debates of the European Parliament
No 4-500/195 shown - there is a very serious danger that the modified product will develop allergies, create resistant strains of bacteria, exterminate traditional varieties, distort the composition of natural fauna, turn whole areas into experimental laboratories, affect the health and life of consumers to an incalculable extent concentrate production in the hands of a few people and lead to job losses and the disappearance of traditional natural crops and entire agricultural regions and populations?
Answer
The experimental release of genetically modified tomatoes notified to the Greek Competent Authorities has been performed in accordance with the requirements of pait Β of Directive 90/220/EEC on the deliberate release into the environment of genetically modified organisms.
There have been 58 notifications concerning experimental releases of genetically modified tomatoes in the Community in 6 other Member States (Spain, Italy, France, Holland, Portugal and the UK) in the period 1991 to 1997.
The majority of the notifications concerns tomatoes modified for altered ripening, altered processing characteristics and increased insect and disease resistance.
No adverse human or environmental effects have been observed from this work.
Part C of Directive 90/220/EEC which has been used for the placing on the market of products containing or consisting of genetically modified organisms such as the Ciba Geigy (now Novartis) maize, does not contain a mechanism for the banning of such products in the absence of safety grounds affecting environmental or human safety.
There is no evidence that the genetically modified maize from Ciba Geigy has been associated with any adverse environmental or human safety issues and accordingly still has a consent to be placed on the market under Directive 90/220/EEC.
The Commission has not, to date (7 May 1997), received any notification under part C of Directive 90/220/EEC for the placing on the market of genetically modified tomatoes and accordingly no authorization under lhe Directive has been made.
For such an authorization, the applicant would have to provide a risk assessment of any safety issues for environmental and human health under Directive 90/220/EEC or relevant sectorial legislation such as the novel food regulation.
Accordingly at this time these tomatoes cannot be marketed in the Community.
Question No 81 by Ulf Holm (H-0366/97)
Subject: Lobbyists in the Commission
There has been a fairly wideranging debate over the past few years concerning lobbyists in EU institutions.
The European Parliament is now compiling a register in which all lobbyists are to be listed.
The relevant parliamentary committee is soon to submit further proposals for rules governing matters such as gifts, meals, etc. in order to regulate lobbying.
Does the Commission keep a similar register of lobbyists seeking to influence Commissioners or Commission officials?
Answer
The Commission has no register equivalent to that envisaged by the European Parliament.
In fact the Commission advocates as open a dialogue as possible with lobbyists and therefore does not impose any system of registration.
On the other hand the Commission has just published its first list containing over 600 pan-European nonprofit making organizations with which it is associated.
The purpose behind publication of this list is to make the institutions' officials more aware of the importance of consulting lobbyists by bringing lesser known and therefore less frequently consulted organizations to greater prominence and attention.
The list is a working document not a seal of approval; it is purely for information. It tells the reader 'who is who' and 'who does what' within the nonprofit making organizations.
Listing does not imply any official recognition by the Commission, nor does it bring any privileges.
Interested organizations place themselves upon the list on a voluntary basis following publication of an announcement in the Official Journal (') and on the basis of the Commission's fragmented data.
The Commission has no set rules as regards lobbyists (approval, registration, code of conduct).
The approach taken by the Commission set out in its Communication of December 1992 (') entitled 'Open and structured dialogue between the Commission and lobbyists' encourages the lobbyists to be self-regulating on the basis of a certain number of minimum criteria published in the communication.
It did not therefore wish to impose any code of conduct on the lobbyists, regarding them as being in the best position to do that.
Question No 82 by Ioannis Theonas (H-0368/97)
Subject: Consequences for Greece arising from the recent publication of the general economic outlook for 1997
In its recent report on the general economic outlook for 1997, the Commission states that only Greece and Italy will be unable to meet the 3 % public deficit target.
In the light of this forecast, the Commission calls on Greece to redouble its efforts to achieve the 3 % target, notably by intensifying its austerity programme and expanding the tax basis, further reducing public spending and extending privatization.
Does the Commission not agree that such a policy will finally sacrifice the objective of development and increase disparities between countries such as Greece and the remaining Member States, thereby putting paid to any idea of convergence?
Does it not agree also that this policy will turn unemployment into an explosive issue and plunge other population groups into poverty and marginalization, resulting in a further decline in consumption and investment and, consequently, the loss of even more jobs?
Answer
On 23 April 1997, the 'Spring 1997 Economic Forecasts for the period 1996-1998' prepared by the Commission were released.
According to these forecasts, the general government deficit in Italy and Greece is expected to be higher than 3 % of GDP in 1997; in the case of Greece, the deficit should reach 4.9 % of GDP.
This figure compares with the objective of 4.2 % of GDP adopted by the Greek authorities in the convergence programme and in the budget for 1997: clearly, both the Commission and the Greek authorities forecast a general government deficit for this year higher than 3 % of GDP.
However, there should be no confusion between the present forecasts and the decisions concerning which Member States will participate in the monetary union on 1 January 1999; these decisions will be taken in Spring 1998 on the basis of the results for 1997 available at that time.
In the 'Broad Economic Policy Guidelines', drawn up in line with Article 103(2) of the Treaty, to be approved by the European Council at Amsterdam (in'June 1997), the Commission, on the basis of the Spring Economic Forecasts, states that in Greece 'sustained efforts on a wide range of fronts are requhed in order to meet the targets of the convergence programme, ...'; it is recalled that the targets of the convergence programme for the deficit are 4.2 % of GDP in 1997 and 2.4 % of GDP in 1998.
* l) OJ C 63 of 5 March 1993.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 83 by Inger Schörling (H-0373/97) Subject: Use of TRI as a solvent
There are good health-related and environmental reasons why TRI has been banned as a solvent in Sweden.
Moreover, adequate alternatives that cause less damage to health and the environment are available for uses of TRI.
Why has the Commission not prohibited the use of TRI as a solvent throughout the EU?
Does it not understand that the EU will be seen as obstructing efforts to improve the environment and look after human health if Sweden is forced to lift its ban on the use of TRI owing to rales on the free movement of goods?
Answer
The Commission refers the honourable Member to its answer to Oral Question H-0759/96 by Mr Gahrton.
The Swedish measures to prohibit the sale, transfer and use of methylene chloride and trichloroethylene for industrial purposes fall within the scope of Article 30 of the EC Treaty which prohibits all measures having equivalent effect to quantitative restrictions on imports.
A Member State may in certain cases rely on Article 36 of the EC Treaty or mandatory requirements recognized by the Court of Justice, inter alia public health or the environment to justify derogations from the principle of free movement of goods.
The Court of Justice has consistently held that it is for the national authorities that invoke Article 36 or any mandatory requirement to prove in each specific case that a measure that hinders intra-Community trade is necessary for the effective protection of such a requirement and that the import of the product concerned would constitute a serious risk.
Under the principle of proportionality, the national measures have to be proportionate to the objective to be attained, i.e. to public health or the environment.
The Commission sent a letter of formal notice to Sweden on 9 October 1996 concerning the Swedish measures on methylene chloride and trichloroethylene.
In its reply, received by the Commission on 6 January 1997, Sweden rejected the Commission's arguments and restated its position that the Swedish measures are needed to protect the health of workers.
Trichloroethylene, a solvent mainly used for metal degreasing by professional users, is classified as carcinogen category 3.
Category 3, which is the lowest category, means that there is a cause for concern for man owing to possible carcinogenic effects but in respect of which the available information is not adequate for making a satisfactory assessment.
A risk assessment is being carried out under Regulation 793/93 on existing substances.
The risk assessment report will describe the risk for the envhonment, the public and workers from exposure to trichloroethylene. In the draft presented by the UK it is indicated that certain occupational exposure situations may pose risks in poorly controlled workplaces.
However, the conclusion cannot yet be drawn that industrial uses of trichloroethylene should be restricted at Community level.
In short, the Commission does see a certain risk and considers that the exposure of workers to trichloroethylene, as for many other chemicals used by industry, needs to be controlled.
There is however a wide range of options available to control exposures in the workplace.
Trichloroethylene is on the Commission's priority list for the development of occupational exposure limits (OEL) for chemical agents at work.
In the course of this work all cunently available data concerning risks to workers' health will be evaluated.
The Commission may subsequently propose appropriate legislative measures within the framework of the existing legislation on occupational safety and health.
In its letter of formal notice, the Commission considered that a ban on the placing on the market of trichloroethylene is excessive in relation to the risk.
Question No 84 by Hervé Fabre-Aubrespy (H-0375/97) Subject: European Community expenditure on information, communication and education
Given the risks of abuse and distortion that may result from information campaigns conducted by or with the financial support of the European Community, and the fact that a national election campaign is in progress, does the Commission not intend to suspend or terminate measures of this type cunently being applied in France?
Answer
The Commission has been developing on French territory, since 1996, three priority actions under the Prince programme approved by me European Parliament and with the active collaboration of that institution.
The three action programmes on information have been the subject of an agreement between the French government and the Commission within the framework of the national dialogue for Europe launched by the French government in 1996.
Because of the elections to be held on 25 May and 1 June, the Commission and the French government agreed jointly to suspend the national dialogue for Europe during the election campaign.
Question No 85 by Anne Mcintosh (H-0002/97)
In view of the recent amendment to the 1986 Equal Treatment Directive, concerning levels of occupational pensions, will the European Commission take this opportunity to folly explain the rationale behind the changes?
Can the Commission indicate the extent to which the equalization of occupational pensions will affect the average pension, and whether this will go up or down?
If the trend is to push the average pension up, what assurances can the European Commission give that it will not result in job losses as businesses seek to offset these higher costs?
Answer
The amendment to which the honourable Member refers does not concern levels of occupational pensions.
It simply seeks to bring Directive 86/378 into tine with Article 119 of the EC Treaty, as interpreted by the Court of Justice in the Barber and subsequent judgments.
In this case law, the Court interpreted Article 119 of the Treaty in such a way that discrimination between men and women in occupational social security schemes was prohibited in general.
The case law automatically invalidated certain provisions of Council Directive 86/37/EEC of 24 July 1986.
The case law and the amended directive require only that there be no discrimination.
They do not in principle require 'levelling up' to the level of. the hitherto more favoured group, nor do they require the setting of particular levels of pension.
Question No 86 by Manuel Medina Ortega (H-0217/97)
Subject: Fraud against the Community transit system in the banana sector
Could the Commission explain how this fraud has been carried out and what measures it has taken or plans to take to curb it?
Answer
Fraudulent abuse of the transit system in the banana sector was first discovered by the Commission in collaboration with the Italian authorities in 1992.
Substantial quantities of South American bananas were shipped to northern Community ports and declared for export to third countries.
A Tl transit procedure was opened with the Customs Office of Milan declared as office of destination.
The bananas were in reality directly delivered to two Italian end-buyers and described as being of Community origin in order to be sold on the internal market at a low cost.
The transit documents for the banana consignments were falsely discharged at the Customs Office of Milan.
Counterfeit return copies of Tl documents were forwarded to customs offices of departure to release the guarantees imposed on the transit operations.
Another fraud was detected in 1993.
The same individuals involved in the previous case organized new consignments from northern Community ports to third countries such as Albania and Turkey via Austria, Germany and Italy.
The goods were hregularly withdrawn from the EC transit regime in Italy.
To avoid seizure of guarantees at the customs offices of departure, the organization established two transit procedures; a first transit from Belgium to Germany or from Germany to Austria and another transit from Germany and Austria to Italian ports where the transit documents were falsely discharged.
The bananas went directly to the Italian end-buyers.
The fraud mechanism was discovered when an unusual number of bananas in transit via Germany was identified by the Italian customs.
As a result.of these investigations, all the main participants in the criminal networks responsible for dispatching bananas into the Community have been anested and their assets have been seized to ensure payment of customs duties totalling 15 MECU.
The Commission described briefly these fraud cases in its 1995 annual report on the fight against fraud.
The Commission would emphasize that in line with its communications since March 1995, considerable attention has been paid to bring about improvements in the transit procedures until the adoption on 30 April of this year of an Action Plan for making these procedures both more efficient and more fraud-proof.
It is particularly important to bear in mind that bananas are considered within the framework of emergency measures of protection to be sensitive products for which the use of the comprehensive guarantee is forbidden.
Question No 87 by Marialiese Flemming (H-0260/97) Subject: Nuclear power stations
When is it planned to close the nuclear power stations cunently in operation in Europe, what programmes already exist for this, what cost estimates are available, and what measures are planned for the safe disposal of the waste occurring?
Answer
At the moment there are eight Member States which use nuclear power stations for the production of electricity - Germany, Belgium, Spain, Finland, France, the Netherlands, the United Kingdom and Sweden - with a net total capacity of approximately 126 Gwe.
Significant reductions are envisaged for Sweden, following a decision by the national Parliament to cease the nuclear production of electricity by the year 2010, for the Netherlands, whose sole nuclear power station is to be shut down between 2000 and 2005 and for the United Kingdom, which is to cease using Magnox and AGR reactors by 2020.
In the absence of any orders for new nuclear power stations in future decades, the great majority of nuclear power stations in service, the largest of which began production between 1977 and 1997 (and a power station with a capacity of 1455 MWe is to be started up in France in 1998) will reach the end of their fives between 2015 and 2040.
Most Member States follow the policy 'of making nuclear power stations safe for a period of approximately 40 years before dismantling them, in order to ensure that the radionuclides with a short half-life have decayed.
Taking account of the nuclear power stations already shut down, it may be envisaged that the production of radioactive waste between 2020 and 2100 will be 3 000 m3-15 000 m3 per plant, depending on the type of plant and other factors.
Dismantling costs are of the order of 15 % of building costs and are revised as a function of inflation rates.
Financing comes from reserves accumulated during the useful life of the power stations in the form of fonds invested under government control or operators' internal reserves.
Disposal of radioactive waste produced may be carried out within the plants such as those already in service in most countries concerned - Centre de l'Aube in France, El Cabril in Spain, VLJ and Losi in Finland, Eram in Germany, Drigg and Dounreay in the United Kingdom and SFR in Sweden.
