It is com monly accepted that development of the energy sector in Asia has so far had negative effects on the envhonment, at national and regional level and for the planet as a whole.
Unless serious efforts are made, the increase of carbon dioxide, acid rain and pollution by suspended particles threatens to eliminate or put at serious risk efforts to restrict pollutant emissions on our planet.
In parallel, large-scale deforestation exacerbates pressures on the envhonment of certain countries.
That aspect often reflects to the cost of the poorer population levels, especially children and women who are forced to traverse large distances in order to cover theh basic energy needs.
