There have been no successes.
I think we must eventually recognize - and this was in fact recognized in the text - that in the area of transfers of finance and technology, and in terms of capacity building, there is an enormous amount of ground to make up.
The decline in development investment and the debt crisis are the mairi obstacles.
In the end, I think that if no solutions are found to them, then the circus should simply be wound up.
Perhaps I am being a little gloomy at this late hour, Mr President, and I hope that the Commissioner still has some glimmers of hope to offer us.
Bjerregaard, Member of the Commission. - (DA) Mr President, I will not prolong the debate unduly at this late hour.
Debates of the European Parliament
Subject: Publication of Schengen documents
The Amsterdam Treaty provides for the Schengen Agreement to become an integral part of the European Union.
The functioning of the Agreement is therefore an important element of the debate on that Treaty, which, however, is made difficult owing to the fact that important parts of the rules on the practical application of Schengen are not in the public domain.
This is true of the SIRENE manual, the common instructions to embassies and consulates and the manual for checks canied out on the external frontier.
Will the Council now ensure that these documents are made public so that the debate on the incorporation of Schengen may proceed on as conect and complete a basis as possible?
Answer
The future Amsterdam Treaty will be accompanied by a protocol incorporating the Schengen legislation into the European Union framework.
That protocol will have an annex listing all the accords, agreements and protocols making up that legislation, together with the decisions and declarations adopted by the executive committee set up by the implementing convention.
At this point, however, it seems pertinent to emphasize that the incorporation of the Schengen documents into the Amsterdam Treaty is subject to the signature and ratification of that Treaty.
It follows that these documents, which are known to the States that have signed the Schengen Agreements, must be brought to the attention of those Member States of the Union that are not party to Schengen.
This disclosure must be as full as possible, including disclosure of documents which, by their nature, are not in the public domain (the Sirene manual, the instructions to consulates, etc.).
The Council's subordinate bodies will have to ensure, in close consultation with those responsible for Schengen, that the confidential nature of certain documents is respected as scrupulously as necessary.
For the great majority of the Schengen texts, publication in some appropriate form should present no problems.
Subject: Inclusion of new types of natural habitat in the nature conservation directive
The Commission has recently introduced a proposal for the inclusion in the nature conservation directive of certain protected types of natural habitat in Finland, Sweden and Austria.
I gather that the adoption of this item is not on the agenda for the Luxembourg Presidency.
Is the Council aware that delaying the changes to the nature conservation directive will create problems for environmental conservation particularly in Finland, where appeals have already been lodged on behalf of landowners against the environment authorities' proposals to designate areas for conservation under the Natura 2000 programme, on the grounds that they are not entirely legally watertight?
Does the Council propose to take a decision as soon as possible during the Luxembourg Presidency?
Answer
The presidency is fully aware of the importance of this proposal to Austria, Finland and Sweden, and intends to submit it to the Council for adoption as soon as possible.
Debates of the European Parliament
Subject: Detailed EU rules in the agricultural sector
Obviously, cucumbers and other foods should be condemned because of decay, discolouration or other damage but to create a common EU 'aesthetic standard' for agricultural produce is tantamount to restricting product diversity and declaring the individual consumer to be incompetent.
Quality control to remove food which is unfit for consumption is acceptable but it is grotesque when a crooked little cucumber which was previously regarded in Sweden as top quality must suddenly be regarded as fit for dumping merely because of the need to comply with EU rules.
Is it not time, once and for all, to scrap the EU's detailed rules for agriculture and allow the consumer to judge what is a top quality cucumber?
Is it intended that the EU's absurd detailed rules should result in top quality food being consigned to the rubbish dump while people in other parts of the world starve?
Answer
The development, from 1966 onwards, of a policy for the application of Community quality standards has been designed to remove from the market products of unsatisfactory quality, to guide production in such a way as to satisfy consumer requirements and to facilitate trade relations based on fair competition, so helping to improve the profitability and transparency of the market.
The quality standards, for those products which the Council has decided should be subject to such standards, are laid down by the Commission after consultation of the Management Committee for Fruit and Vegetables.
However, in its August 1994 communication to the Council and the European Parliament on the development and future of the Community policy in the fruit and vegetables sector, the Commission acknowledged that "these advantages are however sometimes not perceived clearly by the consumer, who is denied access to produce that is fit for consumption and in some cases has traditionally been consumed in his region', together with the fact that the standardization has often been criticized as excessively complex.
For this reason, in connection with the reform of the common organization of the market in fruit and vegetables last October, the Council decided:
- to take due account, when defining the quality standards to be applied in the Community, of the standards recommended by the Working Party on Standardization of Perishable Produce and Quality Improvement set up at the UN's Economic Commission for Europe (ECE (UN) standards), and above all
- not to make it obligatory for certain products to conform to the standards, especially those in a given region which are sold by retailers in that region to meet a traditional and well known local demand..
The Council has thus established the legal basis for the Commission, after consulting the Management Committee for Fruit and Vegetables, to be able to develop a policy of quality standards required by operators in the sector while making due allowance for the kind of concerns expressed by the honourable Member.
Question No 18 by Alfred Lomas (H-0529/97)
What is the reaction of the Council to the dialogue between India and Pakistan about Kashmir and does the Council agree that representatives from Kashmir should be included in such talks?
Answer
On 30 June 1997 the European Union published a declaration welcoming the general agreement between India and Pakistan on a negotiated approach to all the unresolved problems still existing between the two countries, including the problem of Jammu and Kashmir.
The EU paid tribute to the political wisdom of this decision, which was designed to help ease relations between the two major powers of southern Asia and bring a lasting resolution of their differences.
This agreement represents an important step towards reconciliation between India and Pakistan and greater political and economic cooperation.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 19 by Olivier Dupuis (H-0530/97)
Subject: European civilian and military corps
As the number of crisis spots, such as Somalia, Rwanda, the former Yugoslavia and Albania, has increased around the world, the European Parliament has on several occasions called for the setting-up of a European civilian and military corps to carry out peacekeeping and peace-restoring missions (Petersberg missions), especially in its Fassa report (A4-0021/97) and Tmdemans report (A4-0162/97), in which it called on the Council and the Commission to conduct a feasibility study on the subject.
What action has the Council taken on the European Parliament's proposal?
Has the Council carried out the requested feasibility study, either on its own or with the Commission?
If so, when will it be able to forward the study's findings to Members of the European Parliament?
Answer
The Council has not conducted a feasibility study on the question of a European civilian and military corps to carry out peacekeeping and peace-restoring missions, as called for in the Fassa and Tindemans reports.
However, that is not to say that the Union, if it were confronted by a crisis that represented a threat to its security, could not undertake an action or adopt a decision which would involve the establishment of a corps to carry out such missions.
Article J4, paragraph 2, provides that decisions and actions by the Union that would have defence implications can be implemented by the Western European Union if the Council decides to call upon that organization.
This option has already been exercised on two occasions, in principle: in the decision of 27 June 1996 relating to operations to evacuate nationals of Member States when their safety is at risk in a third country, and in the decision of 22 November 1996 relating to the preparation and implementation of the Union's joint action in the Great Lakes region.
In the draft treaty produced by the intergovernmental conference in Amsterdam, this option available to the Union has been very explicitly described by the addition of the following clarifications:
- first, humanitarian and fact-finding missions, peacekeeping missions and peace-restoring missions are mentioned expressly in paragraph 2 of the new Article J7;
- secondly, if it is clearly stated that the WEU shall give the Union access to an operational capacity, in the context of these missions and elsewhere (Article J7, paragraph 1).
The honourable Member will be able to judge for himself the extent to which these anangements conespond to the proposal to give the Union an operational capacity in connection with the Petersberg missions.
Question No 20 by MaLou Lindholm (H-0536/97)
Subject: Follow-up to the Lindholm report on equal treatment and non-discrimination with regard to the European Communities' staff regulations and conditions of employment
In its opinion (904(CNS)) on Parliament's amendments to COM(96)77, the Commission notes that, whilst the substance of the amendments is not being called into question, it is not an appropriate time, after four years' of legislative procedure, to incorporate them.
Given that a new Article 6a was added to the EC Treaty and existing Articles 2 and 3 were supplemented at Amsterdam, authorizing the taking of appropriate action to combat discrimination based for instance on sex and sexual orientation and requiring the Communities to promote equality between men and women, will the Council state whether it shares the Commission's view that this is not an appropriate time to incorporate the amendments Parliament adopted by a large majority, when those amendments were after all intended to help fulfil the objectives of the Treaty and the undertakings entered into as regards employees of the EU, or whether it agrees with Parliament that the amendments should be made now.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 21 by Marianne Eriksson (H-0538/97) Subject: Homosexuals in the EU institutions
One of the Commission's arguments for not incorporating Parliament's amendment concerning equal rights of partners inespective of sex and sexual orientation in its proposal to amend the Communities' staff regulations and conditions of employment - despite the express objective of the proposal being to combat discrimination between staff - has been the financial burden on the Communities that would result from implementing Parliament's amendment.
Has the Council received an assessment from the Commission actually calculating those costs?
If so, does the Council consider that the costs are so high that the principle of equal rights should not apply to all EU staff?
Joint answer
On 6 March 1996 the Commission forwarded to the Council the text of the amended proposal for a Council Regulation amending the staff regulations for officials of the European Communities and the anangements applicable to other Community personnel in the matter of equal treatment.
Following an exchange of letters dated 15 July 1996 and 17 October 1996 between the Council and Parliament, the latter gave a new opinion on this proposal on 20 February this year.
Under the Dutch presidency an initial exchange of views on the amended proposal took place within the Council's competent bodies, during which it became apparent that examination of the proposal would have to be defened pending the results of the proceedings of the intergovernmental conference on the principle of non-discrimination and equality of treatment.
As pointed out by the honourable Member, the draft Treaty of Amsterdam, on which the European Council reached agreement on 17 June this year, includes provisions regarding non-discrimination and equal treatment.
The Luxembourg presidency will supervise the further examination by the Council of the Commission's amended proposal, in the light of the results of the European Council meeting in Amsterdam and all the amendments proposed by Parliament.
Question No 22 by Alexandras Alavanos (H-0537/97) Subject: Commencement of process leading to the accession of Cyprus
The conclusions of the Presidency of the European Parliament at Amsterdam state that 'the necessary final legal editing and harmonization of the texts will be completed with a view to signature of the Treaty in October 1997 at Amsterdam.'
However, the introduction to the conclusions of the Presidency states: 'the European Council meeting in Amsterdam on 16 and 17 June 1997 successfully concluded the IGC with full agreement on a draft Treaty. This opens the way for the launching of the enlargement process in accordance with the conclusions of the Madrid European Council.'
Will the Council confirm that the commencement date of the process leading to the accession of Cyprus, which is due within six months of the end of the IGC, will be calculated on the basis of the Amsterdam summit of 17 June, and that the process will thus get under way by December 1997?
Answer
The European Council in Amsterdam took due note of the fact that, since the intergovernmental conference has ended, the way is now open for the enlargement procedure to go ahead.
The Commission will in a few days' time, probably on 16 July, present to the Council and the European Parliament a detailed communication entitled "Agenda 2000' in which it will set out, inter alia, its views on the launching of the enlargement procedure.
The "General Affairs' Council will study this communication at its meeting on 22 and 23 July, with a view to submitting a detailed report at the European Council meeting in Luxembourg next December.
At that meeting the European Council will take the necessary decisions on the enlargement procedure to enable actual negotiations to begin as soon as possible after December 1997.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 23 by Konstantinos Hatzidakis (H-0540/97)
Subject Refusal by the Turkish Cypriot occupying regime to allow the Greek Cypriot teacher Eleni Foka to return to the occupied part of Cyprus
The illegal Dentash regime in the Turkish-occupied part of Cyprus is guilty of a fresh violation of human rights and international law.
After long preventing the Greek Cypriot teacher, Eleni Foka, who is well known for her educational activities in the occupied territories, from seeking medical care in the free part of Cyprus - a move judged necessary by the UN peacekeeping force - the 'authorities' of this puppet state are now refusing to allow her to return home.
It should be borne in mind that without Eleni Foka there will only be one teacher in the next school year for the Greek Cypriot pupils belonging to three villages in the enclave.
Will the Council say whether it is aware of this state of affairs and what steps it intends to take to ensure that international law is respected and to enable this Greek Cypriot teacher to return to her village and to her teaching duties?
Answer
The Council is familiar with the situation described by the honourable Member in his question.
The Council regrets that the teacher, Mrs Foka, was prevented from returning to Ayia Triada, her home village.
From 8 to 13 July 1997, in New York, an initial round of direct talks took place between the two Cypriot communities, at the invitation and under the aegis of the Secretary-General of the United Nations.
In a declaration on Cyprus by the presidency, on behalf of the EU, published on the eve of the negotiations, the Union called upon the two sides in Cyprus to approach the discussions in a spirit of openness and expressed the hope that the discussions would pave the way to a genuine process of negotiation.
The Council fully supports this initiative by the United Nations which will do everything it can, together with the other actors in the international community, to bring these direct talks to a favourable conclusion.
This will provide the best possible framework for the Council to bring all its influence to bear to bring the parties towards a negotiated, just and lasting solution in Cyprus and put an end to the human rights violations.
The presidency will be keeping a close watch on the situation in Cyprus, especially through the agency of the presidency's representative for Cyprus, Ambassador Heaslip.
Question No 24 by Richard Howitt (H-0542/97) Subject: European Spatial Development Perspective
Following support for the development of a European Spatial Development Perspective at the Informal Council meeting at Noordwijk on 9 and 10 June, why has the Council decided to proceed on an intergovernmental basis, and not sought to commit Community resources to this project?
Is the Council committed to providing a budget to support this work, including the proposed establishment of a European Observatory, outside the Community's budget and at what level?
How does the Council propose to consult Parliament in this respect?
Answer
In answer to the honourable Member's question, I have to admit that space development is not part of the Community's area of responsibility.
Traditionally, this sector is the subject of intergovernmental cooperation within the framework of the European Space Agency.
It was on that basis that the informal Council meeting in Nordwijk on 9 and 10 June 1997 decided to proceed with the preparation of a draft European Spatial Development Perspective.
In this situation, the honourable Member will appreciate that funds from the Community's budget cannot be appropriated to finance the initiative in question.
In any case, I must point out to the honourable Member that representatives of the European Parliament participated in the work of the informal Council in Nordwijk.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 25 by Bernd Posselt (H-0547/97) Subject: Elections in Kosovo
What is the Luxembourg presidency's view of the situation in Kosovo, now that elections, which had been postponed at the USA's request, have been announced for December and have evidently been welcomed by Washington, and what measures are planned to have the voting safeguarded by EU observers or the long envisaged EU office in Kosovo?
Answer
As the honourable Member is aware, the Council is continuing to monitor developments closely in the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, including Kosovo.
Also, on the occasion of the extension of the autonomous trade preferences to Yugoslavia, it repeated its call for the recommendations drawn up by the OSCE to be implemented in full and at an early date.
These recommendations include the opening of a dialogue between the government and the opposition on reforming the electoral law, access to the media and the independence of the judicial system.
The European Union also expressed deep concern about the trial resulting, on 30 May this year in Pristina, in guilty verdicts against twenty Albanians charged with tenorism and other punishable offences under Yugoslavian criminal law.
On 3 July it stressed that the draft law for the municipal elections, cunentiy being studied by the Serbian Parliament, should adopt the OSCE recommendations and encourage the growth of democracy in Yugoslavia.
In fact the Union is fully aware of the implications of the forthcoming presidential and parliamentary elections in Yugoslavia, and the challenges associated with the situation in Kosovo.
It considers that the course of the elections in Kosovo should be evaluated in that context.
Any observer activity by the EU, as part of the efforts made by the international community, can only relate to the elections in Kosovo as part of Yugoslavia, and not to clandestine initiatives launched by the representatives of the ethnic Albanian population in that region.
The Union intends to use all means at its disposal, including the prospects of improving relations between the EU and Yugoslavia, to encourage the authorities in Belgrade to respect internationally recognized standards of freedom of expression and of political association during the forthcoming elections.
This approach is, moreover, in line with the support and cooperation offered by the Union to the OSCE and the other international organizations also concerned by the need to support democratization in the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia.
To this end, the Union remains convinced that all possible pressure must be brought to bear to ensure that the authorities in Belgrade respect the commitments already entered into regarding the presence of OSCE observers at the elections planned for 1997.
In this context, the ways in which the OSCE can act and the possible support of the Union and the Member States are under review.
Finally, with regard to the establishment of an EU presence in Pristina, the Council has not recorded any further significant progress beyond the situation of which the honourable Member was informed in the answer to his oral question H-0071 in February 1997.
Nevertheless, the Council is remaining in contact with the authorities in Belgrade, and has on several occasions reminded them of the importance which the Union attaches to solving this problem.
Question No 26 by Jan Andersson (H-0551/97) Subject: Regional policy aid prompting company relocation
Employees at the Ericsson plant are justifiably concerned about their jobs since the company announced its plans to move printed circuit-card production to Scotiand.
Trade union representatives have contacted the Swedish social-democratic delegation to help clarify whether EU regional aid has played a part in enabling these operations to be moved from Nonköping to Scotland.
Is it possible that regional aid or any other form of Community support helped Ericsson set up in Scotland and, if so, what conclusions does the Council think should be drawn regarding the future allocation of aid?
Debates of the European Parliament
I can however inform you at this stage that, in a recent memorandum to the Council, the Danish delegation asked for a closer study to be made of the effects of structural policy and state aid to the regions on competitiveness within the Community, the objective being to study how best to use the structural policy to benefit the least favoured areas without, as a result, causing enterprises to close down in other parts of the Community.
The question was also raised at the "Industry' Council of 24 April this year, which called upon the Commission to submit a report to it at an early date.
Subject: Restriction of the independence of the mandate exercised by Members of the European Parliament
Following the addition of a new paragraph (4) to Article 138 of the draft Treaty, the European Parliament is now required to lay down regulations and general conditions governing the performance of the duties of its Members after seeking an opinion from the Commission and with the approval of the Council acting by unanimity.
Bearing in mind that the European Parliament is the only EU body that is directly elected by the peoples of Europe, unlike the Commission and the Council, does the Council not believe that this amendment distorts the people's mandate and curtails and essentially abolishes the independence and freedom of the mandate of Members of the European Parliament, as defined in the EP's present Rules of Procedure which clearly state that Members shall not be bound by any instructions and shall not receive a binding mandate?
Does the Council not believe that this amendment is contrary to any rule of democracy and the independence of a body in that it essentially prevents that body from laying down its own organizational rules and thereby runs counter to the recognized principle of interna corporis? Does the Council not believe that this additional curtailing of the competences of the EP further increases the democratic deficit which characterizes decisionmaking in the EU?
Answer
The question asked by the honourable Member regarding the future rules for Members of the European Parliament calls for two types of comment by the Council:
First, it should be emphasized that the text to which the honourable Member refers in his question is the result of steps taken by the European Parliament itself, for the precise purpose of creating rules for Members of the European Parliament.
These steps have resulted in a proposed amendment to the Treaty, tabled by the President of the European Parliament and adopted, with slight changes, by the intergovernmental conference.
In fact those changes were changes not of substance but of procedure: the qualified majority voting in the Council originally envisaged for the approval of the instructions for Members of the European Parliament was changed to unanimous voting.
Secondly, the Council is profoundly convinced that the inclusions of this text in the body of the Treaty represents a considerable advance towards full recognition and establishment of the parliamentary function within the European Union.
Among other merits, this text makes good a deficiency in the Community legislative apparatus by opening up the possibility of adopting uniform rules governing all members of the European Parliament.
Question No 28 by Ioannis Theonas (H-0560/97)
Subject: Arrest and imprisonment by the Turkish authorities of the Turkish composer S. Yurtatapan
Following his revelations concerning relations between the mafia and the security forces in Turkey and the probable involvement of Turkish politicians, the Turkish composer S. Yurtatapan has been anested at Istanbul airport on his return from Germany, having previously helped the only two witnesses in his investigations flee to that country. Mr Yurtatapan is being held in Turkish prisons awaiting trial on the charge of possession of forged passports.
Debates of the European Parliament
Will the Council say what initiatives it intends to take to secure the release of one more Turkish intellectual who is being required to pay the price for the struggle to protect human rights, the rights of the Kurdish people and freedom of expression, and of the scores of other writers, journalists and politicians who are being held under unacceptable conditions in Turkish prisons, despite Turkey's constant pledges to promote democratization and respect for fundamental human rights?
Answer
The Council regrets Mr Yurtatapan's situation and stresses that freedom of expression is a universal basic right.
It attaches the greatest importance to respect for human rights and democracy in Turkey and unhesitatingly condemns any violations of those rights in its dealings with the Turkish authorities.
At the meeting of the EEC-Turkey Association Council on 29 April 1997 the EU restated Turkey's eligibility to become a member of the EU, as expressed in the association agreement of 1963 and in the Commission's opinion of 1989, adopted by the Council.
At the same time, the EU emphasized that the continued strengthening of ties between the EU and Turkey depended on a number of factors, including the matter of respect for human rights.
The EU recalled the establishment in Turkey of a considered programme designed to ensure that the situation regarding respect for human rights in that country be brought into line with accepted international standards.
It reminded Turkey of the keen interest felt in this matter by the European Parliament and the Council, and of the role allocated to the European Parliament by the Union's institutional provisions.
These considerations were duly respected when the European Commission presented its "Agenda 2000' to the European Parliament on 16 July, and will be again on 22 July when it presents to the Council its views on closer ties between the EU and Turkey.
The Council will continue to monitor closely the human rights situation and progress towards democracy in Turkey.
It will do so with particular reference to the spirit of Parliament's invitation to the Council when Parliament gave its assent to the implementation of the final phase of the customs union (on 13 December 1995).
Question No 29 by Hugh Kerr (H-0561/97) Subject: EU-Australia Cooperation Agreement
Why has the Council allowed the Australian Government off the hook regarding human rights by agreeing to sign the Political Declaration With Australia, rather than the Framework Agreement which was first envisaged?
Is the Council not concerned that this may set a precedent for countries who want to avoid a commitment to human rights when negotiating cooperation agreements with the European Union?
Answer
The honourable Member is certainly not unaware that the suspension mechanism allowing the Community to react immediately in cases of human rights violations, approved at the end of May 1995, can by its very nature only apply to agreements with third countries.
Other acts, such as the joint declaration on relations between the EU and Australia, cannot be subject to this mechanism, given that they are acts of a political nature with no legal basis or effects.
Nevertheless, I venture to remind the honourable Member that, in joint declarations, we have stressed:
"our common concern for the advancement of human rights, fundamental freedoms, democ racy and the rule of law, which underpin our policies both internally and internationally';
- and that "we reassert our firm determination to take advantage of our partnership in order to support democracy, the rule of law and respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms'.
It is not a matter for the Council to comment on the reasons that prompted Australia to opt for a joint declaration rather than an agreement.
All that can be said is that if an agreement had been signed with Australia, the Council would have regarded the inclusion of the human rights clause as an essential part of that agreement.
Question No 30 by Antoni Gutiérrez Díaz (H-0567/97)
Subject: Quelling of a pro-employment demonstration during the Amsterdam European Council t
During the Amsterdam Summit on 16 and 17 June 1997, a mass march was held against unemployment and social exclusion, which for no apparent reason was suppressed by the Dutch police who anested over 600 European citizens engaged in a peaceful demonstration.
What steps does the Council intend to take vis-à-vis those responsible for actions which are utterly contrary to the principle of involvement by citizens in issues affecting the European Union?
Question No 31 by Angela Sierra Gonzalez (H-0568/97)
Subject: Demonstrators anested by the Dutch police during the Amsterdam European Council
Several hundred people taking part in a demonstration at the end of the European March for Employment were anested by the Dutch police during the European Council meeting on 16 and 17 June 1997 in Amsterdam.
What is the Council's view of the action taken by the Dutch police?
Joint answer
The Council has never had occasion to discuss the problem to which the honourable Members refer in their questions.
In this connection, it should be pointed out that the maintenance of public order within the territory of the Member States of the European Union is exclusively the province of the police authorities within each State.
The honourable Members will therefore understand that the Council has no authority to comment on a matter which does not fall within the scope of jurisdiction allotted to it by the Treaties.
Question No 32 by Ulf Holm (H-0578/97)
Alcopop is a matter of concern to many people, primarily because it targets young people, particularly young women, more or less direcdy and has become very popular among that age group.
This is extremely alarming, as the UK and Swedish governments in particular have confirmed.
Will Luxembourg, as the country holding the Council presidency, act to put alcopop on the agenda and propose measures to prevent the spread of such drinks among young people?
Does the Council intend to take action to tighten up checks on the marketing of alcopop?
Does the Council intend to amend the existing directive that prevents alcopop from being taxed more heavily than wine and beer?
Answer
The problem of alcopops, to which the honourable Member refers, was raised by the Health Council at its meeting on 5 June, under the heading "Miscellaneous', in response to a memorandum from the Swedish delegation.
However, the Council has not at present been asked to consider any proposals on this subject which would provide a basis for the discussion of possible measures.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 33 by Charlotte Cederschiold (H-0580/97)
The Dutch Justice Minister recently proposed that organized prostitution should be legalized.
Commenting on the proposal, Mrs d'Ancona, chair of Parliament's Committee on Civil Liberties and Internal Affairs, called it 'good news for women'.
I am aware that, as yet, initiatives in this area are confined to the national level; nevertheless, all the Member States have ratified the UN Convention on the abolition of all forms of discrimination against women which stipulates, inter alia, that the signatory states shall take all possible measures to combat sexual exploitation and prostitution.
Does the Council consider that an initiative should be taken at European level with a view to reducing sexual exploitation of women and, if so, does the Council consider that there are reasons for believing that the legalization of sexual procurement would reduce prostitution?
Answer
All that the Council can say about the argument which apparently concerns the honourable Member is that it has not in fact been asked to consider any proposal envisaging the legalization of what were formerly known as brothels.
That being the case, I should particularly emphasize the fact that the Council, like the Member States, respects the United Nations Convention on the abolition of all forms of discrimination against women, of which sexual exploitation and prostitution are the sad consequences.
In this connection, it seems appropriate to remind the honourable Member that the Article 6A inserted into the draft Treaty recently adopted by the Heads of State and Government at the European Council meeting in Amsterdam lays down that:
"Without prejudice to the other provisions of the present Treaty and within the limits of the powers confened thereby on the Community, the Council, acting unanimously in response to a proposal by the Commission and after consulting the European Parliament, may take the necessary measures to combat any discrimination based on sex, race or ethnic origin, religion or belief, handicap, age or sexual orientation.'
That, it seems to me, is a sufficiently clear expression of the determination of both the Council and all the Member States to combat all forms of discrimination practised against any group of human beings.
Admittedly, the way ahead is a long one, and strewn with obstacles rooted in inherited prejudices.
But the Community and the Member States will spare no effort, when the time comes, to give substance to this declaration of principle, which indisputably represents a considerable step forward in the quest for equality.
Question No 34 by Liam Hyland (H-0581/97)
Does the Council under the Luxembourg Presidency intend to convene any meetings of ministers responsible for rural development during the next six months and what assurances can it provide that it will give the development of all aspects of rural policy priority during this period?
Answer
I would recall, in this context, that the process of the reform of the CAP which began in 1992 incorporated a search for solutions to the problems confronted by rural areas.
Although the main purpose of that reform is to improve the structure of farm production, it also takes due account of other requirements relating, for example, to the environment, farm incomes and the rural economy.
Debates of the European Parliament
I refer in particular to the resolution adopted by the European Parliament on this subject.
The Council has also been informed of the work of the Commission, which is expected to present very shortly, within the framework of its "Agenda 2000', a number of ideas designed to fuel debate within the Council on policy for rural areas.
On the same subject, in November 1996, it took due note of the conclusions of the Cork Conference, organized by the Commission. Those conclusions incorporated, among other things, several concerns that have already been expressed by the European Parliament.
The Council will of course examine them carefully, bearing in mind the very close attention that needs to be paid to the problems of the rural world.
Question No 35 by Pat Gallagher (H-0586/97)
Following the Amsterdam Summit, there have been a series of apparently conflicting press reports concerning possible new rules to resolve the quota-hopping problem.
What, if any, progress was made during the Amsterdam Summit on regulations governing fishing licences and specifically on more restrictive provisions governing the landing of catches and the place of residence of crews than cunently applies?
Answer
In the light of the conclusions published after the European Council meeting in Amsterdam, it would seem that the subject raised by the honourable Member was not discussed during the proceedings of the European Council.
In view of this, I should like to call the honourable Member's attention to the fact that quota transfers in the fisheries sector have always been regarded as an area for intergovernmental cooperation in the strictest sense.
Hence the Community institutions do not participate in discussions on this subject.
Question No 36 by Graham Watson (H-0588/97)
What proposals will the Council bring forward to deal with the problems arising from the inconsistencies in Member States' approaches to indirect taxation?
Answer
I should like to draw the attention of the honourable Member to the presidency programme presented to the ECOFIN Council in July: with regard to indirect taxation, the intention is to continue the work begun by the Dutch presidency on the Fiscalis programme and the taxing of energy-generating products.
The presidency will also be embarking on other tasks based on recent proposals by the Commission, including for example the statutes of the Committee on Excise Duties and the Value Added Tax Committee.
Debates of the European Parliament
Many of the major established organizations have long experience and a wealth of knowledge and do effective work.
When government or EU funds are made available to these organizations, it is obviously important to oversee them and require accounts but it is also entirely possible to accord them great trust within a given framework.
In my view, the system of framework agreements could be used to a much greater extent and would be very cost­effective.
Is the Council prepared to contribute towards greater use of framework agreements?
Answer
At its meeting on 5 June this year the Council approved a common position on the basic Regulation on joint financing with the non­governmental organizations.
Article 6 (4) of the common position takes up the Commission's proposal that Community co­financing should be extended to both projects and programmes: multi­year programmes, consortium actions and block grants.
In this context, the Council has taken due note of the Commission's intention to propose the setting­up of a system of multi­year programme contracts with the European NGOs within the framework of the revision of the general conditions for co­financing, which will be presented as soon as possible.
The common position will be forwarded to the European Parliament as soon as the necessary editing of the text by the legal/linguistic experts has been completed.
Questions to the Commission
Question No 53 by Luis Campoy Zueco (H­0517/97) Subject: Cooperation between DG X and DG XVI
In view of the Commission's report on the impact of culture on Community policies (Article 128), Parliament's report on the subject and the report on cohesion and culture, is the Commission planning to establish any kind of stable, lasting relationship between DG X and DG XVI?
Is it planning to introduce a proper 'culture clause' into the programmes for the new generation of regional funds?
Answer
The Commission has on several occasions showed its increasing concern about more integration of cultural aspects into the Structural Funds' operations as well as intensified cooperation between its different services.
The Commission would like to inform Parliament that an intensive and regular cooperation between the different services has already been established.
Examples are the joint formulation of the terms of reference for the pilot actions on intenegional cooperation with a cultural theme under Article 10 of the European Regional Development Fund Regulation or the cooperation of the 1st Report on the Consideration of Cultural Aspects in European Community Action.
Most notably the Communication of 'Cohesion Policy and Culture ­ A contribution to employ­ment', illustrates the important role culture already plays in the Funds' operations and highlights the scope for further action.
Further examples are the reference to culture in the Commission's guidelines to the Member States on the new Objective 2 programmes or the Commission's orientations on the occasion of the mid­term review of the programmes under Objectives 1, 3, 4, 5b and 6.
Whilst the Commission has not yet fixed its proposals on the future of the Structural Funds after 1999, a clause for culture in the new programmes does at this stage not seem appropriate.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 54 by Arlene McCarthy (H-0523/97)
Would the Commission please comment on the concerns of UK local authorities and partners that failure to spend on Rechar projects by July 1997 could not only result in the loss of funds for the 1994-96 period, but also jeopardize the Rechar allocations for 1997-99?
Answer
The guidelines for the Community Initiative RECHAR II were approved by the Commission in June 1994.
At that time, it was proposed that the Initiative would cover the years 1994-1997.
Under Article 5 of the Commission decisions adopting the United Kingdom RECHAR II Programmes, all funding within the programmes would have to be committed by December 1997, and all payments made by December 1999.
However, when the Commission decided the allocation of the Community Initiative reserve last year, additional funding was provided for RECHAR Π for the years 1998 and 1999.
The Commission indicated that the relevant Monitoring Committee could submit a formal request to the Commission for an extension of a RECHAR Π Programme.
Once a programme is extended, commitments will be possible until the end of December 1999, and final payments until the end of December 2001 at the latest
The date of July 1997 does not refer to any regulatory deadline.
However, any proposals to modify programmes, for example because of temporary underspending, should be forwarded to the Commission in good time, to enable decisions to be taken before the end of 1997.
Question No 55 by Hans Lindqvist (H-0527/97)
It is highly likely that the number of areas in the EU cunently receiving aid will be cut back in 1999.
This will also affect Sweden.
Parts of the country which at present lie within the EU's geographical target areas can only expect two more years of EU subsidies.
What is the Commission's strategy for the structural funds after 1999?
Enlargement will result in a steep rise in costs.
Will the present forms of aid disappear or does the Commission consider that a substantial increase in the EU budget with higher contributions from the Member States is the right way forward?
Answer
The regulations governing the Structural Funds and the parts of the accession protocols, for the three new Member States, relating to cohesion will continue to apply until the end of 1999.
The same is true of the eligibility of the areas cunently eligible for the regionalized objectives.
The regions of Sweden that are eligible for Objectives 2, 5b and 6 will therefore continue to benefit from the Structural Funds until the end of 1999.
With regard to the period after 1999, the Commission is in the process of adopting its "Agenda 2000' communication. This includes, among other things, the Commission's guidelines for the Community's future cohesion policy.
The President of the Commission will be presenting this communication in this House tomonow morning.
Debates of the European Parliament
Subject: Cofinanced programme for the modernization of the Greek postal service
Can the Commission explain exactly what is the problem which is preventing the start of this programme, whether it can still be overcome, and if not what will happen to the appropriations earmarked for this purpose?
Answer
The business plan for the modernization of the ELTA was drawn up during 1994-95 and approved by its Board of Management in June 1995.
The "Post Office' operational programme for 1994-99, approved by the Commission in December 1995, largely relates to the implementation of this business plan.
The reason why implementation of this operational programme has not yet really begun is that the organizational structure within the ELTA necessary to ensure the proper realization of this kind of programme of modernization is not yet in place.
It was supposed to take the form of a task force, headed by a team of experts from an international consultancy body and with the necessary expertise in the modernization of postal systems and in the implementing of major capital projects in that field.
A new proposal from the Greek authorities for implementing the modernization of the postal service is now being studied by the Commission.
However, the only solution to the cunent problems lies in positive and swift decisions and actions by the competent authorities.
Failing that, the Community funds earmarked for this operational programme could, in principle, be transfened to other programmes within the Community support framework for Greece.
Question No 57 by Richard Howitt (H-0543/97)
It was announced at the Cohesion Forum that a new-look Objective 2 may be established for the period after 1999 and that part of this funding will be dedicated towards urban projects.
Given that the Commission communication on 'An Urban Agenda' makes no reference to such a reform, can the Commissioner indicate how the eligibility criteria for the cunent URBAN Community initiative may be built into the criteria being discussed for the new Objective 2?
What preliminary assessment does she make as to whether such criteria have been successful in ensuring EU aid is effectively concentrated in tackling social deprivation in Europe's towns and cities?
Answer
The role of the Community in helping to address the social and economic problems facing Europe's urban areas has been considered by the Commission in the context of its preparation of proposals for the future financing of the Community's policies, including structural policies, for the period beginning in the year 2000.
The President of the Commission will present these proposals to Parliament on 16 July.
The Commission expects to be in a position to propose new regulations to the Council and to Parliament for the Structural Funds early in 1998, including the priority objectives and the criteria to be applied.
It is too early at this stage to speculate on the precise content of the proposed regulations.
The cunent URBAN Initiative has been a useful test-bed for any future policies addressing the problems facing Europe's cities, in relation to the identification of seriously affected areas and the kind of measures to be implemented to promote new opportunities.
Question No 58 by Josu Imaz San Miguel (H-0558/97)
Cohesion is one of the fundamental political aims of the European Union, both from a geographical point of view, with respect to the least favoured regions of the European Union, and from the point of view of social cohesion.
Unemployment is one of the factors which contribute most to the lack of both geographical and social cohesion.
The Commission is cunently setting its agenda with the financial perspectives for the year 2000 and beyond.
Does the Commission intend to maintain, or even boost, chapter 2 relating to cohesion in the 15 cunent Member States?
Will the existing objective 2 be maintained beyond the year 2000 or will high-unemployment regions constitute a priority objective for the Structural Funds?
Will the Cohesion Fund continue to be applied in the case of Member States with a per capita GNP below 90 % of the Community average, regardless of membership of EMU?
Answer
The Community's future policy priorities have been the subject of discussion in the context of the preparation by the Commission of proposals for the financial perspectives for the period beginning in the year 2000.
The President of the Commission will present these proposals to Parliament on 16 July.
In early 1998, the Commission expects to be in a position to table proposals for new regulations governing the Structural Funds and the Cohesion Fund.
It is therefore still too early to speculate on the content of future structural polices.
Nevertheless, the honourable Member is referred to the Commission's first report on cohesion, published in November 1996, which is an important input into the Commission's reflections.
In that report, the Commission emphasized the need 'to guarantee longterm support for the poorest regions' and 'to support the process of adjustment affecting different regions, local communities and social groups, to accelerate their adaption to new circumstances and to promote employment'.
Question No 64 by Simon Murphy (H-0496/97)
Subject: 'Loi Evin' and the internal market
In April the Commission closed a complaint by a major sponsor of the Football World Cup in France in 1998; the complaint stated that the French law known as the 'Loi Evin' disrupted the free movement of services in the internal market.
Does rejection of this complaint mean that the Commission considers that national bans on sponsorship by alcoholic drinks companies are proportional?
If so, will the Commission provide Parliament with full details of the factual analysis it made regarding the proportionality assessment of the ban on cross-border sponsorship services arising from the Loi Evin.
If not, can the Commission explain why the complaint was closed?
Answer
The honourable parliamentarian has asked for details regarding an infringement case that was decided on by the Commission last April about the banning in France of a cross-border sponsorship contract by an alcoholic beverage producer.
On substance each case is examined on its own merits.
Other complaints contesting the compatibility with the Treaty of this indiscriminate restriction pursuing the general interest objective of protecting public health would also lead to a proportionality assessment.
In this particular case the Commission considered that in view of the protection of public health objective pursued by the French legislator, it was not advisable to proceed with the infringement procedure.
Question No 65 by Sören Wibe (H-0497/97) Subject: Common maximum rate of VAT in the EU
Will the Commission explain its views and say what it is cunendy doing as regards proposals on the future system of VAT in the EU?
Member States' ministers responsible for VAT have rejected the idea of setting an upper limit on VAT rates in the EU.
Is the Commission planning to continue arguing in favour of such a limit and, if so, on what grounds?
Phase one and phase two of the programme on the new common system of VAT have been finalised as planned.
All relevant proposals scheduled until mid-1997 have been tabled or will be tabled very soon.
After the summer the Commission will go ahead with phase three of the programme.
It is true that the Council of Ministers has not agreed to fix a legally binding maximum level for the standard VAT rate in the form of a directive.
Instead, Member States committed themselves in a statement to the Minutes to make every effort to avoid any widening of the cunent gap between the lowest and the highest rate applied within the Community.
This meets in fact the main concern for the Commission and was the basis for the proposal to fix a maximum rate.
One of the next steps provided for in the work programme is bringing standard rates closer together.
The rationale for this is explained in the work programme, Document [(COM)328/96j\
Question No 66 by Angela Billingham (H-0502/97) Subject: Property in the Canary Islands
I refer to Commissioner Monti's response to my Question (H-0882/96) (') on the Law of Tourism 7/1995 which is due to come into force in Spain in July 1997.
The impact of the law will be to restrict the provision of services in any one complex to a single entity which would therefore have a monopoly on lettings in the complex, removing the freedom available to tourists of using competing providers of letting apartments.
Could the Commission investigate the possibility of this practice being pro-monopoly and anti-competitive?
With regard to the law's objective being to protect tourists as users of services, does the Commission not believe owners of apartments are frequent tourists who put a great deal into the local economy?
Finally, can the Commission produce any evidence that the new law will produce standards of safety, cleanliness and hygiene?
Who can say that a company managing many apartments will provide higher standards of cleanliness and decoration than an individual owner who lets out his own apartment when he or she is not using it?
Answer
It is difficult to see how the system introduced by Law 7/1995 for the management of building complexes tends to create a dominant position.
After all, the monopoly enjoyed by the body in charge of the management of a complex is confined to that complex, and so prevents that body from acquiring a dominant position in the overall market concerned, which is the tourist letting market in the Canary Islands.
Furthermore, no evidence has been produced of any agreement between these bodies, which would be contrary to the rules of competition.
As for the involvement of property owners in the local economy of the Canary Islands, that is undeniable.
A very large proportion of that economy is focused on the tourist industry, hence the objective of Law 7/1995 of "regulating the organization and promotion of the sector as a strategic economic factor'.
These property owners will be subject to certain constraints when trying to let their properties for tourist purposes, but on the other hand, when they themselves are in residence, they will benefit from the provisions of the law as receivers of services.
') Verbatim report of proceedings, 12.11.1996, p. 85.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 67 by Joan Colom i Naval (H-0531/97)
Subject: Entitlement of Community citizens and legal persons to locate businesses in EU Member States other than that in which they are domiciled
The tourist industry is a business that is important to the Spanish economy in general and the Catalan economy in particular.
A substantial part of this business sector is owned by non-Spanish Community citizens or companies; similarly, a significant minority of the Community workforce employed in the industry is also non-Spanish; finally, some establishments are located close to areas of special environmental interest or required for national defence purposes.
Is there any restriction in Spain on the entitlement of non-Spanish EU businesses or citizens to own Spanish tourist businesses?
Is there any restriction on the employment of Community citizens in this sector on grounds of their nationality?
Would such restrictions, if they did exist, be compatible with the acquis communautaire?
Answer
Pursuant to the provisions of Articles 52 and 58 of the EC Treaty, private individuals and companies established under the law of a Member State enjoy the right of freedom of establishment in the other Member States.
Companies may exercise this freedom by transferring their main place of activity or by setting up an agency, branch or subsidiary in another Member State.
Clearly, these individuals and companies must not suffer from any discrimination on the basis of their nationality; nor must they be the subject of measures which, although applied without distinction, nevertheless obstruct the exercise of their freedom of establishment.
The only measures that can be justified are those based on grounds of public order, public safety or public health, or imperative public interest considerations, proportionate to the intended objective.
As regards the existence in Spain of any restrictions on the establishment of companies or non-Spanish nationals, the Commission has received no complaints and no information about any such restrictions.
Question No 68 by Jan Andersson (H-0552/97)
At the most recent Council meeting, a majority of the Member States' Transport Ministers called for an analysis of the effects of abolishing tax-free sales.
Will the Commission carry out such an analysis?
Answer
The Commission must remind the honourable Member that the Ecofin Council, when adopting directives abolishing frontier controls for fiscal purposes, decided to put an end to tax-free sales with effect from 1 July 1999, but allowed for a transitional period which was to be "used to take the necessary steps to alleviate both the social impact in the sectors concerned and the regional problems arising, especially in the frontier regions' (recital of Directive 91/680/EEC).
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 69 by Karin Riis-Jørgensen (H-0573/97)
In a number of large Danish municipalities, the collection of household refuse is carried out by cleaning firms on the basis of longterm contracts.
In some cases, these contracts run until the years 2014 and 2020 but the work has never been put out to tender.
What is the Commission's view of the duration of these contracts in the light of the directive on the provision of services?
What steps will the Commission take to enforce the provisions of that directive in Denmark?
Answer
The question posed by the honourable Member does not contain sufficient information on which to base an answer.
In fact, it does not appear from the question whether the longterm contracts for waste collection amount to service concessions contracts, or, if such is not the case, whether these contracts were awarded before the entry into force on 1 July 1993 of Council Directive 92/50/EEC of 18 June 1992 relating to the coordination of procedures for the award of public service contracts.
Finally, the available information does not allow an assessment of whether the conditions for the application of the exception provided for under Article 6 of the directive may be met in certain cases.
It is therefore not possible to evaluate the conformity of the cases, to which the honourable Member refers, with the provisions of the directive.
If the honourable Member has additional, concrete information on the matter, she is kindly requested to forward it to the Commission in order that it may take appropriate action.
Question No 70 by Ludivina Garcia Arias (H-0576/97)
Having issued a number of constructive and semi-official warnings to the Spanish Government that the parliamentary group supporting it should bring the Law on Digital Television into line with Community law during its passage through Parliament (Articles 30 and 59 of the Treaty and Directive 95/47 (')), the Commission has now rightly decided to use the exceptional urgent procedure to commence infringement proceedings and ask the Court of Justice to take protective measures.
On how many occasions has the Court of Justice been asked to take such measures against a Member State?
Where approved by the Court, have those measures had an immediate effect in the Member States?
Question No 71 by Manuela Frutos Gama (H-0577/97) Subject: Digital television and decoders
What type of protective measures in relation to Spanish legislation on digital television and decoders does the Commission intend to seek from the Court of Justice?
Joint answer
The questions asked by the two honourable Members both relate to the same infringement proceedings.
They raise two different issues: the Commission's adoption of the urgent procedure in this case, and the possibility of seeking protective measures from the Court of Justice.
As to the adoption of the urgent procedure:
As the honourable Members have rightly pointed out, the urgent procedure is not normally employed in the administrative phase prior to proceedings for failure to act.
In actual fact, the special feature of this procedure simply amounts to requiring the Member State to reply within a shorter period than that usually allowed by the Commission.
In the case cited by the honourable Members, the Commission sent on 27 June a formal letter asking for comment, allowing the Member State fifteen calendar days within which to reply.
The Commission uses this accelerated procedure with due regard to the circumstances of each case, and its intention is always to ensure effective respect for the Treaties.
In the present instance, the Spanish law that came into force on 6 May 1997 contains provisions which produce an immediate effect on trade.
In particular, the Commission took due account of the two-month period provided for in the final paragraph of Article 7(a) of this law for it to take effect, and the three-month period envisaged by the first transitional provision.
In these circumstances, if the Commission's action was to be effective it also needed to be swift.
It may be worth recalling, in this context, that the Commission constantly endeavours to expedite infringement proceedings, as indeed it was called upon to do by the European Council in Amsterdam, which stressed the need to apply Community law actively in the Member States and to establish procedures that would enable problems to be resolved more quickly.
As to the protective measures:
It would be premature to say anything about a possible application to the Court of Justice for protective measures.
At this stage the Commission can only reply to the question in general terms.
It is true that, because of the inherent urgency of the matter,' the Commission has contemplated asking for protective measures. The reason is that action by the Commission would be fruitless if the infringement were to persist throughout the time taken to bring proceedings before the Court for failure to act.
According to Article 186 of the Treaty, the Court may order the necessary protective measures in cases brought before it.
The Commission has asked the Court for protective measures in at least eleven (11) cases, within the framework of the procedure laid down in Article 169 of the Treaty.
It has done so each time it felt that such measures were necessary and that the necessary conditions existed for them to be granted.
The Treaty imposes no specific limits on the power of the Court to grant protective measures.
If the necessary conditions exist for it to do so, the Court generally orders the Member State to take all appropriate steps to prevent the proceedings on the merits resulting in a judgment of failure to act, which has no useful effect.
Question No 72 by Concepció Ferrer (H-0473/97) Subject: Japanese leather industry
In response to the complaint lodged by the Confederation of National Tanners' Associations the Commission has opened an inquiry in order to determine whether or not the Japanese leather industry has derived benefit from operating a scheme which contravenes World Trade Organization rules and thus excludes all exports by the EU leather industry.
Other benefits enjoyed by the Japanese leather industry include subsidies to local manufacturers and de facto agreements between importers and dealers which in practice determine import and price levels in agreement with local producers.
Debates of the European Parliament
In view of the importance of the leather industry in certain EU regions, could the Commission say what stage its inquiry has reached?
The Confederation of National Tanners' Association of the Community (COTANCE) lodged a complaint under (EC) Regulation No 3286/94 of 22 December 1994 (the socalled Trade Barriers Regulation), on 24 February 1997.
The Commission decided that the complaint was admissible and an investigation was formally initiated on 9 April 1997.
Community leather exports to Japan are entitled to benefit from the tariff quotas established by Japan after a 1984 GATT panel which requested it to further open its domestic market.
However, COTANCE contends that the Japanese market is virtually closed to imports of finished leather from the European Community and that this is due to the following practices: a complex import licence allocation system leading to a poor exhaustion of tariff quotas, subsidization of local tanning industry and restrictive business practices of the Japanese importers of leather.
Under the Trade Barriers Regulation, the Commission conducts an investigation to verify whether the allegations of the complainant are well founded.
In this context, they collect information through various interested parties and verify it.
The investigation normally lasts 5 to 7 months and, ■ once it is concluded, the Commission reports to the Advisory Committee.
On this basis, a decision may be taken if an action is necessary and in the Community interest.
Thus, the Commission is now carrying out an investigation, both within the Community and Japan, into the Japanese regime of imports of leather.
A report to the Member States will be presented before mid-November.
Question No 73 by Ernesto Caccavale (H-0479/97) Subject: European Children's Rights Day
Given the growing public awareness in Europe of the issue of child protection and with reference to the Declaration of the Rights of the Child adopted by the United Nations on 20 November 1959 and ratified on 20 November 1989 by 187 countries within the UN General Assembly in connection with the International Convention on the Rights of the Child, does the Commission intend to follow the example provided by the French law of 6 April 1996 establishing a National Children's Rights Day and actively promote the establishment of an annual European Children's Rights Day, to be held for the first time on 20 November 1997?
Answer
At the present time the Commission has no plans to propose a specific initiative for a "European Children's Rights Day'.
However, the Commission is always willing to assist the progress of activities which are primarily a matter for the Member States.
Thus, the French initiative refened to in the title of the question is one of the many activities which the Commission is supporting through appropriate budget headings.
Specifically, since September 1989, in the light of the conclusions of the Council of ministers responsible for "family' policy, financial resources have been allocated to assist a better understanding of the deep-seated changes brought about by demographic trends, socioeconomic problems going hand in hand with significant changes to the family structure - increased frequency of divorce, fewer marriages, declining birth rates, increasing abuse of children within the family, trafficking in children for profit, etc.
Question No 74 by Gianfranco Dell'Alba (H-0480/97) Subject: ENEL-ENI agreement to set up a new electricity company
On 5 May 1997 ENEL and ENI (two Italian State-owned enterprises, in other words) signed a memorandum of understanding with a view to setting up a company that may become a leading player on the free market beginning to develop.
ENI and ENEL have virtual monopolies on, respectively, the market supplying gas and fuel oils and the energy generation and distribution market, and it is highly likely that an understanding of the type in question will create further
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The Commission has been informed of the signing by ENI and ENEL of a letter of intent relating to the setting-up of a joint venture to produce electricity.
As things stand at present, and having regard to the absence of any notifiable agreement between the parties, the Commission cannot make any detailed comment on this project.
However, it is reasonable to assume that if the project goes ahead it may give rise to the creation of a concentrative joint enterprise within the meaning of Council Regulation 4064/89 of 21 December 1989.
Consequently, the operation could be subject to authorization, either by the Commission or by the competent national authority, depending upon whether or not it has a Community dimension within the meaning of Regulation 4064/89.
If the Commission should be called upon to review the operation, it would of course take due account of the positions of the two parties in their respective markets and of the expected development of the electricity market before reaching its decision.
Question No 75 by Kirsten Jensen (H-0484/97)
Subject: Exports of pesticides
It has been reported in the Danish press that a Danish-owned company is selling Paraquat to Burma.
Paraquat is a toxic pesticide which is banned in Denmark and other Member States.
Does the Commission intend to take the initiative and prohibit this dangerous substance in the EU?
Is it planning to prohibit sales to nonmember countries of pesticides which are prohibited on the European market?
Does it intend to take the initiative for the introduction of legislation on product liability, pursuant to which producers can be held liable for the effects on the environment and on human health of the pesticides they sell to nonmember countries?
Answer
Paraquat is an active substance used in herbicides.
According to the information available to the Commission, it is cunently authorised for use in 10 Member States (Belgium, Germany, Spain, France, Greece, Italy, Ireland, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands and Portugal) and not authorised in the remaining five (Austria, Denmark, Finland, Luxembourg and Sweden).
Paraquat is included in Annex I of Directive 67/548/EEC concerning classification, labelling and packaging of dangerous substances. The classification has recently been reviewed by experts and a more stringent classification has been proposed.
A decision on this is scheduled to be taken later this year.
Paraquat is also cunendy being reviewed under Regulation 3600/92 dealing with the re-evaluation of all the existing active substances in plant protection products covered by Directive 91/414/EEC.
A decision on inclusion or exclusion of Paraquat in Annex I, the list of authorised active substances of Directive 91/414/EEC is expected in 1998.
The export of chemicals to third countries is regulated within the Community by Council Regulation 2455/92, which makes the voluntary international agreement on export/import of chemicals called the Prior Informed Consent (PIC), negotiated under the auspices of UNEP and FAO, binding for Member States.
This procedure allows importing countries to make informed decisions on whether to allow or not to allow the imports of a chemical which is banned or severely restricted because of its negative effects to humans or the environment.
Paraquat is not at the present time included in the PIC procedure, but it is cunendy being discussed for possible inclusion.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 76 by John Iversen (H-0485/97)
Does the Commission intend to take the initiative for the drawing up of an international code of conduct for multinational companies which produce and sell chemical substances?
Answer
The Commission is not intending to initiate moves for an international code of conduct covering all aspects of the productions and sale of chemical products.
The Commission would, however, like to draw the attention of the honourable Member that it is already extensively involved in a large number of specific actions relating to both European and international aspects of the products and sale of chemicals.
To cite but two examples, the Commission has been in the forefront of, international agreements to limit production and sales of ozone-depleting substances, in particular chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), and work with companies ensuring exports of dangerous chemicals to developing countries are made with the Prior Informed Consent.
The export of chemicals to nonmember countries if regulated within the Community by Council Regulation 2455/92, which makes the voluntary international agreement on export/import of chemicals called the Prior Informed Consent (PIC) negotiated under auspices of UNEP and FAO binding for Member States.
This procedure allows importing countries to make informed decisions on whether to allow or not to allow the imports of dangerous chemicals which are banned or severely restricted because of their negative effects to humans or the environment.
The Commission would also like to refer to its Communication, addressed to the European Parliament as well as to the Council and the Economic and Social Committee, entitled 'An industrial example', of 30 April 1996, in which it identified more than 20 specific actions, many of which would be part of such an all-embracing international code of conduct.
In particular it cites as necessary actions, and invitation to industry to develop the Responsible Care Initiative and the favouring of an international level of environmental protection in the framework of the United Nations and the OECD.
Question No 77 by Maartje van Putten (H-0486/97)
Subject: Alternatives to tests on animals
The Commission is obliged, pursuant to Directive 93/35/EEC (·) amending for the sixth time Directive 767768/EEC (2) on the approximation of the laws of the Member States relating to cosmetic products, to ban the use of experiments on animals for cosmetic purposes with effect from 1 January 1998.
However, it has the option of defenal if - 'despite all reasonable endeavours' - there has been insufficient progress in developing and validating satisfactory alternatives (Article 4(1)).
Can the Commission say in which areas there has been actual progress towards the acceptance of validated methods?
Can the Commission also indicate the timescale for obtaining acceptable alternatives and what efforts it will make to speed up the acceptance of alternatives in regulatory processes?
Subject: Alternatives to tests on animals
Can the Commission also indicate how cunent staff levels can be tailored, as soon as possible, to the extended remit of the ECVAM?
Can it also indicate how the ECVAM's current level of staff relates to the budgets agreed for the ECVAM in the longterm estimates?
Joint answer
The Commission attaches the greatest importance to the wellbeing of animals.
That is why it does everything possible to further the development validation and legal acceptance of alternatives to animal experiments in the field of cosmetic products.
At the present time, within the framework of Biotech II, it is allocating 3.8 MECU to three projects covering these alternative methods.
Also, substantial progress has been made with percutaneous absorption and with local risks to the eyes and skin.
However, these advances are not yet sufficient to result in genuine alternatives.
Adopting the principle of precaution, therefore, the Commission has adopted Directive 97/18 of 17 April 1997 which defers from 1 January 1998 to 30 June 2000 the date from which it will become unlawful for ingredients and combinations of ingredients used in the composition of cosmetic products to be tested on animals.
This period was felt to be sufficient for reliable alternative methods to be attained in the meantime.
With regard to ECVAM (the European Centre for the Validation of Alternative Methods), the Fourth Framework Programme for 1995-98, granted by the CCR of Ispra, gives it a total of 13 MECU in specific appropriations to cover, among other things, laboratory needs and, in particular, research into alternative methods suitable for cosmetics.
As far as human resources are concerned, the relative limitation on the size of the workforce in recent years is now gradually being made good by the new recruitment, starting this year, of at least five operatives for three years.
Question No 79 by Arthur Newens (H-0504/97)
In the Conference of Donor Countries to the Republic of Yemen, hosted by the Commission on 19-20 June 1997, what emphasis was given to the human rights record in that country, in the light of the recent urgency resolution adopted by Parliament and of the report by Amnesty International, which is cunendy awaiting a response from the Yemeni authorities?
Answer
The first Consultative Group Meeting for the Republic of Yemen held in Brussels on 18-19 June 1997, brought together representatives from 13 donor countries, 12 regional and international organizations and a large representation from the Yemeni private sector.
As in similar cases, this Consultative Group meeting was organized by the World Bank.
The Commission offered logistic support including Commission premises to organize the conference.
However, the Commission did neither organize nor formally host the meeting.
The Consultative Group Meeting concentrated essentially on technical issues and questions concerning details of development policy implementation.
16.7.97 and the successful implementation of free and fair parliamentary elections on 27 April 1997 which further increased donors' confidence in the Yemeni reform process.
The Commission delegation raised the question of Human Rights' problems in Yemen referring explicitly to the European Parliament Resolution of 10 April 1997.
This position was only supported by the German delegation.
No other alluded to tfiis question.
In its donor's statement the Commission offered Yemen cooperation on Human Rights matters like training and awareness building.
The Commission will continue to assist Yemen in institutionalising its multi-party democratic system of government.
Donors reiterated their strong endorsement of Yemen's reform policies and pledged further considerable technical and financial assistance, which exceeded the level expected by the World Bank at the outset of the Donors Conference.
Question No 80 by Christine Oddy (H-0513/97)
Is the Commission aware of the cunent inquiry into the murder of 24 Tamil civilians in the village of Kumarapuram in Trincomalee, Sri Lanka in February 1996 by military personnel?
Will the Commission support moves to ensure that the security concerns of remote villages like Kumarapuram in the North and East of Sri Lanka are given special attention and that regular visits are undertaken, under the auspices of the Human Rights Task Force, to record and take action on the concerns of civilians living there?
Answer
Sri Lanka is a country with which the EC has a long standing Cooperation Agreement and the Commission therefore has a deep interest in that country and would naturally like to see internal peace reestablished in it.
The situation in Sri Lanka remains a cause for serious concern for the European Commission.
The Commission is indeed aware of the murder of 24 civilians in Kumarapuam and the investigation into this unhappy affair ordered by the Government of Sri Lanka.
The last session of the responsible Court was on 25 June 1997 and the next one will be held on 30 July 1997.
Unfortunately killing of civilians has been part of the conflict since it started in 1983.
All ethnic communities have suffered from it and all parties to the conflict have been responsible for these acts.
A few months after the above mentioned incident, 14 Sinhalese civilians were killed by the LITE in a remote village near Wilpattu on 11 June 1996.
The only way to protect the civilians would be if all parties to the conflict would undertake to respect the principles of International Humanitarian Law and to find a negotiated settlement to the conflict.
The Sri Lankan Government established in March 1997 a Human Rights Commission with a wide mandate to monitor the human rights situation in the country.
The European Union has in its declarations and also in demarches made to the Government in Colombo urged the Government to protect the civilians and to prevent the violations of International Humanitarian Law.
Question No 81 by Caroline Jackson (H^-0514/97)
Is it aware that EU action originally necessitated by the microbiological contamination of a cooked ready-to-eat product is now unreasonably being translated into controls on all products, including raw frozen product imported into the EU for subsequent processing?
It was decided, therefore, to ban imports of fresh fishery products from India and to submit consignments of frozen and processed fishery products to a microbiological test.
This is the reason to make references, in the recitals of Decision 97/334, to fresh, frozen and processed products and to adopt different measures for products treated in different ways.
The Commission is now evaluating the possibility to authorize the entry into the Community of fresh, frozen products, without microbiological tests, if submitted direcdy after importation to a heat treatment in an EC approved establishment.
If such treatment could assure that the products do not present a microbiological hazard for public health, then the Commission will propose to amend the Decision 97/334 to introduce a derogation for fresh, frozen products in the conditions described.
Question No 82 by José Escudem (H-0516/97)
Subject: Budget heading B3-2003 'shopping list'
Could the Commission say what projects were financed in 1995 and 1996 under budget heading B3-2003?
Could it also say what the selection criteria are for the projects which receive finance and how the funds available under the heading in question can be secured?
Answer
The budget heading B3-2003 "Other cultural schemes implemented in the Community and in cooperation with third countries' had an appropriation of 1.950 MECU in 1995 and 6 MECU in 1996.
50 % of the funds were allocated to heritage projects, in accordance with the rules of the "Raphael' pilot scheme involving third countries.
The selection procedure followed the same rules as the pilot scheme itself: invitation to tender; preselection in accordance with the specifications; selection by a jury of independent experts representing all the Member States.
50 % of the funds were allocated to projects of the "Kaleidoscope' type - emblematic schemes reacting to Parliament's comments on the budget, which determined the beneficiaries, and the sums appropriated.
In 1996 these schemes represented 80 % of the appropriations.
The remainder were allocated to projects relating to associate States of Central Europe, in response to proposals from Commission delegations to those countries and on the basis of the cost-effectiveness criteria applied to the Kaleidoscope programme.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 83 by Michael Elliott (H-0520/97) .
Subject: Animal experimentation - primates
The Commission will be aware of the publicity sunounding the statement by Professor Weissman that primates should be used to research the possible link between BSE and humans and the subsequent suggestion by two Dutch scientists that there were many chimpanzees available at the BPRC in the Netherlands for such research.
In view of the enormous public protest about the moral and scientific unacceptability of canying out BSE transmission research on chimpanzees, would the Commissioner give an assurance that not only would no EU funding be made available but also the Commission will argue strongly against such research?
Answer
In connection with Community research programmes, the Commission's policy has been for the Fourth Framework Programme, and will be for the Fifth Framework Programme, to restrict experiments on animals to cases where they cannot be avoided.
Furthermore, the Commission encourages research designed to develop alternative methods, especially through the European Centre for the Validation of Alternative Methods (ECVAM), located at Ispra in Italy.
When experiments on animals cannot be avoided, they must be carried out in strict compliance with the etiîical and hygienic standards in force in the country where the research takes place.
The wellbeing of the animals must be taken into account in the writing of the research records.
These principles apply, a fortiori, to experiments on non-human primates.
Thus, Directive 86/609/EEC stipulates that there must be strong scientific justification for such experiments.
Furthermore, the Biomedical Primate Research Centre in the Netherlands bars any lethal experiments or experiments causing permanent harm to the animals.
Experiments on primates in the context of BSE research, on condition that primates are suitable for research into prion diseases, would only be conceivable in the event of serious risks to public health and would be subject to rigorous methodological and ethical scrutiny.
Question No 84 by Raul Rosado Fernandes (H-0521/97) Subject: Marketing of cereals (EPAC - Portugal)
The EPAC, a State-owned company, which formerly held a monopoly of the market in cereals in Portugal, received a State contribution of Esc 30 bn, after obtaining a loan of Esc 50 bn to compensate for a management of the firm which brought about a deficit of the same amount in addition to liabilities in the 1996 financial year amounting to Esc 6 bn.
Competing companies asked the Commission a question on this subject.
The Commission responded by instructing the Portuguese Government immediately to suspend the effects of this State guarantee in respect of all commercial activities undertaken by the EPAC in the cereals sector.
Despite this warning, the EPAC tendered on 15 May 1997, at the taxpayer's expense and on unfair terms for other companies operating in the sector, for 150 000 tonnes of maize imported from third countries.
Having bid Esc 1 550 more per tonne than its rivals, it was awarded the contract.
How long is the Commission prepared to tolerate this illegal situation?
What measures can it take to penalize the guilty party, namely Portugal, whose attitude is detrimental both to cereal companies and to stock farmers?
Answer
On 9 July 1997, the Commission adopted a final decision in which it ruled against the aid in the form of a guarantee granted by the Portuguese state to the EPAC.
Consequently, Portugal is required to cancel the guarantee within a maximum period of two weeks.
The Commission held that this State guarantee was illegal because it was granted in breach of the Treaty rules on competition - in other words, before the Commission had been able to give its decision.
In addition, the Commission found that this State guarantee failed to comply with any conditions that could be regarded as compatible with the rules of the Common Market.
The Commission also acknowledged that the measure was likely to produce a particularly destructive impact on the cereals market.
For this reason, and in the absence of any action by the Portuguese authorities to eliminate the risks of distortion of competition, the Commission had previously adopted, on 30 April 1997, a decision requiring Portugal to suspend the effects of this State guarantee.
In cases such as this, involving national aid which is held to be incompatible with the Common Market, the Court of Justice has on several occasions found that the Commission may oblige the Member State to recover from the beneficiaries all the aid unlawfully granted.
Consequendy, and as the case involved a State guarantee whose effect was to subsidize the rate of interest on loans taken out by the EPAC, the Commission felt, in reaching the decision refened to above, that the financial advantage that had been improperly received and would have to be repaid conesponded to the difference between the Community reference rate and the rate actually paid by the EPAC on these loans.
Question No 85 by Bernie Malone (H-0522/97) Subject: Flight-time limitations
Can the Commission provide information about the consultations it is holding as part of its preparations for a draft directive on flight-time limitations and indicate when it is likely to come forward with a formal proposal?
In view of the failure - over a period of five years - of the Joint Aviation Authorities to agree common rules for the regulation of flight time and rest requirements for flying crew, the Commission organized a Forum in June 1996 to assess the situation as perceived by national regulators, air operators and relevant staff.
During this meeting it was apparent that there was still a wide consensus on the need to elaborate common rules in this area in order to ensure the safety of air operations and it was also clearly felt that the Commission should make further efforts to understand the points of dissent and to bridge positions in order to establish common standards, or at least a common basis, for regulating the safety aspects of flight time and rest of crew.
A task force was created for this purpose composed of experts representing the various views in this field, and it me several times between August 1996 and May 1997.
Unfortunately, despite the goodwill and the frank attitudes of all involved in this work, it has not been possible to reach a consensus on what should be a Community scheme in this domain.
It was, therefore, agreed during the last meeting that the Commission should make its own assessment in the light of the material gathered and the contributions received throughout these nine months of work, and it should come forward with a proposal.
The Commission is now preparing such a proposal and intends to discuss it in the task force by the end of July.
The Commission hopes to be able to formalise its position by the end of the year.
Question No 86 by James Fitzsimons (H-0524/97) Subject: Church of Scientology
Has the Commission any powers to investigate the activities of the Church of Scientology within the European Union?
Does it consider there are any mechanisms within the existing Treaty to ban such organizations from operating in the European Union or, if not, does the new Amsterdam Treaty provide the necessary mechanisms to deal with such cults and sects?
Answer
The Commission does not have any investigative powers concerning activities of sects at European level and does not collect specific information about the Church of Scientology.
The Commission is not empowered to pass judgment on the legitimacy or merits of organizations of this kind.
The choice of whether or not to joint them is a matter for the individual and is guaranteed by the freedom of thought and of association.
Should it appear necessary, appropriate measures could be taken under Title VI of the Treaty on European Union to improve judicial and police cooperation on this issue.
Existing tax and labour laws might also come into play, though it is, of course, for the Member States to enforce them.
Other provisions are especially relevant in that they provide for a regime to protect particular interests, such as minors.
The Amsterdam Treaty provides for more specific provisions on police and judicial cooperation, but does not refer explicitly to the phenomenon of cultus and sects.
It also specifies in a declaration to be annexed to the new Treaty, that the 'Union will respect and does not prejudice the status under national law of churches and religious associations or communities in the Member States.
The Union will equally respect the status of philosophical and non-confessional organizations.'
Subject: Trade agreement with South Africa
Despite negotiations, no trade agreement has yet been concluded between the EU and South Africa.
A trade agreement could benefit South Africa as it might result in a boost to exports and jobs, particularly in the agricultural sector.
What efforts is the Commission making to give South Africa the most advantageous terms possible for its agricultural exports to the EU?
Answer
Negotiations on a bilateral Trade and Cooperation Agreement between the European Community and the Republic of South Africa began in June 1995.
The proposed agreement will be of a comprehensive nature, covering all areas of mutual interest including agriculture.
It will also include provisions leading to the gradual establishment of a Free Trade Area between the Parties.
In March 1996, the Community presented its detailed proposals regarding the trade chapter of the agreement.
The South African side formally responded to these proposals in January 1997 and provided supplementary elements of their position during the most recent round of talks, in June 1997.
Further work is required on both sides.
In the meantime, many points of principle as to the scope and nature of a Free Trade Agreement have been agreed between the Community and South Africa in terms of cooperation and improved market access.
Both parties have agreed that reciprocal liberalization measures should be taken, in accordance with WTO principles and taking account of each other's sensitivities.
Since July 1996, South Africa is a full beneficiary of the revised agricultural GSP.
It is the Commission's intention to ensure that the future Trade and Cooperation agreement will provide further market access opportunities for South Africa, both in the industrial and agricultural sector.
Question No 88 by Astrid Thors (H-0532/97)
Subject: Storage of radioactive waste in Estonia
There are reports of a major storage facility in Sillamäe, Estonia for waste from a factory that manufactured products containing uranium.
The storage site is one of the world's largest constructed entirely on the earth's surface.
A fragile protective embankment only 10 metres wide separates the storage area from the Baltic Sea which is constantly eroding it.
According to expert statements, not even the old safety requirements of the Soviet era are respected.
If the site's walls cave in, the Baltic Sea will be faced with the worst disaster that has ever occuned in the area.
Is the Commission aware of the gravity of the situation and what measures is it planning to take to eliminate the risk of such a major disaster, e.g. under PHARE or its nuclear safety programme?
Answer
The European Commission is fully aware of the radioactive waste deposit at Sillamäe in Estonia.
The figures quoted by the honourable Member of Parliament (1, 830 tonnes of uranium as part of 12 million tonnes of radioactive waste) are estimates, and therefore not necessarily proven to be true at this stage: the Commission's information is that whereas the 1, 830 tonnes of uranium are possible, it is a high estimate.
The 12 million tonnes of radioactive waste are almost certainly a large overestimate.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 89 by Ulf Holm (H-0534/97)
The cunent free trade agreement between the EU and Mexico is shortly due to be replaced by a new agreement.
Reports from Mexico say that the free trade agreements with both the EU and NAFTA have had adverse effects, inter alia in the Mexican countryside.
Local production in Mexico is being wiped out by large-scale, intensive agricultural production which is often run with numerous subsidies, using artificial fertilizer and chemical pesticides.
In the light of the above, will the Commission say whether it has carried out any analysis of the social and environmental impact, for example, of the present free trade agreement between the EU and Mexico?
Does the Commission intend to replace the present free trade agreement with an even more comprehensive agreement?
If so, in what sectors?
Answer
In July 1996 the Commission evaluated the conformity of a possible Free Trade Agreement with Mexico - which does not exist yet - with WTO rules, as well as studying the effect of a possible FTA on the Community common policies, and its other partners.
These studies were those required by the Council's Conclusions of 22 June 1995.
The Commission will shortly prepare the further studies required by the Council's Conclusions of 29 April 1997.
These will address the extent to which the agreement will achieve Community economic interest the political and other benefits of the agreement, whether it would support die development of multilateral trading systems, its overall economic effect and its impact on the Community's other external commitments.
Subject: EU negotiations with Mexico on a trade and cooperation agreement
Does the Commission agree that it would have been better if it had negotiated with the Mexican Government after the elections rather than just before?
If not, why not?
In my opinion, it would have been better if Mexico had first been able to show through elections that it was ready for the agreement.
There were, moreover, too many ÜTegularities in the run-up to the elections.
Why did the Commission take it upon itself to depart from the standard wording of the democracy and human rights clause?
Doing so, after all, gives the wrong signal, especially in view of the Mexican Government's reluctance to accept a democracy and human rights clause.
Answer
The Commission regrets that it is unable to share the honourable Member's opinion regarding the timing of the negotiations with Mexico.
First, the following points should be borne in mind:
- that the two parties, in the Solemn Declaration of Paris on 2 May 1995, had confirmed their desire to conclude an agreement at an early date, that the negotiating brief had been approved by the Council in June 1996, that the first session of negotiations had taken place in October 1996,
- - - and that the Council had, in April 1996, approved supplementary guidelines allowing negotiations to be resumed.
Debates of the European Parliament
Secondly, it transpired that the Mexican Government for reasons of internal politics, prefened to conclude the negotiations before the elections, and that it therefore demonstrated greater flexibility than might have been assumed, particularly in accepting, as regards human rights, enacting terms (Article 1, "fundamental aspects' clause - Article 58, "non-performance article' - Article 39, cooperation on human rights) which go very much further than in the Community's other agreements.
It is not conect to say that the Commission departed in the negotiations from the standard clause on democracy and human rights.
In this context, it should be recalled that:
- the text provisionally accepted by the Commission for Article 1, reading "... shall inspire the policies of the parties...' rather than "... internal and external policies ...' does not, according to the confirmed opinion of the legal services of the Commission and the Council, constitute a departure from the negotiating brief,
- while defending this argument, the Commission prefened not to initial this text, in order to have the opportunity to consult all the Member States, especially with regard to this Article 1,
- when it became apparent that the Member States insisted that the text of Article 1 should remain unchanged, Mexico, in evaluating the overall result of the negotiations, declared its willingness to reconsider its position on this point.
It is thus highly probable that negotiations will very shortly end in a result that is entirely satisfactory for the Community as far as the advancement of human rights and democracy in Mexico is concerned.
Question No 91 by MaLou Lindholm (H-0535/97)
Subject: Follow-up to the Lindholm report on equal treatment and non-discrimination with regard to the European Communities' staff regulations and conditions of employment
In its opinion (904(CNS)) on Parliament's amendments to COM(96)77, the Commission notes that, whilst the substance of the amendments is not being called into question, it is not an appropriate time, after four years' of legislative procedure, to incorporate them.
Given that a new Article 6a was added to the EC Treaty and existing Articles 2 and 3 were supplemented at Amsterdam, authorizing the taking of appropriate action to combat discrimination based for instance on sex and sexual orientation and requiring the Communities to promote equality between men and women, will the Commission state the grounds on which it considers this not to be an appropriate time to incorporate the amendments Parliament adopted by a large majority, when those amendments were intended to help fulfil the objectives of the Treaty and the undertakings entered into as regards employees of the EU?
Answer
First, the Commission must make it clear that it has always systematically opposed any form of discrimination, including discrimination based on sexual orientation, and that the amendment to the staff regulations proposed by the Commission envisages, inter alia, an explicit provision in Article la to the effect that officials shall be entitled, under the application of the regulations, to equality of treatment without direct or indirect reference to, for example, their sexual orientation.
This amendment is fully consistent with the decisions of the Amsterdam Council, which proposed amending the Treaty establishing the European Community by inserting a new Article 6A relating to non-discrimination and amending Articles 2 and 3 on equality between men and women.
With regard to the amendments to the Lindholm report adopted by the European Parliament, those amendments refened to, among other things, recognition in the staff regulations of the situation of unmanied couples, whether homosexual or heterosexual, whereas at present the provisions of the regulations govern only the family status founded on marriage.
These amendments went far beyond the intentions and objectives stated in those conclusions of the Amsterdam Council to which the honourable Member refened in her oral question.
The Commission has recently adopted measures favourable to unmarried couples, both officials and other staff.
Those measures involve an initial recognition of cohabitation situations and the granting to those concerned of certain administrative, non-statutory and financially neutral facilities.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 92 by Marianne Eriksson (H-0539/97) Subject: Homosexuals in the EU institutions
One of the Commission's arguments for not incorporating Parliament's amendment concerning equal rights of partners irrespective of sex and sexual orientation in its proposal to amend the Communities' staff regulations and conditions of employment - despite the express objective of the proposal being to combat discrimination between staff - has been the financial burden on the Communities that would result from implementing Parliament's amendment.
Has the Commission assessed the financial implications?
If so, is the cost so high that the Commission considers that the principle of equal treatment should not apply to all EU staff?
Has the Council been notified of the assessment and the arguments it has produced?
Answer
In general terms, the Commission considers that no budgetary justification can be cited in support of maintaining discriminatory treatment that is contrary to the general principles of law.
The Commission must make it clear that it has always systematically opposed any form of discrimination, including discrimination based on sexual orientation, and that the amendment to the staff regulations for officials and other staff of the Community proposed by the Commission and cunently being studied by the Council envisages, inter alia, an explicit provision in Article la to the effect that officials shall be entitled, under the application of the regulations, to equality of treatment without direct or indirect reference to, for example, their sexual orientation.
At present, the staff regulations for Community officials and other staff only take married couples into account for the purposes of granting certain forms of compensation and allowances.
Extending the availability of these benefits to unmanied couples, whether homosexual or heterosexual, as envisaged in certain amendments to the Lindholm report adopted by the European Parliament, was a measure designed not to eliminate a de facto form of discrimination but to treat in the same way two situations that are legally different. One of those situations is cohabitation and the other is marriage, and marriage alone is at present taken into account in the staff regulations.
On the basis of certain projections undertaken by the Commission, initial estimates showed that granting to homosexual or heterosexual couples certain benefits cunently reserved for married couples would result in a quite substantial increase in the burden on the institutions' budgets.
In view of the impossibility of obtaining sufficiently accurate figures in this area, the results of these estimates have not been released.
However, it is essentially for the legal reasons mentioned earlier that the Commission has maintained its proposal as it stands, which does not exclude the possibility that the amendments proposed by the European Parliament may be taken into account in due course, when the amended version of the staff regulations is considered in broader terms.
Question No 93 by Heidi Hautala (H-0544/97) Subject: Criteria for determining sites to be included in the Natura 2000 network
Opinions have been drawn up in Finland concerning the country's proposal for the areas to be included in the Natura 2000 environmental protection programme.
Various authorities, including the Ministry of Transport, are proposing that the entire Natura programme should be the subject of an environmental impact assessment under the terms of the corresponding law and that the impact of the programme on the longterm planning of the transport network should be determined.
Does the Commission regard it as appropriate that a state authority should be recommending criteria for determining sites to be included in the Natura 2000 network which are at odds with the case law of the Court of Justice of the European Communities as reflected in its judgment in Case C 44/95 (the socalled Lappel Bank case)?
Is the Commission aware that in their opinions the local authorities, including the City of Helsinki, are already expressing reservations at this stage with regard to road and port construction projects,
Debates of the European Parliament
16.7.97 disregarding the fact that, as a result, the third most important water bird habitat in the Gulf of Finland would be excluded from the proposal, although there is a very strong case for its inclusion on ornithological grounds?
Can the Commission confirm the criteria for the establishment of the Natura 2000 network?
Answer
The first question relates to the criteria to be used when selecting sites for Natura 2000 network.
It is understood that the Finnish conservation authorities accept that only biological criteria in accordance with the provisions of Annex ΙΠ (Stage 1) of the Habitat Directive and the Santona and Lappel Bank judgments should be used in site selection and when setting site boundaries.
It is for the Ministry of the Environment to see that when the national list of sites is now further developed for government decision on the basis of comments received, inappropriate arguments do not influence this process.
The second part of the question refers to comments of the City of Helsinki pertaining to one particular site, Porvarinlahti Bay, which is proposed to be on the national list.
There is a complaint concerning this site (P-97/4249) mainly because its boundaries are claimed to exclude an area in order to facilitate a proposed major harbour development.
The ornithological value on the basis of the Birds Directive of this site is well established and it has been proposed in the draft national list.
The City of Helsinki will be well aware of the procedure according to Birds and Habitats Directives in this matter.
It is again for the Finnish Ministry of the Environment to see that this matter will be dealt with in accordance with the provisions of Annex ΠΙ (Stage 1) of the Habitats Directive and the Santona and Lappel Bank judgments.
Taking account of socioeconomic matters is again possible afterwards for proposed sites following the procedure laid down in Article 6(3) and (4) of the Habitats Directive.
The third part of the question asks whether the Commission can confirm the criteria for the establishment of the Natura 2000 network.
The Commission can indeed confirm that the criteria to be used are the specific scientific criteria provided in Annex ΙΠ of the Habitats Directive.
Question No 94 by Raymonde Dury (H-0546/97) Subject: Disproportion in the allocation of European grants to Belgium minimum wage earners
A representative of the European Commission is a member of the monitoring committee for the federal programme in respect of Objective 3 and the European Social Fund, chaired by the Minister for Employment and Labour.
Would the Commission state whether its representative mentioned in his reports the clear imbalance between the proportion of disadvantaged persons in the various regions of Belgium and the number of projects approved over the past ten years for each of these regions?
If so, what measures has he put forward to offset this obvious imbalance and what decisions will the Commission take?
Answer
The Commission is aware of the concerns raised by the honourable Member regarding possible unequal distribution of the finances managed by 'TOK-EFD', the NGO promoter managing the Objective 3 Operational Programme of the European Social Fund, under the framework established by the federal Ministry of Labour and Employment.
In considering this matter two particular points need to be borne in mind.
First, Hainaut is an Objective 1 region and is not eligible for funding through the federal programme for Objective 3.
This has an impact on the geographical distribution figures for Objective 3.
Second, no requests for funding from the Public Centres for Social Aid (CPAS) of Wallonia were refused by TOK-EFD prior to November 1996.
In December 1996 the Commission first learned of a refusal and ananged a meeting with the management of TOKEFD.
The subject was also added to the agenda of the next meeting of the
Debates of the European Parliament
Monitoring Committee for the federal Operational Programme.
Question No 95 by Bernd Posselt (H-0548/97)
What opportunities cunently exist for cultural cooperation in the Euro-regions between the EU and the countries of Central and Eastern Europe, and where is the Commission placing the emphasis for the future in this sphere, not least where the cultivation of minorities languages is concerned?
Answer
The PHARE programme for the countries of Central and Eastern Europe has made it possible to finance cultural cooperation activities through programmes such as the Cross-Border and Partnership programmes and a few national programmes, for example in Bulgaria and Romania.
The PHARE programme was organized on a demand-driven basis.
That means that the projects financed are, in the main, those submitted by the countries in question.
It must be acknowledged that, with a few exceptions, cultural cooperation has not yet been identified as a priority.
With more specific reference to the protection of linguistic minorities, the Democracy programme allows the financing of schemes to support ethnic minorities.
Such projects often incorporate action to defend the cultural and linguistic heritage.
In the context of the preaccession strategy, the Community cultural and audiovisual programmes will be opened up for participation by the candidate States on the basis of specific anangements laid down in the additional protocols to the European agreements, thus allowing closer cooperation between them and the Member States, based on the acquis communautaire and the Community's priorities in the cultural and audiovisual fields.
Subject: Operational programme for combating exclusion from the labour market
The operational programme for combating exclusion from the labour market submitted by Greece for the period 1994-1999 and approved by the Commission is a valuable multi-dimensional approach consisting of a large number of complementary measures designed to meet the challenges of poverty and social exclusion.
In view of the high levels of poverty in Greece as a whole, and as the social categories frequently excluded from the support and protection networks are faced with the most acute forms of marginalization, will the Commission state why there is such a low take up rate for the programme and whether there are any thoughts of amending it with a view to strengthening or improving the existing structures?
Answer
As already mentioned in the Commission answer to the honourable Member's written question 586/96, the low credit absorption so far of the Operational Programme 'Combating Exclusion from the Labour Market' is mainly due to the difficulties in applying the Operational Programme's innovative approach as well as difficulties during the 199596 period in particular to establish and apply systems and procedures that would guarantee more effective implementation.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 97 by Niall Andrews (H-0553/97)
Subject: Irish language
Can the Commission confirm that the proposal to add a new third subparagraph to Article 8d of the TEC following the Amsterdam Summit will mean that individual citizens will have the right to write to the Commission and all other Institutions in the Irish language and to receive a reply in Irish?
Answer
As the Amsterdam Treaty is not in force and has not been formally concluded, the Commission believes it would be premature at this stage for it to interpret any provisions of that Treaty.
Question No 98 by Tommy Waidelich (H-0557/97)
Subject: Disruption of the internal market
For some time there have been serious problems with ships flying flags of convenience within the internal market.
Certain countries have attempted to build up fleets at the expense of other countries by granting tax concessions.
This problem has intensified recently since the Netherlands and Norway slashed taxes in the shipping industry.
I have also been informed that there is the same risk of 'flags of convenience' in the road haulage business to the advantage of countries with lighter taxation.
Such measures disrupt the pattern of transport within the Union and have a negative impact on employment in some Member States.
Does the Commission consider that preferential treatment of certain sectors through individual countries offering tax concessions distorts competition in the internal market and does it intend to take the necessary measures to remedy these problems?
Answer
The flagging out of ships formerly registered in EC Member States to socalled flags of convenience has had a negative effect on the development of the Community's maritime industry for many, years.
Studies have identified differences in fiscal treatment between flags of convenience on the one hand and EC registers as being the main distortive factor.
Member States have responded to this situation by drawing up support schemes for their national fleets.
In order to promote a safe and competitive Community fleet with the employment of the highest possible number of Community seafarers, and to increase the effectiveness of the national schemes, the Commission has recently issued revised State aid guidelines for aid to shipping.
These guidelines allow Member States to introduce fiscal relief measures for their national shipping sectors.
The conditions for such aid and the reasons behind the Commission's policy are explained in more detail in the revised guidelines.
The Commission is not aware of the existence of a comparable situation in the road haulage sector. ' *
Question No 99 by Alexandres Alavanos (H-0559/97)
Subject: Refusal of the Turkish-Cypriot regime to allow Eleni Foka to return to the occupied part of Cyprus
On 27.5.1997 Eleni Foka, a Greek-Cypriot teacher from Karpasia, went to the free area of Cyprus in order to receive medical treatment.
Since 13.6.1997 when she wished to return to her home, her school and her pupils in the occupied tenitories, she had been denied access, because she refuses to sign a declaration that she 'recognises' the Turkish-Cypriot 'state' of northern Cyprus.
Will the Commission say whether it is aware of this incident and what action it intends to take to enable this Greek-Cypriot teacher to return home?
Answer The Commission is aware of the situation which the honourable Member has described.
According to the information received by the Commission, the United Nations, which has jurisdiction in matters associated with the enclaves in the northern part of Cyprus, is cunently endeavouring to find a solution to this problem.
The facts recounted by the honourable Member show the importance of reaching a political settlement to the Cyprus question.
Face-to-face talks between Mr Clerides and Mr Denktash began, under the aegis of the UN, on 9 July, and the Commission hopes that these discussions will allow progress to be made towards a political solution.
As the Commission said previously, in its answer to Mr Kaklamanis's written question no 2294/97, a successful outcome to the discussions between Mr Clerides and Mr Denktash will facilitate the negotiations concerning Cyprus's accession to the Community, which are to begin shortly.
The prospect of Cyprus's accession to the Union offers, in fact, a unique opportunity to find a solution to the Cyprus problem.
Accession will benefit both communities in the island; among other things, it will enable problems such as that raised by the honourable Member to be solved in accordance with European principles and legislation.
Question No 100 by Hugh Kerr (H-0562/97) Subject: EU-Australia Cooperation Agreement
Why has the Commission allowed the Australian Government off the hook regarding human rights by agreeing to sign the Political Declaration with Australia, rather than the Framework Agreement which was first envisaged?
Is the Commission not concerned that this may set a precedent for countries who want to avoid a commitment to human rights when negotiating cooperation agreements with the European Union?
Answer
The European Community and its Member States adopted a Joint Declaration with Australia which was signed in Luxembourg on 26 June 1997.
This Joint Declaration reflects the common resolve to cooperate across the many areas in which Australia and the EU have shared interests, to strengthen relations and to endow them with a longterm perspective.
The Declaration underlines the common goal of building upon partnership in order to support democracy, the rule of law and respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms.
The European Union and Australia will consult bilaterally and within the framework of the relevant bodies of the UN on human rights issues in general and, in particular, on how to advance their shared objectives of promoting human rights internationally.
During negotiations aimed at the conclusion with Australia of a Framework Agreement on Trade and Cooperation, this common commitment to the respect and enhancement of human rights was never put into doubt.
Australia questioned, however, the inclusion of provisions enabling a party to take appropriate measures, which may consist in the termination or suspension of the agreement, in case of a human rights infringement.
Following the Council decision of 29 May 1995, these provisions are included in all Community agreements with third countries.
The difference of views on this matter was one of the main reasons that made it impossible to agree on a Framework Agreement with Australia.
Since the Joint Declaration is not legally binding on either side, the above-mentioned provisions do not apply to it.
The EU was not prepared to agree to the exclusion of a binding human rights clause from a Framework Agreement.
Consequently no precedent has been set for countries which may want to avoid binding commitments on human rights.
It is therefore not appropriate to speak of letting the Australian Government off the hook.
Question No 101 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0564/97) Subject: Protection of cultural sites and monuments from commercial exploitation
The Greek Tourist Board (EOT) has already begun to cede ancient theatres to private individuals for commercial exploitation, and limited companies are being set up to manage all its property assets.
Will the Commission say whether the cession of sites and monuments of worldwide historical and archaeological importance to private companies with the aim of generating profit is at odds with resolutions and decisions taken by the European Parliament and the EU in favour of the preservation of the cultural heritage, and outline the measures it intends to take to prevent their exploitation to the detriment of the interests of society as a whole?
Answer
As the honourable Member is aware, within the framework of the principle of subsidiarity and according to Article 128 of the EC Treaty, the protection of the national cultural heritage is the exclusive responsibility of the national competent authorities.
The role of the Community and the Commission in particular is to encourage cooperation between Member States through programmes which aim at preserving and enhancing the common European cultural heritage while at the same time they respect the national and regional cultural diversities.
In this context, the leasing of archaeological sites and of the ancient theatres to private concerns by the Greek authorities does not in any way contravene any Community regulation.
The Commission is certain that the Greek competent authorities will ensure that stringent rules are put in place so that any private lease holder will be obliged to undertake measures for the protection, maintenance and enhancement of the archaeological sites in question as well as to maintain the general public's easy access.
Question No 102 by Ioannis Theonas (H-0565/97)
Subject: Need for measures to prevent a monopoly in the international aviation industry by the merger of Boeing and McDonnell Douglas
The decision taken by the American administration unconditionally to approve the merger of Boeing and McDonnell Douglas which establishes a worldwide monopoly will have adverse effects for the aviation industry and employment, but also for smaller airlines which will have to face not only a trade monopoly but also the problem of exclusive cooperation contracts such as Boeing has already introduced.
It will also have particularly adverse consequences for the defence industry of European countries.
Will the Commission say what measures it intends to take to protect the European aviation and defence industry, European airlines and, above all, consumers who will be required to bear the increased cost caused by a monopoly in this market?
Answer
As the Honourable MEP knows, the concentration has been notified to the Commission on 18 February 1997.
The Commission initiated proceedings under the Merger Control Regulation, in order to see whether the notified concentration would create or strengthen a dominant position.
A Statement of Objections has been sent out on 21 May 1997.
A final deadline for a Commission decision is 31 July 1997.
With regard to the notified operation, the Commission has identified three areas of competition concerns, that is:
Debates of the European Parliament
- the already existing and possible further exclusive agreements with major airlines and their unprecedented duration,
On the contrary of the civil side, the investigation did not reveal dominance concerns in the defence and military sector.
Under the Merger procedure, the Parties may suggest'modifications to the initial concentration plan, with a view to supplying appropriate remedial solutions.
The Commission is cunently exploring whether Boeing would offer remedies likely to remove the competition problems identified.
If adequate remedies are not agreed on in respect to these competition problems, the Commission would then have no other choice than to prohibit the concentration as incompatible with the common market.
Adequate remedies could eventually trigger a conditional clearance by the Commission.
An antitrust investigation has also been carried out in the USA by the Federal Trade Commission.
Throughout the proceedings, in compliance with the bilateral cooperation agreement on competition policy, the European Commission and the FTC have carried out all necessary notifications.
They have in particular informed each other on all important steps of the procedure.
The fact that on 1 July 1997, the FTC has reached a majority decision not to oppose the merger does not alter the serious concerns expressed by the European Commission.
As the criteria laid down in the European and the US merger control systems do not match in all their aspects, it can never be excluded that two competition authorities reach a different conclusion in an individual case.
In such a situation, the merging companies would have to respect the negative decision by the one authority, since the positive decision by the other does not mean that the parties must carry out the merger.
In practice, however, the companies concerned try to satisfy both competition authorities.
Question No 103 by Mary Banotti (H-0566/97)
Is the Commission aware that Eurostar requires its passengers to be on the UK electoral roll to qualify for the Rewards Programme?
For example an EU citizen living and working in the UK and travelling frequendy to mainland Europe via the Eurostar is discriminated against.
Can the Commission investigate whether this proviso infringes Community regulations?
Answer
The Commission is aware that the Eurostar Frequent Traveller Programme, which was introduced in autumn 1996, was initially launched in the UK, France and Belgium, because the company considered these countries as its main market.
The Programme is not restricted to those holding British, French or Belgian citizenship or who appear on the relevant electoral roll.
The only provision is a mailing address in one of these Member States.
The Commission has been informed that, in the near future, there shall be an extension of the Eurostar Frequent Traveller Programme to other than the above mentioned Member States due to the development of the market.
Question No 104 by Marie-Paule Kestelijn-Sierens (H-0569/97) Subject: Results of research into the effectiveness of homeopathy and other therapeutic methods
Can the Commission indicate how the funding available since 1994 for research into homeopathic and other therapeutic methods has been utilized, what precise research has been carried out and whether such research has already yielded any results?
If so, what conclusions can be drawn?
When will such results be forwarded to the European Parliament?
Otherwise, when can they be expected?
Does the Commission intend to continue its research?
Is it providing any additional funding for this?
Under the aegis of the Commission, an advisory group known as the Homeopathic Medicine Research Group was set up, comprising homeopaths, experts in clinical and pharmacological . research and methodologists (statisticians and epidemiologists). A budget of ECU 1 million (budget heading APAS B6-8332) has been allocated to this group.
It was given one year in which fulfil its mandate, which was to consider the possibility of undertaking research into homeopathy and the possible methods and standards.
In August 1996 the group submitted a report including recommendations on the application of specific protocols for homeopathic research and the continuous improvement of such protocols with a view to improving the quality and recognition of clinical research into homeopathy.
That report is available to Parliament, and a copy will be sent directly to the honourable Member.
Despite Parliament's express desire to allocate a budget to research into the efficacy of alternative forms of medicine, it was not legally possible to set up any such research activities within the framework of the BIOMED programme (budget heading B6-7412) because alternative forms of medicine are not included among the objectives of the specific programme as adopted by the Council after consulting Parliament.
With regard to the 5th Framework Programme, in the proposal for a European Parliament and Council Decision presented by the Commission (COM(97) 642 Final) homeopathy is not at present included among the programme's priorities.
Question No 105 by Leen van der Waal (H-0570/97) Subject: Canning of intervention beef for food aid
There has been an enormous increase in the European Union's intervention stocks of beef, not least because of the BSE crisis.
At the same time there is a demand for canned beef products for food aid purposes.
For example, North Korea recently submitted an official request to the Commission for 10 000 tonnes of canned beef.
Why has the Commission still not decided to can some of the beef held in intervention so that it can be disposed of as food aid?
Answer
Although purchases of beef into intervention in recent weeks have somewhat slowed down it is beyond dispute that the Community intervention stocks have been rapidly building up since Spring of 1996.
It is obviously in the interest of all concerned that the disposal of those stocks will be performed in an orderly manner once the market situation permits so.
Turning part of the intervention beef into processed cannot products for food-aid purposes may at first sight appear to be an appropriate measure.
However, food-aid channelled through either bilateral contacts or international organizations is typically very specific with regard to the composition and packing of the products concerned taking into account the distribution to the final recipients.
Rather than producing and storing certain cannot products for which there may never be a formal request for food-aid delivery, as internationally defined, the Commission would rather prefer to respond to requests on an ad hoc basis.
With regard to the specific situation in North Korea, the Commission has been following very closely humanitarian developments for some considerable time.
On 23 May 1997, the Commission announced a forthcoming decision for approximately 155 000 tonnes of food products to be supplied as food aid for vulnerable population groups in North Korea.
This food aid (valued at 46.3 MECU) will be supplied mostly in the form of cereals, about 135 000 tonnes, and
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 106 by Bárbara Dührkop Dührkop (H-0572/97) Subject: The position of President Santer and the Commission on quota hopping
There have been many articles in the press recently about the letter from the Commission President to the British Prime Minister on the problem of quota hopping.
There have been comments to the effect that, judging from Mr Santer's initiative and the substance of his letter, the Commission President's position contradicts that of his fellow-Commissioners and the case-law of the Court of Justice.
Can the Commission confirm, as the Court of Justice has done, that the requirement that the crews of British-registered vessels must be resident in the United Kingdom is contrary to Community legislation?
Can the Commission confirm, as the Court of Justice did in the Jaderow judgment, that Community legislation authorizes vessels to unload their catches in any Community port, and that no one can insist that all or part of such catches be unloaded in a given country?
Answer
The Commission fully endorses the case-law of the Court of Justice regarding the exercise of the freedom of establishment in the fisheries sector.
According to the Court, Community law as it now stands, does not preclude a Member State, in authorizing one of its vessels to fish against national quotas, from laying down conditions designed to ensure that the vessel has a real economic link with that State if that link concerns only the relations between that vessel's fishing operations and the populations dependent on fisheries and related industries.
The Commission is of the opinion that landing obligations or obligations related to the residence of the crew cannot be imposed on fishing operators.
However, fishing operators can prove the existence of an economic link with the flag State either by landing a proportion of catches in the ports of the flag State, or by operating with a proportion of crew normally resident ashore in the flag State or by other relevant means of their choice.
In any case, any national requirements designed to ensure the existence of a real economic link between the fishing vessel and the flag State must be non-discriminatory, alternative and proportionate to their aim.
Subject: Marketing of breast-milk substitutes
In 1981, the WHO adopted a code designed to protect women against the marketing of breast-milk substitutes persuading them not to breastfeed.
According to the WHO, inappropriate use of breast-milk substitutes causes the death of 1.5 million babies every year in the third world alone.
The EU endorses the code and the WHO's subsequent resolutions in Council resolution 92/C 172/01.
Article 2 of that resolution states that EU delegations in third countries should serve as contact points for complaints about marketing in those countries by producers from the Member States.
Under Article 5, the Commission is to forward a report to Parliament and the Council every two years on the application of the resolution.
Some companies receiving aid for exports to third countries of breast-milk substitutes are apparently not complying with the code.
Does the Commission believe that companies in breach of these rules should continue to receive export subsidies?
No 4-504/232
It recognized also that the Community cannot legislate for third countries and therefore that it was for those countries to take the necessary measures to apply the international code in their territory as appropriate.
In respect of this principle the Community designated the Commission and its delegations to serve as contact points and receive any complaints or criticisms from the authorities of third countries with respect to the marketing practices of Community based manufacturers.
As replied to relevant written questions the Commission and its delegations have received no such complaint or criticism from the authorities of a third country which would justify the drawing up of a report to the Council and the European Parliament on the matter or which would induce any action in conformity with the Council resolution.
Export refunds serve two main economic purposes.
Firstly they compensate for the different between the prices of agricultural raw materials on the world and Community markets which enables Community exporters to be competitive on third country markets.
Secondly they help to stabilise agricultural markets within the Community by facilitating the export of Community agricultural products including when exported in the form of certain goods.
As such they have both industrial and agricultural policy objectives.
It is for this reason that processed agricultural goods are eligible for a refund in respect of incorporated agricultural products.
Breast milk substitutes are eligible goods and as such are submitted to the same treatment and conditions as other processed agricultural goods.
There is no apparent reason why breast milk substitutes should be subjected to additional requirements since it is primarily the responsibility of the country where the goods are marketed to determine acceptable marketing/promotional practices and for ensuring that these are enforced and respected.
In addition any measures taken at the Community level would not achieve the objective sought.
Question No 108 by Liam Hyland (H-0582/97) Subject: Aiding Ireland's alternative energy potential
In the context of the Commission's proposals for a new five-year programme for promoting renewable energy sources (1998-2002), in what way is the Altener II programme likely to make a worthwhile contribution to alternative energy policies in Ireland, taking into account the potential of Ireland's natural resources, wind, wave, tide, turf (peat), waste, crop and short-rotation forestry?
Answer
The Commission co-funded, in the framework of the ALTENER I programme, a project in the planning infrastructures sub-sector entitled 'Total Renewable Energy Resource in Ireland' carried out by ESB International and ETSU.
The final report is being analyzed at the moment.
The project aimed at evaluating the Practicable Resources to generate electricity from RES take into account the expected demand at short, medium and long term (2005, 2010, 2020).
This project is not a general plan - heating and electricity production not connected to the grid are not included - nevertheless, the Commission would consider, after analysing it, the possibility of co-funding an implementation plan in the future.
ALTENER II, which has not yet been approved by the Council, will continue to support planning infrastructures (action under art.2b) and will support targeted actions (under art. 2e) intended to
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 109 by Gerard Collins (H-0584/97) Subject: Decrease in Irish projects under the EU's LIFE programme for environmental projects
Can the Commission explain why in 1996 only one project from Ireland secured funding frem the EU's LIFE programme for environmental projects and why in 1997 only two Irish projects are to be cofinanced from this programme?
Answer
During the year 1996 Ireland submitted 9 projects or 1.5 % of the total of 601 projects submitted to LIFE by the Member States.
Of those 9, 3 were preselected in consultation with the Irish authorities to be submitted to the independent expert evaluation groups.
The results of this evaluation were favourable to only one of the 3 projects considered, which therefore received cofinancing from LIFE.
This was a project from ΑΗΡ Manufacturing b.v. relating to the development of an integrated monitoring system for atmospheric emissions.
In 1997 the situation improved, thanks to the efforts made by the Irish authorities to provide information.
Ireland has submitted 18 projects this year, of which 7 were preselected for expert evaluation.
It should be added that the selection undertaken by the Commission, the national authorities and the independent experts is based not on national quotas but on the quality of the project, meaning the purely technical and/or ecological advances that may be found in the demonstration projects.
The Commission's decision on the projects to be financed in 1997 will be reached before the end of July 1997.
Question No 110 by Brian Crowley (H-0585/97) Subject: New EU actions for the elderly and the socially excluded
Following the Amsterdam Summit what course of action does the Commission propose to take to ensure that in the future properly funded actions will be made possible for both elderly persons, NGOs working on their behalf and the socially excluded, with particular regard to the need for a new and comprehensive programme to combat poverty?
Answer
The Commission is in the process of analysing the scope for action which the new Treaty will offer, once it is ratified, in the field of social exclusion, poverty and the elderly.
This analysis is not yet complete.
A first analysis has shown that Article 6A and Articles 117 and 118(2), and the new Chapter on Employment in the new Treaty could offer scope for strengthening Community action in these fields.
Article 6A will allow the Commission in consultation with the European Parliament to propose appropriate action to combat discrimination including that based on age.
Proposals will require the approval of all Member States.
In Article 117 of Chapter 4 (Social Policy), 'the combating of social exclusion' is listed as one of the 6 objectives of social policy.
The specific references tot he European Social Charter of 1961 and the 1989 Community Charter of the Fundamental Social Rights of Workers may broaden the scope for action.
A new paragraph in Article 118(2) of the new Treaty allows for qualified majority decisions by the Council of 'measures designed to encourage cooperation between Member States through initiatives aimed at improving knowledge, developing exchanges of information and best practices, promoting innovative approaches and evaluating experiences in order to combat
Debates of the European Parliament
Questîon No 111 by Pat Gallagher (H-0587/97)
Subject: Conditions in Spital Neuropsihici Institution in Siret, Romania
The European Union, through the Phare programme, has made resources available to the Spital Neuropsihici Institution in Siret.
However, the conditions under which the children are kept and the treatment they receive are still well below the norm in a European Union orphanage/hospital.
Has the Commission undertaken any inspection visits to this institution and, if not, will it now do so as a matter of urgency with a view to satisfying itself that the children in this institution benefit from the highest possible standards of care and treatment?
Answer
In 1993-94 and 1994-6, through the "Energy Programme', PHARE financed the installation of electric heaters and supplies of fuel oil for the institution at Siret, which is under the responsibility of the Ministry of Health.
However, these activities were not such as to bring about any radical improvement in the way the children are kept or the treatment they receive, and their living conditions give cause for concern.
The reasons for this are as follows:
- the building, which is more than a hundred years old and was originally designed as a military banacks, making it difficult if not impossible to convert it properly at a reasonable cost is clearly not suitable for accommodating children;
- the size of the institution, which accommodates more than 500 children and adults, some of them suffering from various physical and mental handicaps, makes its closure risky;
- the institution seems poorly provided with human resources, and their training is not appropriate to the requirements and needs of the children in the actual situation.
The Commission will be contacting the Romanian authorities to consider possible solutions, the most logical of which would be the closure of the institution and the readmission of the children to various other establishments in the region.
Question No 112 by Robin Teverson (H-0589/97)
Subject: Freezing of 5b budget funding in Devon and Cornwall, SW of United Kingdom
Further to the freezing of the South West of England Objetive 5b programme earlier this year, does the Commission expect the programme to be unfrozen?
Answer
The Community funds for the South West England Objective 5b Programme are not frozen.
At the Monitoring Committee on 4 December 1996 it was agreed that a stop to the approval of all projects under Priorities 1 (SME and Business Development) and 2 (Tourism) of the programme should be made in light of the need to consider the findings of the interim Evaluation and to consider the scope for large-scale projects.
At the most recent meeting of the Monitoring Committee dated 30 May 1997, it was agreed that offer letters could be issued to those projects where there were no issues outstanding and which were in the Priority 1 and Priority 2 'Approvals Pending' category.
Since this meeting, the programme Secretariat has been given discretion to issue offer letters to projects from the Priority 1 'Preparation Pool'.
The way forward for the remaining years of the programme will be discussed in full at the next meeting of the Monitoring Committee scheduled for 30 September 1997, when the recommendations of the Interim Evaluation exercise will be considered in detail.
Debates of the European Parliament
Does the Commission agree that it is a grotesque contradiction for the EU to be spending 40 times more money on subsidizing tobacco than it does on anti-cancer programmes?
Will the Commission now take steps to eliminate direct income aids for the tobacco industry?
Answer
The Commission can confirm that the Community spends about 900 million ECU each year on subsidies to tobacco growers.
If all raw tobacco production was to be stopped, the industry would obtain supplies from nonmember countries.
Even now about 70 % of tobacco used in the Community for cigarette production is imported from third countries.
Moreover, the Community has created a Fund which covers research for the least harmful varieties, health information and education.
This Fund is financed by the deduction of up to 1 % from the premium given to the tobacco producers, and the Commission proposes to double this amount.
Question No 114 by Hugh McMahon (H-0332/97)
Subject: Philoxenia programme
What progress has been made in developing the Commission's Philoxenia programme?
Answer
The proposal for a First multiannual programme to Assist European Tourism - 'Philoxenia' (1997-2000), was adopted by the Commission on 30 April 1996 and presented to the Council and to the European Parliament.
The European Parliament, the Economic and Social Committee and the Committee of the Regions have each given an Opinion on the proposal during the course of 1996.
The proposal was examined at several meetings of working groups of the Council during the course of 1996, and had been due to be considered at a meeting of the Council of Ministers planned for December 1996.
This meeting was postponed by the President of the Council following indications that the unanimity required for the adoption of the proposal would not be forthcoming in the Council.
There have been no subsequent discussions of the proposal at the level of the Council during the first semester of 1997 under the Dutch Presidency.
With a view to progress the examination of the Philoxenia programme, the Presidency of the Council has been encouraged to resume discussions on the proposal.
The Commission will continue to deploy its efforts over the coming months with a view to reaching a satisfactory conclusion.
