Debates of the European Parliament
Questions to the Commission
Question No 29 by Sören Wibe (H-0619/97)
Subject: Publication of background documents to decision concerning transport subsidies to the Volvo plant in Umeå
The recendy concluded intergovernmental conference decided to introduce a principle of public access to documents.
Everyone living in the EU is to be entitled to read Council, Commission and Parliament documents.
All EU decisions are to be taken as openly as possible.
The Commission is currendy trying to put an end to Swedish transport subsidies to Volvo Trucks in Umeå.
The Commission's point of view is partly based on a secret report by a firm of consultants.
Not even the Swedish Government has been allowed to see this report.
Volvo has no objections to the report being made public.
Only the Commission insists that the report be kept secret.
In Sweden, it is claimed that the report is probably incorrect.
In the light of the outcome of the Amsterdam Summit regarding public access, does the Commission not think that it is time to make public the consultancy report on which the Commission's position in this matter is based?
Answer
As the honourable Member points out, the recent Amsterdam European Council reinforced the principle of public accessibility of information.
The rules governing access to documents are laid down in the code of conduct concerning public access to Commission and Council documents.
The code recognizes the need to increase transparency and to ensure the necessary availability of documents to the general public, but also contains certain exceptions.
Notably, institutions will refuse access to any document whose disclosure could undermine inter alia inspections and investigations and the protection of commercial and industrial secrecy.
As regards the particular document concerning Volvo, the Commission had to undertake a detailed analysis on the different aspects of this case.
The report in question was prepared only as a part of this analysis and served as an internal document to support the examination of the case.
To put such a document into the public domain before the taking of a final decision would be prejudicial not only to the Commission's investigation in that the document would not be placed in its proper context.
The report contained information that was explicitly labelled 'secret' by the Swedish authorities.
Moreover, when supplying, the information to the Commission (via the Swedish authorities), Volvo expressly requested that it be treated as confidential.
The report was then compiled on the basis of the information supplied and used as a part of the overall case assessment.
It clearly contains sensitive business information and could, therefore, undermine the protection of commercial and industrial secrecy.
It is important to point out that the Commission has not yet taken a decision with regard to the aid in question.
A full account of the analysis performed so far will be laid down in the formal decision regarding the opening of the Article 93(2) procedure, which is to be published in the Official Journal this month (the Commission adopted the decision on 23 July 1997).
This formal enquiry will also allow the Commission to take account of all reactions from Sweden, other Member States, the aid beneficiary as well as competitors.
The honourable Member also suggests that the report or rather its conclusions are probably incorrect.
The facts that appear in the report and form the basis of its recommendations have been checked during the site visit and at several subsequent discussions with the Swedish authorities and with representatives of the company.
All the relevant aspects of the report have been made available to the Swedish Government during the examination of the case at meetings and discussions with the authorities.
Page 102
Question No 34 by Bertel Haarder (H-0657/97)
Subject: Fraud in connection with the EU's financial interests
The Member States are required to provide information about irregularities in connection with the administration of the EU's financial interests and it is their responsibility entirely to recover unduly paid sums.
What is the total amount of unduly paid sums that the Member States have failed to recover and which countries and sectors are most affected?
What initiatives will the Commission take to improve the current rules and methods for recovering unduly paid sums?
Answer
Member States are legally bound to communicate to the Commission the amounts affected by irregularities and the recovery of these amounts both in the areas of expenditure such as the EAGGF-Guarantee and structural actions as well as in the area of income in the form of own resources.
In the Commission's annual reports on the fight against fraud you will find the recovery situation for amounts unduly paid and a breakdown by Member State and sector.
For the EAGGF-Guarantee; for example, in the years 1970 to 1996,21.6 % of the amounts involved in irregularities communicated by Member States were recovered (ECU 1946 million unduly paid, ECU 422 million recovered).
For structural actions a binding legal base for communications was introduced in 1994.
Since then 20.4 % of the amounts of notified irregularities have been recovered (ECU 23 million out of ECU 112.9 million).
These figures relate to cases communicated by the Member States.
The Commission is aware that not all cases of irregularities or recovery operations are communicated.
Member States are regularly reminded of their obligations to report on the situation on recovery in all detected cases.
In order to come to grips with this serious problem, the Commission is carrying out several actions in line with the SEM 2000 exercise as approved by the European Council and following the initiatives in its 1997/98 work programme on the fight against fraud:
• the Commission regularly confronts the Member States with the incomplete notifications and the unsatisfactory recovery on the basis of the facts known to the Commission.
Member States are invited to take the necessary action where recovery appears to be possible;
• the Commission insists on the subsidiary liability of Member States which fail to recover the amounts due by the economic operators concerned.
As far as EAGGF expenditure is concerned, the so-called clearance of accounts procedure allows to charge amounts to the Member State concerned if the non-recovery was due to its negligence.
In the area of traditional own resources, a special interservice working group follows up all major cases in order to clearly identify the responsibilities of importers and Member States respectively;
• in the field of structural funds, the Commission has tabled proposals to clarify the rules for the application of Article 23 of EEC Regulation 4253/88 which are currently under discussion in the funds Committees, and those of Article 24, which include possible penalties for Member States failing to fulfil their obligations;
• as far as own resources are concerned, proposals are under way aimed at clarifying Article 17 of EEC/Euratom Regulation 1552/89 which deals with the responsibilities of Member States in collecting EU resources;
• in the same vein, the Commission has adopted a communication to the Council and the Parliament on the management of preferential tariff arrangements.
Numerous irregularities and fraud are found in this area as well as a very lax level of recovery by the Member States (around 10 %).
The communication proposes a number of actions to rectify this situation.
The Honourable Member's attention is drawn to two more political implications of the failure to recover adequately Community money.
First of all, any shortfall to the budget will have to be compensated anyhow through the fourth resource.
But the effect of this is to shift the burden from the Member State who has faded to recover money on to all Member States through their GNP-contribution.
This is quite unjustified and unfair.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 35 by Jan Wiebenga (H-0682/97)
Nearly two years ago, on 20 September 1995, it was stated in the Commission's contribution to the European Parliament's debate on the Wiebenga, Roth, d'Ancona, Caccavale and Lehne reports that the Commission - like Parliament - was very interested in the idea of setting up an observatory 'for the collection and analysis of data on the subject of migration in all its aspects'.
Since then, the Commission has had both a pre-feasibility study and a feasibility study carried out on the subject.
What were the findings of the studies, and will the Commission forward copies to Parliament?
What conclusions has the Commission drawn from the findings?
In other words, will an observatory for migration be set up and if so, what will its status be, what form will it take and what wUl its terms of reference be?
Answer
The Commission welcomes the renewed interest of the European Parliament in the idea of an immigration 'Observatory', which has been under discussion, as the Honourable Member rightly recalls, for some years.
In particular, it was the subject of a significant passage in the Commission's 1994 Communication on Immigration and Asylum policies.
It must be recognised, however, that there was no strong support at that time in the Council for setting up such an 'Observatory', in the sense of a quasi-independent agency financed by the Community budget.
The Commission nevertheless felt that further research was needed on the idea, particularly in order to ascertain whether there was an information and analysis gap which needed filling, which a possible 'Observatory' might fill.
That was the purpose behind the Pre-feasibility Study, which has already been published, and the subsequent Feasibility Study, which will be published before the end of the year.
In both cases, the Commission can forward copies of them to the European Parliament.
These studies analyzed the activities of all relevant information-gathering institutions and concluded that gaps do exist in the accessibility arid coordination of existing information as well as in timely, reliable and harmonized statistics on immigration-related matters.
The researchers point out also the lack of a comprehensive and up-todate source of information on national policy and legislation, of analysis on migratory's trends and patterns, of an early warning system and of a comprehensive source of information on what research is currently being done.
The conclusion of the Study is that a European Migration Observatory could usefully be set up as a network of existing institutions making a wide use of information and technology.
The Commission is conscious of the importance of avoiding duplication with existing sources of information.
It is therefore currendy working in two main areas: firstly, it is making efforts to improve the current system of analysis and gathering of migration statistics by a deeper involvement of the Commission statistical department (EUROSTAT) in the collection and analysis of immigration statistics.
Secondly, it is exploring the possibility and the legal form for establishing a network of experts on asylum and immigration, equipped with computer technology, possibly by using the Internet as recommended by the study, which could collect and make available information to the widest possible public interested in this field.
Such a network could usefully improve the access of the policy-makers to information, data and researches on asylum and immigration.
It is therefore too soon to give precise answers to the Honourable Member's questions on the status and terms of reference of a possible Observatory' but the Commission will keep the Parliament closely informed of the future possible developments of its activities in this area as well as of ifs financial costs.
Question No 40 by Inger Schörling (H-0602/97) Subject: Use of antibiotics in stock rearing
Antibiotics are the most important and often the only medicine we have for curing bacterial diseases.
Any use of antibiotics may give rise to resistant bacteria.
The more antibiotics are used, the greater such a risk.
In stock rearing, large quantities of antibiotics are used as additives in feed to enhance growth.
These antibiotics may also give rise to resistant bacteria.
Some of the antibiotic preparations used in animal feed are closely related to the antibiotics used on humans.
Resistant bacteria found in stock rearing can be transferred to humans via food.
European consumer organizations have argued that it is enough to demonstrate that there is a risk of resistant bacteria spreading from animals to humans to introduce tight restrictions on the use of antibiotics in stock rearing.
Resistant bacteria may develop into a bigger disaster than BSE if the day arrives when we are unable to cure our most common bacterial diseases.
What is the Commission's position on the use of antibiotics as feed additives to promote growth?
What measures will the Commission take to improve animal protection and to restrict and control the use of antibiotics?
Answer
The use of antibiotics in animal feed is regulated at Community level by Directive 70/524/EEC, which provides for a very strict procedure for the authorization of these substances.
Antibiotics enable animals to assimilate their food better, improving their metabolism and enhancing their resistance to the stresses caused by intensive stock breeding.
This results in the reduction of the animals' morbidity and mortality rates.
The Commission emphasizes that only nine antibiotics are authorized.
Moreover, since the directive entered into force, the Commission has banned fifteen antibiotics, considering that their use should be restricted to human and veterinary medicine.
As a precautionary measure, the Commission recendy banned the use of avoparein, because of concerns about a possible link between its use in animal feed and the increased resistance of certain human pathogens to vancomycin, an antibiotic belonging to the same family as avoparein which is indispensable to human medicine.
However, as the opinion of the scientific committee on animal feed makes clear, the Commission must specify that such a link of cause and effect has not been demonstrated on the basis of current scientific data.
The Commission is currendy setting up a programme to monitor bacterial resistance in animals that have consumed antibiotics, with the aim of detecting the possible appearance and development of resistant bacteria.
Moreover, it is planning to initiate a series of studies into the spread of resistance caused by the use of antibiotics.
As the scientific committee on animal feed recommends, it would be desirable for the scope of these studies and programmes to be broadened to include the medical use of antibiotics as well as their use in animal feed.
In this context, the Commission is currendy exploring the possibility of setting up a network to monitor resistance to antibiotics in the field of human health.
Question No 41 by Mark Watts (H-0613/97) Subject: Slaughter of sheep in France for 'Eid-el-Kabir' festival
In June the Commission confirmed that it had received complaints from animal welfare organizations about the mass ritual slaughter of thousands of sheep in France on 17 and 18 April 1997, contrary to Directive 93/119/EEC ('). and promised that it would consider taking further action including opening infringement proceedings under Article 169.
Video evidence of these events has now been sent to the Commission.
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
The Commission is aware that some practices at certain open-air celebrations of the Muslim festival of Eid-el-Kabir in France do not appear to be in conformity with the Community directives on the protection of animals during slaughter.
The Commission has intervened with the French authorities on several occasions concerning this matter.
France has indicated that it has been felt necessary for the practices to be tolerated in a limited number of localities to protect public order and public hygiene.
This is a sensitive issue involving the religious and cultural practices of ethnic minority populations.
It is therefore important that the Commission does not act precipitously.
However, the Commission cannot, in principle, accept that these infringements of Community legislation should be permitted to continue.
The Commission intends to make further contact with the competent authorities in France in connection with this issue.
It seems reasonable to ask the French authorities to confirm that in future the festival will be organized according to the Community legislation concerned.
If the French authorities fail to provide adequate guarantees concerning future compliance with Community law in this respect, the Commission will consider the opening of the infringement procedure provided for under Article 169 of the EC Treaty.
Subject: Agenda 2000
How will the accompanying measures under new Objective 2 of the structural funds financed by EAGGF Guarantee operate?
Secondly, will areas currendy dependent on fishing be eligible to apply for such funds and what percentage of EAGGF Guarantee subventions will be allocated for such developments?
Pursuant to the Commission proposals set out in 'Agenda 2000'
Rural areas eligible under the new Objective 2 (which targets economic and social restructuring) will benefit from accompanying measures financed from the EAGGF, Guarantee Section.
Those measures will cover all types of support measures for structural adjustment and rural development currendy confinanced by the EAGGF, Guidance Section.
Although it would be premature at this juncture to specify the operational details of this arrangement, the Commission can confirm that it will seek the greatest possible simplicity as regards planning.
The measures will apply alongside those financed from the ERDF, the ESF and, possibly, the FTFG in the same programme at the level of the region eligible under Objective 2.
The areas in crisis dependent on fishing eligible under the new Objective 2 will also have access to the ERDF, the ESF and the FTFG.
All the coastal areas of the Community, without exception, will continue to receive aid for the restructuring of the fisheries sector, over and above the aid granted to changes currendy under way in the sector under Objectives 1 and 2, with appropriations being drawn from the EAGGF, Guarantee Section.
No decision has yet been taken on the areas eligible under Objectives 1 and 2, nor on the respective budgets allocated to those Objectives, nor - all the more so - on the appropriations allocated to fisheries.
Accordingly, it is not possible at this juncture to specify the appropriations from the EAGGF, Guarantee Section, which will be allocated to fisheries.
Question No 43 by Miguel Arias Cañete (H-0624/97) Subject: Agenda 2000 and direct income support
It is planned as part of Agenda 2000 to limit the direct income support received under all the COMs to a maximum amount per farm.
What maximum amount is the Commission currendy considering?
Does the Commission intend to fix a single limit for the whole of the EU, or variable limits depending on the varying productive potential of the Member States?
Answer
In Agenda 2000, the Commission announces its intention to propose the introduction of an individual ceiling covering all direct payments granted under the Common Market Organizations.
No precise amount has been proposed so far, nor any differentiation according to Member States.
This will be part of the detailed legislative proposals that the Commission will table in the coming months.
In addition, the Commission will propose to allow Member States to introduce differentiation criteria according to commonly agreed rules.
While excluding re-nationalization, this is based on the idea that Member States are better placed than the Commission to define differentiation criteria adapted to their specific problems.
Question No 44 by Josu Imaz San Miguel (H-0625/97) Subject: Reform of the COM in milk
The Commission's Agenda 2000 proposal envisages a reform of the market in milk, extending quota arrangements until the year 2006 and progressively cutting prices by 10 % between 2000 and 2006.
This reduction of reference prices would be accompanied by a new annual subsidy of ECU 145 for dairy cows.
Does the Commission agree that in keeping with the spirit of the Cork Declaration, this aid to producers per dairy cow should be adjusted inter alia in terms of the size of the farm, and that it should particularly favour mountainous areas and small dairy farms?
Answer
In fact, the Commission proposes in Agenda 2000 to introduce a new yearly payment for dairy cows, at a level of ECU 145.
Together with the new payment introduced for dairy cows in the beef context, the total dairy cow premium would be ECU 215.
Having said that, at least two elements of Agenda 2000 proposals could contribute to the Honourable Member's preoccupations.
On the one hand, the Commission proposes to allow Member States to introduce differentiation criteria according to commonly agreed rules.
While excluding re-nationalization, this is based on the idea that Member States are better placed than the Commission to define differentiation criteria adapted to their specific problems.
This could be used to differentiate the payments according to the farm size, in favour of small holdings.
On the other hand, the Commission suggests gradually transforming the Less-Favoured Areas scheme into a basic instrument to maintain and promote low-input farming systems.
This could be used in particular to support dairy farming systems in mountainous areas, provided that they respect agreed conditions.
Question No 45 by Alexandros Alavanos (H-0633/97)
Subject: Progress of programmes for 1994-1999 submitted by Greece for the development of forestry and the prevention of forest fires
In a debate in plenary sitting of the European Parliament on 17.7.97 concerning forest fires and the destruction of forests in Greece, Sir Leon Brittan, Vice-President of the Commission, stated, inter alia, that 'the regional development and operational programmes adopted for Greece for 1994-1999 also provide for forestry protection measures and reafforestation measures and that it is up to the
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
Forestry measures are provided for in the 13 regional operational programmes, as well as in the national programme for the agricultural sector, which form part of the Community support framework for Greece, worth a total of ECU 235.2 million of public funds, including ECU 176.4 million from the EAGGF Guidance Section.
The take­up rate for these appropriations has so far been highly satisfactory, since, in the period 1994­1996, it exceeded projections for those years, and also surpassed 50 % of total appropriations for the CSF for the entire period 1994­1999.
In view of the good take­up rate for these appropriations and the importance of these operations, the possibility of requesting an increase is currendy being examined by the Greek authorities.
Question No 46 by Veronica Hardstaff (H-0640/97) Subject: BSE
Can the Commission state how many Member States they are taking infraction proceedings against in relation to Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy and in each case, on what grounds?
Answer
As known, the Commission has opened the first stage of infringement proceedings under Article 169 of the EC Treaty against 10 Member States in respect of their apparent partial faUure to implement and/or enforce some aspects of Community legislation concerning BSE.
The Commis­sion dispatched letters of formal notice to Belgium, Finland, France, Germany, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Italy, Portugal, Spain and Sweden.
The subject matter of the infringement procedures concerns, principally, inadequate implementa­tion of Commission Decision 96/449/EC (heat treatment system for processing animal waste) and failure to sufficientiy control the respect of Commission Decision 94/381/EC prohibiting the use of mammalian tissues in feedingstuffs destined for ruminants.
Details are presented to the European Parliament in the second Progress Report of the European Commission to the temporary committee of the EP on the follow­up of recommendations on BSE, dated 9 July, p. 25­27.
Furthermore, in respect of the measures adopted by the UK to improve the enforcement of Community rules establishing the ban on exports, the Commission may open infringement proceedings if it would appear that those measures do not fulfil in practice the export ban and it would seem particularly appropriate for the Commission to institute proceedings on the point related to the supervisory checks to be performed in meat processing establishments.
Question No 47 by Wayne David (H-0641/97) Subject: BSE
Can the Commission confirm that following the vote in Council on 22 July they will be introducing Community­wide legislation on specified risk materials and when they propose to do so?
Answer A Commission Decision No 97/534/EEC (■) on this matter was adopted on 30 July 1997.
Article 10 of the Decision provides that it shall be applicable from 1 January 1998.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 48 by Mair Morgan (H-0644/97) Subject: BSE
Does the Commission agree that the export ban on British beef is a temporary measure pending a return to normal operation of the Single Market?
Answer Yes.
The ban was imposed to protect human and animal health.
Question No 49 by Ilona Graenitz (H-0663/97) Subject: British meat
Can the Commission say whether, since the ban was introduced, any meat has been taken to the United Kingdom for boning and subsequendy re-exported to the other Member States and, if so, what amounts of meat were involved and from which Member States?
Answer
The Commission does not have the precise information requested.
Indeed, the Commission is not informed of the quantities of bone in meat sent from other Member States to the UK for deboning and re-exportation to the initial Member States.
The legal position at present is set out in Articles 1 and 1(a) of Commission Decision 96/239/EC, as amended by Commission 96/362/EC.
Article 1 forbids the UK to send to other Member States or third countries meat of bovine animals slaughtered in the United Kingdom. Article 1(a) allows the UK to dispatch meat, meat products and meat preparations obtained from bovine animals which were not slaughtered in the United Kingdom, but only from establishments under official veterinary control which have put in place a system of tracing of the raw material which will guarantee the origin of the material throughout die whole production chain.
The United Kingdom, in accordance with the above-mentioned Decision, has forwarded to the Commission and the other Member States the list of establishments meeting these conditions, and each consignment of products sent to other Member States is accompanied by an official health certificate.
Inspections by the Commission have confirmed that the required veterinary control and tracing systems are in place in the listed establishments.
Question No 50 by Vassilis Ephremidis (H-0654/97) Subject: Havoc caused by hailstorm in the Plain of Thessaly
The adverse effects of the common agricultural policy on Mediterranean produce, particularly cotton, were compounded for producers in 17 communities over an area of 8, 000 hectares by the hailstorm which hit the Plain of Thessaly on 9 August, totally destroying their crops.
Does the Commission intend to take immediate measures to compensate the producers affected by the hailstorm and to provide special income support for the current marketing year and, in particular, will it fund the necessary works to provide protection from hailstorms in order to avoid similar disasters in the future?
Answer
The Commission would like to point out that the Community budget contains no instrument for compensating for annual harvest or production losses following a natural disaster, such as that caused by the hailstorm in question.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 51 by Johanna Maij-Weggen (H-0660/97)
Subject: Rice conflict with the Netherlands Antilles/Aruba
Can the Commission Member state how he intends to resolve or has resolved the conflict over rice with the Netherlands Antilles/Aruba?
Answer
The situation in the rice market is alarming and that for the first time since many years: more than 162,000 tonnes of paddy rice have been offered to intervention.
This state of affairs can be attributed on the one hand to this year's relatively important harvest especially in Spain where the drought is over and on the other hand to the increased imports of Indica rice.
The 1996/97 volume of imports has been the same as in 1995/96, i.e. around 522,000 tonnes, however, generally, the rice sector is confronted with an increase of imports from 435,000 tonnes in 93/94 to 522,000 tonnes in 96/97 (20 %).
The Overseas Countries and Territories have become very rapidly the main exporters of rice to the Community on the basis of preferential conditions.
In January 1997, at the request of Italy and Spain, the Commission adopted a safeguard clause to limit those imports within certain quantities.
This measure has been extended by the Council until November 1997.
Unfortunately, the Council is not yet in a position to come to a more sustainable solution for this preferential arrangement although the Commission has already made a proposal to this effect in February 1996.
A draft compromise prepared by the Luxembourg Presidency is currendy being discussed in the Council, where unanimity is required.
Question No 52 by Robin Teverson (H-0666/97)
Subject: Battery cages
The UK Government has declared its support for a total ban on the use of battery cages for chickens in egg production.
Current EU proposals for regulations on the size of future battery cages will make little difference to the extremely unnatural conditions the birds have to live in, and amounts to no more than 'tinkering' with the present system.
Rather than presiding over the introduction of new cages, is the Commission instead prepared to back the UK Government's call for a total ban, which is supported by public opinion and animal welfare organizations?
Answer
Article 9 of the Directive on minimum standards for battery cages requires the Commission to submit a report on scientific developments regarding the welfare of laying hens under various systems of rearing, accompanied by any appropriate adjustment proposals to the Council.
The Scientific Veterinary Committee presented its report on the welfare of laying hens at the end of last year to the Commission.
It is the intention of the Commission to present the report as laid down in Article 9 of the Directive together with a proposal for legislation on the welfare of laying hens later this year to the Council.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 53 by Liam Hyland (H-0673/97) Subject: Young farmers
In view of the continuing decline in the number of farmers in the European Union and in view of the high age profile of farmers, does the Commission have any plans to expand and develop its existing education, training and information programmes in agriculture with a view to encouraging more young people to consider farming as a career?
Answer
The Commission's report on 'Young farmers and the problem of succession in European agriculture' analyses the social and economic difficulties encountered by young farmers, examines the existing Community instruments aiming to promote their establishment as farmers and proposes guidelines and recommendations to improve actions in favour of young farmers, including orientations for education and information.
However, before proceeding to a concrete follow-up to the report, the Commission has still to wait for the final position to be taken by the European Parliament and the Council.
The current reflections on the future of rural development and also on agricultural policy take into consideration the needs of young farmers and the new period of rural development and agricultural policy as a whole are aimed at encouraging young people to consider farming as a career.
Question Nò 54 by María Izquierdo Rojo (H-0592/97) Subject: Investment to counter emigration in the north of Morocco
What concrete form will be taken by Commission aid for setting up industrial estates in the north of Morocco, capable of containing the major problem of emigration?
Answer
To this must be added EIB loans worth ECU 267 million and aid from the Member States worth ECU 471 million. This gives a total of ECU 851 million.
This request is currendy being examined by the Commission.
Question No 55 by Brian Simpson (H-0594/97) Subject: Aviation safety
Would the Commission agree that the elimination of risk is fundamental to safety in the aviation industry?
If so, what steps is it prepared to take to ensure that inflammable vapour is not allowed to collect in empty fuel tanks of EU-registered aircraft, leading to the possibility of an explosion similar to that of TWA flight 800 on 17 July 1996?
Answer
The Commission agrees that improving aviation safety requires that conclusions and lessons drawn from accident investigations must be used as expeditiously as possible in order to try to eliminate the risk of recurrence.
That is why it follows with interest the consultation process launched by the American Federal Aviation Administration to follow on recommendations from the American National Transportation Safety Board on the basis of the investigations into the TWA flight 800 accident.
The Joint Aviation Authorities, with which the Community cooperates in aviation safety, are also participating in this process aimed at finding the best ways of avoiding the recurrence of similar explosions.
Debates of the European Parliament
Any changes to airworthiness rules which would derive from this process would be transposed into Community law according to the procedures laid down by Regulation 3922/91 on the harmonization of technical requirements and administrative procedures in the field of civil aviation.
Question No 56 by Astrid Thors (H-0607/97)
Finnish newspapers reported on 15 July 1997 that the Commission had been a spanner in the works in the Helsinki Commission for the Baltic Environment.
The conduct of the EU prevented or delayed decisionmaking in the Baltic Commission and we have therefore lost opportunities to enhance environmental protection.
Is it true that the Commission obstructed decisions on the environment by the Helsinki Commission, without consulting the Member States, or are there other reasons why the EU Commission was unable to endorse the Baltic Commission's proposals for recommendations and decisions?
Answer
The European Commission occupies since July 1996 and for two years the chairmanship of the Helsinki Commission on behalf of the Community.
This is the reason the Commission is currendy playing an increased role in the affairs of the Helsinki Commission.
In this involvement the European Commission is guided by the principle of the highest degree possible of environmental protection for the Baltic Sea and always in close cooperation with the Member States there and the other third countries which are Contracting Parties to the Baltic Sea Convention.
At times environmental considerations need to be integrated with other Community policies.
The time consuming consultations within the Commission and with the Member States are then conducted in a way that the highest environmental standards are assured.
The European Commission seeks to exercise utmost efficiency in its involvement in the work of the Helsinki Commission within the limits of its existing resources.
Question No 57 by Gary Titley (H-0612/97)
Is the Commission considering the introduction of a levy on the sale of blank audiocassette tapes and copying equipment?
If so, will it ensure that its proposals include an exemption from such a levy for the valuable work of charity services such as the 'talking newspaper' movement for blind people?
Answer
The honourable Member Mr Tidey has asked whether the Commission is considering the introduction of a levy on the sale of blank audiocassette tapes and copying equipment.
Eleven Member States of the Community have at present levy systems in place which remunerate rightholders for private reproduction (home copying) of protected material.
At Community level, no separate initiative is envisaged at this stage.
However the Commission has already undertaken initiatives with the aim of adapting the existing copyright systems to the new technological environment.
On 20 November 1996, it has adopted its Communication on Copyright and Related Rights in the Information Society (COM(96)568 final), which sets out the results of an extensive consultation of interested parties on the subject matter and announces the Commission's Internal Market Programme in the area of copyright for the coming years.
As set out in the Communication in some detail, the existing legal framework will need readjustment where that is necessary for the proper functioning of the Single Market and for the creation of a favourable environment which protects and stimulates creativity and innovative activities across Member States.
In so doing, a fair balance of rights and interests between the different categories of rightholders and between rightholders and users must be safeguarded.
This balance has to reflect the new digital environment with its new risks and opportunities.
Debates of the European Parliament
The Commission is now in the process of drafting the necessary legislative measures, as announced in the Communication.
This draft legislation should be presented to the Commission for adoption very soon.
In this context, the issue of home copying should be dealt with in a flexible way which allows in particular Member States which provide remuneration schemes to maintain exemptions for relevant categories of disabled persons.
When deciding on the appropriate action in this field, the Commission will take due account of all interests involved.
Question No 58 by James Nicholson (H-0620/97)
Would the Commission inform Parliament on progress being made towards the removal of the ban on beef from the United Kingdom?
Question No 59 by Christine Crawley (H-0642/97) Subject: BSE
To what extent has the UK Government fulfilled the terms of the Florence Agreement?
Question No 62 by Arlene McCarthy (H-0650/97) Subject: Ban on export of British beef
The five preconditions of the Florence agreement have now been met by the UK.
In the light of this, would the Commission support a phased lifting of the beef ban?
In the interests of consumer safety and given that BSE is now recognized as a Community-wide problem, does the Commission think that the conditions applied to British beef under the agreement should be applicable throughout the EU?
Joint answer
Under the provisions of the conclusions of the Florence European Council (21 and 22 June 1996), progress has been made in gradually restoring the single market of beef and beef products.
The UK has taken legal measures to comply with the five preconditions of the Florence Agreement.
The selective slaughter programme was delayed until January 1997.
Several Community inspections to verify correct implementation of the preconditions have been carried out.
The last mission with particular emphasis on the situation in Northern Ireland has been undertaken in June 1997.
The mission team reported that full implementation has not been reached.
In Northern Ireland advanced implementation of the preconditions and a low number of BSE cases were observed.
The following two main aspects need special attention:
Further progress should be made with regard to the implementation of the Selective Slaughter Programme in Great Britain before considering the preconditions in this part of the UK referring to this programme as being fulfilled.
The Florence Agreement asks for effective implementation of the over 30 months rule including the destruction of the animals.
Even though the final destruction may be ensured, the very limited current incineration capacity implies that this cannot at present be achieved in the short term.
The UK government has submitted a proposal for a Certified Herd Scheme and a Date-Based Scheme, which would be the basis for a first step to lift the export ban.
The Scientific Veterinary Committee examined the UK proposals for the Certified Herd Scheme at its plenary meeting of 11 June 1997.
The committee found that the proposal was not adequate in its present form and made a number of suggestions.
As a result the UK authorities have submitted a revised scheme which was discussed by a subgroup of the Scientific Veterinary Committee on 1 September 1997.
Based on the report of this subgroup the proposal is under discussion this week at the plenary meeting of the Scientific Veterinary Committee, Animal and Public Health section, with a view to giving the Commission its opinion.
Debates of the European Parliament
The conclusions of the Florence European Council are not designed to define rules to be applied to Member States other than the UK.
However, there are certain aspects included in these conclusions, which were taken into account in the process of developing the legislative framework for the whole Community with regard to TSEs: new rules for bovine identification and movement controls are laid down in Council Regulation No 820/97 of 21.4.1997.
Furthermore, Commission Decision 97/534/EC prohibits the use of material presenting risks as regards TSEs from 1.1.1998;
Question No 60 by Simon Murphy (H-0643/97) Subject: BSE What is the Commission's assessment of the degree of risk of BSE throughout the EU?
Answer
The Commission considers the UK to be a country with a high incidence of BSE with about 170,000 cases in total (3,472 new cases in 1997 until 16.8.97).
In 8 other European countries (') about 550 cases are reported in total of which roughly half in Switzerland (257) and one third in Ireland.
In the countries where no BSE cases have been reported, it is not possible to exclude a certain degree of contamination, given the fact that these countries imported meat and bone meal from the UK and that the incubation period of BSE is several years.
Inspections were carried out in Member States in 1996 and have revealed certain deficiencies in implementing the Community measures on BSE, in particular with respect to surveillance, training and diagnostic facilities, and implementar tion and control of rendering standards and the ban on use of mammalian protein in ruminant feed.
It should also be underlined that currendy no Member State has been found to be BSE-free in accordance to the ΟΓΕ criteria.
As regards third countries, from where the EU might import beef and cattle-based products, Norway, New Zealand, Australia, Canada and the United States have provided the Commission with a dossier concerning their BSE status.
While the dossiers of Australia, Canada and the United States are currendy under assessment by the Scientific Veterinary Committee, the same committee has already provided "a positive opinion on the New Zealand dossier, i.e. on the basis of the evidence provided it regards the BSE risk in that country as negligible.
It is foreseen that the information given by New Zealand will be verified in the framework of the bilateral cooperation agreement between that country and the EU in the field of veterinary matters.
The situation of Norway is less advanced because Norway, which had some imported BSE cases, has first embarked on an eradication programme which is currendy under discussion at the Scientific Veterinary Committee.
Question No 61 by David Thomas (H-0645/97) Subject: BSE
When does the Commission expect the results of scientific scrutiny of position papers from the UK on steps to lift the beef export ban and when will these be put before the Standing Veterinary Committee?
Answer
The Scientific Veterinary Committee examined the UK proposal for the Certified Herd Scheme at its plenary meeting of 11 June 1997.
The committee found that the proposal was not adequate in its present form and made a number of suggestions.
As a result the UK authorities have submitted a revised scheme which was discussed by a subgroup of the Scientific Veterinary Committee on 1 September.
Based on the report of this sub-group, the proposal will be discussed, with a view to giving the Commission its opinion, at the plenary meeting of" the Scientific Veterinary Committee, Animal and Public Health sections on 16 and 17 September.
') New cases in EU Member States in 1997: DE: 1; FR: 2; NL: 2; IRL: 45; PT 11.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 63 by Clive Needle (H-0622/97) Subject: Control of EU funds in Romania
What was the Commission able to achieve at the seminar held in Bucharest in July on the management and control of EU funds allocated within the framework of Community projects or programmes such as Phare, and how does the Commission evaluate the effectiveness to date of such programmes in the reform of the banking and financial sector in Romania?
Answer
The seminar on the management of Community funds in Romania focused on practical problems concerning the implementation of Phare programmes but also discussed management and policy issues.
A number of practical points such as the improvement of quality and the timely submission of programme documents were discussed and each side gave details as to the standards expected.
In more general terms, it was agreed that quantified objectives must be identified and programme progress monitored.
Effectiveness of all Phare programmes, including in the banking and financial sector in Romania, is evaluated in conformity with Phare procedures: day-to-day follow-up by the EC Delegation and supervision. by the programme managers; annual monitoring report of implementation by a specialised unit of the Commission (last monitoring implemented in the first part of 1997 for the projects in the financial sector); ex-post impact assessment by a specialized unit of the Commission (planned for 1997).
Question No 64 by Philippe De Coene (H-0623/97) Subject: Labelling and chocolate products
Article 2 of the Commission proposal for a Directive relating to cocoa and chocolate products intended for human consumption (COM(95)722) (') says that chocolate products containing up to 5 % of vegetable fats other than cocoa butter may be marketed in all the Member States, provided that, in addition to the list of ingredients, that those substances are present, their labelling is supplemented by a clear, neutral and objective statement.
Is this labelling requirement compatible with the decision of the Court of Justice in Case C-51/94 (the Béarnaise judgment) and the provisions of labelling directives 79/112/EEC (2), 96/21/EC (3) and 97/4/EC (4)?
Answer
The Commission considers that its proposal for a directive is compatible with both the decision of the Court of Justice in the 'sauce béarnaise' case and the three labelling^directives to which the honourable Member refers.
With regard to the Court's decision, this concerns national labelling measures which, in the absence of Community harmonization, could constitute a barrier to trade.
The Commission's proposal, on the other hand, aims to introduce a single and uniform labelling rule for the whole Community, thereby preventing the appearance of barriers to trade.
It must therefore be concluded that the decision in question does not apply at present.
Furthermore, the Court accepts - albeit in a national context - that an obligation to mention the presence of an ingredient, in addition to its appearance on the list of ingredients, in order to prevent confusion in the minds of consumers, cannot always be excluded.
In the view of the Commission, this reasoning can be applied by extension to Community legislation and in particular to the case in question.
Indeed, the Commission believes that mentioning the presence of vegetable fat in a chocolate product, in
Debates of the European Parliament
The labelling directives do not conflict in any way with the measure envisaged.
On the contrary, Article 6(6) of Directive 79/112/EEC explicitly provides for the possibility of a measure of this kind.
Question No 65 by Maj-Lis Lööw (H-0626/97)
The Commission's Green Paper on relations between the European Union and the ACP countries on the eve of the 21st century states that the Union should adopt a more coordinated and consistent approach to the ACP countries.
The main focus of the Lomé Convention is aid together with trade and preference rules, the main principle being that the ACP countries are granted tariff-free access to the EU market.
There are exemptions, however, for most products covered by the EU's common agricultural policy, i.e. a number of commodities in which the developing countries are in fact competitive.
Does the Commission intend to take any initiative to bring about greater coordination between aid policy and the common agricultural policy?
Answer
One of the topics in the Commission's Green Paper on relations between the Community and the ACP countries on the eve of the Twentyfirst Century is entitled 'To be more effective, the Union must adopt a more coordinated and consistent approach to the ACP countries'.
As the Green Paper explains, the notion of consistency concerns the need to take the development policy objectives set out in Article 130u of the EC Treaty into account in the policies implemented by the Community.
It should also be recalled that the Community's internal policies cannot be the object of an international commitment on the latter's part.
However, specific measures can be -and are being - carried out to ensure increased consistency between aid policy and, in particular, trade policy, the common agricultural policy and fisheries policy.
When development projects are devised, their impact on the Union's common policies is assessed in order to determine their feasibility, especially when they concern the development of agriculture for export purposes.
Coordination concerns the actions of the donor community as a whole.
In this context, the Green Paper presents two possible options: a) the formulation of a global European strategy vis-à-vis the ACP countries, b) the establishment of an information, monitoring and coordination mechanism for the Member States' and the Community's development policies.
With regard to the Lomé Convention's trade regime, it is true that certain exceptions are maintained for imports of agricultural products covered by the CAP.
It should be noted, however, that the very high level of preference granted to the ACP countries has not made it possible to promote and diversify exports from those countries - exports which continue to be largely based on commodities and geared to the European market.
Rather than the improvement of preference arrangements, it is broadly recognized that the weakness of the ACP countries' global trade performance is essentially connected to a variety of factors which affect their capacity for production and supply.
These issues should therefore be addressed in terms of the need to improve the competitiveness of the ACP countries' economies.
Furthermore, export growth is increasingly linked to measures concerning market access, such as those relating to technical norms, plant health rules, the protection of foreign investment, the promotion of exports, etc.
The midterm revision of the Fourth Lomé Convention is emphasizing these aspects, which are also examined in the Green Paper.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 66 by David Bowe (H-0628/97) Subject: NASA/ESA Cassini Mission
Is the Commission aware that, on the admission of experts at the European Space Agency (ESA), highly efficient solar panels could have been developed by the ESA over a 5-year period for probes to Saturn, which therefore could have replaced the 32kg plutonium-238 RTG in the October 6 NASA/ESA Cassini Mission (they can currently operate as far as Jupiter)?
Under these circumstances does the Commission not agree that in principle only missions powered by such panels should be undertaken, to avoid the hugely disproportionate risks involved in the use of RTGs, and will the Commission now call on the USA and all other states to postpone all RTG-based missions, including Cassini, at least until these panels are available, as well as seeking to have this principle agreed by treaty?
Question No 67 by Nuala Ahern (H-0631/97) Subject: NASA/ESA mission
If so, what is the Commission's position as to the advisability of this project considering the risk of an accident causing considerable radioactive doses to EU citizens, and has it indicated any such position to NASA, ESA or the US Administration?
Does space law as defined by various treaties limit in any way the EU or Member States' rights to object to such missions, or provide procedures for such objections?
Question No 68 by Peter Crampton (H-0634/97) Subject: NASA/ESA Cassini mission
Has the Commission studied NASA's Final Environmental Impact Statement on the NASA/ESA Cassini mission (June 1995) which suggests that a reentry accident could lead to serious radiological contamination of 5 billion people, not to mention the environment, and can the Commission realistically justify such a risk in terms of the scientific knowledge gained?
Question No 69 by Paul Lannoye (H-0659/97) Subject: NASA/ESA Cassini Mission
Concerning the October 6 NASA/ESA Cassini Mission, bearing in mind the previous explosions in Titan IV rockets, and bearing in mind the .1964 accident which led to complete burn-up and traceable global dispersion of a kilogramme of Plutonium 238 from a Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator (RTG), a SNAP-9A (Systems for Nuclear Auxiliary Power) (') and the many other accidents involving RTGs, and that the Cassini project carries 32 times as much Plutonium 238 as the SNAP-9A, has the Commission studied the possible radiological impact on the health of EU citizens and the EU environment from such burn-up and global dispersion of this large amount of such a highly toxic and extremely radioactive substance?
Question No 70 by Carlos Pimenta (H-0661/97) Subject: NASA/Cassini Mission
Is the Commission aware that the proposed October 6th NASA/EAS Cassini Mission involves an Earth 'swing-by' on return from Venus in order to use gravitational forces to catapult the mission to Saturn, that the craft will be travelling at 68 000 km/h only 500 km above the Earth's surface, creating a small but nevertheless real risk of reentry and burn-up of the 32 kg of Plutonium 238, and can it justify the serious risk of radiation exposure to citizens and the environment of the EU and elsewhere, and if so, how?
Aware of the forthcoming launch of the Cassini spacecraft, the Commission is reviewing the situation.
') 'Emergency Preparedness for Nuclear-Powered Satellites', by OECD Nuclear Energy Agency and the Swedish National Institute of Radiation Protection, Stockholm, April 1989.
Debates of the European Parliament
The decision to launch the spacecraft is not in the hands of NASA or ESA, but the President of the United States himself must give his approval to launch the spacecraft from his territory.
At this moment President Clinton stUl has to take the decision.
Therefore the Commission has contacted the Executive Office of the President of the United States to express the concern in Europe, obtain the risk assessments and ask to be kept informed on the latest developments.
An independent safety evaluation of the Cassini mission has been performed in the US as part of the nuclear launch safety approval process by an Interagency Nuclear Safety Review Panel, which is supported by experts from government, industry and academia.
The Executive Office of the President requested a second evaluation in order to be absolutely sure of the safety of the mission.
A final review of environmental aspects continues to be underway in the US prior to the President's decision before the planned launch in mid-October.
The Commission understands that nuclear electrical power sources onboard spacecraft use neither fusion nor fission processes.
Furthermore, the Cassini power source has safety features which aim to prevent it from burning in the atmosphere and designed to mitigate biological or health effects in case of an accident.
It uses, for instance, highly insoluble ceramic-form plutonium dioxide divided among 18 small independent modular units, each with its own heat shield and impact cell.
In its contacts, the Executive Office of the President of the United States has assured the Commission that all the necessary measures are taken in order to minimize any risk of exposing the world's population or the environment to serious radiation risks in case of an accident with the Cassini mission.
The Commission will stay in touch with President Clinton's Executive Office to keep the situation under review.
Question No 71 by Graham Watson (H-0630/97)
Answer
The Commission does not foresee any action related to the harmonization of public holidays in the Community.
Question No 72 by Gianfranco Dell'Alba (H-0636/97)
Subject: Discrepancy between French government and Commission forecasts concerning the 1997 government deficit
The Commission's 'spring forecasts', compiled by the appropriate departments and published at the end of April, stated, among other things, that France's government deficit ratio in 1997 would fall within the 3 % ceiling laid down by the Maastricht Treaty.
However, it is now common knowledge that the ratio stands at about 3.6 %, as, indeed, the outgoing Prime Minister implicitly admitted during the handover of power to the new Government.
The obvious conclusion, therefore, is either that the Commission made a gross error in its forecasts, raising grave doubts as to its perspicacity, or else the figures for France were deliberately rigged.
Answer
Last spring, the Commission estimated that France's public sector borrowing requirement in 1997 would be 3 % of GDP.
Debates of the European Parliament
During the same period, France's eight main economic research institutes forecast that the average deficit in 1997 would be 3.1 % of GDP (see report of National Audit and Budgets Committee of 2.4.1997).
Consequentiy, the Commission's forecasts were completely in Une with those of the majority of private institutes.
Moreover, they were very close to those of other international organizations, such as the OECD and the IMF.
The Commission's budget forecasts for all Member States are established on the basis of available data and unchanged policies - in other words, taking account of measures adopted or announced with sufficient precision - and in accordance with certain economic assumptions.
In this context, the Commission analysed the finance bill and the social security financing bill for 1997, as well the budget results known at the time, and considered that the French Government's objectives in the field of public finances could be achieved.
The Commission would recall, in this regard, that the spring forecasts are based on the budget results on 1 March, which may be different to the operating results at the end of July to which the honourable Member refers.
Furthermore, the freezing of budget appropriations at FF 10 billion decided last March constituted an additional guarantee of the government's will to meet the budgetary objectives.
The Commission budget forecasts are made on thè basis of unchanged policies.
This methodological principle should be borne in mind when comparing the actual public sector borrowing requirement in 1997 with the spring forecasts.
This methodology, which is adopted by the majority of international organizations and economic research institutes, will continue to be applied by the Commission.
Question No 73 by Christine Oddy (H-0639/97)
Subject: Sri Lankan assassination
Is the Commission aware of the assassination of a Sri Lankan MP, Mr A. Thangathurai, in an attack which also killed two school teachers and injured several children as he left a school in Trincomalee.
As a member of the Tamil United Liberation Front (TUFL) he was committed to a nonviolence solution to the current conflict.
What steps will the Commission take to assist in finding a lasting peace?
Answer
Sri Lanka is a country with which the EC has a long-standing Cooperation Agreement and the Commission therefore has a deep interest in that country and would naturally like to see internal peace re-established in it.
The situation in Sri Lanka remains a cause for serious concern for the European Commission.
The Commission is indeed aware of the assassination of a Sri Lankan Member of Parliament, Mr A Thangathuraj in an attack which also killed and injured civilians.
Mr Thangathuraj, a member of the Tamil United Liberation Front (TULF), which supports the ruling People's Alliance government although it has no cabinet post, was known as a moderate person.
Unfortunately the killing of civilians has been part of the conflict since it started in 1983.
All ethnic communities have suffered from it and all parties to the conflict have been responsible for these acts.
Two weeks after the above-mentioned incident, another Member of Parliament, Mr Mahroof, a Muslim member of the United National Party (UNP) has been murdered, also near Trincomalee.
As Mr Thangathuraj, Mr Mahroof was known as a moderate politician in favour of a peaceful settlement of the conflict.
It is widely suspected that the Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelan (Lilt) is behind the assassinations of these two members of parliament.
The European Union has in its declarations and also in demarches made to the government in Colombo urged the government to protect the civilians and to prevent the violations of International Humanitarian Law.
At the same time the European Commission will continue to encourage all the parties involved to reach a peaceful, negotiated setdement of this issue.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 74 by Manuel Medina Ortega (H-0651/97) Subject: Seizure of the fishing vessel 'Albor uno' by Morocco
Can the Commission give details of the circumstances surrounding the seizure by the Moroccan authorities of the factory ship Albor uno, which is based in the port of Las Palmas, and of the measures it has taken or intends to take to secure the vessel's release?
Answer
With a view to resolving the matter of the Albor Uno rapidly, the Commission's delegation in Rabat made repeated representations, both verbal and in writing, to the Moroccan Ministries of Fisheries and Foreign Affairs.
The Commission is able to inform the honourable Member that in the case of naval inspections, the bilateral agreement between the Community and the Kingdom of Morocco provides that the setdement of disputes is the responsibility of vessel owners, who can choose to make amends for the presumed offence with a compromise formula (payment of a fine) or by judicial means (after laying down a guarantee).
According to the latest information on the Albor Uno affair available to the Commission, the owner chose to settle the matter with a compromise formula by paying a fine of 60, 000 dirhams.
The vessel was therefore released on 1 September 1997.
Question No 75 by Patricia McKenna (H-0656/97) Subject: Thorp nuclear reprocessing plant at Sellafield, Britain
On 18 August last Britain's Nuclear Installations Inspectorate (Nil) granted British Nuclear Fuels Ltd (BNFL) 'consent to operate' the Thorp reprocessing plant in Sellafield.
Article 6(a) of Council Directive 80/836/Euratom (') states: 'Every activity resulting in an exposure to ionizing radiation shall be justified by the advantages which it produces.'
Has the Commission received any data from the British authorities to demonstrate how emissions from Thorp will be 'justified' by any advantages?
If so, what data has it received?
Would it agree that Thorp wUl be of no economic benefit to Ireland but that the recent discovery of plutonium in the teeth of Irish teenagers and of rising levels of technetium-99 in Irish seafood indicates that Ireland has been adversely affected by Sellafield's activities?
What action does the Commission plan to take?
Answer
The requirement of justification of a practice is stated in Council Directive 80/836/EURATOM (2) (Basic Safety Standards) as one of the general principles for the limitation of doses for controllable sources.
In a subsequent Council Directive 84/467/EURATOM (3) these standards are amended and in particular Article 6(a) is replaced by the following: 'the various types of activity resulting in an exposure to ionizing radiation shall have been justified in advance by the advantages which they produce'.
Thus the justification principle is meant to apply in advance to a type of activity (e.g. nuclear fuel processing), rather than to an individual undertaking.
In a Commission Communication (85/C347/03) (4) it is further specified that the justification of any type of activity is the duty of the competent authority in Member States and that transposition is generally demonstrated by the existence of the regulations specifically concerning the type of activity.
The new Basic Safety Standards (96/29/EURATOM (5), to be implemented by May 2000) confirm this approach.
Debates of the European Parliament
16.9.97 reviewed as to justification (Article 6.2 of Directive 96/29/EURATOM) whenever new and important evidence about their efficacy or consequences is acquired.
Judgements with regard to justification are the responsibility of the national competent authority.
There is no requirement to inform the Commission on such matters.
The Commission is not in a position either to judge whether Ireland has an economic benefit from nuclear electricity generation and nuclear fuel reprocessing elsewhere.
With regard to the impact of emissions from Thorp in Ireland or in another Member State, the Commission has given an Opinion in application of Article 37 of the Euratom Treaty (92/296/EURATOM).
The Commission is of die opinion that the implementation of the plan for the disposal of radioactive waste from Thorp is not liable to result in radioactive contamination, significant from the point of view of health and the environment in another Member State.
The issue of rising levels of technetium-99 in seafood in the Irish Sea has already been dealt with in the replies to written questions E-0443/97 by Mrs Pollack (') and P-2225/97 by Mrs McKenna.
The recent publication in a scientific journal of the results of a comprehensive study on radioactivity in teeth from adolescent children in the UK and in Ireland has no implications for the health of this population.
While the study indicates a correlation of plutonium activity with distance from Sellafield, even the highest activity concentrations are orders of magnitude below the total alpha activity from natural radiation sources.
The authors of the study themselves conclude that the implied risk increment from plutonium in teeth or in the skeleton is insignificant.
Question No 76 by Heidi Hautala (H-0662/97) Subject: Financing nuclear safety within Tacis and Phare
The Government of Sweden has questioned the financing of nuclear safety under the Tacis programme in its letter dated 9 July to the Director of the programme.
Sweden suspects that there is an unofficial agreement to expand nuclear programmes in the former Soviet Union.
According to Sweden, the boards of the programmes are led to accept nuclear safety financing for new nuclear reactors, since they would be budt anyway even without adequate safety standards.
The Commission is expected to comment on the letter from Sweden at the next board meeting of Tacis/Phare on 25 September.
In addition, Tacis and Phare nuclear safety financing seems to be less conditioned to the shutting-down of the most dangerous nuclear reactors than the EBRD nuclear safety fund NSA.
The laxer rules of Tacis/Phare contracts seem to run the risk of demands for the closure of nuclear reactors within EBRD's NSA.
Can the Commission publish all contracts for EU financing of nuclear safety in Russia and Central and Eastern Europe?
Will the Commission extend the conditions of the EBRD nuclear safety fund NSA to Tacis and Phare programmes?
Will the Commission guarantee that EU financing of nuclear safety in the framework of Tacis and Phare programmes is not used for building new nuclear power stations?
Will the Commission put more emphasis that at present on alternatives to nuclear power such as energy conservation and efficiency, renewable energy sources and gas?
The Government of Sweden indeed has handed over a letter to the Commission on the issue.
A reply is under preparation.
Lists of contacts are published on a regular basis in the Official Journal of the European Communities.
The last list was made public in May 1997 (O.J. S 94 of 16.5.97).
The technical assistance programmes of the European Commission have, wherever possible, supported the NSA programmes by providing technical assistance to the countries concerned with a view to enabling them to fully and timely implement the agreements concluded by the EBRD on behalf of the contributors to the Nuclear Safety Account.
This includes support to the regulatory authorities for the licensing of equipment provided under the NSA and support to perform indepth safety assessments.
Debates of the European Parliament
Such EBRD agreements do not contain a unique set of conditionalities with regard to the shutting down of the least-safe reactors, but range from conditional closure scenarios as in the case of Bulgaria and Lithuania to a sequence of actions to be undertaken by the local parties as in the case of Russia without clearly-formulated closure commitments.
The closure of Chernobyl was negotiated in the context of the G7 and not in the context of the EBRD/NSA.
A further assessment of the energy sectors in the candidate countries can be found in the recentiy-published opinions on application for membership of the European Union.
The 1996 annual reports of Tacis and Phare contain a comprehensive overview of the allocation of funds in the energy sector, including information on future activities in this field.
Copies of these reports will be sent directiy to the honourable Member of Parliament.
Question No 77 by Robert Evans (H-0665/97)
Is the Commission aware of allegations made by the BUAV (British Union for the Abolition of Vivisection) that universities in Belgium are regularly using stray cats and dogs for experiments, contravening Directive 86/609/EEC (')?
Some of the animals involved were found to have been kept in distressing conditions during a raid on one of the universities by the Belgian police.
The BUAV has submitted evidence to, the Belgian Ministry of Agriculture but following their apparent lack of action has submitted an official complaint to the European Commission (No 97/4379).
Can the Commission inform me of the progress of the complaint, what investigations it has made in this regard and what action it is willing to take?
Answer
To protect their rights, the Commission does not reveal the identity of complainants.
However, it can confirm to the honourable Member that Complaint No. 97/4379 against Belgium relates to alleged breaches of the Animal Experiments Directive (CouncU Directive 86/609/EEC (2).
Further to the formal registration of this complaint, the Commission wrote to the Belgian authorities in July 1997.
In its letter, the Commission set out the alleged breaches and asked for information on the action taken against the illegal practices cited as well as more general information on how Belgium ensures the investigation and penalization of breaches of the implementing legislation for the directive.
The Commission is currendy awaiting the Belgian response and assures the honourable Member that it will take whatever action it considers appropriate in the circumstances.
Question No 78 by Frederik Willockx (H-0669/97)
Following the near-accident with the Sirocco attraction in the Walibi amusement park in Belgium, it has been determined that there exist virtually no legal provisions governing safety checks on amusement park attractions.
Can the Commission state whether such legislation exists in the different Member States?
Does it not consider, with a view to the safety of the public and in the interests of uniformity, that it is necessary to lay down, at European level, minimum standards with which the different attractions in amusement parks must be required to comply?
) Council Directive 86/609/EEC on the approximation of laws, regulations and administrative provisions of the Member States regarding the protection of animals used for experimental and other scientific purposes, OJ L-358, 18.12.1986, p.l.
Debates of the European Parliament
Answer
A study on safety in amusement parks and fairgrounds was carried out with Commission funding in 1995.
The study concluded that accidents are more likely to result in hospital stays than other leisure accidents.
It was also found that the requirements on control of the equipment vary considerably between the Member States.
Different national laws are in force.
Some Member States have specific requirements for fairground equipment, while in other Member States they fall under more horizontal product or labour safety legislation, while in some states there are no mandatory requirements at all.
The requirements on inspection of the equipment vary as well.
In some Member States an inspection must be carried out before the equipment can be used, in other Member States recurrent inspection is mandatory, while in other states inspection is only voluntary.
The Commission had planned a directive containing technical requirements for fairground equipment.
Following the Edinburgh Summit of the European Council in 1992 and in view of the priorities set and the principles of subsidiarity, the Commission decided to withdraw it from its legislative programme.
Nevertheless, the Commission has given the European Standardization Organizations a mandate to elaborate a European standard containing technical specifications for fairground equipment.
Once these standards are drawn up, the Commission intends to use them as minimum requirements.
Question No 79 by Konstantinos Hatzidakis (H-0671/97) Subject: Implementation of Greek Ministry of Transport programmes
Recent reports in the Greek press indicate that programmes being joindy funded by the EU and jointly implemented by the Commission and the Greek Ministry of Transport and Communications - such as the programmes for the modernization of the postal system, the raUways and telecommunications, the restructuring of Olympic Airways and the construction of the airport at Spata - are progressing at an unsatisfactory pace and that, consequently, the take-up rate of Community funds is some way behind schedule.
What is the precise stage of implementation of each of these programmes and, in particular, what is the take-up level of Community budget appropriations?
Answer
The state of implementation of the programmes to which the honourable Member refers, which are cofinanced by the European Regional Development Fund under the second Community support framework and by the Cohesion Fund (Spata airport), is as follows:
Modernization of the Greek postal system: the implementation of this operational programme has not yet really begun, since the organizational structure necessary to ensure the proper implementation of such a modernization programme has not yet been created.
It will take the form of a task force led by a team of international experts skilled in both the modernization of public postal systems and the implementation of large-scale capital projects in this field.
Railways: the principal implementation structure (Ergose) has been created and has become operational, with the help of a project manager.
The current take-up rate is 37 %-r
Telecommunications: the section of the programme concerning the improvement of the telecom munications network (OTE) is progressing well.
The current take-up rate is 36 %.
Spata airport: this major project is being implemented rapidly, the take-up rate reaching 22 % between June 1996 and June 1997.
The restructuring of Olympic Airways: which started in 1994, does not involve any Community financing.
It is exclusively financed by the company's own commercial revenues and various aid measures granted by Greece.
The aid measures were initially authorized by the Commission on 7.10.1994 subject to various conditions.
Following nonrespect of these conditions by the Greek authorities, the Commission is presendy re-examining the compatibility of this aid with the common market.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 80 by Mark Killilea (H-0672/97)
According to the results of EUROSTAT's recent publication, a 'Focus on EU Youth, from education to working life', unemployment is the main worry for 70 per cent of young people.
Will the Commission outline what new approaches could usefully be adopted at EU and national level to tackle unemployment among young people?
Answer
Youth employment is a severe problem in the Community with some five million young people without jobs and an average unemployment rate of virtually twice the overall rate (21.5 % compared with 10.7 % in 1995).
Youth integration into working life remains therefore a major concern for the Community.
The new Amsterdam Treaty provides new instruments for a more effective cooperation at European level to fight youth unemployment.
The extraordinary meeting of the European Council this coming November, on the proposal from the Commission, will discuss Employment Guidelines based on ambitious and realistic targets, quantified objectives and will set up monitoring procedures that will enable both MS and the European CouncU to review progress done every year in the implementation of the Guidelines.
In addition, the exchange of best practice between MS will be reinforced and encouraged. '
The new strategy certainly will keep focus on young people and their integration into working life.
Activation of labour market policies has a key role to play by avoiding that young people without jobs drift towards long term unemployment and by improving their employment chances through a better adaptation of measures to individuals' needs.
Considering the high risks of unemployment run by those young people who do not possess a sufficient level of education and training, improving young people's employability is a main line of action which implies new efforts in education and training to provide all young people with fundamental skills and reduce the number of those who do not complete their education.
Reducing non-wage labour costs can also play an important role to facilitate young people's access to work experience.
Further the functioning of the Public Employment Services and other services will be addressed to promote the 'pathway' approach from school to work.
Finally, the possibilities of the structural funds will be fully utilized to provide a European value added to the above efforts.
Question No 81 by Pat Gallagher (H-0675/97)
Can the Commission indicate the steps it has taken and/or intends to take to ensure that the hauliers affected by the blockages on French roads are adequately compensated for the losses they incurred through no fault of their own?
Answer
As the honourable Member will know, the Commission has no legal power to intervene in disputes like those that impeded traffic in France unless and until there is evidence that a Member State Government has been negligent in its duty to maintain freedom of movement.
However, at an early stage during each of the recent disputes in France, the Commission was in repeated contact with the French Government to emphasize that all necessary measures needed to be taken to secure the free circulation of road hauliers and road passenger traffic as required in the Treaty and also to express the need for the French Government to make arrangements to ensure that appropriate compensation could be paid to hauliers who had incurred losses.
During the course of the second dispute - the blockade of French ports - the Commission drew the attention of the French Minister of Transport to the considerable number of complaints received, which had called into question the efficiency and effectiveness of the compensation arrangements and asked for an urgent report on all the compensation claims submitted.
A preliminary response from the French authorities indicates that some 1700 claims had been received by the beginning of June.
The Commission was informed that two ministerial instructions had been sent to the Prefectures in an effort to streamline the processing of the setdement of such cases by the competent local authorities and a team had been set up to expedite the decisions on compensation.
Debates of the European Parliament
Further to that, the Commission has continued to urge the French Government to ensure better performance in responding to claims and has called a consultative meeting of the various Community road haulage associations whose members have been affected in order to exchange information and to examine ways in which the situation could be improved.
Question No 82 by Brian Crowley (H-0678/97)
In its Agenda 2000 communication, the Commission put forward the view that the present 13 Community initiatives should be reduced to 3 broad fields, one of which includes human resources.
Will the Commission give assurances that the Horizon strand of the Employment initiative which is aimed at improving the employability and job prospects of disabled people, will continue to receive priority attention and sufficient funding?
Answer
The Agenda 2000 communication envisages that - within the context of a reduced number of Community Initiatives - one of the three areas of intervention will cover human resources.
Particular attention will be directed at the problems of equal opportunities (in the context of a more cohesive and inclusive society, as stated in another part of the communication).
It is clear to the Commission that people with disabilities often suffer discrimination, particularly in relation to access to the labour market and therefore that, in one form or another, they will be among the groups to be targeted under this measures of the human resources initiative.
Question No 83 by Gerard Collins (H-0679/97)
In the Treaty establishing the European Community, Declaration (No 1), the possibility of introducing a new title on tourism was to be examined, however, such a tide is not included in the Amsterdam Treaty and the importance of tourism to the present Member States and those involved in the next enlargement process is not recognized in Agenda 2000.
Can the Commission indicate the priority it attaches to the role of tourism, particularly its job creating potential taking into account the findings of the European Travel Commission which shows for example that the number of US tourists in Europe in 1996 amounted to 8.9 million with over 2 million coming from Japan and will the Commission also indicate what plans, if any, it has to encourage job creation in the tourism sector?
Answer
The Community is fully aware of the significant contribution which tourism could ensure to the fundamental goals of the Community and in particular to growth and employment.
The proposal for a Council Decision on the First Multiannual Programme to Assist European tourism 'Philoxenia', submitted by the Commission in 1996, aims to contribute to the achievement of these objectives through encouraging the improvement of quality and competitiveness of European tourism.
The proposal is under examination by the Council.
In the meantime, the Commission, and the Presidency of the Council, recognizing employment in the tourism industry as' a priority action, have taken the initiative to organize in Luxembourg on 4 and 5 November 1997 a European Conference on Employment and Tourism with a view to promoting innovative approaches and to propose guidelines for action in this field.
The contribution of honourable members of the Parliament to the conference will be particularly welcome.
The results of the conference will enrich also the discussions which will take place later in November in the framework of the European Summit on Employment.
Moreover, as shown by the Commission in its report to the European Parliament and to the Council of 2 July 1997 on Community measures affecting tourism, the issues of employment, education and vocational training in tourism are considered also in the framework of the implementation of other Community programmes and actions.
Debates of the European Parliament
Question No 84 by Niall Andrews (H-0680/97) Subject: Blocking EU assistance in countries where children are abused
In the context of a serious complaint made by Human Rights Watch against a Government in Central America denouncing the behaviour of its police and security agencies towards street children, the European Union is asked to lay down criteria ensuring that no financial assistance will be given to a juvenile justice system that violates children's rights.
Will the Commission now state how it will actively carry out such a demand as a legitimate means of protecting children wherever EU interests are involved?
Answer
For several years now, the Community has been paying great attention to respect for children's rights, and to defending their dignity and their lives.
In particular, it endeavours to make its own contribution to the serious problem of street children.
It was, furthermore, at the Community's initiative that the UN General Assembly adopted in December 1992 the initial resolution on the tragic fate of street children and that, since 1995, that Assembly and the Human Rights Commission have adopted a resolution each year on children's rights, which deals with the most serious problems faced by children in the world, in particular the tragic fate of street children.
In its resolve to give high priority to a positive approach encouraging respect for human rights and democratic principles, in particular by giving its active support to various initiatives, the European Commission pays particular attention to the situation of children who have been abandoned and ill-treated and supports specific projects implemented by specialist NGOs.
In those countries where allegations have been made of police brutality towards street children, it supports programmes to train pohce officials and all those responsible for administering the juvenile justice system, paying particular attention to rights pertaining to the care and protection of children..
Convinced of the imperative need to pursue such activities on behalf of street children, the Commission takes the view that it would not be expedient to suspend its support in countries where the justice system violates children's rights.
On the contrary, it ensures that the general public are not penalized for the attitude of their government by channelling its assistance through civil society organizations.'
It deems it essential to pursue the dialogue with the governments of the countries concerned, with particular reference to street children, so as to remind them of the international obligations they undertook, in particular when ratifying the Convention on the Rights of the Child, Article 20 whereof lays down that 'a child temporarily or permanendy deprived of his or her family environment, or in whose own best interests cannot be allowed to remain in that environment, shall be entided to special protection and assistance provided by the State'.
As regards the Rio Group of countries, the Foreign Ministers of those countries and of the EU have reiterated the commitment of their governments to respect for the rule of law.
In particular, among the areas which should be included in the dialogue already existing between the two parties, it has been agreed that priority should be given to the implementation of national child protection plans, with particular reference to street children and abandoned children.
Question No 85 by Edgar Schiedermeier (H-0681/97)
Subject: Social dialogue: atypical terms of employment
The agreement reached by the social partners in the context of the social dialogue concerning atypical terms of employment leaves the most important questions open.
Is the Commission taking no action?
Or is it finally making use of its right of initiative?
Answer
The Commission considers that part-time work is an important factor in promoting employment, and it therefore welcomes the framework agreement on this subject concluded on 6 June 1997
